(window.ASH_QB=window.ASH_QB||{})["general-medicine"]=[
 {"q":"A soldier posted in Siachen is brought unconscious to the emergency ward. In order to rule out hypothermia, how will you best measure his temperature?","o":["Oral","Rectal","Infrared tympanic thermometer","Axillary"],"a":1,"e":"In the given clinical scenario, the rectal temperature would be the best site to measure the core body temperature.\n\nAt extremes of high or low temperatures, like in Siachen, the axillary and the oral temperatures are highly misleading and should not be used to measure the core body temperature.\n\nThe infrared tympanic thermometer measures the temperature of the eardrum which is representative of the core body temperature. However, it often ends up measuring the temperature of the ear canal. This is because of inadequate insertion into the ear canal due to poor manufacturing design or the presence of cerumen. Hence, it is not a reliable method of measuring the core body temperature.","t":"Clinical Examination","c":"Clinical Examination and Acid-Base"},
 {"q":"A patient in the emergency ward stays still while lying supine and hesitates to move. He also has very shallow breathing. This attitude of the patient is most likely due to which of the following conditions?","o":["Renal colic","Peritonitis","Acute pancreatitis","Meningitis"],"a":1,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of peritonitis. A patient with peritonitis lies supine without movement and has shallow breathing as any movement exacerbates the pain.\n\nOption A: In renal colic, patients have a restless attitude, often rolling around because of pain.\n\nOption C: In acute pancreatitis, the patient usually bends forward and assumes a knee-chest position because of abdominal pain.\n\nOption D: Patients with meningitis present with neck stiffness.","t":"Clinical Examination","c":"Clinical Examination and Acid-Base"},
 {"q":"An alcohol addict presented with easy fatiguability. On examination, he is found to have pallor. Peripheral smear showed macrocytes and anisocytosis. Which of the following findings is most likely to be seen in this patient?","o":["Atrophic glossitis","Migratory glossitis","Strawberry tongue","Macroglossia"],"a":0,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of megaloblastic anemia likely due to folate deficiency caused by alcohol addiction. Folate deficiency leads to atrophic glossitis because of damage to the epithelial surfaces.\n\nOption B: Migratory glossitis / geographic tongue is due to rapid loss and regrowth of filiform papillae of the tongue. It is a benign inflammatory condition of the tongue associated with psoriasis and reactive arthritis.\n\nOption C: Strawberry tongue is due to the hypertrophy of fungiform papillae. It is seen in scarlet fever and Kawasaki disease.\n\nOption D: Macroglossia is an abnormal enlargement of the tongue and can be congenital or acquired. New-onset macroglossia in adults is highly suggestive of amyloidosis.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0eab761167ac4cde935c3357c5a3ebbcx720x227.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/79f72f2568564fcc809888157e34f555x720x960.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/caec2a95d19f47b296067d62fb78e620x691x559.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4676ad77c8684d88854b7a5328dc953bx512x455.PNG"],"t":"Clinical Examination","c":"Clinical Examination and Acid-Base"},
 {"q":"A woman presents with bluish discoloration of the skin and mucous membranes. All of the following may be responsible for the given symptoms except:","o":["Methemoglobin exceeds 1.5 gm%","Sulfhemoglobin exceeds 0.5 gm%","Reduced hemoglobin exceeds 2 gm%","Oxygen saturation below 85%"],"a":2,"e":"Bluish discoloration of the skin and mucous membranes is suggestive of cyanosis. Cyanosis is seen when the concentration of deoxygenated or reduced hemoglobin exceeds 4 gm/dL.\n\nCyanosis is detectable with methemoglobin ≥1.5 gm% and/or sulfhemoglobin ≥0.5 gm%.\n\nCentral cyanosis is detectable when the oxygen saturation <85%. In dark-skinned individuals, it may be detectable at an oxygen saturation of 75%.\n\nThe types of cyanosis are:\n\nCentral cyanosis\nPeripheral cyanosis\n\nDue to diminished arterial oxygen saturation/presence of abnormal hemoglobin derivative.\n\nDue to diminished peripheral blood flow.\n\nMucous membranes and skin are both affected\n\nMucous membranes of the oral cavity or those beneath the tongue are spared.\n\nDecreases after O2 supplementation\n\nDoes not decrease after O2 supplementation","t":"Clinical Examination","c":"Clinical Examination and Acid-Base"},
 {"q":"Which of the following skin findings is not associated with an endocrine abnormality?","o":["Acanthosis nigricans","Diffuse hyperpigmentation","Casal’s necklace","Purple striae"],"a":2,"e":"Casal’s necklace is not associated with an endocrine abnormality. It is a feature of pellagra, which occurs due to the deficiency of niacin.\n\nOption A: Acanthosis nigricans is seen in multiple endocrinopathies like acromegaly, Cushing’s syndrome, polycystic ovary syndrome, and insulin-resistant diabetes mellitus.\n\nOption B: Diffuse hyperpigmentation is seen in Addison’s disease and ectopic ACTH syndrome.\n\nOption D: Purple striae are seen in Cushing’s disease.","t":"Clinical Examination","c":"Clinical Examination and Acid-Base"},
 {"q":"What are petechiae?","o":["Large extravasation of blood in the skin","Skin hemorrhages measuring 2-3 mm","Skin hemorrhages measuring 1-2 mm","Skin hemorrhages measuring 3-4 mm"],"a":2,"e":"Petechiae are defined as skin hemorrhages appearing as minute non-blanching spots that measure less than 2 mm.\n\nA few conditions causing petechiae have been stated below:\n\n• Infectious diseases: Cytomegalovirus (CMV) infection, infective endocarditis, meningococcemia, and viral hemorrhagic fevers.\n\n• Non-infectious conditions: Vasculitis, thrombocytopenia, leukemia, scurvy, and vitamin K deficiency\n\n• Drugs: Anticoagulants (warfarin, heparin), quinine, etc.\n\nLarge extravasation of blood in the skin is known as ecchymosis.\n\nPurpura are also non-blanching spots but unlike petechiae, they measure ≥ 3 mm in size.","t":"Clinical Examination","c":"Clinical Examination and Acid-Base"},
 {"q":"A 28-year-old woman presented to the clinic with the lesions shown below. Which of the following conditions is/are associated with this lesion?\n\n1. Chronic liver disease\n\n2. Pregnancy\n\n3. Scleroderma\n\n4. Thyrotoxicosis\n\n5.\n\nOsler-Weber-Rendu Syndrome","o":["1 and 5","2, 3 and 5","1, 3 and 4","1, 2 and 4"],"a":3,"e":"The lesions shown in the image are spider naevi (nevus araneus). They are seen in:\n\n• Estrogen excess states such as pregnancy and OCP use\n\n• Chronic liver disease - especially alcoholic liver disease\n\n• Thyrotoxicosis\n\nThey are vascular lesions characterized by the tortuous dilatation of arterioles just underneath the skin. On applying and releasing pressure over the lesion they typically fill from the centre outward.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5a1b5b6d8da744f6b069efc6aa796def.JPEG"],"t":"Clinical Examination","c":"Clinical Examination and Acid-Base"},
 {"q":"You observed the following finding in a patient in the OPD. Which among the following conditions is least likely to cause this finding?","o":["Endocarditis","Bronchiectasis","Tricuspid atresia","Aortic dissection"],"a":3,"e":"The given image shows digital clubbing. Among the given options, aortic dissection is least likely to cause digital clubbing.\n\nClubbing is a selective bulbous enlargement of the distal segments of the fingers and toes due to the proliferation of connective tissue, particularly on the dorsal surface.\n\nCauses of clubbing are:\n\n• Pulmonary\n\n• Bronchogenic carcinoma\n\n• Lung abscess, empyema, bronchiectasis\n\n• Interstitial lung diseases\n\n• Cystic fibrosis\n\n• Sarcoidosis\n\n• Asbestosis\n\n• GI\n\n• Inflammatory bowel disease (Ulcerative colitis, Crohn's disease)\n\n• Sprue, celiac disease\n\n• Neoplasms - esophagus, liver, bowel\n\n• Cirrhosis - juvenile and biliary\n\n• Chronic active hepatitis\n\n• Cardiovascular\n\n• Cyanotic congenital heart disease\n\n• Subacute bacterial endocarditis\n\n• Aortic aneurysm\n\n• Arteriovenous fistula\n\n• Patent ductus arteriosus with right to left shunt\n\n• Atrial myxoma\n\n• Endocrine\n\n• Grave’s disease (thyroid acropachy)\n\n• CNS\n\n• Hemiplegia (unilateral clubbing)\n\n• Skin disease\n\n• Palmoplantar keratoderma\n\n• Fischer-Volavsek syndrome.\n\n• Malignancies\n\n• Thyroid cancer\n\n• Thymus cancer\n\n• Hodgkin disease\n\n• Disseminated chronic myeloid leukemia.\n\nNote: Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) does not cause clubbing. If clubbing is present in a patient with COPD, underlying lung cancer and bronchiectasis must be ruled out.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1a12f7750e044336917752467bdb5571.JPEG"],"t":"Clinical Examination","c":"Clinical Examination and Acid-Base"},
 {"q":"A 40-year-old patient presents with loss of appetite, jaundice, and easy bruisability. He is a chronic alcohol user, and the following abnormality is noted on examination. What is the most likely cause of this nail change?","o":["Scurvy","Hypoalbuminemia","Chronic iron deficiency","Folate deficiency"],"a":1,"e":"The nail change seen in the given image is leukonychia totalis. It is a prominent feature of hypoalbuminemic states. The given clinical scenario is suggestive of liver cirrhosis, which leads to hypoalbuminemia.\n\nLeukonychia is an opaque whitish discoloration of the nail and is seen in the following conditions:\n\n• Dietary deficiency: Protein-energy malnutrition, pellagra, zinc deficiency\n\n• Drugs: Cytotoxic agents, Steroids, Retinoids, Pilocarpine\n\n• Dermatological pathology: Alopecia areata, erythema multiforme, exfoliative dermatitis, and SLE\n\n• Systemic diseases: Liver cirrhosis, renal failure, and cardiac failure\n\n• Miscellaneous: Trauma, malignancy, and toxins (arsenic, fluoride)\n\n• Idiopathic\n\nNote: The leukonychia secondary to arsenic poisoning is termed leukonychia striata or Aldrich–Mees lines.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/85dca4fdf73f4899a24c88ccee55d4e6.JPEG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1c1890878a194ff6820c3a4a36eb6731x720x616.JPEG"],"t":"Clinical Examination","c":"Clinical Examination and Acid-Base"},
 {"q":"A resident is examining a patient with bilateral leg swelling below the knees. He notes that on applying pressure, the indentation does not persist on release. What is the likely cause of the edema?","o":["Myxedema","Nephrotic syndrome","Chronic lymphangitis","Acute lymphatic filariasis"],"a":0,"e":"Bilateral non-pitting edema in the lower limbs is suggestive of myxedema.\n\nWhile chronic lymphangitis and acute lymphatic filariasis can also cause non-pitting edema, they usually have a unilateral/localized presentation.\n\nNephrotic syndrome and heart failure cause bilateral pitting edema.\n\nCauses of generalized edema include:\n\n• Heart failure\n\n• Chronic renal failure\n\n• Nephrotic syndrome\n\n• Liver cirrhosis\n\nCauses of localized edema include:\n\n• Thrombophlebitis\n\n• Chronic lymphangitis\n\n• Resection of regional lymph nodes\n\n• Filariasis","t":"Clinical Examination","c":"Clinical Examination and Acid-Base"},
 {"q":"While examining a patient’s pulse, you note a tapping impulse when you raise his arm above his head. Which of the following conditions would you not suspect in the patient?","o":["Left-sided heart failure","Anemia","Thiamine deficiency","Aortic regurgitation"],"a":0,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of water hammer pulse, which is not seen in left-sided heart failure.\n\nCollapsing pulse/Watson’s water hammer pulse is a rapidly rising pulse that collapses suddenly. This is because arterial pressure falls rapidly during late systole and diastole. It is also called Corrigan's pulse.\n\nThe various causes of the collapsing pulse include\n\n• Physiologically, it can be present in fever and pregnancy. This is attributed to the combination of increased cardiac output and the reduction in systemic vascular resistance.\n\n• Cardiac lesions such as aortic regurgitation, patent ductus arteriosus, systolic hypertension, marked bradycardia, aneurysm of sinus of Valsalva\n\n• High-output states such as anemia, cor pulmonale, thyrotoxicosis, arteriovenous fistula, Paget's disease, beriberi, and liver cirrhosis (secondary to chronic alcoholism)","t":"Clinical Examination","c":"Clinical Examination and Acid-Base"},
 {"q":"A 30-year-old African farmer presents with Winterbottom’s sign. This sign is due to the involvement of:","o":["Deep cervical nodes","Supraclavicular nodes","Posterior cervical nodes","Occipital nodes"],"a":2,"e":"Winterbottom’s sign is due to the involvement of posterior cervical nodes. It is seen in African trypanosomiasis.\n\nAfrican trypanosomiasis is a vector born disease caused by Trypanosoma brucei gambiense/rhodesiense. It is transmitted through the bite of a Tsetse fly.\n\nThe clinical syndrome includes hemolymphatic disease with associated Winterbottom's sign and a maculopapular rash primarily involving the trunk. At a later stage, the patient can present with features suggestive of meningoencephalitis.","t":"Clinical Examination","c":"Clinical Examination and Acid-Base"},
 {"q":"A patient with rheumatic heart disease now presents with fever and the following nail finding. The nail lesions most likely are?","o":["Roth spots","Janeway lesions","Osler nodes","Splinter hemorrhages"],"a":3,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of infective endocarditis (IE). Splinter hemorrhages are seen in the nails of these patients.\n\nSubungual splinter hemorrhages are classically identified as dark-red, linear petechiae in the nail bed.\n\nOther peripheral manifestations of infective endocarditis include the following:\n\n• Petechiae: Common but nonspecific finding\n\n• Purulent purpura\n\n• Osler nodes: Tender subcutaneous nodules usually found on the distal pads of the digits\n\n• Janeway lesions: Nontender maculae on the palms and soles\n\n• Roth spots: Relatively rare retinal hemorrhages with small, clear centers","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a671979e66f14b0bada5b734398b249c.JPEG"],"t":"Clinical Examination","c":"Clinical Examination and Acid-Base"},
 {"q":"A 25-year-old man presented to the OPD with a fever for the past 5 days. On examination, tender lesions were found on his extremities as marked below. What is the most probable diagnosis?","o":["Hand-foot-mouth disease","Infective endocarditis","Cat scratch disease","Rocky Mountain Spotted fever"],"a":1,"e":"The most probable diagnosis for this patient is infective endocarditis.\n\nTender violaceous subcutaneous nodules in the fingers shown in the image are Osler nodes. They usually occur in the pads of fingers and toes but can also occur on the thenar and hypothenar eminences.\n\nOther lesions that are highly suggestive of infective endocarditis include:\n\n• Roth spots: These are exudative, edematous hemorrhagic lesions of the retina with pale centres.\n\n• Janeway lesions: They are nontender, slightly raised haemorrhages on the palms and soles. The image is shown below.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a529cb14e85a40e6af9afc5c406ebecb.JPEG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1cad717c18d34dc19aa657b5a2c344a3x720x526.JPEG"],"t":"Clinical Examination","c":"Clinical Examination and Acid-Base"},
 {"q":"On examination of the chest wall, the presence of thrills indicate:","o":["Palpable S3","Palpable S4","Palpable murmur","Palpable right ventricular systole"],"a":2,"e":"On examination of the chest wall, the presence of thrills indicates a palpable murmur. Thrills are the palpable vibrations produced by turbulent blood flow causing murmurs.\n\nPalpable S3 and S4 cause a double thrust in the apical impulse.\n\nPalpable right ventricular systole is known as heave which is felt in the left parasternal area.","t":"Clinical Examination","c":"Clinical Examination and Acid-Base"},
 {"q":"A patient presents with excessive thirst, unexplained weight loss, visual blurring, genital thrush, and lethargy. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Hyperglycemia","Hyperthyroidism","Hypoglycemia","Diabetic neuropathy"],"a":0,"e":"The most likely diagnosis in the given scenario is hyperglycemia.\n\nThe symptoms of hyperglycemia include:\n\n• Polyuria\n\n• Polydipsia\n\n• Weight loss\n\n• Fatigue\n\n• Blurry vision\n\n• Frequent superficial infections (vaginitis, fungal skin infections)\n\n• Slow healing of skin lesions after minor trauma.\n\nNote: The metabolic derangements occurring in these patients are due to osmotic diuresis and a catabolic state caused by urinary loss of glucose and increased muscle breakdown. Blurred vision results from changes in the water content of the lens.","t":"Clinical Examination","c":"Clinical Examination and Acid-Base"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is the most effective extracellular buffer system at physiological pH?","o":["Bicarbonate buffer","Phosphate buffer","Protein buffer","Ammonia buffer"],"a":0,"e":"The most effective extracellular buffer system at physiological pH is the bicarbonate buffer system.\n\nA molecule with a pKa close to the physiological pH (7.4) is considered an effective buffer. Although the pKa of HCO3- is relatively low (6.1), it makes up 92% of the plasma buffering.\n\nThis is often attributed to the enormous quantity of HCO3- present in the body and the ability of this system to accept protons and form carbonic acid (H2CO3). This further in the presence of carbonic anhydrase is converted to readily excretable H₂O and CO2. It is therefore considered the most efficient extracellular buffering system in the body.\n\nHCO3- + H+↔ H2CO3 ↔ H₂O + CO2","t":"Acid Base Disorders","c":"Clinical Examination and Acid-Base"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not a cause of metabolic alkalosis?","o":["Mineralocorticoid deficiency","Hypokalemia","Thiazide diuretic therapy","Recurrent vomiting"],"a":0,"e":"Mineralocorticoid deficiency does not cause metabolic alkalosis. Its excess causes metabolic alkalosis.\n\nCauses of metabolic alkalosis:\n\nLoss of hydrogen:\n\n• Gastrointestinal loss\n\n• Removal of gastric secretions - vomiting or nasogastric suction\n\n• Chloride-losing diarrhea\n\n• Gastrocolic fistula\n\n• Villous adenoma\n\n• Antacid therapy, particularly if combined with cation exchange resin\n\n• Renal loss\n\n• Loop or thiazide diuretics\n\n• Mineralocorticoid excess (primary aldosteronism, Cushing's, steroids, licorice)\n\n• Posthypercapnic states\n\n• Hypercalcemia, including milk-alkali syndrome\n\n• H+ movement into cells\n\n• Hypokalemia\n\nExogenous Alkali:\n\n• Administration of NaHCO3, sodium citrate/gluconate/acetate/antacids\n\n• Massive blood transfusion\n\n• Antacids- Milk alkali syndrome\n\nContraction alkalosis (loss of ECF volume):\n\n• Loop or thiazide-type diuretics\n\n• Sweat losses in cystic fibrosis\n\n• Gastric losses in achlorhydria\n\nMiscellaneous:\n\n• Bartter's syndrome\n\n• Gitelman's syndrome","t":"Acid Base Disorders","c":"Clinical Examination and Acid-Base"},
 {"q":"Which metabolic abnormality are patients with bulimia nervosa least likely to develop?","o":["Hyponatremia","Hypochloremia","Metabolic alkalosis","Metabolic acidosis"],"a":3,"e":"The metabolic abnormality patients with bulimia nervosa are least likely to develop is metabolic acidosis. Chronic vomiting, as seen in bulimia nervosa leads to metabolic alkalosis, not acidosis.\n\nConditions associated with chronic vomiting lead to hypochloremia, hyponatremia, hypokalemia, and metabolic alkalosis.","t":"Acid Base Disorders","c":"Clinical Examination and Acid-Base"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is false about metabolic alkalosis?","o":["It is associated with hyperkalemia","It is associated with decreased ionic calcium concentration","Can be caused due to Conn's syndrome","Can be caused due to a renin secreting tumor"],"a":0,"e":"Metabolic alkalosis is associated with potassium depletion, not hyperkalemia.\n\nWhile hypercalcemia can be a cause of metabolic alkalosis, alkalosis itself can cause a decrease in ionized calcium. The patients can present with tetany and show signs of hypocalcemia, i.e., Trousseau sign and Chvostek's sign.\n\nConn's syndrome is primary hyperaldosteronism that leads to sodium reabsorption in exchange for hydrogen in the collecting ducts. This causes hypernatremia, hypokalemia, and hydrogen loss in urine and may lead to metabolic alkalosis.\n\nRenin-secreting tumors also lead to an increase in aldosterone levels leading to alkalosis.\n\nCauses of metabolic alkalosis:\n\nLoss of hydrogen:\n\n• Gastrointestinal loss\n\n• Removal of gastric secretions- vomiting or nasogastric suction\n\n• Chloride-losing diarrhea\n\n• Gastrocolic fistula\n\n• Villous adenoma\n\n• Antacid therapy, particularly if combined with cation exchange resin\n\n• Renal loss\n\n• Loop or thiazide diuretics\n\n• Mineralocorticoid excess (primary aldosteronism, Cushing's, steroids, licorice)\n\n• Posthypercapnic states\n\n• Hypercalcemia, including the milk-alkali syndrome\n\n•\n\n• H+ movement into cells\n\n• Hypokalemia\n\nExogenous Alkali:\n\n• Administration of NaHCO3, sodium citrate/gluconate/acetate/antacids\n\n• Massive blood transfusion\n\n• Antacids- Milk alkali syndrome\n\nContraction alkalosis (loss of ECF volume):\n\n• Loop or thiazide-type diuretics\n\n• Sweat losses in cystic fibrosis\n\n• Gastric losses in achlorhydria\n\nMiscellaneous:\n\n• Bartter's syndrome\n\n• Gitelman's syndrome","t":"Acid Base Disorders","c":"Clinical Examination and Acid-Base"},
 {"q":"Metabolic alkalosis is suspected in a patient presenting with multiple episodes of vomiting. Which of the following laboratory results would be seen in his arterial blood gas analysis?\n\nOptions\npH\n\npCO2\n\n(mmHg)\n\nHCO3¯\n\n(mEq/L)\n\n1\n7.48\n32\n21\n\n2\n7.32\n30\n22\n\n3\n7.28\n50\n32\n\n4\n7.55\n48\n30","o":["Option 1","Option 3","Option 2","Option 4"],"a":3,"e":"Among the given options, high pH (7.55), high HCO3- (30 mEq/L), and high pCO2 (48 mmHg) is suggestive of metabolic alkalosis with respiratory acidosis, as a result of compensatory hypoventilation.\n\nMultiple episodes of vomiting result in loss of acids from the body and thus increases the pH.\n\npH\n\npCO2\n\n(mmHg)\n\nHCO3¯\n\n(mEq/L)\n\nInterpretation\n\n1\n\n7.48\n\n(high)\n\n32\n\n(low)\n\n21\n\n(low)\n\nRespiratory alkalosis with compensatory metabolic acidosis\n\n2\n\n7.32\n\n(low)\n\n30\n\n(low)\n\n22\n\n(low)\n\nMetabolic acidosis with compensatory respiratory alkalosis\n\n3\n\n7.28\n\n(low)\n\n50\n\n(high)\n\n32\n\n(high)\n\nRespiratory acidosis with compensatory metabolic alkalosis\n\n4\n\n7.55\n\n(high)\n\n48\n\n(high)\n\n30\n\n(high)\n\nMetabolic alkalosis with compensatory respiratory acidosis","t":"Acid Base Disorders","c":"Clinical Examination and Acid-Base"},
 {"q":"The arterial blood gas analysis of a patient reveals a pH of 7.55, pCO2- 54 mmHg, and HCO3- 40 mEq/L. What is the underlying disorder?","o":["Mixed acid-base disorder","Metabolic alkalosis with compensatory respiratory acidosis","Respiratory acidosis with compensatory metabolic alkalosis","Respiratory alkalosis with compensatory metabolic acidosis"],"a":1,"e":"The underlying disorder in this patient is metabolic alkalosis with compensatory respiratory acidosis.\n\nInterpretation of the ABG:\n\nStep 1: A pH of 7.55 indicates alkalosis.\n\nStep 2: Plasma HCO3- is 40 mEq/L, which in the presence of a high pH indicates metabolic alkalosis.\n\nStep 3: pCO2 is 54 mmHg indicating respiratory acidosis. The increase in pCO2 along with the increase in pH suggests that this is a compensatory response.\n\nStep 4: Assessing the compensatory response:\n\nOn average, the pCO2 rises 0.75 mmHg for every 1.0 mEq/L increment in the plasma HCO3-\n\nIn the above scenario, the plasma HCO3- concentration is increased to 40 mEq/L (The average normal value is considered as 24 mEq/L)\n\nThus, the HCO3- elevation is 40 – 24 = 16 mEq/L\n\nTherefore, pCO2 elevation should be 0.75 × 16 =  12 mmHg\n\nNow, considering that the average value of normal pCO2 is 40 mmHg, the estimated pCO2 measured should be 40 + 12= 52 mmHg.\n\nAs pCO2 is higher than the expected estimate, it is a case of metabolic alkalosis with compensatory respiratory acidosis.","t":"Acid Base Disorders","c":"Clinical Examination and Acid-Base"},
 {"q":"The arterial blood gas analysis of a 50-year-old diabetic patient revealed the following values: pH- 7.2 and HCO3- 15 mEq/L. Which of the following statements is false regarding renal compensation in this patient?","o":["Hydrogen ion secretion is increased","Bicarbonate reabsorption is decreased","Urinary acidity is increased","Urinary ammonia is increased"],"a":1,"e":"The patient has low pH and decreased levels of bicarbonate which is indicative of metabolic acidosis. Bicarbonate reabsorption is increased in renal handling of metabolic acidosis.\n\nIn response to metabolic acidosis, acidification of urine occurs by the following compensatory mechanisms:\n\n• The proximal tubule reabsorbs all the filtered HCO3- (as any loss of HCO3- is equal to the addition of an equimolar amount of H+).\n\n• The collecting duct excretes the daily H+ load (loss of H+ is equal to the addition of an equimolar amount of HCO3-) primarily in the form of urinary ammonia.","t":"Acid Base Disorders","c":"Clinical Examination and Acid-Base"},
 {"q":"In which of the following patients would you expect to find an increased anion gap?","o":["Renal tubular acidosis","Addison's disease","Diarrhoea","Diabetic ketoacidosis"],"a":3,"e":"The anion gap is increased in ketoacidosis. The ketone bodies act as unmeasured anions and increase the anion gap.\n\nThe anion gap is calculated as:\n\nAG = (Na+) – [(Cl-) + (HCO3-)]\n\nThe normal anion gap is 6 - 12 mEq/L (optimally <10 mEq/L).","t":"Acid Base Disorders","c":"Clinical Examination and Acid-Base"},
 {"q":"A 40-year-old man presented with nausea, dizziness, and hyperventilation. His arterial blood gas analysis and serum electrolyte levels revealed metabolic acidosis with a normal anion gap. Which of the following is the possible reason for this condition?","o":["Lactic acidosis","Chronic renal failure","Renal tubular acidosis","Methanol toxicity"],"a":2,"e":"Metabolic acidosis with a normal anion gap is a feature of renal tubular acidosis.\n\nRenal tubular acidosis is a form of hyperchloraemic metabolic acidosis which occurs when the renal damage primarily affects tubular function without much effect on glomerular function. The bicarbonate loss leading to acidosis is mirrored by an increase in chloride concentration. Hence the unmeasured anions are unaffected and thus, the anion gap remains within normal limits.\n\nThe result is a decrease in H+ excretion which is greater than that can be explained by any change in the glomerular filtration rate (GFR). However, if the GFR is significantly depressed eg., renal failure, the retention of fixed acids leads to a high anion gap metabolic acidosis.","t":"Acid Base Disorders","c":"Clinical Examination and Acid-Base"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not associated with type-A lactic acidosis?","o":["Acute pyelonephritis with hypotension","Acute MI with free wall rupture","Cyanide toxicity","Metformin toxicity"],"a":3,"e":"Metformin toxicity is associated with type B lactic acidosis.\n\nLactic acidosis is defined as an increase in plasma lactate levels. The various types of lactic acidosis are:\n\nType A\nType B\n\nOccurs secondary to circulatory insufficiency or tissue hypoxia.\nOccurs in the absence of systemic hypoperfusion.\n\nAssociated with:\n\n• Shock\n\n• Cardiac failure\n\n• Severe anemia\n\n• Carbon monoxide toxicity\n\n• Cyanide toxicity\n\nAssociated with:\n\n• Malignancies\n\n• Renal failure\n\n• Hepatic failure\n\n• Drugs like metformin, ethanol, ethylene glycol, and methanol\n\nD-lactic acidosis is an unusual form of metabolic acidosis. It occurs due to the formation of D-lactate by gut bacteria. It occurs in patients with:\n\n• Short bowel syndrome\n\n• Post-jejunoileal bypass\n\n• Intestinal obstruction.","t":"Acid Base Disorders","c":"Clinical Examination and Acid-Base"},
 {"q":"A 5-year-old boy presents with generalized edema. Urine analysis showed protein 3+ and fat bodies. Which of the following would you expect to see in this condition?","o":["Decrease in unmeasured anions","Decrease in measured anions","Normal anion gap","High anion gap"],"a":0,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of nephrotic syndrome. This leads to a decrease in serum albumin which is an unmeasured anion.\n\nAlbumin at physiological pH is the major contributor to the unmeasured anions of the body. Unmeasured anions consume bicarbonate in the body and reduce its levels. This leads to an increase in the anion gap. Therefore, when these unmeasured anions are reduced the anion gap also reduces due to decreased consumption of the bicarbonate.\n\nNephrotic syndrome causes hypoalbuminemia and thus patients have a reduced anion gap. A fall in serum albumin by 1 g/dL from the normal value (4.5 g/dL) decreases the anion gap by 2.5 mEq/L.\n\nDecreased anion gap (<6 mEq/L) is seen in:\n\n• Increase in unmeasured cations\n\n• An addition of abnormal cations to the blood, such as:\n\n• Lithium (lithium intoxication)\n\n• Cationic immunoglobulins (plasma cell dyscrasias)\n\n• Reduction in the plasma anion albumin concentration (nephrotic syndrome, liver disease, or malabsorption)\n\n• Bromide intoxication\n\n• Hyperviscosity and severe hyperlipidemia, which can lead to an underestimation of sodium and chloride concentrations.","t":"Acid Base Disorders","c":"Clinical Examination and Acid-Base"},
 {"q":"A 54-year-old male patient with renal failure has missed a round of dialysis. He complains of lethargy, anorexia, and vomiting. What is the probable acid-base disorder that we can expect in him?","o":["High anion gap metabolic acidosis","Metabolic alkalosis with compensatory respiratory acidosis","Normal anion gap metabolic acidosis","Mixed acid-base disorder"],"a":0,"e":"In renal failure, there is a loss of kidney function due to the damage affecting glomeruli and tubules. This leads to a high anion gap  metabolic acidosis.","t":"Acid Base Disorders","c":"Clinical Examination and Acid-Base"},
 {"q":"ABG analysis of a patient is shown below. What is the probable underlying acid-base disorder in this patient?\n\npH- 7.1\n\npCO2- 16 mmHg\n\nHCO3- - 6 mEq/L\n\npO2- 78 mmHg","o":["Metabolic acidosis with compensatory respiratory alkalosis","Metabolic alkalosis with compensatory respiratory acidosis","Respiratory acidosis with compensatory metabolic alkalosis","Mixed acid–base disorder"],"a":0,"e":"The underlying acid-base disorder in this patient is metabolic acidosis with compensatory respiratory alkalosis.\n\nInterpretation of the ABG:\n\nStep 1: A pH of 7.1 signifies acidosis.\n\nStep 2: Plasma HCO3- is 6 mEq/L, along with a low pH it signifies metabolic acidosis.\n\nStep 3: pCO2 is 16 mmHg indicating respiratory alkalosis. The decrease in pCO2 along with the decrease in pH suggests that this is a compensatory response.\n\nStep 4: The expected compensatory decrease in pCO2 can be calculated using Winter's formula (Expected PCO2= {[1.5 x HCO3-] +8} ± 2).\n\nExpected PCO2 = {[1.5 x 6] +8} ± 2 = 15 to 19 mmHg\n\nA pCO2 level of 16 mmHg falls within the range given above. Hence, metabolic acidosis is associated with compensatory respiratory alkalosis.\n\n(pCO2 values above or below 15 - 19 mmHg would indicate mixed acid-base disorders)\n\nAlternatively:\n\nThe other method to calculate the expected compensation is a 1.2 mmHg decrease in pCO2 for every 1 mmol/L decrease in bicarbonate.\n\nThe decrease in bicarbonate is 24 - 6 = 18 mEq/L\n\nDecrease in pCO2 is 18 x 1.2 = 22\n\nExpected pCO2 = 40 - 22 = 18 mmHg (roughly = 16 mmHg since the standard values are approximations)\n\nThis method also indicates primary metabolic acidosis with compensatory respiratory alkalosis. However, Winter's formula is more accurate.","t":"Acid Base Disorders","c":"Clinical Examination and Acid-Base"},
 {"q":"A 30-year-old man presents to the emergency department with dyspnea, and altered sensorium. These symptoms were preceded by an asthma attack. What is the probable reason for the patient's presenting symptoms?","o":["Metabolic acidosis","Metabolic alkalosis","Respiratory acidosis","Respiratory alkalosis"],"a":2,"e":"The probable reason for the patient's presenting symptoms is respiratory acidosis.\n\nAsthma attack leads to the narrowing of respiratory passages. This leads to reduced O2 inhalation and reduced CO2 exhalation contributing to the rise of pCO2 in the blood, thereby causing respiratory acidosis.\n\nCauses of respiratory acidosis:\n\n• Central:\n\n• Drugs (anesthetics, morphine, sedatives)\n\n• Stroke\n\n• Infection\n\n• Airway:\n\n• Obstruction\n\n• Asthma\n\n• Lung parenchyma:\n\n• Emphysema\n\n• Pneumoconiosis\n\n• Bronchitis\n\n• Adult respiratory distress syndrome\n\n• Barotrauma\n\n• Neuromuscular:\n\n• Poliomyelitis\n\n• Kyphoscoliosis\n\n• Myasthenia\n\n• Muscular dystrophies\n\n• Miscellaneous:\n\n• Obesity\n\n• Hypoventilation\n\n• Permissive hypercapnia.","t":"Acid Base Disorders","c":"Clinical Examination and Acid-Base"},
 {"q":"A 60-year-old man with a history of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease presents with increased shortness of breath. His ABG analysis is as shown. What is the most probable underlying acid-base disorder?\n\npH- 7.24\n\npCO2- 60 mmHg\n\npO2- 52 mmHg\n\nHCO3- - 27 mEq/L","o":["Metabolic acidosis","Metabolic alkalosis","Respiratory acidosis","Respiratory alkalosis"],"a":2,"e":"The underlying acid-base disorder is respiratory acidosis.\n\nInterpretation of the ABG:\n\nStep 1: A pH of 7.24 signifies acidosis.\n\nStep 2: A plasma HCO3- is 27 mEq/L is within normal limits. This is very likely to be due to compensation as it does not reflect the direction of the pH change.\n\nStep 3: A pCO2 of 60 mmHg along with an acidotic pH signifies respiratory acidosis.\n\nStep 4: The expected compensatory increase in HCO3- can be calculated using the following formula:\n\nIncrease in HCO3- = 0.4 X Δ PaCO2 (as COPD is a chronic condition and the presentation of the patient is not acute)\n\nThus, the increase in HCO3- = 0.4 x (60-40) = 8 mEq/L\n\nTherefore expected HCO3- is 24 + 8 = 32 mEq/L.\n\nThe actual HCO3- measured is 27 mEq/L (which is less than the expected value) indicating that there could be a component of metabolic acidosis also.\n\nBut, the predominant disorder is respiratory acidosis and thus it is the best answer.","t":"Acid Base Disorders","c":"Clinical Examination and Acid-Base"},
 {"q":"A young asthmatic boy was rushed to ED with confusion and gasping for breath. His ABG analysis is as shown. What is the acid-base disorder?\n\npH- 7.25\n\npCO2- 70 mmHg\n\nHCO3‾- 26 mEq/L\n\npO2- 45 mmHg","o":["Respiratory alkalosis, compensated","Respiratory acidosis, partially compensated","Respiratory acidosis, fully compensated","Metabolic alkalosis, uncompensated"],"a":1,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of acute severe asthma. The acid-base disorder is respiratory acidosis, partially compensated.\n\nInterpretation of the ABG:\n\nStep 1: pH is decreased. This signifies acidosis. In acidosis, either pCO2 is increased or plasma HCO3- is decreased.\n\nStep 2: In this case, pCO2 is increased by 30 mmHg. This signifies respiratory acidosis.\n\nStep 3: Plasma HCO3- is increased by 26-24 = 2 mEq/L. This signifies compensation is taking place.\n\nIn acute conditions, for every 10mmHg rise in pCO2 expected rise in plasma HCO3- is 1mEq/L. In this case, pCO2 has risen by 30 mmHg, so the expected rise in HCO3- is 3 mEq/L.\n\nHowever, plasma HCO3- has risen only by 2 mEq/L, which implies compensation is only partial.","t":"Acid Base Disorders","c":"Clinical Examination and Acid-Base"},
 {"q":"A 40-year-old woman was rushed to the ER with chest pain, palpitations, and sweating. On examination, her respiratory rate was 32/min, and carpopedal spasm was noted while measuring BP. Her ECG was normal. Which of the following acid-base disorders is she likely to have?","o":["Metabolic acidosis","Metabolic alkalosis","Respiratory acidosis","Respiratory alkalosis"],"a":3,"e":"The clinical scenario is suggestive of a panic attack. The patient is likely to have respiratory alkalosis.\n\nPanic attacks are characterized by hyperventilation which leads to a washout of CO2 (reduced PaCO2). This further increases the ratio of bicarbonate concentration to PaCO2 and, thereby, increases the pH level causing alkalosis.\n\nAlkalosis promotes the binding of calcium to albumin and reduces the fraction of ionized calcium in the blood. This leads to hypocalcemia without reducing the total calcium levels. Thus, alkalosis can precipitate tetany and laryngospasm, especially in calcium-deficient patients.\n\nInduction of carpopedal spasm when the BP cuff is inflated 20 mm Hg over the systolic BP for three minutes or more is called Trousseau sign. It is a clinical manifestation of hypocalcemia.\n\nCauses of respiratory alkalosis:\n\n• Central nervous system stimulation:\n\n• Pain\n\n• Anxiety, psychosis\n\n• Fever\n\n• Cerebrovascular accident\n\n• meningitis, encephalitis\n\n• Tumor\n\n• Trauma\n\n• Hypoxemia or tissue hypoxia:\n\n• High altitude\n\n• Pneumonia, pulmonary edema\n\n• Aspiration\n\n• Severe anemia\n\n• Drugs or hormones:\n\n• Pregnancy\n\n• Salicylates\n\n• Cardiac failure\n\n• Stimulation of chest receptors:\n\n• Hemothorax\n\n• Flail chest\n\n• Cardiac failure\n\n• Pulmonary embolism\n\n• Miscellaneous:\n\n• Septicemia\n\n• Hepatic failure\n\n• Mechanical hyperventilation\n\n• Heat exposure\n\n• Recovery from metabolic acidosis\n\nNote: While metabolic alkalosis can also cause hypocalcemia, the above scenario of hyperventilation is more suggestive of respiratory alkalosis. Furthermore, hypocalcemic symptoms are more common with respiratory alkalosis.","t":"Acid Base Disorders","c":"Clinical Examination and Acid-Base"},
 {"q":"The arterial blood gas analysis of an unconscious man showed pH- 6.9, HCO3- 13 mEq/L, paCO2- 68 mmHg. The acid-base status of this patient is best described as:","o":["Uncompensated metabolic acidosis","Uncompensated respiratory acidosis","Simultaneous respiratory and metabolic acidosis","Respiratory acidosis with partial renal compensation"],"a":2,"e":"According to the ABG analysis provided, the patient has a mixed acid-base disorder with simultaneous respiratory and metabolic acidosis.\n\nInterpretation of the ABG:\n\nStep 1: A pH of 6.9 signifies acidosis.\n\nStep 2: Plasma HCO3- is 13 mEq/L, along with a low pH it signifies metabolic acidosis.\n\nStep 3: pCO2 is 68 mmHg indicating co-existing respiratory acidosis. The direction of pCO2 change is the same as that of pH change suggesting that this is a mixed acid-base disorder.\n\nStep 4: The expected compensatory change in pCO2 in the metabolic acidosis calculated using Winter's formula would be:\n\npCO2 = [1.5xHCO3-] + 8 ± 2 = 26 - 30 mmHg\n\nThe actual pCO2 measure is very high (68 mmHg) compared to the estimated range of 26 - 30 mmHg indicating the presence of simultaneous metabolic and respiratory acidosis.\n\nNote- If this was a case of uncompensated acidosis, either the bicarbonate or the pCO2 values would have been within the normal limits.","t":"Acid Base Disorders","c":"Clinical Examination and Acid-Base"},
 {"q":"Vasopressin acting on its V2 receptor uses _____ as its second messenger.","o":["Calcium","cGMP","cAMP","Phospholipase C"],"a":2,"e":"Vasopressin acts on the V2 receptor using cAMP as its second messenger.\n\nVasopressin acts on GPCR using different second messengers to accomplish its various actions as follows:\n\n• V1 receptor - vasoconstriction using phospholipase C as its second messenger\n\n• V2 receptor - reabsorption of water through aquaporin 2, using cAMP as its second messenger\n\nVasopressin has a maximum effect on the collecting ducts. It acts by inserting aquaporin 2 present in cytoplasmic vesicles of principal cells on to the apical membrane.","t":"General Principles Of Endocrinology","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following act as the second messenger for secretin?","o":["cAMP","cGMP","Serine kinase","Tyrosine kinase"],"a":0,"e":"cAMP is the second messenger for the hormone secretin. It regulates fluid and electrolyte secretion and also mediates water and nutrient reabsorption.","t":"General Principles Of Endocrinology","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which hormone mediates its action via cyclic GMP?","o":["Oxytocin","Vasopressin","Aldosterone","Nitric oxide"],"a":3,"e":"Nitric oxide mediates its action via cyclic GMP or cGMP.\n\ncGMP is the second messenger associated with smooth muscle relaxation and vasodilation. Atrial natriuretic factor (ANF) and NO (nitric oxide) are the only hormones that mediate their action via cGMP.\n\nNote: Recent studies show that NO is a hormone. NO, which functions as a chemical messenger, has similar properties to hormones (which are known as the body's chemical messengers).","t":"General Principles Of Endocrinology","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"What is the most abundant hormone produced by the pituitary gland?","o":["Vasopressin","GH","ACTH","TSH"],"a":1,"e":"Growth hormone (GH) is the most abundant hormone produced by the pituitary gland.\n\nIt is produced by the acidophil cells or somatotropes of the anterior pituitary, which account for 50% of the anterior pituitary cell population.\n\nOption A: Vasopressin is produced in the hypothalamus and only stored in the posterior pituitary.\n\nOption C: Corticotrophs account for 10% of the anterior pituitary cell population.\n\nOption D: Thyrotrophs account for 5% of the anterior pituitary cell population.","t":"General Principles Of Endocrinology","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Insulin acts on which of the following?","o":["Nuclear receptor","Cytoplasmic receptor","Tyrosine kinase receptor","STK receptor"],"a":2,"e":"Insulin acts on the tyrosine kinase receptor, a cell-surface receptor.","t":"General Principles Of Endocrinology","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is considered the twin hormone of prolactin?","o":["ACTH","GH","Oxytocin","Dopamine"],"a":1,"e":"GH and prolactin are called twin hormones as both are secreted by the acidophil cells of the anterior pituitary. They act on cytokine receptors and show JAK-STAT activity.","t":"General Principles Of Endocrinology","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following hormones is an amino acid derivative?","o":["Thyroid hormone","Insulin","Activin","Parathyroid hormone"],"a":0,"e":"The thyroid hormone is synthesized from tyrosine residues and thus is an amino acid derivative.\n\nInsulin, activin and parathyroid hormone are large protein hormones.","t":"General Principles Of Endocrinology","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following chemical molecules is not a steroid hormones?","o":["Cortisol","Estrogen","DHEA","Inhibin"],"a":3,"e":"Inhibin is a large protein hormone, not a steroid hormone.\n\nAll adrenocortical and reproductive hormones are steroid hormones synthesized from cholesterol precursors except activin and inhibin.","t":"General Principles Of Endocrinology","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which class of hormones does insulin belong to?","o":["Amino acid derivatives","Small neuropeptides","Large protein hormones","Medium-sized peptide"],"a":2,"e":"Insulin is classified as a large protein hormone (>50 amino acid residues) as it is composed of 51 amino acids.\n\nProtein and polypeptide hormones are classified as small peptides (< 50 amino acid residues) and large proteins.","t":"General Principles Of Endocrinology","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"The action of cAMP is mediated by _____","o":["Adenylyl cyclase","Guanylyl cyclase","Protein kinase","IP3-DAG"],"a":2,"e":"The action of cAMP is mediated by protein kinases.\n\ncAMP is a second messenger molecule associated with electrolyte secretion and water and nutrient reabsorption. It is synthesized by adenylyl cyclase but it mediates its actions via protein kinases.","t":"General Principles Of Endocrinology","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"The second messenger, cAMP is synthesized by the enzyme ______","o":["Adenylyl cyclase","cAMP phosphodiesterase","ATPase","Phosphorylase"],"a":0,"e":"cAMP is synthesized by adenylyl cyclase, but it mediates its actions via protein kinases.","t":"General Principles Of Endocrinology","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not activated by the binding of epinephrine to its receptor?","o":["Gi","Gq","Gp","Gs"],"a":2,"e":"The binding of epinephrine to its various receptors results in the activation of different types of G-protein subunits, namely Gq, Gi, and Gs.\n\nAll catecholamines act via G protein-coupled receptors. This stimulates cAMP production and signaling via protein kinases.","t":"General Principles Of Endocrinology","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"The enzyme kinase adds ______ to proteins.","o":["Acetates","Sulphates","Carbonates","Phosphates"],"a":3,"e":"Kinases are a subclass of transferases that catalyze the transfer of a phosphate group from a donor to an acceptor protein. They are associated with the regulation of many essential cellular processes.","t":"General Principles Of Endocrinology","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not a characteristic of growth hormone (GH) deficiency?","o":["Hyperglycemia","Micropenis","Delayed bone age","High-pitched voice"],"a":0,"e":"Hyperglycemia is not a characteristic of GH deficiency. Isolated GH deficiency, especially in children, can lead to a higher propensity to develop hypoglycemia. This is attributed to the relatively unopposed action of insulin.\n\nIsolated GH deficiency in children is associated with the following features:\n\n• Short stature\n\n• Micropenis\n\n• Increased fat\n\n• High-pitched voice\n\n• High propensity to develop hypoglycemia due to relatively unopposed insulin action.","t":"Disorders Of Anterior Pituitary","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A 3-year-old girl is referred to your centre for evaluation of short stature. The child's parents are found to be first cousins. Further evaluation led to the diagnosis of Laron-type dwarfism. What causes this condition?","o":["GH deficiency","GH receptor anomaly","GHRH deficiency","IGF-1 deficiency"],"a":1,"e":"Laron-type dwarfism, also known as GH-insensitivity syndrome, is characterized by GH-receptor anomalies.\n\nLaron-type dwarfism is an autosomal recessive disorder. The primary clinical symptom associated with this condition is short stature. Treatment is by subcutaneous injections of insulin-like growth factor 1 (a growth-promoting hormone), often called IGF-1.","t":"Disorders Of Anterior Pituitary","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A 9-year-old girl who presented with short stature is diagnosed with Turner’s syndrome. The physician decides to initiate treatment with recombinant human growth hormone for short stature. Which of the following side effects is she not likely to develop?","o":["Tinnitus","Hypoglycemia","Pseudotumor cerebri","Slipped capital femoral epiphysis"],"a":1,"e":"GH is a potent counter-regulatory hormone to insulin and leads to hyperglycemia, thus contraindicated in patients with uncontrolled diabetes.\n\nSide effects of GH administration include:\n\n• Dose-related fluid retention\n\n• Hyperglycemia\n\n• Joint pain and carpal tunnel syndrome\n\n• Headache, increased intracranial pressure, hypertension\n\n• Tinnitus\n\n• Pituitary tumor regrowth\n\n• Progression of skin lesions\n\n• Acromegaly\n\n• Slipped capital femoral epiphysis\n\n• Gynecomastia\n\nThe actions of GH are:\n\n• Induces protein synthesis and nitrogen retention\n\n• Impairs glucose tolerance by antagonizing insulin action\n\n• Stimulates lipolysis\n\n• Promotes sodium, potassium, and water retention\n\n• Elevates serum levels of inorganic phosphate\n\n• Linear bone growth occurs\n\n• Stimulates epiphyseal prechondrocyte differentiation","t":"Disorders Of Anterior Pituitary","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Paradoxical response of GH release to thyrotropin-releasing hormone (TRH) is seen in all of the following conditions except______","o":["Prolactinoma","Acromegaly","Malnutrition","Anorexia nervosa"],"a":2,"e":"The paradoxical response of GH release to TRH is not seen in malnutrition patients.\n\nIn normal individuals, GH doesn't respond to TRH stimulation, but paradoxically, the GH levels may increase in response to TRH stimulation in the following conditions:\n\n• Acromegaly\n\n• Prolactinoma\n\n• Anorexia nervosa\n\nThe exact mechanism underlying this paradoxical GH response is poorly understood.","t":"Disorders Of Anterior Pituitary","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A patient presented to the OPD with complaints of progressive enlargement of hands and feet and was diagnosed with acromegaly. What is the most common cause for this condition?","o":["GHRH secretion from hypothalamic hamartoma","GH secretion from somatotrope adenoma","GH and PRL secretion from mixed somatotroph-lactotroph adenoma","GH and PRL secretion from mixed somatotroph-thyrotroph adenoma"],"a":1,"e":"The most common cause of acromegaly is GH secretion from somatotrope adenoma.\n\nCauses of acromegaly:\n\n• Pituitary - 98%\n\n• Somatotrope adenoma - 60% - most common.\n\n• Mixed somatotroph-lactotroph adenoma\n\n• Other plurihormonal tumors.\n\n• Extrapituitary tumor-producing GH: Pancreatic Islet cell tumor\n\n• Excess GHRH secretion\n\n• Central - hypothalamic hamartoma\n\n• Peripheral - bronchial carcinoid","t":"Disorders Of Anterior Pituitary","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following features will you expect to see in a patient with acromegaly?\n\ni. Impaired glucose tolerance\nii. Galactorrhea\niii. Hypertension\niv. Suppression of GH with glucose\nv. Low serum phosphate","o":["i,ii,iii","i ii,iv","ii,iii,v","i,ii,v"],"a":0,"e":"Impaired glucose tolerance, galactorrhea, and hypertension can be seen in patients with acromegaly.\n\nGH secretion is a part of the counter-regulatory defense mechanism against hypoglycemia. Physiological GH secretion is inhibited by hyperglycemia. In acromegaly or gigantism, GH secretion is autonomous and not suppressed by glucose. Thus, its diagnosis can be confirmed by demonstrating the failure of GH suppression with an oral glucose load (impaired oral glucose tolerance test).\n\nGalactorrhea is due to hyperprolactinemia and hypertension is secondary to increased aldosterone levels in acromegaly. GH leads to increased production of vitamin D3 which results in increased phosphate absorption from the intestine and decreased renal excretion. Hence there is increased serum phosphate level.","t":"Disorders Of Anterior Pituitary","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A middle-aged man presented to the OPD with complaints of difficulty in chewing and a gradual change in voice over the last year. Examination revealed coarse facial features. The image of his hand (right side) compared to the physician's hand is shown below. Which of the following long-term complications would you not commonly see in this patient?","o":["Diabetes mellitus","Diabetes insipidus","Coronary heart disease","Cardiomyopathy"],"a":1,"e":"The given scenario and the enlarged hands in the patient, as shown in the image, point towards a diagnosis of acromegaly. Diabetes insipidus is not a feature of acromegaly, but diabetes mellitus can develop in patients with acromegaly.\n\nClinical features of acromegaly are:\n\nSystem\nFeatures\n\nMusculoskeletal\n\n• Gigantism\n\n• Prognathism (protrusion of jaw)\n\n• Carpal tunnel syndrome\n\n• Paresthesia (due to nerve entrapment)\n\n• Proximal myopathy\n\n• Hypertrophy of frontal bones\n\nSkin and soft tissues\n\n• Acral enlargement in hands and feet (due to thickening of soft tissues)\n\n• Hyperhidrosis\n\n• Acanthosis nigricans and skin tags\n\nVisceralomegaly\n\n• Enlargement of most organs especially the tongue, thyroid, salivary gland, liver, spleen, kidney, and prostate\n\nOphthalmic\n\n• Visual field defects (macro-pituitary adenoma)\n\n• Ophthalmoplegia (cranial nerve palsy)\n\n• Headache\n\nMetabolic\n\n• Low renin with increased aldosterone\n\n• Diabetes mellitus (due to GH-induced insulin resistance)\n\n• Hypercalcemia, hyperphosphatemia, and hypercalciuria\n\n• Hypertriglyceridemia\n\nReproductive\n\n• Loss of libido and impotence in males\n\n• Menstrual abnormalities and galactorrhea\n\nCardiopulmonary\n\n• Left ventricular hypertrophy/septal hypertrophy\n\n• Hypertension\n\n• Obstructive sleep apnea\n\nNote: Diabetes insipidus can be seen as a complication post Transsphenoidal Surgery for pituitary adenomas and other sellar tumours not as complication of Acromegaly per se.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/de6d60f51fc34f0290e8efb8778cca56.PNG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2a3e7e46a00140949dc9ab40b169cb9ex1280x1623.JPEG"],"t":"Disorders Of Anterior Pituitary","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"The middle-aged man shown in the image, noticed that his shoes no longer fit him and his finger ring is getting tightened. Insulin-like growth factor-1 (IGF-1) levels are elevated. Which of the following tests will help to confirm the diagnosis?","o":["Insulin-like Growth Factor -1 Binding Protein - 3 (IGFBP-3) level","Random serum GH","Oral glucose tolerance test","MRI brain"],"a":2,"e":"The given scenario and the presence of coarse facial features and prognathism, as shown in the image, points towards a diagnosis of acromegaly. An oral glucose tolerance test with GH levels is used to confirm acromegaly.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ad04fa7d23ec48be92bb0650ee4fd939.JPEG"],"t":"Disorders Of Anterior Pituitary","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"The medical management of acromegaly includes all the following therapeutic strategies except _______","o":["Lanreotide","Pegvisomant","Cabergoline","Levodopa"],"a":3,"e":"Levodopa is not used in the treatment of acromegaly. Studies have proven that it is not as effective as agents like cabergoline.\n\nMedical management of acromegaly includes:\n\n• Somatostatin analogs:\n\n• Octreotide\n\n• Lanreotide\n\n• Pasireotide\n\n• GH-receptor antagonists:\n\n• Pegvisomant\n\n• Dopamine agonists:\n\n• Bromocriptine\n\n• Cabergoline","t":"Disorders Of Anterior Pituitary","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A 27-year-old woman presents to the OPD with infertility and amenorrhea. She also complains of milky discharge from the nipple. Her urine pregnancy test was negative and her prolactin level was 45 ng/ml. Which of the following drugs cannot lead to this condition?","o":["Methyldopa","Bromocriptine","Phenothiazine","Metoclopramide"],"a":1,"e":"This given scenario of infertility, amenorrhea and galactorrhea with elevated prolactin level (normal level is 0-25 ng/ml in a non-pregnant woman) is suggestive of hyperprolactinemia, and bromocriptine does not lead to hyperprolactinemia. It is a dopamine-agonist and hence it inhibits prolactin synthesis.\n\nDopamine agonists like bromocriptine and cabergoline are used in the treatment of hyperprolactinemia.","t":"Disorders Of Anterior Pituitary","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Systemic causes of hyperprolactinemia include all of the following clinical conditions except _______","o":["Chronic renal failure","Epileptic seizures","Cirrhosis","Hyperthyroidism"],"a":3,"e":"Hypothyroidism predisposes to the development of hyperprolactinemia.\n\nBoth enhanced hypothalamic synthesis of TRH and increased pituitary-responsiveness to TRH have been described as the mechanism behind this phenomenon.\n\nOther systemic disorders that predispose to the development of hyperprolactinemia :\n\n• Chronic renal failure\n\n• Cirrhosis\n\n• Pseudocyesis\n\n• Epileptic seizures","t":"Disorders Of Anterior Pituitary","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not a hallmark of hyperprolactinemia in women?","o":["Amenorrhea","Galactorrhea","Infertility","Diminished libido"],"a":3,"e":"The hallmarks of hyperprolactinemia in women are:\n\n• Amenorrhea – (Secondary > Primary)\n\n• Galactorrhea\n\n• Infertility\n\nIn men with hyperprolactinemia, diminished libido, infertility, and visual loss (from optic nerve compression) are the usual presenting symptoms.","t":"Disorders Of Anterior Pituitary","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A patient presents to your OPD with galactorrhea and amenorrhea. Apart from the determination of serum prolactin levels, which other investigation would you like to do?","o":["LH","TSH","FSH","Urinary ketosteroids"],"a":1,"e":"TSH should be measured along with the prolactin level to rule out hypothyroidism which can cause hyperprolactinemia.\n\nNote: However, pregnancy (using qualitative HCG) should be ruled out in all women of the reproductive age group with amenorrhea.","t":"Disorders Of Anterior Pituitary","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A 30-year-old nullipara with a desire to conceive presents with amenorrhea and galactorrhea. UPT is negative. Investigations indicate hyperprolactinemia. What is the drug of choice for clinical management of this condition?","o":["Cabergoline","Bromocriptine","Sandostatin","Pegvisomant"],"a":1,"e":"The drug of choice for clinical management of this patient would be bromocriptine.\n\nBromocriptine is an ergot alkaloid and a short-acting dopamine receptor agonist. Due to its short-term action, it is preferred when pregnancy is desired.\n\nCabergoline is a long-acting dopamine receptor agonist with high D2 receptor affinity. It effectively suppresses PRL secretion for >14 days after a single oral dose.","t":"Disorders Of Anterior Pituitary","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"What is the most common functioning tumor of the pituitary?","o":["GH-secreting adenoma","ACTH-secreting adenoma","Gonadotrope adenoma","PRL-secreting adenoma"],"a":3,"e":"Prolactin-secreting adenoma or prolactinoma is the most common type of functioning pituitary adenoma. Pituitary adenomas are the most common type of pituitary tumor, accounting for one-third of all tumors.","t":"Pituitary Tumours And Sheehan'S Syndrome","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"What are the most common cells involved in non-functioning pituitary adenoma?","o":["Prolactin-producing cells","ACTH-producing cells","Gonadotropin-producing cells","TSH-producing cells"],"a":2,"e":"Non-functioning pituitary adenomas most commonly arise from gonadotropic cells (80%).\n\nPituitary adenomas are classified as functioning or non-functioning based on the secretion of hormones. Non-functioning adenomas are usually asymptomatic in the initial stages but as the tumor grows larger, mass effect produces clinical manifestations such as:\n\n• Headache\n\n• Bitemporal hemianopia\n\n• Diplopia, ptosis\n\n• Compression of pituitary stalk\n\n• Hyperprolactinemia initially - reduced dopamine release leads to loss of inhibition of lactotrophs\n\n• Hypopituitarism in later stages","t":"Pituitary Tumours And Sheehan'S Syndrome","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A 30-year-old woman with a suspected prolactinoma is planned for a brain MRI. What size would the tumor have to be to call it a microadenoma?","o":["&lt; 10 mm","&lt; 20 mm","&lt; 5 mm","&lt; 15 mm"],"a":0,"e":"Pituitary adenomas < 10 mm are called microadenomas.\n\nAsymptomatic pituitary tumors can be discovered incidentally on imaging. They must also be suspected in patients with features of hormone hypersecretion or mass effect. In such cases, MRI with contrast is the best investigation to detect the tumor, as shown below.\n\nLevels of pituitary hormones must also be measured to rule out both hypersecretion and hyposecretion.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d6685836e0f24208af00031ee2b78c4cx720x720.PNG"],"t":"Pituitary Tumours And Sheehan'S Syndrome","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following would not occur in a patient with a pituitary macroadenoma?","o":["Increased insulin sensitivity","Pigmentation of mucous membranes","Low serum thyroxine and TSH levels","Loss of secondary sexual characteristics"],"a":1,"e":"Pituitary macroadenomas do not cause pigmentation of mucous membranes as ACTH levels are low.\n\nIn this condition, there is destruction of the pituitary gland by mass effect. This leads to panhypopituitarism or reduced levels of all hormones, including ACTH. Excessive pigmentation is only seen in cases of excess ACTH.\n\nThe following hormones are deficient in panhypopituitarism:\n\n• ACTH - Secondary adrenal insufficiency\n\n• TSH - Secondary hypothyroidism\n\n• GH - Growth failure and adult hyposomatotropism\n\n• FSH, LH - Hypogonadic hypogonadism\n\n• Prolactin - Lactation failure\n\nAdiponectin, secreted by fat cells normally increases insulin sensitivity. It is usually suppressed by cortisol. In panhypopituitarism, decreased cortisol levels lead to increased insulin sensitivity.","t":"Pituitary Tumours And Sheehan'S Syndrome","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following can occur in a patient with prolactinoma?","o":["Superior quadrantanopia","Inferior quadrantanopia","Priapism","Failure of lactation"],"a":0,"e":"Superior quadrantanopia may be seen in patients with prolactinoma > 10 mm in size (macroadenoma).\n\nIn this condition, visual field defects occur due to compression of the optic chiasm. In the initial stages, the pituitary tumor tends to compress the ventral portion of the optic chiasm. This causes a bitemporal (heteronymous) superior field defect. As the tumor grows larger, the entire optic chiasm gets compressed, leading to complete bitemporal hemianopia, as shown below.\n\nDamage to the optic radiation in the temporal lobe would result in homonymous superior quadrantanopia or pie-in-the-sky appearance.","t":"Pituitary Tumours And Sheehan'S Syndrome","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Patients with which of the following conditions do not need to be screened for pituitary tumors?","o":["MEN1","MEN2","MEN4","Carney Complex"],"a":1,"e":"MEN2 syndrome is not associated with pituitary tumors. It is associated with medullary thyroid cancer and pheochromocytoma.\n\nThe genetic syndromes associated with pituitary tumors include:\n\n• MEN1\n\n• MEN4\n\n• Carney complex tumors\n\n• McCune-Albright Syndrome","t":"Pituitary Tumours And Sheehan'S Syndrome","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is the most common clinical presentation in patients with craniopharyngiomas?","o":["Growth retardation","Bitemporal hemianopia","Headache","Endocrinal disturbance"],"a":2,"e":"Headache is the most common presenting symptom of craniopharyngioma.\n\nCraniopharyngiomas are benign, suprasellar cystic masses. They have a bimodal distribution, occurring predominantly in children but also between the ages of 55 and 65 years. It can present with the following clinical symptoms:\n\n• Headache\n\n• Hypopituitarism\n\n• Hypogonadotropic hypogonadism\n\n• Central diabetes insipidus\n\n• Bitemporal hemianopia\n\n• Raised ICT - vomiting, papilledema, hydrocephalus","t":"Pituitary Tumours And Sheehan'S Syndrome","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A 10-year-old boy is referred for evaluation of progressively worsening headaches and visual field defects. An MRI brain is done and shows the following findings. Which of the following statements is incorrect about this condition?","o":["Composed of epithelial cells","Surgery is curative in most patients","Contains cholesterol crystals","Calcifications are seen in adamantinomatous type"],"a":1,"e":"The given MRI showing a suprasellar mass with calcifications is suggestive of a craniopharyngioma. Surgery is not curative in the majority of patients as the tumor is adherent to surrounding structures.\n\nThey are benign epithelial cell-containing tumors and are of two types:\n\n• Adamantinomatous - calcifications and keratin nodules are seen\n\n• Papillary - calcifications are rare\n\nBiopsy reveals machinery or motor-oil-like fluid within the cyst that contains cholesterol crystals. The treatment of choice is transcranial or transsphenoidal resection. It must be followed by postoperative radiation to prevent recurrence.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8c6b875818094331a298a1c5f874a13e.PNG"],"t":"Pituitary Tumours And Sheehan'S Syndrome","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Patients with which of the following conditions are not at risk for pituitary apoplexy?","o":["Hyperthyroidism","Diabetes mellitus","Sickle cell anemia","Hypertension"],"a":0,"e":"Pituitary apoplexy does not occur in hyperthyroidism. It is a rare endocrine emergency which can occur due to haemorrhage or infarction of the pituitary gland. It may occur as a result of the following conditions:\n\n• Pituitary adenoma (spontaneous)\n\n• Sheehan syndrome\n\n• Acute shock\n\n• Diabetes mellitus\n\n• Hypertension\n\n• Sickle cell anemia","t":"Pituitary Tumours And Sheehan'S Syndrome","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which hormone is the first to fall in Sheehan syndrome?","o":["Prolactin","GH","FSH","ACTH"],"a":1,"e":"Growth hormone (GH) is the first to fall in Sheehan syndrome. The hormones are affected in the order of GH → Prolactin → LH and FSH → ACTH → TSH\n\nSheehan syndrome refers to postpartum pituitary necrosis following severe postpartum hemorrhage (PPH). During pregnancy, the pituitary gland undergoes hypertrophy with increased blood supply. This makes it susceptible to ischemia and infarction when there is severe hypovolemia.\n\nThe clinical presentation depends on the amount of damage to the pituitary.\n\n• Mild - can go unnoticed for many years\n\n• Moderate - develops over months\n\n• Lactation failure\n\n• Failure to resume menses\n\n• Mild fatigue and anorexia\n\n• Severe - endocrine emergency that develops within few days\n\n• Hypotension and shock\n\n• Severe hypoglycemia\n\n• Lactation failure\n\nGH deficiency is very common in Sheehan’s syndrome because somatotrophs are located in the lower and lateral regions of the pituitary gland, and are most likely to be damaged by ischemic necrosis of the pituitary.","t":"Pituitary Tumours And Sheehan'S Syndrome","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"You are a first-year resident in the postpartum ward. During rounds, you see a 28-year-old woman who has been unable to breastfeed since delivery. Her baby was born by normal vaginal delivery that was complicated by severe postpartum hemorrhage. Her blood pressure is 90/50 mm Hg and her pulse is 96/min. What is the next best step in management?","o":["Estrogen","Growth hormone","Thyroxine","Cortisone"],"a":3,"e":"This clinical scenario is suggestive of Sheehan syndrome. Corticosteroids must be supplemented first, as life-threatening manifestations may occur due to the sudden onset of adrenal insufficiency. This is followed by the replacement of other deficient pituitary hormones including thyroid hormone and estrogen administered with or without progesterone.","t":"Pituitary Tumours And Sheehan'S Syndrome","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following are functions of oxytocin?\n\n1. Milk ejection\n\n2. Milk production\n\n3. Milk storage\n\n4. Facilitation of labor\n\n5. Osmoregulation","o":["1 only","1 and 4 only","1, 2 and 4 only","3, 4 and 5 only"],"a":1,"e":"Oxytocin plays a role in milk ejection. It also helps in initiation or facilitation of labour by stimulating the contraction of uterine smooth muscles.\n\nIt stimulates postpartum milk let-down in response to suckling.\n\nProlactin is the hormone responsible for milk production.\n\nOsmoregulation is mediated by hormones such as ADH, aldosterone, etc.","t":"Posterior Pituitary   Adh, Oxytocin & Diabetes Insipidus","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Where are arginine vasopressin (AVP) and oxytocin stored?","o":["Anterior pituitary","Supraoptic nucleus","Paraventricular nucleus","Herring bodies"],"a":3,"e":"AVP/Antidiuretic hormone (ADH) and oxytocin are stored in Herring bodies at the axon terminals of the posterior pituitary. They are synthesized by two types of magnocellular neurons in the supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei of the hypothalamus.","t":"Posterior Pituitary   Adh, Oxytocin & Diabetes Insipidus","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements is true?","o":["AVP binds to V1 receptors on the serosal surface of renal tubular cells","AVP binds to V1 receptors on the luminal surface of renal tubular cells","AVP binds to V2 receptors on the serosal surface of renal tubular cells","AVP binds to V2 receptors on the luminal surface of renal tubular cells"],"a":2,"e":"AVP binds to V2 receptors on the serosal surface (also called basolateral surface) of renal tubular cells.\n\nThis interaction activates adenylyl cyclase and leads to the insertion of water channels called aquaporin 2 on the luminal surface of the cell.","t":"Posterior Pituitary   Adh, Oxytocin & Diabetes Insipidus","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements about ADH is incorrect?","o":["It increases aquaporin expression on apical membrane","It increases urine concentration and decreases urine flow","Post-operative secretion is high","Its secretion occurs when plasma osmolality is low"],"a":3,"e":"ADH secretion reduces when the plasma osmolality becomes low. ADH causes water reabsorption and dilutes the plasma, thereby causing a reduction in plasma osmolality.\n\nThe antidiuretic effect of ADH is mediated via a G protein-coupled V2 receptor that increases intracellular cyclic AMP, thereby inducing translocation of aquaporin 2 (AQP 2) water channels into the apical membrane.\n\nDuring and after surgery, surgical stress results in the release of ADH.","t":"Posterior Pituitary   Adh, Oxytocin & Diabetes Insipidus","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following conditions would you not expect to see in a patient with Wolfram syndrome?","o":["Diabetes mellitus","Sensorineural deafness","Optic atrophy","SIADH"],"a":3,"e":"Diabetes insipidus is a clinical component of Wolfram syndrome, and not SIADH.\n\nCharacteristics of Wolfram syndrome include:\n\n• Diabetes insipidus\n\n• Diabetes mellitus\n\n• Optic atrophy\n\n• Sensorineural deafness\n\n(Mnemonic DIDMOAD)\n\nWolfram syndrome is caused by mutations of the WFS1 gene.","t":"Posterior Pituitary   Adh, Oxytocin & Diabetes Insipidus","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"All of the following statements about diabetes insipidus are true, except:","o":["Low urine osmolality","Dilutional hyponatremia","High plasma osmolality","Diagnosed by water deprivation test"],"a":1,"e":"In diabetes insipidus, hypernatremia is observed in the affected patient.\n\nLow levels of vasopressin lead to the excretion of a large amount of diluted urine. This loss of water from the body leads to hypernatremia.","t":"Posterior Pituitary   Adh, Oxytocin & Diabetes Insipidus","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A man presented with frequent urination and increased thirst. Investigations revealed low urine osmolality and high plasma osmolality. MRI brain was done, which revealed the absence of posterior pituitary bright spot. Which of the following conditions is least likely to lead to this presentation?","o":["Multiple sclerosis","Head injury","Histiocytosis","Viral encephalitis"],"a":0,"e":"The given scenario and the absence of a posterior pituitary bright spot on MRI are suggestive of central diabetes insipidus. Multiple sclerosis causes primary polydipsia, not central DI.\n\nCentral DI is caused by a primary deficiency in AVP secretion which usually results from agenesis or irreversible destruction of the posterior pituitary. It can be differentiated from primary polydipsia with the help of an MRI brain.\n\nThe MRI given below shows the posterior pituitary bright spot. The arrow points at the posterior pituitary and the arrowhead at the anterior pituitary.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/336381c49f4d4c38961b5c588bc4263dx1200x1923.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d939a1468d2245ddae84596e2b6fc8e0x1280x1429.JPEG"],"t":"Posterior Pituitary   Adh, Oxytocin & Diabetes Insipidus","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A patient presented with frequent urination, nocturia, and enuresis. Investigations revealed 24-hour urine output of 7 litres and low urinary osmolality. ADH assay was performed and the recorded values were reported as 1.5 pg/ml. Which of the following drugs would not cause this condition?","o":["Lithium","Cisplatin","Isoniazid","Rifampin"],"a":2,"e":"The given scenario is suggestive of nephrogenic diabetes insipidus. Isoniazid is not a potential cause of this condition.\n\nNephrogenic DI develops due to resistance to the antidiuretic action of AVP. A 24-hour urine volume >3 liters is suggestive of polyuria. An ADH value >1 pg/ml is suggestive of nephrogenic DI.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/77513b9ff8fe4c23baec0ae3bd9b14d7x1200x1923.JPEG"],"t":"Posterior Pituitary   Adh, Oxytocin & Diabetes Insipidus","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A patient presents with polyuria and increased thirst. Investigations reveal hypercalcemia. What could be the cause of polyuria in this patient?","o":["Neurogenic DI","Nephrogenic DI","Iatrogenic polydipsia","Dipsogenic polydipsia"],"a":1,"e":"Metabolic derangements like hypercalcemia and hypokalemia lead to the production of large amounts of dilute urine by causing nephrogenic DI.\n\nOptions C & D: Primary polydipsia is of three types:\n\n• Dipsogenic – Due to inappropriate thirst.\n\n• Iatrogenic – Due to an increase in fluid intake due to its presumed health benefits.\n\n• Psychogenic – Due to psychosis or obsessive-compulsive disorder.","t":"Posterior Pituitary   Adh, Oxytocin & Diabetes Insipidus","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following findings do you expect to see in a patient with central diabetes insipidus?","o":["Low plasma and low urine osmolality","High plasma and high urine osmolality","Low plasma and high urine osmolality","Low urine and high plasma osmolality"],"a":3,"e":"Diabetes insipidus of central or nephrogenic origin are characterized by low urine osmolality and high plasma osmolality.\n\nCondition\n\nPlasma osmolality\n\nUrine osmolality\n\nSodium concentration\n\nCentral and nephrogenic DI\n\nHigh\n\nLow\n\nHypernatremia\n\nPsychogenic polydipsia\n\nLow\n\nLow\n\nHyponatremia\n\nSIADH\n\nLow\n\nHigh\n\nHyponatremia","t":"Posterior Pituitary   Adh, Oxytocin & Diabetes Insipidus","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A 29-year-old lady presented with polydipsia and polyuria. Her symptoms started soon after a road traffic accident 6 months ago. Her blood pressure was recorded at 120/80 mmHg with no postural drop. The daily urinary output was reported to be between 6-8 liters. Clinical investigation results are given below. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nNa = 130 mEq /L\n\nK = 3.5 mEq/ L\n\nUrea = 15 mg/dl\n\nSugar = 65 mg/ dl\n\nPlasma osmolality = 268 mOsm/L\n\nUrine osmolality = 45 mOsm/L","o":["Central diabetes insipidus","Cerebral salt wasting","Psychogenic polydipsia","Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus"],"a":2,"e":"The most likely diagnosis for the given clinical scenario would be psychogenic polydipsia.\n\nThe normal range of urine osmolality is 500-850 mOsm/L and that of plasma osmolality is 280-295 mOsm/L. Hence in the given scenario, both urine and plasma osmolality are low, along with hyponatremia suggestive of psychogenic polydipsia.\n\nCondition\n\nPlasma osmolality\n\nUrine osmolality\n\nSodium concentration\n\nCentral and nephrogenic DI\n\nHigh\n\nLow\n\nHypernatremia\n\nPsychogenic polydipsia\n\nLow\n\nLow\n\nHyponatremia\n\nSIADH and cerebral salt wasting\n\nLow\n\nHigh\n\nHyponatremia","t":"Posterior Pituitary   Adh, Oxytocin & Diabetes Insipidus","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A patient presented with polyuria and polydipsia. She has been on lithium therapy for 6 years for bipolar disorder. Investigations revealed low urine osmolality and high plasma osmolality. Which of the following drugs can be used for the treatment of this condition?","o":["Thiazide diuretics","Salt supplementation","Desmopressin","Vaptans"],"a":0,"e":"The given scenario is suggestive of lithium-induced nephrogenic DI. Among the given options thiazide diuretics is the best suited answer.\n\nThiazides reduce the kidney’s ability to excrete free water by increasing proximal tubular water reabsorption (secondary to volume contraction) and block the ability of DCT to form dilute urine. This effect results in an increase in urine osmolality.\n\nTreatment of diabetes insipidus:\n\n• Central and gestational diabetes insipidus:\n\n• Desmopressin an ADH analog\n\n• Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus:\n\n• Adequate water intake along with a low sodium diet\n\n• Thiazide diuretics\n\n• Potassium-sparing diuretics (triamterene and amiloride): Amiloride is the drug of choice for lithium-induced DI\n\n• Prostaglandin synthesis inhibitor (indomethacin)\n\n• Psychogenic diabetes insipidus:\n\n• Behavioral therapy to reduce fluid consumption","t":"Posterior Pituitary   Adh, Oxytocin & Diabetes Insipidus","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Polyuria characterized by low fixed specific gravity of urine is seen in which of the following conditions?","o":["Diabetes mellitus","Diabetes insipidus","Chronic glomerulonephritis","Potomania"],"a":2,"e":"Fixed specific gravity, in which values remain 1.010 regardless of fluid intake, occurs in chronic glomerulonephritis with severe renal damage.\n\nIn chronic kidney disease, as renal function deteriorates, polyuria can be seen due to the impairment of the kidney's ability to concentrate urine.\n\nIn diabetes insipidus, although specific gravity is low it is not fixed.\n\nPotomania (beer potomania/beer drinker's potomania/ beer drinker's hyponatremia) is a specific hypo-osmolality syndrome related to massive consumption of beer.","t":"Posterior Pituitary   Adh, Oxytocin & Diabetes Insipidus","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following findings will you not expect to see in a patient with SIADH?","o":["Normal serum urea and creatinine levels","Low uric acid levels","Hypernatremia","Normal blood pressure"],"a":2,"e":"SIADH does not cause hypernatremia. SIADH is the most common cause of euvolemic hyponatremia.\n\nThe excess ADH secreted leads to the gain of water in the body. This stimulates baroreceptors to release atrial natriuretic peptide (ANP). ANP leads to the loss of water and salt in the urine, leading to urine sodium level of > 20 mEq/L in these patients. Thus, the level of water reaches a steady state (euvolemia) but the urinary loss of sodium continues to occur, giving rise to hyponatremia.\n\nSIADH is characterized by low levels of uric acid, normal blood pressure, and normal urea and creatinine levels.\n\nSIADH is diagnosed based on the following criteria (Bartter-Schwartz criteria):\n\n1. Reduced serum osmolality\n2. Urine osmolality > Serum osmolality\n3. Clinical euvolemia\n4. Increased levels of urinary sodium\n5. Absence of other causes of hyponatremia like pituitary, renal, adrenal, thyroid or liver diseases.","t":"Syndrome Of Inappropriate Anti Diuretic Hormone Secretion (Siadh)","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A 66-year-old chronic smoker who was diagnosed with small cell carcinoma of the lung presents to the casualty with nausea, confusion, and dizziness. After the initial workup, he is diagnosed to have syndrome of inappropriate ADH secretion (SIADH). This condition is characterized by which of the following findings?","o":["Hyponatremia and urine sodium excretion &gt; 20 mEq/L","Hypernatremia and urine sodium excretion &gt; 20 mEq/L","Hyponatremia and urine sodium excretion &lt; 20 mEq/L","Hypernatremia and urine sodium excretion &lt; 20 mEq/L"],"a":0,"e":"The syndrome of inappropriate ADH secretion (SIADH) is associated with increased urinary excretion of sodium (urine sodium excretion > 20 mEq/L), and hyponatremia.\n\nSmall cell lung cancer is commonly associated with SIADH due to the ectopic release of antidiuretic hormone.","t":"Syndrome Of Inappropriate Anti Diuretic Hormone Secretion (Siadh)","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"SIADH is not observed in which of the following conditions?","o":["Multiple sclerosis","Oat cell carcinoma of lungs","Acute attack of porphyria","Primary pulmonary emphysema"],"a":3,"e":"Primary pulmonary emphysema is not a cause of SIADH. Respiratory causes of SIADH include asthma, pneumothorax and positive-pressure respiration.","t":"Syndrome Of Inappropriate Anti Diuretic Hormone Secretion (Siadh)","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A 47-year-old woman presented with headache and nausea. Examination did not reveal any signs of dehydration and investigations revealed the findings shown below. Which of the following drugs does not lead to this condition?\n\nLaboratory tests\nResults\n\nSerum sodium\n118 mmol/L\n\nSerum osmolality\n267 mOsm/kg\n\nUrine osmolality\n362 mOsm/kg\n\nUrine sodium\n70 mmol/l","o":["Demeclocycline","Vincristine","Carbamazepine","Nicotine"],"a":0,"e":"The given scenario is suggestive of SIADH and demeclocycline does not cause this condition. Demeclocycline causes nephrogenic diabetes insipidus and hence is used in the treatment of chronic SIADH.\n\nThe following features in the given case point towards the diagnosis of SIADH:\n\n• Serum osmolality less than 275 mOsm/Kg\n\n• Urine osmolality more than 100 mOsm/Kg\n\n• Urine sodium more than 20 mmol/L\n\n• Serum sodium level less than 135 mmol/L","t":"Syndrome Of Inappropriate Anti Diuretic Hormone Secretion (Siadh)","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following tumors is not associated with syndrome of inappropriate ADH secretion (SIADH)?","o":["Ewing’s sarcoma","Thymoma","Chromophobe adenoma","Carcinoid"],"a":2,"e":"Chromophobe adenoma is a type of pituitary adenoma that causes symptoms of hypogonadism due to pressure effects on the pituitary stalk. It is not a cause of syndrome of inappropriate ADH secretion (SIADH).","t":"Syndrome Of Inappropriate Anti Diuretic Hormone Secretion (Siadh)","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A child who underwent ventriculoperitoneal shunt surgery for tubercular meningitis with hydrocephalus, develops polyuria and hyponatremia. What could be the probable cause?","o":["Central diabetes insipidus","SIADH","Cerebral salt wasting","Shunt block"],"a":2,"e":"The given scenario is suggestive of cerebral salt wasting. Both SIADH and cerebral salt wasting cause hyponatremia, but polyuria happens in cerebral salt wasting.\n\nCerebral salt wasting results from hypersecretion of atrial natriuretic peptide. It is seen primarily with central nervous system disorders including brain tumors, head trauma, hydrocephalus, neurosurgery, cerebrovascular accidents, and brain death. It is associated with hyponatremia, polyuria and elevated urinary sodium excretion. Treatment is by restoring intravascular volume with sodium chloride and water.\n\nClinical parameters to distinguish between SIADH, cerebral salt wasting, and central diabetes insipidus are:\n\nClinical parameter\nSIADH\nCerebral salt wasting\nCentral DI\n\nSerum sodium\nLow\nLow\nHigh\n\nUrine output\nNormal or low\nHigh\nHigh\n\nUrine sodium\nHigh\nVery high\nLow\n\nIntravascular volume status\nNormal or high\nLow\nLow\n\nVasopressin level\nHigh\nLow\nLow","t":"Syndrome Of Inappropriate Anti Diuretic Hormone Secretion (Siadh)","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A patient who presented with acute confusion and vomiting was diagnosed to have SIADH. This condition can be best treated by which of the following methods?","o":["Infusion of hypertonic (3%) saline","Infusion of hypotonic (0.45%) saline","Infusion of normal (0.9%) saline","Administration of DDAVP"],"a":0,"e":"The given scenario is suggestive of acute symptomatic SIADH and this condition is best treated by the infusion of hypertonic (3%) saline. This mode of treatment also helps in the correction of sodium deficiency that is partly responsible for hyponatremia.\n\nThe other form of treatment is to help reduce the concentration of body water by giving an AVP receptor-2 antagonist, conivaptan. Conivaptan blocks the antidiuretic effect of AVP and increases urine output.\n\nDDAVP is a synthetic analog of AVP and should not be used as it will further increase water retention.","t":"Syndrome Of Inappropriate Anti Diuretic Hormone Secretion (Siadh)","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"What is the drug of choice for the treatment of chronic SIADH?","o":["Furosemide","Tolvaptan","Demeclocycline","Fludrocortisone"],"a":1,"e":"The drug of choice for the treatment of chronic SIADH is tolvaptan.\n\nVaptans are selective V2 receptor antagonists that act by increasing urinary water excretion by blocking the antidiuretic effect of AVP.\n\nTreatment options for chronic SIADH include:\n\n• Restriction of fluid intake to below the level of urine output - This is considered the mainstay of treatment. But it also has certain disadvantages as stated below, and hence adding on a drug like vaptan is necessary in most cases.\n\n• Tolvaptan\n\n• Demeclocycline\n\n• Fludrocortisone\n\nAdvantages of vaptans over fluid restriction:\n\n• Fluid restriction is usually a burden to the patient, unreliable, impractical, and slow to work.\n\n• Increased thirst is a frequently observed characteristic of hyponatremia in SIADH, and is particularly difficult to suppress. Hence, water restriction in SIADH frequently leads to non-compliance in the long run.","t":"Syndrome Of Inappropriate Anti Diuretic Hormone Secretion (Siadh)","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"What causes central pontine myelinolysis?","o":["Hyponatremia","Hypokalemia","Rapid correction of hyponatremia","Rapid correction of hypokalemia"],"a":2,"e":"Rapid correction of hyponatremia may result in central pontine myelinolysis.\n\nCentral pontine myelinolysis is an osmotic demyelination syndrome (ODS), wherein rapid correction of hyponatremia results in water being driven out of the cell which has adapted to a chronic state of hypotonicity, leading to damage to the myelin sheath of these cells.\n\nIt is characterized by the acute onset of quadriparesis, ataxia, and abnormal extraocular movements.","t":"Syndrome Of Inappropriate Anti Diuretic Hormone Secretion (Siadh)","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is a specific sign for Graves' disease?","o":["Goiter","Ophthalmopathy","Thyroid bruit","Pretibial myxedema"],"a":1,"e":"Ophthalmopathy is the specific sign of Graves’ disease.\n\nGlycosaminoglycan deposition, lymphoid infiltrates and fatty infiltration are responsible for ophthalmopathy and dermopathy.\n\nGrave's disease is an autoimmune disorder due to the production of TSH-receptor stimulating immunoglobulins (TSI) thereby causing hyperthyroidism.\n\nIt is characterized by the triad of:\n\n• Thyrotoxicosis\n\n• Infiltrative ophthalmopathy (exophthalmos)\n\n• Dermopathy (pretibial myxedema)\n\nOphthalmopathy can sometimes precede or follow thyrotoxicosis, accounting for some cases of euthyroid ophthalmopathy.","t":"Thyroid Disorders   Clinical Features","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A patient with Graves' disease presents with the following finding. Which statement regarding this condition is false?","o":["Acropachy is strongly associated with this condition.","It occurs in more than 50% of patients with Graves' disease.","It occurs almost always in the presence of moderate or severe form of ophthalmopathy.","Skin changes can occur after trauma."],"a":1,"e":"The given scenario along with the image is suggestive of thyroid dermopathy. It occurs in <5% of patients with Graves’ disease. Thyroid dermopathy is almost always associated with ophthalmopathy.\n\nThyroid dermopathy is most frequently observed over the anterolateral parts of the lower leg. Hence, it is termed pretibial myxedema. Skin changes can occur at other sites, particularly after trauma. The typical lesion is a noninflamed, indurated plaque with a deep pink or purple color and orange peel-textured papules.\n\nThyroid acropachy refers to a form of clubbing rarely observed in Graves’ disease. It is strongly associated with thyroid dermopathy.\n\nThyroid dermopathy usually does not need any active treatment, but if necessary, treatment includes topical, high-potency glucocorticoid ointment under an occlusive dressing or octreotide can be useful in some cases.\n\nThe following image shows thyroid dermopathy and acropachy:","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e457c1b330714277b45f233d1540f036.JPEG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/12274110d5f0483aad85a047442f26f9x720x505.PNG"],"t":"Thyroid Disorders   Clinical Features","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A patient from a hilly area was referred with a partial small bowel obstruction confirmed on CECT. Conservative management was tried for a week, but he continued to deteriorate and was taken up for emergency laparotomy. On post-operative day 7, he developed atrial fibrillation. Investigations revealed elevated free T3 and free T4 levels. What is the most probable cause?","o":["Jod-Basedow phenomenon","Wolff-Chaikoff effect","Basedow disease","Plummer disease"],"a":0,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of the Jod-Basedow phenomenon, likely due to the administration of an iodine-containing contrast agent during CECT.\n\nThis phenomenon is characterized by hyperthyroidism due to enhanced thyroid hormone production following the administration of contrast agents and other iodine-containing substances. This occurs in:\n\n• Patients with autonomous thyroid nodules\n\n• Patients with pre-existing subclinical hypothyroidism (endemic iodine deficiency in hilly areas)\n\nOption B: Wolff-Chaikoff effect is characterized by the transient inhibition of thyroid iodide organification by excess iodine, thereby decreasing thyroid hormone synthesis.\n\nOption C: Basedow disease is another name for Graves' disease.\n\nOption D: Plummer disease is another name for toxic multinodular goitre.","t":"Thyroid Disorders   Clinical Features","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"What is the most common symptom of thyrotoxicosis?","o":["Palpitation","Fatigue and weakness","Heat intolerance","Hyperactivity and irritability"],"a":3,"e":"Hyperactivity and irritability are considered as the most common symptoms of thyrotoxicosis.\n\nThe following table summarizes the symptoms and signs of thyrotoxicosis in descending order of frequency:\n\nSymptoms\nSigns\n\n• Hyperactivity, irritability, dysphoria\n\n• Heat intolerance and sweating\n\n• Palpitations\n\n• Fatigue and weakness\n\n• Weight loss with increased appetite\n\n• Diarrhoea\n\n• Polyuria\n\n• Oligomenorrhea, loss of libido\n\n• Tachycardia, atrial fibrillation in the elderly\n\n• Tremor\n\n• Goitre\n\n• Warm, moist skin\n\n• Muscle weakness, proximal myopathy\n\n• Lid retraction or lag\n\n• Gynecomastia\n\nRarely, there can be an associated mid-systolic heart murmur called the Means-Lerman scratch. This is heard in the upper part of the sternum caused by the rubbing of the pericardium and the pleura, due to the hyperdynamic circulation.","t":"Thyroid Disorders   Clinical Features","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements is not true about thyroid storm?","o":["Symptoms are vomiting, diarrhoea, jaundice and altered mentation","The mortality rate is 10% with treatment","Can happen after non-thyroid surgeries","High doses of propranolol decrease T4 → T3 conversion"],"a":1,"e":"The mortality rate of thyrotoxicosis crisis is almost 30% even with treatment.\n\nThe usual causes for mortality are:\n\n• Cardiac failure\n\n• Arrhythmia\n\n• Hyperthermia","t":"Thyroid Disorders   Clinical Features","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following conditions is not associated with amiodarone therapy?","o":["Transient suppression of thyroid gland","Hypothyroidism","Thyrotoxicosis","Silent thyroiditis"],"a":3,"e":"Silent thyroiditis is not associated with amiodarone therapy.\n\nAmiodarone has the following effects on the thyroid gland:\n\n• Acute, transient suppression of thyroid function i.e., Wolff-Chaikoff effect\n\n• Hypothyroidism in patients susceptible to the inhibitory effects of a high iodine load\n\n• Thyrotoxicosis may be caused by Jod-Basedow phenomenon due to the iodine load, in the setting of multinodular goitre or incipient Graves’ disease. It can also be caused by a thyroiditis-like condition.\n\nPainless thyroiditis, or silent thyroiditis, occurs in patients with underlying autoimmune thyroid disease and has a clinical course similar to that of subacute thyroiditis.\n\nThe condition can occur in up to 5% of women 3–6 months after pregnancy and is then termed postpartum thyroiditis.","t":"Thyroid Disorders   Clinical Features","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements about amiodarone-induced thyrotoxicosis is false?","o":["Type 2 AIT is associated with intrinsic abnormalities of the thyroid gland","Type 2 AIT is due to lysosomal destruction of thyroid gland.","Type 1 AIT is due to Jod-Basedow phenomenon.","Colour flow doppler shows increased vascularity in Type 1"],"a":0,"e":"Type 1 AIT is associated with intrinsic abnormalities of the thyroid gland.\n\nAmiodarone is structurally similar to thyroid hormones and contains 39% iodine by weight. Amiodarone-induced thyrotoxicosis (AIT) is the most common manifestation of the effects of amiodarone on the thyroid.\n\nType 1 AIT\nType 2 AIT\n\nAn underlying disease is present.\n\nE.g., Grave’s disease, Multinodular goitre etc\n\nNo underlying disease\n\nDue to the Jod-Basedow phenomenon\nDue to drug-induced lysosomal destruction of thyroid tissue\n\nIncreased vascularity on colour doppler\nDecreased vascularity on colour doppler\n\nAdministration of amiodarone should be stopped if possible.\n\nNear-total thyroidectomy rapidly decreases thyroid hormone levels and may be the most effective long-term clinical solution.","t":"Thyroid Disorders   Clinical Features","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements is true about non-toxic multinodular goitre?","o":["It is more commonly observed in women","It does not occur in iodine-deficient regions","Most patients with non-toxic MNG have hypothyroidism","Risk of malignancy is higher than in cases of solitary thyroid nodule"],"a":0,"e":"Multinodular goitre (MNG) is more commonly observed in women. It is common in iodine-deficient regions.\n\nMost patients are asymptomatic and euthyroid. Sudden pain may occur due to haemorrhage. TFTs should be performed in all patients to exclude thyrotoxicosis or hypothyroidism.\n\nThe risk of malignancy due to MNG is similar to that due to solitary nodules. Hoarseness, reflecting the involvement of laryngeal nerves implicate malignancy.\n\nMost non-toxic MNGs can be managed conservatively. Levothyroxine if used, should be started at low doses and then increased gradually. Radioiodine therapy is implemented for large goitres as it decreases goitre size and can ablate regions of autonomy.","t":"Thyroid Disorders   Clinical Features","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"What is the major difference between non-toxic and toxic multinodular goitre?","o":["Pathogenesis","Presence of functional autonomy","Polyclonal nodules","Monoclonal nodules"],"a":1,"e":"The major difference between toxic and non-toxic multinodular goiter (MNG) is the presence of functional autonomy in toxic MNG.\n\nThe pathogenesis of toxic MNG appears to be similar to that of non-toxic MNG. In both types, many nodules are polyclonal whereas others are monoclonal and vary in their clonal origins.\n\nMost patients with non-toxic MNG are asymptomatic and euthyroid. However, patients with toxic MNG have subclinical or mild hyperthyroidism.","t":"Thyroid Disorders   Clinical Features","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"What is the most common thyroid malignancy?","o":["Papillary carcinoma","Follicular carcinoma","Medullary thyroid carcinoma","Anaplastic carcinoma"],"a":0,"e":"Papillary carcinoma is the most common thyroid malignancy.\n\nPrevalence of different types of thyroid malignancies:\n\nType of malignancy\nOccurence\n\nPapillary\n80-90%\n\nFollicular\n5-10%\n\nMedullary\n<10%\n\nOthers\n1-2%","t":"Thyroid Disorders   Clinical Features","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"The following patients presented with thyroid nodules. Who has the least risk of developing thyroid carcinoma?","o":["A 55-year-old woman with no voice abnormalities and nodule size of 3 cms","A 65-year-old woman with vocal cord paralysis and hoarse voice and nodule size of 3.5 cms","A 60-year-old man without any voice abnormalities and nodule size of 2.5 cms","A 43-year-old woman with no voice abnormalities and nodule size of 4.5 cm"],"a":0,"e":"Among the options, the female patient with age between 20 and 65 years and no voice abnormalities and nodule size < 4 cm has the least risk for development of thyroid carcinoma.\n\nRisk factors for the development of thyroid carcinoma in patients with thyroid nodules include:\n\n• Age less than 20 or greater than 65 years\n\n• Male gender\n\n• Increased nodule size > 4 cm\n\n• Vocal cord paralysis and hoarse voice\n\n• New or enlarged neck mass\n\n• Family history of thyroid cancer, MEN2, or other syndromes associated with thyroid malignancy (e.g., Cowden’s syndrome, familial polyposis, Carney complex)\n\n• Nodule fixed to adjacent structures\n\n• Exposure to radiation in childhood or adolescence\n\n• Extrathyroidal extension\n\n• Lateral cervical lymphadenopathy","t":"Thyroid Disorders   Clinical Features","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements is not true about thyroid hormone resistance?","o":["Inherited as an autosomal recessive disorder","Characterized by elevated thyroid hormone levels and normal or elevated TSH","Classic forms are caused by mutations in TR-β gene","No treatment is indicated usually"],"a":0,"e":"Thyroid hormone resistance is an autosomal dominant disorder.\n\nIt is characterized by elevated thyroid hormone levels and inappropriately elevated or normal TSH levels.\n\nClassic forms of the disease are attributed to the mutation in TR-β gene. Patients with this condition are often asymptomatic, as hormone resistance is partial and compensated by the increase in thyroid hormone levels.\n\nIn most patients, no treatment is indicated.","t":"Thyroid Disorders   Clinical Features","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Patients with what condition present with normal thyroglobulin levels?","o":["Graves' disease","Toxic multinodular goiter","Thyrotoxicosis factitia","Toxic adenoma"],"a":2,"e":"Serum thyroglobulin levels are normal in patients with thyrotoxicosis factitia, as it is caused by the self-administration of thyroid hormone. Its level is elevated in all other types of thyrotoxicosis.\n\nThyroglobulin (Tg) levels are particularly increased in thyroiditis, implicating destruction of thyroid tissue and release of thyroglobulin.\n\nThe primary course of action for determining the thyroglobulin levels, however, is in the clinical follow-up of thyroid cancer patients.\n\nFollowing total thyroidectomy and radiofrequency ablation, thyroglobulin levels become undetectable. In the absence of anti-Tg antibodies, measurable levels of Tg are indicative of incomplete ablation or recurrent cancer.","t":"Thyroid Disorders   Clinical Features","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A patient presented with fever and painful neck swelling. She gives a history of sore throat and cough one week ago. Lab investigations revealed elevated T3 and T4 levels and elevated ESR. Which of the following statements about this condition is false?","o":["Treatment of choice is supportive care and NSAIDs","Women are generally affected three times more often than men","Both thyrotoxic and hypothyroid phases can be observed","It is autoimmune in origin"],"a":3,"e":"The given scenario is suggestive of De Quervain’s thyroiditis, and it is not autoimmune in origin. It is subacute granulomatous thyroiditis that occurs secondary to viral infections.\n\nMany viruses such as mumps, influenza, coxsackie, adenoviruses, and echoviruses have been clinically implicated in this condition. Peak incidence is observed at 30-50 years and women are generally affected three times more often than men.\n\nClinically, it presents as a painfully enlarged thyroid and is sometimes accompanied by fever.\n\nIt has three distinct phases:\n\n• Thyrotoxic phase\n\n• Hypothyroid phase\n\n• Recovery phase\n\nTreatment includes large doses of aspirin, NSAIDs, steroids and/or beta-blockers to relieve the observable clinical symptoms. Antithyroid drugs do not have any role. In case the hypothyroid phase is prolonged - levothyroxine can be used.","t":"Thyroid Disorders   Clinical Features","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following conditions is not associated with hypothyroidism?","o":["Graves' disease","Hashimoto's thyroiditis","Hypopituitarism","Lithium-induced Goiter"],"a":0,"e":"Graves' disease is not associated with hypothyroidism.","t":"Thyroid Disorders   Clinical Features","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A 4-month-old baby presented with a history of constipation, lethargy, and has poor activities. Clinical examination reveals jaundice, hypotonia and there was no palpable goitre. Laboratory investigations revealed low T4 and elevated TSH levels. Which of the following is not true about this condition?","o":["Most common cause is inborn defects in the synthesis of thyroid hormone","The developmental abnormalities are more common in girls","Associated with macroglossia","High circulating T4 level is needed to normalize TSH"],"a":0,"e":"The given scenario is suggestive of congenital hypothyroidism. The most common cause of this condition is thyroid gland dysgenesis, seen in about 80-85% of cases of neonatal hypothyroidism.\n\nIn the remaining cases of neonatal hypothyroidism, it is due to inborn defects in thyroid hormone synthesis and rarely due to TSH receptor antibodies. These developmental abnormalities are twice as common in girls.\n\nClinical features include:\n\n• Prolonged jaundice\n\n• Umbilical hernia\n\n• Enlarged tongue\n\n• Hypotonia\n\n• Delayed bone maturation\n\nPermanent neurological damage results if treatment is delayed.\n\nT4 requirements are relatively high during the first year of life. A high circulating T4 level is needed to normalize TSH.\n\nEarly treatment with T4 results in normal IQ levels by preventing neurodevelopmental abnormalities.","t":"Thyroid Disorders   Clinical Features","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Patients with autoimmune hypothyroidism have a higher predisposition for all except:","o":["Type I diabetes mellitus","Type II diabetes mellitus","Pernicious anemia","Vitiligo"],"a":1,"e":"Autoimmune hypothyroidism is usually not associated with type II diabetes mellitus.\n\nGenetic risk factors for autoimmune hypothyroidism has been documented with HLA-DR3, HLA-DR4, HLA-DR5. A weak association also exists with CTLA4 gene polymorphism.\n\nThis may explain the relationship between autoimmune hypothyroidism and type I diabetes mellitus, Addison's disease, pernicious anemia, and vitiligo.\n\nLess commonly encountered clinical associations include:\n\n• Celiac disease\n\n• Dermatitis herpetiformis\n\n• Chronic active hepatitis\n\n• Rheumatoid arthritis\n\n• Myasthenia gravis\n\n• Systemic lupus erythematosus\n\n• Sjogren's syndrome.","t":"Thyroid Disorders   Clinical Features","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Most common symptom seen in patients with hypothyroidism is?","o":["Cold intolerance","Weight gain","Tiredness and weakness","Dry skin"],"a":2,"e":"The most common symptom of hypothyroidism is tiredness and weakness.\n\nSymptoms and signs in decreasing order of frequency are given below:\n\nSymptoms\n\nSigns\n\n• Tiredness, weakness\n\n• Dry skin\n\n• Cold intolerance\n\n• Hair loss\n\n• Difficulty concentrating and poor memory\n\n• Constipation\n\n• Weight gain with poor appetite\n\n• Dyspnea\n\n• Hoarse voice\n\n• Menorrhagia (later oligomenorrhea or amenorrhea)\n\n• Paresthesia\n\n• Impaired hearing\n\n• Dry coarse skin, cool peripheral extremities\n\n• Puffy face, hands, and feet (myxedema)\n\n• Diffuse alopecia\n\n• Bradycardia\n\n• Peripheral edema\n\n• Delayed tendon reflex relaxation\n\n• Carpal tunnel syndrome\n\n• Serous cavity effusions","t":"Thyroid Disorders   Clinical Features","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"An elderly patient presents to the ER with symptoms suggestive of myxedema coma. Which of the following is unlikely to be seen in this patient?","o":["Hypoglycemia","Hyponatremia","Hypercapnia","Hyperthermia"],"a":3,"e":"Hyperthermia is least likely in this patient. Clinical manifestations of myxedema coma include decreased consciousness, seizures and hypothermia.\n\nMyxedema coma is a serious condition with a mortality rate of 20-40%. The clinical outcomes are independent of the detected T4 and TSH levels.\n\nClinical features of myxedema crisis include:\n\n• Impaired cognition, ranging from confusion to somnolence to coma (myxedema coma)\n\n• Convulsions and abnormal CNS signs.\n\n• Profound hypothermia (can reach 23°C), hypoventilation, hyponatremia, hypoglycemia, hypoxemia, hypercapnia, and hypotension.\n\n• Rhabdomyolysis and acute kidney injury.","t":"Thyroid Disorders   Clinical Features","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"What is the most common pattern of thyroid hormone levels in a patient with sick euthyroid syndrome?\n\n1. Decreased T3, Normal T4, Normal TSH\n\n2. Decreased T3 , Decreased T4, Normal TSH\n\n3. Normal T3, Normal T4, Increased TSH\n\n4. Normal T3, Increased T4, Normal TSH","o":["1","3","2","4"],"a":0,"e":"The most common hormone pattern observed in sick euthyroid syndrome (SES) is a decrease in total and unbound T3 levels with normal levels of T4 and TSH. This pattern is called low T3 syndrome.\n\nSES is caused by an acute severe illness in the absence of underlying thyroid disease. T4 to T3 conversion via peripheral deiodination is impaired leading to a fall in T3 corresponding to the severity of illness. There is an adaptive increase in the level of reverse T3 (rT3).\n\nIn very sick patients, there can be a dramatic fall in both total T4 and T3 levels. This pattern is called low T4 syndrome and is associated with a poor prognosis.","t":"Thyroid Disorders   Management","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following drugs can cause the condition shown below?","o":["Methimazole","Propylthiouracil","Radioactive iodine","Levothyroxine"],"a":0,"e":"The given image shows the absence of scalp hair and skin, along with calvarial defects(skull bone defects), suggestive of aplasia cutis congenita. This is commonly associated with carbimazole and methimazole.\n\nThese drugs can also cause other defects such as:\n\n• Choanal atresia\n\n• Tracheoesophageal fistula\n\n• Fetal goiter","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/28da72d5a99d4d2197e4cff33f66c680.JPEG"],"t":"Thyroid Disorders   Management","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following patients cannot undergo radioiodine therapy?","o":["Patients with renal disease","Patients with cardiac disease","Lactating mother","Patients with severe ophthalmopathy"],"a":2,"e":"Lactating mothers cannot undergo radioiodine therapy. Pregnancy and breastfeeding are absolute contraindications of radioiodine therapy. Patients can safely conceive 6 months after undergoing radioiodine treatment.\n\nRadioiodine therapy in patients with severe ophthalmopathy can be given with caution along with prednisone to prevent exacerbation of ophthalmopathy.","t":"Thyroid Disorders   Management","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A 25-year-old woman presents with palpitations. She informs you that a urine pregnancy test done one week ago was positive. Her investigations reveal hyperthyroidism. Which of the following antithyroid drugs can be prescribed for her?","o":["Propylthiouracil","Methimazole","Carbimazole","Propranolol"],"a":0,"e":"The patient in the given clinical scenario is in the 1st trimester of pregnancy. Propylthiouracil is used in the 1st trimester of gestation because it has lower teratogenic effects than carbimazole.\n\nCarbimazole and methimazole are associated with teratogenic effects such as\n\n• Aplasia cutis congenita\n\n• Choanal atresia\n\n• Treacheosopageal fistula\n\n• Fetal goiter\n\nHowever, propylthiouracil use is limited to the first trimester due to the risk of hepatotoxicity. It is replaced with carbimazole or methimazole in the 2nd and 3rd trimesters of the pregnancy.\n\nThe lowest effective antithyroid drug dose should be used throughout gestation. This is to maintain the maternal serum-free T4 level in the upper limit of the non-pregnant normal reference range.","t":"Thyroid Disorders   Management","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A patient undergoing radioactive iodine therapy for a non-toxic multinodular goitre later develops altered sensorium, fever, and vomiting. What is the anti-thyroid drug of choice in this patient?","o":["Propranolol","Iodide","Propylthiouracil","Methimazole"],"a":2,"e":"The given scenario is suggestive of thyroid storm and propylthiouracil is the drug of choice in this patient. This is due to its inhibitory action on peripheral T4 → T3 conversion.\n\nPTU is administered in large doses (500–1000 mg loading dose and 250 mg every 4h) administered orally / via nasogastric tube / per rectum.\n\nPropranolol is also given to reduce symptoms like tachycardia and other adrenergic manifestations.","t":"Thyroid Disorders   Management","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A patient presents with a diffusely enlarged thyroid gland. However, she has no features of hyperthyroidism. Which of the following statements about her condition is false?","o":["It is also referred to as simple goitre or colloid goitre","Most commonly caused by iodine deficiency","Surgery is commonly indicated","Thyroid function tests should be performed in all patients with goiter"],"a":2,"e":"The given scenario is suggestive of a non-toxic diffuse goitre. Surgery is rarely indicated in this condition, except for cosmetic reasons, tracheal compression or obstruction of the thoracic inlet.\n\nIt is sometimes called simple goitre - because of the absence of nodules, and colloid goitre - because of the presence of uniform follicles that are filled with colloid. It is most commonly caused by iodine deficiency and is often termed endemic goitre.\n\nMost diffuse goitres are benign and asymptomatic. Examination reveals a symmetrically enlarged, non-tender, generally soft gland without palpable nodules. TFTs should be performed in all patients to exclude thyrotoxicosis or hypothyroidism.\n\nIf markedly enlarged, it can cause tracheal/oesophagal compression.\n\nSubsternal goitre may obstruct the thoracic inlet leading to a positive Pemberton’s sign i.e., facial congestion on bilateral arm elevation due to venous occlusion.","t":"Thyroid Disorders   Management","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements about silent thyroiditis is true?","o":["Painful thyroiditis","Glucocorticoids are not indicated for treatment","It is not associated with thyrotoxicosis","It is not associated with hypothyroidism"],"a":1,"e":"Glucocorticoid treatment is not indicated in silent thyroiditis.\n\nThe condition is called silent thyroiditis, as it is painless. When it occurs 3-6 months after pregnancy, it is termed postpartum thyroiditis. The condition is observed in up to 5% of women and it is 3 times more common in women with Type 1 diabetes mellitus.\n\nTypically, patients experience a brief phase of thyrotoxicosis lasting 2-4 weeks, followed by hypothyroidism for 4-12 weeks, and then clinical resolution. Often, only one phase is apparent.\n\nIn addition to painless goitre, silent thyroiditis can be distinguished from subacute thyroiditis by a normal ESR value and the presence of TPO antibodies.\n\nSevere thyrotoxic symptoms can be managed with a brief course of propranolol, a 20–40 mg dose administered three or four times daily.\n\nThyroxine replacement may be needed for treating the hypothyroid phase but should be withdrawn after 6–9 months, to enable patient recovery.","t":"Thyroid Disorders   Management","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A middle-aged woman with fatigue, depression and weight gain was found to have hypothyroidism. Which of the following statements is false about the treatment of her condition?","o":["The replacement dose of thyroid is 1.6 μg/kg body weight","Complete relief from symptoms might occur within 3-6 months after initiation of treatment","TSH responses should be determined 2 months following the initiation of treatment or change in dose","Once TSH level is stable, TSH measurement is recommended at annual intervals"],"a":1,"e":"The clinical effects of levothyroxine replacement are slow to appear. Patients may not experience full relief from symptoms until 3–6 months after normal TSH levels are restored.\n\nIf there is no residual function, the daily replacement dose of levothyroxine is usually 1.6 μg/kg body weight (typically 100–150 μg), ideally taken at least 30 minutes before breakfast.\n\nTSH responses are gradual and should be determined 2 months following the initiation of treatment or change in dose. Once the full replacement is achieved and TSH levels are stable, TSH measurement is recommended at annual intervals.\n\nIn patients who have developed hypothyroidism following treatment of Graves' disease, lower doses are needed for replacement. This is because there is often underlying autonomous function, necessitating lower replacement doses (typically 75–125 μg/d).","t":"Thyroid Disorders   Management","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A 24-year-old primigravida at 10 weeks of gestational age presents to your OPD. She is on oral levothyroxine supplementation for hypothyroidism. What is the target TSH level to be maintained in this patient?","o":["&lt;2.5 mIu/L","&lt;3.0 mIu/L","&lt;3.5 mIu/L","&lt;4.0 mIu/L"],"a":0,"e":"The target TSH level to be maintained in the first trimester of pregnancy is <2.5 mIU/L. For second and third trimester the target is <3.0 mIU/L.\n\nThyroid function must be evaluated by testing for TSH levels every 4 weeks in the first trimester and less frequently after 20 weeks. The levothyroxine dose may be increased by 50% during pregnancy. Following delivery, doses of thyroxine are usually restored to that of pre-pregnancy levels.","t":"Thyroid Disorders   Management","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"An elderly woman with hypothyroidism presents to the ER with decreased consciousness, hypothermia, sinus bradycardia, and hypotension. Her family members reveal that she has not been taking her thyroid medications recently. Investigations reveal elevated TSH levels and reduced T3 and T4 levels. All of the following can be used in the treatment except:","o":["IV levothyroxine","Liothyronine (T3)","Parenteral steroids","Hypotonic IV fluids"],"a":3,"e":"The given scenario is suggestive of myxedema coma. Hypotonic IV fluids are avoided in the course of treatment of this condition. However, hypertonic saline is used.\n\nHypotonic fluids may exacerbate water retention and dilutional hyponatremia secondary to impaired renal perfusion and inappropriate vasopressin.\n\nTreatment of myxedema coma includes:\n\n• Levothyroxine (T4) can be administered as a single IV bolus of 500 μg. Although further doses are strictly not required, it is continued at 50-100 μg/day.\n\n• An alternative mode of treatment is the IV administration of liothyronine (T3) or via NG tubes with doses ranging from 10-25 μg every 8-12 hours in case T4 to T3 conversion in myxedema is impaired.\n\n• Parenteral hydrocortisone should be administered to treat a condition of impaired adrenal reserve in profound hypothyroidism.\n\n• Hypertonic saline or IV glucose may be recommended for treatment.\n\n• External warming is indicated only if the temperature is less than 30oC.","t":"Thyroid Disorders   Management","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Multiple endocrine neoplasia (MEN) type 1 is also called as ___________ .","o":["Wermer's syndrome","Sipple syndrome","Weber's syndrome","Werner syndrome"],"a":0,"e":"MEN1 is also referred to as Wermer's syndrome.","t":"Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following conditions is not observed in MEN1 syndrome?","o":["Posterior pituitary tumors","Foregut carcinoid","Parathyroid adenoma","Angiofibroma"],"a":0,"e":"Anterior pituitary tumours are associated with MEN1 syndrome, not posterior pituitary tumors.","t":"Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A 35-year-old man presented with abdominal pain, nausea, and diarrhea. He had a history of hyperparathyroidism and a transsphenoidal prolactinoma resection. Which of the following is the most common functional enteropancreatic tumor seen in this condition?","o":["Glucagonoma","Insulinoma","Gastrinoma","VIPoma"],"a":2,"e":"The given scenario is suggestive of MEN1 syndrome and gastrinoma is the most common functional enteropancreatic tumor in MEN1 / Wermer's syndrome.\n\nEnteropancreatic tumors in MEN1 syndrome are:\n\n• Non-functioning pancreatic tumors (most common overall)\n\n• Functioning pancreatic tumors :\n\n• Gastrinoma - 50% (most common among functioning pancreatic tumours)\n\n• Insulinoma - 30%\n\n• Glucagonoma\n\n• VIPoma (least common)","t":"Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is the most common site of gastrinoma in MEN 1 syndrome?","o":["Jejunum","Ileum","Duodenum","Stomach"],"a":2,"e":"Most common site of gastrinoma in MEN 1 syndrome is the first and second part of duodenum.\n\nMEN 1 syndrome is characterized by the triad of tumors involving the parathyroids, pancreatic islets, and anterior pituitary.\n\nGastrinoma is the most common pancreatic islet tumor in MEN 1 syndrome and if it is associated with marked gastric acid production and recurrent peptic ulcers, the condition is called Zollinger Ellison syndrome.","t":"Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A 30-year-old man presents to the OPD with complaints of recurrent episodes of weakness, headache, sweating, and fainting after fasting. He tells that the symptoms improve after the consumption of food. There is a history of MEN1 syndrome running in the family. Serum C peptide levels are found to be elevated. What is the definitive treatment of choice in this patient?","o":["Frequent consumption carbohydrate-rich meals","Octreotide","Diazoxide","Surgery"],"a":3,"e":"The given clinical scenario along with elevated serum C peptide levels points towards a diagnosis of insulinoma in a MEN1 patient. The definitive treatment of choice in this patient is surgery.\n\nInsulinoma is the second most common functioning enteropancreatic tumor in MEN1 syndrome. Investigation of choice is 72-hour fasting glucose and serum insulin levels. These parameters are measured when the patient develops hypoglycemic symptoms during a supervised 72-hour fasting period.\n\nThe treatment of choice is surgery, ranging from enucleation of a single tumor to pancreatectomy.\n\nNon-surgical management of insulinoma includes frequent consumption of carbohydrate-rich meals, octreotide or diazoxide, which are not always successful.\n\nSelpercatinib, a kinase inhibitor was recently approved by the FDA. It is used to treat tumours with a mutation in the RET gene. These are non-small cell lung cancer, medullary thyroid cancer, and other types of thyroid cancers in patients with a mutation in the RET gene.","t":"Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is the most frequently observed cutaneous manifestation of MEN1 syndrome?","o":["Lipoma","Angiofibroma","Collagenoma","Neurofibroma"],"a":1,"e":"The most frequently observed cutaneous manifestation of MEN1 syndrome is angiofibroma.\n\nCutaneous tumors in MEN1 Syndrome are:\n\n• Angiofibroma – 85%\n\n• Collagenoma – 70%\n\n• Lipoma - 33%","t":"Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A 22-year-old man who was diagnosed with MEN1 syndrome presents to your OPD for his annual checkup. Which of the following investigations will you not do as part of annual screening for this patient?","o":["Fasting glucose levels","Serum calcium levels","Secretin study","Serum gastrin levels"],"a":2,"e":"Secretin study is not done as an annual screening test in patients with MEN1 syndrome. It is a provocative test for the diagnosis of gastrinoma.\n\nAnnual biochemical screening tests to be done in patients with MEN1 syndrome are:\n\nTumor\nAge to begin screening\nAnnual screening test\n\nParathyroid\n8\nCalcium, PTH\n\nGastrinoma\n20\nGastrin (+/- gastric pH)\n\nInsulinoma\n5\nFasting glucose, insulin\n\nOther pancreatic NETs\n<10\nChromogranin A; pancreatic polypeptide, glucagon,\nvasoactive intestinal peptide\n\nAnterior pituitary\n5\nProlactin, IGF-I\n\nOther imaging tests like MRI and CT might have to be done for screening of conditions like anterior pituitary tumors or adrenal tumors.","t":"Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"What is the most common component of Sipple’s syndrome?","o":["Medullary thyroid cancer","Hyperparathyroidism","Pituitary adenoma","Gastrinoma"],"a":0,"e":"The most common component of MEN2A syndrome, which is also called Sipple’s syndrome is medullary thyroid cancer.","t":"Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following features would you not expect to see in a patient with MEN2 syndrome?","o":["Pituitary adenoma","Parathyroid adenoma","Medullary thyroid cancer","Pheochromocytoma"],"a":0,"e":"Pituitary adenoma is not a characteristic feature of the symptom complex of MEN2 and its variants, but it is observed in MEN1 and MEN4 syndrome.","t":"Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following features would you not expect to see in a patient with MEN2B syndrome?","o":["Mucosal neuromas","Marfanoid habitus","Medullated corneal nerve fibers","Meningioma"],"a":3,"e":"Meningioma is not commonly seen in MEN2B Syndrome. MEN1 syndrome is associated with meningiomas.","t":"Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"MEN4 syndrome is caused by a mutation in which of the following genes?","o":["PPKAR1A","RET proto-oncogene","PRKAR1B","CDKN1B"],"a":3,"e":"MEN4 is caused by genetic mutations in CDKN1B (cyclin-dependent kinase inhibitor), located on chromosome 12.\n\nMEN1 is caused due to MEN1 gene mutation on chromosome 11.\n\nMEN2 is associated with RET proto-oncogene mutation on chromosome 10.","t":"Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following malignancies would you not expect to see in a patient with MEN4 syndrome?","o":["Pituitary adenoma","Parathyroid adenoma","Medullary thyroid carcinoma","Renal tumors"],"a":2,"e":"Medullary thyroid carcinoma is not associated with MEN4 syndrome, but it is associated with MEN2 syndrome.","t":"Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A patient presented with abdominal pain and swelling in his scrotum and was diagnosed to have testicular carcinoma. There is a history of MEN syndrome running in his family. Which of the following conditions is he likely to have?","o":["MEN4","MEN2A","MEN1","MEN2B"],"a":0,"e":"This patient is likely to have MEN4 syndrome as reproductive organ tumors are a characteristic feature of this condition.","t":"Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"The clinical triad of pheochromocytoma includes all of the following symptoms except:","o":["Headache","Hypertension","Profuse sweating","Palpitation"],"a":1,"e":"The clinical triad of pheochromocytoma includes the following symptoms:\n\n• Headache\n\n• Profuse sweating\n\n• Palpitation (due to tachycardia)\n\nHypertension is the most predominant sign of pheochromocytoma. Though intermittent hypertension is a classical feature, sustained hypertension is also common.","t":"Pheochromocytoma","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following would you not see in a patient with pheochromocytoma?","o":["Pallor","Postural hypotension","High hematocrit","Hypocalcemia"],"a":3,"e":"Hypercalcemia is seen in patients with pheochromocytoma, not hypocalcemia. This is due to the production of parathyroid hormone-related peptide (PTHrP) by the tumor.","t":"Pheochromocytoma","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"In a patient with pheochromocytoma, which of the following clinical features is least likely to be present?","o":["Weight loss","Pallor","Abdominal pain","Oliguria"],"a":3,"e":"Polyuria, not oliguria is seen in patients with pheochromocytoma.\n\nExcess catecholamine levels in this condition can cause polyuria and polydipsia due to hyperglycemia.","t":"Pheochromocytoma","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is an associated finding that can be seen in patients with pheochromocytoma?","o":["Erythema multiforme","Café au lait spots","Vitiligo","Ash leaf macules"],"a":1,"e":"Café au lait spots are seen in neurofibromatosis 1, which is a pheochromocytoma-associated syndrome.\n\nPheochromocytoma-associated syndromes include the following:\n\n• Neurofibromatosis type 1\n\n• Multiple endocrine neoplasia type 2\n\n• Von Hippel–Lindau syndrome\n\n• Paraganglioma syndrome","t":"Pheochromocytoma","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following parameters can help you diagnose a patient with a malignant pheochromocytoma?","o":["Capsular invasion","Number of mitotic spindles","Lymph node involvement","Cellular atypia"],"a":2,"e":"Malignant pheochromocytoma refers to tumors that have undergone metastasis to lymph nodes or distant sites.\n\nThey are histologically and biochemically the same as benign ones. Hence, typical histological criteria like cellular atypia, mitotic spindles, and capsular invasion are insufficient to confirm malignancy in pheochromocytomas.","t":"Pheochromocytoma","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following catecholamines would be elevated in a patient with extra-adrenal pheochromocytoma?","o":["Norepinephrine","Epinephrine","Metanephrines","Vanillylmandelic acid"],"a":0,"e":"Norepinephrine is exclusively secreted in patients with extra-adrenal pheochromocytoma.\n\nThe PNMT (phenylethanolamine-N-methyl transferase) enzyme converts norepinephrine to epinephrine. This enzyme is absent in extra-adrenal sites. Hence, extra-adrenal pheochromocytomas secrete only norepinephrine and cannot secrete epinephrine.\n\nOn the other hand, adrenal pheochromocytomas can secrete norepinephrine, epinephrine, and dopamine in varying amounts depending on the PNMT levels in the tumor.\n\nExtra-adrenal pheochromocytomas or catecholamine-secreting paragangliomas are most commonly found in the abdomen. They commonly arise from the organ of Zuckerkandl.","t":"Pheochromocytoma","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A 31-year-old woman is rushed to the ED with a severe headache and vomiting. Past history includes a total thyroidectomy for thyroid cancer 3 months ago. Her BP is 200/100 mm Hg and pulse is 110/min. On examination, a marfanoid habitus is noted along with the findings shown. Which of the following is likely to be predominantly elevated?","o":["Norepinephrine","Epinephrine","Dopamine","Vanillylmandelic acid"],"a":1,"e":"This clinical scenario with a history of thyroid cancer (likely medullary carcinoma) and the image showing mucosal neuromas points towards MEN2B syndrome. Her current presentation is suggestive of a pheochromocytoma. She is likely to have predominantly elevated levels of epinephrine and its metabolite, metanephrine.\n\nThis is due to the higher expression of the PNMT enzyme associated with the mutations in MEN2 syndromes (both 2A and 2B). The higher levels of epinephrine result in a more symptomatic presentation.\n\nIn contrast, low PNMT expression is seen in von-Hippel-Lindau (VHL) syndrome. This leads to a predominant elevation of norepinephrine and normetanephrine levels. It is less symptomatic compared to MEN2 syndrome.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b07eb89d38e5471685152c4fa967d31c.PNG"],"t":"Pheochromocytoma","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"In a suspected case of pheochromocytoma, which of the following would you avoid while making a diagnosis?","o":["MRI scan","MIBG scan","Image-guided biopsy","Genetic testing"],"a":2,"e":"Biopsy and FNAC should be avoided in patients with pheochromocytoma, as it is a highly vascular tumor.\n\nCT and MRI have similar sensitivity but T2-weighted MRI with gadolinium contrast is the optimal test for diagnosis. MIBG (metaiodobenzylguanidine) scintigraphy can be used to localize the tumor if both CT and MRI are negative.\n\nGenetic testing may be performed as nearly 40% of the tumors are associated with familial syndromes like VHL, neurofibromatosis, and MEN2.\n\nNote: In cases of malignant pheochromocytoma, biopsy may be indicated to confirm adrenal gland metastasis in other sites.","t":"Pheochromocytoma","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A middle-aged male patient is being evaluated for recurrent episodes of palpitations, headaches, and profuse sweating. He was found to have elevated blood pressure readings on multiple occasions over the past 2 months. Which of the following tests has the highest sensitivity and specificity for the diagnosis of this condition?","o":["MRI","24-hour urinary fractionated metanephrines","MIBG scintigraphy","Fractionated plasma metanephrines"],"a":1,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of pheochromocytoma. The measurement of 24-hour urinary fractionated metanephrines has both the highest specificity and sensitivity in the diagnosis of this condition.\n\n24-hour urinary fractionated metanephrines\nPlasma fractionated metanephrines\n\nSensitivity\n98%\n96-100%\n\nSpecificity\n98%\n85-89%\n\nFalse positives\nVery rare\nCan occur due to drugs like levodopa, TCAs, α-blockers, β-blockers, diuretics\n\nProcedure\nCollection of samples over 24 hours may be cumbersome\nRequires only a single blood sample. May be preferred in children.\n\nUses\nPreferred first-line test as it is highly specific\n\nReserved for patients with a high index of suspicion like:\n\n• Family history\n\n• Genetic syndromes like MEN2\n\n• Prior history of pheochromocytoma\n\nImaging modalities like CT and MRI have a sensitivity of around 98-100% and specificity of 70%.","t":"Pheochromocytoma","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is the best method for detecting and localization of tumors associated with pheochromocytomas?","o":["Abdominal CT","Fluorodopa-PET","MIBG scintigraphy","Abdominal MRI"],"a":1,"e":"Among the given options, the fluorodopa-PET scan is the best method for detecting and localization of tumors associated with pheochromocytoma.\n\nIf biochemical testing for pheochromocytoma is positive, an abdominal MRI must be performed first. If MRI is negative or if multiple tumors are suspected, the tumor can be localized by performing one of the following investigations:\n\n• 68 Ga-DOTATATE PET scan - most sensitive, gold standard\n\n• Fluorodopa and 18F-fluorodeoxyglucose (FDG) PET scans - 97% sensitive\n\n• MIBG scintigraphy - least sensitive (94%)","t":"Pheochromocytoma","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A 45-year-old man presented with recurrent episodes of headache, palpitations, and sweating. His blood pressure at presentation was 180/100 mm Hg. Further testing revealed elevated urinary metanephrines. CT abdomen showed a right adrenal mass. What is the drug of choice for the management of this patient?","o":["Labetalol","Nitroprusside","Phenoxybenzamine","Phentolamine"],"a":2,"e":"This clinical scenario is suggestive of pheochromocytoma. The drug of choice to control blood pressure prior to surgery is oral phenoxybenzamine at 0.5–4 mg/kg of body weight. It is a non-specific α-blocker and is preferred due to its long action (3-4 days).\n\nOther α-blockers like oral prazosin and intravenous phentolamine have a shorter duration of action and can be used to manage paroxysms. IV phentolamine is also used for intra-operative management of BP.\n\nα-blockers are usually given for 7 days. Following this, β-blockers are started, usually 2-3 days preoperatively. β-blockers should never be started first because blockade of vasodilatory peripheral β-receptors with unopposed α-receptor stimulation can lead to a further elevation in blood pressure. (Mnemonic: A before B)","t":"Pheochromocytoma","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following should not be used for the initial management, in a patient with pheochromocytoma?","o":["Prazosin","Atenolol","Nitroprusside","Metyrosine"],"a":1,"e":"Beta-blockers like atenolol should never be used as the initial drug or alone in the management of pheochromocytoma.\n\nThey must be given only after adequate alpha blockade has been established. Blocking beta-receptors may cause unopposed stimulation of alpha-receptors, precipitating a hypertensive crisis.\n\nOption A: Prazosin is an alpha-blocker.\n\nOption C: Nitroprusside can be used in case of hypertensive emergencies.\n\nOption D: Metyrosine inhibits catecholamine synthesis. It can be used when other agents have been ineffective.","t":"Pheochromocytoma","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not a part of Averbuch’s chemotherapy protocol?","o":["Dacarbazine","Vinblastine","Cyclophosphamide","Vincristine"],"a":1,"e":"Vinblastine is not included in Averbuch’s chemotherapy protocol for the treatment of malignant pheochromocytoma.","t":"Pheochromocytoma","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A patient is diagnosed with malignant pheochromocytoma. Which of the following is used to treat this condition?","o":["Strontium","Cobalt-60","Phosphorus","MIBG"],"a":3,"e":"131I-MIBG (metaiodobenzylguanidine) is used for the treatment of malignant pheochromocytoma.\n\nMalignant pheochromocytomas occur in 10% of cases and are histologically and biochemically the same as benign ones. The diagnosis of malignant pheochromocytoma is based on documentation of metastatic disease. The treatment of choice is nuclear medicine therapy with any of the following:\n\n• 131I-MIBG at 200-mCi doses at monthly intervals (3-6 cycles)\n\n• DOTATOC (a somatostatin receptor analog) labeled with radioactive lutetium or yttrium\n\nChemotherapy can also be given according to Averbuch's protocol.\n\nNote: 123I-MIBG and Gallium-DOTATATE are used for imaging.","t":"Pheochromocytoma","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"What is the most common cause of Cushing’s syndrome?","o":["Pituitary adenoma","Adrenal adenoma","Ectopic ACTH secretion","Iatrogenic"],"a":3,"e":"The most common cause of Cushing’s syndrome is iatrogenic due to administration of corticosteroids.","t":"Cushing'S Syndrome","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following causes ACTH-dependent Cushing's syndrome?","o":["Cushing’s disease","Adrenal adenoma","Adrenocortical carcinoma","McCune-Albright Syndrome"],"a":0,"e":"Cushing’s disease causes ACTH-dependent Cushing's syndrome. It occurs due to an ACTH-secreting pituitary adenoma.","t":"Cushing'S Syndrome","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements is false regarding Cushing's syndrome?","o":["It is commonly associated with MEN1 syndrome","Bronchial carcinoid is a possible cause","It is characterized by hypokalemia","It is associated with coronary heart disease"],"a":0,"e":"Cushing’s syndrome is rarely associated with MEN1 syndrome.\n\nBronchial carcinoids are a source of ectopic ACTH secretion resulting in ACTH-dependent Cushing's syndrome.\n\nClinical features of Cushing’s syndrome are attributed to the glucocorticoid, mineralocorticoid, and sex steroid actions of cortisol. They include:\n\nSystem\nClinical features\n\nBody fat\n\n• Central or truncal obesity (most common)\n\n• Buffalo hump (fat deposition behind the neck)\n\n• Moon facies\n\nSkin\n\n• Purple striae (over abdomen & flanks)\n\n• Acne and Hirsutism\n\n• Easy bruising\n\n• Facial plethora\n\n• Hyperpigmentation\n\nMusculoskeletal\n\n• Proximal myopathy\n\n• Osteoporosis\n\nMetabolic\n\n• Poorly controlled hyperglycemia\n\n• Dyslipidemia\n\nReproductive\n\n• Oligomenorrhea\n\n• Amenorrhea\n\nRenal\n\n• Hypokalemic alkalosis\n\nCVS\n\n• Atherosclerosis\n\n• Diastolic Hypertension\n\n• Edema\n\nHematologic\n\n• Hypercoagulable state\n\n• Lymphopenia\n\n• Neutrophilia\n\n• Eosinopenia\n\nCNS\n\n• Depression\n\n• Psychosis\n\n• Anxiety","t":"Cushing'S Syndrome","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A patient with Cushing's syndrome is least likely to present with:","o":["Truncal obesity","Osteoporosis","Distal myopathy","Amenorrhea"],"a":2,"e":"Proximal myopathy is commonly seen in Cushing’s syndrome, not distal myopathy.\n\nClinical features of Cushing’s syndrome are attributed to the glucocorticoid, mineralocorticoid, and sex steroid actions of cortisol. They include:\n\nSystem\nClinical features\n\nBody fat\n\n• Central or truncal obesity (most common)\n\n• Buffalo hump (fat deposition behind the neck)\n\n• Moon facies\n\nSkin\n\n• Purple striae (over abdomen & flanks)\n\n• Acne and Hirsutism\n\n• Easy bruising\n\n• Facial plethora\n\n• Hyperpigmentation\n\nMusculoskeletal\n\n• Proximal myopathy\n\n• Osteoporosis\n\nMetabolic\n\n• Poorly controlled hyperglycemia\n\n• Dyslipidemia\n\nReproductive\n\n• Oligomenorrhea\n\n• Amenorrhea\n\nRenal\n\n• Hypokalemic alkalosis\n\nCVS\n\n• Atherosclerosis\n\n• Diastolic Hypertension\n\n• Edema\n\nHematologic\n\n• Hypercoagulable state\n\n• Lymphopenia\n\n• Neutrophilia\n\n• Eosinopenia\n\nCNS\n\n• Depression\n\n• Psychosis\n\n• Anxiety","t":"Cushing'S Syndrome","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following would not be seen in a patient with Cushing’s disease?","o":["Skin hyperpigmentation","hypocoagulable state","Frank psychosis","Diastolic hypertension"],"a":1,"e":"Cushing’s disease, and all other causes of Cushing's syndrome, can result in a hypercoagulable state. This is due to an increase in procoagulant factors and inhibition of fibrinolysis.\n\nCushing's disease is a form of Cushing's syndrome caused by an ACTH-secreting pituitary tumor. The high ACTH levels stimulate melanocytes and cause skin hyperpigmentation. This is especially seen over friction-prone areas like knuckles and elbows.\n\nThe majority of patients with Cushing's syndrome have psychiatric symptoms such as:\n\n• Anxiety,\n\n• Depression, and\n\n• Frank psychosis\n\nMineralocorticoid action of cortisol may lead to diastolic hypertension, hypokalemia, and edema.","t":"Cushing'S Syndrome","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A 28-year-old woman visits a gynecologist with complaints of irregular and infrequent menstrual cycles. She is also upset that she has gained 12 kg over the past few months. Her face appears as shown in the image below. Her blood pressure at this visit is 144/98 mm Hg and she has multiple purple striae over her abdomen. Which of the following investigations should you advise this patient?","o":["Cosyntropin stimulation test","Midnight serum cortisol measurement","Serum testosterone levels and abdominal USG","Serum LH:FSH ratio"],"a":1,"e":"The given clinical scenario along with the image showing moon facies with acne and hirsutism are suggestive of Cushing’s syndrome. It can be diagnosed with the help of a single midnight serum cortisol measurement.\n\nOption A: Cosyntropin (ACTH) stimulation test is used for the diagnosis of Addison's disease.\n\nOptions C and D: Serum testosterone, abdominal USG, and LH: FSH ratio, are used to confirm a diagnosis of the polycystic ovarian syndrome (PCOS). Though PCOS can present with similar features, it is not typically associated with moon facies, purple striae, or hypertension.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f0796bae801c4e2c8563b86a3fe33e25.JPEG"],"t":"Cushing'S Syndrome","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"You can advise any of the following tests for screening of a patient suspected to be having Cushing's syndrome except:","o":["Midnight plasma cortisol measurement","Determination of plasma ACTH levels","24 hour urinary free cortisol measurement","Overnight dexamethasone suppression test"],"a":1,"e":"Determination of plasma ACTH levels is not advised as a screening test for a patient with Cushing's syndrome.\n\nPlasma ACTH determination is done to distinguish between ACTH-dependent and independent causes. It is done after the screening or confirmation of Cushing's syndrome.","t":"Cushing'S Syndrome","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"In patients with which of the following conditions would you expect to see the highest basal ACTH level?","o":["ACTH-secreting pituitary adenoma","Ectopic ACTH-secreting tumors.","Bilateral adrenal hyperplasia","Iatrogenic hypercortisolism"],"a":1,"e":"Basal plasma ACTH levels are highest in ectopic ACTH-secreting tumors.\n\nPlasma ACTH > 15pg/ml is suggestive of ACTH-dependent Cushing's syndrome due to pituitary or ectopic sources. Basal ACTH in pituitary adenoma can be normal or elevated, whereas in an ectopic source the ACTH production is very high. This occurs because a pituitary adenoma retains some sensitivity to the negative feedback mechanism exerted by cortisol, but ectopic tumors remain unaffected.\n\nPlasma ACTH < 5pg/ml is suggestive of ACTH-independent Cushing’s syndrome that can be caused by an adrenal adenoma or exogenous administration of glucocorticoids.","t":"Cushing'S Syndrome","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A patient with suspected Cushing's syndrome is found to have elevated ACTH levels. What is the investigation of choice?","o":["MRI brain","CRH stimulation test","High-dose dexamethasone suppression test","Inferior petrosal sinus sampling"],"a":0,"e":"An elevated ACTH level is suggestive of ACTH-dependent Cushing's syndrome. The investigation of choice for ACTH-dependent Cushing's syndrome is MRI brain, to look for the presence of a pituitary tumour.\n\nIf the MRI brain shows a mass it is suggestive of a pituitary mass of >6 mm in size. However, if the MRI brain does not show a mass it suggests a pituitary tumor of <6mm in size or an ectopic ACTH source.\n\nNote: High-dose dexamethasone suppression test and CRH stimulation test are employed to differentiate pituitary from ectopic ACTH syndrome. A definitive cause can be established only by imaging techniques like MRI for pituitary tumors, CT scan of the chest and abdomen for ectopic ACTH sites. Hence MRI is considered the investigation of choice.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d1dcf226716b4c109464ecf6ce2b2598x1200x2435.JPEG"],"t":"Cushing'S Syndrome","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A 56-year-old man chronic smoker presented with flecks of blood in his sputum. He is currently on treatment for COPD and hypertension. His BMI is 31 kg/m² and he has central obesity. His RBS is 261 mg/dl. During further evaluation, he is found to have high ACTH levels that remained high even after administration of high-dose dexamethasone. What is the underlying cause of this patient's symptoms?","o":["Exogenous steroid intake","Pituitary adenoma","Adrenal cortical adenoma","Ectopic ACTH-secreting tumor"],"a":3,"e":"Elevated ACTH levels that do not get suppressed by a high-dose dexamethasone test are suggestive of Cushing’s syndrome secondary to an ectopic ACTH-secreting tumor. Considering the patient's smoking history and presentation with hemoptysis, it is likely to be a lung tumor.\n\nDynamic testing for Cushing’s syndrome\n\nHigh dose dexamethasone suppression test\nCRH stimulation test\n\nPituitary adenoma\nPositive (suppressed cortisol)\nPositive (increase in ACTH and cortisol)\n\nEctopic ACTH\nNegative\nNegative\n\nIatrogenic: Suppressed ACTH levels that are not affected by dynamic testing\n\nAdrenal mass or hyperplasia: Suppressed ACTH levels that are not affected by dynamic testing\n\nEctopic sources of ACTH include small cell lung cancers and pulmonary carcinoid tumors. The most effective treatment strategy is the management of the underlying SCLC or carcinoid.","t":"Cushing'S Syndrome","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Evaluation of a patient with Cushing's syndrome reveals high ACTH levels that are responsive to CRH and suppressed by high-dose dexamethasone. What is the likely diagnosis?","o":["Steroid intake","Small cell lung cancer","Pituitary adenoma","Adrenocortical adenoma"],"a":2,"e":"CRH responsiveness is seen only in ACTH-dependent hypercortisolism which is due to a pituitary cause. This is because the pituitary tumor contains corticotrophic cells that are responsive to CRH.\n\nDynamic testing for Cushing’s syndrome\n\nHigh dose dexamethasone suppression test\nCRH stimulation test\n\nPituitary adenoma\nPositive (suppressed cortisol)\nPositive (increase in ACTH and cortisol)\n\nEctopic ACTH\nNegative\nNegative\n\nIatrogenic: Suppressed ACTH levels that are not affected by dynamic testing\n\nAdrenal mass or hyperplasia: Suppressed ACTH levels that are not affected by dynamic testing","t":"Cushing'S Syndrome","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A 50-year-old woman was diagnosed with ACTH-dependent Cushing's syndrome. As MRI of the pituitary was normal and further testing was unequivocal, she was scheduled for inferior petrosal sinus sampling. It revealed a baseline petrosal:peripheral ACTH ratio &gt; 2. What is the treatment of choice in this patient?","o":["Bilateral adrenalectomy","Transsphenoidal pituitary surgery","Removal of ectopic tumor","Stereotactic radiotherapy"],"a":1,"e":"Cushing’s syndrome with petrosal: peripheral ACTH ratio > 2 at baseline confirms the presence of an ACTH-secreting pituitary adenoma. The treatment of choice is transsphenoidal pituitary surgery. Radiotherapy is used in case of recurrence of tumors.\n\nInferior petrosal sinus sampling is performed to differentiate pituitary and ectopic sources of ACTH when initial imaging and testing are inconclusive.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4b31658a4af54ca0bdaa1e366acb74c3x1200x2435.JPEG"],"t":"Cushing'S Syndrome","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following would not be used to treat patients with Cushing’s syndrome?","o":["Etomidate","Metyrapone","Ketoconazole","Mitomycin"],"a":3,"e":"Mitomycin is not used in the treatment of Cushing’s syndrome. The drugs used in its management include:\n\n• Metyrapone\n\n• Ketoconazole\n\n• Aminoglutethimide\n\n• Trilostane\n\n• Cyproheptadine\n\n• Mitotane – Mainly in adrenal carcinoma\n\n• Etomidate – Severe cortisol excess","t":"Cushing'S Syndrome","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is the best adrenolytic agent that can be used in the management of patients with adrenocortical carcinoma?","o":["Mitotane","Metyrapone","Ketoconazole","Trilostane"],"a":0,"e":"Mitotane is used in the management of adrenocortical carcinoma due to its adrenolytic properties.\n\nMitotane is an insecticide derivative. It is effective in the treatment of benign Cushing's also, but is rarely used due to its side effects.","t":"Cushing'S Syndrome","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following complications is associated with bilateral adrenalectomy?","o":["Sheehan’s syndrome","Addison's disease","Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome","Nelson's syndrome"],"a":3,"e":"Nelson’s syndrome is a rare complication of total bilateral adrenalectomy (TBA). TBA is a procedure used to control hypercortisolism in Cushing's disease (due to ACTH-secreting pituitary adenoma) when resection of the primary tumor is not possible.\n\nThe loss of feedback inhibition of the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis following TBA increases CRH. This leads to stimulation of ACTH-secreting corticotroph cells, causing enlargement of the original pituitary tumor or the formation of a new one.\n\nNelson's syndrome is associated with a clinical triad of:\n\n• Hyperpigmentation\n\n• Excessive ACTH secretion\n\n• Corticotroph adenoma\n\nScreening is done with fasting plasma ACTH levels and MRI brain is performed at regular intervals. Prophylactic pituitary irradiation may be recommended to prevent its incidence. The primary treatment is transsphenoidal surgery. Patients will also require life-long glucocorticoid replacement, as they do not produce cortisol.\n\nThe use of steroidogenic inhibitors in the treatment of Cushing's disease has decreased the need for bilateral adrenalectomy these days.\n\nOption A: Sheehan’s syndrome refers to postpartum hypopituitarism that is caused by ischemic necrosis of the anterior pituitary as a result of severe postpartum hemorrhage.\n\nOption B: Addison's disease occurs due to autoimmune destruction of adrenal glands.\n\nOption C: Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome refers to adrenal apoplexy that is caused by fulminant meningococcemia.","t":"Cushing'S Syndrome","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not a cause of pseudo-Cushing's syndrome?","o":["Obesity","Alcoholism","Depression","Malignancy"],"a":3,"e":"Malignancy does not cause pseudo-Cushing's syndrome. It may cause ectopic ACTH production, leading to true Cushing’s syndrome.\n\nPseudo-Cushing’s syndrome refers to functional hypercortisolism caused by chronic activation of the HPA axis. It presents with clinical and biochemical features similar to Cushing’s syndrome, making it difficult to differentiate between them. It may occur secondary to the following conditions:\n\n• Severe major depressive disorder\n\n• Polycystic ovarian syndrome\n\n• Insulin-resistant obesity\n\n• Obstructive sleep apnea\n\n• Chronic alcoholism\n\n• End-stage renal disease\n\nIt usually resolves when the underlying condition is treated.","t":"Cushing'S Syndrome","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"What is the most common cause of adrenal insufficiency in India?","o":["Tuberculosis","Exogenous steroid use","Post-partum pituitary insufficiency","Autoimmune adrenal insufficiency"],"a":0,"e":"Tuberculosis is the most common cause of adrenal insufficiency in India and other developing countries.\n\nIdiopathic atrophy due to autoimmune conditions is the most common cause in developed countries.","t":"Adrenal Insufficiency And Hyperaldosteronism","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"In a patient with reduced cortisol and elevated ACTH, which of the following would not be part of your differential diagnoses?","o":["Kearns-Sayre syndrome","Autoimmune polyglandular syndrome type 2","Triple-A syndrome","MEN 4 syndrome"],"a":3,"e":"Elevated ACTH and reduced cortisol levels are seen in primary adrenal insufficiency. It is not a feature of MEN 4 syndrome.\n\nThe following syndromes are associated with primary adrenal insufficiency:\n\n• Autoimmune polyglandular syndrome type 1\n\n• Autoimmune polyglandular syndrome type 2\n\n• Triple-A syndrome\n\n• Smith-Lemli-Opitz syndrome\n\n• Kearns-Sayre syndrome\n\n• IMAGe syndrome","t":"Adrenal Insufficiency And Hyperaldosteronism","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A 50-year-old woman was diagnosed with Hashimoto's thyroiditis and was started on levothyroxine 2 weeks ago. She now presents to your clinic with abdominal pain and dizziness. The left and right images below show her current appearance and her appearance last month, respectively. Which of the following is not a feature of this condition?","o":["Hyperglycemia","Hyponatremia","Hyperkalemia","Metabolic acidosis"],"a":0,"e":"The given image showing skin hyperpigmentation in a patient with severe dizziness and a history of autoimmune thyroiditis points to a diagnosis of Addison’s disease. Hyperglycemia is not a feature of this condition. Hypocortisolism leads to hypoglycemia in these patients.\n\nAddison’s disease is primary adrenal insufficiency characterized by the disruption of glucocorticoid, mineralocorticoid, and androgen secretion. Primary adrenal insufficiency is most commonly caused by autoimmune adrenalitis and coincident autoimmune disease occurring in these patients most frequently includes thyroid autoimmune disease, vitiligo, and premature ovarian failure.\n\nClinical features of Addison’s disease:\n\nHormone\n\nClinical features\n\nHypoaldosteronism\n\nAbdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, salt craving\n\nDizziness, postural hypotension\n\nAKI due to volume depletion\n\nHyponatremia\n\nHyperkalemia, metabolic acidosis\n\nHypocortisolism\nAnorexia, weight loss, fatigue, myalgia, arthralgia\n\nFever (increased IL-1)\n\nDepression\n\nPostural hypotension\n\nHypoglycemia\n\nHyponatremia (loss of feedback inhibition of ADH)\n\nSlightly increased TSH (loss of feedback inhibition)\n\nHypoandrogenism\nLoss of libido\n\nLoss of pubic and axillary hair\n\nElevated ACTH\nHyperpigmentation\n\nLevothyroxine can precipitate adrenal insufficiency or crisis in undiagnosed patients. In patients with concurrent adrenal insufficiency and hypothyroidism, a course of steroids must be given before giving thyroid replacement.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/57617c19d08a4e3aacd60a698f3634ce.PNG"],"t":"Adrenal Insufficiency And Hyperaldosteronism","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"What is the most common biochemical abnormality seen at presentation in patients with adrenal insufficiency?","o":["Hypernatremia","Hypokalemia","Hyponatremia","Hyperkalemia"],"a":2,"e":"Hyponatremia is the most common biochemical abnormality seen at presentation in patients with adrenal insufficiency. It is observed in 80% of the patients at presentation.\n\nHyponatremia is caused by:\n\n• Mineralocorticoid deficiency\n\n• Diminished inhibition of ADH release by cortisol, resulting in mild SIADH during secondary adrenal insufficiency\n\nHyperkalemia is diagnosed in 40% of the patients during initial diagnosis.","t":"Adrenal Insufficiency And Hyperaldosteronism","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A 27-year-old woman is brought to the casualty with severe abdominal pain, vomiting, and diarrhea for the past 3 days. She has been excessively tired and dizzy for the past 2 weeks. On examination, there are patches of hyperpigmented skin. Blood pressure is 70/40 mm Hg and blood glucose is 50 mg/dl. How will you confirm the diagnosis?","o":["Serum Na+, K+, renin","Serum Na+, K+, saline suppression test","Determination of plasma ACTH levels","ACTH stimulation test"],"a":3,"e":"This clinical scenario is suggestive of adrenal insufficiency. The confirmatory test for diagnosis is the ACTH stimulation test or short cosyntropin stimulation test.\n\nHyponatremia and hyperkalemia are seen. Plasma levels of ACTH, renin and aldosterone are used to differentiate between primary and secondary adrenal insufficiency.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f1d16caf468c4eb88705a1ae91299508x1200x2468.JPEG"],"t":"Adrenal Insufficiency And Hyperaldosteronism","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A 20-year-old woman is being evaluated for adrenal insufficiency. You decide to check her serum levels of ACTH, renin, and aldosterone. Which of the following laboratory findings would prompt you to subsequently order a pituitary MRI?","o":["High renin and low aldosterone levels","High ACTH levels","Low renin and high aldosterone levels","Normal renin and aldosterone levels"],"a":3,"e":"Pituitary MRI is indicated in secondary adrenal insufficiency which presents with the following parameters:\n\n• Low-normal ACTH level\n\n• Normal renin level\n\n• Normal aldosterone level\n\nThe following findings are seen in primary adrenal insufficiency:\n\n• High ACTH levels\n\n• High renin\n\n• Low aldosterone levels\n\nPituitary MRIs are not required in primary adrenal insufficiency. The fact that a pituitary MRI is done itself gives a hint towards secondary adrenal insufficiency.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fb5cb8423cd7445f90ae0986a246a898x1200x2468.JPEG"],"t":"Adrenal Insufficiency And Hyperaldosteronism","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A 33-year-old man collapses during a family vacation. At the hospital, blood pressure is found to be 86/52 mm Hg, and random blood sugar is 49 mg/dl. His wife says that he has been on treatment for Addison's disease for two years but had forgotten to pack his medication for the trip. What is the drug of choice for this patient?","o":["Norepinephrine","Hydrocortisone","Dexamethasone","Fludrocortisone"],"a":1,"e":"This clinical scenario is suggestive of an adrenal crisis triggered by missed medication. In this patient who is a known case of adrenal insufficiency, IV hydrocortisone is the drug of choice.\n\nHowever, in patients with newly diagnosed adrenal insufficiency, dexamethasone is used for the treatment of adrenal crisis. This is because dexamethasone is not measured in cortisol assays and would not interfere with the diagnostic tests, whereas hydrocortisone interferes with cortisol assays.\n\nAcute adrenal insufficiency requires the immediate initiation of the following therapeutic strategies:\n\n• Rehydration - saline infusion at an initial rate of 1 L/h with continuous cardiac monitoring.\n\n• Glucocorticoid replacement - bolus injection of 100 mg hydrocortisone, followed by the maintenance dose of 100–200 mg over 24h.\n\n• Mineralocorticoid replacement - initiated once the daily hydrocortisone dose has been reduced to <50 mg as higher doses of hydrocortisone have sufficient mineralocorticoid action.","t":"Adrenal Insufficiency And Hyperaldosteronism","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A patient who presented with an adrenal crisis is undergoing treatment with dexamethasone. Which of the following abnormalities will not improve with this treatment?","o":["Hyponatremia","Postural hypotension","Hypoglycemia","Hyperkalemia"],"a":3,"e":"Hyperkalemia will not improve following administration of dexamethasone. Dexamethasone is a long-acting steroid with glucocorticoid action. It has no effect on hyperkalemia, which is mediated by mineralocorticoids.\n\nHyponatremia is chiefly due to mineralocorticoid deficiency, however, hypocortisolism causes hyponatremia due to loss of feedback inhibition of ADH resulting in SIADH (syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone secretion).\n\nHypocortisolism can also cause postural hypotension as cortisol regulates vascular tone by potentiating the effects of vasoconstrictors. Thus on administration dexamethasone hyponatremia and postural hypotension will improve as dexamethasone has glucocorticoid action but hyperkalemia will not improve.\n\nDexamethasone is used in patients with newly diagnosed adrenal insufficiency as it does not interfere with serum cortisol measurement.\n\nHormone\n\nClinical features\n\nHypoaldosteronism\n\nAbdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, salt craving\n\nDizziness, postural hypotension\n\nAKI due to volume depletion\n\nHyponatremia\n\nHyperkalemia, metabolic acidosis\n\nHypocortisolism\nAnorexia, weight loss, fatigue, myalgia, arthralgia\n\nFever (increased IL-1)\n\nDepression\n\nPostural hypotension\n\nHypoglycemia\n\nHyponatremia (loss of feedback inhibition of ADH)\n\nSlightly increased TSH (loss of feedback inhibition)\n\nHypoandrogenism\nLoss of libido\n\nLoss of pubic and axillary hair\n\nElevated ACTH\nHyperpigmentation","t":"Adrenal Insufficiency And Hyperaldosteronism","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A 62-year-old woman who was diagnosed with a non-functioning pituitary adenoma develops features of adrenal insufficiency. Which of the following is not a feature of this condition?","o":["Hyperpigmentation","Postural hypotension","Hypoglycemia","Lassitude"],"a":0,"e":"A non-functioning pituitary adenoma causes hypopituitarism due to compression of the pituitary stalk. This results in secondary adrenal insufficiency. Hyperpigmentation is not a feature.\n\nThis condition is characterized by an alabaster-like paleness due to the lack of ACTH secretion. In primary adrenal insufficiency, hyperpigmentation occurs as a result of elevated ACTH.","t":"Adrenal Insufficiency And Hyperaldosteronism","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is incorrect with respect to Addisonian pigmentation?","o":["Presence of moles and scars","Represented by palmar creases","Does not affect the oral mucosa","Decreased fibrosis"],"a":2,"e":"Addison's disease causes patchy hyperpigmentation of the oral mucosa.\n\nHyperpigmentation is also present in areas of increased friction including:\n\n• Palmar creases\n\n• Dorsal aspect of the foot\n\n• Nipples and the axillary region","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/685ed0f4d26e4ea29bbcbe21709983bfx720x1443.PNG"],"t":"Adrenal Insufficiency And Hyperaldosteronism","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"What is the most common cause of mineralocorticoid excess?","o":["Bilateral micronodular adrenal hyperplasia","Unilateral adrenal adenoma","Congenital adrenal hyperplasia","Liddle’s syndrome"],"a":0,"e":"Bilateral adrenal hyperplasia is the most common cause of mineralocorticoid excess. It causes primary hyperaldosteronism. Other causes of primary hyperaldosteronism are:\n\n• Conn’s syndrome – Unilateral adrenal adenoma\n\n• Glucocorticoid-remediable hyperaldosteronism (< 1%)\n\nOther causes of mineralocorticoid excess are as follows:\n\n• Congenital adrenal hyperplasia (CAH)\n\n• Syndrome of apparent mineralocorticoid excess (SAME)\n\n• Adrenocortical carcinoma\n\n• Progesterone-induced hypertension\n\n• Cushing’s syndrome\n\nLiddle’s syndrome is due to a mutation of ENaC subunits. This results in reduced degradation of ENaC channels, causing prolonged mineralocorticoid action.","t":"Adrenal Insufficiency And Hyperaldosteronism","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following clinical features will you not expect to see in a patient with Conn's syndrome?","o":["Hypertension","Hypokalemia","Edema","Muscle weakness"],"a":2,"e":"In Conn’s syndrome, even though there is volume expansion, an underlying salt-escape mechanism leads to the absence of pedal edema.\n\nThe volume expansion due to hypernatremia leads to right heart dilatation that stimulates the release of atrial natriuretic peptide (ANP). This leads to compensatory diuresis and lack of edema despite sodium retention.\n\nFeatures of primary hyperaldosteronism:\n\n• Hypokalemia\n\n• Hypertension\n\n• Metabolic alkalosis\n\n• Polyuria (due to ANP)\n\n• Polydipsia (consequence of polyuria)\n\n• Muscle weakness","t":"Adrenal Insufficiency And Hyperaldosteronism","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A 39-year-old man is referred for evaluation of severe hypertension that has not improved despite being on 3 anti-hypertensive drugs. The presence of decreased plasma renin activity in this patient would point to which of the following diagnoses?","o":["Conn syndrome","Bartter syndrome","Chronic kidney disease","Bilateral renal artery hypertension"],"a":0,"e":"Conn syndrome is a cause of primary hyperaldosteronism that presents with hypertension and low levels of renin.\n\nElevated aldosterone levels cause negative feedback in the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system (RAAS) and results in decreased renin production.","t":"Adrenal Insufficiency And Hyperaldosteronism","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is the best initial investigation in a patient with hypokalemic hypertension?","o":["ACTH stimulation test","CT abdomen","Adrenal vein sampling","Aldosterone-renin ratio"],"a":3,"e":"Hypokalemic hypertension is suggestive of hyperaldosteronism. The screening test of choice is a measurement of plasma renin and aldosterone levels to calculate the aldosterone-renin ratio (ARR).\n\nARR must be estimated in patients with hypertension and any one of the following features:\n\n• Drug resistance\n\n• Hypokalemia\n\n• Adrenal mass\n\n• Onset before 40 years of age\n\nPrior to the determination of ARR, hypokalemia correction must be done. If the patient is on spironolactone, it must be stopped 4 weeks prior to testing. Other blood pressure medication may be continued.\n\nARR screen is considered positive when:\n\n• ARR > 750 pmol/L : ng/mL per hour\n\n• Aldosterone > 450 pmol/L\n\nOption A: ACTH stimulation test is used for the diagnosis of Addison’s disease. It presents with hypotension and hyperkalemia.\n\nOptions B and C: If ARR is positive, CT abdomen and adrenal vein sampling are used for confirmation of the diagnosis.","t":"Adrenal Insufficiency And Hyperaldosteronism","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following tests is not performed in patients with an elevated aldosterone-renin ratio?","o":["Saline infusion test","Metyrapone stimulation test","Oral sodium loading test","Fludrocortisone suppression test"],"a":1,"e":"An elevated aldosterone-renin ratio (ARR) is indicative of primary hyperaldosteronism. Metyrapone stimulation test is used in the diagnosis of adrenal insufficiency, not hyperaldosteronism.\n\nA failure of suppression of aldosterone levels below 140 pmol/L indicates autonomous aldosterone production, which is the hallmark of primary hyperaldosteronism. This principle is used in the following tests:\n\n• Saline infusion test - IV administration of 2L of physiological saline over 4 hours\n\n• Oral sodium loading test - oral sodium given over 3 days\n\n• Fludrocortisone suppression test - given IV over 4 days\n\nThese tests may be used to confirm the diagnosis in patients with elevated ARR, but are not necessary in cases of overt hypokalemic hypertension, strongly positive ARR, and high aldosterone.","t":"Adrenal Insufficiency And Hyperaldosteronism","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not a clinical feature of diabetes mellitus?","o":["Weight loss","Osmotic diuresis","Weight gain","Hypovolemia"],"a":2,"e":"Weight loss is a clinical feature of diabetes mellitus, not weight gain.\n\nDiabetes mellitus refers to a group of common metabolic disorders that result in hyperglycemia. It causes the following classical triad of symptoms:\n\n• Polyuria - due to osmotic diuresis\n\n• Polydipsia - triggered by the resulting hypovolemia\n\n• Weight loss - due to catabolic state\n\nNote: Obesity is a risk factor, not a symptom of type 2 diabetes mellitus and plays a role in its pathogenesis. Increase in the adipocyte mass leads to an increase in the level of circulating free fatty acids. Free fatty acids impair glucose utilization in skeletal muscles, promote glucose production by the liver, and impair beta-cell function.","t":"Diabetes Mellitus  Types, Clinical Features And Management","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A 4-year-old boy is brought to the OPD by his worried mother as he urinates more than ten times a day and is always drinking water. She also reports that despite eating more than his brother did at that age, he is not gaining any weight. Random blood glucose is 252 mg/dl. Which of the following statements is true about this condition?","o":["Antibodies against zinc transporters may be present","It is non-insulin dependent","It is associated with a strong family history","Insulin-resistance is the hallmark for pathogenesis"],"a":0,"e":"This clinical scenario is suggestive of type 1 diabetes mellitus. It is an autoimmune disease associated with the following immunologic markers:\n\n• Antibodies against zinc transporter (ZnT-8)\n\n• Islet cells antibodies (ICA)\n\n• Anti-glutamic acid decarboxylase antibodies (GAD)\n\n• Insulin autoantibodies (IAA)","t":"Diabetes Mellitus  Types, Clinical Features And Management","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which chromosome contains the major susceptible gene for type 1 diabetes mellitus?","o":["1","4","6","12"],"a":2,"e":"The major susceptibility gene for type 1 diabetes mellitus is located in the HLA region of chromosome 6.\n\nType I diabetes mellitus occurs in genetically susceptible individuals and is usually triggered by infections. The HLA complex is most commonly involved. The common HLA associations include:\n\n• Causative - HLA-DQA1*0501 and DQB*0201\n\n• Protective - HLA-DQA1*0102, DQB1*0602, DRB1*1501\n\nOther genomic associations of type I diabetes mellitus include the CTLA-4 gene and IL-2 receptor.\n\nType 2 diabetes mellitus is associated with mutations in the calpain-10 gene on chromosome 2. It results in insulin resistance.","t":"Diabetes Mellitus  Types, Clinical Features And Management","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following mechanisms is predominantly responsible for an increase in the fasting plasma glucose level of patients with type 2 diabetes mellitus?","o":["Increased hepatic gluconeogenesis","Decreased peripheral uptake of glucose","Decreased glycogenesis in muscles","Decreased hepatic glycogenesis"],"a":0,"e":"In type 2 diabetes mellitus increased fasting blood sugar is predominantly due to increased hepatic gluconeogenesis.\n\nIn the postprandial period, there is a rise in peripheral blood glucose levels. Due to insulin resistance in type 2 diabetes mellitus, there is reduced peripheral uptake of glucose by insulin-sensitive tissues like the liver and skeletal muscles. This results in reduced glucose utilization by skeletal muscles as well as reduced glycogenesis in the liver and skeletal muscles. This results in increased postprandial plasma glucose levels.\nIncreased hepatic glucose output predominantly accounts for increased FPG levels, whereas decreased peripheral glucose utilization results in postprandial hyperglycemia.","t":"Diabetes Mellitus  Types, Clinical Features And Management","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A 22-year-old woman presents with fatigue and weight loss for the past 6 months. She tells you that her father is also a diabetic. Further evaluation reveals the following. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nBody mass index - 21 kg/m2\n\nFasting blood sugar - 252 mg/dl\n\nGlycosylated hemoglobin - 8.2%\n\nUrinalysis for ketones - Negative","o":["Type 2 diabetes mellitus","Maturity-onset diabetes of the young","Type I diabetes mellitus","Latent autoimmune diabetes in adults"],"a":1,"e":"This clinical scenario is suggestive of maturity-onset diabetes of the young (MODY).\n\nMODY is a monogenic form of diabetes mellitus that is transmitted in an autosomal dominant fashion. It affects young adults < 25 years of age. It occurs due to reduced insulin secretion. Etiological factors of type 1 and 2 diabetes such as insulin resistance, obesity, and antibodies are not associated with this condition.\n\nStable hyperglycemia is usually seen, hence complications like diabetic ketoacidosis are uncommon. Macrovascular complications like hyperlipidemia or hypertension are not seen. It is treated with sulfonylureas.\n\nLatent autoimmune diabetes in adults (LADA) is a form of type 1 diabetes that occurs between 30-50 years of age.","t":"Diabetes Mellitus  Types, Clinical Features And Management","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"All of the following are true about maturity-onset diabetes of the young (MODY) except:","o":["It is an autosomal-dominant trait","MODY 4 is the most common form","Diabetic ketoacidosis is uncommon","Insulinopenia is present"],"a":1,"e":"MODY 3 is the most common form of maturity-onset diabetes of the young (MODY), accounting for two-thirds of all the cases.\n\nMODY is a monogenic form of diabetes mellitus that is transmitted in an autosomal dominant fashion. It affects young adults <25 years of age. There are a total of 11 types of MODY based on mutations involves. Some of the affected genes include:\n\n• Types 1,3,5: HNF (Hepatocyte nuclear transcription factor) mutation\n\n• Type 2: Glucokinase mutation\n\n• Type 4: IPF 1 (Insulin Promotor Factor-1) mutation\n\nThese mutations result in impaired glucose-induced insulin secretion resulting in insulinopenia.","t":"Diabetes Mellitus  Types, Clinical Features And Management","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following would cause type 1 diabetes in an adult patient?","o":["Latent diabetes","Maturity-onset diabetes of the young","Brittle diabetes","Latent autoimmune diabetes in adults"],"a":3,"e":"Type 1 diabetes mellitus in an adult is suggestive of latent autoimmune diabetes in adults (LADA).\n\nLADA is common in thin-built adults between 30-50 years and is associated with autoantibodies. As the progression of the disease is less rapid than in children, clinical features may mimic type 2 diabetes. However, patients usually require insulin treatment within 5 years.\n\nOption A: Patients with latent diabetes become diabetic under stressful conditions. However, upon removal of stress, they become non-diabetic.\n\nOption B: MODY or maturity-onset diabetes of the young is a monogenic form of diabetes that occurs < 25 years. There is impaired insulin secretion, but no antibodies.\n\nOption C: Brittle diabetes is seen in children with type 1 DM. It presents with wide fluctuation in blood sugar levels.","t":"Diabetes Mellitus  Types, Clinical Features And Management","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Patients with all of the following conditions can develop diabetes mellitus except:","o":["Hemochromatosis","Prader-Willi syndrome","Fragile X syndrome","Friedrich’s ataxia"],"a":2,"e":"Fragile X syndrome is not associated with diabetes mellitus.\n\nGenetic syndromes associated with diabetes include:\n\n• Prader-Willi syndrome\n\n• Friedreich’s ataxia\n\n• Wolfram’s syndrome\n\n• Down’s syndrome\n\n• Klinefelter’s and Turner’s syndrome\n\n• Huntington’s chorea\n\n• Laurence-Moon-Biedl syndrome\n\n• Myotonic dystrophy\n\n• Porphyria\n\nDiseases of the exocrine pancreas causing diabetes:\n\n• Hemochromatosis\n\n• Pancreatitis\n\n• Neoplasia\n\n• Cystic fibrosis\n\nEndocrinopathies causing diabetes:\n\n• Acromegaly\n\n• Cushing’s syndrome\n\n• Glucagonoma\n\n• Pheochromocytoma\n\n• Hyperthyroidism","t":"Diabetes Mellitus  Types, Clinical Features And Management","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following patients can be diagnosed with diabetes mellitus?","o":["A patient with RBS of 210 mg/dl","A patient with FBS of 125 mg/dl and 2-hour PPBS of 199 mg/dl","A patient with symptoms of diabetes and RBS of 192 mg/dl","A patient with HbA1c ≥ 6.5 %"],"a":3,"e":"The patient with HbA1c ≥ 6.5 % can be diagnosed with diabetes mellitus as this is an independent criterion for diagnosis.","t":"Diabetes Mellitus  Types, Clinical Features And Management","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A 46-year-old man presents to his physician for a routine health evaluation. He is concerned that he might have diabetes as his three brothers have all been diagnosed with diabetes. He does not have any complaints. Which of the following tests can be employed in this patient?\n\n1. Fasting blood glucose\n\n2. Random blood glucose\n\n3. Oral glucose tolerance test\n\n4. Glycosylated hemoglobin level\n\n5. Postprandial blood glucose","o":["1, 2, 4","1, 4","2, 3, 5","4, 5"],"a":1,"e":"HbA1c and fasting blood glucose are used for screening diabetes mellitus in asymptomatic individuals.\n\nHbA1c refers to glycated hemoglobin and it serves as a long-term indicator for blood glucose levels (8 to 12 weeks).\n\n• Normal < 5.7 %\n\n• Abnormal glucose homeostasis: 5.7 to 6.4 %\n\n• Diabetes mellitus: ≥ 6.5%\n\nHbA1c also serves as a prognostic indicator for glycemic control.","t":"Diabetes Mellitus  Types, Clinical Features And Management","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following can be termed impaired glucose tolerance?","o":["Fasting blood glucose levels between 100 and 125 mg/dL","Random blood glucose levels between 140 and 199 mg/dL","Blood glucose levels between 140 and 199 mg/dL following oral glucose tolerance test","Fasting blood glucose levels between 100 and 199 mg/dL"],"a":2,"e":"Impaired glucose tolerance is defined as blood glucose levels between 140-199 mg/dL following an oral glucose tolerance test (OGTT).\n\nImpaired fasting glucose is when the fasting blood glucose is between 100 to 125 mg/dL.","t":"Diabetes Mellitus  Types, Clinical Features And Management","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"You are counselling an asymptomatic middle-aged woman regarding screening for diabetes mellitus. Based on the ADA recommendations, what will you tell her?","o":["All individuals &gt; 35 years must undergo screening every 3 years","All individuals &gt; 50 years must undergo screening every 5 years","Oral glucose tolerance test would be performed","Random blood glucose levels would be checked"],"a":0,"e":"The ADA recommends screening for type 2 diabetes mellitus in all asymptomatic individuals > 35 years every 3 years.\n\nThe tests used for screening are:\n\n• HbA1c levels\n\n• Fasting blood glucose levels\n\nIndividuals can be screened earlier if they are overweight with BMI >25 kg/m2 and have one of the following risk factors:\n\n• Family history of diabetes\n\n• History of cardiovascular diseases\n\n• History of gestational diabetes mellitus or delivery of a baby > 4 kg\n\n• Obesity (BMI ≥25 kg/m2)\n\n• Physical inactivity\n\n• Hypertension (≥140/90 mm Hg)\n\n• HDL cholesterol < 35 mg/dL\n\n• Triglycerides >250 mg/dL\n\n• Polycystic ovarian syndrome or acanthosis nigricans\n\nNote- If an individual has a test result near the margins of the diagnostic threshold, a close follow-up should be done and repeat the test in 3–6 months.","t":"Diabetes Mellitus  Types, Clinical Features And Management","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A middle-aged woman has been diagnosed with type 2 diabetes mellitus. Her HbA1c level is 11.1%. What is the initial drug of choice?","o":["Insulin","Glimepiride","Metformin","Sitagliptin"],"a":0,"e":"Insulin is the best initial drug of choice when HbA1c is > 9% at diagnosis.\n\nInsulin is used in the treatment of type 2 diabetes in the following cases:\n\n• Inadequate glycemic control with 2 or more oral agents\n\n• HbA1c > 9% at diagnosis\n\nIn patients with HbA1c < 9%, metformin is the drug of choice.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/321a1fd668314bf094066c62c1954768x1280x2077.JPEG"],"t":"Diabetes Mellitus  Types, Clinical Features And Management","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A 45-year-old woman was brought to the emergency department by her relatives after she suffered a brief, seizure-like episode. They tell you that she is taking an oral medication for diabetes. On examination, the patient was disoriented, and CBG was 45 mg/dl. Which of the following drugs most likely played a role in the patient’s current presentation?","o":["Metformin","Pioglitazone","Sitagliptin","Repaglinide"],"a":3,"e":"Repaglinide is an insulin secretagogue and hence poses the risk of causing hypoglycemia.\n\nDrugs that can cause hypoglycemia (insulin secretagogues):\n\n• Sulfonylureas\n\n• Meglitinides: Nateglinide, Repaglinide\n\nThe following drugs are not directly responsible for stimulating insulin secretion and hence do not cause hypoglycemia:\n\n• Metformin\n\n• Alpha-glucosidase inhibitor\n\n• Pioglitazone\n\n• DPP-4 inhibitor: Sitagliptin\n\nManagement of diabetes mellitus\n\nIntervention\nAdvantages\nDisadvantages\n\nLifestyle changes\nReduced complications and mortality\nInsufficient for most patients within 1st year\n\nMetformin\nWeight neutral\n\nGI side effects\n\nContraindicated when GFR < 45 ml/min\n\nInsulin\n\nRapidly effective\n\nLowers HbA1c more than other drugs (by 1.5-3%)\n\nWeight gain\n\nHypoglycemia\n\nGLP-1 agonists\n\nWeight loss\n\nReduces cardiovascular complications\n\nGI side effects\n\nPramlintide\n\nWeight loss\n\nLowers postprandial glucose\n\nGI side effects\n\nHypoglycemia when used with insulin\n\nSulfonylureas\nRapidly effective\n\nWeight gain\n\nHypoglycemia\n\nThiazolidinediones\nLowers insulin requirements\n\nWeight gain, edema, CHF, fractures\n\nMeglitinides\nRapidly effective\n\nWeight gain\n\nHypoglycemia\n\nSGLT-2 inhibitors\n\nWeight loss\n\nBetter cardiovascular and renal outcomes\n\nUTI, vulval candidiasis\n\nDehydration\n\nEuglycemic DKA\n\nDPP-4 inhibitors\nWeight neutral\nAngioedema, urticaria\n\nAlpha-glucosidase inhibitors\nWeight neutral\n\nGI flatulence\n\nRise in LFT","t":"Diabetes Mellitus  Types, Clinical Features And Management","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"All of the following drugs can be used in the treatment of patients with type 1 diabetes except:","o":["Acarbose","Pramlintide","Metformin","Insulin"],"a":2,"e":"Metformin can only be used in the treatment of type 2 diabetes mellitus.\n\nDrugs that can be used for treating both type 1 and type 2 diabetes mellitus include the following:\n\n• Acarbose - reduces the breakdown of starch into sugar in the GI tract and lowers the postprandial glucose rise in both types.\n\n• Pramlintide - an injectable drug that enhances amylin secretion leading to reduced glucagon levels\n\n• Insulin","t":"Diabetes Mellitus  Types, Clinical Features And Management","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A 40-year-old male patient with type 2 diabetes mellitus is now diagnosed with hypertension. Which of the following antihypertensive drugs will you not prefer in this patient?","o":["Hydrochlorthiazide","Losartan","Lisinopril","Furosemide"],"a":0,"e":"Hydrochlorthiazide belongs to the thiazide group of diuretics, that can cause dyslipidemia and hyperglycemia. Hence they are not preferred for use in diabetic hypertensives.\n\nThe antihypertensive drug of choice in diabetes mellitus include angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors (ACE-I) or angiotensin II receptor blockers (ARBs).","t":"Diabetes Mellitus  Types, Clinical Features And Management","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A type 2 diabetic patient who was recently started on treatment complains of frequent urination, fever, and burning micturition. Which of the following drugs is most likely responsible for these symptoms?","o":["Dapagliflozin","Metformin","Glipizide","Exenatide"],"a":0,"e":"This clinical scenario is suggestive of a urinary tract infection (UTI). This adverse effect occurs as a result of dapagliflozin use.\n\nThe group of sodium-glucose co-transporter-2 or SGLT-2 inhibitors includes drugs like dapagliflozin and canagliflozin. These drugs prevent glucose reabsorption in the renal tubules and cause loss of sodium and glucose.\n\n• UTI - due to glucosuria\n\n• Dehydration - due to natriuresis and volume loss\n\n• Euglycemic diabetic ketoacidosis - low blood glucose levels due to glucosuria can reduce insulin release\n\nSGLT-2 inhibitors must be temporarily discontinued for 3-4 days before any scheduled surgery to avoid potential risk for diabetic ketoacidosis.\n\nManagement of diabetes mellitus\n\nIntervention\nAdvantages\nDisadvantages\n\nLifestyle changes\nReduced complications and mortality\nInsufficient for most patients within 1st year\n\nMetformin\nWeight neutral\n\nGI side effects\n\nContraindicated when GFR < 45 ml/min\n\nInsulin\n\nRapidly effective\n\nLowers HbA1c more than other drugs (by 1.5-3%)\n\nWeight gain\n\nHypoglycemia\n\nGLP-1 agonists\n\nWeight loss\n\nReduces cardiovascular complications\n\nGI side effects\n\nPramlintide\n\nWeight loss\n\nLowers postprandial glucose\n\nGI side effects\n\nHypoglycemia when used with insulin\n\nSulfonylureas\nRapidly effective\n\nWeight gain\n\nHypoglycemia\n\nThiazolidinediones\nLowers insulin requirements\n\nWeight gain, edema, CHF, fractures\n\nMeglitinides\nRapidly effective\n\nWeight gain\n\nHypoglycemia\n\nSGLT-2 inhibitors\n\nWeight loss\n\nBetter cardiovascular and renal outcomes\n\nUTI, vulval candidiasis\n\nDehydration\n\nEuglycemic DKA\n\nDPP-4 inhibitors\nWeight neutral\nAngioedema, urticaria\n\nAlpha-glucosidase inhibitors\nWeight neutral\n\nGI flatulence\n\nRise in LFT","t":"Diabetes Mellitus  Types, Clinical Features And Management","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A 65-year-old man is hospitalized with pneumonia. He is a known patient of type 2 diabetes. During the hospital stay, his appetite is poor and his oral intake is irregular. The physician decides to administer rapid-acting insulin based on high blood sugar values. Which of the following would he use?","o":["Lente","Detemir","Glargine","Glulisine"],"a":3,"e":"This clinical scenario describes the sliding scale method. Rapid-acting insulins such as glulisine are used.\n\nSliding scale refers to the administration of insulin as a bolus dose based on the blood sugar level before the meal. Using this principle, the insulin dose is titrated every time according to pre-meal glucose levels to determine insulin requirement. Hence, rapid-acting insulin is preferred.\n\nThis method requires a fixed carbohydrate intake at every meal. It is used for the short-term management of hospitalized patients, especially when the frequency or quantity of food intake is very variable.\n\nIt does not deliver insulin in a physiological manner, hence is not a preferred long-term strategy for glycemic control.","t":"Diabetes Mellitus  Types, Clinical Features And Management","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is incorrect about Afrezza?","o":["It is available as a powder for inhalation","It can cause bronchospasm","It does not cause hypoglycemia","It is a rapid-acting form"],"a":2,"e":"Afrezza is an inhalational form of insulin that can cause hypoglycemia.\n\nIt is a rapid-acting form of insulin that is administered at the beginning of each meal. Peak levels are achieved 12-15 minutes later.\n\nAdverse effects include hypoglycemia, cough, throat pain, or irritation. It may cause acute bronchospasm in patients with asthma, COPD, as well as active smokers. Hence, it is contraindicated in these condition.","t":"Diabetes Mellitus  Types, Clinical Features And Management","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A 60-year-old man presents to the casualty with confusion, vomiting, and abdominal pain. His blood sugar levels are 421 mg/dl and urine ketones are 3+. He is started on IV normal saline and insulin infusion. Which of the following should he be monitored for?","o":["Hypernatremia","Hyperkalemia","Hypokalemia","Hyponatremia"],"a":2,"e":"This clinical scenario is suggestive of diabetic ketoacidosis, which is treated with insulin infusion. Insulin causes an intracellular shift of K+ ions resulting in a decreased extracellular K+ ion concentration. This results in hypokalemia.\n\nNon-diabetic clinical applications of insulin include:\n\n• Treatment of hyperkalemia\n\n• To determine the functions of the anterior pituitary\n\n• To examine the completion of truncal vagotomy","t":"Diabetes Mellitus  Types, Clinical Features And Management","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Dawn phenomenon is clinically suspected when a patient shows which of the following findings?","o":["Early morning hyperglycemia with hypoglycemia at 3 am","Early morning hypoglycemia with normal blood glucose levels at 3 am","Early morning hyperglycemia with normal blood glucose levels at 3 am","Early morning hypoglycemia with hyperglycemia at 3 am"],"a":2,"e":"Dawn phenomenon refers to normal blood glucose levels at 3 am followed by early morning hyperglycemia.\n\nThis occurs due to an early morning surge in plasma growth hormone and cortisol levels, which have an insulin-antagonistic action. In normal individuals, this is countered by increased early morning insulin secretion. However, in diabetic patients, insulin secretion is disrupted which results in the unopposed action of growth hormone. Hence, there will be resulting hyperglycemia.","t":"Diabetes Mellitus  Types, Clinical Features And Management","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A patient is said to have developed insulin resistance when the required insulin dosage is which of the following?","o":["&gt; 200 units per day","&gt; 100 units per day","&gt; 150 units per day","&gt; 250 units per day"],"a":0,"e":"When a patient needs > 200 units of insulin per day, they are said to have developed insulin resistance.\n\nSome causes of insulin resistance are as follows:\n\n• Werner syndrome\n\n• Lipodystrophy syndrome\n\n• Ataxia-telangiectasia\n\n• Polycystic ovarian disease\n\nInsulin resistance can present as non-alcoholic fatty liver disease.","t":"Diabetes Mellitus  Types, Clinical Features And Management","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not a feature of diabetic ketoacidosis?","o":["Low serum sodium","High anion gap","Serum pH is &lt; 7.4","Serum bicarbonate &gt; 15 meq/L"],"a":3,"e":"In diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA), serum bicarbonate is usually < 15. Serum HCO3 > 15 meq/L is suggestive of alcoholic ketoacidosis.\n\nDKA is an acute complication of type 1 diabetes mellitus. It is usually precipitated by absolute or relative insulin deficiency caused by increased metabolic demand (infections) or insulin noncompliance.\n\nThe common symptoms include nausea, vomiting, and severe abdominal pain. The common signs include Kussmaul breathing, fruity odor of the breath, dehydration, tachycardia, hypotension, and delirium.\n\nThe diagnosis of DKA is made based on the following parameters:\n\n• Hyperglycemia > 250 mg/dl\n\n• Ketosis or ketones in urine\n\n• Metabolic acidosis with pH < 7.3\n\n• HCO3 < 15-18 mEq/L\n\n• High anion gap due to ketoacids and lactic acidosis\n\nHyponatremia is seen as a result of hyperglycemia (osmotic shift causes dilutional hyponatremia). Normal sodium levels on admission indicate severe dehydration. Despite a total-body potassium deficit, the serum Potassium at presentation may be mildly elevated, secondary to the acidosis and volume depletion.","t":"Diabetes Mellitus  Complications And Their Management","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A 19-year-old woman with a history of type 1 diabetes is brought to the ER with confusion, vomiting, and abdominal pain. Investigations show blood glucose of 520 mg/dl and urine positive for ketone bodies. Which of the following is the drug of choice?","o":["Regular insulin","Lente insulin","Insulin glargine","70/30 insulin"],"a":0,"e":"The drug of choice for controlling hyperglycemia in a patient with diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA) is short-acting insulin, preferably regular insulin.\n\nIntermediate (such as lente insulin and NPH), long-acting insulins, and oral hypoglycemic agents are never used in the initial management of DKA.\n\nThe mainstay of treatment is the administration of normal saline followed by infusion of short-acting insulin. The precipitating event must be identified and treated. Careful monitoring of K+ levels and replacement in the later stages is required, as insulin therapy causes hypokalemia.","t":"Diabetes Mellitus  Complications And Their Management","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is the leading cause of death in children with diabetic ketoacidosis?","o":["Acute respiratory distress syndrome","Cardiac arrhythmia","Cerebral edema","Acute kidney injury"],"a":2,"e":"Cerebral edema is the leading cause of mortality in children with diabetic ketoacidosis.\n\nThe osmolar gradient generated due to the high blood glucose level results in water shift from the intracellular fluid (ICF) to the extracellular fluid (ECF) space and leads to the contraction of cell volume. Correction with insulin and intravenous fluids can result in a rapid reduction in effective osmolarity, reversal of the fluid shift, and result in cerebral edema.\n\nComplications of diabetic ketoacidosis include:\n\n• Cerebral edema – especially in children\n\n• Cardiac arrhythmia - due to electrolyte imbalance\n\n• Acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS)\n\n• Venous thrombosis\n\n• Acute gastric dilation\n\n• Acute kidney injury\n\n• Intestinal necrosis\n\n• Acute pancreatitis\n\n• Mucormycosis","t":"Diabetes Mellitus  Complications And Their Management","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A patient with diabetic ketoacidosis has severe abdominal pain. Which of the following is the recommended test to rule out acute pancreatitis?","o":["Lipase","Elastase","Amylase","Trypsinogen"],"a":0,"e":"In diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA), severe abdominal pain can mimic acute pancreatitis. Serum lipase is the best test to rule out this condition. It must then be confirmed by imaging (USG or CT abdomen).\n\nSerum amylase is an unreliable indicator as DKA is associated with hyperamylasemia of salivary origin.\n\nSerum and urine trypsinogen may be elevated in early pancreatitis but are not used for diagnosis. Fecal elastase is elevated in chronic pancreatitis.","t":"Diabetes Mellitus  Complications And Their Management","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A 15-year old boy presents with persistent vomiting for one day. Laboratory investigations reveal the following. What is the best sequence of management?\n\nRandom blood glucose: 400 mg/dl\n\nSerum sodium: 133 mEq/L\n\nSerum potassium: 3 mEq/L\n\nUrine ketone bodies: 4+","o":["Start IV fluids, administer IV regular insulin, and then correct hypokalemia","Start IV fluids, administer subcutaneous bolus of insulin and correct hypokalemia concurrently","Start IV fluids and correct hypokalemia first, then administer IV regular insulin","Administer IV regular insulin with KCl first , then start IV fluids once the K+ level is corrected"],"a":2,"e":"This clinical scenario is suggestive of diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA). On confirming the diagnosis, IV fluids must be started. If the initial serum potassium < 3.3 meq/L, insulin should be administered only after the correction of hypokalemia.\n\nThis is because treatment with insulin and fluids can worsen hypokalemia by the following mechanisms:\n\n• Insulin causes the shift of K+ ions into cells\n\n• Resolution of the acidosis also promotes K+ entry into cells\n\n• Urinary loss of K+ salts of organic acids","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d57dbb1b28fa4a118652e49c9db3ad8cx1280x2461.JPEG"],"t":"Diabetes Mellitus  Complications And Their Management","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Distinguishing features between hyperglycemic hyperosmolar state and diabetic ketoacidosis include all of the following except:","o":["Ketosis","Anion gap","Hypotension","Arterial pCO2"],"a":2,"e":"Hypotension and tachycardia are common features of both hyperglycemic hyperosmolar state (HHS) and diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA).\n\nIn DKA, insulin deficiency and an increase in counter-regulatory hormones lead to shunting of free fatty acids to form ketone bodies. The ketoacids increase the anion gap and cause low pH. In HHS, there is still some insulin available. Hence, there are no ketones formed, and the patient has normal pH and pCO2.","t":"Diabetes Mellitus  Complications And Their Management","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"An elderly man is brought to the hospital with confusion and drowsiness. His son says that he has been urinating more frequently than normal and eating very little over the past 1 week. Capillary blood glucose is 800 mg/dl. Which of the following would not be seen in this patient?","o":["Serum osmolality &gt; 350 mOsm/L","Normal pH","Kussmaul breathing","Prerenal azotemia"],"a":2,"e":"This clinical scenario is suggestive of hyperglycemic hyperosmolar state (HHS). Kussmaul's breathing is not a feature of HHS.\n\nHHS is commonly seen in older adults suffering from type 2 diabetes mellitus. Patients can present with the following features:\n\n• Polyuria\n\n• Polydipsia\n\n• Lethargy\n\n• Altered sensorium, coma\n\n• Seizures\n\n• Severe dehydration\n\nThe following lab values are seen:\n\n• Blood glucose of 600-1200 mg/dl\n\n• Serum osmolality > 350 mOsm/L\n\n• Prerenal azotemia due to dehydration\n\n• Pseudohyponatremia may be seen due to hyperglycemia\n\n• Normal pH and ketones\n\nThe mainstay of management is fluid replacement, IV regular insulin, along with potassium replacement.\n\nHeparin can be given prophylactically as volume contraction can increase the risk of venous thrombosis.","t":"Diabetes Mellitus  Complications And Their Management","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"What is the most common pattern of dyslipidemia associated with diabetes mellitus?","o":["Increased triglycerides and decreased HDL","Increased LDL and decreased HDL","Increased VLDL and decreased HDL","Increased triglycerides and increased VLDL"],"a":0,"e":"Dyslipidemia commonly occurs as a complication of type 2 diabetes mellitus. The most common pattern is as follows:\n\n• Increased triglyceride levels - due to increased glucose and free fatty acids, decreased lipolysis of VLDL.\n\n• Decreased HDL levels\n\nThough diabetes does not increase LDL levels, there is increased atherogenicity as the existing LDL molecules are more easily glycated and susceptible to oxidation.","t":"Diabetes Mellitus  Complications And Their Management","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Dyslipidemia is more commonly associated with which of the following?","o":["Type 2 diabetes mellitus","Type 1 diabetes mellitus","Maturity onset diabetes of the young","Gestational diabetes mellitus"],"a":0,"e":"Dyslipidemia is more commonly associated with type 2 diabetes mellitus.\n\nIt is less common in type 1 and gestational diabetes. Maturity onset diabetes of the young (MODY) is not associated with dyslipidemia.\n\nNote: Saroglitazar is a more advanced drug that reduces blood sugar and lipid levels in diabetic patients. It acts on PPAR alpha and gamma receptors.","t":"Diabetes Mellitus  Complications And Their Management","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"You have started a 40-year-old man on treatment for type 2 diabetes mellitus and dyslipidemia. Which of the following is not an appropriate target for this patient?","o":["HbA1c &lt; 7 %","Blood pressure &lt; 130 / 80 mmHg","Triglycerides &lt; 150 mg/dL","Postprandial plasma glucose &lt; 200 mg/dL"],"a":3,"e":"According to the ADA guidelines, a treatment goal of postprandial plasma glucose < 180 mg/dL must be followed in adults with diabetes.\n\nIn younger individuals or those with other cardiovascular risk factors, the target blood pressure is < 130/80 mm Hg. Although not a treatment goal, the ADA also recommends triglyceride levels be maintained at < 150 mg/dl.\n\nNote: Saroglitazar is a more advanced drug that reduces blood sugar and lipid levels in diabetic patients. It acts on PPAR alpha and gamma receptors.","t":"Diabetes Mellitus  Complications And Their Management","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A middle-aged patient with type 2 diabetes is diagnosed with essential hypertension. What is the antihypertensive drug of choice?","o":["Losartan","Hydrochlorothiazide","Atenolol","Amlodipine"],"a":0,"e":"The drug of choice in this diabetic patient with hypertension is losartan.\n\nAngiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors (ACE inhibitors) or angiotensin receptor blockers (ARB) are the first-line drugs in diabetic hypertensives. Among ARBs, the drug of choice is telmisartan (acts on PPAR receptor).\n\nThey reduce both macrovascular and microvascular complications and have a nephroprotective effect. However, they are contraindicated in chronic kidney disease as they can cause a further decline in GFR.","t":"Diabetes Mellitus  Complications And Their Management","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is the earliest manifestation of diabetic nephropathy?","o":["Microalbuminuria","Increased glomerular filtration rate","Decreased glomerular filtration rate","Increased urine PCR"],"a":1,"e":"The earliest manifestation of diabetic nephropathy is an increase in the glomerular filtration rate (GFR).\n\nIt occurs as a result of glomerular hyperperfusion and renal hypertrophy. The following sequence of changes are seen in diabetic nephropathy:\n\n• Increased GFR\n\n• Incipient diabetic nephropathy - microalbuminuria (30–300 mg/day albumin)\n\n• Diagnostic marker to detect early diabetic nephropathy\n\n• Indicator of long-term cardiovascular disease\n\n• Overt diabetic nephropathy - macroalbuminuria ( >300 mg/day albumin)\n\n• End-stage renal disease","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/10dc64a62ec7458781b3d41028a48f9ex1280x1772.JPEG"],"t":"Diabetes Mellitus  Complications And Their Management","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A 15-year-old boy has been on treatment for type 1 diabetes mellitus since the age of 10 years. His physician decides to begin screening for diabetic nephropathy. Which of the following is not always a reliable indicator of this condition?","o":["Long duration of diabetes","Albuminuria of 30-300 mg/day","Presence of diabetic retinopathy","Reduced glomerular filtration rate"],"a":1,"e":"Albumin of 30-300 mg/day refers to microalbuminuria. It can be unreliable in the diagnosis of diabetic nephropathy.\n\nIt is not required to diagnose nephropathy as it is very variable. Non-albuminuric diabetic nephropathy may occur in some patients and regression of albuminuria may be seen in type 1 diabetics.\n\nDiabetic nephropathy can be diagnosed if the following are satisfied:\n\n• Reduced GFR (< 60 mL/min/1.73 m2) with or without persistent albuminuria (> 30 mg/day or urine PCR > 30 mg/g)\n\n• Long duration of diabetes or established diabetic retinopathy\n\n• Other etiologies are ruled out\n\nLong duration in type 1 diabetes refers to 5 years after diagnosis. On the other hand, patients with type 2 diabetes are exposed to hyperglycemia and prediabetes for a long time even before diagnosis. Hence, at the time of diagnosis, patients with type 2 diabetes are considered to have had the disorder for a duration sufficiently long to produce kidney disease.\n\nHowever, a clinical diagnosis of diabetic nephropathy can be made in patients with established retinopathy, even if the duration of diabetes is short.","t":"Diabetes Mellitus  Complications And Their Management","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements about diabetic nephropathy is true?","o":["Pancreatic transplantation can improve proteinuria at an early stage","Strict glycemic control is unlikely to prevent microalbuminuria","Dietary protein restriction can benefit diabetic patients with albuminuria","Microalbuminuria is not an indicator of long-term cardiovascular morbidity"],"a":0,"e":"Pancreatic transplantation can help improve proteinuria at an early stage. Successful pancreatic transplantation can have the following effects:\n\n• Prevention of diabetic nephropathy and regression of albuminuria in early stages.\n\n• Stabilization and improvement of diabetic neuropathy and retinopathy\n\n• Increase in HDL with reduction in LDL and triglycerides\n\nOption B: Strict glycemic control can prevent or revert microalbuminuria.\n\nOption C: The ADA does not suggest restrictions of protein intake in diabetic patients with albuminuria because studies have failed to prove its benefits.\n\nOption D: Microalbuminuria is an indicator of long-term cardiovascular morbidity.","t":"Diabetes Mellitus  Complications And Their Management","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A 50-year-old man with type 2 diabetes mellitus is found to have a 24-hour urinary albumin of 250 mg. Which of the following drugs may be prescribed to reduce the progression of this condition?","o":["Hydrochlorothiazide","Enalapril","Amiloride","Eplerenone"],"a":1,"e":"Angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACEI) inhibitors or angiotensin-receptor blockers (ARB) are recommended to reduce the progression of diabetic nephropathy. It has the following effects:\n\n• Reduction of albuminuria\n\n• Reduces the associated decline in GFR\n\nThe mainstay of management of diabetic nephropathy includes control of blood sugar and blood pressure, inhibitors of the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone(RAAS) system, and inhibitors of SGLT2 to halt the progression of the disease.\n\nRAAS inhibitors include ACE inhibitors, ARBs, or direct renin inhibitors.","t":"Diabetes Mellitus  Complications And Their Management","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A pathologist is studying a renal biopsy sample from a patient with suspected diabetic nephropathy. Which of the following biopsy findings is pathognomonic for this condition?","o":["Diffuse glomerulosclerosis","Nodular glomerulosclerosis","Armani-Ebstein reaction","Basement membrane thickening"],"a":1,"e":"The characteristic lesion of diabetic nephropathy is nodular or intercapillary glomerulosclerosis, also called Kimmelstiel-Wilson nodules. It is pathognomonic for this condition.\n\nThe following image shows Kimmelstiel-Wilson nodules.\n\nOther non-specific findings seen on biopsy include:\n\n• Glomerular basement membrane (GBM) thickening - highly sensitive\n\n• Diffuse glomerulosclerosis - most common pattern, due to diffuse mesangial thickening\n\n• Fibrin cap - accumulation of hyaline material within the capillary loop\n\n• Capsular drop - accumulation of hyaline material attached to the Bowman's capsule\n\n• Armani-Ebstein reaction - collection of glycogen clumps within the renal tubules due to tubular injury\n\nRenal biopsy is done to rule out other causes of renal failure. The indications for renal biopsy in diabetic nephropathy are:\n\n• Absence of diabetic retinopathy\n\n• Absence of edema\n\n• Absence of frothing and proteinuria\n\n• Absence of hypertension\n\n• Presence of microscopic hematuria or features of nephritic syndrome","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/53742981e810484a995a69d417426a9fx720x491.JPEG"],"t":"Diabetes Mellitus  Complications And Their Management","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following types of renal tubular acidosis can develop in individuals with diabetes mellitus?","o":["Type I RTA","Type II RTA","Type III RTA","Type IV RTA"],"a":3,"e":"Type IV renal tubular acidosis (RTA) may occur in individuals with diabetes mellitus types 1 and 2.\n\nType IV RTA occurs due to hyporeninemic hypoaldosteronism. Low plasma renin activity is common in diabetic patients due to:\n\n• Impaired conversion of prorenin to renin\n\n• Volume expansion in diabetic nephropathy suppresses renin release\n\nThese individuals have a tendency to develop hyperkalemia and acidemia, which can be exacerbated by the use of ACEI and ARBs. Hence, these drugs should be avoided.","t":"Diabetes Mellitus  Complications And Their Management","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A 45-year-old man has been diagnosed with type 2 diabetes mellitus. When should you refer him to an ophthalmologist?","o":["Within one year of diagnosis","Immediately at the time of diagnosis","Within five years of diagnosis","Within three years of diagnosis"],"a":1,"e":"Patients with type 2 diabetes should undergo a comprehensive ophthalmological examination to screen for diabetic retinopathy at diagnosis, followed by an annual examination of the fundus.\n\nIn patients with type 1 diabetes mellitus, screening for retinopathy is recommended 3 to 5 years after diagnosis. Annual fundal examination is recommended thereafter.","t":"Diabetes Mellitus  Complications And Their Management","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following patients would you screen for diabetic retinopathy?\n\n1. A 10-year-old boy on treatment for type 1 diabetes for the past 2 years\n\n2. A 48-year-old man newly diagnosed with type 2 diabetes\n\n3. A 25-year-old primigravida with gestational diabetes mellitus\n\n4. A 62-year-old man with diabetic nephropathy\n\n5. A 22-year-old woman with prepubertal onset of type 1 diabetes","o":["2, 4, 5","1, 4, 5","2, 3, 5","1, 2, 4"],"a":0,"e":"Screening for diabetic retinopathy is done at 3-5 years following diagnosis in type 1 diabetes mellitus, whereas, patients with type 2 diabetes must be screened at the time of diagnosis.\n\nAs type 2 diabetes mellitus often has a long asymptomatic period of hyperglycemia before clinical diagnosis, these patients are more likely to have complications at the time of diagnosis itself.\n\nRetinopathy develops in nearly 50-80% of patients by 20 years of diagnosis. The presence of diabetic retinopathy often correlates with pathological findings of diabetic nephropathy.","t":"Diabetes Mellitus  Complications And Their Management","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"What is the most common clinical presentation of diabetic neuropathy?","o":["Autonomic neuropathy","Mononeuropathy","Distal polyneuropathy","Polyradiculopathy"],"a":2,"e":"The most common form of diabetic neuropathy is distal symmetric polyneuropathy. It presents with distal sensory loss and pain in a stocking-and-glove distribution and is a risk factor for foot ulceration.\n\nAutonomic neuropathy is a common complication and results in the following features:\n\n• Postural hypotension\n\n• Silent myocardial infarction\n\n• Hypoglycemia unawareness\n\n• Erectile dysfunction\n\n• Overflow incontinence\n\n• Gastroparesis\n\n• Enteropathy with constipation or diarrhea\n\nMononeuropathy of peripheral (median, ulnar, radial) and cranial (oculomotor) nerves is also seen, as well as mononeuritis multiplex.\n\nDiabetic polyradiculopathy is characterized by severe pain along the distribution of one or more nerve roots.","t":"Diabetes Mellitus  Complications And Their Management","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following findings in a diabetic patient is self-limiting?","o":["Macular edema","Peripheral neuropathy","Amyotrophy","Macroalbuminuria"],"a":2,"e":"Amyotrophy refers to weakness of hip extensors and flexors secondary to polyradiculopathy involving the lumbar plexus and femoral nerve. It is associated with severe pain along the distribution of the nerve roots. It is self-limiting and resolves over 6-12 months.\n\nStrict glycemic control can reduce the incidence and prevent the progression of macular edema, peripheral neuropathy, and macroalbuminuria. However, once developed, they cannot be reversed. Intermittent microalbuminuria with normal GFR can be reversed with strict glycemic control.","t":"Diabetes Mellitus  Complications And Their Management","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"You are evaluating a 65-year-old diabetic woman who has been admitted following a seizure. On admission, she was unconscious, and random blood glucose at admission was 37 mg/dl. The hypoglycemia was corrected and she regained consciousness. The woman says that she had felt normal and had no other symptoms prior to the onset of seizures. What is the likely cause of this presentation?","o":["Medication change from metformin to insulin","Insulin resistance","Autonomic neuropathy","Concurrent use of thiazide diuretics"],"a":2,"e":"This clinical scenario is suggestive of silent hypoglycemia in a diabetic patient. The most likely cause is autonomic neuropathy.\n\nHypoglycemia presents with symptoms like palpitation, tremors, and sweating as a result of sympathetic stimulation. These symptoms are protective and provide advance warning before progression to neuroglucopenia.\n\nHowever, in autonomic neuropathy, there is a reduced release of counterregulatory hormones like catecholamines. Hence, the classical symptoms of hypoglycemia do not occur and the patient directly presents with features of neuroglucopenia. This is called hypoglycemia unawareness.\n\nThe use of beta-blockers can also cause hypoglycemia unawareness.","t":"Diabetes Mellitus  Complications And Their Management","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"You are counselling a patient with longstanding, poorly-controlled diabetes mellitus. Advising tight glycemic control in this patient would not improve which of the following?","o":["Neuropathy","Retinopathy","Peripheral vascular disease","Nephropathy"],"a":2,"e":"In patients with longstanding diabetes mellitus, tight glycaemic control is beneficial for microvascular complications. However, it has no effect on macrovascular complications like peripheral vascular disease (PVD), myocardial infarction, and stroke.\n\nGood glycemic control improves microvascular complications through primary and secondary prevention. It reduces the incidence of complications as well as stabilizes and prevents further progression in affected individuals. Intermittent microalbuminuria with normal GFR can even be reversed with tight glycemic control.\n\nComplication\n\n% risk reduction with tight glycemic control\n\nRetinopathy\n\n47 %\n\nMicroalbuminuria\n\n39 %\n\nNephropathy\n\n54%\n\nNeuropathy\n\n60 %","t":"Diabetes Mellitus  Complications And Their Management","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which among the following statements explains Legacy effect and metabolic memory?\n\n1. Intensive glycemic control in early disease\n\n2. Early detection and management of dyslipidemia\n\n3. Good glycemic control in longstanding disease\n\n4. High HbA1c values in longstanding disease\n\n5. Uncontrolled blood glucose levels in early disease","o":["1 and 5","1 and 2","2 and 3","4 and 5"],"a":0,"e":"Legacy effect or metabolic memory can be observed in relation to glycemic control during early disease in patients with diabetes mellitus.\n\nIntensive glycemic control during the early stages of the disease has a positive effect on long-term microvascular and macrovascular complications (statement 1) even if this intensive glycemic control is not maintained long term. Similarly, uncontrolled blood glucose levels in the early stages causes an increased risk of these complications (statement 5). This phenomenon is called legacy effect and is attributed to metabolic memory.\n\nIn patients with longstanding diabetes, intensive glycemic control at later stages can improve microvascular complications but has no effect on macrovascular complications.","t":"Diabetes Mellitus  Complications And Their Management","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A patient with chronic diabetes presents to the OPD for his annual checkup. A small painless ulcer is noted over his right foot. His BMI is 33 kg/m2. All of the following factors can be implicated in the causation of his foot ulcer except:","o":["Venous stasis","Neuropathy","Immunosuppression","Ischemia"],"a":0,"e":"Venous stasis is not a cause of diabetic foot ulcers and gangrene. They are usually caused by a combination of three factors:\n\n• Ischemia - secondary to atheroma\n\n• Peripheral neuropathy - leads to trophic skin changes\n\n• Immunosuppression - due to excess sugar deposition in tissues, predisposing diabetic patients to infection\n\nCommon sites of ulcers include:\n\n• Great toe\n\n• Metatarsophalangeal areas.","t":"Diabetes Mellitus  Complications And Their Management","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A 25-year-old diabetic woman presents to the OPD with the following skin lesions. What is the likely diagnosis?","o":["Necrolytic migratory erythema","Diabetic dermopathy","Necrobiosis lipoidica","Granuloma annulare"],"a":2,"e":"The given image showing irregular plaques with yellowish discoloration and telangiectasias are suggestive of necrobiosis lipoidica diabeticorum.\n\nIt is an inflammatory granulomatous disorder of the skin due to collagen degeneration and lipid deposition. It is usually seen in young women with diabetes mellitus.\n\nThe lesions usually begin in the pretibial region. It begins as papules that gradually enlarge and darken to form erythematous plaques with irregular margins, atrophic centers, and central ulceration.\n\nOption A: Necrolytic migratory erythema is a feature of glucagonoma. It is a waxing and waning rash that appears as shown below.\n\nOption B: Diabetic dermopathy or shin spots are commonly seen in elderly men. It presents as pigmented pretibial papules as shown below.\n\nOption D: Granuloma annulare presents with small papules arranged in an annular pattern. There is no yellowish discoloration or telangiectasias, as shown below.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/98efa019b5634ea1ae597f1bc962b36f.JPEG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/27f35b60e1a04d2ea9b5a157ae9a51bex720x468.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ce26f3db7f14420585c1ad9b6e2875f6x720x540.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4330817c3e804fae84488637beed43fcx399x599.JPEG"],"t":"Diabetes Mellitus  Complications And Their Management","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Patients with diabetes mellitus are at a higher risk of developing all of the following infections except:","o":["Malignant otitis externa","Rhinocerebral mucormycosis","Emphysematous pyelonephritis","Emphysematous appendicitis"],"a":3,"e":"Emphysematous appendicitis is not a complication of diabetes mellitus. The potentially fatal infectious complications of diabetes include:\n\n• Malignant otitis externa\n\n• Rhinocerebral mucormycosis\n\n• Emphysematous pyelonephritis\n\n• Emphysematous cholecystitis","t":"Diabetes Mellitus  Complications And Their Management","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not a feature of 11-beta-hydroxylase deficiency?","o":["Metabolic acidosis","Hypokalemia","Hypertension","Virilization"],"a":0,"e":"11-beta-hydroxylase deficiency is associated with metabolic alkalosis, not metabolic acidosis.\n\nIt is a rare form of congenital adrenal hyperplasia that presents with the following features:\n\n• Glucocorticoid deficiency:\n\n• Hypoglycemia\n\n• Hyperpigmentation around the genitals due to excess adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH) secretion\n\n• Mineralocorticoid excess:\n\n• Hypokalemia\n\n• Hypertension\n\n• Metabolic alkalosis\n\n• Adrenal androgen excess\n\n• Virilization in females\n\n• Precocious puberty in males","t":"Reproductive Endocrinology","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A neonate with ambiguous genitalia undergoes karyotyping and is confirmed to have a 46XY genotype. His blood pressure is 140/80 mm Hg and serum potassium is 1.9 mEq/L. Which of the following is responsible for these findings?","o":["11-deoxycorticosterone","Cortisol","17-hydroxypregnanediol","17-hydroxyprogesterone"],"a":0,"e":"This clinical scenario with ambiguous genitalia in a male neonate is suggestive of 17 alpha-hydroxylase deficiency. It causes hypertension and hypokalemia due to the accumulation of 11-deoxycorticosterone, which is a mineralocorticoid causing sodium retention.\n\n11-hydroxylase deficiency also leads to the accumulation of 11-deoxycorticosterone.","t":"Reproductive Endocrinology","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following abnormalities will you see in a child with salt-wasting congenital adrenal hyperplasia?","o":["Hypoglycemia","Hypernatremia","Hypertension","Hypokalemia"],"a":0,"e":"Hypoglycemia is a feature of salt-wasting congenital adrenal hyperplasia (CAH).\n\nIt is due to 21-hydroxylase deficiency. It results in hyponatremia, hyperkalemia, and hypotension. Males typically present with salt-wasting crises as they often go undiagnosed at birth due to the presence of normal genitalia.","t":"Reproductive Endocrinology","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is the drug of choice for the prenatal treatment of congenital adrenal hyperplasia due to 21-hydroxylase deficiency?","o":["Dexamethasone","Betamethasone","Prednisolone","Hydrocortisone"],"a":0,"e":"The drug of choice for the prenatal treatment of congenital adrenal hyperplasia (CAH) due to 21-hydroxylase deficiency is dexamethasone, as it readily crosses the placenta.\n\nIt is an experimental approach aimed at reducing the virilization of 46XX fetuses by suppressing fetal adrenal steroid and androgen secretion. It may be considered when there is a high risk of CAH due to strong family history. It is effective only when started before 4 weeks of gestation.\n\nHydrocortisone is given in the children at divided doses of 1–1.5 times the normal cortisol production.","t":"Reproductive Endocrinology","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A female neonate is noted to have ambiguous genitalia at birth. Further testing reveals elevated 17-(OH)-progesterone levels and a blood glucose of 40 mg/dl. What treatment will you recommend?","o":["Betamethasone","Beclomethasone","Dexamethasone","Hydrocortisone"],"a":3,"e":"Ambiguous genitalia, elevated 17-(OH)-progesterone, and hypoglycemia are suggestive of 21-hydroxylase deficiency. The drug of choice is hydrocortisone.\n\nHydrocortisone acts as a potent glucocorticoid with mineralocorticoid action and thus increases salt absorption from the kidneys as well.\n\nBetamethasone, beclomethasone, and dexamethasone have negligible effects as mineralocorticoids.","t":"Reproductive Endocrinology","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is incorrect about galactorrhea?","o":["May be bilateral","Associated with lactation","Associated with prolactinoma","Associated with acromegaly"],"a":1,"e":"Galactorrhea or pathological production of milk is not associated with lactation, which is a physiological condition.\n\nGalactorrhea is the inappropriate discharge of milk-containing fluid from the breast. It may be unilateral or bilateral. It is associated with acromegaly and hyperprolactinemia.\n\nTreatment is with dopamine agonists like bromocriptine or cabergoline if it is due to hyperprolactinemia.","t":"Reproductive Endocrinology","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"In a patient with gynecomastia, you will order all of the following tests except:","o":["Testosterone","Prolactin","Estradiol","Luteinizing hormone"],"a":1,"e":"Determination of prolactin level is not useful in patients with gynecomastia.\n\nFor the diagnosis of gynecomastia, serum levels of the following hormones must be checked:\n\n• Testosterone\n\n• LH\n\n• FSH\n\n• Estradiol\n\n• hCG","t":"Reproductive Endocrinology","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A 32-year-old man is being evaluated for gynecomastia. Laboratory investigations reveal low levels of serum testosterone and luteinizing hormone. What is the likely diagnosis?","o":["Testicular failure","Sertoli cell tumor","Gonadotropin-secreting tumor","Androgen-resistant state"],"a":1,"e":"This clinical scenario of gynecomastia with low LH and testosterone is suggestive of a sertoli cell tumor.\n\nHypogonadotropic hypogonadism is also a possible diagnosis with the above clinical presentation and must be ruled out before diagnosing a Sertoli cell tumor.\n\nThe differential diagnosis of gynecomastia is as follows:\n\n• High LH and low testosterone\n\n• Testicular failure\n\n• Low LH and low testosterone\n\n• Hypogonadotropic hypogonadism\n\n• Sertoli cell tumor - increased primary estrogen production\n\n• High LH and high testosterone\n\n• Androgen-resistant state\n\n• GnRH-secreting tumor","t":"Reproductive Endocrinology","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A mother brings her 17-year-old daughter to the gynecologist as she has not started menstruating yet. Examination reveals well-developed breasts and the absence of pubic and axillary hair. Pelvic examination reveals a short, blind-ended vagina. What is the likely diagnosis?","o":["Turner’s syndrome","Stein-Leventhal syndrome","Premature ovarian failure","Complete androgen insensitivity syndrome"],"a":3,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of complete androgen insensitivity syndrome.\n\nClinical features:\n\n• Female phenotype\n\n• Normal breast development\n\n• Blind-ended vagina with absent uterus and ovaries\n\n• Presence of testes in the abdomen or inguinal region\n\n• Scanty pubic and axillary hair\n\n• Primary amenorrhea in late adolescence\n\nOption A: In Turner’s syndrome, breasts are infantile.\n\nOption B: Stein Leventhal syndrome or polycystic ovarian disorder presents with hirsutism and anovulatory menstrual cycles.\n\nOption C: Premature ovarian failure presents with secondary amenorrhea.","t":"Reproductive Endocrinology","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A baby girl is referred to a pediatric surgeon for suspected bilateral inguinal hernias. Further evaluation reveals the masses to be testes in the inguinal canal. Which karyotype would you expect to find in this child?","o":["46 XX","46 XY","47 XXY","47 XYY"],"a":1,"e":"This clinical picture is suggestive of androgen insensitivity syndrome (AIS). Affected children have a male karyotype or 46 XY with a female phenotype.\n\n46XY individuals with complete AIS (formerly called testicular feminization syndrome) have the following features:\n\n• Female phenotype\n\n• Normal breast development\n\n• Blind-ended vagina with absent uterus and ovaries\n\n• Scanty pubic and axillary hair\n\nMost patients present with\n\n• Inguinal masses in infancy - Due to undescended testes or hernias containing testes\n\n• Primary amenorrhea in late adolescence\n\nGonadectomy is a recommended course of treatment due to the possible risks of malignancy.","t":"Reproductive Endocrinology","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"An 18-year-old boy is brought to the casualty with minor burns following a house fire. He tells you that he did not even smell the smoke. Further probing reveals a history of bilateral orchidopexy at the age of 7. Examination findings include eunuchoid habitus, prepubertal testes, and microphallus. What is the likely diagnosis?","o":["Primary hypogonadism","Klinefelter syndrome","Kallman syndrome","Hyperprolactinemia"],"a":2,"e":"Anosmia and absent secondary sexual characteristics in this patient are suggestive of Kallman syndrome.\n\nIt is an X-linked disorder that causes isolated hypogonadotrophic hypogonadism due to defective development of the olfactory bulb. This results in:\n\n• Defective migration of GnRH-producing neurons that originate from the olfactory epithelium\n\n• Aplasia or hypoplasia of olfactory bulb\n\nThe clinical features include:\n\n• Cryptorchidism\n\n• Absent or delayed pubertal changes due to GnRH deficiency\n\n• Anosmia or hyposmia\n\nOptions A and B: Klinefelter syndrome is a cause of primary or hypergonadotrophic hypogonadism.\n\nOption D: Hyperprolactinemia can cause hypogonadotrophic hypogonadism, but is also associated with gynecomastia, galactorrhea, and bitemporal hemianopia.","t":"Reproductive Endocrinology","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"What is the most common cause of hypergonadotropic hypogonadism in males?","o":["Viral orchitis","Klinefelter’s syndrome","Kallman syndrome","Noonan syndrome"],"a":1,"e":"The most common cause of hypergonadotrophic hypogonadism in males is Klinefelter’s syndrome\n\nIt occurs in males with a 47 XXY karyotype and is associated with testicular dysfunction and male infertility.\n\nCauses of hypergonadotrophic hypogonadism in males and females:\n\n• Congenital\n\n• Klinefelter's syndrome\n\n• Turner's syndrome\n\n• Swyer's syndrome\n\n• Premature ovarian failure associated with fragile X syndrome and galactosemia\n\n• Acquired\n\n• Mumps orchitis\n\n• Testicular torsion, trauma\n\n• Liver cirrhosis\n\n• Ketoconazole\n\n• Premature ovarian failure","t":"Reproductive Endocrinology","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following will you recommend in all patients with androgen insensitivity syndrome?","o":["Gonadectomy","Testosterone replacement","Vaginoplasty","Estrogen replacement"],"a":0,"e":"Gonadectomy is clinically recommended in all patients with androgen insensitivity syndrome (AIS) or testicular feminization syndrome due to the risk of malignancy arising from the abnormally located testes.\n\nOptions B, C, D: These treatment options may be offered to patients with AIS, but it depends on personal preference and whether they identify as males or females.","t":"Reproductive Endocrinology","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A 19-year-old patient is being evaluated for primary amenorrhea. Laboratory investigations reveal decreased estrogen, FSH, and LH. What is the likely diagnosis?","o":["Asherman syndrome","Müllerian agenesis","Kallman syndrome","Turner's syndrome"],"a":2,"e":"Decreased levels of estrogen, FSH, and LH are suggestive of hypogonadotrophic hypogonadism, which is caused by Kallman syndrome.\n\nIt is an X-linked disorder (KAL gene) that causes isolated hypogonadotrophic hypogonadism and anosmia.\n\nCauses of hypogonadotrophic hypogonadism:\n\n• Congenital\n\n• Kallman's syndrome\n\n• Isolated hypogonadotrophic hypogonadism (IHH)\n\n• Prader-Willi syndrome\n\n• CHARGE syndrome\n\n• Acquired\n\n• Pituitary tumors, apoplexy\n\n• Hyperprolactinemia\n\n• Anorexia nervosa, excess exercise\n\n• Irradiation\n\n• Infection\n\n• Trauma\n\nOption A: Asherman syndrome causes secondary amenorrhea due to intrauterine synechiae.\n\nOption B: Müllerian agenesis presents with primary amenorrhea with normal levels of all hormones.\n\nOption D: Turner's syndrome causes primary amenorrhea due to hypergonadotrophic hypogonadism.","t":"Reproductive Endocrinology","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Hypercalcemia is associated with all of the following medical conditions except:","o":["Multiple myeloma","Hyperparathyroidism","Sarcoidosis","Hypothyroidism"],"a":3,"e":"Hypercalcemia is associated with all of the following conditions except hypothyroidism. Hyperthyroidism is however associated with hypercalcemia.\n\nOther options:\n\n1) Multiple myeloma is a hemotological malignancy associated with hypercalcemia.\n\n2) Hyperparathyroidism is associated with sub-periosteal bone resorption resulting in hypercalcemia.\n\n3) Sarcoidosis is associated with hypercalcemia due to increased activity of 1 alpha hydroxylase activity in the macrophages.","t":"Disorders Of Parathyroid And Calcium Homeostasis","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following can cause hypercalcemia in a patient?","o":["Vitamin D deficiency","Amyloidosis","Vitamin A intoxication","Vitamin C intoxication"],"a":2,"e":"Vitamin A intoxication can cause hypercalcemia. This is due to high bone turnover. Ingestion of 50,000–100,000 units of Vitamin A daily can increase calcium levels up to 12–14 mg/dL.\n\nNote: Vitamin D intoxication also leads to hypercalcemia.","t":"Disorders Of Parathyroid And Calcium Homeostasis","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A 66-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department after being found unconscious by her neighbors. She is found to have marked dehydration and serum calcium levels of 16 mg/dl. All of the following can cause this presentation except:","o":["Metastatic breast carcinoma","Hyperparathyroidism","Pancreatitis","Hodgkin’s lymphoma"],"a":2,"e":"This clinical scenario with severe hypercalcemia (> 14.8 mg/dl) is suggestive of a hypercalcemic crisis. Pancreatitis causes hypocalcemia, not hypercalcemia.\n\nHypercalcemic crisis is often caused by malignancies due to an acute rise in calcium levels following bone resorption due to metastases. It is a medical emergency that presents with polyuria, severe dehydration, gastrointestinal symptoms, and altered consciousness. It can lead to cardiac arrest and coma.","t":"Disorders Of Parathyroid And Calcium Homeostasis","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following will not be seen in a patient with hypercalcemia?","o":["Polyuria","Diarrhoea","Depression","Vomiting"],"a":1,"e":"Hypercalcemia is associated with constipation and not diarrhea.\n\nClinical manifestations of hypercalcemia include:\n\nSystem\nClinical features\n\nRenal\nRenal stones (Calcium oxalate > calcium phosphate)\n\nNephrocalcinosis\n\nPolyuria and dehydration\n\nRenal failure\n\nMusculoskeletal\nBone pain, bone cysts\n\nMyalgia and arthralgia\n\nOsteopenia, osteoporosis\n\nGIT\nConstipation\n\nNausea, vomiting, anorexia\n\nAbdominal pain\n\nPeptic ulcer disease\n\nAcute pancreatitis\n\nNeuropsychiatric\nAnxiety, depression, fatigue\n\nCognitive dysfunction\n\nDrowsiness, obtundation, coma\n\nCardiac\nShort QT interval, bradycardia\n\nHypertension\n\nOthers\nEctopic calcification\n\nThe clinical signs associated with primary hyperparathyroidism can be remembered with the mnemonic 'stones, bones, abdominal groans, psychic moans'.","t":"Disorders Of Parathyroid And Calcium Homeostasis","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not seen in patients following excessive intake of calcium?","o":["Chronic renal failure","Milk-alkali syndrome","Burnett's syndrome","Restrictive cardiomyopathy"],"a":3,"e":"Excess calcium ingestion is not associated with restrictive cardiomyopathy. It results in milk-alkali syndrome, commonly associated with excessive intake of absorbable antacids such as milk or calcium carbonate.\n\nIn susceptible individuals, there is a loss of negative feedback, leading to excess calcium absorption when there is excess intake. It results in the following triad of clinical features:\n\n• Hypercalcemia\n\n• Alkalosis - due to HCO3 reabsorption caused by PTH suppression, natriuresis, and water depletion.\n\n• Renal failure - due to damage to renal tubules.\n\nIt is reversible once ingestion of antacid is stopped. However, in severe cases, a chronic form called Burnett’s syndrome can develop. It is associated with irreversible renal damage.","t":"Disorders Of Parathyroid And Calcium Homeostasis","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"What is the most common cause of primary hyperparathyroidism?","o":["Parathyroid hyperplasia","Parathyroid adenoma","MEN 1 syndrome","Iatrogenic conditions"],"a":1,"e":"Parathyroid adenomas are the most common cause of primary hyperparathyroidism.\n\nCauses of primary hyperparathyroidism:\n\n• Parathyroid adenomas: These maybe\n\n• Solitary (more common)\n\n• Multiple\n\n• Part of MEN-1, MEN-2A or MEN-4\n\n• Chief cell parathyroid hyperplasia\n\n• Parathyroid gland carcinoma\n\n• Lithium use\n\n• Hyperparathyroidism-jaw tumor (HPT-JT) syndrome","t":"Disorders Of Parathyroid And Calcium Homeostasis","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following sets of lab values will be seen in a patient with secondary hyperparathyroidism?","o":["Option 3","Option 1","Option 4","Option 2"],"a":0,"e":"Secondary hyperparathyroidism presents with decreased calcium and increased levels of PTH, phosphate, and ALP.\n\nIn this condition, calcium deficiency leads to reactive stimulation of the parathyroid. It is caused by the following:\n\n• Chronic kidney disease\n\n• Vitamin D deficiency\n\n• Gastrointestinal malabsorption\n\n• Liver disease","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/11928fe152444e8e988fe5d8922d3611.JPEG"],"t":"Disorders Of Parathyroid And Calcium Homeostasis","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"All of the following statements are true about primary hyperparathyroidism except:","o":["It causes prolongation of QT interval","Asymptomatic disease can occur","Peptic ulcer disease is seen","Hyperuricemia can occur"],"a":0,"e":"Primary hyperparathyroidism (PHPT) causes shortening of the QT interval.\n\nThe asymptomatic form is the most common presentation in the Western population. But in developing countries, the patients are usually symptomatic. The classical symptoms and signs of PHPT are due to hypercalcemia as well as increased parathyroid hormone (PTH) levels.\n\nThe features of hypercalcemia are as follows:\n\nSystem\nClinical features\n\nMusculoskeletal\n\n• Bone pain\n\n• Skeletal deformities\n\n• Pathological fractures\n\n• Myalgia and arthralgia\n\nRenal\n\n• Renal stones(Calcium oxalate > calcium phosphate)\n\n• Nephrocalcinosis\n\n• Polyuria and dehydration\n\n• Renal failure\n\nGIT\n\n• Constipation\n\n• Nausea, vomiting, anorexia\n\n• Abdominal pain\n\n• Peptic ulcer disease\n\n• Acute pancreatitis\n\nNeuropsychiatric\n\n• Anxiety, depression\n\n• Cognitive dysfunction\n\n• Irritability\n\n• Hallucinations\n\nCardiac\n\n• Short QT interval\n\n• bradycardia\n\n• Hypertension\n\nEctopic calcification\n\n• Cornea: Band keratopathy\n\n• Extravascular tissues: calcinosis\n\n• Small arteries: calciphylaxis\n\nThe features of elevated PTH levels are as follows:\n\n• Osteitis fibrosa cystica\n\n• Osteopenia/osteoporosis\n\n• Hyperuricemia and gout\n\n• Pseudogout\n\n• Increased production of calcitriol\n\n• Nephrolithiasis\n\n• Hypophosphatemia\n\n• Anemia\n\nThe clinical signs associated with primary hyperparathyroidism can be remembered with the mnemonic - “bones, stones, groans, moans with fatigue overtones”","t":"Disorders Of Parathyroid And Calcium Homeostasis","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following would you expect in a patient with primary hyperparathyroidism?","o":["Gallstones","Recurrent abortions","Hypotension","Neuropsychiatric changes"],"a":3,"e":"Neuropsychiatric changes are seen in patients with primary hyperparathyroidism (PHPT).\n\nThe clinical signs associated with primary hyperparathyroidism can be remembered with the mnemonic - “bones, stones, groans, moans with fatigue overtones”\n\nSystem\nClinical features\n\nMusculoskeletal\n\n• Bone pain\n\n• Skeletal deformities\n\n• Pathological fractures\n\n• Myalgia and arthralgia\n\nRenal\n\n• Renal stones(Calcium oxalate > calcium phosphate)\n\n• Nephrocalcinosis\n\n• Polyuria and dehydration\n\n• Renal failure\n\nGIT\n\n• Constipation\n\n• Nausea, vomiting, anorexia\n\n• Abdominal pain\n\n• Peptic ulcer disease\n\n• Acute pancreatitis\n\nNeuropsychiatric\n\n• Anxiety, depression\n\n• Cognitive dysfunction\n\n• Irritability\n\n• Hallucinations\n\nCardiac\n\n• Short QT interval\n\n• bradycardia\n\n• Hypertension\n\nEctopic calcification\n\n• Cornea: Band keratopathy\n\n• Extravascular tissues: calcinosis\n\n• Small arteries: calciphylaxis\n\nNote: Though multiple clinical studies have found an increased incidence of gall stones in primary HPT, there is no reference to this in Harrison's and Williams's endocrinology. Hence, option D would be the most appropriate answer.","t":"Disorders Of Parathyroid And Calcium Homeostasis","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"The spinal x-ray of a patient with elevated ALP levels is shown below. What is the likely diagnosis?","o":["Paget's disease","Hyperparathyroidism","Osteopetrosis","Ankylosing spondylitis"],"a":1,"e":"The above x-ray shows a rugger jersey spine which is a distinct feature of hyperparathyroidism. It is associated with elevated ALP.\n\nIt refers to the prominent hyperdensities at the endplates of successive vertebrae, which mimics the striped appearance of a rugby jersey.\n\nOption A: Paget's disease causes cortical thickening, which results in increased opacity on all sides of the vertebral body. These are called picture frame vertebrae.\n\nOption C: Osteopetrosis presents with normal ALP levels. It results in sclerotic endplates of vertebrae that are termed sandwich vertebrae. Though this finding is similar to rugger jersey spine, it is denser and more sharply defined than rugger jersey spine. In addition, there is sclerosis of inner margins of vertebral bodies, resulting in a bone within a bone appearance.\n\nOption D: Ankylosing spondylitis produces a bamboo spine appearance due to fusion of vertebral bodies.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/03b3c7200ad042e9a66b07dddba62143.JPEG"],"t":"Disorders Of Parathyroid And Calcium Homeostasis","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A middle-aged woman presents with worsening pain and an inability to bear weight on her left leg. She says that she had banged her leg against a chair 3 days ago. X-ray reveals a fracture of her left tibia. In view of this finding, a skeletal survey was performed and reveals the following additional finding. What is the likely cause?","o":["Renal osteodystrophy","Osteomalacia","Hyperparathyroidism","Hypoparathyroidism"],"a":2,"e":"This clinical scenario is suggestive of a pathological fracture of the tibia. The given x-ray shows brown tumors or osteitis fibrosa cystica, which are characteristic of hyperparathyroidism.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c6093631bd6c46e59fb854e3c505d320.JPEG"],"t":"Disorders Of Parathyroid And Calcium Homeostasis","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"This x-ray finding in a patient is characteristic of which of the following conditions?","o":["Hyperparathyroidism","Down's syndrome","Acromegaly","Psoriasis"],"a":2,"e":"The arrows in this x-ray point to arrow-headed fingers or the spade phalanx sign. It is pathognomonic of acromegaly.\n\nAcromegaly is the result of excessive growth hormone (GH) production, most commonly from a macroadenoma of the pituitary. There is osseous enlargement, presenting with spade-like hands and widening of the terminal phalangeal tufts, giving an arrow-head appearance.\n\nOther features in the hand include:\n\n• Widened joint spaces (due to cartilage hypertrophy)\n\n• Generalized osteoporosis and cystic changes in the carpal bones\n\nOption A: Hyperparathyroidism causes subperiosteal bone resorption, which is marked along the radial aspect of the middle phalanges of the index and middle fingers.\n\nOption B: Down's syndrome can cause hypoplasia of the middle phalanx of the 5th finger.\n\nOption C: Psoriasis causes a pencil-in-cup appearance of the distal phalanx.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/955d46f81486420e9a4ea1bbbf794e47.PNG"],"t":"Disorders Of Parathyroid And Calcium Homeostasis","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not used to treat a patient with hypercalcemia due to primary hyperparathyroidism?","o":["Forced diuresis with loop diuretics","Glucocorticoids","Pamidronate","Hydration with saline"],"a":1,"e":"Glucocorticoids have no effect on the serum calcium concentration in primary hyperparathyroidism and normal individuals.\n\nThey are effective in the treatment of hypercalcemia when associated with the following conditions:\n\n• Malignancy\n\n• Vitamin D intoxication\n\n• Sarcoidosis\n\nInitial medical management of hypercalcemia involves the following:\n\n• Normal saline (200-500 ml/hour): It is the mainstay treatment to correct dehydration, and titrated to maintain a urine output of >100 ml/hour\n\n• Pamidronate or zoledronate: These are bisphosphonates and are considered as the drug of choice in primary hyperparathyroidism\n\n• Cinacalcet: This is a calcimimetic that increases the affinity of the glands to extracellular calcium, thereby decreasing parathyroid hormone secretion\n\n• Denosumab: This inhibits RANK-ligand, which acts on osteoclasts\n\n• Calcitonin: This is an activated vitamin D analog that may be used to reduce calcium levels. It should be used within the first 24 hours\n\nForced diuresis refers to aggressive fluid hydration with loop diuretic administration. Although this is an effective method to reduce calcium levels, it requires careful monitoring for adverse effects like fluid overload, hypokalemia, and hypomagnesia. Due to this reason as well as the availability of more effective treatment modalities, forced diuresis is not preferred any longer.\n\nThe definitive management of primary hyperparathyroidism is the surgical removal of the parathyroid gland.","t":"Disorders Of Parathyroid And Calcium Homeostasis","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A patient is brought to the emergency department with confusion, drowsiness, and multiple episodes of vomiting. His family members say that he was diagnosed with bipolar disorder several years ago and has been on medication ever since. Further evaluation reveals a serum calcium level of 16 mg/dl. What should be the next step in the management of this patient?","o":["Saline hydration","Denosumab","IV biphosphonate","Subcutaneous calcitonin"],"a":0,"e":"This clinical scenario is suggestive of a hypercalcemic crisis. The next best step in management is saline hydration. Typically 200-500 ml/hour fluids are given to correct dehydration and titrated to maintain a urine output of >100 ml/hour.\n\nHypercalcemic crisis is a medical emergency associated with serum calcium >14 mg/dl. It is often caused by conditions causing an acute rise in calcium levels such as malignancies or lithium overdose.\n\nIt presents with oliguria, severe dehydration, gastrointestinal symptoms, and altered consciousness. It can lead to cardiac arrest and coma.\n\nInitial medical management of hypercalcemia involves the following:\n\n• Fluids are the mainstay treatment\n\n• Bisphosphonates\n\n• Cinacalcet\n\n• Denosumab\n\n• Calcitonin","t":"Disorders Of Parathyroid And Calcium Homeostasis","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"In a patient with hypocalcemia and hyperphosphatemia, which of the following will you not suspect?","o":["Vitamin D-dependent rickets type 1","Pseudohypoparathyroidism","Chronic kidney disease","DiGeorge syndrome"],"a":0,"e":"Vitamin D-dependent rickets type 1 is associated with hypocalcemia and hypophosphatemia\n\nHypocalcemia with hyperphosphatemia is observed in the following conditions:\n\n• True hypoparathyroidism\n\n• Hereditary hypoparathyroidism\n\n• Acquired hypoparathyroidism – following surgery\n\n• Hypomagnesemia\n\n• Pseudohypoparathyroidism\n\n• Chronic kidney disease\n\n• DiGeorge syndrome","t":"Disorders Of Parathyroid And Calcium Homeostasis","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"All of the following medical conditions are associated with hyperphosphatemia except:","o":["Chronic renal failure","Prolonged phosphate intake","Pseudopseudohypoparathyroidism","Pseudohypoparathyroidism"],"a":2,"e":"Pseudopseudohypoparathyroidism is a type of pseudohypoparathyroidism type 1A associated with normal calcium and normal phosphate. The clinical features are similar to pseudohypoparathyroidism but there is no PTH resistance.\n\nPseudohypoparathyroidism is an autosomal dominant condition caused by a mutation of the alpha subunit of the Gs protein. This causes PTH resistance. It is associated with Albright hereditary osteodystrophy (short stature, round facies, and short fourth metacarpal).\n\nTypes of pseudohypoparathyroidism:\n\nType\nPHP 1A\nPHP 1B\nPHP 1C\nPHP 2\nPPHP\n\nHypocalcemia\nYes\nYes\nYes\nYes\nNo\n\nHyperphosphatemia\nYes\nYes\nYes\nYes\nNo\n\nPTH levels\n↑\n↑\n↑\n↑\nNormal\n\nGS α subunit deficiency\nYes\nNo\nNo\nNo\nYes\n\nAlbright hereditary osteodystrophy\nYes\nNo\nYes\nNo\nYes\n\nPTH resistance\nYes\nYes\nYes\nPartial\nNo\n\nPHP: pseudohypoparathyroidism, PPHP: pseudopseudohypoparathyroidism, PTH: parathyroid hormone","t":"Disorders Of Parathyroid And Calcium Homeostasis","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A 5-year-old boy is brought by his mother with with the following abnormality. Serum levels of vitamin D are found to be low. Which of the following would not be seen in this boy?","o":["Decrease in parathyroid hormone level","Decrease in renal calcium excretion","Increase in renal phosphate excretion","Increase in serum alkaline phosphatase"],"a":0,"e":"The given image shows a windswept deformity of the lower limbs (combination of valgus deformity of one leg with varus deformity of the other leg). This finding along with low serum vitamin D points to a diagnosis of vitamin D deficiency rickets. It is associated with an increase in parathyroid hormone (PTH) levels.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7276beb0e5e24793ae4d416c16177005.GIF"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e33bf8ce8be8488ab64ae81136fdf9c9x1280x1405.JPEG"],"t":"Disorders Of Parathyroid And Calcium Homeostasis","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements is incorrect with respect to hypocalcemia?","o":["Can occur due to hypermagnesemia","Latent tetany is observed","Prolonged QT interval","Not a feature of hyperparathyroidism"],"a":3,"e":"Hypocalcemia is a feature of secondary hyperparathyroidism (SHPT).\n\nHypocalcemia refers to total serum calcium ≤8.4 mg/dl. The causes include:\n\n• Low PTH\n\n• Hypoparathyroidism\n\n• High PTH (SHPT)\n\n• Chronic kidney disease\n\n• Vitamin D deficiency\n\n• Pseudohypoparathyroidism\n\n• Hyperphosphatemia\n\n• Loop diuretics, calcitonin, bisphosphonates\n\n• Massive blood transfusions\n\n• Hyperventilation\n\n• Hypomagnesemia - Mg is essential for PTH release\n\n• Severe hypermagnesemia - higher Mg levels suppress PTH secretion\n\nHypocalcemia causes tetany or increased neuromuscular excitability. It can cause paresthesias, cramps, spasms, and seizures. Latent tetany can only be detected by performing Chvostek and Trousseau's signs. A prolonged QT interval is seen on ECG.","t":"Disorders Of Parathyroid And Calcium Homeostasis","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"The following sign is elicited in a patient. What is it called?","o":["Chvostek’s sign","Trousseau's sign","Tinel's sign","Troisier’s sign"],"a":1,"e":"The above image indicates Trousseau's sign or carpopedal spasm classically seen in patients with hypocalcemia.\n\nTrousseau's sign is elicited by making the forearm ischemic by applying a sphygmomanometer cuff to the arm and keeping the pressure above systolic blood pressure (SBP) for 5 minutes. The hand goes into a painful carpopedal spasm and assumes the accoucheur’s hand position.\n\nHypocalcemia results in tetany or increased neuromuscular irritability. It leads to paresthesia and tingling of extremities and perioral area. Severe hypocalcemia can cause seizures.\n\nOption A: Chvostek's sign is also seen in hypocalcemia. Tapping the facial nerve in front of the tragus will cause spasms of the facial muscles.\n\nOption C: Tinel's sign is seen in carpal tunnel syndrome. It refers to a tingling sensation on tapping over the median nerve.\n\nOption D: Troisier's sign refers to left supraclavicular lymphadenopathy in metastatic abdominal malignancy.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/43e3dd9f8b524397b9ca03f9564f1d13.JPEG"],"t":"Disorders Of Parathyroid And Calcium Homeostasis","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following agents cannot be used to manage a patient with chronic hypocalcemia?","o":["Etidronate","Thiazide","Elemental calcium","Vitamin D analogues"],"a":0,"e":"Etidronate is not used in the management of hypocalcemia. It is a bisphosphonate used in the management of hypercalcemia.\n\nThe goal in the management of hypoparathyroidism is to restore the calcium-phosphate balance. The mainstay is calcium and vitamin D supplements. Other underlying conditions must also be treated.\n\nInstead of loop diuretics, thiazides can be administered on a low sodium diet as they enhance urinary calcium reabsorption.","t":"Disorders Of Parathyroid And Calcium Homeostasis","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"The hormone leptin is derived from which of the following?","o":["Arcuate nucleus","Adipocytes","Chief cells","Paneth cells"],"a":1,"e":"Leptin is an adipocyte-derived hormone.\n\nIt acts through brain circuits (predominantly in the hypothalamus) to influence appetite, energy expenditure, and neuroendocrine function. High leptin levels reduce appetite and increase energy expenditure.\n\nThe arcuate nucleus in the hypothalamus plays an important role in the regulation of appetite.","t":"Obesity","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A 25-year-old woman has been fasting all day as part of a new diet. Which of the following is a gut peptide that will be increased in this woman?","o":["Peptide YY","Ghrelin","Cholecystokinin","Orexin"],"a":1,"e":"In the fasting state, high levels of ghrelin are seen. It is a gut-peptide produced in the stomach and stimulates hunger and appetite.","t":"Obesity","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A 16-year-old boy ate two pieces of cake, a sandwich, and two scoops of ice cream at his friend's birthday party an hour ago. Levels of which of the following are likely to be high in this boy?","o":["Neuropeptide Y(NPY)","Melanin-concentrating hormone(MCH)","α- Melanocyte-stimulating hormone(α-MSH)","Agouti-related peptide (AgRP)"],"a":2,"e":"This boy is likely to have high levels of α-Melanocyte-stimulating hormone (α-MSH). It is a hypothalamic peptide that decreases appetite and hence would be increased in the fed state.","t":"Obesity","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is a key transcription factor involved in the differentiation of mesenchymal preadipocytes to adipose cells?","o":["RARγ","Rev-erbAβ","RXRα","PPARγ"],"a":3,"e":"One of the key transcription factors involved in the differentiation of mesenchymal preadipocytes to adipose cells is the peroxisome proliferator-activated receptor γ (PPARγ). It is a nuclear receptor that also binds the thiazolidinedione class of insulin-sensitizing drugs used in the treatment of type 2 diabetes mellitus.\n\nNote: Saroglitazar has a dual action on PPARα and PPAR𝝲 receptors. It reduces blood sugar and lipid levels in diabetic patients.","t":"Obesity","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements is incorrect regarding adiponectin?","o":["Its levels are increased in obesity","It increases insulin sensitivity","Promotes lipid oxidation","Has vascular protective effects"],"a":0,"e":"Adiponectin levels are reduced in obesity.\n\nIt is an abundant adipose-derived protein with the following characteristics:\n\n• Its levels are reduced in obesity\n\n• It enhances insulin sensitivity and lipid oxidation\n\n• Has vascular-protective effects\n\nOn the other hand, resistin and RBP4 levels, which are also secreted by adipocytes, may induce insulin resistance.","t":"Obesity","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A 2-year-old girl is referred to an endocrinologist with severe obesity and insatiable hunger despite adequate nutrition. Due to the early-onset severe obesity, genetic testing was done and revealed inactivating mutations in the leptin gene. Which of the following neuroendocrine abnormalities is this child most likely to develop?","o":["Central hypothyroidism","Growth retardation","Hypogonadotropic hypogonadism","Diabetes mellitus"],"a":2,"e":"The most prominent neuroendocrine abnormality seen in obese individuals with inactivating mutations in the leptin (ob gene) or leptin receptor (db gene) is hypogonadotropic hypogonadism.\n\nLeptin mutations are responsible for a rare form of obesity that runs in families. These mutations prevent leptin from delivering satiety signals. Hence, the brain perceives starvation despite adequate nutrition. It results in morbid, early-onset obesity.\n\nMutations in the following genes can cause obesity in humans:\n\n• Lep (ob)\n\n• LepR (db)\n\n• POMC\n\n• PC-1\n\n• MC4R","t":"Obesity","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Genetic mutations causing pro-opiomelanocortin (POMC) deficiency presents with all of the following except:","o":["α-MSH deficiency","Cachexia","Red hair pigmentation","Adrenal insufficiency"],"a":1,"e":"Cachexia is not a feature of mutations of the pro-opiomelanocortin (POMC) gene. It is associated with the following characteristics:\n\n• Absence of α-MSH\n\n• Severe obesity - α-MSH inhibits appetite in the hypothalamus\n\n• Pale skin and red hair\n\n• Absence of ACTH\n\n• Secondary adrenal insufficiency","t":"Obesity","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"In a child with obesity, hypogonadism, and mental retardation, which of the following would you not suspect ?","o":["Prader-Willi syndrome","Laurence-Moon-Bardet-Biedl syndrome","Carpenter syndrome","Angelman syndrome"],"a":3,"e":"Children with Angelman syndrome present with mental retardation but do not have obesity and hypogonadism. It occurs due to the deletion of band q12 of the maternal chromosome 15.\n\nThe following syndromes present with obesity, hypogonadism, and mental retardation\n\n• Prader-Willi syndrome - deletion of band q12 of the paternal chromosome 15.\n\n• Laurence-Moon-Bardet-Biedl syndrome\n\n• Carpenter syndrome\n\n• Cohen syndrome\n\nThe following image shows a child with Prader-Willi syndrome.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f5c80982639e4725b4d238f53026d6afx572x903.PNG"],"t":"Obesity","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Obesity is not seen in patients with which of the following conditions?","o":["Craniopharyngioma","Pickwickian syndrome","Prader-Willi syndrome","Sipple syndrome"],"a":3,"e":"Sipple syndrome is characterized by pheochromocytoma which is associated with weight loss.\n\nObesity is seen in the following syndromes:\n\n• Cushing's syndrome\n\n• Prader-Willi syndrome\n\n• Pickwickian syndrome\n\n• Hypothyroidism\n\n• Insulinoma\n\n• Craniopharyngioma and other hypothalamic disorders\n\n• Laurence-Moon-Bardet-Biedl syndrome\n\n• Carpenter syndrome\n\n• Cohen syndrome","t":"Obesity","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A 40-year-old woman is being evaluated for high blood glucose levels. On examination, acanthosis nigricans is noted, and her BMI is 34 kg/m². How would you classify her weight?","o":["Overweight","Class I obesity","Class II obesity","Class III obesity"],"a":1,"e":"According to the WHO classification, a BMI of 34 kg/m² would be classified as class I obesity.\n\n Classification\n\nBMI (kg/m²)\n\n Risk\n\nUnderweight\n\n <18.5\n\n -\n\nNormal weight\n\n 18.5–24.9\n\n -\n\n Overweight\n\n 25–29.9\n\nIncreased\n\n Obesity (Class I)\n\n 30–34.9\n\nHigh\n\nObesity (Class II)\n\n 35–39.9\n\nVery high\n\nExtreme obesity(Class III)\n\n ≥ 40\n\nExtremely high\n\nThe revised BMI classification for Asians/Indians is as follows:\n\n• Underweight - BMI < 18.5 kg/m2\n\n• Normal or lean - BMI 18.5–22.9 kg/m2\n\n• Overweight - BMI 23.0 –24.9 kg/m2\n\n• Obese BMI ≥25 kg/m2\n\nNote: As the question does not specify Asian/Indian, the answer is given according to WHO classification.","t":"Obesity","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A known patient of type 2 diabetes mellitus on oral medication presents to your OPD for review. His blood pressure is 150/90 mm Hg and his BMI is 28.4 kg/m². His fasting glucose is 118 mg/dl and postprandial glucose is 154 mg/dl. He consumes a low-calorie diet and exercises regularly. How will you manage his obesity?","o":["Start him on a more intense diet and exercise regimen","Anti-obesity drugs in combination with proper diet and exercise","Refer for bariatric surgery","Anti-obesity drugs alone as lifestyle changes have not been effective"],"a":1,"e":"This obese man with a BMI of 28.4 kg/m² along with comorbidities must be managed with anti-obesity drugs as an adjunct to comprehensive lifestyle modifications.\n\nThe recommendations for management of obesity are as follows:\n\n• Diet, exercise, behavioral therapy:\n\n• BMI - 25-29.9 with comorbidities\n\n• BMI ≥ 30\n\n• Adjunctive pharmacotherapy:\n\n• BMI - 27-29.9 with comorbidities\n\n• BMI ≥ 30\n\n• Bariatric surgery:\n\n• BMI - 35-39.9 with comorbidities\n\n• BMI ≥ 40","t":"Obesity","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following anti-obesity drugs is a selective 5-HT2C receptor agonist?","o":["Orlistat","Phentermine","Lorcaserin","Topiramate"],"a":2,"e":"Lorcaserin is a selective 5-HT2C receptor agonist with a functional selectivity ~15 times that of 5-HT2A receptors and 100 times that of 5-HT2B receptors.","t":"Obesity","c":"Endocrinology"},
 {"q":"A 4-month-old child was brought with a history of recurrent episodes of jaundice. Blood investigations were suggestive of hereditary spherocytosis. Which of the following liver function test findings is likely to be seen here?","o":["Direct bilirubin &gt;50% of total bilirubin","Direct bilirubin &gt;85% of total bilirubin","Direct bilirubin &gt;15% of total bilirubin","Direct bilirubin &lt;15% of total bilirubin"],"a":3,"e":"The given clinical scenario describes recurrent hemolysis in a patient with hereditary spherocytosis leading to jaundice. In hemolytic conditions, only the unconjugated/ indirect bilirubin is raised and conjugated/direct bilirubin remains normal i.e. <15% of total bilirubin.\n\nBilirubin is synthesized from heme which is released primarily from the breakdown of old RBCs. This bilirubin is in its unconjugated form. It enters the circulation and reaches the liver. It is converted to conjugated bilirubin which is then secreted into the biliary tree.\n\nTherefore, in a healthy person, most of the circulating bilirubin is unconjugated/indirect bilirubin. Hence, the normal liver function tests show indirect bilirubin >85% and direct/conjugated bilirubin <15% of the total bilirubin. The normal bilirubin values are as follows:\n\n• Total bilirubin levels - 1 to 1.5 mg/dL\n\n• Direct bilirubin - <0.3 mg/dL","t":"Hyperbilirubinemias And Tests Of Liver Function","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A 2-week-old baby presented with yellowish discoloration of the skin. Investigations revealed markedly elevated direct bilirubin levels. Which of the following conditions should be suspected in the newborn?","o":["Biliary atresia","Physiological jaundice","Breast milk jaundice","Hemolytic disease of the newborn"],"a":0,"e":"The raised direct bilirubin levels indicate conjugated hyperbilirubinemia. Among the given options, biliary atresia causes conjugated hyperbilirubinemia.\n\nBiliary atresia is a disorder characterised by destruction or absence of bile ducts in a newborn. It leads to obstruction to the flow of bile thereby causing leaking of conjugated bilirubin into the circulation.\n\nOption B: Physiological jaundice of the newborn occurs due to immaturity of the newborn's liver. It is not able to conjugate the excess bilirubin produced after birth. It leads to unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia.\n\nOption C: Breast milk may contain certain factors that inhibit the conjugation of bilirubin leading to unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia.\n\nOption D: Hemolytic disease of the newborn causes excess production of bilirubin due to hemolysis leading to unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia.","t":"Hyperbilirubinemias And Tests Of Liver Function","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Investigations performed in a patient with recurrent jaundice gave the following results. What is the possible cause of jaundice in this patient?\n\nTotal bilirubin - 4 mg/dL\n\nUrine bilirubin +","o":["Hemolytic anaemia","Gilbert syndrome","Criggler-Najjar syndrome","Dubin Johnson syndrome"],"a":3,"e":"The presence of urinary bilirubin and increased total bilirubin indicates conjugated hyperbilirubinemia. Among the given options, Dubin Johnson syndrome causes conjugated hyperbilirubinemia.\n\nOnly conjugated bilirubin can appear in the urine because it is water-soluble and filterable by the kidney. Unconjugated bilirubin is water-insoluble and bound to albumin, hence it cannot be filtered by the kidney.\n\nCauses of unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia:\n\n• Increased bilirubin production-\n\n• Hemolytic anemias\n\n• Hematomas\n\n• Impaired bilirubin uptake and conjugation-\n\n• Gilbert syndrome\n\n• Crigler Najjar syndrome\n\n• Drugs - Probenecid, Rifampin\n\nCauses of conjugated hyperbilirubinemia:\n\n• Hereditary cholestatic syndromes\n\n• Dubin Johnson syndrome\n\n• Rotor syndrome\n\n• Biliary obstruction","t":"Hyperbilirubinemias And Tests Of Liver Function","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is a cause of unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia?","o":["Probenecid","Amoxicillin-clavulanate","Acute hepatitis","Stone in the common bile duct"],"a":0,"e":"Probenecid causes impaired hepatic uptake of bilirubin which leads to unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia.\n\nAmoxicillin-clavulanate, acute hepatitis and stone in the common bile duct are causes that lead to mixed hyperbilirubinemia (both conjugated and unconjugated bilirubin elevated) or predominantly conjugated hyperbilirubinemias.\n\nLevels of conjugated and unconjugated bilirubin are useful in determining pre-hepatic causes of jaundice but are not useful in differentiating between intrahepatic and post-hepatic causes of jaundice.","t":"Hyperbilirubinemias And Tests Of Liver Function","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A 24-year-old woman presents with jaundice. She had been on OCPs for the past month. Her LFT and liver biopsy findings are given below. What is the probable diagnosis?\n\nTotal bilirubin - 12 mg/dL\n\nDirect bilirubin - 10 mg/dL\n\nAST - 28 IU/L\n\nALT - 30 IU/L\n\nGGT - 20 IU/L","o":["Gilbert syndrome","Crigler- Najjar syndrome","Rotor syndrome","Dubin Johnson syndrome"],"a":3,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of Dubin Johnson syndrome (DJS). The LFT describes conjugated hyperbilirubinemia with normal liver enzyme levels. The given image of microscopic examination of liver biopsy shows an accumulation of dark melanin-like pigment in the hepatocytes which is seen in this condition.\n\nDJS is an autosomal recessive condition causing defective expression of MRP 2 protein which is important for the biliary excretion of conjugated bilirubin. The patient has icterus but is otherwise asymptomatic.\n\nDJS also impairs biliary excretion of various compounds like epinephrine metabolites. The dark melanin-like pigment is derived from these metabolites causing the liver to appear black.\n\nRotor syndrome is also an autosomal recessive condition causing conjugated hyperbilirubinemia but it does not present with pigmentation of the liver.\n\nGilbert and Crigler-Najjar syndromes are characterized by unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c71b91e8a4c54685a18d7692c7228b17.PNG"],"t":"Hyperbilirubinemias And Tests Of Liver Function","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"What is the defect seen in patients with Rotor syndrome?","o":["Absent UDP glucuronosyl transferase","Mutation in organic anion transport protein","Mutation in multidrug resistance–associated protein 2","Reduced UDP glucuronosyl transferase"],"a":1,"e":"Rotor syndrome occurs due to mutation in OATP (organic anion transport protein) 1B1 and 1B3.\n\nRotor syndrome is an inherited autosomal recessive disorder with conjugated hyperbilirubinemia. It is a benign disorder with mild elevation of bilirubin levels. It is similar to Dubin Johnson syndrome but the liver does not show any black pigmentation. No treatment is required for these patients.","t":"Hyperbilirubinemias And Tests Of Liver Function","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"While evaluating a patient with jaundice, all of the following investigations would help you to differentiate between Dubin Johnson syndrome and Rotor syndrome except:","o":["Oral cholecystography","Total urinary coproporphyrin","Serum bilirubin levels","Bromsulphalein clearance test"],"a":2,"e":"Serum bilirubin levels are not helpful in differentiating between Dubin Johnson syndrome (DJS) and Rotor's syndrome as both these conditions present with mild elevation of serum bilirubin.\n\nThese two conditions can be differentiated as follows:\n\nTest\nRotor Syndrome\nDubin Johnson syndrome\n\nOral cholecystography\nGall bladder is visualized\n\nGall bladder not visualised\n\nImpaired ability to concentrate the contrast material into bile\n\nTotal urinary coproporphyrin\nElevated\n\nNormal\n\nIncreased excretion of coproporphyrin 1 and decreased excretion of coproporphyrin 3\n\nBromsulphalein (BSP) clearance test\n\nDelayed clearance of BSP\n\nNo reflux\n\nRise in plasma levels of BSP after 90 minutes of injection\n\nReflux of BSP from hepatocytes","t":"Hyperbilirubinemias And Tests Of Liver Function","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"In a patient with unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia, which feature helps confirm the diagnosis of Crigler- Najjar syndrome?","o":["Presence of kernicterus","Absence of hemolysis","UGT1A1 mutation","Autosomal recessive inheritance"],"a":0,"e":"Kernicterus is seen in Crigler-Najjar syndrome but not in Gilbert syndrome. Gilbert syndrome does not cause a significant elevation in unconjugated bilirubin to cause kernicterus.\n\nCrigler-Najjar syndrome and Gilbert syndrome are both caused by various mutations in the UGT1A1 gene. This leads to decreased activity of bilirubin UDP-glucuronosyltransferase enzyme leading to unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia. Both are predominantly inherited in an autosomal recessive pattern.\n\nThese syndromes present with jaundice in infants. The unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia in these syndromes is not associated with any hemolysis. All the other liver function tests are usually normal.\n\nCrigler Najjar syndrome may lead to kernicterus causing severe brain damage. Liver transplantation is required without which the infant may not survive beyond 1-2 years of age. Whereas, Gilbert syndrome is a benign asymptomatic condition that does not need any intervention.","t":"Hyperbilirubinemias And Tests Of Liver Function","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"An infant presented with yellow discolouration of the skin, poor feeding, and seizures. Investigations revealed total bilirubin- 40 mg/dL and unconjugated bilirubin- 34 mg/dL. Phenobarbitone was administered while blood was being arranged for an exchange transfusion. However, there was no improvement. What is the most probable diagnosis?","o":["Gilbert syndrome","Crigler-Najjar syndrome type 1","Crigler-Najjar syndrome type 2","Rotor syndrome"],"a":1,"e":"The given clinical scenario with poor feeding and seizures is indicative of kernicterus. This accompanied with unconjugated hyperbilrubinemia is highly suggestive of Crigler-Najjar syndrome type 1.\n\nNo improvement in bilirubin levels following the administration of phenobarbital further supports the diagnosis.\n\nCrigler-Najjar syndrome is of two types with type 1 having absent/trace enzyme activity and type 2 having <10% of the normal enzyme activity. Hence, type 1 is far more severe and leads to kernicterus and neurological damage. Type 2 may also lead to kernicterus occasionally.\n\nKernicterus occurs because the unconjugated bilirubin is lipid-soluble and hence crosses the blood-brain barrier.\n\nPhenobarbital is an enzyme inducer. Its administration can be used to differentiate between type 1 and type 2 Crigler-Najjar.\n\n• Enzyme induction by phenobarbital leads to improved conjugation of bilirubin leading to a reduction in total bilirubin level in type 2 Crigler-Najjar syndrome.\n\n• No improvement is seen in type 1 as the enzyme activity is absent and therefore cannot be induced.\n\nInherited unconjugated hyperbilirubinemias (Autosomal Recessive) include:\n\nCrigler-Najjar type I\n\nCrigler-Najjar type II\n\nGilbert Syndrome\n\nUDP-GT Activity\nAbsent\nMarkedly reduced\nReduced\n\nSerum bilirubin\n20 - 45 mg/dl\n<20 mg/dl\nGenerally <3 mg/dl\n\nPheno-barbital effect on serum bilirubin\nNo effect\nReduction\nReduction\n\nOutcome\nKernicterus\nUsually benign, kernicterus is rare\nBenign\n\nPrevalence\nRare\nRare\n Common\n\nNote: Rotor syndrome causes conjugated hyperbilirubinemia.\n\nThe normal serum bilirubin (between 1 and 1.5 mg/dL) present in serum represents a balance between input from the production of bilirubin and hepatic/biliary removal of the pigment.\n\nIsolated elevation of the bilirubin:\n\n• Conjugated hyperbilirubinemia- >15% conjugated bilirubin\n\n• Unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia- <15% conjugated bilirubin","t":"Hyperbilirubinemias And Tests Of Liver Function","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"An 8-year-old child presents with jaundice and easy fatiguability. He had a history of ear infection which was treated with amoxicillin-clavulanic acid. Investigations done are shown below. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nHemoglobin - 8 g/dL\n\nTotal bilirubin level - 4.5 mg/dl\n\nDirect bilirubin - 0.8 mg/dl\n\nALT - 45 IU/L\n\nAST - 42 IU/L\n\nOther liver function tests - normal\n\nUltrasound shows the presence of gall stones","o":["Drug induced hepatitis","Hemolytic jaundice","Obstructive jaundice","Gilbert syndrome"],"a":1,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of hemolytic jaundice. It can present with the following features:\n\n• Low hemoglobin\n\n• Unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia\n\n• Pigment gall stones - excess bilirubin in bile\n\n• Development of cholestasis when the gall stones obstruct the flow of bile. This would result in the elevation of conjugated bilirubin and LFTs show a cholestatic picture.\n\nHepatic and obstructive causes of jaundice are not likely as the other liver function tests are normal. The various causes of jaundice can be differentiated as follows:\n\n Jaundice\nPre hepatic\nHepatic\nPost hepatic\n\nCause\n\nExcessive amount of bilirubin is presented to the liver\n\nImpaired cellular uptake/ defective conjugation/ defective secretion of bilirubin\n\nDefective excretion due to mechanical obstruction to the flow of bile\n\nLFT\nElevated unconjugated bilirubin\n\nElevated conjugated and unconjugated bilirubin\n\nMarked elevated levels of AST and ALT (>1000U/L)\n\nElevated conjugated bilirubin\n\nElevated levels of ALP and GGT\n\nExample\nHemolytic anaemia\nHepatitis\nCholedocholithiasis\n\nInherited hyperbilirubinemias like Gilbert syndrome are suspected when there is no associated hemolysis. It is unlikely in this patient because there are clear hemolytic features.","t":"Hyperbilirubinemias And Tests Of Liver Function","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following conditions is associated with increased activity of 5’- nucleotidase?","o":["Bone diseases","Prostate cancer","Chronic renal failure","Cholestatic disorders"],"a":3,"e":"5ʹ-nucleotidase is usually elevated in cholestasis.\n\nEnzymes elevated in cholestasis are :\n\n• Alkaline phosphatase (ALP)\n\n• 5ʹ-nucleotidase\n\n• γ-glutamyl transpeptidase (GGT)","t":"Hyperbilirubinemias And Tests Of Liver Function","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Which among the following is the most specific indicator of liver cell injury?","o":["Alkaline phosphatase","Gamma glutamyl transpeptidase","Alanine aminotransferase","Aspartate aminotransferase"],"a":2,"e":"Among the given options, alanine aminotransferase is the most specific indicator of liver cell injury.\n\nAlanine aminotransferase (ALT) and aspartate aminotransferase (AST) are indicators of liver cell injury. This is because ALT is found primarily in the liver, whereas, AST is found in the liver, cardiac muscle, skeletal muscle, kidneys, brain, pancreas, etc.\n\nGGT and ALP are enzymes that primarily reflect cholestasis.","t":"Hyperbilirubinemias And Tests Of Liver Function","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"In a patient with acute viral hepatitis, what level of serum transaminases will you expect?","o":["10-40 IU/L","&lt;500 IU/L","40-300 IU/L","&gt;1000 IU/L"],"a":3,"e":"In a patient with acute viral hepatitis, serum transaminases are elevated to >1000 IU/L\n\nThe normal levels of serum transaminases are 10-40 IU/L. Marked elevation of serum transaminases >1000 IU/L is seen in conditions with extensive hepatocellular injury. These include:\n\n• Viral hepatitis\n\n• Ischemic injury to the liver in conditions like acute heart failure\n\n• Toxin/drug-induced liver injury\n\nMinor elevations of serum transaminase levels up to 300 IU/L are non-specific and can be found in any liver disease.","t":"Hyperbilirubinemias And Tests Of Liver Function","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A 64-year-old man with jaundice and abdominal pain was found to have an ALP level of 200 mg/dL. His LFT reports from a month ago had shown an ALP level of 50 mg/dL. What is your inference?","o":["Normal finding for age","Could be due to fatty meal intake","Indicative of hemolysis","Suggestive of cholestatic liver disease"],"a":3,"e":"A 4 fold rise of serum ALP (alkaline phosphatase) is suggestive of cholestatic liver disease. However, serum ALP levels cannot differentiate between extrahepatic and intrahepatic cholestasis.\n\nThe normal range for serum ALP is considered 40-125 IU/L. Serum alkaline phosphatase is found in the liver, bone, small intestines, and placenta. Hence, a mild increase in ALP, i.e., 1 - 1.5 times normal, can be seen in these conditions:\n\n• Following a fatty meal intake - influx of intestinal alkaline phosphatase into the blood. Hence, the patient is advised to fast before the blood sample is collected for the test.\n\n• In pregnancy - placental ALP\n\n• In children - rapid bone growth\n\n• In elderly people over 60 years of age\n\nALP can be raised even in conditions like metastatic bone disease, inflammatory bowel disease, Hodgkin's lymphoma, etc. Hence, it needs to be correlated with raised levels of gamma-glutamyltransferase (GGT) and 5' nucleotidase which are more specific for liver disease.","t":"Hyperbilirubinemias And Tests Of Liver Function","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements regarding liver enzymes is true?","o":["ALT is raised to &gt;1000 IU/L in cirrhosis","Absolute levels of aminotransferases correlate with the prognosis","AST:ALT ratio &lt;1 is seen in nonalcoholic fatty liver disease","AST:ALT ratio &gt;1 is seen in chronic viral hepatitis"],"a":2,"e":"AST:ALT ratio <1 is seen in NAFLD (nonalcoholic fatty liver disease).\n\nThe AST:ALT ratio can be interpreted as follows:\n\nAST:ALT\nCondition\n\n<1\nChronic viral hepatitis, Nonalcoholic fatty liver disease\n\n>1\nCirrhosis\n\n>2:1\n\nSuggestive of alcoholic liver disease\n\nAlcohol intake is associated with the deficiency of pyridoxal phosphate which in turn leads to decreased levels of ALT enzyme. So, AST>ALT.\n\n>3:1\nHighly suggestive of alcoholic liver disease.\n\nOption A: Patients with cirrhosis have only mildly elevated or even normal levels of serum transaminases.\n\nOption B: Absolute levels of aminotransferases are not used for predicting prognosis. This is because there is a poor correlation between the degree of liver cell damage and the levels of aminotransferases.","t":"Hyperbilirubinemias And Tests Of Liver Function","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Liver function tests of a patient show isolated elevation of gamma-glutamyltransferase with other liver enzymes within normal limits. All of the following drugs could be responsible for this finding except:","o":["Barbiturates","Griseofulvin","Penicillin","Phenytoin"],"a":2,"e":"Among the given options, penicillin does not lead to elevated gamma-glutamyltransferase (GGT) levels.\n\nGGT is a microsomal enzyme. Hence, drugs that are microsomal enzyme inducers can lead to an increase in GGT levels. However, other liver enzyme levels will be normal. Therefore, an isolated rise in GGT level does not indicate cholestatic liver disease.\n\nDrugs that increase levels of GGT are:\n\n• Barbiturates\n\n• Carbamazepine\n\n• Ethanol\n\n• Griseofulvin\n\n• Rifampicin\n\n• Phenytoin","t":"Hyperbilirubinemias And Tests Of Liver Function","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements regarding prothrombin time is false?","o":["Indicates prognosis in acute liver disease","Indicates prognosis in chronic liver disease","Measures factors II, V, VIII, X","Measures factors II, V, VII, X"],"a":2,"e":"Prothrombin time (PT) can help in estimating the levels of factors II, V, VII, and X.\n\nPT evaluates the extrinsic and the common pathway of coagulation. Factor VII is required in the extrinsic pathway and factors II, V, X are required in the common pathway.\n\nCoagulation factors are produced by the liver (except factor VIII) and they have a short half-life. Hence, they indicate the synthetic function of the liver and are very useful in assessing acute hepatic dysfunction. Factor VIII is produced by vascular endothelial cells.\n\nProlonged PT that is not corrected by vitamin K administration indicates poor prognosis in both acute and chronic liver disease.","t":"Hyperbilirubinemias And Tests Of Liver Function","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Which among the following is a marker of chronic liver disease?","o":["Prothrombin","Albumin","5'- nucleosidase","Alkaline phosphatase (ALP)"],"a":1,"e":"Serum albumin is a marker of chronic liver disease because it is synthesized by the hepatocytes and has a long half-life of 18–20 days. Serum albumin is not a good indicator of acute or mild hepatic dysfunction.\n\nHypoalbuminemia is more common in chronic liver disorders such as cirrhosis and usually reflects severe liver damage. In hepatitis, albumin levels <3 g/dL should raise the possibility of chronic liver disease.\n\nProthrombin time is used to assess acute parenchymal liver disease. 5' nucleosidase is elevated in cholestasis. Elevation of ALP (<3 fold) is not specific to liver diseases.","t":"Hyperbilirubinemias And Tests Of Liver Function","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following can be seen in a patient with chronic liver disease?","o":["Hypergammaglobulinemia","Hypogammaglobulinemia","Rise in beta globulin levels","Rise in alpha globulin levels"],"a":0,"e":"Chronic liver disease is associated with hypergammaglobulinemia. Chronic liver diseases with hypergammaglobulinemia include autoimmune hepatitis, primary biliary cirrhosis, and alcoholic liver disease.\n\nThe liver produces all globulins except gamma globulin which are produced by B cells. In chronic liver diseases like liver cirrhosis, there is impaired reticuloendothelial function in hepatic sinusoids. Hence the liver fails to clear the bacterial antigens. This causes an increased production of gamma globulin.","t":"Hyperbilirubinemias And Tests Of Liver Function","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not a marker for liver fibrosis?","o":["Procollagen peptide III (PIIINP)","Hyaluronic acid","Tissue inhibitor of metalloproteinase 1(TIMP1)","Telopeptide"],"a":3,"e":"Telopeptide is not a marker for liver fibrosis. It is a marker for bone resorption.\n\nVarious non-invasive markers to detect hepatic fibrosis are given below:\n\nNon-invasive test for fibrosis\nComponents tested\n\n Fibrotest/FibroSure\n\n• α2-macroglobulin\n\n• Haptoglobin\n\n• Apolipoprotein A-1\n\n• GGT\n\n• Total bilirubin\n\nEnhanced Liver Fibrosis (ELF) assay\n\n• Hyaluronic acid\n\n• Procollagen peptide III (PIIINP)\n\n• Tissue inhibitor of metalloproteinase (TIMP)\n\nTransient elastography (FibroScan)\n\n• Ultrasound waves are used to measure liver stiffness\n\nFibrospect II\n\n• Hyaluronic acid\n\n• Alpha 2 macroglobulin\n\n• Tissue inhibitor of metalloproteinase 1 (TIMP 1)","t":"Hyperbilirubinemias And Tests Of Liver Function","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"In which of the following patients planned for a liver biopsy, would you not be required to use the transjugular approach?","o":["A patient with prolonged INR","A patient with significant ascites","A patient with splenomegaly","A morbidly obese patient"],"a":2,"e":"Liver biopsy can be performed in a patient with splenomegaly via the percutaneous approach.\n\nWhen the percutaneous approach is not advisable, liver biopsy can be performed via the transjugular approach. In this approach, a cannula is inserted via the jugular vein which is then passed into hepatic veins through which the biopsy sample is taken. Therefore, any bleeding resulting from the procedure will drain into the hepatic veins.\n\nIndications of transjugular liver biopsy:\n\n• Percutaneous approach contraindicated\n\n• Significant ascites\n\n• Prolonged INR\n\n• Coagulopathies\n\n• Difficulty in accessing the liver via the percutaneous route\n\n• Morbid obesity","t":"Hyperbilirubinemias And Tests Of Liver Function","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is the most common cause of cirrhosis in India?","o":["Non alcoholic fatty liver disease","Alcoholic liver disease","Hepatitis B infection","Hepatitis C infection"],"a":1,"e":"Alcoholic liver disease is the most common cause of cirrhosis in India.\n\nThe most common cause of cirrhosis worldwide is chronic viral hepatitis - Hepatitis C and B. NASH is expected to overtake it in the near future.","t":"Alcoholic Liver Diseases And Non Alcoholic Fatty Liver Disease","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"What is the minimum amount of ethanol a man must have consumed for him to be at increased risk for alcoholic liver disease?","o":["35 g/day","20 g /day","28g/day","14 g/day"],"a":2,"e":"The minimum amount of ethanol that leads to an increased risk of developing alcoholic liver disease is 28g/day. In women, the minimum is 14g/day.\n\nThe risk factors for alcoholic liver disease are:\n\n• Quantity of alcohol consumption - The risk of developing alcoholic liver disease increases when alcohol consumption is >14 drinks/ week in men and >7drinks/week in women. A standard drink contains 14g of ethanol which is present in -\n\n• 120 mL of wine\n\n• 350ml of beer\n\n• 45ml of spirits (gin, rum, whiskey, vodka, tequilla)\n\n• Gender - Women have increased susceptibility to alcoholic liver disease\n\n• Hepatitis C infection - Increases the severity and decreases survival in alcoholic liver disease\n\n• Genetics - A variation in patatin-like phospholipase domain-containing protein 3 (PNPLA3) gene confers an increased risk of progressive liver disease","t":"Alcoholic Liver Diseases And Non Alcoholic Fatty Liver Disease","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A 45-year-old chronic alcoholic presents with right upper quadrant abdominal pain and nausea. His ultrasound abdomen revealed grade 2 fatty liver. Which among the following findings is unlikely to be seen in his condition?","o":["AST : ALT &lt; 2","Prolonged prothrombin time","Low platelet counts","Elevated ALP"],"a":0,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of alcoholic liver disease. It is usually characterized by an AST: ALT > 2. This is because there is a deficiency of pyridoxal 5'- phosphate that is a cofactor for the enzymatic activity of ALT. Therefore, ALT levels fail to rise and there is a reversal of the AST: ALT ratio.\n\nProthrombin time (PT) is prolonged due to the deficiency of coagulation factors and, usually, does not respond to the administration of parenteral vitamin K. Platelet counts are often reduced early in the disease, which is reflective of portal hypertension with hypersplenism.\n\nElevated ALP levels are seen due to enzymatic induction by alcohol and mild cholestasis.","t":"Alcoholic Liver Diseases And Non Alcoholic Fatty Liver Disease","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is unlikely to be seen in patients with Zieve's syndrome?","o":["Cholestatic jaundice","Hemolytic anaemia","Transient hyperlipidemia","Thrombocytosis"],"a":3,"e":"Zieve's syndrome is not associated with thrombocytosis.\n\nZieve's syndrome is a rare syndrome occurring due to severe alcoholic hepatitis characterized by a triad of:\n\n• Cholestatic jaundice\n\n• Hemolytic anemia\n\n• Transient hyperlipidemia\n\nIt resolves with abstinence from alcohol.","t":"Alcoholic Liver Diseases And Non Alcoholic Fatty Liver Disease","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"You are using a CAGE questionnaire to screen a patient for alcohol abuse. Which of these is not the correct expansion of this questionnaire?","o":["C - drink to calm your nerves","A - criticism over drinking annoys you","G - feel guilty about drinking","E - had a drink as eye opener"],"a":0,"e":"C stands for feeling the need to cut down your drinking in the CAGE questionnaire.\n\nThe CAGE questionnaire is used for identifying alcohol abuse. It can be expanded as below:\n\n• C - Have you ever felt you ought to cut down on your drinking?\n\n• A - Have people annoyed you by criticizing your drinking?\n\n• G - Have you ever felt guilty or bad about your drinking?\n\n• E - Have you ever had a drink first thing in the morning to steady your nerves or get rid of a hangover (eye-opener)?\n\nOne 'yes' response raises a suspicion of an alcohol use problem and more than one is a strong indication of abuse or dependence.","t":"Alcoholic Liver Diseases And Non Alcoholic Fatty Liver Disease","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"What parameters are used in calculating discriminant function?","o":["Prothrombin time and bilirubin","Bilirubin, ascites and hepatic encephalopathy","Prothrombin time, ascites and hepatic encephalopathy","Prothrombin, Bilirubin and creatinine"],"a":0,"e":"Discriminant function (DF) is calculated using prothrombin time and bilirubin level. It is also known as the Maddrey score and it assesses the severity and mortality risk in alcoholic hepatitis.\n\nDF = 4.6 X (the prolongation of the prothrombin time above control [seconds]) + serum bilirubin (mg/dL)\n\nDF ≥ 32 indicates severe disease and is an indication for starting glucocorticoids.","t":"Alcoholic Liver Diseases And Non Alcoholic Fatty Liver Disease","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A patient with alcoholic hepatitis has just been admitted to your ward. Prior to starting treatment, the senior doctor asks you to calculate his discriminant function. What is the cutoff value for determining severe disease?","o":["26","36","22","32"],"a":3,"e":"Severe alcoholic hepatitis is defined as discriminant function (DF) ≥ 32.\n\nSevere alcoholic hepatitis is defined as:\n\n• Discriminant Function (DF) ≥ 32\n\n• Model for End-stage Liver Disease (MELD) score ≥ 21\n\n• Presence of hepatic encephalopathy","t":"Alcoholic Liver Diseases And Non Alcoholic Fatty Liver Disease","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Which among the following is true about alcoholic cirrhosis?","o":["Hepatocyte hyperplasia","Macronodular cirrhosis","Mixed micro and macronodular cirrhosis","Absence of Mallory Denk bodies"],"a":2,"e":"Alcoholic cirrhosis is characterized by mixed micro-and macronodular. If a mixed pattern is not an option, the best choice is micronodular cirrhosis.\n\nCirrhosis is the late stage of alcoholic liver disease. It is characterised by extensive fibrosis. Alcoholic cirrhosis begins as micronodular cirrhosis. With the cessation of alcohol use, larger nodules may form, resulting in a mixed micronodular and macronodular pattern.\n\nThe various conditions in which cirrhosis is seen are listed below:\n\nMacronodular cirrhosis\nMicronodular cirrhosis\n\nChronic viral hepatitis\n\nα1 Antitrypsin deficiency\n\nDrugs & toxins\n\nHereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia\n\nCryptogenic cirrhosis\n\nWilsons disease (Micro & Macro nodular >> Macronodular)\n\nAlcohol\n\nPrimary biliary cirrhosis\n\nLarge bile duct obstruction\n\nHemochromatosis\n\nIntestinal bypass\n\nCystic fibrosis\n\nIndian childhood cirrhosis\n\n• Macronodular cirrhosis- Parenchymal nodules >3 mm\n\n• Micronodular cirrhosis- Parenchymal nodules <3 mm","t":"Alcoholic Liver Diseases And Non Alcoholic Fatty Liver Disease","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Prednisolone therapy for alcoholic hepatitis can be started in a patient with all of the following except:","o":["Glasgow score ≥9","Serum creatinine ≥1 mg/dL","Spontaneous hepatic encephalopathy","SOFA score &gt;2"],"a":3,"e":"SOFA score >2 indicates sepsis and glucocorticoids are avoided in septic patients.\n\nGlucocorticoids are indicated in severe alcoholic hepatitis which is defined as:\n\n• Discriminant Function (DF) ≥ 32\n\n• Model for End-stage Liver Disease (MELD) score ≥ 21\n\n• Glasgow alcoholic hepatitis score ≥ 9\n\n• Presence of hepatic encephalopathy\n\nPrednisolone is given at a dose of 32 mg orally daily for 4 weeks and then slowly tapered off.\n\nGlucocorticoid therapy is avoided in:\n\n• Active GI bleeding requiring blood transfusions\n\n• Active infection requiring antibiotic treatment\n\n• Preexisting chronic renal disease with a serum creatinine level greater than 2 mg/dL\n\n• Pancreatitis\n\n• Obvious contraindications to glucocorticoid therapy","t":"Alcoholic Liver Diseases And Non Alcoholic Fatty Liver Disease","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A 34-year-old man presents with yellowish discoloration of the skin, abdominal pain, and fever. He reports consuming one-quarter of whisky every day for the past 10 years. He is a known case of chronic kidney disease. His lab reports are given below. Which of the following is used in his treatment?\n\nAST- 170 IU/L\n\nALT- 64 IU/L\n\nTotal bilirubin- 11 mg/dL\n\nGGT- 478 U/L\n\nSerum creatinine- 3.5 mg/dL\n\nProthrombin time - 16 secs ( Control - 11 secs)","o":["Nutritional support alone","Prednisolone","Betamethasone","Pentoxifylline"],"a":3,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of severe alcoholic hepatitis. Since the patient is a known case of chronic kidney disease, pentoxifylline is used in his treatment.\n\nSevere alcoholic hepatitis is defined by a discriminant function ≥ 32. In the given scenario,\n\nDF = 4.6 X (the prolongation of the prothrombin time above control [seconds]) + serum bilirubin (mg/dL)\n\n = 4.6 x (16-11) + 11 = 34.\n\nThus, the patient cannot be managed by nutritional support alone.\n\nPentoxifylline is a TNF alpha inhibitor used either by itself or along with glucocorticoids in the treatment of severe alcoholic hepatitis. It is preferred in patients with kidney diseases and conditions where glucocorticoids are contraindicated.\n\nGlucocorticoid therapy is avoided in:\n\n• Active GI bleeding requiring blood transfusions\n\n• Active infection requiring antibiotic treatment\n\n• Preexisting chronic renal disease with a serum creatinine level greater than 2 mg/dL\n\n• Pancreatitis\n\n• Obvious contraindications to glucocorticoid therapy","t":"Alcoholic Liver Diseases And Non Alcoholic Fatty Liver Disease","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements is not true about nonalcoholic fatty liver disease?","o":["Results from fat accumulation in more than 5% of hepatocytes","Strongly associated with obesity and insulin resistance","Associated with reduced serum ferritin levels","Diagnosis of NASH requires presence of inflammation"],"a":2,"e":"Nonalcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD) is associated with increased levels of serum ferritin.\n\nNonalcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD) is defined as hepatic steatosis in the absence of any secondary cause for fat accumulation. Histologically, this requires fat accumulation in more than 5% of hepatocytes and it can be divided into:\n\n• Nonalcoholic fatty liver (NAFL)- steatosis without inflammation\n\n• Nonalcoholic steatohepatitis (NASH) - steatosis with inflammation\n\nNAFLD is strongly associated with obesity and insulin resistance. Its diagnosis requires demonstration of hepatic steatosis and exclusion of significant alcohol consumption and other causes of hepatic fat accumulation.","t":"Alcoholic Liver Diseases And Non Alcoholic Fatty Liver Disease","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A non-diabetic patient has been diagnosed with nonalcoholic steatohepatitis (NASH). Which of the following is the first-line therapy for it?","o":["Vitamin A","Vitamin E","Betaine","Ursodeoxycholic acid"],"a":1,"e":"The first-line therapy for nondiabetic patients with nonalcoholic steatohepatitis(NASH) is vitamin E.\n\nManagement of NAFLD includes:\n\n• Vitamin E - first-line therapy in non-diabetics\n\n• Insulin sensitizers- first-line therapy in diabetics\n\n• Immunizations for Hepatitis A and B\n\n• Weight loss\n\n• Abstinence from alcohol\n\nNote: The FDA in 2024 has approved Resmetirom, a thyroid hormone receptor-beta (THR-beta) agonist for noncirrhotic NASH with moderate to advanced fibrosis.","t":"Alcoholic Liver Diseases And Non Alcoholic Fatty Liver Disease","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Which among the following hepatitis viruses is a DNA virus?","o":["Hepatitis A","Hepatitis B","Hepatitis C","Hepatitis D"],"a":1,"e":"Hepatitis B is a DNA virus.\n\nThe hepatotropic viruses include:\n\nHepatitis virus\nFamily\nGenetic material\n\nA\nPicorna virus\nRNA\n\nB\nHepadna virus\nDNA\n\nC\nFlavivirus\nRNA\n\nD\nDeltavirus\nRNA\n\nE\nHepeviridae\nRNA","t":"Viral Hepatitis","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is the most common route of spread of hepatitis E virus?","o":["Sexual transmission","Blood transfusion","Feco-oral","IV drug use"],"a":2,"e":"The most common route of the spread of hepatitis E is feco-oral.\n\nHepatitis E virus is the most common cause of acute hepatitis in adults, especially in endemic countries like India. The most common cause of acute hepatitis in children is the hepatitis A virus.\n\nRoutes of transmission of hepatitis viruses:\n\nHepatitis\nVertical route\nNeedle\nBlood transfusion\nSexual\nFeco-oral\n\nHep B\n\nHBeAg positive mother >90%\n\nHBsAg with Anti-HBe mother 10-15%\n\n6-30%\n\n1 in 2 lakh\n\n(Most common blood transfusion-related virus).\n\nVariable\n -\n\nHep C\n5%\n1.8-6%\n1 in 18 lakh\n5%\n -\n\nHep A\nNil\nNil\nRare\nRare\nMost common\n\nHep E\nRare\nNil\nNil\nNil\nMost common","t":"Viral Hepatitis","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements is true regarding hepatitis E?","o":["Can be fatal in pregnant women","Can cause chronic hepatitis in immunocompetent","Most common route of spread is vertical transmission","Has a carrier state"],"a":0,"e":"Fatality due to Hepatitis E is highest in pregnant women. In the general population, hepatitis E can lead to fatal fulminant hepatitis in only 1–2% of cases. However, in pregnant women, this is raised to 20% of cases.\n\nHEV does not have a carrier state and can cause chronic hepatitis only in immunocompromised individuals. Its most common route of spread is feco-oral.\n\nNote - HEV is also known as E-NANB, i.e., Enterically transmitted Non-A Non-B hepatitis virus.It often appears in epidemics; hence, it is also called epidemic NANB.","t":"Viral Hepatitis","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is associated with the highest risk of developing fulminant hepatitis?","o":["Hepatitis A","Hepatitis B","Hepatitis E","Hepatitis D"],"a":3,"e":"Acute viral hepatitis caused by the hepatitis D virus is associated with the highest risk of developing fulminant hepatitis.\n\nHowever, fulminant hepatitis is most commonly caused by hepatitis E, especially in endemic countries such as India. This is due to the greater prevalence of hepatitis E infections compared to hepatitis D.","t":"Viral Hepatitis","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A pregnant woman comes to your clinic for her first antenatal checkup in her 3rd trimester. Laboratory workup reveals that she is HBsAg positive and HBeAg positive. What is the chance of vertical transmission of hepatitis B to the child?","o":["25-30%","50%","10-15%","&gt;90 %"],"a":3,"e":"The chance of vertical transmission of hepatitis B from a mother who is HBsAg positive and HBeAg positive is >90%. In contrast, when the mother is HBsAg positive but HBeAg negative or HBsAg positive with Anti Hbe, the risk of vertical transmission is low.\n\nTo prevent vertical transmission of HBV, HBsAg positive pregnant women with high HBV DNA titres should receive tenofovir prophylaxis from the 28th week of pregnancy until birth. Infants born to these women are given hepatitis B immunoglobulin along with the first dose of the hepatitis B vaccine.\n\nThe presence of HBeAg indicates active replication of HBV. Therefore, HBeAg is the qualitative marker of infectivity. HBV DNA titre is the quantitative marker of infectivity.\n\nRoutes of transmission of hepatitis viruses:\n\nHepatitis\nHep B\nHep C\nHep A\nHep E\n\nVertical route\n\nHBsAg and HBeAg positive mother >90%\n\nHBsAg positive with Anti HBe mother- 10-15%\n\n5%\nNil\nRare\n\nNeedle\n6-30%\n1.8-6 %\nNil\nNil\n\nBlood transfusion\n1 in 2 lakh\n(Most common blood transfusion-related virus)\n1 in 18 lakh\nRare\nNil\n\nSexual\nVariable\nYes\nRare\nNil\n\nFeco-oral\n-\n-\nMost common\n Most common","t":"Viral Hepatitis","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"What is the first marker to rise in a patient with acute hepatitis B?","o":["Anti-HBs","HBsAg","HBeAg","Anti-HBc"],"a":1,"e":"The first virologic marker to rise in acute hepatitis B is HBsAg i.e., Hepatitis B surface antigen. It is detectable in serum within 1–12 weeks of infection. Usually, HBsAg becomes undetectable 1–2 months after the onset of jaundice. If it persists beyond 6 months, it indicates chronic hepatitis B.\n\nHowever, very rarely, patients may have an HBsAg negative status even during active infection due to infection with escape mutant HBV. These escape mutants have mutations within the S gene and they are known to be responsible for occult hepatitis B infections.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/51c2de547f714f7ab327d295ec860e0fx1279x1466.JPEG"],"t":"Viral Hepatitis","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Which serological marker can be used to diagnose acute hepatitis B in the window period?","o":["Anti HBs","HBsAg","HBeAg","Anti HBc"],"a":3,"e":"The diagnosis of acute hepatitis B in the window period or serological gap can be made by the detection of the anti-HBc antibody.\n\nThe window period is the period between the disappearance of HBsAg to anti-HBs seroconversion. During this window period, anti-HBc is the only detectable antibody.","t":"Viral Hepatitis","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A 35-year-old male patient presented with right upper quadrant abdominal pain and jaundice. His lab reports are given below. What is the likely causative organism for his condition?\n\nTotal bilirubin - 20 mg/dL\n\nAST - 450 IU/L\n\nALT - 400 IU/L\n\nHBsAg +ve\n\nHBeAg -ve\n\nHBV DNA - 50000 IU/mL","o":["HBV surface mutant","HBV precore mutant","HBV escape mutatnt","Vaccine-derived HBV"],"a":1,"e":"Increased viral load and negative HBeAg suggest a pre-core mutant variety of hepatitis B virus. These strains have mutations in the pre-core region of the C gene, leading to a stop codon that prevents HBeAg synthesis. These strains can be detected in the serum by HBV DNA analysis.\n\nInfection with hepatitis B mutants is difficult to treat and causes infections of prolonged duration. They have a higher risk of progressing to liver cirrhosis.\n\nTypes of hepatitis B mutants are:\n\nType\nFeature\n\nPre-core mutants\n\nDefect in pre-core region C\n\nNo HBeAg\n\nEscape mutants\n\nMutation in S region\n\nHBsAg resists neutralisation by anti-HBs\n\nSeen in\n\n• Infants born to HBeAg positive mothers\n\n• Liver transplant recipient\n\n• Associated with passive and active immunization\n\nYMDD mutation\n\nMutation in P region i.e. YMDD locus which codes for reverse transcriptase\n\nSeen in patients on lamivudine therapy","t":"Viral Hepatitis","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"The lab report of a 40-year-old man is shown below. What is the most likely cause for this serological picture?\n\nHBsAg -ve\n\nAnti-HBs -ve\n\nIgG anti-HBc +ve","o":["Recovering from hepatitis B","Hepatitis B in the remote past","Immunization with hepatitis B vaccine","Window period of HBV infection"],"a":1,"e":"The given lab findings are most likely to be in patients who had hepatitis B in the remote past i.e., patients in late convalescence period. In such conditions, the patient will have positive IgG anti-HBc and negative HBsAg.","t":"Viral Hepatitis","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"An intern sustained a needle-prick injury while establishing an IV line on a patient in the casualty. The patient was subsequently found to be HBsAg positive. The presence of which of the following markers in the intern would reliably indicate an early infection?","o":["IgM anti HBsAg","IgM anti HBc","HBsAg","HBeAg"],"a":1,"e":"The most reliable indicator of recent HBV infection is IgM anti-HBc in serum. However, the first virologic marker to rise in acute hepatitis B is HBsAg i.e. surface antigen.\n\nHBsAg is detectable in serum within 1–12 weeks of infection. Anti-HBc is demonstrable in the serum about 1-2 weeks after this, and may sometimes persist in the serum even longer than an anti-HBs antibody. IgM anti-HBcAg is seen early in the infection and persists for about 6 months. It indicates a recent infection. IgG anti-HBcAg antibody is seen later i.e. after 6 months and indicates remote infection.\n\nHBcAg is not detectable in the serum of patients with HBV infection as it is present intracellularly.\n\nNote: The risk of transmission of HBV from percutaneous routes (eg., needle prick) is about 6-30% and it is about 100 times more infectious than HIV.","t":"Viral Hepatitis","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"During your casualty rotation, you accidentally injure yourself while suturing a patient. You had taken the full schedule of hepatitis B vaccination during your first year of medical school. Which of the following markers will you get measured to determine the need for HBIG administration?","o":["Anti HBeAg","IgM Anti HBc","IgG Anti HBc","Anti HBsAg"],"a":3,"e":"The need for administration of hepatitis B hyperimmune globulin (HBIG) in a previously vaccinated individual can be determined based on anti-HBsAg levels. It is the marker for determining the efficacy of hepatitis B vaccination.\n\nThe complete hepatitis B vaccine schedule consists of three intramuscular doses at 0, 1, and 6 months.\n\nPost Exposure Prophylaxis:\n\nThe prophylaxis can be done using the hepatitis B vaccine or hepatitis B immunoglobulin (HBIG).\n\nVaccination status of the exposed person\nRecommended prophylaxis\n\nUnvaccinated\nHBIG (0.06 mL/kg) and initiate hepatitis B vaccine series\n\nKnown responder to previous vaccination (anti-HBs titer>10 mIU/ml)\nNo action required\n\nNon-responder to previous vaccination (anti-HBs titre <10 mIU/ml)\nHBIG: 2 doses (1 month apart) OR HBIG: 1 dose + initiate hepatitis B revaccination.","t":"Viral Hepatitis","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following markers can be used to differentiate between hepatitis D superinfection and coinfection?","o":["IgM anti-HDV","IgM anti-HBc","HBV DNA","HBsAg"],"a":1,"e":"IgM anti-HBc can be used to differentiate between hepatitis D coinfection and superinfection in patients with HBV infection.\n\nCo-infection:\n\n• Simultaneous infection with both HBV and HDV\n\n• IgM anti-HBc detectable in co-infection as its presence indicates recent infection\n\nSuperinfection:\n\n• HDV infection of an individual chronically infected with HBV\n\n• IgM anti-HBc will not be detectable\n\n• IgG anti-HBc will be present","t":"Viral Hepatitis","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A male patient presents with complaints of jaundice, loss of appetite, and weight loss for the past several months. After a full laboratory workup, a liver biopsy was performed which appeared as shown in the given image. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Acute hepatitis A","Chronic hepatitis B","Chronic hepatitis C","Chronic hepatitis A"],"a":1,"e":"The given histopathological image shows ground-glass hepatocytes which are a hallmark of chronic hepatitis B infection.\n\nThese ground-glass hepatocytes are liver parenchymal cells that have an eosinophilic, granular, and glassy appearance on light microscopy. This appearance is due to the accumulation of HBsAg (hepatitis B surface antigen) in the smooth endoplasmic reticulum.\n\nLiver biopsy findings in viral hepatitis:\n\nVirus\nLiver Biopsy Findings\n\nHepatitis A\nHepatocyte swelling, monocyte infiltration, Councilman bodies\n\nHepatitis B\nGround glass appearance\n\nHepatitis C\nLymphoid aggregates with a focal area of macrovesicular steatosis\n\nHepatitis D\nSimilar to HBV\n\nHepatitis E\nPatchy necrosis","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7a1becbf69474b7f819e616c722e43f4.JPEG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fdb81691f8794ee6b43612d6a6db1725x1280x1157.JPEG"],"t":"Viral Hepatitis","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following pairs of viral hepatitis and its management is incorrectly matched?","o":["Acute viral hepatitis E - Supportive care","Acute viral hepatitis B - Antiviral therapy","Acute viral hepatitis A - Supportive care","Acute viral hepatitis C - Antiviral therapy"],"a":1,"e":"Antiviral therapy is not indicated in acute hepatitis B infection. It is only indicated in chronic hepatitis B.\n\nThe majority of acute hepatitis (A, B, and E) infections are self-limiting, resulting in viral elimination and complete healing within 6 months. Hence, they need only supportive management. Only 5% of acute hepatitis infections become chronic.\n\nIn acute hepatitis C infection, recovery is rare, and the risk of progression to chronic hepatitis is high(∼85%). Thus antiviral therapy is started irrespective of the disease stage.","t":"Viral Hepatitis","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is an indication for antiviral therapy in chronic hepatitis B infection?","o":["IgG anti-HBc positive","HbeAg negative with HBV DNA &lt;2000 IU/L","ALT &gt; 2 times upper limit of normal","Normal Fibroscan report with ALT &lt; 2 ULN"],"a":2,"e":"ALT > 2 times upper limit of normal is an indication for antiviral therapy in chronic hepatitis B infection.\n\nThe decision to initiate antiviral therapy is primarily based on the presence or absence of cirrhosis, and parameters such as HBeAg, alanine aminotransferase (ALT) level, and HBV DNA level.\n\nThe following are the indications for starting antivirals in chronic hepatitis B:\n\n• Patients without cirrhosis\n\n• HBeAg + with HBV DNA >20,000 IU/mL and ALT level >2 times the upper limit of normal\n\n• HBeAg -ve with HBV DNA >2000 IU/mL and ALT level >2 times the upper limit of normal\n\n• Patients with compensated cirrhosis and an HBV DNA >2000 IU/mL\n\n• Patients developing acute liver failure or decompensated cirrhosis\n\nRecommended first-line antiviral drugs:\n\n• Entecavir\n\n• Tenofovir alafenamide\n\n• Tenofovir disoproxil fumarate\n\nPegylated interferon alfa can also be used. Treatment is aimed at HBeAg seroconversion, but most patients may need to continue antiviral therapy indefinitely.","t":"Viral Hepatitis","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A 32-year-old man presented with complaints of fatigue for the past 1 month. His lab reports are given below. What is the most appropriate next step in the management of this patient?\n\nTotal bilirubin - 1.2 mg/dL\n\nALT - 30 IU/L\n\nHBsAg - positive\n\nHBeAg - negative\n\nHBV DNA - 1400 IU/mL","o":["Tenofovir","Lamivudine and IFN","Entecavir","Serial monitoring"],"a":3,"e":"HBeAg negative and HBV DNA <2000 IU/mL is suggestive of HBV carrier. Serial monitoring of both biochemical and virological parameters is done for such patients.\n\nIndications for starting antiviral therapy in chronic hepatitis B are:\n\n• Patients without cirrhosis\n\n• HBeAg + with HBV DNA >20,000 IU/mL and ALT level >2 times the upper limit of normal\n\n• HBeAg -ve with HBV DNA >2000 IU/mL and ALT level >2 times the upper limit of normal\n\n• Patients with compensated cirrhosis and an HBV DNA >2000 IU/mL\n\n• Patients developing acute liver failure or decompensated cirrhosis","t":"Viral Hepatitis","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is recommended as first-line therapy in a patient with chronic hepatitis B?","o":["Pegylated interferon","Telbivudine","Lamivudine","Entecavir"],"a":3,"e":"First-line drugs used for the treatment of chronic hepatitis B include entecavir and tenofovir.\n\nSecond-line drugs for this condition include:\n\n• Lamivudine\n\n• Adefovir\n\n• Telbivudine\n\nPegylated interferon can also be used in its treatment.","t":"Viral Hepatitis","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"In which of the following populations should surveillance for hepatocellular carcinoma be done?\n\n1. HBV carriers with cirrhosis\n\n2. Asian male HBV carriers between ages of 30-40 years\n\n3. Asian female HBV carriers over age 50\n\n4. HBV carriers with a family history of HCC\n\n5. Patients with high serum levels of HBV DNA and ongoing liver injury","o":["1, 3, 4, 5","2, 3, 4","1, 2, 4, 5","1, 2, 3"],"a":0,"e":"Surveillance for hepatocellular carcinoma is recommended for Asian male HBV carriers over the age of 40 years.\n\nThe most common etiological factor for hepatocellular carcinoma in the world is the hepatitis B virus (HBV) infection. Surveillance programs have been implemented to screen for HCC in high-risk individuals because it is more cost-effective than the treatment of HCC.\n\nSurveillance for hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC) is recommended in:\n\n• HBV carriers with cirrhosis\n\n• Patients with high serum levels of HBV DNA and ongoing liver injury\n\n• HBV carriers born in Africa and over the age of 20\n\n• Asian male HBV carriers over age 40\n\n• Asian female HBV carriers over age 50\n\n• HBV carriers with a family history of HCC\n\nNote:\nThe CDC recently updated its guidelines for Hepatitis B screening. The report includes screening for all adults >18 years old using the triple-panel test (HBsAg, Anti-HBs, total anti-HBc) and expanded risk-based testing in certain high-risk populations.","t":"Viral Hepatitis","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A 35-year-old male comes to the OPD with intermittent jaundice for the past 2 months. You suspect acute viral hepatitis. Which of the following will you not evaluate in this patient?","o":["HBsAg","IgM anti HBc","Anti HCV","IgM anti HBe"],"a":3,"e":"IgM anti-HBe is not one of the markers that are evaluated in a suspected case of acute viral hepatitis.\n\nInvestigations done in suspected acute viral hepatitis are:\n\n• IgM anti-HAV - to detect acute hepatitis A infection which is the most common cause of acute hepatitis in children\n\n• HBsAg and IgM anti-HBc - to detect acute hepatitis B infection, where HbsAg is the first marker to rise and IgM anti-HBcAg is the most reliable marker of acute infection\n\n• Anti HCV - to evaluate for acute hepatitis C infection\n\n• IgM anti-HEV (rarely)- to detect acute hepatitis E infection which is one of the causes of acute viral hepatitis in adults.","t":"Viral Hepatitis","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following groups of individuals need not be screened for Hepatitis C infection?","o":["Patients with HIV infection","Patients with unexplained elevation of aminotransferase levels","Patients who have undergone long-term haemodialysis","Household contacts of patients with hepatitis C"],"a":3,"e":"HCV screening is not indicated for household contacts of persons with hepatitis C. However, hepatitis B virus screening is recommended in household contacts of HBV-infected persons.\n\nIndications of HCV infection screening:\n\n• Persons with a history of using injection drugs\n\n• Persons with HIV infection\n\n• Long term hemodialysis\n\n• Persons with an unexplained elevation of aminotransferases\n\n• Recipient of blood or organs from a donor found to be positive for hepatitis C\n\n• Children born to women with Hepatitis C\n\n• Health care, public safety, and emergency medical personnel following needle injury or mucosal exposure to HCV contaminated blood\n\n• Sexual partners of persons with Hepatitis C","t":"Viral Hepatitis","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is unlikely to be seen as an extrahepatic manifestation in patients with hepatitis C infection?","o":["Mixed cryoglobulinemia","Lichen planus","Porphyria cutanea tarda","Polyarteritis nodosa"],"a":3,"e":"Polyarteritis nodosa is not characteristically associated with hepatitis C infection. However, it is a common extrahepatic manifestation of hepatitis B viral infection.","t":"Viral Hepatitis","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A 35-year-old man who presented with weight loss and abdominal discomfort is diagnosed with chronic hepatitis C . All the following factors can determine the progression of liver disease in this patient except:","o":["Age","Duration of infection","HCV RNA level","Body mass index"],"a":2,"e":"While HCV RNA is considered the gold standard for establishing a diagnosis of hepatitis C, it has no role in determining the progression of liver disease in patients with chronic hepatitis C.\n\nThe likelihood of progression of liver disease in patients with chronic hepatitis C is increased by:\n\n• Longer duration of infection - most important determinant\n\n• Older age\n\n• Obesity - patients with a high body mass index are at an increased risk of developing fibrosis\n\n• Diabetes mellitus and insulin resistance\n\n• Concomitant liver disorder (eg., alcoholic liver disease, chronic hepatitis B, hemochromatosis)\n\n• Concomitant HIV infection.\n\nThe best prognostic indicator in chronic hepatitis C is the rate of fibrosis and baseline inflammation seen in liver histology.","t":"Viral Hepatitis","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A 47-year-old man presented with generalized weakness, fatigue, and fever. His lab investigations are given below. What is the preferred treatment regimen for this patient?\n\nALT - 35 IU/L\n\nHCV - detectable","o":["Velpatasvir and sofosbuvir","Ribavirin and lamivudine","Telaprevir and simeprevir","α-interferon and dasabuvir"],"a":0,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of hepatitis C for which the preferred treatment regimen is velpatasvir 100 mg and sofosbuvir 400 mg.\n\nThe goal of treatment is a sustained virologic response (SVR), which is defined as the absence of a virus in the blood (undetectable HCV RNA), 12 weeks after completion of treatment. This can be achieved by the use of pan-genotypic directly acting antiviral drugs, irrespective of the disease stage.","t":"Viral Hepatitis","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not a non-structural protein 5A inhibitor used in the treatment of patients with hepatitis C infection?","o":["Sofosbuvir","Daclatasvir","Ledipasvir","Velpatasvir"],"a":0,"e":"Sofosbuvir is not an NS5A inhibitor but is an NS5B nucleoside/nucleotide polymerase inhibitor.\n\nThe directly acting antivirals used in the treatment of Hepatitis C infection include:","t":"Viral Hepatitis","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Autoimmune hepatitis is suspected in a woman presenting with fatigue, pruritus, and arthralgia. She has a past history of Hashimoto's thyroiditis. Which of the following is not part of the simplified scoring system for the given condition?","o":["ANA","Typical histological features","Elevated IgG","Presence of viral markers"],"a":3,"e":"Presence of viral markers is not a part of the simplified scoring system for diagnosis of autoimmune hepatitis.\n\nThe simplified scoring system includes four variables. They are:\n\n1. Autoantibodies\n\n2. Elevated serum IgG level\n\n3. Typical compatible histologic features\n\n4. Absence of viral hepatitis markers\n\nThe clinical presentation in autoimmune hepatitis is variable and shares features with acute and chronic hepatitis of other aetiologies. They usually present with an insidious onset of non-specific symptoms such as fatigue, anorexia and eventually jaundice. It can then progress to extensive liver necrosis and liver failure.\n\nAutoimmune hepatitis is associated with several autoimmune diseases such as:\n\n• Rheumatoid arthritis\n\n• Systemic lupus erythematosus\n\n• Thyroiditis and Grave's disease\n\n• Haemolytic anemia","t":"Autoimmune Disorders Of Hepatobiliary System","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following drugs is associated with Autoimmune hepatitis (AIH)?","o":["Minocycline","5 Aminosalicylic acid","Piroxicam","Nitroglycerine"],"a":0,"e":"Minocycline is associated with the development of drug-induced AIH.\n\nOther drugs associated with AIH include nitrofurantoin, methyldopa, diclofenac, and possibly statins.","t":"Autoimmune Disorders Of Hepatobiliary System","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A 20-year-old lady is suspected to have autoimmune hepatitis. Which of the following antibodies is unlikely to be present in her serum?","o":["Cytoplasmic antineutrophil cytoplasmic antibodies (cANCA)","Antinuclear antibody (ANA)","Smooth muscle antibody(SMA)","Anti-soluble liver antigen (SLA)"],"a":0,"e":"cANCA is not seen in autoimmune hepatitis. It is positive in Wegner's granulomatosis. pANCA is what is seen in autoimmune hepatitis.\n\nThere are 2 types of autoimmune hepatitis:\n\nType I (classic) autoimmune hepatitis is often seen in young women. It is linked to HLA-DR3 or HLA-DR4. It responds well to steroids and has a better prognosis. The autoantibodies that are seen in type I are:\n\n• ANA - antinuclear antibodies\n\n• ASMA - anti-smooth muscle actin\n\n• pANCA - perinuclear anti-neutrophil cytoplasmic\n\n• anti-SLA - anti-soluble liver antigen\n\nType II autoimmune hepatitis is often seen in children. It is linked to HLA-DRB1 or HLA-DQB1. It usually leads to fibrosis, cirrhosis and liver failure. The autoantibodies that are seen in type II are:\n\n• Anti-LKM 1 - anti-liver kidney microsomes\n\n• Anti-liver cytosol 1","t":"Autoimmune Disorders Of Hepatobiliary System","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Match the following:\n\nA. Anti-LKM 1\ni. Autoimmune hepatitis ll\n\nB. Anti-SLA\nii. Drug-induced hepatitis\n\nC. Anti-LKM 2\n\niii. Autoimmune hepatitis l\n\nD. Anti-LKM 3\niv. Chronic hepatitis D","o":["A-i , B-iii , C-ii , D-iv","A-iv , B-ii , C-iii , D-i","A-ii , B-iii , C-i , D-iv","A-i , B-iii , C-iv , D-ii"],"a":0,"e":"• Anti LKM 1- Autoimmune hepatitis II\n\n• Anti SLA - Autoimmune hepatitis I\n\n• Anti LKM 2 - Drug-induced hepatitis\n\n• Anti LKM 3 - Chronic hepatitis D","t":"Autoimmune Disorders Of Hepatobiliary System","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A 24-year-old woman presenting with abdominal pain is diagnosed with autoimmune hepatitis. The mainstay of management in this patient is:","o":["Glucocorticoids","Azathioprine","Mycophenolate mofetil","Methotrexate"],"a":0,"e":"The mainstay of management in autoimmune hepatitis is glucocorticoid therapy.\n\nGlucocorticoids are started at high doses and tapered down to a low maintenance dose. The dose of glucocorticoids is often reduced by combining with azathioprine.\n\nThe benefits of glucocorticoid + azathioprine combination therapy are:\n\n• Reduction in the duration of therapy.\n\n• Avoid life-threatening complications of steroids (e.g., cushingoid features, hypertension, diabetes, osteoporosis).","t":"Autoimmune Disorders Of Hepatobiliary System","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A 23-year-old female patient with a diagnosis of autoimmune hepatitis did not respond well to glucocorticoids. Which among the following is false regarding her further management options?","o":["Increase the dose of glucocorticoids","Glucocorticoids with high dose azathioprine","Switch to Mycophenolate mofetil","Liver transplant is contraindicated"],"a":3,"e":"Liver transplant is not contraindicated in refractory autoimmune hepatitis even though the risk of recurrence is high.\n\nThe medical management of refractory autoimmune hepatitis include:\n\n• High dose glucocorticoids\n\n• Glucocorticoids + high dose azathioprine\n\n• Addition of cyclosporine, tacrolimus, or mycophenolate mofetil.","t":"Autoimmune Disorders Of Hepatobiliary System","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A young lady has been on treatment for autoimmune hepatitis for 2 weeks, she has now come for follow-up investigations. Which of the following suggests a poor prognosis in this patient?","o":["Bridging necrosis","Decreased globulins","Increased serum bilirubin","Increased serum albumin"],"a":2,"e":"Persistently increased serum bilirubin after 2 weeks of therapy suggests a poor prognosis.\n\nEarly signs of poor prognosis include:\n\n• Failure of serum bilirubin to reduce after 2 weeks of therapy.\n\nEarly signs of good prognosis include:\n\n• An increase in serum albumin.\n\n• A decrease in serum bilirubin, globulins and aminotransferases.\n\n• Improvement of symptoms.\n\nHistopathologic improvement can often take months to years.","t":"Autoimmune Disorders Of Hepatobiliary System","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"The most common symptom of primary biliary cholangitis (PBC) is","o":["Pruritus","Fatigue","Jaundice","Right upper quadrant pain"],"a":1,"e":"The most common symptom of primary biliary cholangitis(PBC) is fatigue.\n\nPrimary biliary cholangitis(PBC) is a granulomatous autoimmune condition characterized by the destruction of interlobular hepatic bile ducts.\n\nMost patients with PBC are asymptomatic at the time of the diagnosis. Fatigue and pruritus are both common presenting symptoms. However, the most common symptom is fatigue.\n\nOther symptoms and signs are -\n\n• Right upper quadrant pain\n\n• Hyperpigmentation\n\n• Xanthomas and xanthelasma\n\n• Jaundice\n\n• Hepatosplenomegaly.","t":"Autoimmune Disorders Of Hepatobiliary System","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A 44-year-old woman with rheumatoid arthritis is now diagnosed with primary biliary cholangitis. Which of the following is false regarding her lab values?","o":["Positive Anti-LKM- 2 antibodies","Elevated alkaline phosphatase (ALP)","Elevated gamma-glutamyl transferase (GGT)","Positive antimitochondrial antibodies"],"a":0,"e":"Anti-LKM 2 antibodies are not seen in primary biliary cholangitis. They are seen in drug-induced hepatitis.\n\nThe laboratory findings of primary biliary cholangitis include :\n\n• Liver function tests\n\n• Elevated ALP\n\n• Elevated GGT and 5'- nucleotidase\n\n• Elevated ALT and AST\n\n• Serology -\n\n• Antimitochondrial antibodies - positive in 95% of patients. These are mostly directed against the pyruvate dehydrogenase complex.\n\n• Antinuclear antibodies\n\n• Elevated HDL, LDL, and VLDL\n\n• Elevated serum IgM\n\nHyperbilirubinemia is seen once cirrhosis has developed.","t":"Autoimmune Disorders Of Hepatobiliary System","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A patient with a diagnosis of primary biliary cholangitis has been taking ursodeoxycholic acid (UDCA) twice daily. She now presents with ascites and variceal bleeding. What is the most appropriate treatment for this patient?","o":["Initiating methotrexate","Continuing ursodeoxycholic acid","Add obeticholic acid to the treatment","Consider liver transplantation"],"a":3,"e":"The presence of ascites and variceal bleeding suggest decompensated liver disease and the appropriate treatment is liver transplantation.\n\nThe first-line drug in primary biliary cholangitis is ursodeoxycholic acid (UDCA). It only slows the rate of progression of the disease. It does not provide any symptomatic benefit or cure the disease.\n\nObeticholic acid is another drug used in PBC as monotherapy or in combination with UDCA. It is a farnesoid X receptor agonist.\n\nLiver transplantation is considered when there are signs of decompensation or portal hypertension such as ascites, variceal bleeding, encephalopathy, coagulopathy, or malnutrition.","t":"Autoimmune Disorders Of Hepatobiliary System","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A middle-aged woman presented with fatigue, pruritis, and xanthomas over the elbows. A biopsy of her bile duct revealed florid duct lesions. What is the diagnosis?","o":["Klatskin tumor","Primary sclerosing cholangitis (PSC)","Primary biliary cholangitis (PBC)","Intrahepatic cholestasis"],"a":2,"e":"Florid duct lesion is characteristic of primary biliary cholangitis (PBC). It is the lymphocytes and plasma cells all centered around bile ducts.\n\nPrimary sclerosing cholangitis has the characteristic \"onion-skin\" concentric fibrosis surrounding the bile ducts.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9ff3c6f5666749f4a2d2d4e8016ad8ecx400x600.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e75392d5fce54c5d8c8ad5fd37671675x944x746.PNG"],"t":"Autoimmune Disorders Of Hepatobiliary System","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements about primary sclerosing cholangitis is false?","o":["Affects both intra and extrahepatic bile ducts","Liver biopsy definitively establishes the diagnosis","more common in males","Leads to development of cholangiocarcinoma"],"a":1,"e":"Liver biopsy does not establish the diagnosis in primary sclerosing cholangitis. The diagnosis requires imaging with MRCP.\n\nPrimary sclerosing cholangitis (PSC) is a chronic progressive disorder characterized by inflammation and fibrosis of intra and extrahepatic biliary ducts resulting in strictures and cholestasis. The cause is unknown. Most patients have underlying ulcerative colitis. It has an association with HLA DRw52. Patients present with symptoms of cholestasis such as fatigue and pruritus.\n\nThe laboratory findings include:\n\n• Liver function tests - Elevated ALP and aminotransferases.\n\n• Increased serum IgM levels\n\n• Positive p-ANCA\n\nMRCP is the investigation of choice. It shows multiple strictures in intra and extrahepatic bile ducts. Liver biopsy reveals a concentric \"onion skin\" fibrosis. This is however not pathognomic of PSC.\n\nThere is no definitive treatment. It involves the administration of UDCA and liver transplantation.\n\nPSC can lead to the development of cholangiocarcinoma\n\nThe image below shows onion-skin fibrosis in PSC.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4d9dd76ef1104b139a9a0c337bef4888x754x600.PNG"],"t":"Autoimmune Disorders Of Hepatobiliary System","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"In acute liver failure, encephalopathy occurs within ______ of the development of clinical symptoms.","o":["6 months","6 weeks","4 months","4 weeks"],"a":3,"e":"In acute liver failure, encephalopathy occurs within 4 weeks of the development of the clinical symptoms.\n\nAcute liver failure is the rapid worsening of the liver function in patients without cirrhosis or any preexisting liver disease. The patient initially suffers from an acute injury to the liver by viruses, drugs, etc. Following this, the function of the liver rapidly worsens and patient develops coagulopathy and encephalopathy within 4 weeks of the development of jaundice or other clinical symptoms.","t":"Acute Liver Failure And Liver Transplantation","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"What is the most common cause of acute liver failure in India?","o":["Alcohol","Viral infection","Drug induced","Malaria"],"a":1,"e":"The most common cause of acute liver failure (ALF) in India is a viral infection. Among the viruses, hepatitis E virus is the most common cause of acute liver failure.\n\nIn developed countries, acetaminophen overdose is the most common cause of acute liver failure.","t":"Acute Liver Failure And Liver Transplantation","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A small child was rushed to the ED by his parents. He had accidentally consumed wild mushrooms while they were trekking. Examination showed features of encephalopathy and his liver function tests were deranged. Which of the following is least likely to be seen in this condition?","o":["Hyperglycemia","Hypomagnesemia","Hypokalemia","Hyponatremia"],"a":0,"e":"The given scenario is suggestive of Amanita phalloides (wild mushrooms) toxicity causing acute liver failure. Hypoglycemia is seen in acute liver failure and not hyperglycemia. It occurs due to impaired gluconeogenesis and glycogenolysis.\n\nFeatures of acute hepatic failure are:\n\n• Hepatic encephalopathy and cerebral edema- There are increased levels of ammonia (neurotoxin) in the body as a result of decreased elimination by the liver.\n\n• Coagulopathy- There is a decrease in the synthesis of coagulation factors leading to the elevated prothrombin time and INR.\n\n• Increased risk of infections- It occurs as a result of decreased reticuloendothelial activity, decreased synthesis of complements, acute phase reactants, etc.\n\n• Renal failure- This is more commonly seen in drug/toxin-induced hepatic failure.\n\n• Metabolic complications like hypoglycemia, hyponatremia, hypokalemia, hypophosphatemia, hypomagnesemia.","t":"Acute Liver Failure And Liver Transplantation","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A patient is said to have hyperacute liver failure if encephalopathy occurs within?","o":["3 days","7 days","10 days","14 days"],"a":1,"e":"Hyperacute liver failure is defined as the development of encephalopathy within 7 days of the appearance of symptoms.\n\nAcute liver failure can be subcategorized into three groups depending on the time interval between the onset of jaundice and encephalopathy.\n\n• Hyperacute- encephalopathy develops within 7 days or less than 7 days\n\n• Acute- encephalopathy develops between 8-28 days\n\n• Subacute- encephalopathy develops between 28 days to 6 months\n\nPatients with hyperacute liver failure are more likely to develop coagulopathy and cerebral edema, but most of the patients will recover without the need for liver transplantation. On the other end, patients with subacute liver failure have less severe coagulopathy and cerebral edema, but most of them will need liver transplantation.","t":"Acute Liver Failure And Liver Transplantation","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A young patient with Hepatitis E virus infection develops acute liver failure. Which of the following scores can be used in assessing the prognosis of this patient?","o":["Pitt score","Child Pugh score","MELD score","MDF score"],"a":2,"e":"In a patient with acute liver failure, MELD score and King's College Hospital criteria can be used to assess the prognosis.\n\nOption A: Pitt score is used used to predict mortality in patients having bloodstream infections.\n\nOption B: The child-Pugh score is used to assess the severity of cirrhosis.\n\nOption D: Maddrey's Discriminant Function (MDF) score suggests which patients with alcoholic hepatitis may have a poor prognosis and benefit from steroid administration.","t":"Acute Liver Failure And Liver Transplantation","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A young patient is admitted to the hospital after ingestion of a large number of acetaminophen tablets. To predict the prognosis using King's College hospital criteria in this patient, which of the parameters are not used?","o":["Arterial pH","Prothrombin time","Serum creatinine","Serum bilirubin"],"a":3,"e":"Serum bilirubin is not included in King's College Hospital criteria in acute liver failure due to paracetamol overdose.\n\nThe King's College Hospital Criteria is a prognostic tool for patients with acute liver failure (ALF). The criteria is divided into acetaminophen and non-acetaminophen causes of liver failure.\n\nLiver transplantation is indicated in acetaminophen-induced ALF if there is a presence of\n\n• Either pH <7.3\n\n• Or all of the following\n\n• Prothrombin time >100 sec or INR >6.5\n\n• Serum creatinine level >3.4 mg/dL (300 μmol/L) or anuria\n\n• Grade 3-4 encephalopathy\n\nLiver transplantation is indicated in non-acetaminophen causes of ALF if there is a presence of\n\n• Either prothrombin time >100 sec or INR >6.7\n\n• Or any three of the following\n\n• Unfavorable etiology (seronegative hepatitis or drug reaction)\n\n• Age <10 or >40 yr\n\n• Acute or subacute category (duration of jaundice >7 days)\n\n• Serum bilirubin level >17.5 mg/dL (300 μmol/L)\n\n• Prothrombin time >50 sec or INR >3.5","t":"Acute Liver Failure And Liver Transplantation","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"For patients with acute liver failure, which of the following is not an absolute contraindication for liver transplantation?","o":["AIDS","Active alcohol abuse","Metastatic hepatocellular carcinoma","Portal vein thrombosis"],"a":3,"e":"Portal vein thrombosis is not an absolute contraindication to liver transplantation. This is because portal vein thrombosis can be overcome by constructing a graft from the donor liver portal vein to the recipient’s superior mesenteric vein.\n\nAbsolute contraindications of liver transplantation are:\n\n• Uncontrolled extra-hepatobiliary infection or untreated sepsis\n\n• Uncorrectable life-limiting congenital anomalies\n\n• Active substance or alcohol abuse\n\n• Malignancies- Hepatocellular carcinoma with metastatic spread, incurable malignancies outside the liver, cholangiocarcinoma\n\n• AIDS\n\n• Advanced cardiopulmonary disease\n\nNote: Milan criteria- Patients with primary hepatocellular malignancy can be considered for liver transplantation if there is:\n\n• A single lesion ≤5 cm.\n\n• Up to three separate lesions all <3 cm without any evidence of gross vascular invasion, nodal or distant metastases.","t":"Acute Liver Failure And Liver Transplantation","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Which among the following is the most common indication of liver transplantation in children?","o":["Neonatal hepatitis","Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency","Biliary atresia","Congenital hepatic fibrosis"],"a":2,"e":"Biliary atresia is the most common indication of liver transplantation in children.\n\nIndications of liver transplant in children:\n\n• Biliary atresia\n\n• Neonatal hepatitis\n\n• Congenital hepatic fibrosis\n\n• Alpha-1-antitrypsin deficiency\n\n• Wilsons disease\n\n• Glycogen storage disorders\n\n• Lysosomal storage disorders\n\nThe most common indication of liver transplant in an adult is the cirrhotic liver. Cirrhosis may be the consequence of viral hepatitis, autoimmune hepatitis, chronic use of alcohol, or biliary cirrhosis.","t":"Acute Liver Failure And Liver Transplantation","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"When should a liver transplant assessment be considered in a patient with cirrhosis of the liver?","o":["When he develops spontaneous bacterial peritonitis","When he has ascites which responds to diuretics","MELD score more than 10","Child Pugh class A"],"a":0,"e":"Liver transplant assessment should be considered in a patient with cirrhosis of the liver when he develops spontaneous bacterial peritonitis.\n\nCirrhosis is the most common indication of liver transplantation in adults. However, not all cases of cirrhosis require liver transplantation. It is considered\n\n1. When the patient either develops the complication of cirrhosis like:\n\n• Persistent hepatic encephalopathy\n\n• Spontaneous bacterial peritonitis\n\n• Portal hypertensive bleeding\n\n• Diuretic resistant ascites\n\n• Hepatorenal syndrome\n\n2. Or has a poor liver function\n\n• MELD score > 12\n\n• Child-Pugh grade C","t":"Acute Liver Failure And Liver Transplantation","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A surgeon has removed a part of the liver from the live donor and has stored it in the University of Wisconsin (UW) solution. Which of the following is not a component of this solution?","o":["Lactobionic acid","Glutathione","Allopurinol","Mannitol"],"a":3,"e":"Mannitol is not a component of the University of Wisconsin (UW) solution. It is the most commonly used preservation solution during the transplantation of the liver and kidneys. The organ can be stored in this solution for up to 20 hours.\n\nComponents of UW solution are:\n\n• Lactobionate- Prevents cellular swelling and reperfusion injury\n\n• Raffinose and hydroxyethyl starch- Prevents endothelial injury\n\n• Glutathione and allopurinol- free radical scavengers\n\n• Adenosine- required for regeneration of ATP","t":"Acute Liver Failure And Liver Transplantation","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is a false statement regarding liver transplantation (LT)?","o":["The ideal graft weight to recipient weight ratio (GRWR) in living donor transplant is &gt;0.8 %.","In domino LT, liver grafts with metabolic diseases is used as they are structurally and functionally normal.","Extended criteria donors and paired-exchange LT decrease waitlist mortality.","Reduced size LT involves dividing the whole liver into right lobe for an adult patient and left lateral segments for pediatric patients."],"a":3,"e":"The false statement regarding liver transplantation is option D. Reduced-size liver transplantation involves the resection of a full-size liver to appropriately fit in the abdominal cavity of small adults/adolescents. It is reserved only for urgent transplantation. On the contrary, split LT involves dividing the whole donor liver into a left lateral segment (segments II and III) for a pediatric patient and a right lobe (segments I, IV-VIII) for an adult patient.\n\nOption A: The required graft volume in a living donor transplant is measured by graft weight to recipient weight ratio (GRWR) or ratio of graft volume (GV) to recipient standard liver volume (SLV). The ideal GRWR is >0.8 % or GV/SLV is > 35%. The remnant donor liver mass is kept at >30% for safe regeneration.\n\nOption B: Domino liver transplant involves transplanting a liver from a donor with metabolic disease (eg: familial amyloid polyneuropathy) to a recipient with end-stage liver disease (eg: hepatocellular carcinoma). The donor also receives an LT (healthy donor) and is prioritized over the end-stage liver disease recipient. As a liver with metabolic diseases is structurally and functionally normal, it can prolong the recipient’s life by at least 15-20 years as they will not develop the metabolic disease immediately.\n\nOption C: Extended criteria donors/ECDs (liver grafts extracted from donors after circulatory death) and paired-exchange technique are used to decrease waitlist mortality. However, ECDs have poor graft function due to prolonged hypoxia and are associated with higher infection transmission and biliary complications.","t":"Acute Liver Failure And Liver Transplantation","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Auxiliary liver transplantation is beneficial in which of the following conditions:","o":["Acute liver failure","Hepatocellular carcinoma","Genetic Hemochromatosis","Chronic viral hepatitis"],"a":0,"e":"Auxiliary liver transplantation is beneficial in acute liver failure.\n\nAuxiliary liver transplantation involves implanting a healthy liver graft heterotopically or orthotopically while leaving the whole or part of the native liver intact. The auxiliary partial orthotopic LT (APOLT) technique is used in patients with acute liver failure and metabolic diseases (primary hypercholesterolemia, glycogen storage disorders, organic acidemia, etc). In acute liver failure, this technique helps in native liver regeneration and immunosuppression withdrawal. In children with metabolic liver diseases, this technique helps in future gene therapy.\n\nOptions B, C, and D are chronic liver diseases not suitable for auxiliary liver transplantation.","t":"Acute Liver Failure And Liver Transplantation","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following sentences is true regarding split liver transplantation?","o":["It is done in living donor","Left lateral lobe is donated to the paediatric recipient","It is the most common type of liver transplant","Caudate lobe is donated to the adult recipient"],"a":1,"e":"In a split liver transplant, the left lateral lobe is donated to the pediatric recipient.\n\nSplit liver donation: This method is done when the liver from the deceased donor is suitable for transplant in an adult as well as a pediatric recipient. The liver is split into two. The left lateral segment (2 and 3) is donated to the pediatric recipient, left medial and right segments (4, 5, 6, 7, and 8) is donated to the adult recipient.","t":"Acute Liver Failure And Liver Transplantation","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A patient who underwent liver transplantation is started on tacrolimus for immunosuppression. His family physician prescribed him fluconazole for the treatment of a fungal infection. This will lead to","o":["Toxicity of fluconazole","Transplant rejection","No effect on both the drugs","Toxicity of tacrolimus"],"a":3,"e":"In a patient who is on tacrolimus, the addition of fluconazole may lead to toxicity of the tacrolimus.\n\nTacrolimus and cyclosporine are calcineurin inhibitors and are the common agents used for the immunosuppression following liver transplantation. They are metabolized by the CYP3A enzyme. Fluconazole can inhibit this enzyme and lead to increased levels of tacrolimus and cyclosporine.","t":"Acute Liver Failure And Liver Transplantation","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A patient presented with fever and pain abdomen. He had undergone liver transplantation a month ago and is taking immunosuppressants. On examination, abdominal guarding was present and on auscultation, no bowel sounds were heard. Doppler showed hepatic artery thrombosis. Which of the following medications is he most likely on?","o":["Tacrolimus","Sirolimus","Cyclosporine","Mycophenolate mofetil"],"a":1,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of peritonitis due to hepatic artery thrombosis. Sirolimus is known to cause hepatic artery thrombosis in liver transplant recipients.\n\nSirolimus is an mTOR inhibitor and prevents T cell activation. It is not used commonly in liver transplant recipients because of the increased frequency of hepatic artery thrombosis in the first month posttransplantation. However, they can be used in patients with calcineurin inhibitor-related nephrotoxicity.","t":"Acute Liver Failure And Liver Transplantation","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"All of the following complications can be seen in patients with liver transplantation except","o":["Arrhythmias","Seizures","Hepatic artery thrombosis","T cell lymphoma"],"a":3,"e":"Patients with liver transplantation are more likely to develop B cell lymphomas and not T cell lymphoma.\n\nComplications of liver transplantation\n\n• Rejection of the transplanted liver\n\n• Hepatic artery thrombosis\n\n• Posttransplantation lymphoproliferative disorders- It can range from a mild polyclonal proliferation of B cells to B cell lymphomas.\n\n• Congestive heart failure and arrhythmias\n\n• Increased susceptibility to infections as a result of immunosuppression\n\n• Renal dysfunction due to acute tubular necrosis or nephrotoxicity of the tacrolimus and cyclosporine\n\n• Seizures are commonly associated with cyclosporine or tacrolimus toxicity.","t":"Acute Liver Failure And Liver Transplantation","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A liver biopsy is performed in a patient who is suspected to have acute rejection following liver transplantation. Which of the following findings in the biopsy will confirm the presence of acute rejection?","o":["Fibrosis","Mononuclear infiltration","Endotheliitis","Focal parenchymal necrosis"],"a":2,"e":"The presence of endotheliitis confirms the presence of acute rejection of the liver transplant.\n\nRejection following liver transplantation can be acute or chronic.\n\nPatients usually present with\n\n• Malaise\n\n• Fever\n\n• Right upper quadrant pain\n\nLaboratory investigations will show\n\n• Elevated levels of serum bilirubin\n\n• Aminotransferases\n\n• Gamma-glutamyl transpeptidases\n\n• Liver biopsy- for confirming the diagnosis and planning the treatment\n\nLiver biopsy of the acute rejection is characterized by the presence of mixed portal cellular infiltrate, bile duct injury, and endothelial inflammation (endotheliitis).\n\nChronic rejection is characterized by the presence of focal parenchymal necrosis, mononuclear cell infiltration, progressive cholestasis, and fibrosis. In some patients, this process will lead to the loss of intrahepatic bile ducts, which is called vanishing bile duct syndrome.","t":"Acute Liver Failure And Liver Transplantation","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A 40-year-old male patient developed fever, malaise, and anorexia after a month of liver transplantation. On evaluation, he has leukopenia, thrombocytopenia, and deranged liver function tests. Which of the following organisms is most likely to have caused this?","o":["Cytomegalovirus","Varicella zoster virus","Epstein-Barr virus","Herpes simplex virus"],"a":0,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of post-solid transplant infection most likely due to cytomegalovirus(CMV). It is the most common cause of post-solid organ transplants in India. Epstein-Barr virus (EBV), herpes, and varicella are also known to cause infection post-solid organ transplantation but to a lesser extent.\n\nCMV disease in recipients can present with fever, leukopenia, thrombocytopenia, malaise, and arthralgia. They usually present within 1-4 months after transplant. The diagnosis of CMV infection is established by nucleic acid testing (NAT) using the polymerase chain reaction (PCR).\n\nManagement includes the use of anti-viral drugs like ganciclovir, valganciclovir, foscarnet, cidofovir and intravenous immunoglobulins.","t":"Acute Liver Failure And Liver Transplantation","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following primary diseases has the higher chance of recurrence, after liver transplantation?","o":["Sclerosing cholangitis","Autoimmune hepatitis","Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency","Primary biliary cirrhosis"],"a":1,"e":"Among the given options, autoimmune hepatitis has the highest chance of recurrence after liver transplantation.\n\nAlthough some cases of sclerosing cholangitis and primary biliary cirrhosis recur, data for recurrence of autoimmune hepatitis is more supportive.\n\nAlpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency does not recur after liver transplantation.","t":"Acute Liver Failure And Liver Transplantation","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"What is the most common gene mutation associated with hemochromatosis?","o":["H49D","C282Y","G97C","A480H"],"a":1,"e":"The most common mutation seen in hemochromatosis is a cysteine to tyrosine substitution at position 282 (C282Y) in the HFE gene.\n\nHFE mutations are the most common cause of hereditary hemochromatosis and are inherited as an autosomal recessive trait.","t":"Hemochromatosis And Wilson'S Disease","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Patients with which of the following conditions are unlikely to develop iron overload?","o":["Sideroblastic anemia","Non-alcoholic steatohepatitis","Porphyria cutanea tarda","Thalassemia minor"],"a":3,"e":"Thalassemia minor does not result in iron overload. Secondary hemochromatosis or iron overload is characterized by the deposition of iron in parenchymal cells.","t":"Hemochromatosis And Wilson'S Disease","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following clinical manifestations of hemochromatosis is incorrectly matched with its mechanism of onset?\n\nClinical feature\nMechanism\n\n1.\nHypothyroidism -\nIron deposition in pituitary\n\n2.\nArthropathy -\nDeposition of calcium pyrophosphate crystals\n\n3.\nSkin hyperpigmentation -\nIncreased melanin production\n\n4.\nSexual dysfunction -\nIron deposition in testes","o":["1","2","3","4"],"a":3,"e":"Sexual dysfunction (decreased libido, impotence, amenorrhea) in hemochromatosis occurs as a result of hypogonadotropic hypogonadism. This is due to iron deposition in the pituitary gland, not testes.\n\nIron deposition occurs in the following sites:\n\nOrgan\nManifestation\n\nLiver\nHepatomegaly\n\nSkin\n\nSlate-grey or bronze skin hyperpigmentation: increased melanin synthesis due to iron deposition\n\nPancreas\npancreatic diabetes/type 3C diabetes\n\nPituitary\n\nHypogonadotropic hypogonadism\n\nSecondary hypothyroidism\n\nHeart\n\nDilated cardiomyopathy\n\nCongestive heart failure\n\nArrhythmias\n\nJoints\n\nArthropathy is due to:\n\n• Iron deposition\n\n• Calcium pyrophosphate deposition (pseudogout)","t":"Hemochromatosis And Wilson'S Disease","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A 7-year-old boy undergoes genetic testing as his brother has been diagnosed with hereditary hemochromatosis. He is found to be homozygous for the C282Y mutation. Which of the following is the strongest indicator that he will develop symptoms of the disease?","o":["Plasma iron &gt;180 µg/dL","Total iron binding capacity &gt;200 µg/dL","Transferrin saturation &gt;50%","Serum ferritin level &gt;1000 μg/L"],"a":3,"e":"Serum ferritin > 1000 μg/L is the strongest predictor of disease expression among individuals homozygous for the C282Y mutation.\n\nMany patients with C282Y mutations are initially asymptomatic and do not show features of iron overload. In such patients, serum ferritin can indicate whether they are likely to develop the disease as ferritin levels reflect the total iron stores.\n\nAs ferritin levels may be high following inflammatory conditions, combined measurement of ferritin and transferrin saturation is done in a practical setting to avoid false positives. It is also repeated once inflammation subsides. If these tests are abnormal, genetic testing for HFE mutations is done.","t":"Hemochromatosis And Wilson'S Disease","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A 47-year-old diabetic man is worried as the skin over his face, neck, and dorsum of hands has turned greyish over the past two months. He also complains of abdominal and joint pain. On examination, gynecomastia and hepatomegaly are noted. What is the treatment of choice for this condition?","o":["Phlebotomy","Deferoxamine","Deferasirox","Liver transplantation"],"a":0,"e":"This clinical scenario is suggestive of hemochromatosis. The treatment of choice is the removal of excess iron by phlebotomy.\n\nPhlebotomy is an inexpensive, convenient, and safe method of treatment. In this process, 500 mL of blood is removed 1-2 times weekly which removes 200-250 mg of iron. Weekly phlebotomy should be continued until the serum ferritin level is ≤100 μg/L and every 3 months thereafter. It is contraindicated in severe anemia and hypoproteinemia.\n\nChelating agents bind to iron to form complexes that get excreted and are the drugs of choice. Drugs such as deferoxamine and deferasirox are commonly used. However, they are less effective and more expensive than phlebotomy.\n\nLiver transplantation is indicated either in end-stage liver disease or in refractory cases.\n\nSupportive management includes avoiding iron-containing substances such as red meat, multi-vitamin tablets, etc. Alcohol should be completely avoided as it can aggravate the disease.","t":"Hemochromatosis And Wilson'S Disease","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A 30-year-old man with hereditary hemochromatosis has been started on weekly phlebotomy. All of the following features will improve with this treatment except:","o":["Hepatomegaly","Skin pigmentation","Cardiac failure","Arthropathy"],"a":3,"e":"In hemochromatosis, removal of excess iron through phlebotomy has no effect on arthropathy and hypogonadism.","t":"Hemochromatosis And Wilson'S Disease","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is incorrect about Wilson's disease?","o":["Gain-of-function mutation of ATP7B","AP1S1 gene is essential for normal functioning","Siblings of affected children have 1 in 4 risk of the disease","Mutation on chromosome 13"],"a":0,"e":"Wilson’s disease is caused due to a loss-of-function mutation in the ATP7B gene on Chr.13q. The gene encodes a copper transporting ATPase (ATP7B) which has 2 functions:\n\n• Maintains copper in bound form by binding copper to apoceruloplasmin\n\n• Helps excrete free copper in bile.\n\nThus, an ATP7B deficiency leads to excess deposition of free copper within tissues. It is an autosomal recessive condition. So, siblings of affected children have a 1 in 4 chance.\n\nIntracellular trafficking of ATP7B (Wilson's disease) and ATP7A (Menke's disease) genes are regulated by an adaptor protein encoded by the AP1S1 gene. Mutations in this gene result in a syndrome with features of both Wilson's and Menke's diseases. It is called MEDNIK syndrome:\n\n• Mental retardation\n\n• Enteropathy\n\n• Deafness\n\n• Neuropathy\n\n• Ichthyosis\n\n• Keratodermia.","t":"Hemochromatosis And Wilson'S Disease","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"In patients with Wilson's disease, Kayser-Fleischer rings are most commonly associated with:","o":["Hepatitis","Dystonia","Osteoarthritis","Cholelithiasis"],"a":1,"e":"The presence of the Kayser-Fleischer (KF) ring in Wilson's disease is most commonly associated with neuropsychiatric manifestations such as dystonia.\n\nIn majority of the cases, neuropsychiatric manifestations follow hepatic manifestations. It is due to the deposition of free copper in the basal ganglia (leading to bilateral degeneration and atrophy) and cortex. The features include:\n\n• Dysarthria (most common): Irregular word spacing, hypophonic or spastic speech\n\n• Dystonia: Vacuous smile i.e mask-like face with an open mouth\n\n• Parkinsonism: Bradykinesia, postural instability, rigidity\n\n• Subcortical dementia: Slow mental processing, memory loss\n\n• Psychiatric symptoms: Loss of emotional control, depression, hyperactivity\n\n• Decreased scholastic performance: Deterioration in handwriting, clumsiness\n\n• Autonomic symptoms: Excessive sweating and salivation, sexual dysfunction\n\n• Wing beating tremors (seen in advanced disease): High amplitude tremor that is prominent with arms abducted, elbows flexed and palms facing downward.","t":"Hemochromatosis And Wilson'S Disease","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A 22-year-old woman is being evaluated in the emergency department for a 3-day history of dark-colored urine and abdominal distension. On examination, she is conscious and oriented. Icterus is present. The following investigations are done. Which of the following would you suspect?\n\nHemoglobin\n9.2 g/dl\n\nHematocrit\n26%\n\nReticulocytes\n5%\n\nPlatelets\n1.3 x 105/mm3\n\nSerum total iron\nReduced\n\nSerum ferritin\nNormal\n\nSerum ALT\n110 IU/L\n\nSerum AST\n240 IU/L\n\nSerum ALP\n30 IU/L\n\nTotal bilirubin\n13 mg/dl\n\nAnti-Hbs IgG\nPositive\n\nUSG Abdomen\nNodular liver and enlarged spleen","o":["Hemochromatosis","Acute viral hepatitis","Primary biliary cirrhosis","Wilson's disease"],"a":3,"e":"The given laboratory values indicate hepatic disease and hemolytic anemia (low Hb with high reticulocyte count). These lab findings along with clinical features point to a diagnosis of Wilson's disease.\n\nThe clinical features of Wilson’s disease include:\n\nSystem\nClinical features\n\nHepatic\n(most common)\n\nJaundice\nAbdominal pain\nHepatomegaly\nFatigue\nAscites\nVariceal bleed\nCoagulation defects\n\nHigh AST & ALT with low ALP\n\nNeurological\nDysarthria\nDystonia\nParkinsonism\nSubcortical dementia\nDepression\nWing beating tremors\nDecreased scholastic performance\n\nOphthalmic\nKayser-Fleischer (KF) rings\nSunflower cataract\n\nOthers\nHemolytic anemia (Coombs negative)\nAcute pancreatitis\nFanconi's syndrome\nHyperparathyroidism\nCardiomyopathy\nSpontaneous abortions\nInfertility\n\nOption A: Hemochromatosis presents with clinical features of iron overload. Serum ferritin and total iron are elevated.\n\nOption B: Acute viral hepatitis presents with positive HbsAg, whereas anti-Hbs IgG indicates past infection. There is tender hepatomegaly with very high levels of transaminases (>1000 IU/L).\n\nOption C: Primary biliary cirrhosis presents with jaundice, severe itching, and pale stools. There are high levels of direct bilirubin, ALP, and positive anti-mitochondrial antibodies.","t":"Hemochromatosis And Wilson'S Disease","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following findings would be seen in a patient with Wilson's disease?\n\n1. Decreased total serum copper\n\n2. Increased total serum copper\n\n3. Decreased serum ceruloplasmin\n\n4. Increased urinary copper\n\n5. Decreased free serum copper","o":["2, 3, 4","1, 3, 4","2, 4, 5","2, 3, 5"],"a":1,"e":"The following findings will be seen in a patient with Wilson's disease:\n\n• Decreased total serum copper\n\n• Decreased serum ceruloplasmin\n\n• Increased urinary copper\n\nLab markers\nChange in Wilson's disease\nReason\n\nSerum ceruloplasmin\n↓↓↓\nATP7B deficiency results in reduced bound copper\n\n24h urinary copper\n↑↑\nExcess free copper excreted in urine\n\nTotal serum copper\n↓\nDue to marked reduction in ceruloplasmin\n\nNormally, >90% of copper exists in bound form and <10% in free form. In Wilson’s disease, despite elevated levels of free copper, the marked reduction in ceruloplasmin results in a net reduction of total serum copper i.e.,\n\n↓total serum copper =↑free copper + ↓↓ceruloplasmin","t":"Hemochromatosis And Wilson'S Disease","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A 15-year-old boy is brought with complaints of abnormal posture. His scholastic performance used to be above average but has deteriorated over the last 3 months. Dystonia and increased muscle tone are noted on clinical examination. The following picture is seen on ophthalmic examination. What is the gold standard for the diagnosis of this condition?","o":["Serum copper","Serum ceruloplasmin","Urinary copper","Liver copper"],"a":3,"e":"This clinical scenario and image showing Kayser-Fleischer (KF) ring with sunflower cataract are suggestive of Wilson's disease. The gold standard for diagnosis is the estimation of liver copper following biopsy.\n\nA copper level of >200 μg/g dry liver tissue is diagnostic for Wilson's disease. It is measured using neutron activation analysis or atomic absorption spectrometry.\n\nSunflower cataract is a rare finding of Wilson’s disease. It occurs due to the deposition of copper in the center of the lens.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/41dc0459eb9d4723bd1a642607366813.JPEG"],"t":"Hemochromatosis And Wilson'S Disease","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A pathologist is preparing a tissue sample from the liver biopsy of a patient with Wilson's disease. What stain will he use?","o":["Perl stain","Rubeanic acid","Supravital stain","Schmorl’s method"],"a":1,"e":"In Wilson’s disease, liver biopsy samples are stained using rubeanic acid to visualize the cytoplasmic copper.\n\nAnother stain used for copper is the rhodanine stain. However, copper staining is not reliable for diagnosis and a quantitative copper assay must be performed.\n\nThe given image shows the staining of copper by rubeanic acid.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c1cf4ceffb114fc8ba6b1e72116bc007x800x600.PNG"],"t":"Hemochromatosis And Wilson'S Disease","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A 17-year-old girl was brought with involuntary, writhing movements of her limbs. On examination, she is anicteric with dystonia and hyperreflexia. Investigations revealed mildly elevated transaminases and low serum ceruloplasmin levels. Which of the following is the safest drug that can be initiated for this patient?","o":["Zinc","D-Penicillamine","Trientine","Tetrathiomolybdate"],"a":2,"e":"This clinical scenario is suggestive of Wilson's disease with predominantly neurological features. The safest drug that can be initiated for this patient is trientine. This is because D-penicillamine can worsen neurological symptoms and would not be tolerated by this patient.\n\nThe copper chelating agents, D-penicillamine and trientine are the first-line drugs in the management of Wilson's disease. D-penicillamine is the recommended drug due to its availability and cost-effectiveness compared to trientine.\n\nHowever, D-penicillamine can precipitate various adverse effects including aplastic anemia, myasthenia gravis and membranous nephropathy. It can also worsen neurological symptoms. Hence, trientine is administered in those who cannot tolerate D-penicillamine.\n\nElemental zinc is commonly used as a maintenance therapy along with chelating agents. It can also be used as first-line therapy in pregnancy.\n\nTetrathiomolybdate is currently under phase 2 clinical trials. It has been identified to be effective in patients with neuropsychiatric manifestations but is not approved for clinical use.\n\nLiver transplantation is the definitive treatment for advanced liver disease.","t":"Hemochromatosis And Wilson'S Disease","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"For a patient with Wilson's disease, you would administer pyridoxine along with which of the following drugs?","o":["Zinc","Penicillamine","Trientine","Tetrathiomolybdate"],"a":1,"e":"Penicillamine should always be co-administered with pyridoxine (25 mg/day) as it is an antagonist to pyridoxine.","t":"Hemochromatosis And Wilson'S Disease","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"The Nazer’s prognostic index for Wilson’s disease includes all of the following parameters except:","o":["Serum bilirubin","AST","ALT","Prothrombin time"],"a":2,"e":"ALT is not a part of Nazer’s prognostic index.\n\nNazer’s prognostic index is used to establish disease severity in Wilson’s disease patients who present with hepatic decompensation.","t":"Hemochromatosis And Wilson'S Disease","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following conditions is unlikely to lead to cirrhosis in a patient?","o":["Alpha 1 antitrypsin deficiency","Cystic fibrosis","Nonalcoholic steatohepatitis","Hepatitis A"],"a":3,"e":"Hepatitis A infection is an acute self-limiting condition that does not lead to cirrhosis.\n\nCirrhosis is a late stage of chronic liver disease pathologically characterized by hepatic fibrosis, distortion of hepatic architecture, and formation of regenerative nodules.\n\nThe causes of cirrhosis are:\n\n• Chronic viral hepatitis - Hepatitis B and C\n\n• Alcoholic liver disease\n\n• Nonalcoholic fatty liver disease\n\n• Autoimmune hepatitis\n\n• Biliary cirrhosis -\n\n• Primary biliary cholangitis\n\n• Primary sclerosing cholangitis\n\n• Cardiac cirrhosis\n\n• Inherited metabolic liver diseases -\n\n• Cystic fibrosis\n\n• Hemochromatosis\n\n• Wilson's disease\n\n• α1 Antitrypsin deficiency\n\n• Cryptogenic cirrhosis\n\n• Drugs - methotrexate, isoniazid","t":"Cirrhosis And Complications","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"While examining a chronic alcoholic patient, you notice the given finding. Which of the following is false regarding it?","o":["Occurs due to alterations in the metabolism of estrogen","Presence of even 1 lesion is abnormal","Commonly seen over the superior vena cava territory","Compression results in blanching followed by reappearance after release"],"a":1,"e":"The given image shows spider telangiectasia and the presence of >2 to 3 of these are considered abnormal.\n\nSpider telangiectasias are dilated arterioles with a prominent central arteriole with radiating vessels appearing in the shape of a spider. They are generally seen in the superior vena cava territory. Compression results in blanching followed by reappearance after the release of pressure. They occur due to a hyperestrogenic state which is caused by defective estrogen metabolism in liver cirrhosis.\n\nClinical features seen in cirrhosis include:\n\n• Spider telangiectasias\n\n• Palmar erythema\n\n• Terry's nails - pallor of the proximal nail bed\n\n• Clubbing\n\n• Gynaecomastia\n\n• Caput medusae - dilated abdominal veins.\n\n• Parotid enlargement\n\nThe image below shows Terry's nails.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/12149eea511347dba64938cc75f801dc.JPEG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1439eb59bb694818b832990dac1e927cx800x1067.JPEG"],"t":"Cirrhosis And Complications","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Clinically significant portal hypertension is defined as a hepatic venous pressure gradient greater than _____ mm Hg.","o":["3","10","5","12"],"a":1,"e":"Clinically-significant portal hypertension is defined as hepatic venous pressure gradient (HVPG) ≥10 mm Hg.\n\nHVPG is the difference between the wedged hepatic venous pressure (WHVP) and the free hepatic venous pressure (FHVP). It represents the pressure difference between the portal vein and IVC.\n\nInterpretation of HVPG values:\n\nHVPG value\nInterpretation\n\n1-5 mmHg\nNormal\n\n>5 mmHg\nPortal hypertension\n\n≥10 mmHg\nClinically significant portal hypertension, varices can develop\n\n≥12 mmHg\nRisk of variceal bleeding and ascites","t":"Cirrhosis And Complications","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is a posthepatic cause of portal hypertension?","o":["Budd-Chiari syndrome","Schistosomiasis","Portal vein thrombosis","Banti's syndrome"],"a":0,"e":"Budd-Chiari syndrome is a posthepatic cause of portal hypertension.\n\nBudd Chiari syndrome is a congestive hepatopathy characterized by the obstruction of hepatic venous outflow. The most common cause is due to hepatic vein thrombosis. Obstruction of two or more major hepatic veins causes Budd-Chiari syndrome. It is characterized by hepatomegaly, ascites, and abdominal pain.","t":"Cirrhosis And Complications","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following drugs is used to reduce the intrahepatic resistance in portal hypertension?","o":["Propranolol","Carvedilol","Octreotide","Terlipressin"],"a":1,"e":"Carvedilol is used to reduce the intrahepatic resistance in portal hypertension.\n\nThe pharmacologic management of portal hypertension includes:\n\n• Drugs that reduce the portal blood flow\n\n• Non-selective β blockers - Propranolol and nadolol are preferred\n\n• Somatostatin and its analogs - Octreotide, lanreotide, and vapreotide\n\n• Vasopressin and analogs - Terlipressin\n\n• Drugs that decrease the intrahepatic resistance\n\n• Carvedilol - due to its α blockade property","t":"Cirrhosis And Complications","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Ascitic tap done on a patient with abdominal distention revealed a SAAG of 1.4g/dL. What is the likely cause of his ascites?","o":["Portal hypertension","Nephrotic syndrome","Peritoneal tuberculosis","Biliary leak"],"a":0,"e":"The serum ascites albumin gradient of ≥1.1g/dL is suggestive of portal hypertension.\n\nSAAG helps distinguish ascites caused by portal hypertension from other causes of ascites.","t":"Cirrhosis And Complications","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A patient is said to have refractory ascites if the ascites does not resolve even after:\n\n1. Dietary sodium restriction\n\n2. 100mg / day spironolactone\n\n3. 160mg / day furosemide\n\n4. 100mg / day furosemide\n\n5. 400mg / day spironolactone","o":["1, 2 and 4","4 and 5","1, 3 and 5","2 and 3"],"a":2,"e":"Refractory ascites is defined as the inability to mobilize ascitic fluid despite:\n\n• Compliance with dietary sodium restriction\n\n• Administration of maximum doses of diuretics -\n\n• 400mg of spironolactone per day and\n\n• 160mg of furosemide per day","t":"Cirrhosis And Complications","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"What is the minimum absolute neutrophilic count that should be present in the ascitic fluid to diagnose a patient with spontaneous bacterial peritonitis?","o":["&gt;250/µL","&gt;150/µL","&gt;300/µL","&gt;200/µL"],"a":0,"e":"The diagnosis of spontaneous bacterial peritonitis(SBP) is made when the fluid sample has an absolute neutrophil count >250/μL.\n\nSBP is the spontaneous infection of the ascitic fluid without an intraabdominal source. Infection with E.coli and other gut bacteria is most commonly seen. However, gram-positive bacteria like Streptococcus viridans, Staphylococcus aureus, and Enterococcus sp., can also be found. Abdominal paracentesis is the investigation of choice. It can be managed with antibiotic therapy.","t":"Cirrhosis And Complications","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A cirrhotic patient admitted for ascites complained of abdominal pain on the 2nd day of his hospital stay. On examination, he was febrile. His lab reports are given below. Which of the following is false about his condition?\n\nTotal WBC count- 12 400×106/litre\n\nC reactive protein- 4.3 mg/dl\n\nSerology- positive for HBs-antigen\n\nAbsolute neutrophil count in ascitic fluid- 743 cells/μl","o":["E.coli is the most commonly isolated organism","Prophylaxis against this condition is recommended in upper GI bleed","Third generation cephalosporins are used for treatment","Weekly antibiotic prophylaxis is recommended to prevent recurrence"],"a":3,"e":"The given clinical scenario along with ANC ≥ 250 cells/μl in ascitic fluid is suggestive of spontaneous bacterial peritonitis (SBP). Daily antibiotic prophylaxis is recommended in patients who have had an episode of SBP to prevent a recurrence.\n\nSBP is the spontaneous infection of the ascitic fluid without an intraabdominal source. The most common causative organisms are E.coli and other gut bacteria. The diagnosis of SBP is made when the ascitic fluid has an absolute neutrophil count >250/μl.\n\nAccording to the latest recommendations, weekly antibiotic prophylaxis is no longer recommended. For patients with low protein ascites (<1.5 g/dL [15g/L]) along with either impaired renal function or liver failure, we use prolonged outpatient trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole (one double-strength tablet daily). Alternatives include ciprofloxacin 500 mg per day or norfloxacin 400 mg per day.\n\n For patients on chronic trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole therapy, renal function should be monitored by checking serum electrolytes, blood urea nitrogen (BUN) and creatinine every one to six months depending on the stability of patient. If there is worsening renal function, trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole should be discontinued.","t":"Cirrhosis And Complications","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following cannot be used to predict the risk of bleeding of oesophageal varices?","o":["Severity of cirrhosis","Size of the varices","Location of the varices","Number of varices"],"a":3,"e":"The risk of bleeding in a patient with oesophageal varices cannot be predicted based on the number of varices present.\n\n The factors that are used to predict the risk of variceal bleeding are:\n\n• Severity of cirrhosis (Child’s class, MELD score)\n\n• History of a previous variceal bleed\n\n• Location of the varices - varices with a thinner layer of supporting tissue are likely to bleed more\n\n• Size of the varices - larger varices have a higher risk of bleeding\n\n• Endoscopic appearance of varices - Presence of red wale sign, hematocystic spots, diffuse erythema, and cherry red spots are associated with increased risk of bleeding\n\n• Variceal pressure","t":"Cirrhosis And Complications","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"In a patient with which of the following conditions would you not recommend a transjugular intrahepatic portosystemic shunt?","o":["Ectopic varices","Refractory ascites","Budd Chiari syndrome","Hepatic encephalopathy"],"a":3,"e":"Transjugular intrahepatic portosystemic shunt (TIPS) is not recommended in hepatic encephalopathy as it can aggravate its symptoms.\n\nTIPS creates a low-resistance channel between the hepatic vein and an intrahepatic branch of the portal vein. It uses a percutaneous approach under angiographic guidance. This reduces portal pressure.\n\nIt is used in the management of:\n\n• Oesophageal varices - in patients who fail medical or endoscopic management and have a poor surgical outcome\n\n• Refractory ascites\n\n• Gastric varices\n\n• Ectopic varices\n\n• Portal hypertensive gastropathy\n\n• Hepatorenal syndrome\n\n• Budd Chiari syndrome","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/564defbe79384fd299c2ca53488b2b4cx600x1199.JPEG"],"t":"Cirrhosis And Complications","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A middle-aged man with hematemesis is rushed to the casualty. On examination, he is icteric, PR-122 /min and BP- 88/64 mmHg. The following finding is seen on his abdomen. Which of the following is false regarding his initial management?","o":["pRBC is used to maintain Hb between 7-9g/dL","Prophylactic antibiotics given before endoscopy","IV octreotide is used to acheive hemostasis","Upper GI endoscopy is only performed after 12 hours"],"a":3,"e":"The given clinical scenario along with the image showing caput-medusae is suggestive of acute esophageal variceal bleeding due to portal hypertension. Upper GI endoscopy is performed within 12 hours after fluid resuscitation in variceal bleeding.\n\nThe management of esophageal variceal bleeding involves:\n\n• Resuscitation with IV fluids, airway protection, and oxygen\n\n• Blood transfusion to maintain Hb between 7-9g/dL\n\n• Antibiotic prophylaxis with ceftriaxone before endoscopy\n\n• An attempt at hemostasis with octreotide or terlipressin or vasopressin\n\n• Upper GI endoscopy within 12 hours after fluid resuscitation to confirm the source of the bleed\n\n• Endoscopic variceal ligation of sclerotherapy\n\n• In case, of failure or rebleeding - TIPS\n\nNonselective beta-blockers like propranolol and nadolol are used in the prophylaxis of variceal bleeding.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d06079aeadcc4fbcbceee016f0dfbfb6.JPEG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/843bb40225884e0b847c52871b9948cfx1200x1551.JPEG"],"t":"Cirrhosis And Complications","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is false about the given image?","o":["Esophageal balloon should be deflated for about 5 mins every 6 hours","Gastric balloon should be inflated first","Pressure in the oesophageal balloon should not exceed 60 mm Hg.","Endotracheal intubation prior to tube insertion reduces the risk of pulmonary aspiration"],"a":2,"e":"The given image shows Sengstaken-Blakemore tube. Pressure in the oesophageal balloon should not exceed 45 mm Hg.\n\nIt is used for balloon tamponade in variceal hemorrhage when medical therapy fails or is not available. Initially, only the gastric balloon should be inflated, with 200– 250 mL of air, as this will usually control bleeding. The oesophageal balloon is used in continued bleeding, it should be deflated for about 5 minutes every 6 hours to avoid oesophageal mucosal damage.\n\nEndotracheal intubation prior to tube insertion reduces the risk of pulmonary aspiration.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/08faaae1be1a46a7a9ce4f762e69edb5.JPEG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4a9cf7ed3c29401c93358df67f1872a7x1280x1825.JPEG"],"t":"Cirrhosis And Complications","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A patient with hereditary hemochromatosis shows marked confusion and gross disorientation. He is drowsy but responds to pain and voice stimuli. What is the most likely grading of hepatic encephalopathy in this patient?","o":["Grade 1","Grade 2","Grade 3","Grade 4"],"a":2,"e":"Gross disorientation with an intact response to pain and voice suggest grade 3 hepatic encephalopathy.\n\nHepatic encephalopathy is the alteration of mental status and cognitive function occurring in liver failure. This occurs due to the failure of removal of gut-derived neurotoxins like ammonia by liver. These neurotoxins enter the brain leading to the development of symptoms.","t":"Cirrhosis And Complications","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"What is the earliest symptom of hepatic encephalopathy?","o":["Mood changes","Asterixis","Altered sleep pattern","Disorientation"],"a":2,"e":"The earliest symptom of hepatic encephalopathy is an alteration in sleep pattern.\n\nOther signs and symptoms of hepatic encephalopathy include:\n\n• Asterixis also called the flapping tremor\n\n• Mood changes and inappropriate behavior\n\n• Disorientation and confusion","t":"Cirrhosis And Complications","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A cirrhotic patient was admitted following variceal bleeding. On the 3rd day of his hospital stay, he appears to be drowsy and confused. His wife reports that he had been awake throughout the previous night. On examination, flapping tremors were noticed. Which of the following is least likely to have precipitated his symptoms?","o":["Antibiotics","Peritoneal tap","Diuretics","Urinary tract infection"],"a":0,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of hepatic encephalopathy. Antibiotics are unlikely to precipitate hepatic encephalopathy.\n\nThe common precipitating factors of hepatic encephalopathy are :\n\n• Gastrointestinal bleeding\n\n• Infections like SBP or UTIs\n\n• Peritoneal tap\n\n• Constipation\n\n• Renal failure\n\n• Hypovolemia\n\n• Hypoxia\n\n• Drugs - sedatives, antidepressants, diuretics\n\n• Hypoglycemia\n\n• Hypokalemia","t":"Cirrhosis And Complications","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following antibiotics could be used in the treatment of patients with hepatic encephalopathy?","o":["Rifaximin","Azithromycin","Doxycycline","Rifampicin"],"a":0,"e":"Rifaximin can be used in the treatment of hepatic encephalopathy. It lowers blood ammonia levels.\n\nTreatment of hepatic encephalopathy includes:\n\n• Correction of precipitating factors\n\n• Lactulose - decreased GI transit time due to cathartic actions leading to increased elimination of ammonia\n\n• Rifaximin - lowers the blood ammonia levels\n\n• L-ornithine L-aspartate - stimulate the urea cycle, with resulting loss of ammonia","t":"Cirrhosis And Complications","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not part of the diagnostic criteria of hepatorenal syndrome?","o":["50% increase in creatinine from baseline within 7 days","Rise in creatinine of 0.3 mg/dl in 48 hours","Presence of shock","No recent use of nephrotoxic agent"],"a":2,"e":"The absence of shock is a part of the diagnostic criteria for hepatorenal syndrome.\n\nHepatorenal syndrome is a progressive renal failure in patients with acute or chronic liver disease.\n\nThe diagnostic criteria for hepatorenal syndrome include:\n\n• Cirrhosis with ascites\n\n• Diagnosis of acute kidney injury (AKI)\n\n• Increase in serum creatinine of ≥ 0.3mg/dL in 48 hours, OR\n\n• 50% increase in serum creatinine from baseline within 7 days\n\n• Absence of shock\n\n• No evidence of recent use of nephrotoxic agents\n\n• Absence of intrinsic renal disease","t":"Cirrhosis And Complications","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following clinical features is most likely to be seen in patients with hepatopulmonary syndrome?","o":["Trepopnea","Nocturnal dyspnea","Orthopnea","Platypnea"],"a":3,"e":"Among the given options, platypnea is most likely to be seen in patients with hepatopulmonary syndrome.\n\nHepatopulmonary syndrome is characterized by a triad of liver disease, hypoxemia, and intrapulmonary vascular dilatations. Most patients have dyspnea on exertion. The classic clinical manifestations are:\n\n• Platypnea - Increase in dyspnea when the patient moves from supine to upright position\n\n• Orthodeoxia - Decrease in arterial oxygen tension when the patient moves from supine to upright position\n\nThe hypoxemia is due to a ventilation-perfusion mismatch which causes an increased alveolar-arterial (A-a) gradient. The most sensitive test for diagnosis is contrast bubble echocardiography.\n\nLiver transplantation is the definitive treatment of this condition following which the symptoms subside.","t":"Cirrhosis And Complications","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A 52-year-old man presents with oliguria, abdominal distension, and pedal edema. His medical records show long-standing cirrhosis with multiple hospitalisations for ascites and hepatic encephalopathy. His lab reports are given below. What is the best treatment for his condition?\n\nAST- 148 U/L\n\nALT- 64 U/L\n\nTotal bilirubin- 8.9 mg/dL\n\nSr. Creatinine- 7.6 mg/dL\n\nBlood urea nitrogen- 84 mg/dL","o":["Midodrine","Liver transplant","Intravenous albumin","TIPS"],"a":1,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of hepatorenal syndrome. The best treatment modality for it is liver transplantation.\n\nHepatorenal syndrome is characterised by a rise in:\n\n• AST\n\n• ALT\n\n• Total bilirubin\n\n• Serum creatinine\n\n• Blood urea nitrogen (BUN).\n\nManagement of hepatorenal syndrome include:\n\n• Norepinephrine\n\n• Octreotide\n\n• Terlipressin\n\n• Midodrine\n\n• Intravenous albumin\n\n• Liver transplantation- definitive treatment","t":"Cirrhosis And Complications","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following conditions is not a MELD exception?","o":["Hepatopulmonary syndrome","Portopulmonary hypertension","Primary hyperoxaluria","Chronic Hepatitis B"],"a":3,"e":"Chronic Hepatitis B is not a MELD exception.\n\nMELD (Model for End-stage Liver Disease) score is used to prioritize patients awaiting liver transplantation. However, there are certain diseases that impair patient survival and are not accounted for in the MELD score. Patients with these diseases are given upgraded MELD points to accurately represent the mortality risk and these conditions are called MELD exceptions.\n\nThe MELD exceptions include:-\n\n• Portopulmonary hypertension\n\n• Hepatocellular carcinoma\n\n• Familial amyloid polyneuropathy\n\n• Primary hyperoxaluria (necessitating liver-kidney transplantation)\n\n• Cystic fibrosis with liver disease\n\n• Highly selected cases of hilar cholangiocarcinoma.\n\n• Hepatopulmonary syndrome\n\n• Hepatic artery thrombosis 7–14 days post-liver transplantation.","t":"Cirrhosis And Complications","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is an epithelial mucosal defense mechanism of the gastric mucosa?","o":["Layer of mucous","Alkaline tide","Microvascular bed","Restitution"],"a":3,"e":"Restitution is an epithelial mucosal defense mechanism. It is the process by which healthy surface epithelial cells from the adjoining areas migrate to restore a damaged region of the gastric mucosa.\n\nThe gastric mucosa has well-established mucosal defense mechanisms against various assaults like HCl, pepsin, alcohol, etc. These include:\n\n• Pre-epithelial defense: A layer of mucous over the surface epithelium. This is secreted by the surface epithelium and contains HCO3- which maintains neutral pH at the cell surface\n\n• Epithelial defense: It is constituted by the surface epithelial cells and their intercellular tight junctions that perform the following functions\n\n• Act as a physical barrier\n\n• Restitution is the migration of healthy cells to damaged areas\n\n• Rapid renewal of surface epithelial layer\n\n• Generating factors that prevent cellular damage such as heat shock proteins\n\n• Sub-epithelial defense:\n\n• Rich microvascular bed of the gastric submucosa that provides HCO3- and washes away the toxic metabolites.\n\n• Alkaline tide- It is caused by the release of HCO3- into the gastric capillaries by the parietal cells. This HCO3- helps in the quick neutralization of HCl.","t":"Peptic Ulcer Disease And Related Disorders","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A 50-year-old woman complains about a burning pain in her chest. She reports using NSAIDs for her constant knee pain. This drug impairs her gastric mucosal defense mechanism primarily by:","o":["Irritant effect on gastric mucosa","Stimulating acid production","Inhibiting prostaglandin synthesis","Inhibiting nitric oxide synthesis"],"a":2,"e":"The NSAIDs impair the mucosal defense mechanism primarily by inhibiting prostaglandin synthesis.\n\nNSAIDs also decrease nitric oxide synthesis and have an irritant effect on the gastric mucosa but inhibition of COX enzymes/ prostaglandin synthesis is the most prominent factor.\n\nAlmost all the mucosal defense mechanisms are primarily mediated by prostaglandins synthesized by the gastric mucosal cells. Apart from the prostaglandins, the other factors that play a role in mucosal defense include:\n\n• Nitric oxide (NO)- Causes vasodilation thereby increasing the mucosal blood flow.\n\n• Several growth factors like EGF, TGF, FGF- Play a role in epithelial regeneration and restitution.","t":"Peptic Ulcer Disease And Related Disorders","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"All of the following are true about the risk factors of NSAIDs associated peptic ulcer disease except:","o":["Smoking increases the risk","H pylori infection increases the risk","Even low dose of a NSAID can cause ulcers","Concomitant use of glucocorticoids decreases the risk"],"a":3,"e":"Concomitant use of glucocorticoids with NSAIDs increases the risk of peptic ulcer disease.\n\nOther risks factors for NSAID-induced PUD include:\n\n• H pylori infection\n\n• Serious or multisystem disease\n\n• Alcohol and smoking\n\n• Concomitant use of the following drugs with NSAIDs increases the risk of PUD and its complications:\n\n• Glucocorticoids\n\n• Anticoagulants\n\n• Clopidogrel\n\n• Multiple NSAIDs\n\nPUD may occur even with low doses of NSAIDs. No dose of NSAIDs is considered completely safe.","t":"Peptic Ulcer Disease And Related Disorders","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"What pattern of gastritis is most common in patients with H.pylori-related duodenal ulcers?","o":["Autoimmune gastritis","Non-atrophic pangastritis","Granulomatous gastritis","Antral predominant gastritis"],"a":3,"e":"Antral predominant gastritis is associated with duodenal ulcers. Antral predominant gastritis is associated with increased acid production by the parietal cells. This subjects the duodenum to increased acid exposure, leading to the formation of duodenal ulcers.\n\nH. pylori infection may result in the following patterns of gastritis:\n\n• Non-atrophic pangastritis- mostly asymptomatic and sometimes associated with MALToma.\n\n• Corpus predominant gastritis- affects the corpus and causes atrophy. This leads to the loss of acid-producing parietal cells that are primarily found in the corpus. Hence, it is associated with decreased acid production. This is also a predisposing factor for the development of gastric cancer.\n\n• Antral predominant gastritis- spares the parietal cells. Hence, it is associated with increased acid production.\n\nOption A: Autoimmune gastritis is caused due to autoantibodies against the parietal cells. It is not associated with H. pylori.\n\nOption C: Granulomatous gastritis is characterized by the presence of granulomas It is associated with Crohn's disease and sarcoidosis.","t":"Peptic Ulcer Disease And Related Disorders","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A patient presents with frequent epigastric pain with burning sensation for the past 2 years. The pain usually occurs 2-3 hours after a meal and is relieved by taking food. He also says that he sometimes wakes up at night because of severe pain. The most probable cause for his symptoms is:","o":["Gastric ulcer","Duodenal ulcer","Esophagitis","GERD"],"a":1,"e":"Epigastric pain with a burning sensation that usually occurs 2-3 hours after a meal and is relieved by taking food suggestive of duodenal ulcer.\n\nThe following patterns of pain are usually seen in peptic ulcer disease:\n\n• Duodenal Ulcer- Pain occurs 1.5 to 3 hours after a meal and is frequently relieved by antacids or food. Pain may awaken the patient from sleep.\n\n• Gastric Ulcer- Pain/discomfort may be precipitated by food. Nausea and weight loss are common.\n\n• Perforated ulcer- The bed of the ulcer tunnels through the entire thickness of the gastric wall and opens into the peritoneum. This presents with sudden onset severe abdominal pain and leads to peritonitis. Radiography shows gas under the diaphragm.\n\n• Penetrating ulcer- It is a type of perforation where the ulcer tunnels into an adjacent organ like the pancreas/liver. In this case, the pain is no longer relieved by food or antacids and might radiate to the back.\n\nMany patients with peptic ulcers, especially elderly patients, may not have these symptoms and may directly present with a complication like bleeding or perforation.","t":"Peptic Ulcer Disease And Related Disorders","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is true about gastric ulcers?","o":["Usually occur in 4th decade","Rarely malignant","Low basal acid production","High stimulated acid production"],"a":2,"e":"Gastric ulcers are associated with low basal acid production and low stimulated acid production.\n\nGastric acid secretion occurs in two patterns:\n\n• Basal acid output- Occurs in a circadian pattern. It is mediated by ACh from the vagus and histamine.\n\n• Stimulated acid production- Occurs in response to food. It is mediated by gastrin in addition to other factors.\n\n Gastric Ulcer\n Duodenal Ulcer\n\nLess common\nMore common\n\nOccurs later in life, with a peak incidence in the 6th decade\nOccurs earlier in life\n\nBasal and stimulated acid production is low/normal, except in type 3 gastric ulcers\nBasal and nocturnal acid production is increased\n\nCan represent malignancy and should be biopsied\nRarely malignant\n\nMost common site: Antrum (distal to acid-secreting mucosa) on the lesser curvature\nMost common site: 1st part of the duodenum ( within 3cms from the pylorus)\n\nPain after food intake\nPain before food intake, and relief on taking food","t":"Peptic Ulcer Disease And Related Disorders","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"H pylori causes gastric mucosal injury due to the presence of:","o":["Cag A","VacA","LPS","Hop proteins"],"a":1,"e":"H.pylori produces vacuolating exotoxin (Vac A) which causes gastric mucosal injury. Vac A inhibits CD4+ T-cells and disrupts the function of B cells, macrophages & mast cells.\n\nCag A positive strains of H pylori cause an increased risk of gastric cancer.","t":"Peptic Ulcer Disease And Related Disorders","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"You are a first-year resident. You are asked to prescribe an H.pylori eradication regimen to a patient with peptic ulcer disease. Which of the following drugs will you not include in it?","o":["Bismuth subsalicylate","Erythromycin","Amoxicillin","Metronidazole"],"a":1,"e":"Erythromycin is not effective in H pylori eradication.\n\nNote:\n\nVonoprazan, a potassium-competitive acid blocker, has been recently approved by the FDA for the treatment of peptic acid-related disorders.","t":"Peptic Ulcer Disease And Related Disorders","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"In which of the following patients presenting with epigastric pain and discomfort is H.pylori eradication therapy not indicated:","o":["46-year-old man with a family history of gastric cancer in the father","40-year-old woman with rheumatoid arthritis on NSAIDs for 5 years","58-year-old man with 35-pack years history of smoking","36-year-old man with GERD on PPI's for 3 months"],"a":3,"e":"The need for H pylori eradication therapy in patients with GERD on long-term PPIs is not well established and is controversial.\n\nH pylori eradication therapy is offered to patients diagnosed with peptic ulcer disease (PUD) as it greatly decreases the recurrence of ulcers. In addition to this, it should also be offered to patients at increased risk of PUD and gastric cancer such as:\n\n• Patients with long-term NSAIDs usage. Eg. Rheumatoid arthritis\n\n• Patients taking gastric acid inhibitors for >1 year\n\n• Patients with increased exposure to risk factors of gastric cancers. Eg. heavy smokers, exposure to dust, coal\n\n• Patients with previous history/family history of gastric neoplasm","t":"Peptic Ulcer Disease And Related Disorders","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Eradication of H.pylori infection in patients with peptic ulcer disease is determined by?","o":["Serology","Urea breath test","Biopsy urease test","Culture"],"a":1,"e":"Eradication of H. pylori infection can be determined by a urea breath test.\n\nA stool antigen test can also be employed.","t":"Peptic Ulcer Disease And Related Disorders","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"H.pylori increases the risk of:","o":["GERD","Barrett's esophagus","Adenocarcinoma of cardia of stomach","Gastric MALT lymphoma"],"a":3,"e":"H.pylori increases the risk of gastric MALT lymphoma.\n\nH.pylori is protective against the following:\n\n• GERD\n\n• Barrett's esophagus\n\n• Adenocarcinoma of esophagus\n\n• Adenocarcinoma of the cardia of the stomach\n\nH. pylori are inversely related to gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD) and its sequelae, which include Barrett's esophagus and esophageal adenocarcinoma.\n\nH.pylori strains positive for Cag A may be particularly protective against the development of esophageal adenocarcinoma. Thus, colonization with H. pylori, particularly Cag A strains, may be protective against the more severe forms of GERD.\n\nEradication of H. pylori is associated with mild worsening of gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD) in patients with corpus-dominant or pangastritis. It improves symptoms in those with antral-dominant gastritis.","t":"Peptic Ulcer Disease And Related Disorders","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"The most common complication in patients with peptic ulcer disease is:","o":["Perforation","GI bleeding","Gastric outlet obstruction","Gastrocolic fistula"],"a":1,"e":"GI bleeding is the most common complication of peptic ulcer disease.\n\nOther complications:\n\n• Perforation- second most common\n\n• Gastrocolic fistulas\n\n• Gastric outlet obstruction\n\n• Rarely malignancy","t":"Peptic Ulcer Disease And Related Disorders","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A 47-year-old woman presented to the ER with an altered sensorium. Her husband reported that she has had abdominal pain, vomiting, and constipation for the past 4 days. She has been self-medicating for gastritis. Her lab investigations are as shown. Which of the following drugs is she most likely on that could have caused her symptoms?\n\nRenal function test:\n\n Sr. Creatinine- 6.4 mg/dl\n\n Sr. Urea- 50 mg/dl\n\nABG analysis:\n\n pH- 7.52\n\n PaCO2- 46.3 mmHg\n\n PO2- 71.1 mmHg\n\n HCO3¯- 36 mEq/L","o":["Aluminium hydroxide","Magnesium hydroxide","Calcium carbonate","Proton pump inhibitors"],"a":2,"e":"The given clinical features indicating hypercalcemia and investigations showing renal insufficiency, metabolic alkalosis are suggestive of milk-alkali syndrome. Long-term use of calcium carbonate can lead to this condition.\n\nIn susceptible individuals, there is a loss of negative feedback, leading to excess calcium absorption when there is excess intake. It results in the following triad of clinical features:\n\n• Hypercalcemia\n\n• Alkalosis- due to HCO3 reabsorption caused by PTH suppression, natriuresis, and water depletion\n\n• Renal failure- due to damage to renal tubules\n\nIt is reversible once ingestion of antacid is stopped. However, in severe cases, a chronic form called Burnett’s syndrome can develop. It is associated with irreversible renal damage.","t":"Peptic Ulcer Disease And Related Disorders","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A patient is admitted to the ICU after he suffered trauma to his head. This patient is prone to develop what type of gastric ulcers?","o":["Curling ulcers","Cushing ulcers","Stress ulcers","Refractory ulcers"],"a":1,"e":"The gastric ulcers associated with head trauma are Cushing ulcers. Head trauma leads to vagal stimulation which in turn leads to increased gastric acid production.\n\nOption A: Curling ulcers are seen in severe burns.\n\nOption C: Stress ulcers are seen in conditions like shock, sepsis, etc. They are thought to be caused by local ischemia and splanchnic vasoconstriction.\n\nOption D: Refractory ulcers are those that do not heal even after adequate medical therapy.","t":"Peptic Ulcer Disease And Related Disorders","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"You would diagnose a patient to have a refractory gastric ulcer if the ulcer fails to heal after:","o":["12 weeks","8 weeks","10 weeks","14 weeks"],"a":0,"e":"A gastric ulcer is said to be refractory if it fails to heal after 12 weeks.\n\nA refractory duodenal ulcer is that which fails to heal after 8 weeks.\n\nManagement of refractory PUD:\n\n• Rule out other diseases or causes\n\n• High dose PPI\n\n• Surgery","t":"Peptic Ulcer Disease And Related Disorders","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A middle-aged woman presents with acidity, abdominal pain, diarrhea, and weight loss for the past few months. Her symptoms are not relieved by taking antacids. An endoscopic evaluation shows ulcers on the second part of the duodenum. Which of the following conditions should be suspected in this patient?","o":["Stress ulcer","H. pylori infection","Malignant duodenal ulcer","Zollinger Ellison syndrome"],"a":3,"e":"The given scenario of symptoms not improving with antacids, associated diarrhea, and the unusual location of ulcers are highly suggestive of Zollinger Ellison syndrome.\n\nZollinger Ellison syndrome (ZES) is a peptic ulcer disease caused by unregulated gastrin release from a neuroendocrine tumor/gastrinoma. About 60% of the tumors are malignant, hence, it is important to identify ZES.\n\nThe clinical presentation of ZES is very similar to that of peptic ulcer disease. The features which help in suspecting ZES include:\n\n• Unusual locations- the second part of the duodenum and beyond\n\n• Refractory to medical therapy\n\n• Recurrence after acid-reducing surgery\n\n• Presenting with complications like bleeding and perforation\n\n• Not associated with H. pylori or NSAID ingestion\n\nPatients of ZES often have associated diarrhea and involvement of the esophagus (esophagitis, ulceration, or stricture).","t":"Peptic Ulcer Disease And Related Disorders","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A 35-year-old man presented with recurrent duodenal ulcers with poor response to medical therapy. He had a history of hyperparathyroidism and transsphenoidal prolactinoma resection. Which among the following is the next appropriate step?","o":["Urea breath test","Secretin test","Serum gastrin levels","Endoscopic ultrasound"],"a":2,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of Zollinger Ellison syndrome (ZES). The next appropriate step to evaluate the patients for ZES is by measuring the serum gastrin levels. History of hyperparathyroidism and transsphenoidal prolactinoma resection indicates MEN1 syndrome. ZES is commonly associated with MEN1.\n\nThe normal gastrin levels are <150 pg/mL. An increase of >10 times the normal levels is highly suggestive of ZES.\n\nSecretin test is a gastrin provocative test. It is done when the gastrin levels and the acid secretory studies are inconclusive for ZES. It is the most sensitive and specific gastrin provocative test for the diagnosis of gastrinoma.\n\nEndoscopic ultrasound is done to localize the tumor. It is done after the diagnosis of ZES has been established.","t":"Peptic Ulcer Disease And Related Disorders","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following investigations employs somatostatin analogues to detect gastrinomas?","o":["CECT","PET/CT with Ga-DOTATATE","OctreoScan","Endoscopy"],"a":2,"e":"OctreoScan detects gastrinomas using their property of uptake of somatostatin analogues.\n\nOctreoScan detects endocrine tumors that express receptors for somatostatin in conditions such as Zollinger-Ellison syndrome (ZES) and multiple endocrine neoplasia (MEN) syndromes.\n\nOther modalities used for localizing the tumor in ZES include:\n\n• Endoscopic ultrasound (EUS)\n\n• PET/CT with Ga-DOTATATE is the investigation of choice when available.\n\nNote: PET/CT with Ga-DOTATATE is the gold-standard test for localizing tumors in pheochromocytoma.","t":"Peptic Ulcer Disease And Related Disorders","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following viruses cause infectious gastritis in immunocompromised patients?","o":["Cytomegalovirus","Norovirus","Enterovirus","Coxsackie virus"],"a":0,"e":"Viruses that cause infectious gastritis in immunocompromised individuals include cytomegalovirus and Herpes simplex virus.","t":"Peptic Ulcer Disease And Related Disorders","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"An anemic patient on evaluation showed macrocytes with hypersegmented neutrophils on peripheral smear. Serology revealed anti-parietal cell antibodies. Which of the following is most likely to be associated with her condition?","o":["Zollinger Ellison syndrome","VIPoma","Type A gastritis","Type B gastritis"],"a":2,"e":"The given peripheral smear findings and serology are suggestive of pernicious anemia. It is usually seen in type A gastritis.\n\nType A autoimmune gastritis is caused by circulating antibodies against the parietal cells. The destruction of parietal cells leads to the deficiency of intrinsic factor that is necessary for the absorption of vitamin B12. The resulting B12 deficiency causes pernicious anemia.","t":"Peptic Ulcer Disease And Related Disorders","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"An 84-year-old woman living in an assisted care facility is rushed to the hospital with an upper GI bleed. On arrival, she is febrile and the nurse notes a sudden spurt of blood from her nasogastric tube. The following investigations are done. Which of the following is responsible for this patient's condition?\n\nCRP- 23 mg/L\n\nUpper GI endoscopy- Extensive gastric ulceration with necrotic tissue and edematous mucosa\n\nCT abdomen- Thickened gastric wall","o":["H.pylori","E.coli","Cytomegalovirus","Herpes simplex virus"],"a":1,"e":"The given clinical scenario along with her endoscopy findings are suggestive of phlegmonous gastritis. It is caused due to E.coli.\n\nPhlegmonous gastritis is a potentially life-threatening disorder characterized by marked and diffuse acute inflammatory infiltrates of the entire gastric wall accompanied by necrosis. It usually affects the immunocompromised and elderly individuals.\n\nOrganisms associated with phlegmonous gastritis are:\n\n• E.coli\n\n• Streptococci\n\n• Staphylococci\n\n• Proteus\n\n• Haemophilus species\n\nOther uncommon forms of gastritis are:\n\n• Eosinophilic gastritis: Presents as intestinal obstruction\n\n• Russel’s body gastritis: Pseudotumor on endoscopy\n\n• Granulomatous gastritis is seen in Crohn’s disease\n\n• Lymphocytic gastritis is seen in celiac disease","t":"Peptic Ulcer Disease And Related Disorders","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A patient presents with chronic diarrhea, bloating sensation, and weight loss. History is significant for stools that are bulky, pale, foul-smelling, and difficult to flush. Further testing reveals fecal fat excretion of 8.2g/day. Which of the following would be a differential diagnosis?","o":["Short bowel syndrome","Primary lactase deficiency","Pernicious anemia","Wilson's disease"],"a":0,"e":"The above scenario is suggestive of steatorrhoea, which is seen in short bowel syndrome.\n\nSteatorrhoea is defined as excess fat in stools (>7g/day) due to poor absorption of lipids. It is characterized by stools are bulky, pale, foul-smelling, and difficult to flush.\n\nShort bowel syndrome (SBS) is a malabsorption disorder caused by a lack of functional small intestine. It can lead to the typical features of malabsorption such as abdominal pain, diarrhoea, steatorrhoea, weight loss and anaemia.\n\nOption B: Primary lactase deficiency leads to defective absorption of lactose. It does not affect lipid absorption.\n\nOption C: Pernicious anemia is due to an absence of gastric parietal cell-intrinsic factor which is necessary for the absorption of vitamin B12.\n\nOption D: Wilson's disease is associated with increased absorption of copper and is not a part of malabsorption syndrome.","t":"Evaluation Of Diarrhea","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is incorrect about lactose intolerance?","o":["It may be seen in celiac disease","It can mimic irritable bowel syndrome","Delayed gastric emptying decreases the symptoms","Rapid small-intestinal transit time decreases the symptoms"],"a":3,"e":"Rapid small-intestinal transit time increases the symptoms of lactose intolerance.\n\nLactose intolerance can be due to:\n\n• Primary lactase deficiency- a genetically determined deficiency or absence of lactase enzyme\n\n• Secondary lactase deficiency- due to small intestinal mucosal diseases such as celiac disease\n\nFactors affecting symptoms of lactose intolerance:\n\n• Amount of lactose in the diet.\n\n• Rate of gastric emptying- symptoms are more likely when gastric emptying is rapid (post-gastrectomy and skimmed milk intake).\n\n• Small intestinal transit time- more rapid small intestinal transit time makes symptoms more likely.\n\n• Antibiotic use- results in decreased colonic microflora which in turn decreases the production of easily absorbed short-chain fatty acids from non-absorbed lactose. This can worsen the symptoms.","t":"Evaluation Of Diarrhea","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is unlikely to be present in a patient with large bowel diarrhea?","o":["Steatorrhea","Small volume stools","Blood and mucus in stools","Tenesmus"],"a":0,"e":"Small bowel diarrhea is associated with steatorrhea, not large bowel diarrhea.\n\nFeatures\nSmall bowel\nLarge bowel\n\nVolume of stools\nLarge\nSmall\n\nFrequency of stool\nNo change\nIncreased\n\nBlood and mucus in stools\nAbsent\nPresent\n\nTenesmus\nAbsent\nPresent\n\nSteatorrhea\nPresent\nAbsent\n\nVomiting\nMay be present\nInfrequent\n\nTenesmus refers to the constant urge to defecate with little or no stool passed.","t":"Evaluation Of Diarrhea","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"All are true regarding osmotic diarrhea except :","o":["Fasting improves the symptoms","Stool osmolar gap &gt;100 mOsm","Increased osmolarity of stool is due to unabsorbed dietary nutrients","Are associated with enterotoxigenic E. coli"],"a":3,"e":"Enterotoxigenic E. coli (ETEC) is associated with secretory diarrhea.\n\nFeature\nOsmotic diarrhea\nSecretory diarrhea\n\nDefinition\nDiarrhea due to poorly absorbed osmotically active substances in the colonic lumen.\nDiarrhea due to excessive secretion of NaCl, HCO3-, and water into the gut.\n\nFasting\nImproves symptoms\nNo effect\n\nStool osmolar gap (mOsm)\n>100\n<50\n\nCauses\nMalabsorption syndrome\n\nTumors - VIPoma\n\nToxins - ETEC, V. cholera\n\nStool osmolar gap = measured osmolality - calculated osmolality\n\n = (290 to 300) - 2 (stool Na+ + stool K+)","t":"Evaluation Of Diarrhea","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A 32-year-old woman presents with complaints of abdominal pain and recurrent diarrhea for several months. History reveals that she regularly goes on camping trips. Stool osmolality was 200 mOsm/kg H2O and the stool osmolar gap was 20 mOsm/kg H2O. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Secretory diarrhea","Osmotic diarrhea","Factitious diarrhea","Infectious diarrhea"],"a":2,"e":"The most likely diagnosis is factitious diarrhea.\n\nFactitious diarrhoea is a form of Münchausen's syndrome. In patients with diarrhea, stool osmolality is always >290-300 mOsm/kg H2O. A stool osmolality of <290 mOsm/kg H2O is suggestive of dilution with urine or water to the sample, by the patient. In this case stool osmolality can be lower than plasma osmolality (<250 mOsm/kg) or very high (>400 mOsm/kg) due to purposeful dilution with hypotonic/concentrated solute by the patient to the stool sample.\n\nThe stool osmolar gap is used to differentiate between osmotic and secretory diarrhea. A gap of:\n\n• <50 suggests secretory diarrhoea\n\n• >100 suggests osmotic diarrhoea\n\nStool osmolar gap = measured osmolality - calculated osmolality\n\n = (290 to 300) - 2 (stool Na+ + stool K+)","t":"Evaluation Of Diarrhea","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"All are true regarding bile acid diarrhea except :","o":["Seen in limited ileal disease","No or mild steatorrhea","Normal bile acid pool size","Good response to low fat diet"],"a":3,"e":"Bile acid diarrhea has no response to a low-fat diet.\n\nFeatures\nBile acid diarrhea\nFatty acid diarrhea\n\nThe extent of ileal disease\nLimited\nExtensive\n\nIleal bile acid absorption\nReduced\nReduced\n\nFecal bile acid loss compensated by hepatic synthesis\nYes\nNo\n\nFaecal bile acid excretion\nIncreased\nIncreased\n\nSteatorrhea\nNone or mild\n>20gm\n\nResponse to cholestyramine\nYes\nNo\n\nResponse to a low-fat diet\nNo\nYes","t":"Evaluation Of Diarrhea","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is the most common viral cause of diarrhea in adults?","o":["Enterovirus","Rotavirus","Norovirus","Cytomegalovirus"],"a":2,"e":"The most common viral cause of diarrhoea in adults (and overall) is norovirus (Norwalk virus).\n\nIn children, the most common cause is rotavirus.","t":"Evaluation Of Diarrhea","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A father brings his 12-year-old son with complaints of severe abdominal pain and diarrhea. The pain is localized to the right iliac fossa. USG abdomen reveals enlarged mesenteric lymph nodes and a normal appendix. Which of the following is the likely causative organism?","o":["Yersinia enterocolitica","Campylobacter jejuni","Shigella dysenteriae","Salmonella enterica"],"a":0,"e":"Yersinia enterocolitica causes diarrhea with symptoms resembling appendicitis, also called pseudoappendicitis.\n\nThe patients present with abdominal pain which can be localized to the right iliac fossa, thus mimicking appendicitis. USG shows a normal appendix and may show enlarged mesenteric lymph nodes.\n\nAppendectomy is not indicated for Yersinia infection pseudoappendicitis.\n\nCausative agent\nSpecial features\n\nCampylobacter jejuni\n\nTriggers Guillain–Barré syndrome,\n\nSmall bowel lymphoproliferative disorder\n\nShigella dysenteriae\nSevere colitis affecting all 4 layers\n\nClostridium difficile\nToxic megacolon\n\nNontyphoid Salmonella\nAortic /Endovascular infection","t":"Evaluation Of Diarrhea","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not associated with travellers' diarrhoea?","o":["Shigella","Norovirus","Giardia","Enterovirus"],"a":3,"e":"Enterovirus is not an etiological agent for travelers' diarrhea.\n\nThe various causes of travelers' diarrhea are as follows:\n\n• Bacterial\n\n• Enterotoxigenic E. coli (ETEC)- most common\n\n• Shigella\n\n• Salmonella\n\n• S. aureus\n\n• B. cereus\n\n• Viral\n\n• Norovirus\n\n• Rotavirus\n\n• Parasite\n\n• Giardia lamblia\n\n• Entamoeba histolytica.","t":"Evaluation Of Diarrhea","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A child was brought with complaints of watery diarrhea for 48 hours. On examination, tachycardia and decreased skin turgor were present. Stool microscopy revealed the following organisms. Which of the given findings is likely to be seen in this patient?","o":["Hypokalemic acidosis","Hypokalemic alkalosis","Hyperkalemic acidosis","Hyperkalemic alkalosis"],"a":0,"e":"The image shows the characteristic fish in stream appearance of Vibrio, suggestive of cholera. Diarrhea due to Vibrio cholerae results in hypokalemic acidosis.\n\nCholera exotoxin activates adenylate cyclase (↑ cAMP) leading to loss of bicarbonate, water, and electrolytes leading to hypokalemic acidosis.\n\nNote: The other cause for hypokalemic acidosis is renal tubular acidosis.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0db7402f334b4b6798a977288818ea3a.JPEG"],"t":"Evaluation Of Diarrhea","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A 19-year-old woman presents to the ER with complaints of acute onset nausea, vomiting, and diarrhea. She gives a history of consumption of street food earlier the same day. Which of the following organisms is likely to be responsible for her condition?","o":["Vibrio cholerae","Salmonella enterocolitis","Enterotoxigenic E. coli","Staphylococcus aureus"],"a":3,"e":"The above scenario is suggestive of acute onset gastroenteritis. Since the symptoms appeared shortly after ingestion, they are caused due to preformed toxins. From the given options, Staphylococcus aureus is associated with preformed toxins.\n\nEtiology of gastroenteritis are as follows :\n\n1. Preformed toxin:\n\n• Staphylococcus aureus\n\n• Bacillus cereus\n\n• Clostridium perfringens\n\n2. Toxin forming:\n\n• Vibrio cholerae\n\n• Enterotoxigenic E. coli (ETEC)\n\n3. Invasive:\n\n• Shigella\n\n• Salmonella\n\n• Yersinia\n\n• Campylobacter","t":"Evaluation Of Diarrhea","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not a diagnostic criterion for irritable bowel syndrome?","o":["Abdominal pain related to defecation","Persistent diarrhoea after a 48 hour fast","Change in frequency of stool","Change in form of stool"],"a":1,"e":"Persistent diarrhoea after a 48-hour fast is not a diagnostic criteria for IBS.","t":"Irritable Bowel Syndrome","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"The most common pattern seen in irritable bowel syndrome patients is _______","o":["Diarrhoea only","Constipation only","Prolonged constipation followed by diarrhoea","Constipation alternating with episodes of diarrhoea"],"a":3,"e":"The most common pattern in IBS patients is constipation alternating with episodes of diarrhoea.\n\nBased on bowel movement patterns, IBS is classified as:\n\n• IBS-C: Irritable bowel syndrome-constipation predominant\n\n• IBS-D: Irritable bowel syndrome-diarrhoea predominant\n\n• IBS-M: Irritable bowel syndrome-mixed\n\nThe majority of patients change subtypes over the course of illness.\n\nNote: Abdominal pain is a pre-requisite for the diagnosis of IBS. This means that for the diagnosis of IBS, recurrent abdominal pain must be present.","t":"Irritable Bowel Syndrome","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A patient presents with a 3-month history of crampy abdominal pain associated with diarrhea. Which of the following features, if present, will support a diagnosis of irritable bowel syndrome?","o":["Steatorrhea","Nocturnal diarrhoea","Persistent diarrhoea after a 48 hour fast","Onset of symptoms during a period of stress"],"a":3,"e":"The onset of symptoms during a period of stress or emotional upset is a feature that supports a diagnosis of irritable bowel syndrome (IBS).\n\nFeatures in favour of IBS:\n\n• Abdominal pain that improves/worsens with defecation\n\n• Association with stress or emotional symptoms\n\n• Small volume of stools\n\nFeatures not in favour of IBS:\n\n• Onset of symptoms in old age\n\n• Presence of fever and weight loss\n\n• Persistent diarrhoea after a 48h fast\n\n• Nocturnal diarrhoea\n\n• Steatorrhoea","t":"Irritable Bowel Syndrome","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not implicated in the pathogenesis of irritable bowel syndrome?","o":["Visceral hypersensitivity","Activation of mid-cingulate cortex","Increased serotonin","Decreased colonic motor potentials"],"a":3,"e":"Decreased colonic motor potentials are not implicated in the pathogenesis of irritable bowel syndrome (IBS). On the contrary, increased colonic motor potentials are seen.\n\nThe pathogenesis of IBS is poorly understood. However, the following have been implicated in its pathogenesis:\n\n• Increased colonic motor potentials lead to increased high-amplitude propagating contractions (HAPCs) which causes diarrhea.\n\n• Visceral hypersensitivity leads to exaggerated sensory responses to visceral stimulation by food. This explains the postprandial symptoms in IBS.\n\n• CNS dysregulation - dysfunctional activation of the mid-cingulate cortex and prefrontal lobes leads to increased perception of visceral pain.\n\n• Abnormal serotonin pathways - serotonin-containing enterochromaffin cells are increased in IBS patients.\n\n• Altered gut flora","t":"Irritable Bowel Syndrome","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A 35-year-old housewife has been diagnosed with irritable bowel syndrome - diarrhea predominant. Which neurotransmitter is associated with this condition?","o":["Acetylcholine","Neuropeptide Y","Vasoactive intestinal peptide","Serotonin"],"a":3,"e":"Serotonin is associated with irritable bowel syndrome diarrhoea type (IBS-D).\n\nAbnormal serotonin pathways is one of the causes for symptoms in IBS. The serotonin containing enterochromaffin cells are increased in a subset of IBS-D patients. This provides a rationale for the use of serotonin antagonists in the treatment of this condition.","t":"Irritable Bowel Syndrome","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following parts of the brain are associated with irritable bowel syndrome?\n\n1. Paracentral lobule\n\n2. Mid cingulate cortex\n\n3. Prefrontal lobe\n\n4. Temporal cortex","o":["1 &amp; 2","3 &amp; 4","1 &amp; 4","2 &amp; 3"],"a":3,"e":"Mid cingulate cortex and prefrontal lobe are associated with IBS.\n\nMid cingulate cortex: Modulation of this region is associated with changes in the subjective unpleasantness of pain.\n\nPrefrontal lobe: Dysfunction of this area leads to the increased perception of visceral pain.","t":"Irritable Bowel Syndrome","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not a risk factor for post-infectious irritable bowel syndrome?","o":["Smoking","Depression","Male gender","Toxicity of infecting bacterial strain"],"a":2,"e":"Male gender is not a risk factor for post-infectious IBS.\n\nPost-infectious IBS develops following a gastrointestinal infection with microbes such as Campylobacter, Salmonella and Shigella.\n\nRisk factors for developing post-infectious IBS:\n\n• Young age and female gender\n\n• Prolonged duration of illness\n\n• Toxicity of infecting bacterial strain\n\n• Smoking\n\n• Depression\n\n• Hypochondriasis","t":"Irritable Bowel Syndrome","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"In a suspected case of irritable bowel syndrome, which of the following investigations is not recommended?","o":["Complete blood count","Sigmoidoscopy","Stool examination for ova and parasites","Stool fat test"],"a":3,"e":"A stool fat test is not recommended in a suspected case of irritable bowel syndrome (IBS).\n\nIBS is a clinical diagnosis that can be made without performing many tests if the patient presents with typical symptoms. However, it is recommended to perform the following tests in most patients to rule out other conditions:\n\n• Complete blood count\n\n• Sigmoidoscopic examination\n\n• Stool examination for ova and parasites\n\n• 3-week lactose-free diet\n\nIn patients >40 years, air-contrast barium enema or colonoscopy should also be performed.","t":"Irritable Bowel Syndrome","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is a marker of inflammatory bowel disease, which helps to rule out irritable bowel syndrome?","o":["Faecal fat","Faecal lactoferrin","Faecal trypsin","Faecal chymotrypsin"],"a":1,"e":"Fecal lactoferrin is the marker of IBD that is used to rule out irritable bowel syndrome. Fecal lactoferrin is a highly sensitive and specific marker for detecting intestinal inflammation.\n\nFecal calprotectin is another marker of IBD and it is used to rule out irritable bowel syndrome and bacterial overgrowth. It correlates well with histologic inflammation, predicts relapses, and detects pouchitis.\n\nOption A: Fecal fat is normally digested by pancreatic lipase and absorbed from the jejunum. In disorders of exocrine pancreatic function, fat absorption decreases, resulting in steatorrhea and increased fecal fat.\n\nOptions C & D: Fecal trypsin, chymotrypsin are pancreatic enzymes, therefore a decrease in fecal levels below normal suggests a disorder of the exocrine pancreas.","t":"Irritable Bowel Syndrome","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements is false regarding the FODMAPs diet recommended for a patient with irritable bowel syndrome?","o":["It includes fermentable oligosaccharides","Diet low in FODMAPs reduces symptoms in IBS patients","Sorbitol is an example of FODMAP","FODMAPs are highly absorbed by the small intestine"],"a":3,"e":"FODMAPs are poorly absorbed in the small intestine.\n\nFODMAPs stands for Fermentable Oligosaccharides, Disaccharides, Monosaccharides, And Polyols. Foodstuff containing lactose, fructose, and sorbitol are some examples of FODMAPs.\n\nSince FODMAPs are poorly absorbed, they are fermented by gut bacteria in the colon to produce gas and osmotically active carbohydrates. So, a diet low in FODMAPs is recommended to reduce symptoms in IBS patients.","t":"Irritable Bowel Syndrome","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A 35-year-old woman with constipation-predominant irritable bowel syndrome presents with worsening abdominal pain and bloating. Which of the following drugs can be prescribed for her condition?","o":["Loperamide","Lubiprostone","Alosetron","Eluxadoline"],"a":1,"e":"Lubiprostone is used in the treatment of constipation-predominant IBS.\n\nLubiprostone is a chloride channel activator that promotes the secretion of water and chloride into the bowel and improves bowel movements.\n\nLoperamide, alosetron, and eluxadoline (opioid mu receptor agonist) are used in the treatment of diarrhea-predominant IBS.","t":"Irritable Bowel Syndrome","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"You have prescribed paroxetine to a patient with irritable bowel syndrome. This medication is most effective in reducing which of the following symptoms?","o":["Diarrhoea","Constipation","Abdominal pain","Bloating"],"a":1,"e":"Paroxetine is most effective in treating constipation. It is a selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor (SSRI) that accelerates oro-caecal transit.\n\nNote: Citalopram is an SSRI that is effective in the management of pain in IBS. It blunts perception of rectal distension and reduces the magnitude of gastrocolic reflex.","t":"Irritable Bowel Syndrome","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Drug 'X', a 5 HT3 receptor antagonist, is prescribed to treat a patient with diarrhea-predominant irritable bowel syndrome. Identify the drug 'X'.","o":["Alosetron","Tegaserod","Lubiprostone","Ondansetron"],"a":0,"e":"Alosetron is a 5-HT3 receptor antagonist used in the treatment of IBS–D.\n\nOptions B and C: Tegaserod and lubiprostone are used in the treatment of constipation-predominant irritable bowel syndrome (IBS–C).\n\nOption D: Ondansetron is used in the treatment of nausea and vomiting.","t":"Irritable Bowel Syndrome","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A patient with irritable bowel disease was started on a drug. A few days after administration of the drug, the patient complained of acute GI distress suggestive of ischemic colitis. What was the drug taken by the patient?","o":["Lubiprostone","Linaclotide","Alosetron","Tegaserod"],"a":2,"e":"Ischaemic colitis is a side-effect of alosetron.\n\nAlosetron is a 5-HT3 receptor antagonist that is used to reduce diarrhoea in irritable bowel syndrome.\n\nOption A: Lubiprostone is associated with nausea and diarrhea.\n\nOption B: Linaclotide is associated with diarrhea.\n\nOption D: Tegaserod is associated with cardiac toxicity.","t":"Irritable Bowel Syndrome","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A 28-year-old female patient comes to you for advice. She is a known case of irritable bowel syndrome. She asks you when is the best time to take antispasmodics for her postprandial abdominal pain. What would you suggest?","o":["30 minutes before meals","30 minutes after meals","Along with meals","When the pain starts"],"a":0,"e":"The best time to take antispasmodics for postprandial pain is 30 min before meals.\n\nAntispasmodics such as dicyclomine inhibit the gastrocolic reflex and thus reduce pain due to intestinal spasms. It is best taken 30 min before meals so that the effective blood levels are achieved shortly before the anticipated onset of pain.","t":"Irritable Bowel Syndrome","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following genes is associated with Crohn's disease?","o":["NOD2","JAK2","BRAF V600E","STAT3"],"a":0,"e":"The gene associated with Crohn's disease is the NOD2 gene. It is present on chromosome 16.\n\nNucleotide-binding oligomerization domain 2 (NOD2) acts as a cytoplasmic sensor of microbial products. Defective NOD2 gene results in defective innate immunity. When the innate immunity is unable to clear the bacteria, there is an exaggerated response of the immune system (T-cells) resulting in damage to the host tissue.\n\nOption B: JAK2 mutation is associated with polycythemia vera.\n\nOption C: BRAF V600E mutation is associated with melanoma, papillary thyroid cancer, and serous ovarian cancers.\n\nOption D: STAT3 mutations are associated with the autosomal dominant hyper-IgE syndrome.","t":"Inflammatory Bowel Disease   Clinical Features And Diagnosis","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following conditions are not associated with inflammatory bowel disease?","o":["Turner's syndrome","Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome","Hermansky-Pudlak syndrome","Klinefelter syndrome"],"a":3,"e":"Klinefelter syndrome is not commonly associated with inflammatory bowel disease (IBD).\n\nThe genetic disorders associated with IBD are:\n\n• Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome\n\n• Immunodysregulation polyendocrinopathy enteropathy X-linked (IPEX) syndrome\n\n• Turner's syndrome\n\n• Hermansky-Pudlak syndrome\n\n• Glycogen storage disease","t":"Inflammatory Bowel Disease   Clinical Features And Diagnosis","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"In patients who have undergone an appendectomy, there is a lower risk for developing which of the following diseases?","o":["Ulcerative colitis","Crohn’s disease","Carcinoma colon","Diverticulosis"],"a":0,"e":"Appendectomy is protective against ulcerative colitis.\n\nSmoking is also seen to have a protective effect on ulcerative colitis but it is a risk factor for developing Crohn's disease.","t":"Inflammatory Bowel Disease   Clinical Features And Diagnosis","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Early-onset inflammatory bowel disease is associated with:","o":["JAK2 mutation","FOXP3 mutation","IL-10 deficiency","NOD 2 mutation"],"a":2,"e":"Early-onset IBD is due to IL-10 receptor dysfunction and IL-10 deficiency. They are associated with severe refractory IBD in early life.","t":"Inflammatory Bowel Disease   Clinical Features And Diagnosis","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is true about immune dysregulation polyendocrinopathy enteropathy X-linked (IPEX) syndrome?","o":["B cell defect","Due to FOXP3 gene mutation","Associated with Crohn's disease-like enteropathy","Recurrent staphylococcal skin infections"],"a":1,"e":"IPEX (immune dysregulation, polyendocrinopathy, enteropathy, and X-linked) syndrome is caused by mutations in the FOXP3 gene, which leads to a deficiency of T-regulatory cells, resulting in immune dysfunction.\n\nIPEX is associated with:\n\n• Ulcerative colitis-like autoimmune enteropathy\n\n• Neonatal type 1 DM\n\n• Thyroiditis\n\n• Dermatitis","t":"Inflammatory Bowel Disease   Clinical Features And Diagnosis","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following infections should you rule out before diagnosing a patient with IBD on the basis of clinical symptoms and biopsy findings?","o":["Mycobacterium Avium","Herpes simplex","Campylobacter jejuni","Cytomegalovirus"],"a":2,"e":"Campylobacter enteritis should be ruled out before diagnosing a patient with IBD.\n\nCampylobacter enteritis can mimic the clinical presentation and biopsy appearance of ulcerative colitis or Crohn’s disease (IBD). Hence, it should be ruled out, especially in persons with a history of:\n\n• Foreign travel\n\n• Significant animal contact\n\n• Immunodeficiency\n\n• Exposure incurring a high risk of transmission.\n\nIt can also cause a relapse of established ulcerative colitis.\n\nA definitive diagnosis of campylobacter infection can be made by culture or real-time PCR.","t":"Inflammatory Bowel Disease   Clinical Features And Diagnosis","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following serological markers is positive in ulcerative colitis?","o":["Anti-pancreatic antibody","Anti-flagellin antibody","ASCA","ANCA"],"a":3,"e":"Antineutrophil cytoplasmic antibody (ANCA) positivity is a feature of ulcerative colitis.\n\nASCA (anti-Saccharomyces cerevisiae antibody), anti-pancreatic antibody, and anti-flagellin antibody positivity are seen in Crohn's disease.\n\nSerological Markers in IBD\n\nCrohn's disease\nUlcerative colitis\n\nASCA\n\nOMP-C\n\nAnti I2\n\nAnti-pancreatic antibody\n\nAnti-flagellin antibody\n\np-ANCA\n\nAnti-goblet cell Antibody","t":"Inflammatory Bowel Disease   Clinical Features And Diagnosis","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following sites is most commonly involved in Crohn’s disease?","o":["Terminal ileum","Rectosigmoid colon","Transverse colon","Proximal duodenum"],"a":0,"e":"The most commonly affected site in Crohn's disease is the terminal ileum.\n\nOther commonly affected sites in Crohn's disease include the small intestine and the colon. Although it can affect any part of the GI tract from mouth to anus, the rectum is usually spared.\n\nOn the other hand, the rectum is almost always involved in ulcerative colitis.","t":"Inflammatory Bowel Disease   Clinical Features And Diagnosis","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A patient presented with acute severe small bowel obstruction to the ER. An emergency small bowel resection was done. The resected specimen is shown below. What could be the likely diagnosis?","o":["Ulcerative colitis","Crohn's disease","Intestinal TB","Intestinal lymphoma"],"a":1,"e":"The above specimen of the small bowel shows creeping fat which is a feature of Crohn's disease (CD).\n\nMesenteric fat is normally seen along the longitudinal axis of the intestine. However, in Crohn's disease, excessive fibrofatty proliferation results in mesenteric fat wrapping around the inflamed bowel. This is called creeping fat.\n\nStrictures and fistulae are the commonly associated complications of long-standing Crohn's disease. Strictures can cause bowel obstruction.\n\nOther gross features seen in Crohn's disease are:\n\n• Transmural inflammation- full-thickness inflammation involving all the layers of the bowel\n\n• Aphthous ulcers- small, shallow ulcers surrounded by erythema\n\n• Longitudinal ulcers- linear or serpiginous deep ulcers\n\n• Skip lesions- involvement of discontinuous segments of the bowel with intervening normal mucosa\n\n• Cobblestone appearance- deep, longitudinal ulcerations interlaced with intervening normal mucosa\n\nThe image below shows cobblestone appearance in Crohn's disease.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/90f2f8714ea142eeb82b32536c7763f6.PNG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cc3fbf00b245495ead14949edb9dd9a5x640x640.JPEG"],"t":"Inflammatory Bowel Disease   Clinical Features And Diagnosis","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A 28-year-old man presented with diarrhea, bloody stools, weight loss, and intermittent fever for the past 2 months. Endoscopy revealed the following. What is the most probable diagnosis?","o":["Crohn’s disease","Intestinal TB","Ulcerative colitis","Salmonella infection"],"a":0,"e":"The clinical presentation along with the endoscopy image showing a cobblestone appearance are suggestive of Crohn's disease (CD).\n\nEndoscopic findings in Crohn's disease include:\n\n• Aphthous ulcers - small, shallow ulcers surrounded by erythema\n\n• Longitudinal ulcers - linear or serpiginous deep ulcers\n\n• Cobblestone appearance - deep, longitudinal ulcerations interlaced with intervening normal mucosa\n\n• Strictures\n\n• Fistulas\n\nStrictures and fistulae are the commonly associated complications of long-standing Crohn's disease.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f7c558150e1b49c28456993999cf740f.PNG"],"t":"Inflammatory Bowel Disease   Clinical Features And Diagnosis","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A 70-year-old chronic smoker is being evaluated for chronic diarrhea, steatorrhea, and weight loss. On examination, pallor was noted. Peripheral smear revealed macrocytes and hypersegmented neutrophils. Stool examination showed elevated calprotectin and a positive guaiac test. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Ulcerative colitis","Diverticulitis","Crohn’s disease","Irritable bowel syndrome"],"a":2,"e":"The above scenario is suggestive of Crohn's disease presenting as jejunoileitis. This is evidenced by the presence of features of malabsorption such as steatorrhea, chronic diarrhea, and vitamin B12 deficiency anemia.\n\nThe fecal calprotectin and lactoferrin are elevated in inflammatory conditions like inflammatory bowel disease, but not in irritable bowel syndrome.\n\nThe stool guaiac test or guaiac fecal occult blood test (gFOBT) reveals the presence of microscopic levels of blood suggesting Crohn's disease. Gross bleeding per rectum is more characteristic of ulcerative colitis.\n\nAdditionally, smoking is a risk factor for Crohn's disease. However, it is a protective factor in ulcerative colitis.","t":"Inflammatory Bowel Disease   Clinical Features And Diagnosis","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A 30-year-old woman presented with 7-8 episodes of bloody diarrhea. On examination, she was pale and febrile, with a pulse rate of 104 per minute. As she was not responding to broad-spectrum antibiotics, a colonoscopy was done which revealed ulcerations and pseudopolyps. What is the most probable diagnosis?","o":["Moderate Crohn’s disease","Severe Crohn’s disease","Moderate ulcerative colitis","Severe ulcerative colitis"],"a":3,"e":"Tachycardia, bloody diarrhea, pseudopolyps, and ulcerations on colonoscopy strongly suggest severe ulcerative colitis.\n\nGiven below is the Truelove and Witts severity index for ulcerative colitis.\n\nMild\nModerate\nSevere\n\nBowel movements\n< 4 per day\n4 – 6 per day\n> 6 per day\n\nBlood in stool\nSmall\nModerate\nSevere\n\nFever\nNone\n< 37.5ºC\n> 37.5ºC\n\nTachycardia\nNone\n< 90 mean pulse\n>90 mean pulse\n\nAnemia\nMild\nHb > 75% of normal Hb\nHb < 75% of normal Hb\n\nESR\n< 30mm\n < 30mm\n > 30mm\n\nEndoscopic appearance\n\nErythema\n\nDecreased vascular markings\n\nFine granularity\n\nMarked erythema\n\nAbsent vascular markings\n\nCoarse granularity\n\nContact bleeding\n\nNo ulcerations\n\nSpontaneous bleeding\n\nUlcerations","t":"Inflammatory Bowel Disease   Clinical Features And Diagnosis","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A young man presents to the OPD with complaints of chronic diarrhea, right lower abdominal pain, and weight loss. On examination, you note a low-grade fever and a palpable mass in the right lower quadrant. Colonoscopy revealed sparing of the rectum. The following endoscopic findings may also be seen in his condition except:","o":["Aphthous ulcer","Longitudinal ulcers","Fine granular mucosa","Stricture"],"a":2,"e":"The above clinical scenario is suggestive of Crohn's disease presenting as ileocolitis. Fine granular mucosa is seen in ulcerative colitis, not Crohn's disease.\n\nIleocolitis presents as right lower abdominal pain, fever, and diarrhea. Pain is usually colicky and is relieved by defecation. It may mimic acute appendicitis in the early stages.\n\nEndoscopic findings in Crohn's disease include:\n\n• Aphthous ulcers - Small, shallow ulcers surrounded by erythema\n\n• Longitudinal ulcers - Linear or serpiginous deep ulcers\n\n• Cobblestone appearance - Deep, longitudinal ulcerations interlaced with intervening normal mucosa\n\n• Strictures\n\n• Fistulas.","t":"Inflammatory Bowel Disease   Clinical Features And Diagnosis","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"What is the earliest radiographic feature of Crohn's disease?","o":["Ram's horn sign","Rose-thorn appearance","Aphthous ulcer","String sign of Kantor"],"a":2,"e":"Aphthous ulcers seen in Crohn's disease are the earliest radiological signs on small bowel follow-through study.\n\nThey are shallow ulcers that are often multiple and separated by normal intervening mucosa. They are also the earliest macroscopic findings of Crohn's disease.\n\nFeatures on barium small bowel follow-through in Crohn's disease include:\n\n• Mucosal ulcers:\n\n• Aphthous ulcers initially\n\n• Deep ulcers (more than 3 mm depth)\n\n• Longitudinal fissures\n\n• Transverse stripes\n\n• Severe mucosal ulcers lead to cobblestone appearance\n\n• Sinus tracts and fistulae\n\n• Creeping fat: widely separated loops of the bowel due to fibrofatty proliferation\n\n• Thickened folds due to edema\n\n• Pseudo-diverticula formation: due to contraction at the site of ulcer with ballooning of the opposite site\n\n• String sign of Kantor: tubular narrowing due to spasm or stricture depending on the chronicity\n\n• Rose thorn appearance\n\n• Partial obstruction.\n\nCT and MR enterography have a higher sensitivity for the diagnosis of Crohn’s disease. They may reveal additional features such as:\n\n• Fat halo sign - submucosal fat deposition\n\n• Comb sign - engorgement of the vasa recta.\n\nIn gastric Crohn's disease, conical narrowing of the antrum may be seen. This is called Ram’s horn sign.\n\nThe image below shows aphthous ulcers (focal contrast with surrounding haloes of mucosal edema)\n\nThe image below shows the string sign of Kantor:\n\nThe image below shows rose thorn appearance:\n\nThe image below shows cobblestone appearance:","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e8369274678d462d88ef6015dcc260e2x400x348.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3832630101ca499399e0ecefc993d17bx1024x1024.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9d762ce1a5e847478b4632ad07ee5820x1280x1422.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7c044727b64b4d398e59645f9784edbfx1280x1432.JPEG"],"t":"Inflammatory Bowel Disease   Clinical Features And Diagnosis","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Which among the following is the earliest radiologic change seen on barium enema in ulcerative colitis?","o":["Collar button ulcer","Fine mucosal granularity","String sign","Ram's horn sign"],"a":1,"e":"The earliest radiological change of ulcerative colitis seen on barium enema is fine mucosal granularity.\n\nDouble-contrast barium enema allows for exquisite detail of the colonic mucosa and also allows the bowel proximal to strictures to be assessed. It is however contraindicated if acute severe colitis is present due to the risk of perforation.\n\nEarly changes in ulcerative colitis:\n\n• Mucosal granularity\n\n• Fine granular pattern\n\n• Mucosal stippling\n\n• Collar button ulcers\n\n• Deep collar stud ulcers.\n\nThe below contrast radiograph shows granular mucosa typical of ulcerative colitis.\n\nOption A: Collar button ulcers are small deep ulcers in which the button surface corresponds to the eroded ulcer base and the button neck corresponds to the undermined and preserved mucosa. They are also seen in ulcerative colitis.\n\nOption C: String sign represents circumferential inflammation and fibrosis, resulting in long segments of luminal narrowing as shown below. It is more characteristic of Crohn's disease.\n\nOption D: Ram's horn sign is seen in gastric Crohn's disease.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9123e77137da479f82f1d235586fc643x512x924.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/edd61e6f4fc944eca4606d81e19a32b2x1024x1024.JPEG"],"t":"Inflammatory Bowel Disease   Clinical Features And Diagnosis","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following findings is seen in patients with ulcerative colitis?","o":["Rose thorn appearance","Pseudopolyps","Skip lesions","Pseudodiverticula"],"a":1,"e":"Pseudopolyps are seen in ulcerative colitis. They are formed due to alternating inflammation and regeneration of the ulcerated mucosal epithelium in ulcerative colitis.\n\nOther gross findings in ulcerative colitis include:\n\n• Continuous mucosal inflammation of the colon most commonly involving the rectum\n\n• Fine mucosal granularity\n\n• Collar button ulcers\n\n• Pseudopolyps.\n\nRose thorn appearance, skip lesions, and pseudodiverticula are features of Crohn's disease.","t":"Inflammatory Bowel Disease   Clinical Features And Diagnosis","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A 37-year-old man was referred to medical gastroenterology with complaints of chronic bloody diarrhea. Examination revealed blood on rectal examination. A colonoscopy was done as shown below. What is the likely diagnosis?","o":["Ulcerative colitis","Crohn’s disease","Ishchemic colitis","Solitary rectal ulcer syndrome"],"a":0,"e":"In the above scenario, colonoscopy shows continuous involvement of erythematous rectosigmoid mucosa and has a fine granular surface resembling sandpaper. This along with the clinical features is suggestive of ulcerative colitis.\n\nThe image below shows a gross specimen of the colon with diffuse involvement of mucosa and ulcers (without skip lesions) in ulcerative colitis.\n\nThe image below shows a gross specimen of jejunum showing the cobblestone appearance as seen in Crohn's disease.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/44c42a275ae7483aa2e287c812cea386.PNG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c86b5c3d363b480980464329422b2939x1280x960.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/706a9da544104085b168223487c4b5b7x640x640.JPEG"],"t":"Inflammatory Bowel Disease   Clinical Features And Diagnosis","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following complications is unlikely to occur in a patient with ulcerative colitis?","o":["Haemorrhage","Toxic megacolon","Malignant change","Fistula"],"a":3,"e":"Fistula is not a complication of ulcerative colitis. It is seen in Crohn’s disease.\n\nHemorrhage, malignant change, and toxic megacolon are complications of ulcerative colitis.\n\nNote: Stricture formation may be a complication of ulcerative colitis, but it is seen only in 5-10% of patients. They are more commonly seen in Crohn's disease.","t":"Inflammatory Bowel Disease   Clinical Features And Diagnosis","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A patient with inflammatory bowel disease presents with the following lesion. Which of the following is incorrect regarding this condition?","o":["Can occur years before the onset of bowel symptoms","Steroids should be avoided","Can develop years after colectomy","Associated with severe disease"],"a":1,"e":"The given image shows pyoderma gangrenosum. It is very difficult to treat and often requires IV antibiotics and IV glucocorticoids. Other drugs used in its management include dapsone, cyclosporine, and infliximab.\n\nPyoderma gangrenosum is a dermatological extraintestinal manifestation of inflammatory bowel disease. It begins as a pustule and progresses to an ulcer with necrotic tissue and exudates. It may occur years before the onset of bowel symptoms and is usually associated with severe disease.\n\nLesions are commonly found on the dorsal surface of the feet and legs but may occur on the arms, chest, and even the face. It responds poorly to colectomy and may even develop years after the surgery.\n\nOther dermatological manifestations associated with IBD include:\n\n• Erythema nodosum\n\n• Sweet syndrome (neutrophilic dermatosis)\n\n• Perianal skin tags\n\n• Psoriasis\n\n• Metastatic Crohn's disease (cutaneous granulomas).","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/33a63f2c81144a7ead5aec64462f0d7e.JPEG"],"t":"Inflammatory Bowel Disease   Complications And Treatment","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A 37-year-old man presented with hematuria and dysuria for 5 days. He also complains of chronic diarrhea and weight loss for 6 months. His urine microscopy revealed the following stones. What is the most likely disorder associated with this finding?","o":["Crohn’s disease","Ulcerative colitis","Irritable bowel syndrome","Lactose intolerance"],"a":0,"e":"The given urinary crystal is the bipyramidal or envelope-shaped crystals of calcium oxalate dihydrate. Calcium oxalate stones are more commonly associated with Crohn's disease.\n\nIn Crohn's disease (especially post small bowel resection), fatty acids are not absorbed because of ileal dysfunction. These fatty acids in the gut lumen bind to calcium. Because of this, the dietary oxalates which normally bind to the calcium are now left unbound. This leads to increased absorption of dietary oxalates leading to hyperoxaluria and oxalate stones.\n\nNote: Celiac disease is also associated with calcium oxalate stones.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/21b6acc92e5b4e9a829425348b332c2c.PNG"],"t":"Inflammatory Bowel Disease   Complications And Treatment","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Perianal examination of a patient revealed the following finding. Which condition is this associated with?","o":["Ulcerative colitis","Crohn’s disease","Ischemic colitis","Ileocecal tuberculosis"],"a":1,"e":"The above image shows a large hemorrhoidal perianal skin tag. These are found in patients with Crohn's disease, especially those with colon involvement.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4341cba1f82b489fa904e3f6e0d276fd.PNG"],"t":"Inflammatory Bowel Disease   Complications And Treatment","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A 28-year-old man was brought to the casualty with a high fever and abdominal pain for the past 2 days. His history is significant for bloody diarrhea over the past few months. The abdominal radiograph of this patient is given below. Identify the condition.","o":["Toxic megacolon","Intussusception","Sigmoid volvulus","Pneumatosis intestinalis"],"a":0,"e":"The clinical scenario and radiograph are suggestive of toxic megacolon. It is seen in severe ulcerative colitis.\n\nAbdominal radiography in toxic megacolon shows transverse or right colonic dilation >6 cm and loss of haustrations.\n\nToxic megacolon may present with fever, abdominal pain, and abdominal distension or features of sepsis.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/130cd246bba54be5a6b7c5336787ec33.JPEG"],"t":"Inflammatory Bowel Disease   Complications And Treatment","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A patient with inflammatory bowel disease presents with ocular pain, photophobia, and blurred vision. Which among the following is the most likely cause?","o":["Uveitis","Episcleritis","Scleritis","Conjunctivitis"],"a":0,"e":"Uveitis is the most common cause of the given symptoms in a patient with inflammatory bowel disease (IBD).\n\nUveitis is an ophthalmic manifestation of IBD. It is associated with both ulcerative colitis and Crohn's disease. It can develop during remission and is seen even after bowel resection. Prompt intervention with glucocorticoids is required to prevent scarring and visual impairment.\n\nThe other common ophthalmic manifestations in IBD are conjunctivitis and episcleritis. Episcleritis, more common in Crohn's, is a benign disorder presenting with mild ocular burning.","t":"Inflammatory Bowel Disease   Complications And Treatment","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A 34-year-old man with Crohn’s disease presents with red tender swellings on the lower leg as shown below. What is the likely diagnosis?","o":["Pyoderma gangrenosum","Erythema nodosum","Sweet’s syndrome","Ecthyma gangrenosum"],"a":1,"e":"The given clinical scenario suggests a diagnosis of erythema nodosum seen in inflammatory bowel disease (IBD).\n\nErythema nodosum is a dermatologic manifestation of IBD. It is seen in both ulcerative colitis and Crohn's disease. It manifests as tender erythematous nodules on the anterior surface of the lower legs, ankles, thighs, and arms.\n\nOption A: Pyoderma gangrenosum is a dermatological manifestation of IBD. It begins as pustular lesions which then ulcerate with necrotic foci and exudation.\n\nOption C: Sweet’s syndrome is acute febrile neutrophilic dermatosis, seen in ulcerative colitis.\n\nOption D: Ecthyma gangrenosum is a skin infection caused by Pseudomonas aeruginosa, often seen in immunocompromised patients. It presents as pustules/vesicles which progress to necrotic ulcers.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3e402666312646af894043d450dac7a0.JPEG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bab2382f5fbc42ff8a3041bfbe17124bx640x480.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e3143b2f02254c7ea92ecd571a79bc70x685x514.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c250c41b14fe4cee900baaf8dfb0f9c2x719x507.PNG"],"t":"Inflammatory Bowel Disease   Complications And Treatment","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"What are the cardiopulmonary manifestations associated with inflammatory bowel disease?\n\n1. Endocarditis\n\n2. VSD\n\n3. Pleuropericarditis\n\n4. Interstitial lung disease\n\n5. COPD","o":["1,2,5","4,5","2,3","1,3,4"],"a":3,"e":"The cardiopulmonary manifestations of inflammatory bowel disease (IBD) are:\n\n• Endocarditis\n\n• Myocarditis\n\n• Pleuropericarditis\n\n• Interstitial lung disease\n\nBoth ulcerative colitis and Crohn's disease have an increased risk of venous and arterial thrombosis.","t":"Inflammatory Bowel Disease   Complications And Treatment","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A 36-year-old man presents with worsening fatigue, jaundice, and pruritis for the last 3 months. Past history revealed recurrent episodes of bloody diarrhea and weight loss over the past year. Lab investigations show raised ALP, GGT, and bilirubin, and an MRCP is done as shown below. Which of the following is most likely to be associated with his current presentation?","o":["Chronic pancreatitis","Celiac disease","Ulcerative colitis","Crohn’s disease"],"a":2,"e":"The above clinical scenario along with MRCP showing multiple areas of intrahepatic narrowing with alternating areas of normal and dilated segments of bile ducts is suggestive of primary sclerosing cholangitis. PSC is more common in ulcerative colitis than in Crohn's disease.\n\nAdditionally, recurrent episodes of bloody diarrhea with weight loss seen in this patient also point toward a possible diagnosis of ulcerative colitis.\n\nPrimary sclerosing cholangitis (PSC) is an extraintestinal manifestation of IBD characterized by intrahepatic and extrahepatic duct inflammation/fibrosis. Around 50-75% of patients with primary sclerosing cholangitis (PSC) have inflammatory bowel disease (IBD) whereas, only 5% of IBD cases develop PSC.\n\nOption A: Chronic pancreatitis is associated with a chain of lakes appearance- dilated main duct with clubbing and dilatation of the side branches. Patients can present with steatorrhoea i.e., the passage of offensive-smelling large stools due to fat malabsorption.\n\nThe image below shows chain of lakes appearance on MRI:\n\nOption B: Celiac disease may be associated with primary biliary cholangitis (PBC), rather than primary sclerosing cholangitis.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/57458a2351b14376b5e4cc3c64da1b13.JPEG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/11fbf2fa355a44b791013599b688a447x1280x1541.JPEG"],"t":"Inflammatory Bowel Disease   Complications And Treatment","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"After initiating treatment for patients with IBD, in which of the following extraintestinal manifestations would you expect to see an improvement?","o":["Arthritis","Ankylosing spondylitis","Uveitis","Pyoderma gangrenosum"],"a":0,"e":"Arthritis worsens with exacerbation of bowel disease and improves with the treatment of bowel symptoms or surgery.\n\nAnkylosing spondylitis, uveitis, and pyoderma gangrenosum do not correlate with disease activity in the bowel. They may occur during the remission of disease or even after surgery.","t":"Inflammatory Bowel Disease   Complications And Treatment","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A patient is diagnosed with ulcerative colitis on endoscopy. He is hemodynamically stable but currently has 3-4 episodes of blood-tinged loose stools per day. Which of the following drug would you prefer for treating this patient?","o":["5-aminosalicylic acid","Azathioprine","Budesonide","6-mercaptopurine"],"a":0,"e":"The above scenario is suggestive of mild ulcerative colitis. The preferred drug for mild ulcerative colitis (UC) is 5-ASA agents (5-aminosalicylic acid).\n\nGiven below is the Truelove and Witts severity index for ulcerative colitis.\n\nMild\nModerate\nSevere\n\nBowel movements\n< 4 per day\n4 – 6 per day\n> 6 per day\n\nBlood in stool\nSmall\nModerate\nSevere\n\nFever\nNone\n< 37.5ºC\n> 37.5ºC\n\nTachycardia\nNone\n< 90 mean pulse\n>90 mean pulse\n\nAnemia\nMild\nHb > 75% of normal Hb\nHb < 75% of normal Hb\n\nESR\n< 30mm\n < 30mm\n > 30mm\n\nEndoscopic appearance\n\nErythema\n\nDecreased vascular markings\n\nFine granularity\n\nMarked erythema\n\nAbsent vascular markings\n\nCoarse granularity\n\nContact bleeding\n\nNo ulcerations\n\nSpontaneous bleeding\n\nUlcerations\n\nTreatment of Ulcerative Colitis\n\nMild\nModerate\nSevere\n\nInduction\n5-ASA\n5-ASA + Oral steroids\nIV steroids + IV anti TNF alpha + IV cyclosporine\n\nMaintainance\n5-ASA\n5-ASA + Azathioprine/6-MP\nOral cyclosporine or infliximab\n\nNote: Mirikizumab ( an IL-23 inhibitor) was recently FDA approved for the treatment of moderate to severe ulcerative colitis. It reduces stool frequency, rectal bleeding and bowel urgency.","t":"Inflammatory Bowel Disease   Complications And Treatment","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A 36-year-old man presented with complaints of 7-8 episodes of bloody diarrhea per day. He is pale and febrile. Heart rate is 110 bpm and ESR is 50mm. Colonoscopy revealed continuous mucosal involvement with ulcerations and mucosal hemorrhages. Which of the following drugs would you not prefer in the management of this patient?","o":["Mesalamine","Infliximab","IV steroids","Cyclosporine"],"a":0,"e":"The above scenario is suggestive of severe ulcerative colitis. Mesalamine (5-ASA) is ineffective in the management of severe ulcerative colitis (UC). It is used in the treatment of mild-moderate UC.\n\nGiven below is the Truelove and Witts severity index for ulcerative colitis.\n\nMild\nModerate\nSevere\n\nBowel movements\n< 4 per day\n4 – 6 per day\n> 6 per day\n\nBlood in stool\nSmall\nModerate\nSevere\n\nFever\nNone\n< 37.5ºC\n> 37.5ºC\n\nTachycardia\nNone\n< 90 mean pulse\n>90 mean pulse\n\nAnemia\nMild\nHb > 75% of normal Hb\nHb < 75% of normal Hb\n\nESR\n< 30mm\n < 30mm\n > 30mm\n\nEndoscopic appearance\n\nErythema\n\nDecreased vascular markings\n\nFine granularity\n\nMarked erythema\n\nAbsent vascular markings\n\nCoarse granularity\n\nContact bleeding\n\nNo ulcerations\n\nSpontaneous bleeding\n\nUlcerations\n\nTreatment of Ulcerative Colitis\n\nMild\nModerate\nSevere\n\nInduction\n5-ASA\n5-ASA + Oral steroids\nIV steroids + IV anti TNF alpha + IV cyclosporine\n\nMaintainance\n5-ASA\n5-ASA + Azathioprine/6-MP\nOral cyclosporine or infliximab\n\nNote: Mirikizumab ( an IL-23 inhibitor) was recently FDA approved for the treatment of moderate to severe ulcerative colitis. It reduces stool frequency, rectal bleeding and bowel urgency.","t":"Inflammatory Bowel Disease   Complications And Treatment","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following drugs have no role in the management of patients with ulcerative colitis?","o":["Oral salicylates","Hydrocortisone","Metronidazole","Cyclosporine"],"a":2,"e":"Metronidazole has no role in the management of ulcerative colitis. It is an antibiotic that is useful in active inflammatory, fistulous, and perianal Crohn's disease.\n\nNote: Mirikizumab ( an IL-23 inhibitor) was recently FDA approved for the treatment of moderate to severe ulcerative colitis. It reduces stool frequency, rectal bleeding and bowel urgency.","t":"Inflammatory Bowel Disease   Complications And Treatment","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A patient with Crohn's disease is started on infliximab. Which of the following conditions is the patient is at increased risk of developing?","o":["Hodgkin’s Lymphoma","Non-Hodgkin lymphoma","Hairy cell Leukaemia","Follicular lymphoma"],"a":1,"e":"Patients with Crohn's disease on infliximab are at increased risk of non-Hodgkin lymphoma (NHL) and hepatosplenic T-cell lymphoma (HS-TCL).\n\nInfliximab is the first biologic therapy approved for Crohn's disease. It is an IgG1 antibody against TNF-alpha.\n\nDrugs used in treatment of Crohn’s disease:\n\n1. Oral budesonide (mainly for mild to moderate)\n\n2. Oral prednisolone\n\n3. Methotrexate\n\n4. Anti TNF alpha\n\n5. Azathioprine\n\n6. 6-mercaptopurine\n\n7. Monoclonal antibodies\n\n8. Antibiotics\n\nInfliximab is also used in the treatment of moderate to severely active ulcerative colitis.","t":"Inflammatory Bowel Disease   Complications And Treatment","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Ustekinumab is effective in all of the following conditions except:","o":["Rheumatoid arthritis","Ulcerative colitis","Psoriatic arthirtis","Crohn's disease"],"a":0,"e":"Ustekinumab has no role in rheumatoid arthritis. It is effective against both Crohn's disease and ulcerative colitis.\n\nIt can also be used in the management of plaque psoriasis and psoriatic arthritis.\n\nIt is an IgG1 monoclonal antibody that blocks the activity of IL-12 and IL-23.\n\nBiological therapy in inflammatory bowel disease:\n\n• Anti-TNF therapies:\n\n• Infliximab\n\n• Adalimumab\n\n• Certolizumab\n\n• Anti-integrins:\n\n• Natalizumab\n\n• Ustekinumab\n\n• Vedolizumab\n\nNote: Mirikizumab (an IL-23 inhibitor) was recently FDA approved for the treatment of moderate to severe ulcerative colitis. It reduces stool frequency, rectal bleeding and bowel urgency.","t":"Inflammatory Bowel Disease   Complications And Treatment","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following drugs has no role after induction of remission has been achieved in a patient with IBD?","o":["5-ASA agents","Glucocorticoids","Azathioprine","Infliximab"],"a":1,"e":"Glucocorticoids play no role in maintenance therapy in inflammatory bowel disease.\n\nThey are used for induction in Crohn's disease and moderate to severe ulcerative colitis. Once clinical remission has been induced, they should be tapered gradually.","t":"Inflammatory Bowel Disease   Complications And Treatment","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A patient with IBD was brought to the ER with acute epigastric pain radiating to the back. His lab investigations revealed elevated serum amylase and lipase. He gives a history of being started on a new drug for maintenance therapy of his IBD 2 weeks back. The causative drug is most likely to be:","o":["Methotrexate","Tacrolimus","5-ASA","Azathioprine"],"a":3,"e":"The above scenario is suggestive of pancreatitis. Azathioprine causes pancreatitis in some patients within the first few weeks of therapy. It is completely reversible on discontinuing the drug.\n\nAzathioprine and 6-mercaptopurine (purine analogues) also have an increased risk of toxicity as some individuals may lack the enzyme necessary for their metabolism. This may lead to hepatitis, bone marrow suppression, etc. Hence, the patient should be monitored regularly with blood counts and liver function tests.","t":"Inflammatory Bowel Disease   Complications And Treatment","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"What is nutritional therapy in inflammatory bowel disease used for?","o":["Maintaining remission in Crohn's disease","Maintaining remission in ulcerative colitis","Inducing remission in Crohn's disease","Inducing remission in ulcerative colitis"],"a":2,"e":"Nutritional therapy is used for inducing remission in Crohn's disease.\n\nDietary antigens may stimulate an immune response. Hence, nutritional therapies like total parenteral nutrition, enteral nutrition (peptide-based preparations), etc. can be used to provide bowel rest in active Crohn's disease. These interventions cannot be used for maintaining remission.\n\nUlcerative colitis does not respond to dietary intervention.\n\nDrugs used in treatment of Crohn’s disease:\n\n1. Oral budesonide (mainly for mild to moderate)\n\n2. Oral prednisolone\n\n3. Methotrexate\n\n4. Anti TNF alpha\n\n5. Azathioprine\n\n6. 6-mercaptopurine\n\n7. Monoclonal antibodies\n\n8. Antibiotics","t":"Inflammatory Bowel Disease   Complications And Treatment","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"What is the treatment of choice in a patient with refractory ulcerative colitis?","o":["Mucosal proctectomy with ileal pouch–anal anastomosis","Proctectomy with ileorectal anastomosis","Colectomy with ileostomy","Ileorectal anastomosis"],"a":0,"e":"The treatment of choice in refractory ulcerative colitis (UC) is mucosal proctectomy with ileal pouch–anal anastomosis (IPAA).\n\nAs ulcerative colitis is a mucosal disease, the rectal mucosa is dissected and removed down to the dentate line of the anus. The ileum is made into a pouch that serves as neorectum.","t":"Inflammatory Bowel Disease   Complications And Treatment","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is unlikely to be seen in patients with malabsorption syndrome?","o":["Anemia","Constipation","Tetany","Abdominal pain"],"a":1,"e":"Constipation is not a feature of malabsorption syndrome.\n\nMalabsorption syndrome refers to a spectrum of conditions of various etiologies that arises due to diminished absorption of one or more dietary nutrients.\n\nThe general features of malabsorption syndrome are abdominal pain, diarrhea, steatorrhea, and weight loss.\n\nAnemia may be present when there is defective absorption of iron, folate, or vitamin B12. Hypocalcemia and tetany can be seen due to defective absorption of vitamin D.","t":"Malabsorption Syndromes","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following tests is useful for the assessment of intestinal mucosal function in a patient presenting with symptoms of malabsorption?","o":["D-xylulose test","D-xylose test","Galactomannan test","Schilling test"],"a":1,"e":"D-xylose test is useful for the assessment of intestinal mucosal function.\n\nD-xylose is a monosaccharide that is directly absorbed by the intestinal mucosa without undergoing enzymatic digestion.\n\nThe test is performed by administering 25g of D-xylose orally followed by the collection of urine 5h later. The test is considered abnormal (positive) if urinary D-xylose is <4.5g. A positive D-xylose test indicates abnormal small intestinal mucosa.","t":"Malabsorption Syndromes","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"The result of a D-xylose test showed urinary excretion of the monosaccharide at 3.6g after 5 hours. Which of the following can be a differential diagnosis?","o":["Irritable bowel syndrome","Ulcerative colitis","Pancreatic insufficiency","Crohn’s disease"],"a":3,"e":"A urinary D-xylose <4.5g after 5 hours of oral D-xylose (abnormal) can be seen in Crohn’s disease.\n\nD-xylose is a monosaccharide that is directly absorbed by the small intestinal mucosa without undergoing enzymatic digestion. An abnormal (positive) D-xylose test indicates abnormal small intestinal mucosal absorption.\n\nA positive D-xylose test is seen in:\n\n• Celiac disease\n\n• Whipple’s disease\n\n• Tropical sprue\n\n• Short bowel syndrome\n\n• Crohn’s disease\n\nThe test is of limited clinical value today and mostly has been replaced by small intestinal biopsy.","t":"Malabsorption Syndromes","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Patients with which of the following conditions are likely to suffer from malabsorption?","o":["Small bowel diverticulum","Anterior colon resection","Ulcerative colitis","Sigmoid volvulus"],"a":0,"e":"A small bowel diverticulum can result in malabsorption.\n\nA small bowel diverticulum is a blind pouch protruding from the small intestine that communicates with the lumen of the gut. It becomes a potential site for bacterial overgrowth which causes malabsorption. The growing gut bacteria depletes vitamin B12 and deconjugates bile acids leading to steatorrhea, diarrhea, and macrocytic anemia.\n\nAnterior colon resection, ulcerative colitis, and sigmoid volvulus affect the large intestine and therefore do not cause malabsorption.","t":"Malabsorption Syndromes","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"What is the most common cause of short bowel syndrome in adults?","o":["Crohn's disease","Mesenteric vascular occlusion","Necrotizing enterocolitis","Small intestinal perforation"],"a":1,"e":"The most common cause of short bowel syndrome in adults is mesenteric vascular occlusion.\n\nShort bowel syndrome (SBS) is a malabsorption disorder caused by a lack of functional small intestine. In adults, the common causes are :\n\n• Mesenteric vascular disease (e.g: mesenteric vascular occlusion.)\n\n• Primary mucosal and submucosal disease (e.g: Crohn’s disease)\n\n• Operations without any preexisting small-intestinal disease (e.g: after trauma)\n\nIn children, necrotizing enterocolitis is the most common cause.","t":"Malabsorption Syndromes","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Patients with which of the following conditions are unlikely to have protein malabsorption?","o":["Enterokinase deficiency","Hartnup syndrome","Cystinuria","Homocystinuria"],"a":3,"e":"Homocystinuria is not a protein malabsorption disorder. It is a disorder of methionine metabolism that leads to increased homocysteine levels.\n\nProteins are broken down by pancreatic enzymes and are absorbed as amino acids and peptides. So, protein malabsorption usually occurs only in pancreatic insufficiency. Additionally, there are three rare genetic disorders that lead to protein malabsorption. They are:\n\n• Enterokinase deficiency- Defective conversion of the proenzyme trypsinogen to trypsin. It is characterised by:\n\n• Diarrhoea\n\n• Growth retardation\n\n• Hypoproteinemia\n\n• Hartnup disease- Defective transport of tryptophan and other neutral amino acids. It is characterised by:\n\n• Pellagra-like rash\n\n• Neuropsychiatric symptoms\n\n• Cystinuria- Defective transport of cystine and other dibasic amino acids. It is characterised by:\n\n• Renal calculi\n\n• Chronic pancreatitis","t":"Malabsorption Syndromes","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A Schilling test was performed on a patient with vitamin B12 deficiency and the results are given below. What is the inference?","o":["Ileal disease","Pernicious anemia","Small intestinal bacterial overgrowth","Chronic pancreatitis"],"a":0,"e":"The above findings on Schilling test show reduced absorption of vitamin B12 irrespective of all interventions. This is suggestive of an ileal disease.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/015a7e6ffb2c4be68f24018495ba0303.JPEG"],"t":"Malabsorption Syndromes","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"An 11-year-old boy presented with steatorrhea and chronic diarrhea. An evaluation revealed delayed intellectual development and ataxia. Acanthocytes were seen on the peripheral blood smear. Ophthalmological examination showed the following. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Gaucher's disease","Abetalipoproteinemia","Whipple's disease","Protein losing enteropathy"],"a":1,"e":"The image showing retinitis pigmentosa and presence of acanthocytes along with the given clinical features is suggestive of abetalipoproteinemia.\n\nAbetalipoproteinemia is a rare genetic disorder in which there is defective chylomicron formation. Chylomicrons are required for the absorption and transport of fats and fat-soluble vitamins.\n\nIt presents in early childhood with steatorrhoea and diarrhea, hypolipidemia, failure to thrive, ataxia, sensory and motor neuropathies.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1ed17c0567d541cda63d1953178a1ad6.JPEG"],"t":"Malabsorption Syndromes","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"The findings associated with abetalipoproteinemia are :","o":["Figure A,B &amp; C","Figure B &amp; C","Figure A &amp; B","Figure A &amp; C"],"a":2,"e":"Figure A shows vacuoles (unabsorbed fat) in the cytoplasm of the enterocytes. Figure B shows acanthocytes. These findings are associated with abetalipoproteinemia.\n\nFigure C shows distended macrophages in the lamina propria and periodic acid–Schiff (PAS)-positive granules as seen in Whipple's disease.\n\nAcanthocytes are abnormal red blood cells with spiked cell membranes due to thorn-like projections. Causes of acanthocytes in peripheral smear are:\n\n• Abetalipoproteinemia\n\n• Renal disease\n\n• Liver disease\n\n• Hemolytic anemia","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0fa8bf0b71ce48b19a98c6377e5ae09a.JPEG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1cbc5b38a1c54e5597dfe0c96d46abc6x1280x1573.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1c369e8b531e40989efc34cd918a7e85x834x772.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/348ec69c00cb45e685040e337b70e39cx682x786.PNG"],"t":"Malabsorption Syndromes","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"All are true about the types of celiac disease except :","o":["Latent type is associated with HLA DQ 2,8","Atypical type has a negative serology","Silent type has a positive serology","Classic type presents with diarrhea and steatorrhea"],"a":1,"e":"Atypical celiac disease has a positive serology with atypical symptoms.\n\nType of celiac disease\nSerology\nFeatures\n\nA\nClassic\n+\nDiarrhea, steatorrhea\n\nB\nAtypical\n+\nIron deficiency anemia, osteopenia, ataxia, peripheral neuropathy, infertility\n\nC\nSilent\n+\nNo features\n\nD\nLatent\n-\nHLA DQ 2,8\n\nSerology in celiac disease refers to either anti-tissue transglutaminase antibodies (anti-tTG) or anti-endomysial antibodies.\n\nNote: Tropical sprue and celiac disease have similar clinical, endoscopic and histologic findings. A diagnosis of tropical sprue must be considered with a negative celiac serology.","t":"Malabsorption Syndromes","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"What is the most common extraintestinal manifestation of celiac disease?","o":["Dermatitis herpetiformis","Infertility","Anemia","Peripheral neuropathy"],"a":0,"e":"The most common extraintestinal manifestation of celiac disease is dermatitis herpetiformis (DH).\n\nDH is characterized by intensely pruritic small papules and vesicles on the extensor surfaces of extremities, buttocks, and neck. It can be considered a celiac disease of the skin.\n\nOther extraintestinal manifestations of celiac disease are:\n\n• Anemia\n\n• Low bone density\n\n• Ataxia\n\n• Peripheral neuropathy\n\n• Infertility\n\nCeliac disease is often associated with other conditions such as type 1 diabetes mellitus, autoimmune thyroid disease, IgA deficiency, bird fancier's lung, and Down syndrome.\n\nThe image below shows dermatitis herpetiformis.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6da17930748548a4905ed1233b1f0441x720x472.JPEG"],"t":"Malabsorption Syndromes","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A patient who presented with severe malabsorption is found to be positive for IgA tissue trans-glutaminase antibodies. What will be the next best step?","o":["Do an intestinal biopsy","Check for anti-endomysial antibodies","Check for anti-gliadin antibodies","Start gluten-free diet"],"a":0,"e":"In this patient with positive serology, a biopsy should be done next, to confirm the diagnosis of celiac disease.\n\nTissue transglutaminase Ab (tTG-IgA) is the best screening test. IgA endomysial antibodies (EMA) are most specific.\n\nBut a small-intestinal biopsy is required to establish a diagnosis of celiac disease, before starting treatment.","t":"Malabsorption Syndromes","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A 30-year-old man presented with chronic diarrhea, steatorrhea, and weight loss. IgA tissue trans-glutaminase antibodies were found to be positive. Duodenal biopsy revealed the following. Which of the following complications would you not expect in this patient?","o":["Intestinal lymphoma","Intestinal ulcers","Pancreatic insufficiency","Collagenous sprue"],"a":2,"e":"The above scenario along with the presence of IgA tissue trans-glutaminase antibodies and biopsy showing villous atrophy, crypt hyperplasia, lamina propria infiltrates indicate a case of celiac disease. Pancreatic insufficiency is not a complication of celiac disease.\n\nThe complications of celiac disease are:\n\n• Intestinal lymphomas\n\n• Intestinal ulcers- also known as ulcerative jejunoileitis\n\n• Collagenous sprue- development of a subepithelial collagen layer","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0a98f21957e449a3912a90d8026b5aef.JPEG"],"t":"Malabsorption Syndromes","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A 46-year-old lady with celiac disease is recommended a gluten-free diet. Which among the following can she consume?","o":["Wheat","Barley","Rye","Rice"],"a":3,"e":"Rice can be consumed on a gluten-free diet.\n\nThe primary management of celiac disease is avoiding gluten in the diet.\n\nDiet avoided (gluten)\n\n“BROW”\n\nDiet Allowed\n\n“So MRP ”\n\nBarley\nSoya bean\n\nRye\nMaize\n\nOats\nRice\n\nWheat\nPotato","t":"Malabsorption Syndromes","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"When will you diagnose a patient with refractory celiac disease?","o":["No remission of disease despite gluten free diet","Intestinal ulcers on biopsy","No response to steroids","Development of a subepithelial collagen layer"],"a":0,"e":"Refractory celiac disease is diagnosed when there is no remission of disease despite a strict gluten-free diet for 6-12 months.\n\nPatients are symptomatic and have persistent small intestinal villous atrophy on biopsy. They respond well to steroids.","t":"Malabsorption Syndromes","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Match the following disorders with their respective sites of involvement:\n\n 1. Celiac disease\n\n 2. Whipple's disease\n\n 3. Tropical sprue\n\n i. Proximal > Distal small intestine\n\n ii. Proximal small intestine\n\n iii. Panintestine","o":["1-ii , 2-i , 3-iii","1-iii, 2-ii , 3-i","1-i , 2-iii , 3-ii","1-ii, 2-iii, 3-i"],"a":0,"e":"The sites involved in the given malabsorption syndromes are as follows:\n\nDisorder\nSite of involvement\n\nCeliac disease\nProximal small intestine\n\nWhipple's disease\nProximal > Distal small intestine\n\nTropical sprue\nWhole small intestine (panintestine)","t":"Malabsorption Syndromes","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"All are true about Whipple's disease except :","o":["Caused by gram positive organism Tropheryma whipplei","Culture of organism from biopsy specimen helps in early diagnosis","Males affected more than females","Multiple systems are affected"],"a":1,"e":"Tropheryma whipplei is difficult to culture from the biopsy specimen. Hence, it cannot be used for the early diagnosis of Whipple's disease.\n\nWhipple's disease is a chronic multisystem disorder caused by the gram-positive bacteria Tropheryma whipplei. It primarily causes malabsorption along with central nervous system & cardiac problems. It mostly affects men and presents with fever, diarrhea weight loss, and arthralgia.\n\nThe diagnosis of Whipple's disease is made through a biopsy which shows the presence of PAS-positive macrophages in the small intestine and other organs with evidence of disease.","t":"Malabsorption Syndromes","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"What is the most common CNS manifestation associated with Whipple’s disease?","o":["Progressive dementia","Supranuclear ophthalmoplegia","Opsoclonus myoclonus syndrome","Oculomasticatory myorrhythmia"],"a":0,"e":"The most common CNS manifestation associated with Whipple’s disease is progressive dementia.\n\nOther important CNS manifestations in Whipple's disease are:\n\n• Personality and mood changes\n\n• Progressive supranuclear ophthalmoplegia\n\n• Focal neurologic presentations\n\n• Oculomasticatory myorrhythmia - an unusual combination of rhythmic eye and jaw movements which is pathognomonic of Whipple's disease.\n\nWhipple's disease associated with CNS symptoms has a poor prognosis.\n\nNote: Opsoclonus myoclonus syndrome is a paraneoplastic syndrome associated with neuroblastoma.","t":"Malabsorption Syndromes","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"What is the most common cardiovascular presentation seen in patients with Whipple's disease?","o":["Endocarditis","Pericarditis","Myocarditis","Pericardial effusion"],"a":0,"e":"The most common cardiovascular presentation in Whipple's disease is culture-negative endocarditis.\n\nOther cardiovascular findings include:\n\n• Congestive heart failure\n\n• Pericarditis\n\n• Myocarditis","t":"Malabsorption Syndromes","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A 38-year-old man presented with chronic diarrhea, weight loss, and joint pains. A duodenal biopsy was done. Which of the following drug is used in the maintenance phase of treatment of this condition?","o":["Penicillin G","Ceftriaxone","Meropenem","Trimethoprim / Sulfamethoxazole"],"a":3,"e":"The above image shows PAS-positive foamy macrophages present in the lamina propria. This along with the given features suggests Whipple's disease. Cotrimoxazole is used in the maintenance phase of the treatment of Whipple's disease.\n\nTreatment of Whipple's disease:\n\n• Induction phase- ceftriaxone or meropenem or penicillin G + streptomycin - 2 weeks\n\n• Maintenance phase- trimethoprim / sulfamethoxazole (TMP/SMX) for 1 year. It has high CNS penetration so it is used in maintenance to prevent relapse.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5573c6e5e2654b418593f975c1d02e85.JPEG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3bf8575962fd4d0a934ad25cdcd28d9dx589x732.PNG"],"t":"Malabsorption Syndromes","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A 42-year-old man presented with diarrhea, floating stools, and weight loss for 3 months. Celiac serology was negative. Biopsy of the small intestinal mucosa revealed villous atrophy and crypt hyperplasia with normal mucosal thickness. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Celiac disease","Whipple’s disease","Tropical sprue","Radiation enteritis"],"a":2,"e":"The history of malabsorptive symptoms, a negative celiac serology, and the biopsy findings are suggestive of tropical sprue.\n\nImprovement in symptoms after folic acid intake is a hallmark of tropical sprue.\n\nTropical sprue is an acquired malabsorptive condition of probable infectious aetiology. It is seen in people from the tropics such as India, Southeast Asia and South America.\n\nTropical sprue and celiac disease have similar clinical, endoscopic and histologic findings. However, a positive celiac serology (anti-tissue transglutaminase and anti-endomysial antibodies) can be used to rule out tropical sprue.","t":"Malabsorption Syndromes","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following would you prescribe for a patient with tropical sprue?","o":["Tertacycline + Vitamin B12","Metronidazole + Folic acid","Amoxicillin + Vitamin B12","Tetracycline + Folic acid"],"a":3,"e":"The treatment of tropical sprue is tetracycline + folic acid.\n\nBecause of marked folate deficiency, folic acid is most often given together with broad spectrum antibiotics for up to 6 months.","t":"Malabsorption Syndromes","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following drug is used for the management of a patient with small intestinal bacterial overgrowth?","o":["Ciprofloxacin","Azithromycin","Rifaximin","Streptomycin"],"a":2,"e":"Rifaximin is the preferred drug for the management of small intestinal bacterial overgrowth (SIBO).\n\nThe antibiotics are given for around 3 weeks. The other drugs that can be used are :\n\n• Tetracyclines\n\n• Cephalosporins\n\n• Metronidazole\n\n• Amoxicillin-Clavulanic acid\n\nRifaximin is the antibiotic used in hepatic encephalopathy to lower blood ammonia levels.","t":"Malabsorption Syndromes","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"An upper GI endoscopy is performed in a patient with dyspeptic symptoms. It shows the presence of thickened gastric folds. Which of the following differential diagnoses would you not consider in this patient?","o":["Zollinger–Ellison syndrome","Lymphoma","Menetrier's disease","Pancreatic rests"],"a":3,"e":"Pancreatic rests are not a differential diagnosis for hypertrophied gastric folds. These usually present as a single lump in the stomach and are formed by ectopic pancreatic tissue.\n\nThickened gastric folds can represent a local malignant process, inflammatory process, or hormonal response. The differential diagnoses of large gastric folds include:\n\n• Menetrier's disease\n\n• Zollinger–Ellison syndrome - hypergastrinemia leads to parietal cell hyperplasia and prominent folds\n\n• Malignancy- lymphoma, infiltrating gastric cancer\n\n• Infections- histoplasmosis, tuberculosis.","t":"Other Gastrointestinal Conditions","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Which part of the stomach is usually spared in Menetrier's disease?","o":["Body","Fundus","Antrum","Greater curvature"],"a":2,"e":"The antrum is usually spared in Menetrier's disease.\n\nFoveolar hyperplasia results in the development of large rugal folds in the body and fundus of the stomach, sparing the antrum. These hypertrophied rugal folds give a cobblestone or cerebriform appearance to the gastric mucosa.\n\nThe image given below shows the endoscopic appearance of Menetrier's disease.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8a896d94923e43e98f48f67eb02dce1fx720x572.PNG"],"t":"Other Gastrointestinal Conditions","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A 46-year-old man presented with a history of diarrhea for 5 months, occasional abdominal pain, and weight loss. Upper GI endoscopy showed the following finding. Which of the following is least likely to occur in this patient?","o":["Hypoproteinemia","Hypochlorhydria","Edema","Hematemesis"],"a":3,"e":"The given case scenario and endoscopy image showing large, rugal gastric folds are suggestive of Menetrier's disease. Among the given options, hematemesis is least likely to occur in this patient.\n\nLarge, gastric rugal folds in Menetrier's disease occur due to massive overgrowth of mucous cells (foveolar cells) lining the stomach.\n\nPatients usually present with epigastric pain, nausea, diarrhea, vomiting, anorexia, peripheral edema, and weight loss. Occult gastrointestinal bleeding may occur, but overt bleeding is unusual. If overt bleeding is seen, it is usually due to superficial mucosal erosions.\n\nThey develop protein-losing gastropathy, leading to hypoproteinemia as a result of increased secretion of gastric mucus. There is also a decrease in the number of parietal cells which leads to decreased acid secretion (hypochlorhydria) in the stomach. They are at an increased risk of developing gastric carcinoma.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d091ceff66964fafb175205c2efe0671.JPEG"],"t":"Other Gastrointestinal Conditions","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A 6-year-old child with vomiting, abdominal pain, weight loss, and peripheral edema underwent an upper GI biopsy. Histology showed foveolar hyperplasia and glandular atrophy within the gastric mucosa. Which of the following is most commonly associated with this condition?","o":["Cytomegalovirus","Helicobacter pylori","Herpes simplex virus","Epstein Barr virus"],"a":0,"e":"The given case scenario is suggestive of Menetrier's disease. In children, it occurs commonly due to the infection by cytomegalovirus.\n\nThe histopathological features of Menetrier's disease include:\n\n• Foveolar hyperplasia - hyperplasia of surface and glandular mucosa\n\n• Reduction in oxyntic glands, parietal cells, and chief cells\n\n• Dilated, tortuous gastric glands\n\n• Elongation of pits of gastric glands\n\n• Eosinophilic infiltration in lamina propria\n\n The image given below shows the histopathological appearance of Menetrier's disease.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a504d1e25da94c3fba378932f639ed18x510x802.GIF"],"t":"Other Gastrointestinal Conditions","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Overexpression of which of the following causes Menetrier’s disease?","o":["TNF-α","TGF-α","TNF-β","TGF-β"],"a":1,"e":"Overexpression of TGF-α (transforming growth factor-alpha) causes Menetrier's disease. This overexpression results in overstimulation of the epidermal growth factor receptor (EGFR) pathway and increased proliferation of mucous cells, resulting in mucous cell (foveolar) hyperplasia.","t":"Other Gastrointestinal Conditions","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A 24-year old woman underwent extensive workup for unexplained weight loss, vomiting, and diarrhea. She was suspected to have Menetrier's disease. Which of the following investigations will confirm the diagnosis?","o":["Endoscopic full thickness biopsy","CECT abdomen","Endoscopic punch biopsy","MRI abdomen"],"a":0,"e":"Menetrier's disease is confirmed by endoscopic full-thickness biopsy.\n\nA full-thickness biopsy allows for the visualization of the cells on the surface as well as the pits of the gastric glands. The histopathological specimen shows the following features:\n\n• Foveolar hyperplasia - hyperplasia of surface and glandular mucosa\n\n• Reduction in oxyntic glands, parietal cells, and chief cells\n\n• Dilated, tortuous gastric glands\n\n• Elongation of pits of gastric glands\n\n• Eosinophilic infiltration in lamina propria\n\nA punch biopsy can only sample the surface of the gastric gland and hence is not preferred.\n\nThe image given below shows the histopathological appearance of Menetrier's disease.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/abeebf33b0684d36876efdcc4306ae2bx510x802.GIF"],"t":"Other Gastrointestinal Conditions","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A middle-aged man presented with a history of dyspepsia. He has been taking pantoprazole for the last few months, however, his symptoms persisted. Upper GI endoscopy was done, and a full-thickness gastric biopsy was taken. The histopathological appearance is shown below. What is the treatment of choice?","o":["Partial gastrectomy","Lansoprazole","Cetuximab","Octreotide"],"a":2,"e":"The given clinical scenario and the image showing foveolar hyperplasia are suggestive of Menetrier's disease. The treatment of choice is cetuximab.\n\nIncreased epidermal growth factor receptor(EGFR) signaling has been implicated in the pathogenesis of Menetrier's disease. Cetuximab is an EGFR inhibitor, and blocks this signaling pathway and helps in the improvement of the symptoms. Therefore, it is the first line of treatment.\n\nMedical therapy with proton pump inhibitors (lansoprazole) and somatostatin analogues (octreotide) may also be tried to manage symptoms. If the patient continues to have protein loss despite medical therapy, then total gastrectomy is indicated.\n\nThe image given below shows the histopathological appearance of Menetrier's disease.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/167e84cdb0214f509a276945f61412b5.JPEG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2893d6001a614a14b08e59c4273c28d8x510x802.GIF"],"t":"Other Gastrointestinal Conditions","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"What is the most common site for a MALToma?","o":["Duodenum","Stomach","Ileum","Cecum"],"a":1,"e":"The most common site for mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue (MALT) lymphoma or MALToma is the stomach.\n\nMALToma is a type of extranodal marginal zone lymphoma that most commonly occurs in the stomach and to a lesser extent in the salivary glands, lungs, small intestine, etc. It occurs as a result of the malignant proliferation of B cells in the marginal zone of mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue (MALT).","t":"Other Gastrointestinal Conditions","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is false about MALTomas?","o":["Associated with autoimmune conditions","History of chronic gastritis usually seen","Usually diagnosed in elderly patients","Arises from T lymphocytes"],"a":3,"e":"MALToma arises from B lymphocytes present in the marginal zone of the lymphoid tissue.\n\nThe majority of the cases of MALToma are associated with H. pylori induced chronic gastritis. This infection results in an increased proliferation of lymphocytes in the mucosa of the stomach causing MALToma. It is initially low grade and if the infection is not treated, it later progresses to high grade.\n\nThe patients usually present with non-specific symptoms like epigastric pain and discomfort. It is usually diagnosed in patients around 60 years of age. It is also associated with autoimmune diseases like Sjogren's syndrome and Hashimoto's thyroiditis.","t":"Other Gastrointestinal Conditions","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A 62-year-old woman underwent an endoscopic biopsy of a gastric tumor. The biopsy shows dense lymphocytic infiltration with lymphoepithelial lesions in the gastric glands. Which of the following investigations will confirm your diagnosis?","o":["Cytogenetic testing","Immunophenotyping","Electron microscopy","Fluorescent microscopy"],"a":1,"e":"The given clinical scenario and lymphoepithelial lesions seen in the gastric glands is suggestive of MALToma. The diagnosis is confirmed by immunophenotyping.\n\nHistopathological examination of a MALToma shows the presence of lymphoepithelial lesions. These are caused due to the invasion of gastric glands and crypts by the neoplastic lymphocytes. These lymphocytes are positive for CD19, CD20 and negative for CD5 and CD10. Biopsy or aspiration of the bone marrow is also done to see the bone marrow involvement.\n\nRadiological investigations like CT scans, MRIs, and PET scans are useful in identifying the extent of the tumor and infiltration of the tumor to the adjacent organs.\n\nNote: Cytogenetic testing will be helpful in identifying chromosomal abnormalities.\n\nThe image given below shows the histopathological appearance of a MALToma.\n\nThe below image shows a sample of a MALToma showing positive immunophenotyping for CD20.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5f4b112e053444ba947e7dd3500ee35cx336x249.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/df0742cc1f53437a88dea0b200aa40cdx1142x856.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ea71b04f171a45c1a15c149c2afb6e35x336x242.JPEG"],"t":"Other Gastrointestinal Conditions","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Immunophenotyping from a gastric tumor in an elderly man showed the following findings. The tumor was found to be limited to the gastric mucosa. What is the treatment of choice?\n\nImmunomarker\nStaining\n\nCD 5\nNegative\n\nCD 10\nNegative\n\nCD 19\nPositive\n\nCD 20\nPositive","o":["Rituximab and cyclophosphamide","Three-dimensional conformal radiotherapy","Omeprazole, clarithromycin and amoxicillin","Radical gastrectomy"],"a":2,"e":"The given case scenario is suggestive of a low-grade gastric MALToma. The treatment of choice for this condition is H. pylori eradication therapy which can be done with the OCM regimen (omeprazole, clarithromycin and metronidazole).\n\nImmunophenotyping in MALTomas shows positive staining for CD19 and CD20 which is suggestive of a B-cell neoplasm. Negative staining for CD5 and CD10 is seen, suggesting it arises from the marginal zone of lymphoid tissues.\n\nChronic infection with H. pylori leads to the development of MALTomas. When these lesions are limited to the mucosa, treatment of the infection alone is sufficient to cause regression of the tumour.\n\nIn high-grade tumors or the tumors which invade into the adjacent or distant organs, radiotherapy and chemotherapy are required to treat the tumor. Radiotherapy is usually three-dimensional conformal radiotherapy. The chemotherapeutic agents used in the treatment of MALToma are rituximab, cyclophosphamide, fludarabine, doxorubicin, vincristine, and prednisone. Radical gastrectomy has a limited role and is done when the disease is confined to the stomach.","t":"Other Gastrointestinal Conditions","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"A 65-year-old smoker presents with postprandial abdominal pain and diarrhea for the past 1 year. On examination, she is malnourished, and a bruit is heard in the epigastrium. What is the most probable diagnosis?","o":["Acute mesenteric ischemia","Chronic mesenteric ischemia","Wilkie's syndrome","Nutcracker syndrome"],"a":1,"e":"The given case scenario is suggestive of chronic mesenteric ischemia.\n\nChronic mesenteric ischemia occurs due to atherosclerosis of the celiac or mesenteric arteries. It is common in older women, and they usually have a history of smoking, coronary artery disease, cerebrovascular disease, or peripheral artery disease.\n\nThe important clinical features of chronic mesenteric ischemia are:\n\n• Postprandial abdominal pain occurs as a result of a mismatch between the increased demand and decreased blood flow to the intestine. This is called intestinal angina\n\n• Weight loss- patients avoid eating due to postprandial pain and this leads to significant weight loss\n\n• Diarrhea- occurs as a result of decreased absorption\n\n• Physical examination may reveal epigastric bruit in almost half of the patients.\n\nThe gold standard investigation to diagnose the blockade is mesenteric angiography. Once the disease is confirmed, the patients have to be started with antiplatelets and mesenteric angioplasty and stenting should be performed.\n\nOption A: Acute mesenteric ischemia present with acute abdomen, bloody diarrhea and nausea. Angiography will show occlusion of superior mesenteric artery.\n\nOption C: Wilkie's syndrome occurs due to compression of the third portion of the duodenum by the superior mesenteric artery. It can present similarly, however is associated with vomiting and rapid weight loss. It usually occurs in young, asthenic women.\n\nOption D: Nutcracker syndrome occurs due to compression of the left renal vein, between the aorta and the superior mesenteric artery. It presents with varicoceles and hematuria.","t":"Other Gastrointestinal Conditions","c":"Gastroenterology and Hepatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is an example of large vessel vasculitis?","o":["Polyarteritis nodosa","Takayasu disease","Behcet's disease","Cogan's syndrome"],"a":1,"e":"Takayasu disease is an example of large vessel vasculitis.","t":"Large And Medium Vessel Vasculitis","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"Biopsy performed on a patient with suspected vasculitis showed the presence of granulomas. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Microscopic polyangiitis","Behcet's disease","Polyarteritis nodosa","Churg- Strauss syndrome"],"a":3,"e":"Churg-Strauss syndrome is the most likely diagnosis as it is a granulomatous vasculitis.\n\nThe vasculitis can be classified as:\n\nGranulomatous vasculitis\nNon-granulomatous vasculitis\n\nGiant cell arteritis\n\nTakayasu arteritis\n\nGranulomatosis with polyangiitis\n\nChurg-Strauss syndrome\n\nPolyarteritis nodosa\n\nKawasaki disease\n\nBehcet's disease\n\nMicroscopic polyangiitis\n\nHenoch-Schonlein purpura","t":"Large And Medium Vessel Vasculitis","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"A 42-year-old man presented with complaints of fever, malaise, and weight loss. His lab investigations showed mild anemia, elevated ESR, and serology was positive for cANCA. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Cryoglobulinemic vasculitis","Granulomatosis with polyangiitis","Microscopic polyangiitis","Churg-Strauss syndrome"],"a":1,"e":"Granulomatosis with polyangiitis (Wegener’s granulomatosis) is the most likely diagnosis as it is associated with cANCA.","t":"Large And Medium Vessel Vasculitis","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not true about giant cell arteritis?","o":["Seen in young women","May lead to ischemic optic neuropathy","Good response to glucocorticoid therapy","Anemia is present"],"a":0,"e":"Giant cell arteritis is common in older women (>50 years of age).\n\nGiant cell arteritis is an inflammatory vasculitis of the large vessels, commonly seen in females over 50 years of age. It usually involves the temporal artery.\n\nIt is characterized by fever, anemia, and headache. Depending on the artery involved, the patient may have tenderness over the affected artery, scalp pain, tongue and jaw claudication. Untreated patients may develop ischemic optic neuropathy.\n\nLaboratory investigations reveal elevated ESR and normochromic normocytic anemia. It shows good response to glucocorticoid therapy.","t":"Large And Medium Vessel Vasculitis","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"A 65-year-old woman came with complaints of headache for the past 1-month. She also reported aching of the jaw during her meals. Over the last week, she has experienced episodes of painless visual loss which lasted for around 10 minutes. Which of the following is the best investigation for the diagnosis of her condition?","o":["Temporal artery biopsy","Colour doppler of temporal artery","CT angiography","MR angiography"],"a":0,"e":"The given clinical presentation of headache, jaw claudication, and painless visual loss is suggestive of giant cell arteritis. The best investigation for the diagnosis of giant cell arteritis is temporal artery biopsy.\n\nHistopathology shows panarteritis with the presence of mononuclear cells and giant cells. The biopsy may not be positive in all patients due to patchy histologic findings. Since involvement of the vessel may be segmental, positive yield is increased by obtaining a biopsy segment of 3–5 cm together with serial sectioning of biopsy specimens.\n\n Note : According to the 21st edition of Harrison's Medicine, as involvement of the vessel may be segmental, getting a biopsy segment of 3–5 cm together with serial sectioning of biopsy specimens would help to increase a positive yield. And Robbin's 10th edition mentions that an adequate biopsy requires at least a 1-cm segment.","t":"Large And Medium Vessel Vasculitis","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"A 50-year-old woman presents to your OPD with complaints of pain and stiffness of the shoulders which is worse in the morning. She works as a bus conductor and tells you that she has difficulty raising her shoulders to ring the bell. On examination, there is diffuse tenderness over the shoulders. Which of the following vasculitis is closely associated with her presentation?","o":["Polyarteritis nodosa","Granulomatosis with polyangiitis","Takayasu arteritis","Giant cell arteritis"],"a":3,"e":"The given clinical presentation of painful, tender, and stiff shoulders with restricted movement points toward a diagnosis of polymyalgia rheumatica, which is closely associated with giant cell arteritis.\n\nPolymyalgia rheumatica is characterized by pain and stiffness (worst in the morning) in the muscles of the neck, shoulder, back, hips, and thigh. On examination, diffuse tenderness is present without localization to any specific structures. Pain restricts the active range of motion but passive range of motion and muscle strength are normal.\n\nESR and CRP are typically elevated in polymyalgia rheumatica whereas creatine phosphokinase (CPK) lies within the normal range. It shows a good response to prednisone therapy.","t":"Large And Medium Vessel Vasculitis","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"A 60-year-old woman presents with severe right-sided throbbing headache. She also gives history of pain and stiffness of the shoulders and hips. Her lab values show an ESR of 80 mm/hr. What is the next best step in her management?","o":["Start beta-blockers","Obtain non contrast head CT","Perform temporal artery biopsy","Start steroids"],"a":3,"e":"The given clinical presentation of headache and symptoms suggestive of polymyalgia rheumatica with raised ESR points towards a diagnosis of giant cell arteritis (GCA), for which the next best step is initiation of steroids (prednisone).\n\nAlthough temporal artery biopsy is the diagnostic investigation for GCA, it should not delay the initiation of steroids as it helps in reducing symptoms and preventing visual loss. The biopsy findings usually remain positive for at least 1 month after initiation of steroids.\n\nNewer drugs used in the treatment of giant cell arteritis are tocilizumab (anti-IL-6 receptor) and abatacept (CTLA4-Ig).","t":"Large And Medium Vessel Vasculitis","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"Which is the most common artery involved in Takayasu arteritis?","o":["Subclavian artery","Common carotid artery","Internal carotid artery","External carotid artery"],"a":0,"e":"The most common artery involved in Takayasu arteritis is the subclavian artery.\n\nPatients present with pain, claudication, and weakness of the affected limb. The arterial pulse in the affected limb may be weak or absent.\n\nThe order of arteries affected in Takayasu arteritis is subclavian artery > common carotid artery > abdominal aorta > renal artery > aortic arch = vertebral > coeliac axis > iliac > pulmonary > coronary artery.","t":"Large And Medium Vessel Vasculitis","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"A 27-year-old woman presents to the hospital with fever, malaise, and arthralgia for a duration of 1 month. She also complains of pain, numbness, and weakness in her right arm, which has been increasing over the past 1 week. Clinical examination reveals weak radial pulses, and her laboratory investigations show elevated ESR. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Coarctation of aorta","Giant cell arteritis","Takayasu arteritis","Syphilitic aortitis"],"a":2,"e":"The above clinical features with a marked weakening of pulse in the upper extremities and laboratory findings of elevated ESR are suggestive of Takayasu arteritis. It is also known as pulseless disease.\n\nTakayasu arteritis is a systemic disease and may present with generalized and vascular symptoms. It commonly affects adolescents or young females. Generalized symptoms like fever, night sweats, weight loss, malaise, arthralgias, and anorexia may occur long before vascular symptoms.\n\nVascular symptoms depend on the vessel involved:\n\n• Arch of the aorta and its branches:\n\n• Subclavian artery (most commonly affected) - It leads to weak pulses in the upper extremities. Hence, it is also known as pulseless disease. It may also lead to Raynaud’s phenomenon.\n\n• Carotid arteries- It leads to weak pulses, blindness, and neurological deficits.\n\n• Aortic arch- It may lead to congestive heart failure.\n\n• Abdominal aorta, celiac axis, and superior mesenteric artery - leads to abdominal pain, nausea, and vomiting\n\n• Iliac vessels - leads to claudication of the legs\n\n• Renal arteries - leads to systemic hypertension\n\n• Pulmonary artery - leads to pulmonary hypertension\n\n• Coronary arteries (least commonly affected) - lead to myocardial infarction\n\nInvestigations:\n\n• Laboratory tests reveal an elevated ESR level, anemia, and elevated immunoglobulin levels.\n\n• Arteriography is done as a confirmatory test.\n\nTreatment includes glucocorticoid therapy, methotrexate, immunotherapy with anti-TNF agents, and surgery.","t":"Large And Medium Vessel Vasculitis","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"In a patient with suspected Behcet's disease which of the following tests can be used to confirm the diagnosis?","o":["Foshay test","Tzanck test","Pathergy test","Cardiolipin antibody test"],"a":2,"e":"Pathergy test can be used for confirming Behcet's disease. It is a nonspecific inflammatory skin reaction to scratch or intradermal saline injection and is a specific manifestation.\n\nThe diagnostic criteria in Behcet's syndrome are the presence of recurrent oral ulcerations plus two of the following findings:\n\n• Recurrent genital ulcerations\n\n• Ocular lesions\n\n• Skin lesions\n\n• Positive pathergy test\n\nOption A: Foshay test is used in cat-scratch disease.\n\nOption B: Tzanck test is used to diagnose herpes infection.\n\nOption D: The cardiolipin antibody test is used to detect antiphospholipid antibodies.","t":"Large And Medium Vessel Vasculitis","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"A 35-year-old woman presented with recurrent oral and genital ulcerations. An inflammatory skin reaction was observed after intradermal administration of normal saline. Which of the following statements is incorrect about her condition?","o":["Associated with HLA B51","Associated with painless scarring oral ulcers","Associated with erythema nodosum","Associated with peripheral vein thrombosis"],"a":1,"e":"Recurrent oral and genital ulcerations with a positive pathergy test are suggestive of Behcet's syndrome. It is associated with oral ulcers that are painful and heal without scarring.\n\nBehcet's syndrome usually affects young individuals and is associated with HLA-B51 and anti-Saccharomyces cerevisiae antibodies.\n\nThe clinical features of Behcet's syndrome include:\n\n• Oral ulcers - painful, non-scarring\n\n• Genital ulcers - painful, scarring\n\n• Ocular involvement - scarring and bilateral panuveitis\n\n• Skin involvement - folliculitis, acne-like rashes, erythema nodosum, and pyoderma gangrenosum\n\n• Arthritis and peripheral vein thrombosis","t":"Large And Medium Vessel Vasculitis","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is a medium vessel vasculitis?","o":["Polyarteritis nodosa","Behcet's disease","Microscopic polyangiitis","Cogan's syndrome"],"a":0,"e":"Polyarteritis nodosa (PAN) is a medium vessel vasculitis.","t":"Large And Medium Vessel Vasculitis","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"A 47-year-old man presented to the hospital with the findings shown in the image below. He also complained of pain in the upper and lower limbs. On examination, his BP was 160/100 mmHg and his peripheral pulses were palpable. Serology was negative for ANCA. What is the most probable diagnosis?","o":["Takayasu arteritis","Microscopic polyangiitis","Cryoglobulinemic vasculitis","Polyarteritis nodosa"],"a":3,"e":"The most probable diagnosis is polyarteritis nodosa.\n\nThe image shows the gangrene of the fingers. The presence of myalgia, the involvement of cutaneous vessels (gangrene with palpable peripheral pulses), renal vessels causing hypertension and serology negative for ANCA make polyarteritis nodosa (PAN) the most probable diagnosis.\n\nPolyarteritis nodosa (PAN) is necrotizing arteritis of the medium-sized blood vessels. It can affect the skin, peripheral nerves, renal vessels, mesenteric vessels, heart, and brain. Lungs are usually spared in PAN.\n\nThe disease initially starts with non-specific symptoms like fever, malaise, arthralgia, and myalgia. Later, depending on the artery involved, patients may develop the following symptoms:\n\n• Skin findings - ulcers, digital gangrene, and livedo reticularis\n\n• Renal arteries - hypertension and renal failure\n\n• Mesenteric arteries - abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, bleeding, and a bowel perforation\n\nOther options:\n\n1) Takayasu arteritis- It is a large vessel vasculitis. Also known as pulseless disease as it may be associated with weak upper extremity pulses.\n\n2) Microscopic polyangiitis- It is a small vessel vasculitis. It is positive for p-ANCA. Presentation is similar to granulomatosis with polyangiitis but without upper airway involvement.\n\n3) Cryoglobulinemic vasculitis- It is frequently associated with hepatitis C. Circulating cryoglubulins and rheumatoid factor can be found in blood. Most common clinical manifestations are cutaneous vasculitis, peripheral neuropathy, glomerulonephritis and arthritis.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/684813a21f1c48508ab4c8d82bdaa9b2.JPEG"],"t":"Large And Medium Vessel Vasculitis","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"In a patient diagnosed with polyarteritis nodosa which of the following arteries are characteristically spared?","o":["Renal arteries","Mesenteric arteries","Pulmonary arteries","Bronchial arteries"],"a":2,"e":"Pulmonary arteries are characteristically spared in polyarteritis nodosa (PAN). PAN mainly involves renal and visceral arteries.\n\nHistopathology of the involved arteries usually shows the following findings:\n\n• Transmural necrotizing vasculitis of the arteries and adjacent veins. Venules are spared.\n\n• Renal arteries - arteritis without glomerulonephritis\n\n• Fibrinoid necrosis\n\n• Absence of granuloma and eosinophilia","t":"Large And Medium Vessel Vasculitis","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"What is the most common cause of vasculitis in children in India?","o":["Henoch-Schonlein purpura","Kawasaki disease","Juvenile idiopathic arthritis","Rheumatic fever"],"a":1,"e":"The most common cause of vasculitis in children in India is Kawasaki disease.\n\nKawasaki disease has a higher incidence in children in the Indian subcontinent.\n\nThe most common vasculitis worldwide is Henoch-Schonlein purpura, whereas in the Indian subcontinent and other Asian countries the most common vasculitis is Kawasaki disease.","t":"Large And Medium Vessel Vasculitis","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following findings would you not expect in a patient diagnosed with small-vessel vasculitis?","o":["Palpable purpura","Hematuria","Hemoptysis","Visual loss"],"a":3,"e":"Ischemic optic neuropathy associated with giant cell arteritis (large vessel vasculitis) can present as visual loss.\n\nSmall vessel vasculitis is the inflammation of arterioles, capillaries, or venules. It starts with nonspecific symptoms like fever, fatigue, and arthralgia. Since it mainly affects the small vessels and capillaries present in the lungs, kidney, and skin, the majority of the patients will have:\n\n• Hemoptysis\n\n• Glomerulonephritis (hematuria, proteinuria)\n\n• Palpable purpura","t":"Small Vessel Vasculitis","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following structures is most commonly involved in cutaneous small-vessel vasculitis?","o":["Muscular arteries","Arterioles","Postcapillary venules","Capillaries"],"a":2,"e":"The structure most commonly involved in cutaneous small-vessel vasculitis is postcapillary venules.\n\nCutaneous small-vessel vasculitis is defined as vasculitis which is limited to the small vessels of the skin. There is no systemic vasculitis and glomerulonephritis. It most commonly affects postcapillary venules.\n\nIt is characterized by the presence of leukocytoclasis (presence of neutrophil debris in and around the blood vessels).","t":"Small Vessel Vasculitis","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"Which is the most common form of vasculitis associated with connective tissue disorders?","o":["Medium and small vessel vasculitis","Medium vessel vasculitis","Small vessel venulitis","Small vessel arteritis"],"a":2,"e":"The most common form of vasculitis associated with connective tissue disorders is small-vessel venulitis.\n\nConnective tissue disorders that can cause vasculitis are:\n\n• Systemic lupus erythematosus\n\n• Rheumatoid arthritis\n\n• Relapsing polychondritis\n\n• Sjögren's syndrome","t":"Small Vessel Vasculitis","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"A pathology resident is examining biopsy specimens of patients with clinical features of vasculitis. In which of the following conditions would he report granulomatous inflammation of the vessels?\n\n1. Giant cell arteritis\n\n2. Churg-Strauss syndrome\n\n3. Microscopic polyangiitis\n\n4. Polyarteritis nodosa\n\n5. Takayasu arteritis","o":["1, 2, and 3","2, 4, and 5","1, 2, and 5","1, 3, 4, and 5"],"a":2,"e":"Biopsy findings of giant cell arteritis, Churg-Strauss syndrome, and Takayasu arteritis show granulomatous inflammation of vessels as they are granulomatous vasculitis.\n\nThe vasculitis diseases can be classified as:\n\nGranulomatous vasculitis\nNon-granulomatous vasculitis\n\nGiant cell arteritis\n\nTakayasu arteritis\n\nGranulomatosis with polyangiitis\n\nChurg-Strauss syndrome\n\nPolyarteritis nodosa\n\nKawasaki disease\n\nBehcet's disease\n\nMicroscopic polyangiitis\n\nHenoch-Schonlein purpura","t":"Small Vessel Vasculitis","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"A 45-year-old woman presents with pain over the forehead aggravated on bending and occasional hemoptysis. Laboratory investigations reveal elevated ESR and red cell casts in the urine. Her serology is positive for cANCA. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Goodpasture's syndrome","Granulomatosis with polyangiitis","Microscopic polyangiitis","Henoch-Schonlein pupura"],"a":1,"e":"The given clinical scenario with cANCA positivity is suggestive of granulomatosis with polyangiitis (Wegener's granulomatosis). It is characterized by the involvement of the upper respiratory tract (sinusitis), lower respiratory tract (occasional hemoptysis), and kidneys (red cell casts).\n\nIt is a granulomatous, necrotizing vasculitis of the small vessels that mainly affects:\n\n• Upper respiratory tract- presenting with sinusitis, otitis media, and nasal septal perforation.\n\n• Lower respiratory tract- presenting with hemoptysis.\n\n• Kidney (glomerulonephritis)- presenting with proteinuria, hematuria, and hypertension.\n\nApart from these organs, the disease can also affect the eye and skin. Skin lesions present as palpable purpura or subcutaneous nodules. Laboratory investigations show anemia, leucocytosis, elevated ESR, and ANCA positivity.\n\nOptions A and C: In Goodpasture's syndrome and microscopic polyangiitis, the symptoms of upper respiratory tract involvement are very rare.\n\nOption D: Henoch-Schonlein purpura is commonly seen in children. It presents with palpable purpura, joint pain, abdominal pain, and glomerulonephritis.","t":"Small Vessel Vasculitis","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"A patient who presented with skin rashes, otitis media and hematuria has been diagnosed with granulomatosis with polyangiitis. Which of the following drugs would you not use in the management of this patient?","o":["Rituximab","Cyclophosphamide","Abatacept","Methotrexate"],"a":2,"e":"Abatacept is a disease-modifying antirheumatic drug (DMARD) used in the treatment of rheumatoid arthritis.\n\nImportant drugs used in the management of granulomatosis with polyangiitis (Wegener's granulomatosis) are:\n\n• Cyclophosphamide\n\n• Rituximab (anti CD20 antibody)\n\n• Glucocorticoids\n\n• Methotrexate","t":"Small Vessel Vasculitis","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"A middle-aged woman presents with hemoptysis, proteinuria, and the clinical presentation given below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Churg-Strauss syndrome","Leprosy","Systemic lupus erythematosus","Granulomatosis with polyangiitis"],"a":3,"e":"The given clinical scenario showing saddle nose deformity along with hemoptysis, and proteinuria is suggestive of granulomatosis with polyangiitis (Wegener's granulomatosis).\n\nInvolvement of upper respiratory tract in granulomatosis with polyangiitis manifests as:\n\n• Paranasal sinusitis, serous otitis media\n\n• Nasal ulcers, purulent or bloody nasal discharge\n\n• Nasal perforation leading to the destruction of septum and saddle nose deformity\n\n• Subglottic tracheal stenosis","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3b64a3c5391d49d5858c7bb88196b2b0.JPEG"],"t":"Small Vessel Vasculitis","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not a feature associated with Wegener's granulomatosis?","o":["Pulmonary nodules","Venous thrombosis","Non-necrotizing vasculitis","Glomerulonephritis"],"a":2,"e":"Wegener's granulomatosis is associated with necrotizing vasculitis.\n\nWegener's granulomatosis predominantly affects the small blood vessels of the upper respiratory tract, lungs, and kidneys. Lung involvement will show multiple, bilateral cavitating nodules and pulmonary infiltrates on chest X-ray.\n\nThe radiograph below shows cavitating nodules in a patient with granulomatosis with polyangiitis (Wegener's granulomatosis).\n\nRenal involvement initially appears as focal or segmental glomerulonephritis on biopsy. If left untreated, it can lead to rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis (RPGN). Patients with Wegener's granulomatosis have an increased incidence of venous thrombosis, like deep vein thrombosis and pulmonary embolism.\n\nThe diagnosis is confirmed by the presence of necrotizing granulomatous vasculitis on biopsy. This finding is commonly seen in the tissues obtained from lungs, skin, upper airways, and rarely kidneys.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d96048a027c146f7baa734dfd21c0f99x358x285.PNG"],"t":"Small Vessel Vasculitis","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"A 57-year-old man presented with complaints of fever, weight loss, and hemoptysis. His serology was positive for pANCA and biopsy showed small vessel vasculitis with neutrophil infiltration. Which of the following is a feature associated with his condition?","o":["Non-necrotizing vasculitis","Fibrinoid necrosis","Intravascular granuloma formation","Immune complex mediated vasculitis"],"a":1,"e":"The given clinical presentation along with pANCA positivity and biopsy findings of leukocytoclastic (neutrophil infiltration) vasculitis point towards a diagnosis of microscopic polyangiitis. It is associated with fibrinoid necrosis.\n\nMicroscopic polyangiitis, also known as hypersensitivity vasculitis, causes segmental fibrinoid necrosis of small arterioles and venules. It is associated with neutrophil infiltration, and hence also called leukocytoclastic vasculitis.\n\nHistopathology of the affected tissue will show necrotizing vasculitis of arterioles, venules, and capillaries without any granulomas. It is caused by ANCA (antineutrophil cytoplasmic antibodies) and is not mediated by the immune complex. It primarily affects the kidneys and lungs.","t":"Small Vessel Vasculitis","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following features is not useful to distinguish between microscopic polyangiitis and granulomatosis with polyangiitis?","o":["Upper airway involvement","Granulomas","Pulmonary nodules","Necrotizing vasculitis"],"a":3,"e":"Both microscopic polyangiitis and granulomatosis with polyangiitis are associated with necrotizing vasculitis. Hence, this feature is not useful to distinguish between them.","t":"Small Vessel Vasculitis","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"A 47-year-old housewife presented with recurrent attacks of asthma and pain and numbness along the distribution of the median and ulnar nerve. Her serology was positive for pANCA. Further evaluation is unlikely to reveal which of the following findings?","o":["Peripheral blood eosinophilia","Tissue eosinophilia","Extravascular granuloma","Large vessel vasculitis"],"a":3,"e":"The given clinical scenario of asthma, mononeuritis multiplex, and pANCA positivity is suggestive of Churg-Strauss syndrome (eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis). It affects small to medium-sized arteries.\n\nLaboratory investigations will show peripheral blood eosinophilia. Some patients may be positive for pANCA (antineutrophil cytoplasmic antibodies). Histopathology will show the presence of necrotizing vasculitis, tissue eosinophilia, and extravascular granulomas.\n\nHistopathology of ANCA associated vasculitides can be remembered as:\n\n• Microscopic polyangiitis: necrotizing vasculitis\n\n• Wegener's granulomatosis: necrotizing vasculitis with granulomas\n\n• Churg Strauss syndrome: necrotizing vasculitis with extravascular granulomas and eosinophilia","t":"Small Vessel Vasculitis","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"A 6-year-old boy presents to the ER with colicky abdominal pain. His mother tells you that he refuses to walk due to knee pain. On examination, palpable purpura is noted. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Henoch Schonlein purpura","Hemolytic uremic syndrome","Juvenile Idiopathic arthritis","IgA nephropathy"],"a":0,"e":"The given clinical presentation of palpable purpura, colicky abdominal pain, and arthritis, point towards a diagnosis of Henoch Schonlein purpura (HSP) also known as IgA vasculitis.\n\nIt is a small vessel vasculitis that occurs due to IgA immune complex deposition within the wall of the blood vessels. It is commonly seen in children and is characterized by:\n\n• Palpable purpura without thrombocytopenia and coagulopathy.\n\n• Arthralgia- commonly affecting hip, knee, or ankle joints.\n\n• Abdominal pain\n\n• Glomerulonephritis: proteinuria, microscopic hematuria, RBC casts in urine.\n\nThe skin biopsy will show small vessel vasculitis, with IgA and C3 deposition in the affected organs by immunofluorescence.\n\nOption B: Hemolytic uremic syndrome is characterized by a triad of microangiopathic hemolytic anemia, thrombocytopenia, and acute kidney injury.\n\nOption C: Juvenile idiopathic arthritis (JIA) is characterized by fever of at least 2 weeks duration associated with arthritis, evanescent erythematous rash, hepatosplenomegaly, generalized lymphadenopathy, and serositis.\n\nOption D: Patients with IgA nephropathy or Berger's disease present with gross hematuria, often associated with a preceding or ongoing viral infection of the upper respiratory tract (synpharyngitic presentation).","t":"Small Vessel Vasculitis","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"A patient receiving anti-tubercular therapy has been recently diagnosed with gout. Which of the following statements is incorrect about this condition?","o":["The crystals are positively birefringent","Ethambutol can precipitate gout","More common in males","Most commonly involves the first MTP joint"],"a":0,"e":"In gout, the crystals are negatively birefringent on polarized light microscopy of the joint aspirate.\n\nGout is a metabolic disease that is characterized by the deposition of monosodium urate crystals in joints and connective tissue. It is more common in males.\n\nIt usually presents as monoarthritis that may be acute and episodic or chronic. Acute gout presents with a joint that is red, hot, swollen, and extremely tender on examination. The metatarsophalangeal joint of the great toe is the most frequently involved joint.\n\nSynovial fluid analysis of joint aspirate is diagnostic and shows the following features:\n\n• Increased leukocyte count with a neutrophilic predominance\n\n• Negatively birefringent crystals on polarized light microscopy of the synovial fluid\n\nAntitubercular drugs like pyrazinamide and ethambutol cause hyperuricemia by inhibiting uric acid excretion in the kidney.\n\nMonosodium urate crystals as seen on polarized light microscopy:","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/09a02e26bed341bcace78953fef96282x958x650.PNG"],"t":"Crystal Arthropathies","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"An elderly man presented to the OPD with painful swelling of his left big toe. Synovial fluid examination showed negatively birefringent needle-shaped crystals. What are these crystals made of?","o":["Calcium apatite","Monosodium urate","Calcium pyrophosphate","Ammonium urate"],"a":1,"e":"The given clinical presentation and polarised microscopy showing negatively birefringent needle-shaped crystals point towards a diagnosis of gout. In gout, the negatively birefringent needle-shaped crystals are due to the deposition of monosodium urate crystals.\n\nMonosodium urate crystals in gout:\n\nOption A: Calcium apatite crystals can accumulate in areas of tissue damage, hypercalcemia, or hyperparathyroid states.\n\nOption C: Calcium pyrophosphate crystals are positively birefringent rhomboid-shaped crystals that are seen in pseudogout.\n\nCalcium pyrophosphate crystals seen in pseudogout:\n\nOption D: Ammonium urate crystals can cause urinary bladder stones which are radiolucent but may develop a calcific coating making it visible on X-rays.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8f37da9847d549ffbdc59cde53673245x958x650.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/050601b9855c49e5bdfd7d25dcd1c97fx796x478.PNG"],"t":"Crystal Arthropathies","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following synovial fluid crystals cannot be identified using polarized light microscopy?","o":["Monosodium urate","Calcium pyrophosphate","Calcium apatite","Calcium oxalate"],"a":2,"e":"Calcium apatite crystals cannot be identified using polarized light microscopy.\n\nPolarized light microscopy can identify crystals such as:\n\n• Monosodium urate\n\n• Calcium pyrophosphate\n\n• Calcium oxalate","t":"Crystal Arthropathies","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"A patient has a history of multiple episodes of painful swelling of ankles and big toes which is relieved with NSAIDs. You would avoid all of the following drugs in her except:","o":["Furosemide","High dose salicylates","Cyclosporine","Pyrazinamide"],"a":1,"e":"The given scenario of multiple painful swelling of ankles and toes that responded to NSAIDs is suggestive of gout. High-dose salicylates (>3g/day) do not precipitate gout.\n\nAlthough high-dose salicylates inhibit both the secretion and reabsorption of urate, the inhibition of reabsorption is more than the inhibition of secretion, and therefore the net effect is uricosuric.\n\nOn the other hand, low-dose salicylates (<2 to 3g/day) are associated with uric acid retention. They inhibit renal tubular secretion of uric acid resulting in hyperuricemia and may precipitate gout.\n\nAspirin is not used to treat acute attacks of gout due to its uric acid retention property. However, low-dose aspirin used for cardiovascular prophylaxis need not be discontinued during the treatment of a gout flare.","t":"Crystal Arthropathies","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"A patient with chronic gout now presented to the emergency room after sustaining trauma to his left foot. Identify the abnormality in his X-ray given below.","o":["Podagra","Martel's sign","Chopart's fracture","March fracture"],"a":1,"e":"The given X-ray of a patient with chronic gout shows Martel's sign.\n\nMartel's sign also known as the G sign, or rat-bite erosion, refers to the punched-out lytic lesion and overhanging margin of the new bone along the edge of erosion seen in chronic tophaceous gout.\n\nOption A: Podagra is the inflammation of the base of the great toe (metatarsophalangeal joint) during flares of gout. It is not a radiological sign.\n\nOption C: Chopart's fracture is a fracture between talonavicular and calcaneocuboid joints.\n\nOption D: March fracture is fracture neck of 2nd or 3rd metatarsal.\n\nX-ray showing Martel's sign:","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/562c763a24f3463094b486cfa12a6fbf.JPEG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/11c19f642a9a4ba69095241f3e137221x592x749.JPEG"],"t":"Crystal Arthropathies","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"A 52-year-old man presented to the OPD with complaints of severe joint pain in his big toe with history of multiple similar episodes in the past. An ultrasound of the first metatarsophalangeal joint was performed. What is the characteristic finding depicted in the image given below?","o":["Double wall sign","Double density sign","Double contour sign","Double barrel sign"],"a":2,"e":"The characteristic finding depicted in the given ultrasound image of a patient with chronic gout is the double contour sign.\n\nDouble contour sign is characterized by an echogenic line on the outer surface of the joint cartilage, due to the deposition of monosodium urate crystals on the surface of articular cartilage. It is seen in chronic gout.\n\nOption A: Double wall sign also known as Rigler's sign is an abdominal radiograph finding seen in pneumoperitoneum. Here both the inner and outer walls of the bowel are visible.\n\nOption B: Double density sign on chest X-ray PA view is seen due to left atrial enlargement. Here, the right border of the left atrium becomes laterally displaced and convex producing this appearance.\n\nOption D: Double barrel sign is a USG finding seen in common bile duct (CBD) obstruction causing dilatation of CBD and its lumen size becomes comparable to portal vein giving the appearance of a double-barrel.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1f6b6510aa674aeda89f1bb5b201eb8f.PNG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1ac8a3327d2747ea9bf11e2016fe28c1x720x717.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8497114404504df98cae855383ae5ea3x720x810.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/757b032ff9744721a07a6c6ea22505f7x791x459.PNG"],"t":"Crystal Arthropathies","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"A middle-aged man presented to the ER with severe pain and swelling as shown in the image below. He told that he had consumed 2 bottles of beer a few hours before. Which of the following drugs is not used in the management of this patient?","o":["Indomethacin","Prednisone","Allopurinol","Colchicine"],"a":2,"e":"The given scenario is suggestive of an acute attack of gout. Allopurinol is used in the management of chronic gout.\n\nUrate-lowering drugs such as allopurinol, probenecid, and lesinurad are not initiated during acute attacks.\n\nThe treatment options for an acute attack of gout include:\n\n• NSAIDs: Indomethacin, naproxen, ibuprofen, and diclofenac are highly effective\n\n• Colchicine: It acts by inhibiting neutrophil migration and reduces inflammation. It is used orally and is most effective if given early in an attack.\n\n• Glucocorticoids: Systemic glucocorticoids may be used in polyarticular involvement. If the disease is limited to one joint, intraarticular triamcinolone may be given.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/cfd271532c0f44a896869cbba781b408.JPEG"],"t":"Crystal Arthropathies","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following drugs is the best agent for the long-term treatment of chronic gout in a 50-year-old patient?","o":["Probenecid","Colchicine","Allopurinol","NSAIDS"],"a":2,"e":"Allopurinol is the best agent for the long-term treatment of chronic or recurrent gout.\n\nHypouricemic drugs that can be used for definitive treatment of chronic gout include:\n\n• Xanthine oxidase inhibitors- allopurinol, febuxostat\n\n• Uricosuric agents- probenecid, lesinurad\n\n• Recombinant uricases- rasburicase and pegloticase","t":"Crystal Arthropathies","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"A 58-year-old man with history of gout is now diagnosed with hypertension. Which of the following is the drug of choice for this patient?","o":["Hydrochlorothiazide","Metolazone","Losartan","Furosemide"],"a":2,"e":"Losartan is the drug of choice for the management of hypertension in a patient with gout because of its uricosuric action.\n\nThiazides (hydrochlorothiazide, metolazone) and loop diuretics (furosemide) cause reduced excretion of uric acid from the renal tubules and result in hyperuricemia. Therefore they are not preferred for management of hypertension in patients with gout.","t":"Crystal Arthropathies","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"Which joint is most commonly affected in calcium pyrophosphate disease?","o":["Wrist","Knee","Metacarpophalangeal joint","Metatarsophalangeal joint"],"a":1,"e":"The knee joint is most commonly affected in calcium pyrophosphate (CPPD) disease or pseudogout. Other sites include the wrist, shoulder, ankle, elbow, and hands.\n\nAcute CPPD arthropathy is commonly seen in the elderly and may be associated with conditions such as primary hyperparathyroidism, hypothyroidism, and hemochromatosis.\n\nX-ray of the affected joint may reveal chondrocalcinosis, which represents calcium deposition in articular cartilage. Polarised light microscopy of the joint aspirate demonstrates positively birefringent rhomboid crystals, as shown below:","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8fdf5c9ac3844718be547602f5e72e62x796x478.PNG"],"t":"Crystal Arthropathies","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"What is the most common cause of secondary Sjogren's syndrome?","o":["Systemic lupus erythematosus","Rheumatoid arthritis","Scleroderma","Primary biliary cirrhosis"],"a":1,"e":"The most common cause of secondary Sjögren’s syndrome is rheumatoid arthritis.\n\nSjögren's syndrome is a chronic autoimmune disorder that affects the lacrimal and salivary glands. It also affects other organ systems, such as lungs, kidneys, and nervous system. It is seen in association with:\n\n• Rheumatoid arthritis\n\n• Systemic lupus erythematosus\n\n• Scleroderma\n\n• Mixed connective tissue disease\n\n• Primary biliary cirrhosis\n\n• Vasculitis\n\n• Chronic active hepatitis C","t":"Sjogren'S Syndrome And Scleroderma","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"Which is the most common extraglandular manifestation seen in patients diagnosed with Sjogren's syndrome?","o":["Primary biliary cirrhosis","Vasculitis","Arthritis","Renal tubular acidosis"],"a":2,"e":"The most common extraglandular manifestation seen in patients with Sjögren’s syndrome is arthritis or arthralgia.\n\nThe extraglandular manifestations in Sjögren’s syndrome are:\n\n• Arthralgia or arthritis - most common manifestation. It is usually non-erosive and leads to Jaccoud's arthropathy. Jaccoud's arthropathy is a deforming non-erosive arthropathy characterized by the ulnar deviation of the second to 5th fingers with metacarpophalangeal joint subluxation.\n\n• Raynaud's phenomenon\n\n• Interstitial lung disease - most common type is non-specific interstitial pneumonia (NSIP)\n\n• Renal tubular acidosis - Type 1 (distal) RTA\n\n• Primary biliary cirrhosis\n\n• Lymphoma - MALToma\n\nThe give X-ray shows Jaccoud's arthropathy:\n\nThe given image shows a patient with Jaccoud's arthropathy of the hands:","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/177185cae72145cebef0694fd856d852x462x600.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ea29d5d4ed174da0a7adf294fa341693x986x732.JPEG"],"t":"Sjogren'S Syndrome And Scleroderma","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"A middle-aged woman presented with complaints of persistent dry eyes and dry mouth since past few months. On examination, dry mucous membranes, bilateral parotid region swelling, and conjunctivitis were noted. This condition is associated with which of the following?","o":["HLA-DR2","HLA-DR4","HLA-DR3","HLA-B27"],"a":2,"e":"Dry eyes and dry mouth along with parotid swelling and conjunctivitis are suggestive of Sjögren’s syndrome. It is associated with HLA-DR3 allele.\n\nHLA allele\nDisease\n\nHLA DR2, HLA-DR3\nSLE\n\nHLA-DR3\nSjogren's syndrome\n\nHLA-DR4\nRheumatoid arthritis\n\nHLA-B27\nSeronegative arthritis","t":"Sjogren'S Syndrome And Scleroderma","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"A software engineer presented with complaints of dryness and itching of eyes on staring at his laptop screen. On examination, parotid swelling was noted and the test shown below was positive. The serology of this patient is likely to be positive for which of the following antibodies?","o":["Anti topoisomerase","Anti centromere","Anti U1-RNP","Anti Ro/SS-A"],"a":3,"e":"The given scenario of dry eyes, parotid swelling, and a positive Schirmer test is suggestive of Sjögren's syndrome. It is associated with anti-Ro/SS-A and anti-La/SS-B autoantibodies.\n\nThe presence of anti-Ro/SS-A and anti-La/SS-B indicate extra glandular involvement and poor prognosis.\n\nNote: Anti-Ro and anti-La in SLE indicate a good prognosis, due to decreased risk of nephritis and vasculitis.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d54558cf5d494b4f969a54453199ddbc.JPEG"],"t":"Sjogren'S Syndrome And Scleroderma","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"A 55-year-old woman presents with dry mouth, and difficulty swallowing solid food. Physical examination finds enlargement of her parotid glands along with marked dryness of her buccal mucosa. Laboratory examination finds the presence of both SS-A and SS-B antibodies. Which of the following statements is not true about this condition?","o":["About 75% of patients have rheumatoid factor","Patients are at high risk for development of B-cell lymphomas","Glomerular lesions are common","Lacrimal gland is a major target for this disease"],"a":2,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of Sjogren's syndrome. In this condition, glomerular lesions are extremely rare.\n\nDefects of tubular function, including renal tubular acidosis, uricosuria, and phosphaturia, are often seen and are associated histologically with tubulointerstitial nephritis.\n\nLacrimal and salivary glands are the major targets of this disease, while the respiratory and gastrointestinal tracts and the vagina may also be involved. The characteristic decrease in tears and saliva (sicca syndrome) is the result of lymphocytic infiltration and fibrosis of the lacrimal and salivary glands. The infiltrate contains predominantly activated CD4+ helper T cells and some B cells, including plasma cells.\n\nAntibodies detected are:\n\n• Rheumatoid factor - seen in 75% of patients, whether or not coexisting rheumatoid arthritis is present\n\n• Anti-nuclear antibodies (ANAs)\n\n• SS-A (Ro) - associated with early disease onset, longer disease duration, and extra-glandular manifestations, such as cutaneous vasculitis and nephritis\n\n• SS-B (La)\n\nThese patients are at high risk for the development of B-cell lymphomas. About 60% of patients have another accompanying autoimmune disorder, such as rheumatoid arthritis, and these patients also have the symptoms and signs of that disorder.\n\nBiopsy of the lip (to examine minor salivary glands) is essential for the diagnosis of Sjögren's syndrome.\n\nThe image shows an enlargement of the salivary gland with intense lymphocytic and plasma cell infiltration with ductal epithelial hyperplasia in the salivary gland.\n\nNote: Mikulicz syndrome is characterized by lacrimal and salivary gland enlargement from any cause, including sarcoidosis, lymphomas, and other tumors.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f14b28bf51124f3289fcb5a746bbcc2ex1280x1174.JPEG"],"t":"Sjogren'S Syndrome And Scleroderma","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"A 35-year-old woman presented with complaints of thickening of her skin and the finding shown below. On workup, ANA was positive and immunofluorescence showed nucleolar pattern. What is the most common extracutaneous manifestation associated with her condition?","o":["Pulmonary fibrosis","Myositis","Scleroderma renal crisis","Raynaud's phenomenon"],"a":3,"e":"The given image is suggestive of microstomia. The findings of skin thickening, microstomia, positive ANA, and immunofluorescence showing nucleolar pattern point towards a diagnosis of systemic sclerosis. The most common extracutaneous manifestation of systemic sclerosis (scleroderma) is Raynaud's phenomenon.\n\nRaynaud's phenomenon is characterized by episodes of reversible vasoconstriction in the fingers and toes on exposure to cold. Rarely, it affects the lips, tip of the nose, and earlobes. These episodes classically occur in the following sequence:\n\n• Affected part turning pale due to vasoconstriction.\n\n• Blue due to ischemia, often associated with numbness or pain.\n\n• As blood flow returns, the area turns red with a burning sensation.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0415bbcbc915465ca92c44c9f12378c3.JPEG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8467c02c823442868d464d175a7dcb4ax498x1345.JPEG"],"t":"Sjogren'S Syndrome And Scleroderma","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"A 14-year-old girl has the pallor of extremities followed by pain and cyanosis on exposure to cold. Which of the following diseases is she likely to develop in the later stages of life?","o":["Myasthenia gravis","Scleroderma","Mikulicz syndrome","Dermatomyositis"],"a":1,"e":"The above clinical scenario suggests that the patient has Raynaud phenomenon, which is associated with the risk of developing scleroderma in later stages of life.\n\nRaynaud phenomenon is manifested as episodic vasoconstriction of the arteries and arterioles of the extremities, causing pallor of extremities followed by pain and cyanosis. It is seen in virtually all patients with systemic sclerosis or scleroderma and precedes other symptoms in 70% of cases.\n\nThe image shows features of Raynaud's phenomenon.\n\nSystemic sclerosis is characterized by progressive fibrosis involving the skin, gastrointestinal tract, and other tissues. Fibrosis may be the result of the activation of fibroblasts by cytokines produced by T cells.\n\nFocal, and sometimes diffuse, subcutaneous calcifications may develop, especially in patients with CREST syndrome. The most ominous manifestation is malignant hypertension.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/db06da13868c4bfab2f410614c57a112x498x1345.JPEG"],"t":"Sjogren'S Syndrome And Scleroderma","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements is false about Raynaud’s phenomenon?","o":["Secondary Raynaud’s is associated with more severe symptoms","Secondary Raynaud’s develops predominantly after the age of 30 years","Primary Raynaud's is usually ANA negative","Primary Raynaud’s is associated with digital tissue necrosis and ulcers"],"a":3,"e":"Primary Raynaud's phenomenon is not associated with tissue necrosis and ulcers.\n\nFeatures\nPrimary Raynaud's phenomenon\nSecondary Raynaud's phenomenon\n\nAge & gender\nYoung women (<30y)\nOlder women (>30y)\n\nAssociations\nNo underlying cause\nScleroderma, Sjögren's syndrome and SLE\n\nFamily history\nYes\nNo\n\nANA test\nNegative\nPositive\n\nSeverity - duration, frequency & pain\nLess severe\nMore severe\n\nDigital tissue necrosis, ulceration & gangrene\nNo\nYes\n\nNailfold capillaroscopy\n\n(Image below)\n\nNormal capillaries in evenly spaced parallel loops\nDistorted capillaries with widened & irregular loops","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/154d53a6118c4185a23957ac0fcb5dcbx1280x2049.JPEG"],"t":"Sjogren'S Syndrome And Scleroderma","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"What is the most common histological pattern of interstitial lung disease seen in patients with systemic sclerosis?","o":["Non specific interstitial pneumonia","Usual interstitial pneumonia","Desquamative interstitial pneumonia","Cryptogenic organizing pneumonia"],"a":0,"e":"The most common histological pattern of interstitial lung disease in systemic sclerosis (scleroderma) is non-specific interstitial pneumonia (NSIP). It commonly affects the lower lobes and is characterised by ground-glass opacities.\n\nNote: Usual interstitial pneumonia (UIP) is the most common type of ILD in rheumatoid arthritis.","t":"Sjogren'S Syndrome And Scleroderma","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following drugs are implicated in systemic sclerosis-like illness?\n\n1. Cyclophosphamide\n\n2. Pentazocine\n\n3. Bosentan\n\n4. Bleomycin\n\n5. Cocaine","o":["1, 3, and 4","2, 3, and 5","1, 4, and 5","2, 4, and 5"],"a":3,"e":"Drugs implicated in systemic sclerosis-like illness are:\n\n• Bleomycin\n\n• Pentazocine\n\n• Cocaine\n\n• L-tryptophan-containing dietary supplements\n\nChemicals implicated in systemic sclerosis-like illness are:\n\n• Heavy metals\n\n• Polyvinyl chloride (PVC)\n\n• Toluene\n\n• Trichloroethylene\n\nCyclophosphamide and bosentan are used in the treatment of systemic sclerosis (SSc). Cyclophosphamide reduces the progression of ILD associated with SSc. Bosentan reduces the risk of new ulcers in Raynaud's phenomenon associated with SSc.","t":"Sjogren'S Syndrome And Scleroderma","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"A 38-year-old woman presents with ulceration over her fingers. She has history of Raynaud's phenomenon and GERD since 3 years. Which of the following can be used as a screening test for her condition?","o":["Antinuclear antibody (ANA)","Anti centromere antibody","Anti topoisomerase I (Scl-70)","Anti-cardiolipin antibody"],"a":0,"e":"The presence of digital ulcers, Raynaud's phenomenon, and GERD are suggestive of systemic sclerosis (scleroderma). Antinuclear antibody (ANA) is the preferred screening test as it is the most sensitive test for both limited and diffuse variants of systemic sclerosis.\n\nOption B: Anti centromere antibody is the most specific test for CREST syndrome.\n\nOption C: Anti topoisomerase I (Scl-70) is the most specific test for diffuse cutaneous systemic sclerosis.\n\nOption D: Anti-cardiolipin antibody is seen in SLE and APLA syndrome.","t":"Sjogren'S Syndrome And Scleroderma","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"A 47-year-old woman presents with complaints of swollen fingers, heartburn, and occasional joint pains. She gives a history suggestive of Raynaud's phenomenon. Physical examination reveals taut, thickened skin over her hands and fingers with limited mobility. The presence of which of the following antibodies will confirm her diagnosis?","o":["Antinuclear antibody","Anticentromere antibody","Anti-topoisomerase I antibody","Anti-RNA polymerase III antibody"],"a":1,"e":"The given scenario of skin thickening in the hands and fingers (sclerodactyly), Raynaud's phenomenon, heartburn (GERD), and occasional arthralgia is suggestive of limited cutaneous systemic sclerosis (lcSSc). The presence of anticentromere antibody (ACA) will confirm the diagnosis of limited cutaneous systemic sclerosis.\n\nAntinuclear antibody (ANA) is positive in most cases of systemic sclerosis. The absence of this antibody suggests considering an alternate diagnosis.","t":"Sjogren'S Syndrome And Scleroderma","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"A 53-year-old woman presented with thickening of skin over the trunk and proximal parts of the limbs. On workup, her serology was found to be positive for anti-topoisomerase I. Which of the following features is not likely to be associated with her condition?","o":["Severe arthralgia","Critical limb ischemia","Interstitial lung disease","Carpal tunnel syndrome"],"a":1,"e":"The presence of skin thickening over the trunk and proximal parts of the limbs along with positive anti-topoisomerase I antibodies is suggestive of diffuse cutaneous systemic sclerosis (dcSSc). Critical limb ischemia is commonly associated with limited cutaneous systemic sclerosis (lcSSc).","t":"Sjogren'S Syndrome And Scleroderma","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not a component of CREST syndrome?","o":["Calcinosis cutis","Renal failure","Esophageal dysmotility","Sclerodactyly"],"a":1,"e":"Renal failure is not a component of CREST syndrome.\n\nCREST syndrome is seen in limited cutaneous systemic sclerosis (lcSSc) and consists of the following:\n\n• Calcinosis cutis\n\n• Raynaud’s phenomenon\n\n• Esophageal dysmotility\n\n• Sclerodactyly\n\n• Telangiectasia","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5b1c8ade86304eecb5d229324d11e418x1280x2560.JPEG"],"t":"Sjogren'S Syndrome And Scleroderma","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following antibodies is likely to be positive in patients who develop scleroderma renal crisis?","o":["Anti centromere","Anti Ku","Anti U1-RNP","Anti RNA polymerase III"],"a":3,"e":"Anti-RNA polymerase III antibody is associated with scleroderma renal crisis.\n\nScleroderma renal crisis is scleroderma with accelerated hypertension, acute kidney injury and renal failure. The antibodies associated with it are:\n\n• Anti-RNA polymerase III\n\n• Topoisomerase I\n\n• U3-RNP\n\nTarget Antigen\nCharacteristic Clinical Association\n\nTopoisomerase I\nEarly ILD, cardiac involvement, scleroderma renal crisis\n\nRNA Polymerase III\nScleroderma renal crisis, increased cancer risk, GAVE (gastric antral vascular ectasia)\n\nU3-RNP\nPAH, ILD, scleroderma renal crisis, and myositis\n\nTh/To\nInterstitial lung disease (ILD) and Pulmonary artery hypertension (PAH)\n\nKu\nOverlap syndrome","t":"Sjogren'S Syndrome And Scleroderma","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"An elderly man presents to the ER with severe headache, blurring of vision, and oliguria. Examination reveals BP of 200/100mmHg and papilledema. Skin thickening is noted over his hands, with contractures in the wrist and fingers. Investigations reveal serum creatinine of 3mg/dL and peripheral smear shows schistocytes. What is the drug of choice?","o":["Direct renin inhibitors","ACE inhibitors","Steroids","Calcium channel blockers"],"a":1,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of scleroderma renal crisis. The drug of choice is ACE inhibitors.\n\nScleroderma renal crisis is a medical emergency characterized by acute onset malignant hypertension and kidney failure, usually in the absence of previous evidence of significant kidney disease. The use of steroids is associated with an increased risk of scleroderma renal crisis.\n\nClinical features of scleroderma renal crisis are:\n\n• Accelerated hypertension - headache, vomiting, blurring of vision, hypertensive retinopathy, and hypertensive encephalopathy.\n\n• Acute kidney injury - oliguria, mild proteinuria, microscopic hematuria, renal failure.\n\n• Pulmonary edema, congestive heart failure.\n\n• Microangiopathic hemolytic anemia (schistocytes), thrombocytopenia.\n\nIn patients with persistent hypertension, the addition of angiotensin II receptor blockers, calcium channel blockers, and direct renin inhibitors should be considered.","t":"Sjogren'S Syndrome And Scleroderma","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"In patients with sarcoidosis, which of the following is the most commonly affected organ?","o":["Lungs","Eyes","Liver","Skin"],"a":0,"e":"Sarcoidosis most commonly affects the lungs.\n\nSarcoidosis is a multisystem inflammatory disease characterized by non-caseating granulomas. It commonly involves the lungs, skin, liver, and eyes.","t":"Sarcoidosis","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"A 35-year-old woman with history of sarcoidosis presents with skin lesions shown in the image below. Identify the condition.","o":["Acne rosacea","Lupus pernio","Heliotrope rash","Malar rash"],"a":1,"e":"The given condition is lupus pernio which is a cutaneous manifestation of sarcoidosis.\n\nLupus pernio is a chronic inflammatory skin lesion, which is diagnostic for a chronic form of sarcoidosis. It involves the bridge of the nose, the area beneath the eyes, and the cheeks and often is purple in color.\n\nOther options:\n\nOption A: Acne rosacea is a skin disorder with onset in middle‐aged adults (30–50 years old) and involves the convexities of the face.\n\nOption C: Heliotrope rash presents as violaceous lesions around eyelids. It is seen in dermatomyositis.\n\nOption D: Malar rash or butterfly rash is classically seen in SLE.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d6e8e64d2a6c4ec284fcf07fa6f8c05d.JPEG"],"t":"Sarcoidosis","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"A 24-year-old man presented with fever, productive cough, and weight loss. On examination, the following skin lesions were seen. His chest X-ray showed hilar lymphadenopathy. Which is the next best investigation for the management of this patient?","o":["Sputum AFB smear","Serum ACE levels","CD4/CD8 ratio in BAL","Lymph node biopsy"],"a":0,"e":"The given clinical features along with examination findings of lupus vulgaris is suggestive of tuberculosis. The next best investigation is AFB smear microscopy of the sputum sample.\n\nLupus vulgaris is a manifestation of cutaneous tuberculosis. It has a characteristic slow enlarging plaque with an elevated border and central atrophy.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/45ddc805a0764f759024ee776ced1d8d.JPEG"],"t":"Sarcoidosis","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"A man came to the clinic with fatigue, cough, and breathing difficulty. His chest X-ray showed bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy and lung biopsy revealed non-caseating granuloma. What is the most common ocular manifestation associated with this condition?","o":["Anterior uveitis","Scleritis","Episcleritis","Conjunctivitis"],"a":0,"e":"The given clinical features along with bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy and non-caseating granuloma are suggestive of sarcoidosis. The most common ocular manifestation in sarcoidosis is anterior uveitis.\n\nAnterior uveitis presents with photophobia, blurry vision, and lacrimation. Other ocular manifestations include retinitis, pars planitis, and dry eyes.","t":"Sarcoidosis","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"Which is the most common cranial nerve to be involved in sarcoidosis?","o":["Optic nerve","Facial nerve","Oculomotor nerve","Trigeminal nerve"],"a":1,"e":"The most common cranial nerve to be involved in sarcoidosis is the facial nerve leading to facial palsy.\n\nNeurosarcoidosis frequently manifests as follows:\n\n• Cranial nerve involvement- optic neuritis (CN II), extraocular muscles involvement (CN III, IV, VI) and facial palsy (CN VII)\n\n• Space occupying lesions\n\n• Diabetes insipidus - due to anterior hypothalamus involvement\n\n• Basilar meningitis","t":"Sarcoidosis","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following features would you not expect in a patient diagnosed with Lofgren’s syndrome?","o":["Arthritis","Parotid enlargement","Bilateral hilar adenopathy","Erythema nodosum"],"a":1,"e":"Parotid enlargement is not a feature of Lofgren's syndrome. Lofgren’s syndrome is an acute form of sarcoidosis that has a good prognosis, with most of the patients experiencing disease resolution within 2 years.\n\nIt is characterized by:\n\n• Arthritis\n\n• Erythema nodosum\n\n• Bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy\n\nHeerfordt–Waldenström syndrome also known as uveoparotid fever is a variant of sarcoidosis characterized by:\n\n• Fever\n\n• Parotid enlargement\n\n• Facial palsy\n\n• Anterior uveitis","t":"Sarcoidosis","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"A 44-year-old construction worker, who is a known case of sarcoidosis, has now presented with polyuria, constipation, and myalgia for the past 5 days. An ECG was taken which showed a short QT interval. Which of the following is responsible for this condition?\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000 \u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000 \u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000\u0000","o":["Increased bone resorption","Decreased renal excretion of calcium","Increased production of vitamin D","Increased production of PTH related peptide"],"a":2,"e":"The given clinical features along with a short QT interval points towards hypercalcemia. Hypercalcemia in a patient with sarcoidosis is due to the increased production of vitamin D by the non-caseating granulomas.\n\nHypercalcemia and hypercalciuria are seen in some patients with sarcoidosis. The increased production of vitamin D (1,25-dihydoxyvitamin D) results in increased intestinal absorption of calcium, leading to hypercalcemia.\n\nVitamin D supplementation and increased sun exposure in patients with sarcoidosis can precipitate hypercalcemia.","t":"Sarcoidosis","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"A patient was evaluated for unexplained cough and shortness of breath. Investigations revealed elevated serum calcium and a chest X-ray showed hilar lymphadenopathy. A lymph node biopsy was taken to confirm the diagnosis. Which of the following is least likely to be seen in the histopathological examination?","o":["Non-caseating granuloma","Asteroid bodies","Schaumann bodies","Masson bodies"],"a":3,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of sarcoidosis, in which the histopathological examination of lesions does not show Masson bodies. Masson bodies are polypoid plugs of loose organizing connective tissue seen in cryptogenic organizing pneumonia.\n\nThe cardinal histopathologic feature of sarcoidosis, irrespective of the organ involved, is the non-caseating granuloma. Two other microscopic features are sometimes seen in the granulomas:\n\n• Schaumann bodies- inclusion bodies with concentric rings of calcification\n\n• Asteroid bodies- stellate inclusions in the giant cells","t":"Sarcoidosis","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"What is the most common abnormality of liver function in sarcoidosis?","o":["Elevation of transaminases","Elevation of alkaline phosphatase","Coagulopathy","Elevation of bilirubin"],"a":1,"e":"The most common abnormality of liver function in sarcoidosis is the elevation of alkaline phosphatase (ALP) levels.\n\nSarcoidosis of the liver presents as a granulomatous disease that causes intrahepatic cholestasis. As the disease advances it leads to elevated liver enzymes followed by elevated bilirubin levels.","t":"Sarcoidosis","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"A middle-aged woman presented to you with shortness of breath and dry cough for the past 3 months. On examination, the following skin lesions were seen. You will order all of the following investigations except:","o":["Serum angiotensin converting enzyme levels","CD4/CD8 counts in the blood","Biopsy of the lymph nodes","Gallium scan"],"a":1,"e":"The given clinical scenario and examination findings of erythema nodosum are suggestive of sarcoidosis. CD4/CD8 ratio in bronchoalveolar lavage (BAL) fluid, not blood, is used for diagnosis of sarcoidosis.\n\nErythema nodosum is inflammation of the subcutaneous fat (panniculitis) seen in sarcoidosis.\n\nThe investigations used in the diagnosis of sarcoidosis include:\n\n• Serum ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme) levels - elevated\n\n• BAL- lymphocytosis and increased CD4/CD8 ratio. A ratio of >3.5 in BAL is strongly supportive of sarcoidosis.\n\n• PET-CT scan - to identify areas of granulomatous disease\n\n• Gallium scan shows the following signs:\n\n• Panda sign- due to bilateral involvement of parotid and lacrimal glands in sarcoidosis\n\n• Lambda sign- due to bilateral hilar and right paratracheal lymph nodes which resembles lambda (λ)\n\n• Biopsy showing non-caseating granulomas in a patient with features of sarcoidosis confirms the diagnosis.\n\nThe image given below shows the Panda sign:","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/897c632cd7554555b86daaee54002eee.JPEG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/de1d5da24b3e4eb5b78552a2a99de166x720x602.PNG"],"t":"Sarcoidosis","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"The chest radiograph of a young man with sarcoidosis shows pulmonary infiltrates in the upper lobes of his lungs without hilar lymphadenopathy. What is the grade of lung involvement according to Scadding scoring system?","o":["Grade I","Grade II","Grade III","Grade IV"],"a":2,"e":"According to the Scadding scoring system, the presence of pulmonary infiltrates alone represents grade III.\n\nThe Scadding scoring system is used to grade lung involvement in sarcoidosis. It is based on the chest radiograph findings:\n\n• Grade I: Hilar lymphadenopathy, often with right paratracheal involvement. No pulmonary infiltrates.\n\n• Grade II: Hilar lymphadenopathy + pulmonary infiltrates\n\n• Grade III: Pulmonary infiltrates only\n\n• Grade IV: Fibrosis\n\nSarcoidosis predominantly involves the upper lobes of the lungs.","t":"Sarcoidosis","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following lymph nodes is not involved in the Garland triad?","o":["Right paratracheal nodes","Right hilar nodes","Left hilar nodes","Left paratracheal nodes"],"a":3,"e":"Garland triad does not involve left paratracheal lymph nodes.\n\nGarland triad also known as the 1-2-3 sign or Pawnbrokers sign is a lymph node enlargement pattern in sarcoidosis that involves:\n\n• Right paratracheal nodes (1)\n\n• Right hilar nodes (2)\n\n• Left hilar nodes (3)","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/40bf19de5e8b4ca4b1d69b09a2310ecex720x652.JPEG"],"t":"Sarcoidosis","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is the first-line drug for a patient diagnosed with sarcoidosis with multiple organ involvement?","o":["Prednisolone","Hydroxychloroquine","Methotrexate","Infliximab"],"a":0,"e":"The first-line drug for sarcoidosis with multiple organ involvement is prednisolone.\n\nManagement of sarcoidosis:\n\n• Asymptomatic patients- monitored for disease progression\n\n• Localized/ limited cutaneous and ocular lesions- treated using topical steroids\n\n• Multisystem involvement:\n\n• Systemic steroids\n\n• Other drugs like hydroxychloroquine, methotrexate, and infliximab are considered when the steroids are ineffective or not tolerated","t":"Sarcoidosis","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following conditions is not associated with a significant elevation of angiotensin-converting enzyme levels?","o":["Sarcoidosis","Hyperthyroidism","Diabetes","Miliary TB"],"a":2,"e":"Diabetes is associated with a mild elevation of angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) levels.\n\nSignificant elevation of ACE levels is seen in:\n\n• Sarcoidosis\n\n• Silicosis\n\n• Leprosy\n\n• Gaucher’s disease\n\n• Hyperthyroidism\n\n• Disseminated granulomatous infections such as miliary tuberculosis","t":"Sarcoidosis","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"What is the most common inflammatory myopathy seen in the elderly?","o":["Polymyositis","Dermatomyositis","Inclusion body myositis","Myositis secondary to overlap syndrome"],"a":2,"e":"The most common inflammatory myopathy seen in elderly patients (>50 years) is inclusion body myositis.\n\nInclusion body myositis is a type of inflammatory myopathy. It is characterized by progressive, asymmetric muscle weakness most commonly involving the wrist, finger flexors, and quadriceps. Muscles of the face and neck may also be involved, presenting as dysphagia.\n\nInclusion body myositis is named so because of the presence of inclusion bodies containing amyloid-like protein seen on microscopy.","t":"Dermatomyositis And Related Disorders","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"An IT professional complained of weakness of fingers while using the keyboard. It has been progressing over the past 2 months. It is more severe on his right hand than his left hand. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Polymyositis","Dermatomyositis","Inclusion body myositis","Necrotizing myopathy"],"a":2,"e":"The given history of progressive and predominantly asymmetric muscle weakness is most likely suggestive of inclusion body myositis.\n\nInclusion body myositis is a type of inflammatory myopathy. It is seen in people older than 50 years of age. It is characterized by progressive, asymmetric muscle weakness most commonly involving the wrist, finger flexors, and quadriceps. Muscles of the face and neck might also be involved, leading to dysphagia.\n\nPolymyositis, dermatomyositis, and necrotizing myopathy have predominantly symmetric muscle involvement.","t":"Dermatomyositis And Related Disorders","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"Patients with which of the following myopathies are generally resistant to immunosuppressive therapies?","o":["Dermatomyositis","Polymyositis","Inclusion body myositis","Necrotizing myopathy"],"a":2,"e":"Patients with inclusion body myositis are generally resistant to immunosuppressive therapies.","t":"Dermatomyositis And Related Disorders","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following group of muscles are unlikely to be involved in a patient diagnosed with an inflammatory myopathy?","o":["Ocular muscles","Pharyngeal muscles","Neck flexors","Quadriceps muscles"],"a":0,"e":"Ocular muscles are unlikely to be involved as they are spared in inflammatory myopathy. They are not involved even in advanced, untreated cases.\n\nThe pattern of muscle involvement in various inflammatory myopathies is:\n\n• Proximal muscles are most commonly involved in dermatomyositis and polymyositis, causing difficulty in getting up from a chair, brushing hair, etc.\n\n• Proximal and distal muscles are involved in inclusion body myositis, such as wrist, finger flexors, and quadriceps.\n\n• Pharyngeal and neck-flexor muscles are often involved in all forms of inflammatory myopathy, causing dysphagia or difficulty in holding up the head. Respiratory muscles may also be affected in advanced cases.","t":"Dermatomyositis And Related Disorders","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"Which inflammatory myopathy is most frequently associated with other autoimmune diseases?","o":["Inclusion body myositis","Polymyositis","Dermatomyositis","Necrotizing autoimmune myopathy"],"a":1,"e":"Inflammatory myopathy most frequently associated with other autoimmune diseases is polymyositis.","t":"Dermatomyositis And Related Disorders","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"A physician is suspecting dermatomyositis in a 27-year-old woman with complaints of proximal myalgia and dysphagia. The presence of which of the following signs would be pathognomonic of this condition?","o":["Gottron's papules","V sign","Gottron's sign","Shawl sign"],"a":0,"e":"The presence of Gottron's papules is pathognomonic of dermatomyositis. They are erythematous to violaceous papules that occur symmetrically over the extensor aspects of metacarpophalangeal and interphalangeal joints.\n\nGottron's sign, on the other hand, is erythematous rash over the extensor surfaces of joints such as the knuckles, elbows, knees, and ankles.\n\nV sign is confluent macular erythema over the lower anterior neck and upper anterior chest.\n\nShawl sign is the confluent macular erythema over the upper back and lateral aspect of arms.\n\nAll these signs are present in dermatomyositis, but Gottron's papules and heliotrope rash are pathognomonic.","t":"Dermatomyositis And Related Disorders","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"A 50-year-old woman presented with difficulty in activities like climbing stairs, getting up from a chair, and combing hair. She is a known case of ovarian cancer. The following finding was noted on examination. What is the most probable diagnosis?","o":["Polymyositis","Dermatomyositis","Inclusion body myositis","Discoid lupus erythematosus"],"a":1,"e":"The given image shows upper eyelid lilac erythema or heliotrope rash. The given clinical presentation of proximal myopathy with the characteristic heliotrope rash and history of an internal malignancy point towards a diagnosis of dermatomyositis.\n\nClassic cutaneous signs in dermatomyositis:\n\n• Heliotrope rash/ eyelid erythema- violaceous erythema of the upper eyelids. Pathognomonic of dermatomyositis.\n\n• V sign- confluent macular erythema over the lower anterior neck and upper anterior chest\n\n• Shawl sign- confluent macular erythema over the upper back and lateral aspect of arms\n\n• Gottron’s papules- erythematous papules and plaques over the extensor surface of the metacarpophalangeal and interphalangeal joints. Pathognomonic of dermatomyositis.\n\n• Gottron's sign- erythematous rash over the extensor surfaces of joints such as the knuckles, elbows, knees, and ankles\n\n• Mechanic’s hands- rough, hyperkeratotic lines on the lateral and palmer areas of the fingers\n\n• Scalp erythema and hair loss\n\n• Facial rash- confluent erythema in the malar distribution involving the cheeks, nasal bridge, and nasolabial folds\n\n• Nail fold changes\n\n• Flagellate erythema- linear erythema on the upper back with pruritus\n\n• Poikiloderma- mottled pattern of hyperpigmented and hypopigmented macules with telangiectasias\n\n• Cutaneous calcinosis- accumulation of calcium deposits in the skin\n\nFacial rash in dermatomyositis-\n\nEyelid erythema/ heliotrope rash in dermatomyositis-\n\nV- sign in dermatomyositis-\n\nShawl sign in dermatomyositis-\n\nFlagellate erythema in dermatomyositis-\n\nGottron papules in dermatomyositis-\n\nPoikilodermatous changes in dermatomyositis-","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b553311a4ee6493caa04c6971b642b09.JPEG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cf33f399e1604ae5ab78b868af2157c1x170x388.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2d1c6a2a57d24e0795c05dc55959dd59x720x286.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/77148658d1dc441bbaf18ea3d020ed07x480x366.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d0c00f5be662447db4d75b0a8773fff4x720x540.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b309aa0df983413ea5b0d5bc097eff9ax893x597.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cf547c642bf94a608c07b823667917b8x768x656.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9d9f4497b8b54874b759f9801bbc744ex512x262.PNG"],"t":"Dermatomyositis And Related Disorders","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following would help you to differentiate between dermatomyositis and polymyositis in a patient presenting with muscle weakness?","o":["Elevated creatine kinase","Interstitial lung disease","Erythematous rash","Involvement of proximal muscles"],"a":2,"e":"An erythematous rash helps in differentiating between the two conditions as it is seen only in dermatomyositis.\n\nOption A: Elevated levels of creatine kinase are seen in all the inflammatory myopathies.\n\nOptions B and D: The involvement of proximal muscles more than the distal muscles and interstitial lung disease is seen in both polymyositis and dermatomyositis.","t":"Dermatomyositis And Related Disorders","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"A 50-year-old woman was recently diagnosed with dermatomyositis. She should be screened for which of the following conditions?","o":["Diabetes mellitus","Malignancy","HIV","Hypertension"],"a":1,"e":"This patient should be screened for malignancy as dermatomyositis is often associated with underlying malignancies.\n\nThe most common cancer associated with dermatomyositis is ovarian cancer. Other malignancies that are commonly associated with dermatomyositis are:\n\n• Breast cancer\n\n• Melanoma\n\n• Colon cancer\n\n• Non-Hodgkin’s lymphoma","t":"Dermatomyositis And Related Disorders","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"A 30-year-old woman complained of difficulty reaching high cabinets and carrying grocery bags. It has been progressing over the past 3 months. Which is the initial investigation of choice in this patient?","o":["ESR","Creatine kinase","Electromyogram","hs-CRP"],"a":1,"e":"The given scenario is suggestive of an inflammatory myositis for which the initial investigation of choice is creatine kinase (CK).\n\nCreatine kinase is a marker of muscle breakdown. It is increased in dermatomyositis and polymyositis. However, it may be normal in inclusion body myositis.\n\nThe most specific investigation for inflammatory myositis is a muscle biopsy.","t":"Dermatomyositis And Related Disorders","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"A middle-aged woman presented with muscle weakness and pain over her shoulders. On examination, the following rash and mechanic's hands were noted. Positive titers of which of the following antibodies are associated with her condition?","o":["Anti-Mi-1","Anti-Jo1","Anti-PM/Scl","Anti-SRP"],"a":1,"e":"The given clinical scenario and findings of Gottron papules (image) are suggestive of dermatomyositis. Positive titers of Anti-Jo-1 (antisynthetase) antibodies are associated with interstitial lung disease, arthritis, and mechanic's hands in patients with dermatomyositis.\n\nOther autoantibodies seen in dermatomyositis are:\n\n• Anti-Mi-2 - highly specific to dermatomyositis\n\n• Anti-SRP (anti-signal recognition protein) - associated with severe myositis\n\n• Anti–PM-Scl - associated with overlapping myositis and scleroderma\n\n• Anti-p155/140 or anti-transcription intermediary factor (TIF)-1γ - seen in cancer-associated myositis\n\n• Anti-MDA5 (anti-CADM 140) - associated with rapidly progressive lung disease and cutaneous ulcer","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f25b372f2d8b42ebb17838e7b815de6e.JPEG"],"t":"Dermatomyositis And Related Disorders","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"A patient with dermatomyositis presented with cough, hemoptysis, and weight loss. After a detailed evaluation, she was diagnosed with lung cancer. Which of the following autoantibodies is strongly associated with this condition?","o":["Anti-Mi-2","Anti- Jo1","Anti PM/SCL","Anti 155/140"],"a":3,"e":"Anti-155/140 antibody is strongly associated with dermatomyositis with malignancy.\n\nAdenocarcinomas of the cervix, lung, ovaries, and pancreas account for the majority of cancers associated with inflammatory myopathies.\n\n Antibody\nClinical association\n\nAnti-Jo-1 and other antisynthetase antibodies\n\nPolymyositis\n\nDermatomyositis with interstitial lung disease, arthritis, and mechanic's hands\n\nAnti-Mi-2\nDermatomyositis with rash more than myositis\n\nAnti-MDA5(anti-CADM 140)\nDermatomyositis with rapidly progressive lung disease and cutaneous ulcers\n\nAnti- 155/140 or\n\nAnti-transcription intermediary factor (TIF)-1γ\n\nCancer-associated myositis\n\nAnti-SAE\nCancer-associated dermatomyositis, dermatomyositis with rapidly progressive lung disease","t":"Dermatomyositis And Related Disorders","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"A bodybuilder complains that he is facing difficulty in doing squats and is too weak to climb stairs. He denies any headaches or changes in vision. A quadriceps muscle biopsy reveals perimysial inflammation and perifascicular atrophy. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Inclusion body myositis","Dermatomyositis","Polymyositis","Necrotizing autoimmune myopathy"],"a":1,"e":"The clinical presentation and muscle biopsy finding of perimysial inflammation and perifascicular atrophy makes dermatomyositis the most likely diagnosis.\n\nMuscle biopsy findings in various inflammatory myositis are as follows:\n\nDermatomyositis\nPolymyositis\nInclusion body myositis\n\nPerimysial infiltrates\nEndomysial infiltrates\nEndomysial infiltrates\n\nPerivascular infiltrates and perifascicular atrophy\nPerivascular infiltrates\n Perivascular infiltrates with amyloid inclusion bodies and rimmed vacuoles\n\nCD4+ cellular infiltrates\nCD8+ cellular infiltrates\nCD8+ cellular infiltrates","t":"Dermatomyositis And Related Disorders","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"A housemaid presents to you with generalized skin thickening and numbness of fingers on exposure to cold. She also complains of muscle pain, and difficulty while walking and climbing stairs. Investigations reveal the following. The presence of which of the following antibodies is diagnostic of this condition?\n\nANA - positive\n\nAnti-Scl-70 antibodies - positive\n\nCreatine kinase - elevated\n\nMuscle biopsy - perifascicular atrophy","o":["Anti – Jo 1","Anti PM/Scl","Anti centromere","U3- RNP"],"a":1,"e":"The given clinical scenario having features of both systemic sclerosis and dermatomyositis points towards a diagnosis of overlap syndrome. The presence of anti-PM/Scl antibodies is diagnostic of overlap syndrome.\n\nAnti-PM/Scl is a specific antinuclear antibody directed against a nucleolar-protein complex.\n\nOverlap syndrome also known as mixed connective tissue disease usually involves a combination of three connective tissue diseases:\n\n• Systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE)\n\n• Progressive systemic sclerosis (PSS)\n\n• Polymyositis (PM) or dermatomyositis (DM)","t":"Dermatomyositis And Related Disorders","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"A 35-year-old woman presents with severe generalized body ache, fatigue, sleep disturbances, and low mood for the past 4 months. On examination, increased pain sensitivity and tender bony prominences are noted. Laboratory and radiological investigations are normal. What is the most probable diagnosis?","o":["Polymyositis","Dermatomyositis","Depression","Fibromyalgia"],"a":3,"e":"The most probable diagnosis for the given clinical scenario is fibromyalgia.\n\nFibromyalgia is characterized by chronic widespread musculoskeletal pain and tenderness accompanied by other non-specific somatic symptoms like fatigue and sleep disturbance, for >three months.\n\nIt is more common in females. On examination, they have tenderness in multiple soft tissue anatomical locations. Laboratory findings are normal.\n\nPatients with fibromyalgia may have an increased prevalence of other syndromes associated with pain and fatigue, like chronic headaches, chronic fatigue syndrome, irritable bowel syndrome, interstitial cystitis/ painful bladder syndrome, and pelvic pain syndromes.\n\nOptions A and B: Polymyositis and dermatomyositis have positive laboratory findings such as specific antibodies and radiological findings of multiorgan involvement (ex: interstitial lung disease in polymyositis).\n\nOption C: Although the given clinical scenario closely resembles clinical depression, the examination findings showing tenderness and pain sensitivity favor the diagnosis of fibromyalgia.","t":"Dermatomyositis And Related Disorders","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements is false about fibromyalgia?","o":["More common in females","Aerobic exercise improves symptoms","SPECT studies show hyperperfusion of the thalamus","Opioid analgesics are ineffective in pain management"],"a":2,"e":"Single-photon emission computed tomography (SPECT) studies done on fibromyalgia patients show hypoperfusion of the thalamus.\n\nFibromyalgia is characterized by chronic widespread musculoskeletal pain and tenderness. It is more common in females.\n\nPatients with fibromyalgia have an increased prevalence of other syndromes associated with pain and fatigue, including chronic fatigue syndrome, chronic headaches, irritable bowel syndrome, interstitial cystitis/ painful bladder syndrome, and pelvic pain syndromes.\n\nNon-pharmacological treatment strategies for fibromyalgia include physical conditioning and aerobic exercise. Opioid analgesics should be avoided in fibromyalgia as they are not effective and are associated with opioid-induced hyperalgesia that can worsen both the symptoms and function.","t":"Dermatomyositis And Related Disorders","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"Seronegative spondyloarthritides are associated with which of the following?","o":["HLA-B27","HLA-B16","HLA-B47","HLA-DR4"],"a":0,"e":"Seronegative spondyloarthritides are associated with HLA-B27.\n\nThe spondyloarthritides are a group of overlapping disorders that share certain clinical features and genetic associations (HLA-B27). These disorders include:\n\n• Psoriatic arthritis\n\n• Ankylosing spondylitis\n\n• Reactive arthritis\n\n• Enteropathic arthritis\n\n• Juvenile onset spondyloarthritis\n\n• Undifferentiated spondyloarthritis\n\nUsually, rheumatoid factor (RF) assay is negative in spondyloarthritides. Hence, they are also referred to as seronegative arthritis.","t":"Seronegative Spondyloarthritides","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"A 26-year-old man presented with pain and stiffness in his lower back. His X-ray spine is given below. Which of the following is true about his condition?","o":["Steroids are the first-line therapy","Symptoms improve with activity","It is associated with HLA B47","Associated with low serum IgA levels"],"a":1,"e":"The given clinical features along with the classical bamboo-spine appearance on X-ray point towards a diagnosis of ankylosing spondylitis (AS). In ankylosing spondylitis, the symptoms improve with activity.\n\nAnkylosing spondylitis (AS) is an inflammatory disorder that affects the axial skeleton primarily, and may also involve peripheral joints. It is strongly associated with HLA-B27. It usually presents in the mid-twenties.\n\nIt presents with lower back pain that improves with activity and worsens with rest or inactivity. Early morning stiffness is a characteristic feature. It is associated with elevated serum IgA levels. NSAIDs are the first line of pharmacological therapy.\n\nIt is also associated with extra-articular manifestations like uveitis, interstitial lung disease, aortic regurgitation, etc.\n\nNSAIDs are the first-line treatment for ankylosing spondylitis.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9de73614c8cf4fb4b0688bfc618ccab7.PNG"],"t":"Seronegative Spondyloarthritides","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"What are the key cytokines involved in the pathogenesis of ankylosing spondylitis?","o":["Interferon γ &amp; IL-23","Interferon γ &amp; IL-17","TNF-α &amp; IL-13","TNF-α &amp; IL-17"],"a":3,"e":"The key cytokines involved in the pathogenesis of ankylosing spondylitis are TNF-α and IL-17. IL-23 also plays a role in pathogenesis.","t":"Seronegative Spondyloarthritides","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"A patient with Crohn's disease presents with back pain and stiffness. The physician suspects ankylosing spondylitis and orders an MRI for evaluation. Which of the following joints will be the earliest to be involved?","o":["Costovertebral joint","Lumbosacral joint","Sacroiliac joint","Sacrococcygeal joint"],"a":2,"e":"The earliest joint to be involved in ankylosing spondylitis is the sacroiliac joint.\n\nAnkylosing spondylitis (AS) is an inflammatory disorder that primarily affects the axial skeleton. It is more common in males, usually presenting between 20-30 years of age.\n\nAnkylosing spondylitis (AS) and peripheral arthritis can occur as extra-intestinal manifestations of inflammatory bowel disease (IBD). They are more common in Crohn's disease than in ulcerative colitis.","t":"Seronegative Spondyloarthritides","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"A 28-year-old male patient with low backache and early morning stiffness for the past 1 year is diagnosed with ankylosing spondylitis. Which of the following is not associated with the haplotype related to this condition?","o":["Behcet's disease","Reiter's syndrome","Reactive arthritis","Psoriatic spondylitis"],"a":0,"e":"Ankylosing spondylitis is related to the HLA-B27 haplotype. This haplotype is not associated with Behcet's disease. It is associated with HLA B51.\n\nAnkylosing spondylitis is a chronic inflammatory disease that mainly affects the spine and sacroiliac joint. Individuals who inherit this class I HLA allele (HLA-B27) have a 100–200 fold greater chance (odds ratio or relative risk) of developing the disease compared with those who do not carry HLA-B27. Many autoimmune diseases are associated with different class II HLA alleles.","t":"Seronegative Spondyloarthritides","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"The following test is performed in a patient to evaluate back pain associated with stiffness. Identify the test.","o":["Gaenslen test","Thomas test","Adam's test","Schober's test"],"a":3,"e":"The test performed is modified Schober’s test. This test is done in patients with suspected ankylosing spondylitis.\n\nIt is used to detect reduced flexion of the spine. The test involves marking two points- at the lumbosacral junction and 10 cm above it. The patient is then asked to bend forward with knees fully extended and the distance between the two points is measured.\n\nThis distance normally increases by ≥2 cm. An increase of <2 cm indicates reduced flexion.\n\nIn the image given below the distance increases by 5cm which is normal.\n\nOption A: Gaenslen's test is used to detect primary chronic inflammation of the sacroiliac joint.\n\nOption B: Thomas test is used to detect fixed flexion deformity of the hip.\n\nOption C: Adam's forward bending test is performed on a patient to differentiate structural from non-structural scoliosis.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3327816e95c0486b8e4fe85addc4af21.JPEG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7a2669d7e36d45b8a5219f5344c1f866x1280x1305.JPEG"],"t":"Seronegative Spondyloarthritides","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"Which is the most common extra-articular manifestation seen in patients diagnosed with ankylosing spondylitis?","o":["Anterior uveitis","Cauda equina syndrome","Inflammatory bowel disease","Acne fulminans"],"a":0,"e":"The most common extraarticular manifestation of ankylosing spondylitis (AS) is anterior uveitis.\n\nIt usually presents with unilateral pain, photophobia and increased lacrimation. It is also associated with other spondyloarthritides like reactive arthritis, psoriatic arthritis and enteropathic arthritis.\n\nOther extra-articular manifestations of AS include:\n\n• GIT: ileitis, colitis, inflammatory bowel disease\n\n• Cardiovascular: increased risk of IHD, aortic insufficiency, heart block\n\n• Respiratory: upper lobe fibrosis\n\n• Neurological: cauda equina syndrome\n\n• Skin: acne fulminans, hidradenitis suppurativa","t":"Seronegative Spondyloarthritides","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following conditions predominantly involve the upper lobe of the lung?","o":["Rheumatoid arthritis","Asbestosis","Scleroderma","Ankylosing spondylitis"],"a":3,"e":"Ankylosing spondylitis predominantly involves the upper lobe of the lung leading to pulmonary fibrosis.\n\nRheumatoid arthritis, scleroderma, and asbestosis predominantly involve the lower lobes of the lungs.","t":"Seronegative Spondyloarthritides","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"A patient who presented with chronic lower back pain has been diagnosed with ankylosing spondylitis. What is the first line of pharmacological therapy?","o":["TNF α inhibitors","Opioids","NSAIDS","Steroids"],"a":2,"e":"Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) are the first line of pharmacological therapy for ankylosing spondylitis (AS).\n\nManagement of ankylosing spondylitis includes:\n\n• First-line management:\n\n• Physiotherapy to maintain posture and range of motion.\n\n• NSAIDs- reduce pain and tenderness, increase mobility, and slow down the disease progression.\n\n• TNF α inhibitors- in patients who do not respond to NSAID therapy. It is not used in all patients because of side effects like reactivation of TB, hypersensitivity reactions, pancytopenia, drug-induced psoriasis, etc.\n\n• Anti-IL-17 antibodies- in patients with failed anti-TNF therapy.\n\n• Surgery- improves mobility in patients with severe arthritis and joint stiffness.","t":"Seronegative Spondyloarthritides","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"A 27-year-old man with ankylosing spondylitis is not responding to standard therapy. His physician decides to prescribe him an anti-IL-17 monoclonal antibody. Which of the following drugs would he prescribe?","o":["Ustekinumab","Secukinumab","Inflximab","Adalimumab"],"a":1,"e":"Secukinumab is an anti-IL-17 monoclonal antibody used in patients with ankylosing spondylitis who are not responding to NSAIDs and anti-TNF therapy.\n\nManagement of ankylosing spondylitis includes:\n\n• First-line management:\n\n• Physiotherapy to maintain posture and range of motion.\n\n• NSAIDs- reduce pain and tenderness, increase mobility, and slow down the disease progression.\n\n• TNF α inhibitors- in patients who do not respond to NSAID therapy. It is not used in all patients because of side effects like reactivation of TB, hypersensitivity reactions, pancytopenia, drug-induced psoriasis, etc.\n\n• Anti-IL-17 antibodies- in patients with failed anti-TNF therapy.\n\n• Surgery- improves mobility in patients with severe arthritis and joint stiffness.\n\nOption A: Ustekinumab is an anti-IL-12/23 monoclonal antibody used in the treatment of psoriasis.\n\nOptions C and D: Infliximab and adalimumab are anti-TNF-α monoclonal antibodies used in the treatment of ankylosing spondylitis.","t":"Seronegative Spondyloarthritides","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"A 15-year-old boy presents with generalized malaise and painful swelling of his right knee joint since 10 days. He also complains of pain and discomfort during urination. He was treated for diarrhea last month. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Reactive arthritis","Enteropathic arthritis","Hemolytic uremic syndrome","Pyogenic arthritis"],"a":0,"e":"The given scenario describes an acute arthritis following an infection which is also associated with urethritis and generalized malaise. These features are suggestive of reactive arthritis.\n\nReactive arthritis also known as Reiter's syndrome is a seronegative spondyloarthritis that occurs as a consequence of a recent enteric or urogenital infection. It is associated with HLA B27. It is also associated with extra-articular manifestations such as:\n\n• Urogenital- urethritis, prostatitis, cervicitis\n\n• Ocular- conjuctivitis, anterior uveitis\n\n• Mucocutaneous- oral ulcers, keratoderma blennorrhagica, circinate balanitis\n\nIn some patients it presents as a triad of:\n\n• Arthritis\n\n• Urethritis\n\n• Conjunctivitis","t":"Seronegative Spondyloarthritides","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"A patient presents with painful knee swelling, dysuria, and conjunctivitis. Which of the following infectious agents is least likely to cause this?","o":["Shigella dysenteriae","Neisseria gonorrhoea","Campylobacter jejuni","Chlamydia trachomatis"],"a":1,"e":"The given scenario of arthritis, urethritis, and conjunctivitis is suggestive of reactive arthritis. It is not caused by Neisseria gonorrhea.\n\nDisseminated gonococcal infection causes gonococcal arthritis. It comprises localized septic arthritis and arthritis-dermatitis syndrome.\n\nReactive arthritis is usually caused due to LPS (lipopolysaccharide) component of the gram-negative organisms or cross-reactivity with synovial antigens. The common causative organisms include:\n\n• Chlamydia trachomatis (most common)\n\n• Salmonella\n\n• Shigella\n\n• Yersinia\n\n• Campylobacter","t":"Seronegative Spondyloarthritides","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"A middle-aged man presented with pain and swelling of his right knee for one week. It started progressing to the ankle joints and feet for the past 2 days. He gives a history of diarrhea and burning micturition 4 weeks ago. On examination, his soles showed the following lesions. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Syphilitic arthritis","Enteropathic arthritis","Psoriatic arthritis","Reactive arthritis"],"a":3,"e":"The given scenario of acute arthritis following a recent infection and additive pattern (involvement of new joints within few days) and examination finding of keratoderma blennorrhagica (image) are suggestive of reactive arthritis.\n\nKeratoderma blennorrhagica is a mucocutaneous manifestation of reactive arthritis. It presents as vesicles and pustules that lead to hyperkeratotic lesions or crusting on the palms and soles.\n\nOther mucocutaneous manifestations of reactive arthritis include:\n\n• Oral ulcers - superficial, transient, and often asymptomatic\n\n• Circinate balanitis - consists of vesicles that rupture to form painless superficial erosions on the glans penis","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d76ecdc80eab4a1690982f5a4da0438b.JPEG"],"t":"Seronegative Spondyloarthritides","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"An elderly man presents with pain and deformity of his toes. The clinical findings are shown in the image below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Rheumatoid arthritis","Osteo arthritis","Psoriatic arthritis","Septic arthritis"],"a":2,"e":"The given clinical image showing dactylitis, nail changes and telescoping of toes is suggestive of psoriatic arthritis.\n\nClinical features of psoriatic arthritis include:\n\n• Psoriatic nail changes- yellowing and hypertrophy\n\n• Dactylitis- inflammation of the toes\n\n• Telescoping (shortening) of toes due to osteolysis\n\n• Severe deformity- suggestive of arthritis mutilans\n\n• Enthesitis- heel pain at the sites of ligament or tendon attachment\n\nPsoriatic arthritis of the hands commonly involves proximal and distal interphalangeal joints (PIP, DIP) and metacarpophalangeal joints (MCP). The image below shows psoriatic arthritis of the hands:","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/165e4c1b3d214f1e8c9904d3e59ab6a1.JPEG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/aacc4dca2e204e7c9a018ccd427f544ax720x1280.JPEG"],"t":"Seronegative Spondyloarthritides","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"A 16-year-old girl is being evaluated for arthritis and uveitis. Which of the following would you not consider as a differential diagnosis?","o":["Psoriatic arthritis","Enteropathic arthritis","Juvenile onset spondyloarthritis","Reactive Arthritis"],"a":2,"e":"Juvenile onset spondyloarthritis (JSpA) would not be considered as a differential diagnosis as it has no associated extra-articular manifestations like uveitis, lung involvement, etc.\n\nIt is a seronegative spondyloarthritis with onset between 7-16 years of age. It usually presents as asymmetric arthritis predominantly involving the lower extremities.","t":"Seronegative Spondyloarthritides","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not a part of the SAPHO syndrome?","o":["Synovitis","Acne","Psoriasis","Hyperostosis"],"a":2,"e":"Psoriasis is not a part of SAPHO syndrome.\n\nThe SAPHO syndrome is characterized by:\n\n• Synovitis- arthritis\n\n• Acne- acne conglobata\n\n• Pustulosis- palmoplantar pustulosis, hidradenitis suppurativa\n\n• Hyperostosis- sternoclavicular and spinal hyperostosis\n\n• Osteitis- sterile osteomyelitis","t":"Seronegative Spondyloarthritides","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"Uveitis is an uncommon manifestation in patients with which of the following conditions?","o":["Ankylosing spondylitis","Psoriatic arthritis","Reiter's syndrome","Rheumatoid arthritis"],"a":3,"e":"Uveitis is a rare manifestation of rheumatoid arthritis (RA). Ocular manifestation in RA includes scleritis, episcleritis, and keratoconjunctivitis.\n\nUveitis is a common ocular manifestation of seronegative arthritis e.g. Reiter's syndrome, ankylosing spondylitis, psoriatic arthritis, and juvenile rheumatoid arthritis (JRA).","t":"Seronegative Spondyloarthritides","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"What type of hypersensitivity reaction is seen in systemic lupus erythematosus?","o":["Type I","Type II","Type III","Type IV"],"a":2,"e":"Type III hypersensitivity reaction (immune complex deposition) is seen in systemic lupus erythematosus.\n\nSystemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) is an autoimmune condition in which autoantibodies are directed against a part of the nucleus such as double-stranded DNA, histone, ribonuclear proteins, etc. The presence of these autoantibodies leads to the deposition of immune complexes and activation of the complement system.","t":"Sle And Aps","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"Patients with which of the following complement deficiencies are at an increased risk of developing systemic lupus erythematosus?","o":["C1q","C5","C3","C1 inhibitor"],"a":0,"e":"C1q complement deficiency is a risk factor for the development of systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE).\n\nRisk factors for SLE are:\n\n• Genetic\n\n• Genetic defects resulting in C1q, C2, C4 complement deficiencies\n\n• Presence of HLA DR3\n\n• Mutation in the TREX1 gene\n\n• Environmental\n\n• Epstein-Barr virus (EBV) infection\n\n• Oral contraceptive pills/ hormone replacement therapy\n\n• Smoking\n\n• Prolonged exposure to silica","t":"Sle And Aps","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"A 25-year-old marathon runner presents with complaints of joint pain and rashes over the face. She says the rashes appear on exposure to the sun. On physical examination, a butterfly rash is seen over her face sparing the nasolabial folds. Which of the following antibodies will you evaluate to screen this patient?","o":["Anti-dsDNA antibodies","Anti-Sm antibodies","Antinuclear antibodies","Anti-histone antibodies"],"a":2,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE). The best screening test in this condition is to evaluate for the presence of antinuclear antibodies (ANAs).","t":"Sle And Aps","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"A school teacher presented with painful oral ulcers, photosensitive rashes, and arthralgia. Presence of which of the following antibodies would be diagnostic of her condition?","o":["Anti Ro/SS-A antibodies","Anti-Sm antibodies","Antinuclear antibodies","Anti-dsDNA antibodies"],"a":1,"e":"The given clinical scenario of oral ulcers, photosensitive rash, and arthralgia is suggestive of systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE). Anti-Sm antibodies are most specific for SLE and hence their presence is diagnostic of this condition.","t":"Sle And Aps","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"An SLE patient is found to have a butterfly rash. Which of the following is an incorrect statement regarding this finding?","o":["Most common acute rash in SLE","Spares the nasolabial folds","Increased risk of squamous cell carcinoma","Associated with scaling"],"a":2,"e":"Butterfly rash or malar rash in a patient with SLE is not associated with an increased risk of squamous cell carcinoma.\n\nIt is the most common type of acute skin rash seen in SLE. It is a photosensitive, erythematous, and scaly rash that is prominent over the cheeks and the nose. It spares the nasolabial folds.\n\nCommon causes of malar rash are as follows:\n\n• Erysipelas\n\n• Cellulitis\n\n• Systemic lupus erythematosus\n\n• Rosacea\n\n• Pellagra\n\n• Dermatomyositis","t":"Sle And Aps","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"A 40-year-old man with history of SLE presented to your OPD with shortness of breath. It is associated with chest pain that worsens on inspiration. On auscultation, you notice a pleural friction rub. Investigations reveal the following. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nPFT - restrictive pattern, reduced lung volumes\n\nDLCO - reduced\n\nCT chest - elevated hemidiaphragm","o":["Lupus pneumonitis","Interstitial fibrosis","Shrinking lung syndrome","Intra-alveolar hemorrhage"],"a":2,"e":"The given clinical scenario along with elevated hemidiaphragm and no evidence of ILD on chest CT is suggestive of shrinking lung syndrome (SLS), which is a rare complication of systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE).\n\nIt is characterized by dyspnea and episodes of pleuritic chest pain. These symptoms occur in the absence of pleural or parenchymal involvement.\n\nPFT shows decreased lung volume with a restrictive pattern, and DLCO is often reduced. Radiological investigations show an elevated hemidiaphragm with no evidence of ILD.\n\nThe following X-ray shows shrinking lung syndrome.\n\nPulmonary manifestations seen in SLE are:\n\n• Pleuritis with or without pleural effusion- most common pulmonary manifestation.\n\n• Lupus pneumonitis - It is the inflammation of the lung tissue (pneumonitis) due to SLE (lupus).\n\n• Interstitial lung fibrosis\n\n• Shrinking lung syndrome\n\n• Intra-alveolar hemorrhage","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3d626efc66bc46af80f1c32000255b15x512x512.PNG"],"t":"Sle And Aps","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"What is the most common neurological manifestation seen in patients with systemic lupus erythematosus?","o":["Seizures","Depression","Cognitive impairment","Polyneuropathy"],"a":2,"e":"The most common neurological manifestation seen in patients with systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) is cognitive impairment.\n\nIt can manifest as impaired memory and reasoning. Other common neurological manifestations in SLE are psychosis, depression, and seizures.\n\nThe most common manifestations of different systems involved in SLE are:\n\n• Musculoskeletal- arthritis and arthralgia\n\n• Skin- photosensitivity\n\n• Kidneys- proteinuria\n\n• Neurologic- cognitive impairment\n\n• Cardiac- pericarditis\n\n• Pulmonary- pleuritis\n\n• Hematologic- anemia","t":"Sle And Aps","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"A young girl is being evaluated for low mood, anhedonia, weight loss, and insomnia since past 1 month. On examination, multiple hesitation marks were seen on her left forearm. She was diagnosed with systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) one year back. Which of the following autoantibodies is associated with this presentation?","o":["Anti-ribosomal P antibodies","Anti-Ro/SSA antibodies","Anti-neuronal antibodies","Anti-histone antibodies"],"a":0,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of depression in a patient with SLE. It is associated with anti-ribosomal P antibodies.\n\nAntiribosomal P antibody is associated with psychosis or depression in SLE.","t":"Sle And Aps","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"What is the most common cardiac manifestation seen in patients with systemic lupus erythematosus?","o":["Pericarditis","Myocarditis","Libman Sack endocarditis","Myocardial infarction"],"a":0,"e":"Pericarditis is the most common cardiac manifestation seen in patients with systemic lupus erythematosus.\n\nOther cardiac manifestations include:\n\n• Myocarditis\n\n• Libman-Sacks endocarditis.\n\nPatients with SLE are at an increased risk for myocardial infarction, usually due to accelerated atherosclerosis.\n\nThe most common manifestations of different systems involved in SLE are:\n\n• Musculoskeletal- arthritis and arthralgia\n\n• Skin- photosensitivity\n\n• Kidneys- proteinuria\n\n• Neurologic- cognitive impairment\n\n• Cardiac- pericarditis\n\n• Pulmonary- pleuritis\n\n• Hematologic- anemia","t":"Sle And Aps","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is a feature of arthritis in SLE?","o":["Non-deforming arthritis","Predilection for large joints","Asymmetric involvement","Non-erosive arthritis"],"a":3,"e":"Non-erosive arthritis is a common feature of arthritis in SLE.\n\nFeatures of arthritis in SLE are:\n\n• Symmetrical and polyarticular arthritis\n\n• A predilection for the small joints of the hand (the proximal interphalangeal and carpal joints)\n\n• Non-erosive and deforming arthritis (reducible)\n\nIn contrast to rheumatoid arthritis which is characterized by erosive, deforming joint involvement, SLE has non-erosive and deforming arthritis (reduciable).","t":"Sle And Aps","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"A young woman who is a known case of SLE develops proteinuria. The doctor suspects lupus nephritis and performs a renal biopsy. It shows the presence of wire loop appearance without glomerular proliferation. In which class of lupus nephritis will you categorize this patient's biopsy findings?","o":["Mesangial proliferative lupus nephritis","Focal lupus nephritis","Diffuse lupus nephritis","Membranous lupus nephritis"],"a":2,"e":"Wire loop appearance without glomerular proliferation is seen in diffuse lupus nephritis (Class IV).\n\nThe capillaries are thickened due to the subendothelial deposits and appear as wire loops. It is the most common and most severe form of lupus nephritis.\n\nRenal involvement is common in patients with SLE. The majority of the patients will present with\n\n• Proteinuria, nephrotic syndrome\n\n• Microscopic hematuria, hypertension\n\n• Urinary casts\n\n• Abnormal renal function tests- elevated serum creatinine\n\n• Low C3 and C4 levels","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/61f60a4aeb574c178459f81e107d7445x1279x911.JPEG"],"t":"Sle And Aps","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"A 40 year old female presented to the OPD for her annual routine check-up. she is a patient of SLE diagnosed a decade ago, among the given options what is the most common cause of death in such patients?","o":["Cardiovascular disease","Renal failure","Infections","Disseminated intravascular coagulation"],"a":0,"e":"The most common cause of death in patients with SLE after 10 years of disease is accelerated atherosclerosis.\n\nThe leading causes of death in the first 10 years of the disease are infections and renal failure; subsequently, Cardiovascular events become increasingly frequent causes of mortality.","t":"Sle And Aps","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following drugs is recommended for all patients with SLE?","o":["Hydroxychloroquine","NSAIDs","Methotrexate","Systemic steroids"],"a":0,"e":"Hydroxychloroquine is recommended for all patients with SLE. It decreases the symptoms, reduces flares and organ damage.\n\nImportant drugs used in the management of SLE are:\n\n• NSAIDs- used for a shorter period to reduce pain and fever. They are not used in all patients because of the increased risk of atherosclerosis and renal injury.\n\n• Hydroxychloroquine or chloroquine- should be used in all patients with SLE\n\n• Glucocorticoids\n\n• topical glucocorticoids- in mild skin lesions\n\n• systemic steroids- in severe skin lesions and organ involvements.\n\n• Immunosuppressive agents- used in combination with glucocorticoids in severe skin lesions and organ involvement. They help to reduce the dose and toxicity of glucocorticoids. Important agents are:\n\n• Methotrexate\n\n• Azathioprine\n\n• Mycophenolate mofetil and cyclophosphamide- immunosuppressive agents of choice in renal involvement.\n\n• Biologic drugs- like belimumab and rituximab are used when there is no response to conventional drugs.","t":"Sle And Aps","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"A patient with a history of SLE presented with hematuria and proteinuria. Investigations revealed elevated creatinine and low C3, C4 levels. He was unresponsive to steroid therapy and his physician prescribed him belimumab for further management. What is the mechanism of action of this drug?","o":["TNF inhibitor","IL-1 antagonist","Anti CD-20 antibody","BAFF inhibitor"],"a":3,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of lupus nephritis. Belimumab is a BAFF inhibitor used in patients with lupus nephritis who do not show response to initial therapy with steroids.\n\nBelimumab is a human monoclonal antibody that inhibits the soluble form of a B cell survival factor (known as BLyS or BAFF) which promotes the formation of memory B cells and autoantibodies. Belimumab inhibits this factor and therefore is used in the management of SLE.\n\nIt is used in combination with either cyclophosphamide or MMF in patients with lupus nephritis who do not show response to initial therapy with steroids.\n\nCD20 is an antigen present on the B cells. Rituximab is an anti-CD20 monoclonal antibody that is used in patients who are resistant to the standard therapies.","t":"Sle And Aps","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"A 60-year-old man presented with fever, malaise, skin rashes, and arthralgia. On further evaluation, his serology was positive for ANA and anti-histone antibodies. Use of which of the following drugs could have led to this condition?","o":["Rifampin","Bupivacaine","Warfarin","Hydralazine"],"a":3,"e":"The given clinical scenario with positive ANA and anti-histone antibodies point towards a diagnosis of drug-induced lupus erythematosus. Hydralazine which is a vasodilator is associated with the development of drug-induced lupus erythematosus.\n\nDrug-induced lupus erythematosus is an autoimmune condition caused due to exposure to certain medications. It completely resolves after the causative drug is discontinued. Important drugs associated with this condition are:\n\n• Sulfonamides\n\n• Hydralazine\n\n• Isoniazid\n\n• Infliximab\n\n• Procainamide\n\n• Propylthiouracil\n\n• ACE inhibitors\n\n• Thiazides\n\n• TNF-alpha antagonists","t":"Sle And Aps","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"What is the ratio of occurrence of drug-induced lupus erythematosus in females and males?","o":["9:1","3:1","2:1","1:1"],"a":3,"e":"The female to male ratio in drug-induced lupus erythematosus (DILE) is 1:1.\n\nThe female to male ratio in systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) is 9:1.\n\nSystemic lupus erythematosus (SLE)\nDrug-induced lupus erythematosus (DILE)\n\nAge\nCommon in young individuals\nCommon in elderly (50-70 years)\n\nFemale: male ratio\n9:1\n1:1\n\nOrgans involved\nCan affect many organs\nSkin is mainly involved. Renal and brain involvement are rare\n\nComplement levels\nReduced C3, C4 levels\nNormal C3, C4 levels\n\nAntibodies\n\n-Antinuclear\nCommon\nCommon\n\n-Antihistone\nRare\nCommon\n\n-Anti ds-DNA\nCommon\nRare","t":"Sle And Aps","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements is true regarding drug-induced lupus erythematosus?","o":["Renal involvement is rare","Commonly associated with anti ds-DNA","Common in females","Reduced C3 and C4 levels"],"a":0,"e":"Renal involvement is rare in drug-induced lupus erythematosus.\n\nSystemic lupus erythematosus (SLE)\nDrug-induced lupus erythematosus (DILE)\n\nAge\nCommon in young individuals\nCommon in elderly (50-70 years)\n\nFemale: male ratio\n9:1\n1:1\n\nOrgans involved\nCan affect many organs\nSkin is mainly involved. Renal and brain involvement are rare\n\nComplement levels\nReduced C3, C4 levels\nNormal C3, C4 levels\n\nAntibodies\n\n-Antinuclear\nCommon\nCommon\n\n-Antihistone\nRare\nCommon\n\n-Anti ds-DNA\nCommon\nRare","t":"Sle And Aps","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"Antiphospholipid syndrome is associated with all of the following except:","o":["Recurrent fetal loss","Thrombocytosis","Arterial thrombosis","Libman-Sacks endocarditis"],"a":1,"e":"Thrombocytosis is not associated with antiphospholipid syndrome (APS). On the contrary, thrombocytopenia is seen.\n\nAPS is an autoantibody-mediated condition that is characterized by arterial thrombosis, venous thrombosis, and recurrent fetal loss. These autoantibodies bind to the phospholipids present on the endothelial cells and activate coagulation and thrombus formation.\n\nImportant manifestations of venous thrombosis are:\n\n• Deep vein thrombosis of legs\n\n• Pulmonary embolism\n\n• Livedo reticularis\n\n• Cerebral venous thrombosis\n\nImportant manifestations of arterial thrombosis are:\n\n• Stroke, transient ischemic attacks\n\n• Retinal artery thrombosis\n\n• Myocardial ischemia\n\n• Libman-Sacks endocarditis- due to the vegetations formed by platelet fibrin microthrombi\n\n• Leg ulcers, gangrene of the digits\n\nImportant obstetric manifestations are:\n\n• Preeclampsia\n\n• Recurrent fetal loss\n\n• Premature birth\n\nImportant hematological abnormalities are:\n\n• Thrombocytopenia- occurs due to increased platelet aggregation and thrombus formation\n\n• Coombs positive hemolytic anemia- seen when autoantibodies attack red blood cells\n\n• Thrombotic microangiopathic syndromes- thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (TTP) and hemolytic uremic syndrome (HUS)","t":"Sle And Aps","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"A 34-year-old woman comes for evaluation of recurrent fetal losses. She has had 4 spontaneous abortions, all of them at &lt;10 weeks of gestational age, and has a history of deep vein thrombosis. Which of the following antibodies is not useful in making a diagnosis in this condition?","o":["Lupus anticoagulant","Anti-cardiolipin","Anti-beta-2 glycoprotein","Anti-beta-2 microglobulin"],"a":3,"e":"The given clinical history of >3 spontaneous abortions at <10 weeks of gestational age along with a history of deep vein thrombosis suggests a diagnosis of antiphospholipid syndrome (APS). Anti beta-2 microglobulin is not useful in diagnosing this condition.\n\nFor the diagnosis of antiphospholipid syndrome, high titers of one or more of the following three antibodies should be demonstrated along with at least one clinical symptom of thrombosis or pregnancy complications:\n\n• Lupus anticoagulant (LA) - most specific\n\n• Anticardiolipin (aCL) antibodies - most sensitive\n\n• Anti-beta-2 glycoprotein antibodies","t":"Sle And Aps","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"A 40-year-old man presented with painful swelling of his right calf muscle. The clinical test shown below was positive. Investigations revealed elevated D-dimer, high titres of lupus anticoagulant and anti-cardiolipin antibodies. You would use all of the following drugs for his management except:","o":["Warfarin","Glucocorticoids","Aspirin","Hydroxychloroquine"],"a":1,"e":"The given findings of positive Moses' sign with elevated D-dimer, lupus anticoagulant, and anti-cardiolipin antibodies are suggestive of antiphospholipid syndrome (APS). Glucocorticoids are not useful in the general management of APS, they are used only in the treatment of catastrophic APS.\n\nSqueezing of the relaxed calf muscles from side to side is painful as the thrombosed deep veins in the calf are always tender. This test is known as Moses’ sign.\n\nThe clinical manifestations in APS occur due to the activation of coagulation and thrombus formation. Therefore, anticoagulants and antiplatelet agents are the mainstays in the treatment. Important drugs used are:\n\n• Warfarin- is an anticoagulant that inhibits vitamin K dependant epoxidase.\n\n• Low molecular weight heparins (LMWH)- warfarin cannot be used in pregnancy due to its teratogenicity. In these situations, LMWH is used for anticoagulation.\n\n• Aspirin- is an antiplatelet agent.\n\n• Hydroxychloroquine- is an immunomodulatory agent that can be used to prevent recurrent thrombosis.\n\n• Statins\n\nNote: The only condition where corticosteroids are beneficial is catastrophic APS. It is a life-threatening rapidly progressive thromboembolic disease involving simultaneously three or more organs. It is seen in very few patients.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4bea1ea5a4424743b5a89390f5eabefa.GIF"],"t":"Sle And Aps","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is associated with rheumatoid arthritis?","o":["HLA B27","HLA DR4","HLA DR3","HLA DR5"],"a":1,"e":"Rheumatoid arthritis is associated with HLA DR4.","t":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"Which part of the spine is most commonly affected in patients with rheumatoid arthritis?","o":["C1 - C2","C6 - C7","L4 - L5","T1 - T2"],"a":0,"e":"The atlantoaxial joint (C1 & C2) is the most commonly affected part of the spine in patients with rheumatoid arthritis.\n\nThe joints in rheumatoid arthritis are bilaterally and symmetrically involved. The commonly affected joints are:\n\n• Metacarpophalangeal (MCP) joints\n\n• Wrists\n\n• Proximal interphalangeal (PIP) joints\n\n• Metatarsophalangeal joints (MTP) joints\n\n• Atlantoaxial joint\n\nThere is relative sparing of the DIP joint in rheumatoid arthritis. The involvement of DIP joints should direct suspicion towards osteoarthritis.","t":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"Identify the following deformity seen in a patient complaining of pain and swelling of her hands.","o":["Swan neck deformity","Boutonnière deformity","Bouchard's nodes","Hitchhiker's deformity"],"a":1,"e":"The image is showing flexion of the PIP joint with hyperextension of the DIP joint is suggestive of Boutonnière deformity seen in patients with rheumatoid arthritis.\n\nHeberden’s and Bouchard’s nodes are nodules in osteoarthritis that are palpable in the distal interphalangeal joint and proximal interphalangeal joints respectively.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d41db27579814e5c96499ea3664417d9.JPEG"],"t":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"A patient presents to your clinic with the following deformity. In which of the following conditions is this deformity seen?","o":["Osteoarthritis","Gout","Rheumatoid arthritis","Psoriatic arthritis"],"a":2,"e":"The given image shows a swan-neck deformity seen in a patient with established rheumatoid arthritis(RA). Swan-neck deformity is hyperextension at the proximal interphalangeal (PIP) joint and flexion at the distal interphalangeal (DIP) joint.\n\nSome of the other small joint deformities of the hand in a patient with RA include:\n\nBoutonnière deformity with flexion at the PIP joint and hyperextension at the DIP joint.\n\nZ-line deformity with hyperextension at the DIP joint and subluxation of the 1st metacarpophalangeal (MCP) joint.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d11071926ea94aabbd90a7b98c53a229.JPEG"],"t":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"Which is the most common cardiac valvular lesion seen in patients with rheumatoid arthritis?","o":["Mitral regurgitation","Aortic stenosis","Aortic regurgitation","Mitral stenosis"],"a":0,"e":"The most common cardiac valvular lesion seen in patients with rheumatoid arthritis is mitral regurgitation.","t":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following would you not expect to see in a patient diagnosed with Felty syndrome?","o":["Splenomegaly","Neutropenia","Hepatomegaly","Rheumatoid arthritis"],"a":2,"e":"Hepatomegaly is not seen in Felty syndrome.\n\nFelty syndrome is defined by the clinical triad of:\n\n• Neutropenia\n\n• Splenomegaly\n\n• Rheumatoid arthritis\n\nIt occurs in the late stages of rheumatoid arthritis (RA).\n\nOther syndromes associated with rheumatoid arthritis are:\n\n• Caplan’s syndrome- characterized by both rheumatoid arthritis and pneumoconiosis. It is associated with development of multiple peripheral basilar nodules in the lungs.\n\n• Secondary Sjogren’s syndrome- development of keratoconjunctivitis sicca or xerostomia in association with connective tissue disorder.","t":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"Which is the most common type of lymphoma seen in patients with rheumatoid arthritis?","o":["Mantle cell lymphoma","Hodgkin's lymphoma","Diffuse large B cell lymphoma","Marginal zone lymphoma"],"a":2,"e":"The most common histopathologic type of lymphoma seen in patients with rheumatoid arthritis is diffuse large B cell lymphoma (DLBCL).\n\nThe risk of developing lymphoma increases if the patient has Felty’s syndrome or high levels of disease activity.","t":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"A patient complains about difficulty doing chores due to morning stiffness of the joints. Investigations show elevated ESR, CRP, and positive Rheumatoid factor. Which of the following is not likely to be seen in this condition?","o":["Hyperandrogenism","Hip fractures","Keratoconjunctivitis sicca","Congestive heart failure"],"a":0,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of rheumatoid arthritis, which is associated with hypoandrogenism.\n\nIt is characterized by low levels of serum testosterone, LH, and DHEA.\n\nThe conditions associated with rheumatoid arthritis are:\n\n• Cardiovascular disease, congestive heart failure\n\n• Osteoporosis - hip fractures are more likely to occur\n\n• Ocular - keratoconjunctivitis sicca, episcleritis, scleritis, keratitis\n\n• Secondary Sjogren's syndrome\n\n• Hypoandrogenism in men and postmenopausal women","t":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following radiological features would help to differentiate between rheumatoid arthritis and SLE?","o":["Juxta-articular osteoporosis","Ulnar deviation","Articular erosions","Swelling of PIP joint"],"a":2,"e":"Articular erosions on X-ray is seen in rheumatoid arthritis (RA), but not in SLE.\n\nJuxta-articular osteoporosis, swelling of PIP joint, and ulnar deviation can be seen in both SLE and RA.\n\nJaccoud arthropathy is a deforming non-erosive arthropathy characterized by the ulnar deviation of the second to 5th fingers with metacarpophalangeal joint subluxation seen in SLE.\n\nThe give X-ray shows Jaccoud's arthropathy:\n\nThe given image shows a patient with Jaccoud's arthropathy of the hands:","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9af726f2c71f4f47847780615b61c65dx462x600.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4a4967d74a494db59838dd2e542b4890x986x732.JPEG"],"t":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"What is the most common cause of death in patients with rheumatoid arthritis?","o":["Interstitial lung disease","Coronary artery disease","Atlantoaxial subluxation","Gastrointestinal bleeding"],"a":1,"e":"The most common cause of death in patients with rheumatoid arthritis is cardiovascular disease.","t":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"In a suspected case of rheumatoid arthritis, which of the following markers should be used to diagnose this condition?","o":["Anti-nuclear antibodies","Rheumatoid factor","Anti- CCP antibody","Anti dsDNA antibody"],"a":2,"e":"The most appropriate marker to diagnose rheumatoid arthritis (RA) is the anti-CCP (cyclic citrullinated peptide) antibody as it is the most specific marker for RA.\n\nAnti-CCP, ESR, and CRP values correlate with the disease activity.\n\nRheumatoid factor can occur in other autoimmune diseases as well as in chronic infections. Therefore it is not considered specific.","t":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is the drug of choice for the treatment of a patient with rheumatoid arthritis?","o":["Hydroxychloroquine","Sulfasalazine","Methotrexate","Infliximab"],"a":2,"e":"Methotrexate which is a DMARD is the drug of choice for the treatment of rheumatoid arthritis (RA).\n\nA combination regimen is used when there is a failure to achieve adequate improvement with methotrexate only.\n\nMethotrexate in low doses causes adenosine release and suppresses joint inflammation. However, in high doses, it causes inhibition of dihydrofolate reductase (DHFR).","t":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is a Janus kinase inhibitor used in patients with rheumatoid arthritis?","o":["Anakinra","Tocilizumab","Abatacept","Tofacitinib"],"a":3,"e":"The Janus kinase inhibitor used in the treatment of rheumatoid arthritis is tofacitinib.\n\nTofacitinib, baricitinib, and upadacitinib are Janus kinase inhibitors used as monotherapy or combination therapy in rheumatoid arthritis.\n\nOption A: Anakinra is an IL-1 antagonist.\n\nOption B: Tocilizumab is an IL-6 antagonist.\n\nOption C: Abatacept acts by inhibiting co-stimulation of T cells.","t":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"A 28-year-old pregnant lady visits your OPD with complaints of painful swelling of her hands since past 2 months. Investigations reveal positive rheumatoid factor and anti-CCP antibodies. Which of the following DMARDs should not be prescribed to her?","o":["Rituximab","Leflunomide","Hydroxychloroquine","Sulfasalazine"],"a":1,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of rheumatoid arthritis (RA). Leflunomide should not be prescribed in pregnancy as it is a category X drug.\n\nDMARDs classified as Category X in pregnancy (contraindicated in pregnancy) are:\n\n• Methotrexate\n\n• Leflunomide","t":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"What is the isotype of the rheumatoid factor most commonly measured in the laboratory?","o":["Ig M","Ig G","Ig A","Ig D"],"a":0,"e":"IgM isotype is the rheumatoid factor (RF) most commonly measured in laboratories.\n\nIgM RF has been found in 75–80% of patients with rheumatoid arthritis. IgG and IgA isotypes of RF have also been identified.\n\nHowever, RF is not specific for rheumatoid arthritis and can also be found in the following conditions:\n\n• Primary Sjögren’s syndrome\n\n• Systemic lupus erythematosus\n\n• Subacute bacterial endocarditis\n\n• Hepatitis B and C","t":"Rheumatoid Arthritis","c":"Rheumatology"},
 {"q":"Which among the following about idiosyncratic asthma is false?","o":["Presents in childhood","Normal IgE levels","Commonly have concomitant nasal polyps","Negative skin test for common inhalant allergens"],"a":0,"e":"Idiosyncratic or intrinsic asthma does not present in childhood. It typically shows a later onset of disease (adult-onset asthma).","t":"Asthma & Copd","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"A 36-year-old woman presented with wheezing after taking aspirin for a headache. Which of the following is true regarding this condition?","o":["Raised serum IgE levels","Positive skin test for inhaled allergens","Associated with nasal polyps","A type of extrinsic asthma"],"a":2,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of aspirin-sensitivity asthma. It is closely associated with nasal polyps.\n\nAspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease (AERD), also known as Samter’s triad or aspirin-sensitive asthma. It is a chronic medical condition that consists of:\n\n• Intrinsic asthma\n\n• Recurrent sinus disease with nasal polyps\n\n• Sensitivity to aspirin and other NSAIDs\n\nIt has normal serum IgE levels and a negative skin test for inhaled allergens.","t":"Asthma & Copd","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"What is the best way to diagnose patients with suspected bronchial asthma?","o":["Chest X-ray","Skin test for inhaled allergens","FEV1 values","Demonstration of reversible obstruction"],"a":3,"e":"The best way to diagnose bronchial asthma is by the demonstration of reversible obstruction of the airways. Following the administration of an inhaled beta 2 agonist, a 12% and 200 ml increase in the FEV1 demonstrates the reversibility of airway obstruction.\n\nOptions A and B: Chest X-ray and skin test for inhaled allergens are not diagnostic of bronchial asthma.\n\nOption C: FEV1 values are measured to calculate FEV1/FVC ratio to diagnose any obstructive pathology and not specifically for asthma alone.","t":"Asthma & Copd","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"A young woman is rushed to ED due to wheezing and gasping for breath. She is unable to speak in full sentences. Her husband reports that she has had a cough with fever for 3 days. On examination, PR- 121/min, RR- 32/min, and has cyanosis. Her ABG analysis and chest X-ray are given below. What is the likely diagnosis?\n\nABG analysis:\n\npH- 7.48\n\nPaO2- 64 mmHg\n\nPaCO2- 32 mmHg\n\nHCO3- 25 mEq/L","o":["Acute respiratory distress syndrome","Asthma with impending failure","Acute pneumothorax","Acute severe asthma"],"a":3,"e":"The given clinical scenario of dyspnea, tachycardia along with the ABG analysis suggestive of respiratory alkalosis and chest-Xray showing hyperinflated lungs is pointing to a diagnosis of acute severe asthma. A history of cough with fever is indicating a probable respiratory infection to have triggered the asthma exacerbation.\n\nFeatures of acute severe asthma vs asthma with impending respiratory failure:\n\nAcute severe asthma\nAsthma with impending respiratory failure\n\nDyspneic, speaks in words\nMute patient\n\nAgitated patient\nSilent / confused patient\n\nHeart rate > 120 /min, tachycardia\nRelative bradycardia\n\nPulsus paradoxus present\nPulsus paradoxus absent (due to respiratory muscle fatigue)\n\nAccessory muscles of respiration used\nParadoxical breathing is seen\n\nLoud wheeze throughout the respiration\nSilent chest\n\nPeak expiratory flow rate < 40 ml/min\nPeak expiratory flow rate < 25 ml/min\n\nRespiratory alkalosis\nRespiratory acidosis\n\nHyperventilation leading to low or normal PaCO2\nNormal or rising PaCO2\n\nNote: The X-ray is not showing the features of ARDS and pneumothorax, hence they are ruled out as the right options.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/080872a670a842b0b3b3b4f511f2526a.JPEG"],"t":"Asthma & Copd","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"Which of the following drugs is not recommended in the treatment of patients with bronchial asthma?","o":["Sodium cromoglycate","Zafirlukast","Roflumilast","Dupilumab"],"a":2,"e":"Roflumilast is a phosphodiesterase-4 inhibitor used in the treatment of COPD, not asthma.\n\nDrugs used in the treatment of bronchial asthma\n\nBronchodilators\n\n• Beta-2 sympathomimetics: Salmeterol, formoterol\n\n• Methylxanthines: Theophylline, aminophylline\n\n• Anticholinergics: Ipratropium bromide, tiotropium bromide\n\nAnti-inflammatory\n\n• Corticosteroids (inhalational): Beclomethasone, budesonide, fluticasone\n\n• Corticosteroids (systemic): Prednisolone, hydrocortisone\n\n• Mast cell stabilizers: Sodium cromoglycate, ketotifen\n\n• Leukotriene antagonists: Montelukast, zafirlukast\n\n• Anti-IgE: Omalizumab\n\n• Anti-IL5: Mepolizumab, reslizumab\n\n• Anti-IL4 receptor: Dupilumab","t":"Asthma & Copd","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is false regarding leukotriene modifiers in the management of bronchial asthma?","o":["Ketotifen belongs to this group","Associated with Churg- Strauss syndrome","Protect against exercise-induced asthma","Diminishes nocturnal symptoms of asthma"],"a":0,"e":"Ketotifen is an example of a mast cell stabilizer, not leukotriene modifiers.\n\nLeukotriene modifiers are a group of agents used for mild persistent asthma. They block LT1-receptors thus causing reduced eosinophilic inflammation and causing bronchodilator. They include montelukast, zafirlukast, and zileuton.\n\nLeukotriene modifiers are less effective than inhaled corticosteroids (ICS) in controlling asthma. They are used as add-on therapy in some patients whose asthma is not controlled with low doses of ICS.\n\nChurg–Strauss syndrome (CSS) is a rare granulomatous small vessel vasculitis, that affects the heart, kidneys, peripheral nerves, etc. It occurs against a background of long-standing asthma and marked peripheral eosinophilia. Several cases of CSS are associated with the use of montelukast and zafirlukast.","t":"Asthma & Copd","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"Treatment of choice for patients with type 2 brittle asthma is:","o":["Inhaled Salbutamol","Subcutaneous salbutamol","Subcutaneous terbutaline","Subcutaneous adrenaline"],"a":3,"e":"The treatment of choice for type II brittle asthma is subcutaneous adrenaline.\n\nBrittle asthma is a type of severe asthma where patients have very serious and often, life-threatening attacks despite appropriate therapy. It is of two types:\n\nType 1\nType 2\n\nA persistent pattern of variability in PEFR (peak expiratory flow rate) readings\n\nGenerally normal or near-normal PEFR readings but few unpredictable exacerbations occur without an obvious trigger\n\nOral steroids, infusion of bronchodilators, and dietary exclusion of allergens may be helpful\n Respond only to subcutaneous epinephrine","t":"Asthma & Copd","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"Which of the following class of drugs cannot be used in the treatment of patients with bronchial asthma?","o":["IL-4 receptor antagonists","Anti–IL-5 antibodies","Anti IgE antibody","IL- 1 antagonist"],"a":3,"e":"The treatment options for asthma do not include IL-1 antagonists (anakinra). Anakinra is used in the treatment of rheumatoid arthritis.\n\nCytokines mediating airway inflammation in asthma: IL-4, IL-5, IL-13, TGF-β, and chemokines.\n\nOmalizumab is a monoclonal antibody against IgE. It is available for the treatment of children ≥6 years of age with documented allergen-induced asthma that is inadequately controlled by inhaled corticosteroids.\n\nThe new treatment options for asthma under clinical trial are:\n\n• Mepolizumab: anti–IL-5 antibody\n\n• Benralizumab: anti–IL-5 receptor antibody\n\n• Dupilumab: anti–IL-4 receptor antibody","t":"Asthma & Copd","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"A 6-year-old asthmatic boy is rushed to the casualty. He is not responding to the commands and is having fast shallow breathing. On auscultation, the chest is silent and paradoxical breathing is present. ABG analysis shows respiratory acidosis. What is the best step in management for him?","o":["Hyperbaric oxygen therapy","MDI with salbutamol and corticosteroids","Immediate endotracheal intubation","Intravenous magnesium sulfate"],"a":2,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of severe asthma with impending respiratory failure. The best step in the management is immediate endotracheal intubation.\n\nFeatures of asthma with impending respiratory failure are:\n\n• Mute or confused patient\n\n• Paradoxical breathing\n\n• Silent chest on auscultation\n\n• Relative bradycardia\n\n• Peak expiratory flow rate (PEFR) <25ml/min\n\n• Arterial blood gas analysis shows respiratory acidosis.\n\nOption A: Hyperbaric oxygen is given in CO poisoning and high altitude pulmonary edema.\n\nOption B: MDI with salbutamol and corticosteroids is only useful for acute severe asthma.\n\nOption D: Intravenous magnesium sulfate is not routinely used.","t":"Asthma & Copd","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements is false regarding COPD?","o":["Increased resistance is seen in airways ≤2 mm diameter","Changes in large airways cause cough and sputum","Ventilation-perfusion mismatching is seen","Rise in post bronchodilator FEV1&gt;12%"],"a":3,"e":"The rise in post-bronchodilator FEV1>12% is a feature of asthma, not COPD. It suggests reversible airflow obstruction.\n\nChronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) is a chronic respiratory disease characterized by the presence of airflow obstruction that is partially reversible.\n\nCigarette smoking is the most common cause of COPD. Cigarette smoke exposure affects large airways, small airways, and alveoli:\n\n• Changes in large airways cause cough and sputum\n\n• Changes in small airways (≤2mm) and alveoli are responsible for physiologic alterations\n\nThe major site of increased resistance in most individuals with COPD is in airways ≤2 mm diameter.\n\nCharacteristic cellular changes include goblet cell metaplasia. Goblet cells are mucus-secreting cells that replace surfactant-secreting Club cells.\n\nNonuniform ventilation and ventilation-perfusion mismatching are characteristic of COPD.","t":"Asthma & Copd","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"What happens to Reid index in patients with chronic bronchitis?","o":["Increases","Decreases","Remains same","Increases initially and then decreases"],"a":0,"e":"Reid index is increased in chronic bronchitis.\n\nReid index is the ratio of the thickness of the mucous gland layer to the thickness of the bronchial wall.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b41a1fc073494da7b25ccb1d8eb442bdx1280x1391.JPEG"],"t":"Asthma & Copd","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"Which of the following findings is least likely to be seen in patients with COPD?","o":["Barrel chest","Hoover's sign","Cyanosis","Clubbing"],"a":3,"e":"Clubbing is not a sign of COPD. Its presence should trigger the investigation of the causes of clubbing, particularly lung cancer.\n\nSigns of COPD:\n\n• Signs of respiratory distress\n\n• Tripod posture - facilitates the use of accessory muscles of respiration\n\n• Pursed lip breathing - seen in emphysema\n\n• Central cyanosis\n\n• Signs of cachexia\n\n• Severe weight loss\n\n• Bitemporal wasting\n\n• Signs of hypercapnia (type 2 respiratory failure) - more common in chronic bronchitis\n\n• Barrel-shaped chest - increase in the anteroposterior diameter\n\n• Enlarged lung volumes\n\n• Positive Hoover's sign - paradoxical inward movement of the chest during inspiration\n\n• Early inspiratory coarse crackles\n\n• Normal vesicular breath sounds of reduced-intensity with bilaterally prolonged expiration\n\n• Blue bloaters - cyanotic (blue) and edematous (bloaters), chronic bronchitis predominant features\n\n• Pink puffers - thin, non-cyanotic (pink) at rest, and severely dyspneic (puffers), emphysema predominant features\n\nCauses of clubbing:\n\n• Pulmonary\n\n• Bronchogenic carcinoma\n\n• Lung abscess, empyema, bronchiectasis\n\n• Chronic interstitial pneumonitis\n\n• Cystic fibrosis\n\n• Sarcoidosis\n\n• Gastrointestinal\n\n• Inflammatory bowel disease\n\n• Sprue\n\n• Neoplasms - esophagus, liver, bowel\n\n• Cardiovascular\n\n• Cyanotic congenital heart disease\n\n• Subacute bacterial endocarditis\n\n• Aortic aneurysm\n\n• Arteriovenous fistula\n\n• Endocrine\n\n• Hyperthyroidism (Grave’s disease)\n\nThe image below shows the characteristic tripod posture in a patient with COPD.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f427aee2ac5f4865be9410361221c7bex960x1280.JPEG"],"t":"Asthma & Copd","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"A 28-year-old coal miner presented with breathing difficulties. He reported that his symptoms get aggravated at work. His chest X-ray showed features of emphysema. On evaluation, he was found to have an α1-antitrypsin deficiency. Which pathological type of emphysema is most likely present in this patient?","o":["Centri-acinar","Pan-acinar","Irregular","Para-septal"],"a":1,"e":"The pathological type most likely to be present in this patient is pan-acinar emphysema. This type is commonly seen in patients with α1-antitrypsin deficiency.","t":"Asthma & Copd","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"An elderly man is being evaluated for exertional dyspnea, cough, and weight loss. His chest CT is given below. What is it suggestive of?","o":["Emphysematous lung","Atypical pneumonia","Calcified granulomas","Interstitial fibrosis"],"a":0,"e":"The CT scan shows emphysematous lungs, with reduced parenchymal markings and focal areas of low attenuation. This represents the emphysematous destruction of the lung, indicative of hyperinflation.\n\nRadiographic studies in COPD show:\n\n• Emphysematous bullae\n\n• A paucity of parenchymal markings\n\n• Hyperlucency suggests the presence of emphysema\n\n• Increased lung volumes and flattening of the diaphragm suggest hyperinflation\n\nOption B: Atypical pneumonia shows patchy bilateral ground-glass opacities.\n\nOption C: Calcified granulomas are seen as dense, bright nodules.\n\nOption D: Interstitial fibrosis shows a honeycomb pattern of the lung parenchyma as shown below.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/34ead242e43644ef935196bd29c60e7f.JPEG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2a4c2e6ed9534c45ae3752d9ca23c784x246x157.JPEG"],"t":"Asthma & Copd","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"A 55-years old chronic smoker presented with chronic cough with expectoration. He has been having similar episodes for the past 2 years. He also reported that he had to rest every few minutes while walking due to breathlessness. Pulmonary function tests showed FEV1/FVC of 0.5 and FEV1 of 50%. Which of the following is not a feature of the given condition?","o":["Irreversible airflow limitation","Affects small airway disease","Decrease in residual volume","Associated with ineffective repair of elastin"],"a":2,"e":"The clinical picture is highly suggestive of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease(COPD). An increase in the residual volume is a feature of COPD.\n\nCOPD is defined as a chronic respiratory disease characterized by the presence of airflow obstruction that is partially reversible.\n\nCOPD is the clinical expression of abnormalities of airways and/or alveoli and includes:\n\n• Emphysema is defined pathologically as the abnormal and permanent dilation of airways distal to the terminal bronchiole, along with the destruction of their walls.\n\n• Chronic bronchitis is defined clinically as the presence of persistent cough with sputum production on most of the days for ≥3 months in 2 consecutive years.\n\n• Small airways diseases are conditions affecting airways of ≤2 mm, resulting in inflammation and narrowing of the same.","t":"Asthma & Copd","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"Chest X-ray of an emphysematous patient is as shown. What is the best investigation to measure his lung volume?","o":["Body plethysmography","Gas dilution","Fractional exhaled NO","DLCO"],"a":0,"e":"Body plethysmography is the best investigation to measure lung volume in an emphysematous patient with bullous lesions as seen on the X-ray.\n\nTo measure absolute lung volumes, two techniques are used:\n\n1. Body plethysmography: It is used to measure the volume of any gas containing space in the thorax including bullae. It is accurate in both obstructive and restrictive diseases.\n\n2. Inert gas dilution technique: It is used to measure the volume of regions that are well ventilated which excludes trapped gas or bullae. It is accurate in restrictive diseases only.\n\nThe amount of gas trapped in bullae can be measured by subtracting lung volumes measured by inert gas dilution technique from lung volume measured by body plethysmography.\n\nFractional exhaled nitric oxide (FENO) is a non-invasive test used in the diagnosis and monitoring of bronchial asthma. NO levels are increased in asthmatics and related to the eosinophilic inflammation in the airways.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c6cb2450349c4f668a25e9ca1e9b2a76.PNG"],"t":"Asthma & Copd","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"A 66-year-old chronic smoker presents with progressive shortness of breath and chronic cough. On examination, he has a barrel chest and has pursed-lip breathing. Hyperresonance and inspiratory wheezes are heard. Pulmonary functions are likely to reveal all of the following, except:","o":["Decreased vital capacity","Increased diffusion capacity for carbon monoxide","Increased total lung capacity","Decreased FEV1/FVC ratio"],"a":1,"e":"The given clinical diagnosis is suggestive of COPD and the underlying pathological change is likely to be emphysema. It is associated with a decreased diffusion capacity for carbon monoxide (DLCO).\n\nEmphysema is defined pathologically as the abnormal and permanent dilation of airways distal to the terminal bronchiole, along with the destruction of their walls. This leads to hyperinflation due to air trapping and impaired diffusion of gases.\n\nPulmonary function tests in COPD:\n\n• TLC: Normal or increased\n\n• FEV1: Decreased (<80% predicted)\n\n• FVC: Decreased\n\n• FEV1/FVC: Decreased\n\n• DLCO: Decreased\n\nDiseases associated with lowered DLCO:\n\n• Emphysema\n\n• Interstitial Lung disease\n\n• Primary pulmonary hypertension\n\nNote - Bronchial asthma is associated with an increased DLCO.","t":"Asthma & Copd","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"The Gold's criteria for severe COPD are:","o":["FEV1/FVC &lt;0.7 and FEV1 ≥ 30%","FEV1/FVC &lt;0.7 and FEV1 &lt; 30%","FEV1/FVC&lt;0.7 and FEV1&lt; 20%","FEV1/FVC&lt;0.7 and FEV1 ≥ 50%"],"a":0,"e":"Gold's criteria for severe COPD (stage III) are FEV1/FVC <0.7 and FEV1 ≥ 30% but <50%\n\nThe severity of COPD is assessed by Global Initiative for Chronic Obstructive Lung Disease (GOLD) classification:\n\nGOLD Stage\n\nSeverity\n\nSpirometry\n\nI\n\nMild\n\nFEV1/FVC <0.7 and FEV1 ≥80% predicted\n\nII\n\nModerate\n\nFEV1/FVC <0.7 and FEV1 ≥50% but <80% predicted\n\nIII\n\nSevere\n\nFEV1/FVC <0.7 and FEV1 ≥30% but <50% predicted\n\nIV\n\nVery severe\n\nFEV1/FVC <0.7 and FEV1 <30% predicted\n\nNote: FEV1/FVC and FEV1 predicted are measured after the administration of a bronchodilator.\n\nRecent update: PRISm refers to a group of COPD patients with apparent normal spirometry, but with emphysema (as seen on radiographic evaluation) and a wide range of lung function impairment. These patients have a higher mortality risk as compared to other COPD patients. The possible risk factors are exposure to smoking, high BMI, and reduced total lung capacity. Spirometry findings are low to normal FEV1, and normal FEV1/FVC ratio.","t":"Asthma & Copd","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"A patient presents to the OPD with shortness of breath. He reports walking slower than his peers due to breathlessness. He has had two hospitalizations for similar complaints this year. His post-bronchodilator PFTs are FEV1/FVC &lt; 0.55 and FEV1 55%. Under what category would he be classified based on GOLD criteria?","o":["Moderate COPD, Group C","Moderate COPD, Group E","Severe COPD, Group A","Severe COPD, Group D"],"a":1,"e":"This patient belongs to moderate COPD, group E category.\n\nThe severity of COPD assessed by the Global Initiative for Lung Disease (GOLD) is classified as:\n\nGOLD Stage\n\nSeverity\n\nSpirometry\n\nI\n\nMild\n\nFEV1/FVC <0.7 and FEV1 ≥80% predicted\n\nII\n\nModerate\n\nFEV1/FVC <0.7 and FEV1 ≥50% but <80% predicted\n\nIII\n\nSevere\n\nFEV1/FVC <0.7 and FEV1 ≥30% but <50% predicted\n\nIV\n\nVery severe\n\nFEV1/FVC <0.7 and FEV1 <30% predicted\n\nAccording to GOLD 2023 guidelines, COPD patients are classified into Group A, B, or E based on the severity of dyspnoea, frequency of exacerbations and hospitilization. Dyspnoea may be assessed using the modified MRC Dyspnoea scale or COPD Assessment Test (CAT score)\n\n>=2 moderate exacerbations or >=1 leading to hospitalisation\n\nGroup E:\n LABA+LAMA\n\nconsider LABA+LAMA+ICS* if blood eosinophil count >=300\n\n0 or 1 moderate exacerbation (not leading to hospital admissions)\n\nGroup A: mMRC 0-1, CAT<10\n\n Bronchodilator\n\nGroup B: mMRC >=2, CAT >=10\n\n LABA + LAMA*\n\n* single inhaler therapy maybe more convenient and effective than multiple inhalers\n\nICS - inhaled corticosteroids\nLAMA - Long-acting Muscarinic Antagonists\nLABA - Long-acting Beta Agonists\n\nModified MRC (mMRC) dyspnoea scale:\n\n• 0- Breathless only with strenuous exercise\n\n• 1- Breathless when hurrying on a level or walking up a hill\n\n• 2- Walks slower than peers or has to stop for breath when walking on level\n\n• 3- Stops for breath after walking about 100m or for a few minutes\n\n• 4- Breathless at rest","t":"Asthma & Copd","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is false regarding acute exacerbations in COPD patients?","o":["May not be accompanied with fever","Viral infection is a common precipitating factor","Glucocorticoids are contraindicated","May present with congestive heart failure"],"a":2,"e":"Glucocorticoids are used during an exacerbation of COPD. They hasten recovery and reduce the chances of future exacerbations.\n\nExacerbations are episodes of increased dyspnoea and cough, with a change in the amount and character of sputum. They are often triggered by an infection with one-third of cases having a viral etiology. They may or may not have other signs of illness such as fever, myalgias, and sore throat. In severe cases, they may present with pneumonia and congestive heart failure.\n\nNote: While oral glucocorticoids are contraindicated in long-term treatment due to side effects, they are used for acute exacerbation of COPD.","t":"Asthma & Copd","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"A 45-year-old obese chronic smoker is rushed to the ER with difficulty in breathing and a productive cough. He has a past history of two similar episodes this year. On examination, RR- 34/min, with use of accessory muscles and central cyanosis is present. ABG is as shown. Chest X-ray shows increased vascular markings. What is the best initial step of management?\n\nABG Analysis:\n\npH- 7.33\n\nPaO2- 50 mmHg\n\nPaCO2- 48 mmHg\n\nHCO3‾- 27 mEq/L","o":["Low flow humidified oxygen","High flow humidified oxygen","Salbutamol and budesonide nebulisation","Slow infusion of theophylline"],"a":0,"e":"The given clinical scenario along with the ABG and X-ray findings is suggestive of acute exacerbation of COPD with respiratory failure. The best initial step of management is low-flow humidified oxygen.\n\nOxygen supplementation is a crucial component of acute therapy. Administering O2 should target a SpO2- 88-92% or PaO2- 60-70 mmHg. This helps to minimize the risk of worsening hypercapnia with excess supplemental oxygen.\n\nThe given ABG shows respiratory acidosis as PaCO2 >45 mmHg, and pH<7.35. In respiratory acidosis, a trial of non-invasive ventilation is given. Hence, low-flow humidified oxygen is the first step in management.\n\nOption B: High flow oxygen causes dryness, crusting epistaxis, and damages the lung hence not used.\n\nOption C: Salbutamol and budesonide nebulization are not effective as initial management in exacerbation of COPD with respiratory failure.\n\nOption D: Methylxanthines are not recommended in the management of acute exacerbations due to their side effect profiles.","t":"Asthma & Copd","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"In patients with which of the following conditions is NIPPV contraindicated?","o":["COPD","Central hypoventilation syndrome","Cardiovascular instability","Chest wall disorders"],"a":2,"e":"Noninvasive positive-pressure ventilation (NIPPV) is contraindicated in cardiovascular instability. NIPPV can impede venous filling of the heart, decrease cardiac output, and worsen/precipitate hypotension. Moreover, in unstable states with impending cardiovascular collapse, invasive ventilation is preferred.\n\nIndications of NIPPV:\n\n• Apnea - central and obstructive\n\n• Central hypoventilation syndrome\n\n• Neuromuscular and chest wall disorders\n\n• Respiratory failure - PaCO2 >45 mmHg\n\nContraindications of NIPPV:\n\n• Cardiovascular instability\n\n• Impaired mental status or inability to cooperate\n\n• Copious secretions or the inability to clear secretions\n\n• Craniofacial abnormalities\n\n• Trauma precluding effective fitting of the mask","t":"Asthma & Copd","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not used in the long-term treatment of COPD patients?","o":["Theophylline","Azithromycin","Oral glucocorticoids","N Acetylcysteine"],"a":2,"e":"Oral glucocorticoids are not used in the long-term treatment of COPD because of an unfavorable benefit/risk ratio.\n\nChronic use of glucocorticoids is associated with significant side effects, including osteoporosis, weight gain, cataracts, glucose intolerance, and increased risk of infection.\n\nTreatment of COPD:\n\n• Smoking cessation\n\n• Anticholinergic agents- Ipratropium bromide\n\n• Beta Agonists- Long-acting inhaled β agonists, such as salmeterol or formoterol are commonly used.\n\n• Methylxanthines- theophylline produces improvements in expiratory flow rates and vital capacity.\n\n• Phosphodiesterase inhibitors - roflumilast is used when FEV1<50%.\n\n• Antibiotics- macrolides like azithromycin reduce exacerbations when used over one year\n\n• N-acetylcysteine has been used in patients with COPD for both its mucolytic and antioxidant properties\n\n• Oxygen therapy\n\nNon-pharmacologic therapies:\n\n• Lung volume reduction surgery\n\n• Lung transplantation","t":"Asthma & Copd","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"Which of the following organisms can cause both typical and atypical pneumonia?","o":["Legionella","Pneumococcus","Mycoplasma","Staphylococcus aureus"],"a":0,"e":"Legionella pneumonia presents as both typical and atypical pneumonia.\n\nPneumonia is an infection of the lung parenchyma. Pneumonia that develops outside the hospital setting is commonly referred to as community-acquired pneumonia (CAP). There are two types of community-acquired pneumonia.\n\n1. Typical Pneumonia\n\n2. Atypical Pneumonia","t":"Pneumonia","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"There is an outbreak of legionella pneumonia in the hospital. Which of the following is least likely to have played a role in the transmission of the organism?","o":["Home split air conditioners","Cooling towers","Evaporative condensers","Humidifiers"],"a":0,"e":"Home split air-conditioning units do not use water to cool the air, so they are not a risk factor for legionella transmission.\n\nThe following are the risk factors for transmission of legionella:\n\n• Contamination of water supplies in large facilities such as hospitals, hotels, apartment buildings\n\n• Contaminated water sources such as hot tubs, birthing pools, fountains, whirlpools\n\n• Water using machinery- cooling towers, evaporative condensers, humidifiers","t":"Pneumonia","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"A 55-year-old smoker presents with high-grade fever, shortness of breath, diarrhea, and confusion. Gram-staining of the sputum revealed neutrophilia with no organisms. Chest x-ray showed bilateral multilobar infiltrates. Lab values are given below. Which pathogenic agent is suspected?\n\nParameters\nValues\n\nALT\n63 IU/L\n\nAST\n54 IU/L\n\nBilirubin\n0.8 mg/dl\n\nS. Na\n129 mEq/l","o":["Legionella","Pneumocystis jirovecii","Klebsiella pneumoniae","Streptococcus pneumoniae"],"a":0,"e":"The given clinical scenario is highly suggestive of atypical pneumonia caused by legionella.\n\nClinical features of Legionnaire's disease:\n\n• High-grade-fever( >104 F)\n\n• Cough with scanty sputum\n\n• Extrapulmonary manifestations involve\n\n• gastrointestinal- diarrhea, abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting\n\n• neurological- most common-confusion, headache, encephalopathy\n\n• Relative bradycardia\n\n• Occurrence of illness in an environment in which water supply is at risk of contamination\n\n• Failure to respond to beta-lactams and aminoglycoside antibiotics\n\nInvestigations:\n\n• On gram-staining of the sputum, numerous neutrophils with no organisms\n\n• Hyponatremia\n\n• Elevated liver enzymes","t":"Pneumonia","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"Urinary antigen detection test is recommended for diagnosing patients with?","o":["Mycoplasma pneumonia","Chlamydia pneumonia","Streptococcus pneumonia","Legionella pneumonia"],"a":3,"e":"A urinary antigen detection test is recommended for the diagnosis of Legionella pneumonia. The sensitivity (70%) and specificity (99%) of the test are high.\n\nThe test can detect antigen even after the initiation of appropriate antibiotic therapy. Urinary antigen detection test can also be used for Streptococcus pneumonia but is not used due to the availability of better alternatives.\n\nOther investigations for Legionella:\n\n• DFA (direct fluorescent antibody)- is highly specific but not sensitive\n\n• Culture- Gold standard, BCYE media is used\n\n• DNA detection with PCR\n\nTreatment: Drugs of choice are azithromycin and respiratory fluoroquinolones (levofloxacin, moxifloxacin).","t":"Pneumonia","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"A middle-aged man presents with fever, cough with scanty sputum, and fatigue. He had a throat infection a week ago. His blood investigations and chest X-ray are given below. Which is the most likely causative agent for his condition?\n\nParameter\nValues\n\nHb\n8.3 g/dl\n\nLDH\n560 U/L\n\nAST\n23 IU/L\n\nALT\n24 IU/L\n\nUnconjugated bilirubin\n1.8 mg/dl","o":["Pneumococcus","Mycoplasma","Legionella","Chlamydia"],"a":1,"e":"The given clinical scenario along with chest X-ray and lab values is suggestive of atypical pneumonia caused by Mycoplasma pneumoniae. The blood investigations show decreased hemoglobin, elevated LDH, and elevated unconjugated bilirubin suggestive of hemolytic anemia.\n\nMycoplasma is the most common bacteria causing atypical pneumonia and the second most common cause of pneumonia.\n\n Clinical Presentation:\n\n1. Pulmonary manifestations:\n\n• URTI- tracheobronchitis/ pharyngitis\n\n• Pneumonia( seen in only 10% patients)\n\n2. Extrapulmonary manifestations:\n\n• Cold agglutinin disease( IgM mediated autoimmune hemolytic anemia)\n\n• Erythema multiforme or Steven Johnson syndrome\n\n• Arthralgia with adenopathy\n\n• Guillain Barre Syndrome\n\n• Myocarditis\n\n• Pericarditis\n\n• Encephalitis\n\n• Bullous myringitis\n\nChest X-ray shows patchy consolidation at the hilum and fanning to the periphery.\n\nAzithromycin is the drug of choice. Doxycycline is the second-best drug for the management of atypical pneumonia.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/91cf3f95086f43849546514041782d90.PNG"],"t":"Pneumonia","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not true for patients with Mycoplasma pneumonia?","o":["Causative organism cannot be cultured routinely","Diffuse infiltrates in the lung are seen","Cefotaxime is the drug of choice","Serology is useful in diagnosis"],"a":2,"e":"Cefotaxime is not the drug of choice for the treatment of Mycoplasma pneumonia. Cefotaxime is a cell-wall synthesis inhibitor, hence it is not effective on M.pnemoniae as they lack a cell wall.\n\nInvestigations:\n\n• PCR of respiratory secretions\n\n• Chest X-ray shows-\n\n• Patchy consolidation at the hilum and fanning to the periphery\n\n• Diffuse peribronchial and perivascular interstitial infiltrates\n\n• Airspace consolidation\n\n• Reticulonodular opacification\n\n• HRCT shows- areas of ground-glass attenuation\n\n• On PPLO agar (Dienes method) shows- fried-egg appearance.\n\nThe diagnosis is made by serology/PCR. Azithromycin is the drug of choice for management. Doxycycline is the second-best drug for the management of atypical pneumonia.","t":"Pneumonia","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"A chronic alcoholic presents to the OPD with fever, shortness of breath, chest pain, and productive cough. On examination, there is bronchial breathing on the right side and a dull note on percussion. A chest x-ray is done. What is the most likely causative agent?","o":["Streptococcus pneumoniae","Klebsiella pneumoniae","Pseudomonas aeruginosa","Pneumocystis jirovecii"],"a":1,"e":"The given clinical scenario and chest X-ray are suggestive of lobar pneumonia caused by Klebsiella pneumoniae.\n\nKlebsiella pneumonia is associated mostly with alcoholics and shows upper lobe involvement. The patients present with red-currant jelly sputum.\n\nChest X-ray shows:\n\n• Cavitation\n\n• Pneumatocele (also seen in S. aureus)\n\n• Bulging fissure sign: Classically seen in Klebsiella pneumonia. It represents expansive lobar consolidation causing fissural bulging; or displacement of copious amounts of inflammatory exudates within the affected parenchyma. It is seen in any space-occupying process in the lung such as pulmonary hemorrhage, lung abscess, and lung tumors.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9973a50c7abc45aa9bffb70c5ff55e20.JPEG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/181707c837f44001bd7687f1c96e8b45x1280x1414.JPEG"],"t":"Pneumonia","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is the best biomarker for lobar pneumonia?","o":["C-reactive protein","Procalcitonin","Fibrinogen","Serum amyloid A"],"a":1,"e":"The best biomarker for lobar pneumonia is procalcitonin.\n\nThe most commonly used biomarkers of severe inflammation are C-reactive protein (CRP) and procalcitonin (PCT). Levels of these acute phase reactants increase in the presence of bacterial pathogens. PCT testing distinguishes between bacterial (mostly lobar pneumonia) and viral infection to decide the need for antibacterial therapy or discontinuation of treatment.\n\nCRP, fibrinogen, and serum amyloid A are other acute phase reactants.","t":"Pneumonia","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"A 71-year-old man presents to the OPD with high-grade fever, productive cough. On examination, the respiratory rate was found to be 32/ min, heart rate of 102/ min, and blood pressure of 90/60 mmHg. How will you manage this patient?","o":["Admit to ICU and treat with azithromycin and ceftriaxone","Admit to the ward and treat with levofloxacin and doxycycline","Treat him on OP basis with azithromycin and ceftriaxone","Admit to the ward and treat with azithromycin and levofloxacin"],"a":0,"e":"The given clinical scenario is highly suggestive of lobar pneumonia and the patient belongs to a CURB-65 score of 3. The patient can be best managed by a combination of azithromycin and ceftriaxone in an ICU setting. After culture sensitivity, specific management is done.","t":"Pneumonia","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"A patient in the ICU is diagnosed with community acquired-MRSA. What is the best line of management?","o":["Ceftriaxone + Azithromycin + Ampicillin","Ceftriaxone + Moxifloxacin + Linezolid + Clindamycin","Ceftriaxone + Carbenepem + Moxifloxacin","Ceftriaxone + Linezolid"],"a":1,"e":"The Empiric treatment of choice for ICU in patients with suspected CA-MRSA is:\n\n• β-lactam (e.g., ceftriaxone) + Fluoroquinolone/Azithromycin\n\n PLUS\n\n• Linezolid / Vancomycin with or without Clindamycin to cover CA-MRSA","t":"Pneumonia","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"An epileptic patient presented with fever, breathlessness, and productive cough for 2 days. The last episode of seizures occurred a week ago. On examination, PR-102/min, RR-30/min, and oral cavity revealed a gingival infection. Bronchial breath sounds were heard on auscultation on the right side. What is the drug of choice for his condition?","o":["Clindamycin","Azithromycin","Moxifloxacin","Ceftriaxone"],"a":0,"e":"The given clinical scenario is highly suggestive of lobar pneumonia due to aspiration. The drug of choice is clindamycin as anaerobes are implicated.\n\nAnaerobes play a significant role only when an episode of aspiration has occurred days to weeks before presentation for pneumonia. The combination of an unprotected airway (e.g., in patients with alcohol or drug overdose or a seizure disorder) and significant gingivitis constitutes the major risk factor.\n\nAnaerobic pneumonia is often complicated by abscess formation and significant empyemas or parapneumonic effusions.","t":"Pneumonia","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"In an immunocompromised patient, which of the following is the drug of choice for prophylaxis of pneumocystis jirovecii?","o":["Clotrimazole","Sulfadiazine-Pyrimethamine","Trimethoprim-Sulfamethoxazole","Sulfadoxine-Pyrimethamine"],"a":2,"e":"Trimethoprim-Sulfamethoxazole combination (commonly known as cotrimoxazole) is the first-line drug for both prophylaxis and treatment of Pneumocystis jirovecii infections in immunocompromised patients.\n\nCotrimoxazole acts by causing a sequential block of folate metabolism. CD4+ T cell counts below 200 cells/μL are an indication to start prophylaxis.","t":"Pneumonia","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is false regarding patients with Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia?","o":["Glucocorticoids are indicated if Pao2 &lt;70 mmHg","Recent oropharyngeal candidiasis is an indication for prophylaxis","Elevation of LDH is a common finding","Aerosolized pentamidine is used in the treatment"],"a":3,"e":"Aerosolised pentamidine is not used in the treatment of PCP. It is used in PCP prophylaxis.\n\nDiagnosis of PCP:\n\n• Histopathological staining of induced sputum examination\n\n• Bronchoscopy with BAL (most sensitive)\n\n• Elevated LDH\n\nTreatment and Prophylaxis:\n\n• Drug of choice- trimethoprim/sulfamethoxazole (TMP-SMX)\n\n• IV pentamidine- treatment of choice for severe disease in the patient unable to tolerate TMP-SMX\n\n• Glucocorticoids- indicated if PaO2 <70 mmHg or with an A–a gradient >35 mmHg\n\n• Other drugs used- dapsone/pyrimethamine + leucovorin, clindamycin/primaquine, and atovaquone\n\n• Aerosolised pentamidine is an alternative used in PCP prophylaxis.\n\nProphylaxis is indicated when CD4+ T cell count <200/uL ( CD4 cell %<15), recent oropharyngeal candidiasis, and prior PCP infection.","t":"Pneumonia","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"A 36-year-old male HIV patient presents to the ED with a cough with bloody sputum. He also has night sweats, chills, and difficulty in breathing. A chest X-ray and examination of the sputum show the following findings. Which of the following is the drug of choice for his condition?","o":["Amikacin","Cotrimoxazole","Minocycline","Linezolid"],"a":1,"e":"The given clinical scenario along with the chest X-ray and gram-staining is suggestive of Nocardiosis. The drug of choice is cotrimoxazole.\n\nNocardia is a gram-positive and acid-fast bacteria. Nocardia pneumonia has a sub-acute presentation with mostly cough with thick sputum and fever. Generally, hemoptysis is uncommon. Chest radiography shows nodular infiltrations involving bilateral lung fields.\n\nExtrapulmonary manifestations are common, the brain is the most commonly involved organ. It is associated with dense bilateral infiltrates with cavitating nodules and subacute brain abscesses.\n\nThe drug of choice is cotrimoxazole. Severe disease is treated with a combination of cotrimoxazole, amikacin, and ceftriaxone, or imipenem.\n\nNote: Mnemonic SNAP:\n\nSulfamethoxazole-trimethoprim (Cotrimoxazole) is the drug of choice for Nocardia.\n\nFor Actinomyces, Penicillins are the drugs of choice.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/dfacc98188c14e3e8fb1003ce4308a40.JPEG"],"t":"Pneumonia","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements is false regarding patients with parapneumonic effusion?","o":["Diabetes is a risk factor","pH &lt;7.2 may be a finding in pleural fluid analysis","Pleural fluid protein &gt;4 gm/dL is an indication of tube thoracostomy","USG is helpful in characterizing the effusion"],"a":2,"e":"Pleural Fluid Protein >4 gm/dL is not an indication of tube thoracostomy.\n\nParapneumonic effusion is pleural effusion associated with bacterial pneumonia, lung abscess, or bronchiectasis. When it worsens, it can result in the collection of frank pus in the pleural space. This is known as empyema.\n\nRisk factors include diabetes, immunosuppression, alcoholism, cancer, and poor dental hygiene.\n\nInvestigations:\n\n• Chest X-ray:\n\n• Loculations or encystments (complicated parapneumonic effusion)\n\n• Intrapleural air-fluid level (bronchopulmonary fistula)\n\n• Parenchymal infiltrations (underlying pneumonia)\n\n• Ultrasound: Shows echogenic pleural fluid with septations and helps in characterizing the effusion\n\n• Pleural fluid analysis:\n\n• Exudative neutrophilic effusion\n\n• Low pH <7.2, low glucose <60 mg/dL, high LDH >1000 IU/L in complicated parapneumonic effusion\n\n• CECT: Split pleura sign seen in empyema\n\nTreatment:\n\n• Antibiotics: Tailored according to the microorganism\n\n• Therapeutic thoracocentesis\n\n• Tube thoracostomy is indicated if:\n\n• Pleural fluid pH <7.2\n\n• Frank pus\n\n• Microorganism isolated in Gram stain or culture of pleural fluid","t":"Pneumonia","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is the most common interstitial lung disease?","o":["Non-specific interstitial pneumonia","Desquamative interstitial pneumonia","Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis","Respiratory bronchiolitis associated lung disease"],"a":2,"e":"The most common interstitial lung disease (ILD) is idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis (IPF).\n\nILD refers to the remodeling of interstitial space, the region between the epithelium and endothelium. The main pathology is fibrosis, mediated by TGF-ß and platelet-derived growth factor.\n\nClassification of Interstitial Lung Disease (ILD)\n\nILDs of known etiology\n\n• Occupational: Coal worker's pneumoconiosis, silicosis\n\n• Treatment-related: Radiation, drug-induced\n\n• Connective tissue disease-related: Rheumatoid arthritis, Sjogren's syndrome, scleroderma\n\nILDs of unknown etiology\n\n• Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis (IPF): Most common type\n\n• Non-specific interstitial pneumonia (NSIP)\n\n• Cryptogenic organizing pneumonia (COP)\n\n• Acute interstitial pneumonia (AIP)/Hamman-Rich syndrome\n\n• Respiratory bronchiolitis associated ILD (RBILD)\n\n• Desquamative interstitial pneumonia (DIP)\n\n• Lymphocytic interstitial pneumonia (LIP)\n\n• Pleuroparenchymal fibroelastosis\n\nGranulomatous\n\n• Sarcoidosis\n\n• Hypersensitivity pneumonitis\n\nOther forms\n\n• Lymphangioleiomyomatosis (LAM)\n\n• Pulmonary Langerhans' cell histiocytosis (PLCH)\n\n• Pulmonary alveolar proteinosis (PAP)","t":"Interstitial Lung Diseases And Pneumoconiosis","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"An 80-year-old woman was found to have a restrictive disease with reduced lung volumes on a pulmonary function test. A high-resolution CT of the thorax was done and is shown below. Which of the following is not a feature of this condition?","o":["Clubbing of the digits","Inspiratory coarse crackles","Breathlessness on exertion","Non-productive cough"],"a":1,"e":"The above HRCT shows a honeycomb appearance of lung parenchyma due to interstitial fibrosis in a restrictive lung disorder that is suggestive of interstitial lung disease (ILD). Inspiratory coarse crackles are not a feature of interstitial lung disease.\n\nAuscultating a patient with ILD will reveal bilateral basal end-inspiratory fine crackles also called velcro crackles or rales.\n\nPatients with interstitial lung disease usually present with a non-productive cough and breathlessness on exertion. Clubbing may be seen on examination, especially in advanced cases.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/18d10d402a1e43fc831cad99b33a568b.PNG"],"t":"Interstitial Lung Diseases And Pneumoconiosis","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"A 70-year-old woman presents with breathlessness on exertion and dry cough for 4 weeks. She has swollen joints and early morning stiffness for which she is on treatment for a few years. On auscultation, inspiratory fine crackles were heard. An HRCT was ordered and it showed bilateral, subpleural and basilar honeycombing pattern. PFT of the patient is likely to show which of the following patterns?","o":["FEV1/FVC ratio &lt;0.7 and DLCO increases","FEV1/FVC ratio &lt;0.7 and DLCO decreases","FEV1/FVC ratio &gt;0.7 and DLCO decreases","FEV1/FVC ratio &gt;0.7 and DLCO increases"],"a":2,"e":"The given clinical scenario along with the HRCT findings are suggestive of interstitial lung disease (ILD). FEV1/FVC ratio >0.7 and decreased DLCO are likely to be seen in the PFT. A history of swollen joints and early morning stiffness hints at rheumatoid arthritis. It is associated with the usual interstitial pneumonia (UIP) pattern of ILD.\n\nIn ILD, restrictive pattern is seen on pulmonary function test, which is characterized by:\n\n• Decreased total lung capacity (TLC)\n\n• Decreased forced vital capacity (FVC)\n\n• Decreased residual volumes (RV)\n\n• Decreased DLCO (diffusing capacity of the lungs for carbon monoxide)\n\n• FEV1/FVC ratio - normal or increased, >0.7","t":"Interstitial Lung Diseases And Pneumoconiosis","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not a characteristic HRCT finding of usual interstitial pneumonia (UIP)?","o":["Honeycombing and loss of lung architecture","Ground-glass opacities","Traction bronchiectasis","Bibasilar subpleural reticular opacities"],"a":1,"e":"Ground-glass opacities on HRCT are not a characteristic finding in UIP.\n\nHoneycombing, loss of lung architecture, traction bronchiectasis, and basilar subpleural reticular opacities are seen in UIP pattern of ILD on HRCT.","t":"Interstitial Lung Diseases And Pneumoconiosis","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"Which of the following conditions is also known as Hamman-Rich syndrome?","o":["Usual interstitial pneumonia","Non-specific interstitial pneumonia","Acute interstitial pneumonia","Cryptogenic organising pneumonia"],"a":2,"e":"Acute interstitial pneumonia (AIP) is also known as Hamman–Rich syndrome.\n\nAIP is a rare, idiopathic, fulminant interstitial lung disease (ILD) that is similar in presentation to ARDS. It is often preceded by a viral upper respiratory tract infection.\n\nHistologically, it shows diffuse alveolar damage. HRCT shows bilateral patchy, ground-glass opacities. Lung biopsy shows diffuse alveolar damage with hyaline formation.","t":"Interstitial Lung Diseases And Pneumoconiosis","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"A 52-year-old woman presented with dyspnoea. General physical examination revealed deformity of the hand as shown below. There is restricted mobility in the small joints and loss of wrinkle lines on the facial skin. An HRCT of the thorax showed diffuse subpleural, symmetric, ground-glass opacities of the lungs. Which of the following histopathological finding is likely to be seen in the lung biopsy of this patient?","o":["Homogenous interstitial involvement","Temporal heterogeneity of lesions","Spatial heterogeneity of lesions","Honeycombing pattern"],"a":0,"e":"The given clinical scenario and the image showing sclerodactyly suggestive of scleroderma along with the HRCT findings point to a diagnosis of non-specific interstitial pneumonia (NSIP). It is characterized by homogeneous interstitial involvement.\n\nNSIP is often associated with connective tissue disease but can also be drug-induced. The common drugs implicated in NSIP are:\n\n• Bleomycin\n\n• Busulfan\n\n• Methotrexate\n\n• Amiodarone\n\nIt usually affects middle-aged woman who have never smoked. There is uniformity of interstitial involvement across the biopsy section, and this may be predominantly cellular or fibrosing. HRCT shows homogenous involvement with symmetrical ground-glass opacities.\n\nNon-specific interstitial pneumonia (NSIP) responds to steroids. Other treatment modalities include cytotoxic drugs like azathioprine or cyclophosphamide and also biologics like rituximab.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/40ba29b9fdbe4b75b4f3fb036fe18df6.PNG"],"t":"Interstitial Lung Diseases And Pneumoconiosis","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not a smoking-related interstitial lung disease (ILD)?","o":["Desquamative interstitial pneumonia","Respiratory bronchiolitis associated ILD","Pulmonary langerhans cell histiocytosis","Pulmonary lymphangioleiomyomatosis"],"a":3,"e":"Pulmonary lymphangioleiomyomatosis (LAM) is not related to smoking.\n\nIt is usually seen in young females with tuberous sclerosis. LAM can lead to pneumothorax or chylous effusions.\n\nPulmonary function testing usually reveals an obstructive or mixed obstructive-restrictive pattern. HRCT shows thin-walled cysts surrounded by normal lung parenchyma.\n\nThe smoking-related ILDs are:\n\n• DIP - Desquamating interstitial pneumonia.\n\n• RB-ILD - Respiratory bronchiolitis associated ILD.\n\n• LCH - Langerhans cell histiocytosis.","t":"Interstitial Lung Diseases And Pneumoconiosis","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"A man presented with progressively worsening cough with sputum, breathlessness, easy fatiguability, and loss of appetite for 6 months. PFT was suggestive of a restrictive disorder. Trans-bronchial biopsy showed cells positive for CD1a and langerin (CD207). Which of the following is not a feature of this condition?","o":["They may present with spontaneous pneumothorax","Cysts and nodules are seen on HRCT","It is a smoking related interstitial lung disease","It typically affects elderly smokers"],"a":3,"e":"The given scenario is suggestive of pulmonary Langerhans cell histiocytosis (LCH). It is a smoking-related ILD that typically affects young adults. Smoking cessation leads to improvement in the condition.\n\nThe specific immunohistochemical markers for LCH are CD1a and langerin (CD207). On electron microscopy, the presence of Birbeck granules in the cytoplasm is characteristic (as shown below).\n\nHRCT shows bilateral irregularly shaped cysts with nodules, predominantly in the upper lobe. There is a risk of spontaneous pneumothorax in this condition due to cyst rupture.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/91c8ab4609bd4999b6bdd06ae52272ebx576x641.PNG"],"t":"Interstitial Lung Diseases And Pneumoconiosis","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements is false regarding pulmonary alveolar proteinosis?","o":["Acquired due to antibodies formed against GM-CSF","Copious, watery PAS-negative sputum","X-ray shows bilateral diffuse opacities","Treatment is whole lung lavage"],"a":1,"e":"Chunky, gelatinous, PAS-positive sputum is a feature of pulmonary alveolar proteinosis (PAP).\n\nPAP is a condition characterized by the accumulation of surfactants in alveoli and terminal airways. It can be due to impaired clearance or abnormal production of surfactants.\n\nPAP can be of three forms:\n\n• Primary (most common): There is impaired GM-CSF-dependent surfactant clearance by the alveolar macrophages. It can be due to:\n\n• Autoantibodies against GM-CSF (acquired/idiopathic/autoimmune PAP)\n\n• Mutation in GM-CSF receptors (hereditary PAP)\n\n• Secondary: There is a reduction in the number or ability of alveolar macrophages that causes impaired surfactant clearance. It can be due to:\n\n• Silicosis\n\n• Immunodeficiency disorders\n\n• Hematopoietic disorders and malignancies\n\n• Congenital: It affects neonates and children. There is abnormal surfactant production due to mutation in the genes coding for:\n\n• Surfactant protein B\n\n• Surfactant protein C\n\n• ATP binding cassette protein member 3\n\nAbout 30% of patients are asymptomatic. Symptomatic patients can have persistent dry cough, progressive dyspnea, and pleuritic chest pain\n\nX-ray shows bilateral, diffuse opacities and HRCT shows a crazy-paving pattern (as shown below). The treatment is whole lung lavage.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/07b886c279534102a5d40828c041f07ax1279x970.PNG"],"t":"Interstitial Lung Diseases And Pneumoconiosis","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"Farmer’s lung is associated with which of the following organisms?","o":["Histoplasmosis","Coccidiomycosis","Thermophilic actinomycetes","Cryptococcosis"],"a":2,"e":"Farmer’s lung is a hypersensitivity pneumonitis caused due to exposure to mouldy hay, which contains thermophilic actinomycetes, principally Thermopolyspora polyspora.","t":"Interstitial Lung Diseases And Pneumoconiosis","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"A daily-wage worker from the cement industry got a chest X-ray as a part of his routine health check-up. What could be the cause of the findings shown below?","o":["Berylliosis","Anthracosis","Asbestosis","Silicosis"],"a":2,"e":"The given X-ray shows irregular opacities, which appear to be pleural thickenings i.e., pleural plaques. The sign along with a history of working in the cement industry suggests a diagnosis of asbestosis.\n\nWorkers of the cement, shipping, and textile industries are at a higher risk of developing asbestosis. Asbestos tends to involve the pleura first.\n\nThe earliest manifestation is benign asbestosis-related pleural effusion. With time, the plaques calcify resulting in the formation of thick pleural plaques. The pleural thickenings can be seen on the X-ray. This is the “holly leaf sign”.\n\nLung parenchymal involvement occurs after years of exposure (at least 10 years). They present with cough and exertional dyspnoea. It predominantly affects the lower lobes of the lung, in contrast to other forms of pneumoconiosis, which affect the upper lobes.\n\nPulmonary function testing in asbestosis typically shows a restrictive pattern with a decrease in both lung volumes and diffusing capacity.\n\nThe image below shows thick plueral plaques.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0005768cd80343ec942734c3a9183dd0.JPEG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/128aeb50c7cc4a66bcc03408bb51890bx1280x1446.JPEG"],"t":"Interstitial Lung Diseases And Pneumoconiosis","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"A woman has been working for a company that manufactures roofing sheets for 12 years, but the company does not provide her with personal protective equipment. Which of the following cancers is commonly seen in such individuals?","o":["Pleural mesothelioma","Peritoneal mesothelioma","Adenocarcinoma of lung","Laryngeal carcinoma"],"a":2,"e":"Workers in the roofing industry are exposed to asbestos. Lung cancer (of all histologic types) is the most common cancer associated with asbestos exposure.\n\nSmoking along with asbestos exposure multiplies the risk to a great extent. Cough and hemoptysis are common presentations.\n\nPleural mesothelioma is the most specific cancer associated with asbestos exposure, but not the most common. Smoking does not seem to be associated with mesothelioma. Pleuritic chest pain or a mass on the radiographic image are common presentations.","t":"Interstitial Lung Diseases And Pneumoconiosis","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"Which among the following is a common concern for a patient presenting with chronic dry cough, working in the granite mining industry?","o":["Asbestosis","Silicosis","Farmer's lung","Anthracosis"],"a":1,"e":"Silicosis is a common concern for people working in granite mining industries.\n\nSilica is found in rock, granite, slate, and sandstone. People working in mining, stone cutting, sandblasting, glass and cement manufacturing, and quarrying are exposed to silica. The active molecule that causes silicosis is free silica or crystalline silica (quartz).\n\nSilicosis can present as:\n\n• Acute silicosis - due to heavy exposure in closed spaces and they manifest within 2 years.\n\n• Chronic silicosis (simple silicosis) - due to long-term (10 to 30 years), less intense exposure.\n\n• Complicated silicosis- simple silicosis may progress to complicated silicosis that presents with chronic cough and progressive dyspnoea on exertion.\n\nNote: Silicosis is the most common pnemoconiosis in the world.","t":"Interstitial Lung Diseases And Pneumoconiosis","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"A 45-year-old man comes to the emergency room with an acute onset of dyspnoea. He tells you that he has been working in the sandblasting industry for about a year and the company does not provide personal protective equipment. He also gives a history of on and off dry cough. CT scan shows the following. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Complicated silicosis","Acute silicosis","Simple silicosis","Pulmonary Alveolar Proteinosis"],"a":1,"e":"A history of working in the sandblasting industry for about a year without PPE suggests a heavy exposure to silica. This along with the CT finding of bilateral nodules, interstitial fibrosis, and crazy pavement pattern suggests a diagnosis of acute silicosis.\n\nAcute silicosis is seen rarely. It manifests within 2 years of heavy exposure. Common presentations are dyspnoea and hypoxemia.\n\nThe X-ray may show diffuse miliary infiltrates or consolidation. HRCT may also show bilateral centrilobular nodules and ground-glass opacification. Bronchoalveolar lavage yields a PAS-positive milky white fluid.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/acca81a77941475c81f0363916e15abd.JPEG"],"t":"Interstitial Lung Diseases And Pneumoconiosis","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"A 30-year-old man has recently started work in the stone cutting industry. The company provides him with a respirator mask but they do not use dust control measures. If he works in similar conditions for 20 more years, which of the following will you most likely see in his HRCT scan image?","o":["A typical crazy pavement pattern","Small round opacities in upper lobes","Thin-walled cysts surrounded by normal lung parenchyma","Thick calcified pleural plaques"],"a":1,"e":"In this scenario, the man works in the stone cutting industry, indicating exposure to silica. He is expected to have less intense, long term exposure owing to a few but substandard measures to control exposure to silica. Such individuals are more likely to develop simple silicosis. These patients are asymptomatic, and HRCT shows small round opacities in the upper lobes.\n\nThis can progress to complicated silicosis. Here, the nodules coalesce and form masses leading to impairment of lung function. They present with chronic cough and progressive dyspnoea on exertion. This may progress further to cause progressive massive fibrosis.\n\nPFT shows a mixed obstructive and restrictive pattern. Calcification of hilar lymph nodes is seen in a few cases, called eggshell calcification. This is depicted in the image below.\n\nOption A: A crazy pavement pattern is seen with acute silicosis.\n\nOption C: Thin-walled cysts surrounded by normal lung parenchyma are seen in pulmonary lymphangiomyomatosis (LAM).\n\nOption D: Pleural plaques are seen in patients suffering from asbestosis.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b5512bdb47434480897e0abc14fa12d8x601x546.PNG"],"t":"Interstitial Lung Diseases And Pneumoconiosis","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"Which of the following pneumoconiosis is associated with an increased incidence of tuberculosis?","o":["Asbestosis","Silicosis","Coal worker's pneumoconiosis","Farmer's lung"],"a":1,"e":"Silicosis is associated with an increased incidence of tuberculosis.\n\nSilica causes pulmonary alveolar macrophage dysfunction and hence, patients with silicosis have a higher risk of developing lung infections from organisms like M. Tuberculosis, atypical mycobacteria and fungi.\n\nRadiographic imaging of silicotuberculosis shows cavitation.","t":"Interstitial Lung Diseases And Pneumoconiosis","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"Choose the correct option with respect to Caplan’s syndrome.","o":["Rheumatoid arthritis + pneumoconiosis","Rheumatoid arthritis + anterior uveitis","Hilar adenopathy + polyarthritis + erythema nodosum","Arthritis + urethritis + conjunctivitis"],"a":0,"e":"Caplan's syndrome also called rheumatoid pneumoconiosis, is a combination of rheumatoid arthritis and pneumoconiosis.\n\nIt manifests as pneumoconiotic nodules along with features of seropositive rheumatoid arthritis. Caplan syndrome was first described in coal miners and later in patients with silicosis too.\n\nOption C: Hilar adenopathy, polyarthritis, and erythema nodosum are characteristic of Lofgren syndrome.\n\nOption D: Arthritis, urethritis, and conjunctivitis are characteristic of Reiter syndrome.","t":"Interstitial Lung Diseases And Pneumoconiosis","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"A factory worker presents to the clinic with fever, tiredness, and a chronic dry cough. He says his symptoms are worst on the first day of the week, improve by the end of the week, and are almost non-existent over the weekends. What could be the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Bagassosis","Byssinosis","Berylliosis","Bornholm’s disease"],"a":1,"e":"The above characteristic pattern of symptoms is called Monday fever or Monday chest tightness and is due to byssinosis.\n\nByssinosis is seen commonly in cotton factory workers due to cotton dust exposure. It is also caused due to flax, hemp, and jute dust.\n\nIt is due to occupational hypersensitivity reaction with symptoms occurring (or recurring) on the first day of the workweek and diminishing by the weekend due to development of tolerance.","t":"Interstitial Lung Diseases And Pneumoconiosis","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"Which interstitial lung disease is likely to be seen in a patient suffering from systemic sclerosis?","o":["Respiratory bronchiolitis associated ILD","Non-specific interstitial pneumonia","Acute interstitial pneumonia","Bronchiolitis obliterans organising pneumonia"],"a":1,"e":"Non-specific interstitial pneumonia (NSIP) pattern of ILD is the most common pulmonary manifestation in systemic sclerosis.\n\nThe CT scan of NSIP shows ground-glass opacities. It responds to steroids given along with azathioprine or cyclophosphamide.\n\nOther connective tissue diseases associated with ILD:\n\n• Rheumatoid arthritis\n\n• Sjögren syndrome\n\n• Dermatopolymyositis\n\n• Systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE).","t":"Interstitial Lung Diseases And Pneumoconiosis","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"A 45-year-old man who has worked as a miner for the past 25 years, comes to the OPD with complaints of dyspnoea and cough. Pulmonary function tests and a lung biopsy are done. The histopathology of the sample is shown below. Which type of emphysema can this condition give rise to?","o":["Panacinar emphysema","Centrilobular emphysema","Paraseptal emphysema","Irregular emphysema"],"a":1,"e":"The given clinical scenario and the microscopic image showing abundant black-colored pigment within the macrophages of lung parenchyma is characteristic of pulmonary anthracosis/coal worker's pneumoconiosis. It may give rise to centrilobular emphysema.\n\nThe inhaled carbon particles are engulfed by the lung macrophages. They accumulate in the connective tissue along lymphatics or in lymphoid tissue along the bronchi or the hilum, which causes the blackening of the tissues. Aggregates of carbon dust are present as coal macules or nodules which consist of carbon-laden macrophages.\n\nIt is commonly seen in people who work in environments where there is high air pollution with carbon particles, such as coal mines. It is usually benign and less than 10% of the cases develop progressive massive fibrosis which may lead to pulmonary dysfunction, pulmonary hypertension, and cor pulmonale. Coal workers may also develop emphysema or chronic bronchitis, independent of smoking.\n\nUnlike asbestosis, this condition does not predispose to cancer.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4df730716ed948e7b2403d0816133070.JPEG"],"t":"Interstitial Lung Diseases And Pneumoconiosis","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"An old woman presents with a persistent productive cough and clubbing. On auscultation, wheeze and crackles are present. There is no history of fever. Which of the following is the investigation of choice to confirm the diagnosis?","o":["HRCT","CECT","Helical CT","Spiral CT"],"a":0,"e":"The clinical scenario is suggestive of bronchiectasis. HRCT is the investigation of choice - most specific.\n\nBronchiectasis is a suppurative lung disease with abnormal and irreversible dilatation of the bronchi. It is an end-stage condition of various diseases (infections, congenital or autoimmune disorders) that result in the destruction of the bronchial wall and its surrounding supporting tissues.\n\nRied’s classification divides bronchiectasis into 3 types based on the phenotype of the affected bronchi:\n\n• Cylindrical or tubular (most common)\n\n• Saccular or varicose\n\n• Cystic\n\nTraction bronchiectasis is a type of bronchiectasis seen in conditions causing extensive pulmonary fibrosis (tuberculosis, idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis). The mechanical traction due to fibrosis pulls apart the adjacent bronchi, making them distorted and abnormally dilated. There is no destruction of the bronchial wall.\n\nClinically, they have a chronic productive cough, clubbing, crackles, and rhonchi.","t":"Bronchiectasis And Lung Abscess","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"A 30-year-old woman is admitted to the hospital with a fever and cough. She has a history of repeated chest infections for several years and coughs up yellow-green purulent sputum with some streaky hemoptysis. What could be the underlying condition based on the CT given below?","o":["Cystic fibrosis","Tuberculosis","Amyloidosis","Lung abscess"],"a":0,"e":"The given clinical scenario and the CT showing signet ring sign is suggestive of bronchiectasis which can be due to cystic fibrosis.\n\nA history of repeated chest infections is an indication of cystic fibrosis and a major risk factor for the development of bronchiectasis.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/723f274c00c14489a232f1638772d821.PNG"],"t":"Bronchiectasis And Lung Abscess","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is an unlikely complication in patients with bronchiectasis?","o":["Cor pulmonale","Amyloidosis","Lung cancer","Empyema"],"a":2,"e":"Lung cancer is not a known complication of bronchiectasis.\n\nComplications of bronchiectasis include:\n\n• Massive hemoptysis\n\n• Empyema and lung abscess\n\n• Respiratory failure\n\n• Pulmonary hypertension and cor pulmonale\n\n• Secondary amyloidosis (AA type)\n\nChronic and recurrent infections lead to secondary amyloidosis (AA type) and involve the kidneys.","t":"Bronchiectasis And Lung Abscess","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"A 58-year-old white man presents with a 6-month history of productive cough, weight loss, fever, and night sweats. HRCT shows a cluster of grapes appearance. A biopsy shows features of a non-tuberculous mycobacterial infection. Cystic fibrosis is ruled out. Which of the following drugs is not to be used in the treatment regimen?","o":["Macrolide","Ethambutol","Bronchodilators","Mucolytic dornase"],"a":3,"e":"The clinical scenario is suggestive of bronchiectasis secondary to a non-tuberculous mycobacterial (NTM) infection. Mucolytic dornase is recommended routinely in cystic fibrosis (CF)-related bronchiectasis but not in non-CF bronchiectasis.\n\nMacrolides (azithromycin or clarithromycin) combined with rifampicin and ethambutol are used in the treatment of non-CF bronchiectasis.\n\nTreatment of bronchiectasis:\n\n1. Antibiotic therapy: This is used to treat exacerbations and is given for 10-14 days. Commonly administered antibiotics include:\n\n• Amoxicillin or clarithromycin: First-line drugs\n\n• Ciprofloxacin: For Pseudomonas aeruginosa\n\n2. Airway clearance:\n\n• Postural drainage: This is done by gravity-assisted positioning of the patient and chest physiotherapy.\n\n• Mucolytic treatment: only in cystic fibrosis patients\n\n• Normal or hypertonic saline (≥3%) nebulization\n\n• Humidified air inhalation\n\n• Drugs like N-acetylcysteine or carbocysteine\n\n3. Others:\n\n• Inhaled bronchodilators like long-acting β2 agonists and anticholinergics\n\n• Long-term oxygen therapy: In patients with chronic respiratory failure\n\n• Pulmonary rehabilitation\n\n• Adequate hydration\n\n• Resection of a focal area of suppuration, lung transplantation: In refractory cases","t":"Bronchiectasis And Lung Abscess","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"A college student presents with complaints of fever, chronic productive cough, steatorrhea. There is a past history of recurrent lung infections and multiple hospitalizations with similar complaints. Which of the following is false about this condition?","o":["An autosomal-recessive disorder","Abnormality in CFTR gene leads to defective calcium transport","Most common mutation is F508 deletion","Males have infertility in adult life"],"a":1,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of cystic fibrosis. Abnormalities in cystic fibrosis transmembrane conductance regulator protein (CFTR) gene lead to defective chloride transport and not calcium transport.\n\nCystic fibrosis is an autosomal recessive disorder affecting the epithelial cells of various exocrine glands of the body. It is caused by mutations in the gene coding for CFTR protein. The most common CFTR mutation is F508 deletion.\n\nThe most prominent clinical features of cystic fibrosis are chronic respiratory diseases (fever, productive cough, recurrent lung infections) and pancreatic insufficiency (steatorrhea).\n\nMale patients with cystic fibrosis usually have infertility due to congenital absence of bilateral vas deferens and defective sperm transport, resulting in azoospermia.","t":"Bronchiectasis And Lung Abscess","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"A 17-year-old boy presents to the clinic with an acute respiratory tract infection. He has a history of asthma associated with recurrent respiratory tract infections and sinusitis. Genetic testing in childhood had revealed CFTR mutations. Which of the following is likely to be decreased in this patient?","o":["Sweat chloride","Serum bicarbonate level","Sperm count","Immunoreactive trypsinogen"],"a":2,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of cystic fibrosis. In these patients, sperm count is most likely to be decreased.\n\nMale patients with cystic fibrosis usually have infertility due to congenital absence of bilateral vas deferens and defective sperm transport, resulting in obstructive azoospermia.\n\nOption A: Sweat chloride levels are elevated (≥60 mEq/L) due to abnormalities in the CFTR protein leading to defective chloride transport.\n\nOption B: Serum bicarbonate levels are increased due to excessive loss of salt in the sweat resulting in hypochloremic alkalosis.\n\nOption D: Immunoreactive trypsinogen levels are increased due to exocrine pancreatic insufficiency. Measurement of these levels is a diagnostic screening test in newborns.","t":"Bronchiectasis And Lung Abscess","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"A young boy is being evaluated for cystic fibrosis. Sweat chloride shows values of 38 and 44 mEq/L (normal&lt;40). What is the next best test to confirm the diagnosis of cystic fibrosis?","o":["72-hour fecal fat","Immunoreactive trypsinogen levels","DNA analysis","Trans epithelial nasal potential difference"],"a":2,"e":"DNA analysis is used to confirm the diagnosis of cystic fibrosis in patients with equivocal (one normal and one abnormal value) or frankly normal sweat chloride values.\n\nThe diagnosis is confirmed if both of the following criteria are met:\n\n• Presence of\n\n• Clinical symptoms affecting at least one organ system (respiratory, gastrointestinal, or genitourinary) or\n\n• Positive newborn screening or\n\n• A sibling with cystic fibrosis\n\n• Evidence of dysfunction of the CFTR gene by\n\n• Two positive sweat chloride test results obtained on separate days or\n\n• Identification of two CFTR mutations by genetic testing or\n\n• An abnormal measurement of nasal potential difference\n\nOption A: 72-hour fecal fat is not used in the diagnosis of CF.\n\nOption B: Measuring the levels of immunoreactive trypsinogen is a screening test in newborns.\n\nOption D: Trans epithelial nasal potential difference measurement can be used as an adjunct to sweat test, but it has limited availability and is predominantly used as a research tool.","t":"Bronchiectasis And Lung Abscess","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"A 15-year-old boy has been having recurrent hospitalizations for infections, since 2 years of age. After a detailed evaluation, a diagnosis of Kartagener's syndrome was made. Which of the following features is not a component of the triad seen in this condition?","o":["Situs inversus totalis","Chronic sinusitis","Male infertility","Bronchiectasis"],"a":2,"e":"Male infertility is typical in Kartagener syndrome, but not a component of the triad.\n\nKartagener’s syndrome is primary ciliary dyskinesia characterized by the classical triad of:\n\n• Situs inversus\n\n• Chronic sinusitis\n\n• Bronchiectasis\n\nPrimary ciliary dyskinesia (also called immotile cilia syndrome) is an autosomal recessive disorder affecting ciliary motility. It occurs due to ultrastructural defects in the dynein arm of cilia. This causes the retention of secretions due to a failed mucociliary clearance mechanism. Clinically, this manifests as chronic sinusitis and recurrent infections that ultimately lead to bronchiectasis.\n\nAs ciliary function is necessary to ensure proper rotation of the developing organs in the chest and abdomen, its absence, will lead to situs inversus or a partial lateralizing abnormality.\n\nMale patients with this condition tend to be infertile, due to asthenospermia (immotile spermatozoa due to impaired sperm flagella function).","t":"Bronchiectasis And Lung Abscess","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"Which is the most common cause of lung abscess?","o":["Tuberculosis","Pneumonia","Aspirated oropharyngeal secretion","Sepsis"],"a":2,"e":"The most common cause of lung abscess is aspirated oropharyngeal secretion.\n\nLung abscess represents necrosis and cavitation of the lung following microbial infection. They are broadly divided into two categories:\n\n1. Primary lung abscesses: usually arise from aspiration, often caused principally by anaerobic bacteria (most common)\n\n2. Secondary lung abscesses: arise in the setting of an underlying condition, such as a post-obstructive process (e.g., a bronchial foreign body or tumor) or a systemic process (e.g., HIV infection or another immunocompromising condition)","t":"Bronchiectasis And Lung Abscess","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"A bed-ridden patient with a Ryles tube tried to pull out the tube. This led to aspiration following which he developed fever with chills and cough. X-ray findings were suggestive of lung abscess. Which of the following pathogens is unlikely to have caused it?","o":["Bacteroides","Streptococcus milleri","Microaerophilic streptococci","Staphylococcus aureus"],"a":3,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of a primary lung abscess. Staphylococcus is involved in the formation of secondary lung abscess, not primary.\n\nMicrobial pathogens causing lung abscess:\n\nClinical conditions\n\nPathogens\n\nPrimary lung abscess (due to aspiration)\n\nAnaerobes (e.g., Peptostreptococcus spp., Prevotella spp., Bacteroides spp., Streptococcus milleri), microaerophilic streptococci\n\nSecondary lung abscess (due to underlying conditions)\n\nStaphylococcus aureus, gram-negative rods (e.g., Pseudomonas aeruginosa, Enterobacteriaceae), Nocardia spp., Aspergillus spp., Legionella spp., Pneumocystis jirovecii\n\nEmbolic lesions\n\nStaphylococcus aureus (often from endocarditis), Fusobacterium necrophorum (Lemierre’s disease)","t":"Bronchiectasis And Lung Abscess","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"A 64-year-old man was admitted to the emergency department after being found unconscious due to alcohol intoxication. On physical examination, his dental hygiene was poor. Chest auscultation revealed fixed amphoric breath sounds over the right hemithorax. Which of the following lung lobes is most likely to be affected?","o":["Posterior upper lobe","Anterior upper lobe","Apical upper lobe","Posterior lower lobe"],"a":0,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of a primary lung abscess due to aspiration. Most common lobes involved in primary lung abscess are posterior upper lobes and superior lower lobes, given the predisposition of aspirated materials to be deposited in these areas.\n\nThe right lung is affected more commonly than the left because the right mainstem bronchus is less angulated.\n\nIn secondary abscesses, the location of the abscess may vary with the underlying cause.","t":"Bronchiectasis And Lung Abscess","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"A middle-aged man presents with persistent fever and productive cough for 6 weeks with night sweats and anemia. The following is his CXR. What is the likely diagnosis?","o":["Carcinoma Lung","Tuberculosis","Emphysematous bullae","Lung abscess"],"a":3,"e":"The clinical scenario described along with the chest X-ray image showing air-fluid level is suggestive of a lung abscess.\n\nThe patients usually present with complaints of fever, cough with sputum production, and chest pain. Patients with putrid lung abscesses may report foul-smelling sputum. Patients with chronic lung abscesses usually have a history of night sweats, fatigue, and anemia.\n\nOn physical examination:\n\n• Poor dentition/gingival disease\n\n• Amphoric and/or cavernous breath sounds on lung auscultation\n\n• Digital clubbing\n\n• The absence of a gag reflex\n\nCXR features of lung abscess:\n\n• A lung cavity with an air-fluid level\n\n• The cavity wall is thick and irregular, and a surrounding pulmonary-infiltrate is often present\n\n• Computed tomography (CT) is more sensitive than chest radiography and is used to detect small cavities\n\nThe two x-ray images given below show thick-walled cavities with air-fluid levels on lateral and PA views respectively.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8329674ec81149899d5fd8072810c0dc.PNG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f43b3bae31474f25a8d16af8e5e76e2ex624x1150.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/032da72a34064443be803fb65cb6905ax1280x960.PNG"],"t":"Bronchiectasis And Lung Abscess","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"A 60-year-old male patient presented with 2 weeks of productive cough, fever, and shortness of breath. A chest radiogram was done as shown below. Which of the following is the most appropriate therapy for him?","o":["Cephalexin","Ciprofloxacin","Clindamycin","Vancomycin"],"a":2,"e":"In the above clinical scenario, the patient most likely has a lung abscess as depicted by the radiograph. Clindamycin is the drug of choice in the treatment of lung abscesses.\n\nWhen there are contraindications to clindamycin, a combination of penicillin and metronidazole is likely to be as effective as clindamycin.\n\nAnaerobes of oral origin are the most common causative organism and require broad-spectrum antibiotic coverage. Vancomycin, ciprofloxacin, and cephalexin have no significant activity against anaerobes.\n\nBelow is a chest X-ray showing a lung abscess (yellow dotted circle) with an air-fluid level (arrow).","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/cefa96e10afc4421928f61cee7ffb1a1.JPEG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f5a87f32c10c49f08fae8321ce628a8fx1280x1172.JPEG"],"t":"Bronchiectasis And Lung Abscess","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"A patient with fever, productive cough, night sweats, and clubbing is diagnosed with lung abscess. Which of the following is an unlikely complication of this condition?","o":["Bronchiectasis","Brain abscess","Lung carcinoma","Hemoptysis"],"a":2,"e":"Lung carcinoma is unlikely in patients with lung abscesses. Lung carcinoma may cause lung abscess (in 10-15% of cases) but lung abscess is not a premalignant condition.\n\nComplications of lung abscess:\n\n• Empyema\n\n• Pneumatocele\n\n• Bronchopleural fistula\n\n• Bronchiectasis\n\n• Metastatic infections like brain abscess\n\n• Life-threatening hemoptysis\n\n• Massive aspiration of lung abscess contents","t":"Bronchiectasis And Lung Abscess","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"Which of the following tests is not used to diagnose a patient with suspected allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis?","o":["Peripheral eosinophilia","Serum IgE &gt; 1000 IU/ml","Positive skin prick test","Culture of Aspergillus fumigatus from sputum"],"a":3,"e":"The culture of Aspergillus fumigatus from sputum is not a diagnostic criterion for allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis (ABPA). The sensitivity of sputum culture in ABPA is low.\n\nABPA is diagnosed serologically, based on the presence of:\n\n• Serum IgE > 1000 IU/ml.\n\n• Positive skin prick test or IgE levels against A. fumigatus extract.\n\n• Detection of Aspergillus-specific antibodies.\n\n• Eosinophilia >500 cells/μL.","t":"Bronchiectasis And Lung Abscess","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"A patient with acute myeloid leukemia underwent induction therapy. Within the next few days, he developed a cough and fever. On evaluation, HRCT showed a halo sign. Eventually, the patient deteriorated and died due to respiratory compromise. On autopsy, lung histopathology showed infarction and invasion of blood vessels by Aspergillus hyphae. Which of the following is not true about this condition?","o":["Fungal hyphae are broad and aseptate","Yeast forms are not seen in infected tissue","Most of the patients are asymptomatic","Exposure to hay barns is a risk factor."],"a":0,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of aspergillosis. Fungal hyphae of Aspergillus species are narrow, septate, and have branching at 45o.\n\nYeast forms of Aspergillus are not seen in infected tissue.\n\nExposure to the high number of conidia that are found in construction sites, hay barns, moldy barks, and composts is a risk factor.\n\nClinical features:\n\n• Most of the patients are asymptomatic\n\n• Fever\n\n• Cough\n\n• Chest discomfort\n\n• Trivial hemoptysis\n\n• Shortness of breath\n\nHalo sign-on HRCT refers to a localized ground glass appearance representing an area of hemorrhagic infarction surrounding a nodule. It is seen in invasive aspergillosis, a distinct entity where there is a direct invasion of hyphae into the lung tissue.\n\nThe air crescent sign describes the crescent of air that can be seen in invasive aspergillosis. It usually heralds recovery and is the result of increased granulocyte activity.\n\nMonod sign refers to the gas that surrounds a mycetoma (most commonly an aspergilloma) in a pre-existing pulmonary cavity.","t":"Bronchiectasis And Lung Abscess","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"A 66-year-old smoker presented with chronic productive cough and shortness of breath. Physical examination revealed a barrel chest and an expiratory wheeze on auscultation. All of the following findings would be seen in his pulmonary function tests except?","o":["Normal residual volume","Decreased FVC","Normal vital capacity","Decreased FEV1/FVC"],"a":0,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD). It is characterized by an increased residual volume.\n\nAccording to Uptodate, decreased vital capacity and increased total lung capacity is indicative of hyperinflation seen in COPD. FVC can be used a proxy for VC in normal individuals but in COPD FVC is less than VC due to air trapping, airflow limitation, and small airway collapse.","t":"Pulmonary Function Tests","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"An 8-year-old boy presents with intermittent wheeze and cough. His mother reports that these episodes are precipitated by physical exercise. There is a history of atopic dermatitis in his mother. Pulmonary function tests are performed. Which of the following findings are least likely to be seen in him?","o":["Decreased forced expiratory volume in one second","Increased residual volume","Decreased mean expiratory flow rate","Decreased diffusion capacity"],"a":3,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of asthma. The diffusing capacity of the lungs for carbon monoxide (DLCO) tends to be normal or high in these patients.","t":"Pulmonary Function Tests","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"Decreased maximum mid-expiratory flow rate is seen in obstruction of:","o":["Medium airways","Large airways","Small airways","Trachea"],"a":2,"e":"Decreased maximum mid-expiratory flow rate (MMFR) is seen in obstruction of small airways.\n\nMMFR is the average expiratory flow rate during the middle 50% of the vital capacity i.e., forced expiratory flow rate between 25 and 75% of the vital capacity.\n\nThis is the most sensitive measurement of early airflow obstruction, particularly in small airways.","t":"Pulmonary Function Tests","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"Patients with which of the following conditions is likely to have pulmonary function test findings as given below:\n\n PFT\n Observed\n Predicted\n\n FVC\n 60%\n 80%\n\n FEV1\n 70%\n >80%\n\n FEV1/FVC\n 1.1\n 0.7","o":["Bronchiectasis","Bronchiolitis","Pleural effusion","COPD"],"a":2,"e":"Low FEV1 and FVC with a high FEV1/FVC ratio are characteristics of restrictive lung disease. Among the options, pleural effusion is a restrictive lung disease.\n\n• Obstructive lung diseases\n\n• Asthma\n\n• COPD\n\n• Bronchiectasis\n\n• Bronchiolitis\n\n• Restrictive lung diseases\n\n• Intrinsic\n\n• Interstitial lung disease\n\n• Extrinsic\n\n• Myasthenia gravis\n\n• Kyphoscoliosis\n\n• Pleural effusion","t":"Pulmonary Function Tests","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"A 76-year-old retired worker from a granite quarry presents with progressive dyspnea and a chronic cough. He has never smoked cigarettes. His chest X-ray is shown below. Which of the following patterns would you see on his pulmonary function tests?","o":["FEV1/FVC decreases, compliance decreases","FEV1/FVC increases and compliance increases","FEV1/FVC decreases and compliance increases","FEV1/FVC increases and compliance decreases"],"a":3,"e":"The chest X-ray shows calcification of the hilar lymph nodes called eggshell calcification. The clinical scenario along with the chest X-ray is suggestive of silicosis. It is a restrictive lung disease characterized by increased FEV1/FVC and decreased compliance.\n\nIt is an occupational interstitial lung disease.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/dcde655455f14c169eafefd8b654f137.PNG"],"t":"Pulmonary Function Tests","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"An elderly woman presents with breathlessness on exertion. On auscultation, bilateral basal rales are heard. A chest CT is done and is shown below. Which of the following is likely to be increased in her?","o":["Total vital capacity","Forced vital capacity","Lung compliance","A-a gradient"],"a":3,"e":"The given clinical scenario along with the honeycomb pattern of lung parenchyma due to interstitial fibrosis is suggestive of interstitial lung disease (ILD). The alveolar-arterial gradient (PAO2-PaO2) is increased in ILD. It is a restrictive lung disease.\n\nIn ILD, the alveolar-capillary bed is damaged and thus there is a decrease in diffusion capacity. Therefore, while the PAO2 (Alveolar O2) remains normal, the PaO2 (arterial) decreases, and the alveolar-arterial difference increases.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d7b9235aabc742e7abcf6c0a9fc8b85c.JPEG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/eef830d2d0f94fab86d0f1c385c6ab06x1200x2175.JPEG"],"t":"Pulmonary Function Tests","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"The diffusing capacity of the lungs for carbon monoxide is decreased in patients with:","o":["Alveolar haemorrhage","Congestive heart failure","Asthma","Emphysema"],"a":3,"e":"Diffusing capacity of the lungs for carbon monoxide (DLCO) is decreased in patients with emphysema.\n\nDLCO measures the ability of the lungs to transfer gas from inhaled air in the alveoli to the red blood cells in pulmonary capillaries.\n\nIn general, the DLCO is used to identify the cause of dyspnea or hypoxemia. It is also used to monitor disease progression in interstitial lung disease, and identify pulmonary hypertension in patients at risk, such as those with systemic sclerosis.\n\nDLCO is decreased in patients with:\n\n• Interstitial lung disease\n\n• Emphysema (COPD usually have normal DLCO)\n\n• Bronchiolitis obliterans\n\n• Pulmonary hypertension\n\n• Thromboembolic disease\n\n• Chronic congestive Heart Failure\n\nDLCO is increased in patients with:\n\n• Alveolar hemorrhage\n\n• Acute Congestive heart failure\n\n• Asthma","t":"Pulmonary Function Tests","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"A 30-year-old woman presents with shortness of breath and fatigue. She has a medical history of limited cutaneous systemic sclerosis. Her pulmonary function test results are given below. What is the most probable diagnosis?","o":["Pulmonary artery hypertension","Interstitial lung disease","Congestive heart failure","Bronchiectasis"],"a":0,"e":"The above PFT pattern shows near-normal FVC, increased FEV1%, and markedly decreased DLCO. This along with the given history is suggestive of pulmonary artery hypertension(PAH). It is more commonly associated with limited cutaneous systemic sclerosis.\n\nOption B: Interstitial lung disease is a restrictive lung disease. In ILD, the FVC is usually,<80% of the predicted value. It is more commonly associated with diffuse cutaneous systemic sclerosis.\n\nOptions C and D: Congestive heart failure and bronchiectasis have obstructive pathology. They would have decreased FEV1% and normal DLCO.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ce1a98738515471db75429cf0dae9e35.JPEG"],"t":"Pulmonary Function Tests","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"A flow-volume loop of a patient is given below. What is the likely diagnosis?","o":["Pulmonary edema","Interstitial lung disease","Tracheal stenosis","Emphysema"],"a":2,"e":"The likely diagnosis is tracheal stenosis. The flow-volume loop shows flattened inspiratory and expiratory curves, suggestive of fixed upper-airway obstruction.\n\nA normal flow-volume loop begins on the X-axis (Volume axis): at the start of the test, both flow and volume are equal to zero. After the starting point, the curve rapidly mounts to a peak: Peak expiratory flow (PEF).\n\nThen, the PEF the curve descends (=the flow decreases) as more air is expired. A normal, non-pathological F/V loop will descend in a straight or a convex line from the top (PEF) to the bottom (FVC).\n\nThe forced inspiration that follows the forced expiration has roughly the same morphology, but the PIF (Peak Inspiratory Flow) is not as distinct as PEF.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/07e927b0582c4cf1a8085b7c3f99fb63.JPEG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ebba99f0060f4851881c61e92cf1a4f0x1280x4291.JPEG"],"t":"Pulmonary Function Tests","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is seen in patients with brain stem injury?","o":["Low pO2 and high pCO2","Normal pO2 and high pCO2","Low pO2 and low pCO2","Low pO2 and normal pCO2"],"a":0,"e":"Patients with brain stem injury develop type 2 respiratory failure. It is characterized by low pO2 and high pCO2.\n\nAcute respiratory failure refers to acute impairment in gas exchange between the lungs and the blood causing hypoxia with or without hypercapnia.","t":"Respiratory Failure And Ards","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"A 45-year-old man underwent emergency surgery following a road traffic accident. The surgery was performed under general anesthesia. Two days later, he developed features of respiratory failure. Which of the following types is likely to be present in him?","o":["Type 1","Type 2","Type 3","Type 4"],"a":2,"e":"The features of respiratory failure in the perioperative period of this patient is most likely due to lung atelectasis. Type 3 respiratory failure is most likely to be present in him.\n\nType 3 respiratory failure is also known as perioperative respiratory failure.\n\nAfter general anesthesia, there is a decrease in functional residual capacity due to a reduction in muscular tone. This leads to airway closure and the collapse of the dependent lung units.\n\nAtelectasis can be treated by frequent changes in position, chest physiotherapy, upright positioning, and control of incisional and abdominal pain. Non-invasive positive-pressure ventilation may also be used to reverse regional atelectasis.","t":"Respiratory Failure And Ards","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"A patient is admitted for myocardial infarction complicated by severe mitral regurgitation. He suddenly develops shortness of breath, decreased urine output, and drowsiness. Blood pressure is 86/40 mmHg, pulse 120 bpm, and respiratory rate 24 per minute. Which of the following statements is false?","o":["Up to 40% of cardiac output may be distributed to the respiratory muscles","Patient will develop type 2 respiratory failure","Intubation and mechanical ventilation are treatment of choice","Batwing appearance can be expected on an x-ray"],"a":1,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of cardiogenic shock. He is likely to develop type 4 respiratory failure, not type 2.\n\nPatients in cardiogenic shock often have respiratory distress secondary to acute pulmonary edema (batwing appearance on x-ray). This increases the requirement of oxygen delivery to the respiratory muscles from <5% to up to 40% of cardiac output.\n\nHypoperfusion and increased oxygen requirement lead to inadequate perfusion of respiratory muscles which causes type 4 respiratory failure.\n\nIntubation and mechanical ventilation are the treatment of choice.","t":"Respiratory Failure And Ards","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"A 47-year-old construction site worker is admitted for fever, nausea, vomiting, and pain abdomen radiating to the back. The next day he develops sudden onset breathlessness and hypoxia. He has been drinking daily for the past 10 years. He had a myocardial infarction 6 months back. His chest X-ray is given below. What is the most probable diagnosis?","o":["Interstitial lung disease","ARDS","Hospital acquired pneumonia","Left ventricular failure"],"a":1,"e":"The given clinical scenario along with the chest X-ray finding is suggestive of ARDS (acute respiratory distress syndrome) secondary to acute pancreatitis. Acute pancreatitis gives rise to inflammatory processes and endothelial damage that results in ARDS.\n\nCXR features in ARDS:\n\n• Early findings - normal or diffuse alveolar opacities (consolidation), bilateral and which obscure the pulmonary vascular markings\n\n• Late findings - these opacities progress to more extensive consolidation that is diffuse\n\nClinical disorders commonly associated with ARDS:\n\n• Direct lung injury:\n\n• Pneumonia\n\n• Aspiration of gastric contents\n\n• Pulmonary contusion\n\n• Near-drowning\n\n• Toxic inhalation injury\n\n• Indirect lung injury:\n\n• Sepsis\n\n• Severe trauma- multiple fractures, flail chest, head trauma, burns\n\n• Multiple transfusions\n\n• Drug overdose\n\n• Pancreatitis\n\n• Post-cardiopulmonary bypass","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ff2271a228f643c4ae5d1db9183c8628.PNG"],"t":"Respiratory Failure And Ards","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is least likely to be present in patients with ARDS?","o":["Decreased pulmonary compliance","Increased pulmonary artery pressure","Increased left atrial pressure","PCWP &lt;18 mm Hg"],"a":2,"e":"Increased left atrial pressure is not likely to be present in ARDS.\n\nARDS is characterized by\n\n• PCWP of ≤18 mmHg with normal LA pressure\n\n• Decreased pulmonary compliance\n\n• Increased pulmonary artery pressure\n\n• Severe hypoxemia","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1f5811521ca64b0294bc67e57e96fba2x1280x1016.JPEG"],"t":"Respiratory Failure And Ards","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"A fisherman was rushed to the ED following a near-drowning incident. On arrival, he was unconscious and was not breathing. He was resuscitated and stabilized. A few hours later, he developed sudden onset breathlessness and low saturation. Chest X-ray showed diffuse alveolar opacities. He was started on mechanical ventilation. What will be the recommended ventilator setting for him?","o":["Low tidal volume and high PEEP","Low tidal volume and low PEEP","High tidal volume and high PEEP","High tidal volume and low PEEP"],"a":0,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of ARDS secondary to drowning. The ventilator setting recommended is low tidal volume and high PEEP.\n\nDrowning causes direct lung injury. The aspiration of water into the lungs damages the surfactant. This disrupts the alveolar-capillary membrane and leads to alveolar edema, resulting in ARDS.\n\nThe ventilator settings recommended in ARDS are:\n\n• Low tidal volume: To avoid barotrauma due to overdistention of the alveoli.\n\n• High PEEP (positive end-expiratory pressure): This prevents the collapse of alveoli at the end of the expiration and improves oxygenation.","t":"Respiratory Failure And Ards","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is false regarding lung cancer?","o":["It is the most common cancer in Indian men","Tobacco smoking is the most common cause","Adenocarcinoma is the most common histologic type","It is the most common cause of cancer death"],"a":0,"e":"The most common cancer seen in Indian men is lip and oral cavity cancer, not lung cancer.\n\nMost common cancer:\n\n• In men globally- Lung cancer\n\n• In Indian men- Lip and oral cavity cancer\n\n• In women globally- Breast cancer\n\n• In Indian women- Breast cancer\n\nTobacco smoking is a primary cause of the development of lung cancer. It increases 10 fold greater risk of developing lung cancer compared to those who have never smoked.\n\nIt is the most common cause of cancer death.","t":"Neoplasms Of The Lung","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"What is the most common type of lung cancer in light smokers?","o":["Squamous cell carcinoma","Adenocarcinoma","Small cell carcinoma","Large cell carcinoma"],"a":1,"e":"The most common lung cancer in light smokers (<10 pack-year history) is adenocarcinoma.\n\nWHO classification of lung carcinomas:\n\nThe WHO classification system divides epithelial lung cancers into four major cell types:\n\n• Small-cell lung cancer (SCLC)\n\n• Non-small-cell lung carcinomas (NSCLCs)\n\n• Adenocarcinoma\n\n• Squamous cell carcinoma\n\n• Large-cell carcinoma\n\nAdenocarcinoma is most commonly seen in:\n\n• Lifetime non-smokers\n\n• Light smokers (<10 pack-years history)\n\n• Women\n\n• Younger adults (<60 years)\n\nIn smokers, the most common types are small-cell carcinoma and squamous cell carcinomas.","t":"Neoplasms Of The Lung","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"A 60-year-old man with a 35 pack-year history of smoking presented for a routine check-up. His brother was diagnosed with lung cancer at a similar age. Which of the following would be preferred to screen him for lung cancer?","o":["Chest X-Ray","Low dose CT","High resolution CT","CECT"],"a":1,"e":"Low-dose CT/ thin-slice spiral chest CT would be preferred to screen him for lung cancer. He belongs to the high-risk population to develop lung cancer.\n\nThe high-risk population is individuals whose age is ≥60 years and who have a smoking history of ≥10 pack-years.\n\nLow dose CT scans are shown to detect more lung nodules and cancers than the standard CXR in selected high-risk populations. Also, up to 85% of cancers thus detected are classified as stage I. Early staged cancers are considered potentially curable with surgical resection.\n\nHere, the aim is to reduce medical radiation exposure as much as possible, low dose CT is effective in screening for lung cancer, and is thus preferred.","t":"Neoplasms Of The Lung","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is false about hypertrophic osteoarthropathy?","o":["Platelets have a role in the development of HOA","Primary HOA disappears in adulthood","HOA is commonly associated with lung malignancies","Skin changes are more common in secondary HOA"],"a":3,"e":"Skin changes are more common in primary HOA, not secondary HOA.\n\nHypertrophic osteoarthropathy is a condition characterized by the clubbing of the digits. In advanced stages, it is characterized by the periosteal new bone formation and synovial effusion. The exact mechanism of its development is unclear. However, platelets are said to have a role in the development of HOA via platelet-derived growth factor.\n\nThere are two types of HOA:\n\n Primary HOA\n Secondary HOA\n\nInherited autosomal dominant condition\n\nAcquired condition, secondary to\n\n• Lung neoplasms\n\n• Mesothelioma\n\n• Suppurative lung infections\n\n• Congenital heart disease\n\n• Thyroid acropachy\n\n• Inflammatory bowel disease\n\nSkin changes like thickening, seborrhea, excessive sweating are commonly seen\nSkin changes are unusual\n\nThe symptoms disappear in adulthood\nThe symptoms persist till the underlying cause is treated","t":"Neoplasms Of The Lung","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"A middle-aged non-smoker presented with a dry persistent cough and hemoptysis. A CT scan showed a mass in the periphery of his right lung. A biopsy was taken, cells showed an acinar pattern with mucin formation. The cells were positive for thyroid transcription factor 1 and napsin A. What other feature is most likely to be seen in this patient?","o":["Hypertrophic osteoarthropathy","Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome","Hypercalcemia","SIADH"],"a":0,"e":"The given clinical scenario along with the biopsy findings is suggestive of lung adenocarcinoma. It is most commonly associated with hypertrophic osteoarthropathy.\n\nAdenocarcinomas are mainly seen in the periphery of the lung. They grow in various patterns like acinar, lepidic, papillary, micropapillary, and solid with mucin formation. Cells are positive for markers like thyroid transcription factor 1 and napsin A.\n\nOption C: Hypercalcemia due to ectopic production of PTH-related peptide is seen in squamous cell carcinoma.\n\nOptions B and D: However, SIADH, Cushing’s syndrome, Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome are associated with small-cell lung carcinoma.","t":"Neoplasms Of The Lung","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"A female patient comes with complaints of unintentional weight loss and right shoulder pain for the past 6 months. She reports that the pain radiates to her arm and is not relieved by NSAIDs. Chest X-ray is given below. Which nerve roots are likely to be compressed?","o":["C5, C6","C8, T1, T2","C6. C7","C3, C4, C5"],"a":1,"e":"The given clinical scenario along with the X-ray findings is suggestive of Pancoast syndrome / superior sulcus tumor. This affects the C8, T1, and T2 nerve roots.\n\nIt presents with pain that radiates to the ulnar distribution of the arm, often with the destruction of the 1st and 2nd ribs. Patients with the central or endobronchial growth of the primary tumor may present with a cough, hemoptysis, wheeze, stridor, dyspnea, or post-obstructive pneumonitis.\n\nRegional spread can cause\n\n• Tracheal obstruction\n\n• Esophageal compression with dysphagia\n\n• Recurrent laryngeal paralysis with hoarseness\n\n• Phrenic nerve palsy with the elevation of the hemidiaphragm and dyspnea\n\n• Sympathetic nerve paralysis with Horner’s syndrome (enophthalmos, ptosis, miosis, and anhydrosis)","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/92fe9f5eea6549d48981cc92ffe0a458.JPEG"],"t":"Neoplasms Of The Lung","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"A COPD patient presented to the emergency with an acute exacerbation. On evaluation, a chest X-ray revealed a solitary pulmonary nodule. Which of the following patterns of calcification would suggest a likely malignancy in this patient?","o":["A dense central nidus","Popcorn calcification","Multiple punctate foci","Asymmetric calcification"],"a":3,"e":"Asymmetric calcification is suggestive of a malignant lesion.\n\n Benign lesion\n Malignant lesion\n\nLack of growth over a period of >2 years\nThe growth of the lesion is evident in comparison with an old X-ray\n\nPatterns of calcification:\n\n• Dense central nidus\n\n• Multiple punctate foci\n\n• Bull's eye granuloma\n\n• Popcorn ball hamartomas\n\nPatterns of calcification:\n\n• Asymmetric\n\n• Lack of calcification\n\nAssociated chest symptoms are uncommon\nchest symptoms like cough, hemoptysis are common\n\nComplications are uncommon\nComplications like atelectasis, pneumonitis, and effusion are common","t":"Neoplasms Of The Lung","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"What is the treatment of choice in patients with adenocarcinoma of the lung with EGFR mutation?","o":["Bevacizumab","Paclitaxel","Imatinib","Gefitinib"],"a":3,"e":"Adenocarcinomas with EGFR mutation are exquisitely sensitive to tyrosine kinase inhibitors of the EGFR signal pathway such as gefitinib and erlotinib.\n\nOption A: Bevacizumab is a monoclonal antibody against VEGF.\n\nOption B: Paclitaxel- a taxane, is a plant alkaloid and chemotherapeutic agent.\n\nOption C: Imatinib is a tyrosine kinase inhibitor effective in the treatment of CML and GIST.","t":"Neoplasms Of The Lung","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"A 55-year-old man presents with persistent cough and hemoptysis. The intern notices that he has moon-like facies and purple striae on his abdomen. On evaluation, a chest X-ray revealed a mass in his right lung. Which of the following type of lung carcinoma could be the cause of this patient's features?","o":["Adenocarcinoma","Bronchoalveolar carcinoma","Small cell carcinoma","Squamous cell carcinoma"],"a":2,"e":"In the given clinical scenario the patient has features of Cushing's syndrome, secondary to small-cell lung cancer. This occurs due to ectopic ACTH production. It can also seen in pulmonary carcinoids.\n\nMost effective management is treating underlying SCLC or carcinoid.","t":"Neoplasms Of The Lung","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"An elderly man was rushed to the ED with confusion and multiple episodes of vomiting. His wife reported that he had increased frequency of urination and constipation for the past 3 days. He is a known case of lung cancer. ECG showed a shortened QT interval. What could be the primary cause of his symptoms?","o":["Ectopic secretion of ACTH","Syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone secretion","Ectopic secretion of PTH related peptide","Tumor lysis syndrome"],"a":2,"e":"The primary cause of his symptoms is ectopic secretion of PTH related peptide. Lung cancers are associated with several paraneoplastic syndromes. Squamous cell lung cancer is associated with hypercalcemia due to ectopic PTH related peptide. Hypercalcemia may present as vomiting, abdominal pain, constipation, increased frequency of urination, kidney stones, bone pain and shortened QT interval.","t":"Neoplasms Of The Lung","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"A 62-year-old woman presents with vomiting, diarrhea, and decreased urine output since morning. On the previous day, she received chemotherapy for her stage 4 lung cancer. On examination, she is drowsy, HR-110/min and RR-30/min. All of the following biochemical findings can be seen in this patient except:","o":["Hyperuricemia","Hypercalcemia","Hyperkalemia","Hyperphosphatemia"],"a":1,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of tumor lysis syndrome (TLS). It is associated with hypocalcemia, not hypercalcemia.\n\nTLS is caused by the destruction of a large number of rapidly proliferating neoplastic cells. It is usually seen within 1-5 days of receiving chemotherapy. It has also been observed with the administration of glucocorticoids, letrozole, tamoxifen, rituximab and gemtuzumab. Rarely, spontaneous necrosis of malignancies causes TLS.\n\nClinical features:\n\n• Nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, lethargy\n\n• Pruritus, renal colicky pain, oliguria\n\n• Muscle cramps, carpopedal spasm, tetany\n\nBiochemical findings:\n\n• Hyperuricemia\n\n• Hyperkalemia\n\n• Hypocalcemia\n\n• Hyperphosphatemia\n\nDue to these biochemical changes, there is an increased risk of arrhythmias, acidosis, and acute kidney injury.","t":"Neoplasms Of The Lung","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not used in the management of patients with tumor lysis syndrome?","o":["Sodium bicarbonate","Rasburicase","Aggressive hydration","Febuxostat"],"a":0,"e":"Sodium bicarbonate is not recommended in the treatment of tumor lysis syndrome (TLS).\n\nUrinary alkalinization by sodium bicarbonate decreases the solubility of xanthine, hypoxanthine, and calcium phosphate. This potentially increases the risk of intratubular crystallization.\n\nManagement of TLS includes:\n\n• Aggressive hydration\n\n• Allopurinol\n\n• Rasburicase - recombinant urate oxidase\n\n• Febuxostat - non-purine selective xanthine oxidase inhibitor","t":"Neoplasms Of The Lung","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"Which of the following patients with non-small lung carcinoma is a suitable candidate to undergo a potentially curative surgical resection:","o":["60-year-old smoker with a tumor within 2cm of carina","45-year-old woman with malignant pleural effusion","55-year-old non-smoker with tumor involving the main pulmonary artery","57-year old light smoker with ipsilateral mediastinal node metastases"],"a":3,"e":"Patients with ipsilateral mediastinal node metastases are suitable candidates for curative resection.\n\nIn patients with NSCLC, the following are contraindications to potentially curative resection:\n\n• Extra-thoracic metastases\n\n• Vocal cord involvement\n\n• Superior vena cava syndrome\n\n• Involvement of the main pulmonary artery\n\n• Cardiac tamponade\n\n• Phrenic nerve paralysis\n\n• Malignant pleural effusion\n\n• Tumor within 2 cm of the carina\n\n• Metastasis to the contralateral lung\n\n• Metastases to supraclavicular lymph nodes\n\n• Contralateral mediastinal node metastases","t":"Neoplasms Of The Lung","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"Sleep apnea is defined as a temporary pause in breathing during sleep lasting at least :","o":["30 seconds","20 seconds","15 seconds","10 seconds"],"a":3,"e":"Sleep apnea is defined as a temporary pause in breathing during sleep lasting at least 10 seconds.\n\nSleep apneas can be further divided into:\n\n1. Obstructive apneas - Sleep apnea with persistent respiratory effort.\n\n2. Central apneas - Sleep apnea with an absence of respiratory effort.\n\nThe gold standard for the diagnosis of obstructive sleep apnea/hypopnea syndrome (OSAHS) is an overnight polysomnogram (PSG).","t":"Sleep Apnea","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"A patient is referred to the sleep clinic for polysomnography. Which of the following is not a component of it?","o":["EEG","Skeletal muscle activation","Peripheral pulse oximetry","Arterial blood gases"],"a":3,"e":"Arterial blood gases are not part of polysomnography. However, they are usually measured to rule out alveolar hypoventilation secondary to lung disease or chest wall abnormalities.\n\nAn overnight polysomnogram is a multi-parametric test and is the gold standard for the diagnosis of obstructive sleep apnea. It includes:\n\n• EEG\n\n• Eye movements (EOG)\n\n• Muscle activity or skeletal muscle activation (EMG)\n\n• ECG\n\n• Respiratory airflow and respiratory effort indicators\n\n• Peripheral pulse oximetry\n\n• Sleep latency\n\n• Sleep efficiency\n\n• Arousal index\n\nOther sleep disorders diagnosed by polysomnography:\n\n• Narcolepsy\n\n• Idiopathic hypersomnia\n\n• Periodic limb movement disorder\n\n• REM behavior disorder\n\n• Parasomnias","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f42d580177c6405fbb97e3d1119839bfx1280x1919.JPEG"],"t":"Sleep Apnea","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"A middle-aged obese patient presented with complaints of daytime tiredness and decreased exercise tolerance. Echocardiology revealed reduced ejection fraction. The sleep study showed periods of apnea without respiratory efforts. Which of the following is not true regarding his condition?","o":["Transient cessation of neural drive to all respiratory muscles","Associated with Cheyne Stokes breathing","Is due to decreased sensitivity to pCO2","Can be induced by CPAP at high pressure"],"a":2,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of central sleep apnea (CSA), and it is caused by increased sensitivity to pCO2. Thus changes in pCO2 levels during sleep lead to an unstable breathing pattern that manifests as hyperventilation alternating with apnea. Crescendo-decrescendo breathing pattern known as Cheyne-Stokes respiration is seen.\n\nRisk factors for CSA:\n\n• Heart failure\n\n• Opioid medications\n\n• Hypoxia (e.g., breathing at high altitude)\n\n• Continuous positive airway pressure (CPAP) at high pressures seems to induce central apnea; this condition is referred to as complex sleep apnea.","t":"Sleep Apnea","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"Which of the following drugs is not a risk factor for the development of central sleep apnea?","o":["Opioids","Strychnine","Barbiturates","Gelsemium"],"a":1,"e":"Strychnine is not a risk factor as it does not cause respiratory depression. It is a CNS stimulant. Only drugs that cause respiratory centre depression are risk factors for the development of central sleep apnea.\n\nOption A: Opioids depress the respiratory centre in a dose-dependent manner.\n\nOption C: Barbiturates cause respiratory depression in high doses.\n\nOption D: Gelsemium is an ergot-type alkaloid and is a CNS depressant. Often used in homoeopathy, in large doses, paralysis and death from respiratory failure can occur.\n\nThe pink shaded area shows an apneic period without any respiratory efforts.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/54c34c118bf94acca18594a775ab350cx664x361.PNG"],"t":"Sleep Apnea","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"A patient comes to the sleep clinic and the following pattern is seen on polysomnography. What type of breathing pattern is it?","o":["Hyperventilation respiration","Cheyne-Stokes respiration","Kussmaul's respiration","Biot's respiration"],"a":1,"e":"The breathing pattern shown in the image is suggestive of Cheyne-Stokes respiration. It is characterized by progressively deeper and sometimes faster breathing, followed by a gradual decrease that results in a temporary stop in breathing called apnea.\n\nIt is caused by an increased sensitivity of the respiratory center to arterial PCO2. There is an apneic phase, during which arterial PO2 falls and arterial PCO2 rises. These changes in the arterial blood gas content stimulate the respiratory center, resulting in hyperventilation and hypocapnia, followed by recurrence of apnea. It is present in 40% of patients with advanced HF and usually is associated with low cardiac output.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4a511af3d36e414390406b6a5b62c1a3.JPEG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4487a95715b74bbfab7d2f4f2aae2bd4x1280x2311.JPEG"],"t":"Sleep Apnea","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"A 40-year-old man presents with daytime sleepiness and impaired concentration and memory. On examination, his BMI is 41 kg/m2, blood pressure is 160/100 mm of Hg. His awake arterial blood gas analysis is given below. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nParameter\nValue\n\nPaO2\n66mmHg\n\nPaCO2\n50mmHg\n\nHCO3\n30mEq/L","o":["Obstructive sleep apnea","Narcolepsy","Obesity hypoventilation syndrome","Central sleep apnea"],"a":2,"e":"The most probable diagnosis of this patient is obesity hypoventilation syndrome.\n\nObesity hypoventilation syndrome (OHS) or Pickwickian syndrome is defined as the presence of awake alveolar hypoventilation in an obese individual which cannot be attributed to other conditions associated with alveolar hypoventilation.\n\nPatients with OHS are obese and give a history of daytime hypersomnolence, loud snoring, choking during sleep, fatigue, impaired concentration, and memory. On examination usually, there is a small oropharynx and a thick neck.\n\nOHS may be associated with the following findings:\n\n• Elevated serum bicarbonate (>27 mEq/L)\n\n• Hypercapnia (PaCO2 >45 mmHg)\n\n• Hypoxemia (PaO2 <70 mmHg)\n\n• Polycythemia\n\nOptions A and D: In obstructive sleep apnea and central sleep apnea, the awake ABG values are usually normal.\n\nOption B: There are no ABG changes in narcolepsy.","t":"Sleep Apnea","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"A 55-year-old man is brought to the OPD by his wife for gasping episodes during sleep and excessive daytime sleepiness. She reported that he even dozed off while driving yesterday and nearly caused an accident. He is being treated for hypertension, gastro-oesophageal reflux, and type 2 diabetes. What else would be the ideal therapy for his symptoms?","o":["Supplemental oxygenation therapy","Uvulopalatopharyngoplasty","Continuous positive airway pressure therapy","Implantable neuro-stimulation of the hypoglossal nerve"],"a":2,"e":"The given clinical scenario suggests a diagnosis of obstructive sleep apnea/hypopnea syndrome (OSAHS). The ideal therapy is continuous positive airway pressure therapy (CPAP).\n\nOption A: Supplemental oxygenation can improve oxygen saturation but there is little evidence that it improves OSAHS symptoms.\n\nOption B: Uvulopalatopharyngoplasty is the most common surgery done in OSAHS but it is reserved for patients who cannot tolerate CPAP.\n\nOption D: Unilateral neuro-stimulation of the hypoglossal nerve through a surgically implanted device is a recently tested therapy for OSAHS. It can be used as an alternative in selected patients who cannot tolerate CPAP.","t":"Sleep Apnea","c":"Respiratory Medicine"},
 {"q":"Urine sample of a patient has been collected for analysis. How soon should it be analyzed?","o":["Within 6-8 hours","Within 2-4 hours","Within 4-6 hours","Within 1-2 hours"],"a":1,"e":"Urine samples should be analyzed within 2-4 hours after collection to avoid cell lysis and precipitation of solutes. If urine analysis is not possible in the given time, then the sample can be stored at 4-8 oC for a maximum of 12 hours.\n\nUrine samples can be collected using the following techniques:\n\n• Midstream sample during spontaneous voiding\n\n• Fresh specimen from an indwelling catheter\n\n• Suprapubic needle aspiration of the bladder, especially in infants","t":"Urine Analysis","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"All of the following determine the color of urine except:","o":["Chemical content","Concentration","pH","Solubility of solutes"],"a":3,"e":"The solubility of solutes is not a determining factor of the color of urine. Determinants of the color of urine are:\n\n• Chemical content\n\n• Concentration\n\n• pH (Normal pH is 4.5-8)\n\nThe normal color of urine is pale straw due to the presence of urochrome.","t":"Urine Analysis","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following drugs can cause green-colored urine in a patient?","o":["Triamterene","Rifampicin","Desferoxamine","Imipenem"],"a":0,"e":"Green urine can be caused by triamterene, a potassium-sparing diuretic.","t":"Urine Analysis","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"What value of urine specific gravity is called isosthenuria?","o":["1.020","1.010","1.015","1.025"],"a":1,"e":"Isosthenuria is defined as a urine specific gravity of 1.010, which is similar to that of plasma. The normal specific gravity of urine is around 1.020 to 1.030.\n\nSpecific gravity and osmolality are measures of the concentration or relative density of urine.\n\n• Specific gravity: The weight of a solution relative to that of equal amounts of water. It is measured by refractometry or using reagent strips.\n\n• Osmolality: The gold standard for relative density. It is defined as the number of osmoles of solute per kilogram of solvent. It is measured directly with an osmometer.\n\nIsosthenuria means that the kidney is neither concentrating nor diluting the urine. This is seen in chronic renal failure (CRF) and acute tubular necrosis (ATN).\n\nAbnormality\nCauses\n\nIsosthenuria\n\nCRF, ATN\n\nHyposthenuria (low specific gravity)\nDiabetes insipidus\n\nHypersthenuria (high specific gravity)\n\nSIADH, Severe dehydration","t":"Urine Analysis","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"A patient is being evaluated for the following appearance of his urine. A urine dipstick test is performed and the result is negative. What is the likely diagnosis?","o":["Myoglobinuria","Haemoglobinuria","Porphyria","Urolithiasis"],"a":2,"e":"The given image shows reddish discoloration of the urine sample. A negative urine dipstick indicates absence of RBCs and is suggestive of porphyria.\n\nA urine dipstick test is the first step to be done in a patient with high coloured urine to help identify true hematuria. A positive dipstick test identifies heme that is present in red blood cells, haemoglobin or myoglobin. A negative dipstick test implies that the reddish discolouration is due to pigments from certain food items (beetroot), drugs or porphyrias.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a752673ac55a48a1b2b9d0f6e883eac5.JPEG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2561cc7741834852b75af241659fa2d6x1280x2287.JPEG"],"t":"Urine Analysis","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following patients are considered to have significant hematuria based on their urine analysis findings?\n\n1. A 30-year-old patient with RBC >3 / HPF on 2 occasions\n\n2. A 23-year-old woman with RBC >100 / HPF on 1 occasion\n\n3. A 56-year-old man with RBC >10 / HPF on 1 occasion\n\n4. An 18-year-old student with RBC >3 / HPF on 3 occasions\n\n5. A 40-year-old patient with gross hematuria","o":["1,2,3,4 and 5","2,4 and 5","1 and 4","2, 3 and 5"],"a":1,"e":"Hematuria is defined as the presence of 2-5 RBCs / high-powered field (HPF). It is considered significant or persistent in the presence of one of the following:\n\n• RBCs > 3 / HPF on 3 or more occasions\n\n• RBCs > 100 / HPF on 1 occasion\n\n• Gross hematuria","t":"Urine Analysis","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"A patient with hematuria, generalized edema, and abdominal distension is suspected to have a glomerular pathology. Which of the following is likely to be seen in the urine?","o":["Bright red cells","Acanthocytes","Beta-2 microglobulin","Waxy casts"],"a":1,"e":"Acanthocytes are a subtype of dysmorphic red blood cells with protruding blebs that are seen in glomerular disease.\n\nThe 40-5-1 rule can be used to identify glomerular diseases based on urinalysis:\n\n• Dysmorphic RBCs - 40% (can range from 10-80%)\n\n• Acanthocytes - 5% (can range from 2-5%)\n\n• Even a single RBC cast\n\nHematuria can be either due to systemic or renal causes. Renal causes can be further subdivided into glomerular and non-glomerular causes as given below:\n\nGlomerular\nNon-glomerular\n\nDysmorphic RBCs seen (abnormal shape and size)\n\nIsomorphic RBCs seen (normal shape and size)\n\nRBC casts may be present\n\nRBC casts always absent\n\nMCV is reduced (<72 fl)\n\nMCV is normal\n\nProtein excretion > 1g /day\n\nProtein excretion < 1g /day\n\nThe cause is usually medical\n\nThe cause is usually surgical","t":"Urine Analysis","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"An 18-year-old boy presents to the clinic with a history of malaise. He was diagnosed with mild sensorineural hearing loss nearly 10 years ago. He also has a history of recurrent hematuria. What is the likely diagnosis?","o":["Fanconi’s anemia","Alport syndrome","Autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease","IgA nephropathy"],"a":1,"e":"This clinical scenario is suggestive of Alport syndrome. The clinical features include:\n\n• Recurrent hematuria and nephritic syndrome\n\n• Sensorineural hearing loss\n\n• Anterior lenticonus\n\n Other causes of recurrent hematuria include:\n\n• Alport syndrome\n\n• IgA nephropathy\n\n• Autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease (ADPKD)\n\n• Papillary necrosis\n\n• Renal cell carcinoma","t":"Urine Analysis","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is true about proteinuria?","o":["Proteinuria is always due to glomerular pathology","Albuminuria upto 150 mg/day is considered normal","Nephrotic range proteinuria is &gt; 3.5 g/day","Proteins are reabsorbed in the DCT"],"a":2,"e":"Nephrotic-range or heavy proteinuria refers to excretion of > 3.5 g/day or > 1000 mg/m2/day in children.\n\nType of Proteinuria\n\n Total proteinuria (mg/day)\n\nAlbuminuria (mg/day)\n\nNormal-range\n\n<150\n\nMainly Tamm-Horsfall protein (70-75 mg/day)\n\n< 30\n\nModerately increased\n150-500\n30-300\n\nSeverely increased\n> 500\n> 300\n\nOrthostatic proteinuria\n150-500\n —\n\nNormally, the following mechanisms prevent proteinuria:\n\n• The glomerular basement membrane acts as a barrier to prevent the loss of negatively charged and large proteins.\n\n• Reabsorption of small proteins in the proximal convoluted tubule (PCT).\n\nThe various types of proteinuria are:\n\n1. Glomerular - increased filtration of proteins due to disruption of basement membrane\n\n2. Tubular - damage to tubules prevents protein reabsorption in the PCT\n\n3. Overflow - excess production of low molecular weight proteins such as light chains\n\n4. Postrenal - infections or stones can lead to secretion of IgA and IgG by the urinary tract","t":"Urine Analysis","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"All of the following findings in a patient are suggestive of non-glomerular proteinuria except:","o":["Proteinuria &lt; 1 g/day","Type of protein excreted is β2-microglobulin","Albumin : β2-microglobulin ratio is reduced","Absence of N-acetyl glucosaminidase"],"a":3,"e":"Non-glomerular proteinuria is characterized by the presence of N-acetyl glucosaminidase (NAG).\n\nThis form of proteinuria occurs as a result of tubular injury. N-acetyl glucosaminidase (NAG) is a lysosomal enzyme present in the proximal tubular cells and is released into the urine following any injury to the tubules.\n\nLow-molecular-weight proteins such as β2-microglobulin are normally reabsorbed in the PCT. When there is tubular injury, the proportion of these proteins in urine increases. This lowers the albumin: β2-microglobulin ratio in urine.\n\nIn glomerular proteinuria, there is increased filtration of macromolecules like albumin across the glomerular capillary wall. NAG is absent as there is no tubular injury.\n\nGlomerular proteinuria\nNon-glomerular proteinuria\n\nType of protein excreted\n\nHigh molecular weight (Albumin)\nLow molecular weight (β2-microglobulin)\n\n24 hr protein excretion\n\n> 1-2 g/day\n< 1 g/day\n\nRBC casts\n\nPresent\nAbsent\n\nAlbumin: β2 microglobulin ratio (Normal 100:1)\n\n1000:1\n10:1\n\nN-acetyl glucosaminidase (NAG)\n\nAbsent\nPresent","t":"Urine Analysis","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"A patient presents with recurrent kidney stones. The microscopic examination of urine is shown below. What is the type of stone seen in this patient?","o":["Struvite","Oxalate","Uric acid","Cystine"],"a":3,"e":"The image shows a hexagonal crystal, suggesting cystine stones. These stones are a recurrent feature in patients with cystinuria.\n\nCrystals in urine are usually associated with kidney stones. Crystals form in the urine depends upon a variety of factors including:\n\n• The degree of concentration of constituent molecules\n\n• The urine pH\n\n• The presence of inhibitors of crystallization\n\nCalcium oxalate and uric acid crystals can sometimes be found in normal urine that has been left to stand, due to crystal formation ex vivo.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/cc83aaafc54845c78d04cf7a2d26964d.JPEG"],"t":"Urine Analysis","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is incorrect about casts in urine?","o":["Width of cast depends on the size of tubule in which it is formed","Casts in urine are specific to kidneys","Site of formation is proximal convoluted tubule","Casts are made up of uromodulin"],"a":2,"e":"The sites of formation of casts are the collecting ducts and DCTs (distal convoluted tubules).\n\nCasts are cylindrical structures formed by the aggregation of Tamm-Horsfall mucoprotein or uromodulin. This protein is secreted in the thick ascending loop of Henle. They assume the shape and size of the lumen in which they are formed. Several factors favour cast formation, such as urine stasis, low pH and high concentration. Casts are the only elements in urine sediments that are specifically of renal origin.","t":"Urine Analysis","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"A patient with cryptococcal meningitis was started on amphotericin B. Two days later, he is found to have decreased urine output. Urinalysis reveals granular casts. What could be the possible diagnosis?","o":["Mycotoxic nephropathy","Acute tubular necrosis","Acute interstitial nephritis","Papillary necrosis"],"a":1,"e":"Muddy-brown granular casts are seen in acute tubular necrosis, a type of intrinsic renal failure. ATN can occur secondary to nephrotoxicity or hypovolemia.","t":"Urine Analysis","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following casts are associated with a poor prognosis?","o":["Epithelial casts","WBC casts","Hyaline casts","Broad casts"],"a":3,"e":"Broad casts are associated with a poor prognosis.\n\nThey refer to wider waxy casts that are seen in advanced chronic kidney disease. It implies dilation of the tubules.","t":"Urine Analysis","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not a risk factor for chronic kidney disease?","o":["Childhood obesity","Diabetes mellitus","Family history of kidney disease","Large for gestational age at birth"],"a":3,"e":"Small for gestational age (SGA) at birth is a risk factor for chronic kidney disease (CKD), not LGA.\n\nImportant risk factors for CKD or chronic renal failure are:\n\n• SGA\n\n• Childhood obesity\n\n• Hypertension\n\n• Diabetes mellitus\n\n• Autoimmune disease\n\n• Family history of kidney disease\n\n• Previous episode of acute kidney injury\n\n• Structural abnormalities of the urinary tract\n\n• Autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease","t":"Chronic Kidney Disease","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"What is the most common cause of chronic kidney disease?","o":["Diabetic nephropathy","Glomerulonephritis","Hypertension","Adult polycystic kidney disease"],"a":0,"e":"Diabetic nephropathy is the most common cause of chronic kidney disease (CKD).\n\nThe leading causes of CKD in decreasing order of frequency are as follows:\n\n• Diabetic nephropathy\n\n• Glomerulonephritis\n\n• Hypertension-associated CKD\n\n• ADPKD (Adult or autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease)","t":"Chronic Kidney Disease","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"Patients with which of the following conditions are unlikely to develop chronic renal failure?","o":["Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis","Membranous glomerulonephritis","Membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis","Focal segmental glomerulosclerosis"],"a":0,"e":"Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis (PSGN) is least likely to cause chronic renal failure. It is a rapidly progressive condition and a cause of acute renal failure.","t":"Chronic Kidney Disease","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following parameters are used in the staging of chronic kidney disease?","o":["Serum albumin and creatinine","Serum urea and creatinine","Urine albumin and eGFR","Serum creatinine and eGFR"],"a":2,"e":"Stages of chronic kidney disease (CKD) are stratified by estimated GFR (eGFR) and degree of albuminuria in order to predict the risk of progression of CKD. Serum creatinine is not directly used in the staging of chronic kidney disease.\n\nStages of Chronic Kidney Disease based on GFR\n\nStage\n\nGFR\n\n(ml/min/1.73sq.mt)\n\nDescription\n\n1\n≥90\nNormal or increased GFR with other evidence of Kidney Damage\n\n2\n60-89\nSlight decrease in GFR, with other evidence of kidney damage\n\n3A\n45-59\nModerate decrease in GFR with or without other evidence of Kidney Damage\n\n3B\n30-44\n\n4\n15-29\nSevere decrease in GFR, with or without other evidence of kidney damage\n\n5\n<15\nEstablished renal failure, also known as End-Stage Renal Disease","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d58da6d44999490aa2d4b3d9d43081eax1280x1836.JPEG"],"t":"Chronic Kidney Disease","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"In patients with chronic kidney disease, at which stage do the complications of kidney failure start appearing?","o":["Stage 1","Stage 2","Stage 3-4","Stage 4-5"],"a":2,"e":"The features of kidney failure become prominent as the GFR progressively declines to stages 3 and 4. The following complications occur as a result of loss of kidney function:\n\n• Volume overload\n\n• Hyperkalemia\n\n• Metabolic acidosis\n\n• Hyperphosphatemia\n\n• Mineral and bone disorders\n\n• Hypertension\n\n• Anemia\n\n• Dyslipidemia","t":"Chronic Kidney Disease","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"A 66-year-old woman is being evaluated for new-onset uremic encephalopathy. What is her GFR likely to be?","o":["&lt; 50 mL/min/1.73 m2","&lt; 15 mL/min/1.73 m2","&lt; 40 mL/min/1.73 m2","&lt; 30 mL/min/1.73 m2"],"a":1,"e":"Clinical features of uremia start to appear in patients with GFR < 15 mL/min/1.73 m2 or end-stage renal disease (ESRD). By this stage, renal clearance is severely impaired and the accumulation of uremic toxins leads to several complications including:\n\n• Uremic encephalopathy\n\n• Pruritus\n\n• Malnutrition\n\n• Anorexia, nausea, fatigue\n\n• Platelet dysfunction\n\n• Sexual dysfunction\n\n• Pericarditis, pleuritis\n\n• Peripheral neuropathy\n\nStages of Chronic Kidney Disease\n\nStage\n\nGFR (ml/ min/ 1.73 m2)\n\nDescription\n\n1\n≥90\nNormal or increased GFR with other evidence of Kidney Damage\n\n2\n60-89\nA slight decrease in GFR, with other evidence of kidney damage\n\n3A\n45-59\nModerate decrease in GFR with or without other evidence of Kidney Damage\n\n3B\n30-44\n\n4\n15-29\nSevere decrease in GFR, with or without other evidence of kidney damage\n\n5\n<15\nEnd-stage renal disease","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1fbd695152d540608dff011721d1af58x1280x1836.JPEG"],"t":"Chronic Kidney Disease","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is incorrect about uremic syndrome?","o":["It occurs due to accumulation of toxins","There is loss of kidney function and hormone regulation","Systemic inflammation is seen","Can be eliminated by removing urea through intensive dialysis"],"a":3,"e":"Uremic syndrome cannot be eliminated by removing urea alone. It is a complex condition occurring due to the accumulation of multiple toxins.\n\nThe pathophysiology of uremic syndrome involves the following:\n\n• Accumulation of toxins (uremia) - urea, creatinine, other products of protein metabolism like urates, hippurates, polyamines, indoles.\n\n• Loss of kidney function leading to fluid and electrolyte imbalance and hormone dysregulation.\n\n• Progressive systemic inflammation with vascular and nutritional impairment.\n\nClinical features secondary to loss of kidney function tend to develop in parallel with declining GFR. However the features of uremia and, hence, uremic syndrome, usually occur only in stage 5 or ESRD.","t":"Chronic Kidney Disease","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"A 60-year-old man with CKD has been on dialysis for 3 months. He now presents with intense body pains and muscle pain. He suffered from 2 fractures over the last 2 months. On evaluation, he was found to have decreased PTH levels and normal calcium levels. He has been regularly taking oral vitamin D and calcium tablets. What is the likely diagnosis?","o":["Osteomalacia","Adynamic bone disease","Osteitis fibrosa cystica","Tumoral calcinosis"],"a":1,"e":"This clinical scenario in a CKD patient is suggestive of adynamic bone disease.\n\nIn CKD, metabolic bone disorders (renal osteodystrophy) can be of three types:\n\nOsteitis fibrosa cystica\nOsteomalacia\nAdynamic bone disease\n\nHigh bone turnover\n\nLow bone turnover\n\nLow bone turnover\n\nRenal calcium loss\nDecreased calcitriol formation leads to low calcium\n\nIt is an iatrogenic condition\n\nIncreased intake of vitamin D or calcium-containing phosphate binders or dialysis.\n\nHigh PTH occurs in response to low calcium (secondary hyperparathyroidism)\n\nLow vitamin D leads to defective mineralization\n\nDecreased calcium leads to a mild increase in PTH, which stimulates osteoblasts. Thus the bone volume is maintained.\n\nHigh vitamin D decreases PTH levels, which leads to decreased bone volume.\n\nMineralization is defective because of aluminium, which comes from dialysis.\n\nTreatment:\n\nVitamin D + calcium\n\nTreatment:\n\nVitamin D + calcium\n\nTreatment:\n\nWithdrawal of offending agent","t":"Chronic Kidney Disease","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"A middle-aged woman with chronic kidney disease presents to the OPD with exercise intolerance, fatigue, and shortness of breath. On examination, she is found to have severe pallor. Which of the following is least likely to be a cause for these findings?","o":["Deficiency of erythropoietin","Iron deficiency","Hyperparathyroidism","Sickle cell anemia"],"a":3,"e":"Sickle cell anemia is not a feature of chronic kidney disease (CKD).\n\nCommon causes of anemia in CKD include:\n\n• Erythropoietin deficiency\n\n• Diminished red blood cell survival\n\n• Bleeding diathesis\n\n• Iron deficiency\n\n• Vitamin B12 and folate deficiency\n\n• Hyperparathyroidism (due to bone marrow fibrosis)\n\n• Hemoglobinopathy","t":"Chronic Kidney Disease","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not seen in patients with chronic renal failure?","o":["Peripheral neuropathy","Hyperthyroidism","Decreased vitamin D levels","Osteomalacia"],"a":1,"e":"Hyperthyroidism is not a feature of chronic renal failure.\n\nOption A: Peripheral neuropathy can occur secondary to uremia.\n\nOptions C and D: In chronic renal failure, calcitriol (vitamin D3) production is decreased, leading to metabolic bone disease, which in turn leads to low serum calcium levels, secondary hyperparathyroidism, and osteomalacia.","t":"Chronic Kidney Disease","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"What is the most common neurological disorder seen in patients with CKD?","o":["Dementia","Restless leg syndrome","Peripheral neuropathy","Encephalopathy"],"a":2,"e":"The most common neurological disorder seen in patients with CKD is peripheral neuropathy.\n\nThe various neuromuscular manifestations of CKD include:\n\n• Memory and concentration impairment\n\n• Dialysis dementia - due to aluminum intoxication\n\n• Restless leg syndrome\n\n• Peripheral neuropathy\n\n• Hiccups\n\n• Cramps\n\n• Uremic encephalopathy\n\n• Confusion and lethargy\n\n• Tremors\n\n• Myoclonus\n\n• Asterixis\n\n• Seizures\n\n• Coma","t":"Chronic Kidney Disease","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"A 27-year-old woman goes to her family doctor as she is unable to fall asleep at night. She complains of tingling and crawling sensations over her legs at night. She also feels a strong urge to move her legs, which relieves the symptoms for a while. Which of the following is incorrect about this condition?","o":["Autosomal dominant inheritance","Associated with low serum ferritin","Most common cause is chronic kidney disease","Most common movement disorder in pregnancy"],"a":2,"e":"This clinical scenario is suggestive of restless legs syndrome (RLS) or Willis-Ekbom disease. Chronic kidney disease (CKD) is not the most common cause of this condition.\n\nRLS is a neurological sleep disorder that can be primary or secondary. The primary form is more common. It shows autosomal dominant inheritance in most cases, but can be idiopathic. Secondary RLS is associated with the following:\n\n• Iron deficiency\n\n• Pregnancy - most common movement disorder\n\n• Peripheral neuropathy\n\n• Diabetes mellitus\n\n• CKD\n\n• Parkinson's disease\n\n• Multiple sclerosis\n\nIt is characterized by an uncontrollable, restless urge to move the legs. It tends to worsen during periods of inactivity and at evening or night. It is accompanied by crawling, tingling or burning sensations. These symptoms show partial or complete improvement with movement. This cycle may continue for a while and result in insomnia.\n\nPrimary RLS is managed by improving sleep hygiene and dopamine agonists (pramipexole or ropinirole) if symptoms persist. Iron supplements can also be given. In secondary RLS, the underlying cause must be treated.","t":"Chronic Kidney Disease","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"What is the most likely cause of convulsions in a patient with CKD?","o":["Hypokalemia","Hyperkalemia","Hypercalcemia","Water intoxication"],"a":3,"e":"The most likely cause of convulsions in a patient with CKD is water intoxication. Volume overload in these patients as well as damage to the blood-brain barrier by uremic toxins causes cerebral edema and seizures.","t":"Chronic Kidney Disease","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"What is most common cause of mortality in patients with chronic kidney disease?","o":["Cardiovascular disease","Sepsis","Uremia","Dialysis-related complications"],"a":0,"e":"Cardiovascular disease is the leading cause of morbidity and mortality in patients at every stage of CKD.","t":"Chronic Kidney Disease","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following would be elevated in patients with chronic kidney disease?","o":["Fibrinogen","Fetuin","Albumin","Globulin"],"a":0,"e":"Chronic kidney disease (CKD) is associated with elevated levels of fibrinogen.\n\nCKD is a pro-inflammatory state characterized by altered levels of acute-phase reactants (APR)\n\n• Elevated levels of positive APR like CRP and fibrinogen (associated with high ESR).\n\n• Reduced levels of negative APR like albumin and fetuin.","t":"Chronic Kidney Disease","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"What is the predominant electrolyte abnormality seen in patients with advanced CKD?","o":["Hyponatremia","Hypokalemia","Non-anion gap metabolic acidosis","High anion gap metabolic acidosis"],"a":3,"e":"High anion gap metabolic acidosis is the predominant electrolyte abnormality in a patient with advanced CKD. In advanced CKD, impaired renal functioning causes decreased urinary excretion of acids. These retained organic acids result in a high anion gap.\n\nIn early CKD, potassium retention is associated with bicarbonate loss. This gives rise to hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis with a normal anion gap in early CKD.\n\nThe following electrolyte abnormalities are seen in CKD:\n\n• Hyperkalemia\n\n• Hypocalcemia\n\n• Hyperphosphatemia\n\n• Hyperuricemia\n\n• Hypermagnesemia\n\nHypokalemia is uncommon in CKD and usually reflects markedly reduced dietary potassium intake, especially in association with excessive diuretic therapy or concurrent GI losses.","t":"Chronic Kidney Disease","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"Patients with CKD have increased levels of all of the following except:","o":["Insulin","PTH","Estrogen","FGF-23"],"a":2,"e":"Serum estrogen levels are low in CKD patients. This leads to menstrual abnormalities and infertility.\n\nOption A: Plasma levels of insulin are elevated in CKD patients due to impaired clearance by the affected kidneys. Hence, insulin dose must be reduced in these patients.\n\nOption B: Elevated parathyroid hormone levels in CKD are secondary to derangements in the homeostasis of calcium, phosphate, and vitamin D.\n\nOption D: FGF-23 (fibroblast growth factor) is part of a family of phosphatonins that promotes renal phosphate excretion. The levels of this hormone increase early in the course of CKD.","t":"Chronic Kidney Disease","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"A patient presents to the emergency with chest pain that is radiating to his shoulder, shortness of breath, and diaphoresis. He is a known case of chronic kidney disease. Which of the following investigations is best for diagnosis?","o":["Serial troponin levels over 6 hours","Troponin levels on admission and 1 hour later","Troponin and CK-MB levels at admission","Serial CK-MB levels over 6 hours"],"a":0,"e":"This clinical scenario is suggestive of acute myocardial infarction (MI) in a patient with chronic kidney disease (CKD). A serial rise in cardiac troponin levels over 3-6 hours following presentation is needed to make a diagnosis. An interval of 1-hour is not considered sufficient.\n\nMinor chronic elevations in serum troponins are commonly observed in patients with CKD even in the absence of cardiovascular disease. Hence, a single elevated troponin value in such patients cannot be used to diagnose MI.\n\nCK-MB is far less sensitive and specific than troponins. Hence, it is not preferred.","t":"Chronic Kidney Disease","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is the most sensitive test to detect early diabetic nephropathy in a patient?","o":["Urine albumin","Renal ultrasound","Creatinine clearance test","Serum BUN level"],"a":0,"e":"Microalbuminuria is the most sensitive and earliest finding of diabetic nephropathy. It refers to urine albumin of 30–300 mg/24-hour urine. It is seen in the stage of incipient diabetic kidney disease.\n\nHowever, microalbuminuria is a very variable finding and is not required to diagnose nephropathy. Non-albuminuric diabetic nephropathy may occur in some patients and regression of albuminuria may be seen in type 1 diabetics.\n\nDiabetic nephropathy can be diagnosed if the following are satisfied:\n\n• Reduced GFR (< 60 mL/min/1.73 m2) with or without persistent albuminuria (> 30 mg/day or urine PCR > 30 mg/g)\n\n• Long duration of diabetes or established diabetic retinopathy\n\n• Other etiologies are ruled out","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6fc3e5ac8f754d6687b8fc57d9f17ad7x1280x1772.JPEG"],"t":"Chronic Kidney Disease","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"A patient with chronic kidney disease is found to have normal-sized kidneys on ultrasonography. Which of the following diagnoses is least likely?","o":["Diabetic nephropathy","HIV nephropathy","Amyloidosis","Hypertensive nephropathy"],"a":3,"e":"Hypertensive nephropathy is unlikely in this patient as it usually presents with shrunken kidneys.\n\nLongstanding CKD due to most causes results in bilaterally small kidneys. Causes of CKD with normal size kidneys are:\n\n• Diabetic nephropathy (kidney size may be increased at the onset)\n\n• Amyloidosis\n\n• HIV nephropathy","t":"Chronic Kidney Disease","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"A 65-year-old hypertensive patient has recently been diagnosed with stage 3 chronic kidney disease. According to the ESC 2018 guidelines, what target blood pressure would you choose for this patient?","o":["140/90 mm Hg","140/80 mm Hg","130/90mmHg","120/80 mmHg"],"a":1,"e":"According to the ESC 2018 guidelines, the target blood pressure in hypertensive patients with CKD is < 140/ 80 mm Hg regardless of age.","t":"Chronic Kidney Disease","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"A 50-year-old man who has been on dialysis for 2 years presents with bone pain and bowing of legs. Further evaluation reveals hyperparathyroidism and hyperphosphatemia. What is the initial treatment of choice in this patient?","o":["Sevelamer","Cinacalcet","Pamidronate","Calcitriol"],"a":0,"e":"This clinical scenario is suggestive of secondary hyperparathyroidism (HPT) and hyperphosphatemia in a patient with CKD. Sevelamer is the best initial treatment for this patient. It is a phosphate binder that reduces the absorption of phosphate from the gastrointestinal tract.\n\nOther phosphate binders that can be used include:\n\n• Calcium containing\n\n• Calcium acetate\n\n• Calcium carbonate\n\n• Non-calcium containing\n\n• Sevelamer\n\n• Lanthanum\n\nCalcitriol and cinacalcet are used to decrease PTH levels in secondary hyperparathyroidism. However, they also cause increased serum phosphate levels. Hence, they should not be given as initial treatment in patients with hyperphosphatemia.\n\nBisphosphonates like pamidronate are used in the treatment of primary hyperparathyroidism.","t":"Chronic Kidney Disease","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is the most appropriate definitive treatment for anemia in patients with chronic kidney disease?","o":["IV iron sucrose","Darbepoetin","Blood transfusion","Oral iron supplements"],"a":1,"e":"Anemia in patients with chronic kidney disease (CKD) is primarily due to decreased erythropoietin production. Hence, human recombinant erythropoietin (EPO) such as darbepoetin is the most appropriate treatment and is considered the mainstay.\n\nHowever, an optimal response to treatment with EPO occurs only when iron stores are adequate. Hence, iron supplements (oral or IV) must be given first to ensure the effectiveness of EPO therapy.\n\nOther treatment options for anemia in CKD are:\n\n• Vitamin B12 and folate supplementation\n\n• Blood transfusion - in severe anemia and failure to EPO therapy","t":"Chronic Kidney Disease","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following should not be used in a patient with CKD?","o":["Ramipril","Diclofenac","Loop diuretics","Insulin"],"a":1,"e":"Diclofenac should not be used in a patient with CKD as it is nephrotoxic.\n\nRenal blood flow is regulated by prostaglandin E2. Aspirin, diclofenac and other NSAIDs inhibit cyclooxygenase. This decreases prostaglandin E2 concentration, thereby causing a reduction in blood flow and GFR.\n\nNephrotoxic drugs include the following:\n\n• NSAIDs\n\n• Antifungals (amphotericin B)\n\n• Antibiotics\n\n• Aminoglycosides\n\n• Vancomycin\n\n• Cephalosporins\n\n• Sulfonamides\n\n• Streptomycin\n\n• Antivirals\n\n• Acyclovir\n\n• Foscarnet\n\n• Chemotherapy drugs\n\n• Methotrexate\n\n• Platins\n\n• Immunosuppressants (cyclosporine)","t":"Chronic Kidney Disease","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"A 50-year-old man with type 2 diabetes has developed stage 4 CKD secondary to diabetic nephropathy. He currently takes insulin for his diabetes. What changes need to be made to his regimen?","o":["No adjustment required","Decreased insulin dose","Increased insulin dose","Add DPP 4 inhibitor"],"a":1,"e":"In CKD patients with diabetes, the dose of insulin must be reduced.\n\nIn CKD patients, the plasma levels of insulin are elevated due to diminished renal degradation of insulin by the impaired kidney. This impaired renal clearance of insulin can lead to hypoglycemia. Hence, the dose of insulin must be reduced to prevent this.","t":"Chronic Kidney Disease","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following criteria is diagnostic of acute kidney injury in a patient?","o":["Rise in creatinine 1.5 times the baseline value within 7 days","Rise in creatinine ≥ 0.3mg/dl from baseline within 7 days","Rise in creatinine ≥ 0.5 mg/dl from baseline within 48 hours","Urine volume &lt;0.5 mL/kg/h for 24 hours"],"a":0,"e":"A rise in creatinine 1.5 times the baseline value within 7 days is diagnostic of acute kidney injury (AKI).\n\nAKI is an abrupt decrease in kidney function within hours to days. According to the KDIGO definition, it is defined as any of the following:\n\n• Increase in serum creatinine by ≥0.3 mg/dL within 48 hours OR\n\n• Increase in serum creatinine to ≥1.5 times the baseline within 7 days OR\n\n• Urine volume <0.5 mL/kg/h for 6 hours.","t":"Acute Kidney Injury","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"Patients with urine output below what level are said to have anuria?","o":["50 mL/day","70 mL/day","150 ml/day","100 mL/day"],"a":3,"e":"Anuria is defined as urine output less than 100 mL/day. Oliguria is defined as urine output less than 400 mL/day.\n\nAcute kidney injury (AKI) can have varied clinical features depending on the etiology. The history and physical examination in AKI aims mainly at:\n\n• Finding the etiology\n\n• Identifying the occurrence of any complications\n\nThe following clinical features may be seen:\n\n• Oliguria or anuria\n\n• Gross hematuria\n\n• Renal colic pain (in renal stones)\n\n• Loss of skin turgor, dry mouth, hypotension, tachycardia (in hypovolemia)\n\n• Prostatomegaly and/or a pelvic mass (in post-renal obstruction)\n\n• Dysuria, fever with chills and rigors, increased frequency of micturition, renal angle tenderness (in pyelonephritis)\n\n• Edema, hypertension (volume overload)\n\n• Encephalopathy, seizures, platelet dysfunction (uremia)","t":"Acute Kidney Injury","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following causes prerenal AKI?\n\n1. Myeloma light chains\n\n2. Snakebite\n\n3. ACE inhibitors\n\n4. Rhabdomyolysis\n\n5. NSAIDs","o":["1, 3, and 4","1, 2 and 4","2, 3 and 5","3, 4 and 5"],"a":3,"e":"NSAIDs, Rhabdomyolysis and ACE inhibitors cause prerenal acute kidney injury (AKI).\n\nThe use of NSAIDs causes reduced production of vasodilatory prostaglandins. This leads to decreased renal perfusion and glomerular capillary pressure. NSAIDs can also lead to acute interstitial nephritis, a cause of renal AKI. However, this is relatively uncommon.\n\nACE inhibitors cause inhibition of efferent arteriole vasoconstriction, leading to reduced renal perfusion.\n\nRhabdomyolysis causes AKI by both prerenal mechanisms (due to volume sequestration) and ATN (due to precipitation of myoglobin in the renal tubules).\n\nNephrotoxins like myeloma light chains, snake venom, myoglobin (due to rhabdomyolysis) cause acute tubular necrosis (ATN). This leads to renal or intrinsic AKI.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9794a1e8c242417fb6431c0de1c214b9x1200x2480.JPEG"],"t":"Acute Kidney Injury","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"A 48-year-old woman presented with severe abdominal pain, vomiting, and dark-coloured urine for the past 3 days. She has been on a combination painkiller for rheumatoid arthritis for 10 years. On examination, severe pallor was noted and BP was 180/120 mmHg. CT abdomen is shown below. What is the diagnosis?","o":["Septic acute tubular necrosis","Chronic kidney disease","NSAID-induced nephropathy","Acute interstitial nephritis"],"a":1,"e":"This clinical scenario and CT scan showing papillary calcifications are highly suggestive of chronic kidney disease caused by analgesic nephropathy.\n\nThis condition is more common in females secondary to excessive prolonged use of a combination of analgesics. The mechanism of injury is medullary hypoxia leading to papillary necrosis and finally causes chronic tubulointerstitial nephritis.\n\nPatients can remain asymptomatic for a long time. Sloughing or obstruction of papillae can cause abdominal pain and hematuria. It increases the risk of urothelial malignancies. The following CT findings can be seen:\n\n• Papillary calcifications\n\n• Small kidneys\n\n• Irregular contour\n\nNote: NSAID-induced nephropathy causes acute interstitial nephritis. It is a type IV hypersensitivity reaction. The features include non-oliguric AKI, fractional excretion of sodium (FeNa) < 1, and hyperkalemia. It is seen within a few days of use and is reversible.\n\nThe image below shows papillary calcifications:\n\nThe given image shows papillary calcification on ultrasonography:","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6e59be2efb644ef8986c8033d7144c86.JPEG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/dc78bb1d5d3345e183a34a8cc76acb70x1280x985.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/407599495057445c963279473d5bb345x1280x1811.JPEG"],"t":"Acute Kidney Injury","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"A 38-year-old woman presents with nausea, vomiting, fatigue, and oliguria for the past three days. On further investigation, urine osmolality was 800 mOsm/kg, urinary sodium 10 meq/L, and BUN: creatinine ratio was 20:1. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Pre-renal AKI","Renal AKI","Post renal AKI","Acute cortical necrosis"],"a":0,"e":"The given clinical scenario is highly suggestive of prerenal AKI based on the following:\n\n• Urine osmolality >500 mOsm/kg\n\n• Urinary sodium < 20 meq/l\n\n• BUN: Creatinine ratio of 20:1\n\nDifferences between prerenal, and renal AKI:\n\nIndices\nPrerenal\nRenal\n\nSpot urine sodium (mEq/L)\n<20\n>40\n\nFractional excretion of sodium (FENa)\n<1%\n>2%\n\nFractional excretion of urea (FEUrea)\n<35%\n>35%\n\nUrine osmolality (mOsm/kg)\n>500\n<350\n\nBUN to serum creatinine ratio\n>20:1\n<10:1\n\nUrine creatinine/plasma creatinine\n>40:1\n<20:1\n\nUrinary sediment\nNil or hyaline casts only\nRenal tubular epithelial casts, muddy brown granular casts","t":"Acute Kidney Injury","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"A 35-year-old man was admitted following a crush injury to his left leg. Two days later, his urine/plasma creatinine levels are &lt; 20. What is the likely cause?","o":["Rhabdomyolysis","Congestive heart failure","Hepatorenal syndrome","Hypovolemia"],"a":0,"e":"This clinical scenario and urine/ plasma creatinine ratio of <20 is suggestive of renal AKI due to rhabdomyolysis-induced acute tubular necrosis.\n\nRhabdomyolysis is a serious complication of crush injuries. It leads to muscle breakdown, releasing a high amount of myoglobin in the blood. Myoglobinuria can lead to acute kidney injury by the following mechanisms:\n\n• Direct toxic effect of myoglobin and heme on the PCT\n\n• Tubular obstruction by precipitation of pigmented casts\n\n• Renal vasoconstriction can cause FeNa < 1 (unlike other renal causes of AKI)\n\nIndices\nPrerenal\nRenal\n\nSpot urine sodium (mEq/L)\n<20\n>40\n\nFractional excretion of sodium (FENa)\n<1%\n>2%\n\nFractional excretion of urea (FEUrea)\n<35%\n>35%\n\nUrine osmolality (mOsm/kg)\n>500\n<350\n\nBUN to serum creatinine ratio\n>20:1\n<10:1\n\nUrine creatinine/plasma creatinine\n>40:1\n<20:1\n\nUrinary sediment\nNil or hyaline casts only\nRenal tubular epithelial casts, muddy brown granular casts","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6bedd3c2ca6e4c58b056a4ea49e4feb8x1200x2480.JPEG"],"t":"Acute Kidney Injury","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"A 38-year old woman presents with severe muscle pain and high-coloured urine for the past 3 days. She joined a gym 2 weeks ago and has been strenuously working out. She was diagnosed with hypothyroidism 8 years back. Work-up reveals high serum creatine kinase, uric acid, and creatinine. Which of the following is likely to be present in this patient?","o":["Hypophosphatemia","Hypomagnesemia","Hypocalcemia","Hypokalemia"],"a":2,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of rhabdomyolysis. Hypocalcemia is seen in this condition.\n\nThe causes of rhabdomyolysis include:\n\n• Hypothyroidism (precipitated by exertion, alcohol, infection)\n\n• Crush injury\n\n• Electric shock\n\n• Prolonged seizures\n\n• Malignant hyperthermia\n\n• Exertion\n\n• Drugs like fibrates, statins\n\n• Polymyositis / Dermatomyositis\n\n• McArdle's disease (GSD-5) causes recurrent rhabdomyolysis.\n\nPatients often present with muscle pain and high-colored urine. The following features are seen:\n\n• High serum CK (> 5 times normal)\n\n• High serum myoglobin\n\n• High serum LDH\n\n• Hyperkalemia\n\n• Hypermagnesemia\n\n• Hyperphosphatemia\n\n• Hypocalcemia\n\n• Hyperuricemia\n\n• Once AKI develops, high serum creatinine and urea\n\nThe treatment includes administration of large amounts of IV fluids (5-6 liters), alkalization of urine, and hemodialysis.","t":"Acute Kidney Injury","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"A 54-year-old man presented with reduced urine output, swelling of his feet, and eyelid puffiness for 1 week. He also complains of back pain for the past 3 months, unresponsive to painkillers. Investigations reveal the following. What is the possible cause of renal failure in this patient?\n\nHemoglobin - 7.1 g/dl\nSerum urea - 63 mg/dl\nSerum creatinine - 3.9 mg/dl\nUrine analysis - Positive for Bence-Jones proteins","o":["Hypokalemia","Hyperkalemia","Hypercalcemia","Hypocalcemia"],"a":2,"e":"Bence-Jones proteins on urine analysis are suggestive of multiple myeloma. It causes acute kidney injury (AKI) by the following mechanisms:\n\n• Hypercalcemia - leads to intense renal vasoconstriction and reduced perfusion\n\n• Myeloma light chains - cause direct tubular toxicity.","t":"Acute Kidney Injury","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"Non-oliguric renal failure may be seen in patients with which of the following conditions?\n\n1. Congestive heart failure\n\n2. Aminoglycoside toxicity\n\n3. Contrast-induced nephropathy\n\n4. Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis\n\n5. Acute interstitial nephritis","o":["2, 3, and 5","2 and 3","1, 3, and 5","2, 4, and 5"],"a":0,"e":"Non-oliguric renal failure may be seen in the following conditions:\n\n• Acute interstitial nephritis\n\n• Acute tubular necrosis (aminoglycoside toxicity, contrast-induced nephropathy)\n\nAll the causes of acute tubular necrosis (ATN) cause non-oliguric AKI except acyclovir (oliguric AKI).\n\nThe difference in urine output between oliguric and non-oliguric AKI may be due to variations in GFR or in the rate of tubular reabsorption. Non-oliguric forms may be associated with less severe injury.\n\nOther options: Prerenal causes such as congestive heart failure cause oliguric AKI. In post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis, oliguria occurs as a result of decreased GFR.","t":"Acute Kidney Injury","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"A 47-year-old electrician was rushed to the hospital following an electrical shock. Examination reveals electrical burns. During the course of treatment, he developed an arrhythmia and was managed appropriately. What arterial blood gas finding would you expect in this patient?","o":["Hyperkalemic alkalosis","Hypokalemic alkalosis","Hyperkalemic acidosis","Hypokalemic acidosis"],"a":2,"e":"Electrical burns result in rhabdomyolysis. It presents with hyperkalemic metabolic acidosis.\n\nMuscle breakdown in rhabdomyolysis leads to hyperkalemia which causes arrhythmias. It also causes high anion gap metabolic acidosis due to the increase in H+ ions and liberation of organic anions.","t":"Acute Kidney Injury","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"A patient with acute renal failure was found to have metabolic acidosis on evaluation. What is the likely cause?","o":["Decreased excretion of ammonium","Loss of HCO3","Increased aldosterone levels","Increased production of H+"],"a":0,"e":"In acute renal failure, metabolic acidosis occurs due to decreased excretion of ammonium. This impairs the excretion of H+ ions as NH4 ions.\n\nNormally, H+ are excreted via the kidney in combination with ammonia which acts as a urinary buffer.\n\nIn AKI, there is a decreased capacity of tubules to secrete ammonia. Due to reduced amounts of ammonia, the production of NH4 is impaired. Hence, H+ ions cannot be excreted, which leads to metabolic acidosis.","t":"Acute Kidney Injury","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"A 75-year-old obese woman, who is a known diabetic presented with chest pain. Coronary angiography was done. Her S.creatinine levels before and after the procedure are given below. Which of the following is not a risk factor for the complication she developed?\n\nS.creatinine before procedure - 0.7 mg/dL\n\nS.creatinine after procedure - 3.5 mg/dL","o":["Age","Diabetic nephropathy","Recent myocardial infarction","Obesity"],"a":3,"e":"The given clinical scenario shows >0.5 mg/dL increase in S.creatinine levels, which is indicative of contrast-induced nephropathy for which obesity is not a risk factor.\n\nContrast-induced nephropathy is defined as a 25% or 0.5 mg/dL increase from the baseline value of serum creatinine within 48-72 hours of intravenous contrast administration. It occurs due to tubular damage and as a result, the patient develops non-oliguric transient nephropathy.\n\nRisk factors for contrast medium-induced nephropathy:\n\nPatient-related:\n\n• eGFR < 60 mL/min/1.73 m2 before intra-arterial administration\n\n• eGFR < 45 mL/min/1.73 m2 before intravenous administration\n\n• Patient conditions that increase the risk:\n\n• Diabetic nephropathy\n\n• Dehydration\n\n• Congestive heart failure (NYHA grade 3–4) and low LVEF\n\n• Recent myocardial infarction (<24 h)\n\n• Intra-aortic balloon pump\n\n• Peri-procedural hypotension\n\n• Low hematocrit level\n\n• Age over 70\n\n• Concurrent administration of nephrotoxic drugs\n\n• Known or suspected acute renal failure\n\nProcedure-related:\n\n• Intra-arterial administration of contrast medium\n\n• High-osmolality agents\n\n• Large doses of contrast medium\n\n• Multiple contrast medium administrations within a few days\n\nSide effects of iodinated contrast material include:\n\n• Idiosyncratic anaphylactoid reaction - patient develops bronchospasm and hypotension\n\n• Dose-dependent side effect – contrast-induced nephropathy seen especially with the use of high osmolar agents\n\nPrevention of contrast-induced nephropathy:\n\n• IV fluids (isotonic saline or sodium bicarbonate) started 1 hour before and continued for at least 3-6 hours after the procedure\n\n• Low volume low osmolar contrast should be used\n\n• N-acetyl cysteine may be given prior to contrast exposure","t":"Acute Kidney Injury","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is false regarding contrast-induced nephropathy?","o":["It resolves within a week","N-acetyl cysteine can be used","Dialysis-requiring AKI is very common","Hypoxia due to nephrotoxic agents is the cause"],"a":2,"e":"Dialysis-requiring AKI is uncommon in contrast-induced nephropathy.\n\nContrast-induced AKI is generally associated with the use of high osmolar contrasts such as:\n\n• Iodinated contrast agents\n\n• High-dose gadolinium\n\nMechanism of contrast-induced injury includes:\n\n• Medullary ischemia and hypoxia: By inducing intense vasoconstriction\n\n• Toxic tubular damage: By direct injury\n\nIt usually develops within 24-48 hrs. Serum creatinine peaks by 3-5 days and normalizes by 7-10 days. It manifests mainly as a non-oliguric renal failure and has an excellent prognosis.\n\nContrast-induced AKI can be prevented by:\n\n• Use of iso-osmolar or non-ionic contrast agents (least toxic)\n\n• Administering minimum required quantity of contrast\n\n• Pre- and post-hydration (IV normal saline 1 mL/kg/h x 12 h prior and post-exposure)\n\n• N-acetyl cysteine may be given prior to contrast exposure.","t":"Acute Kidney Injury","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not a marker of kidney injury?","o":["Osteopontin","Cystatin C","Alanine aminopeptidase","Acid phosphatase"],"a":3,"e":"Acid phosphatase is not a marker of kidney injury.\n\nThe various markers of kidney injury are as follows:\n\n• IL-18\n\n• Cystatin C\n\n• NGAL (neutrophil gelatinase-associated lipocalin)\n\n• KIM-1 (kidney injury molecule-1)\n\n• LFABP (liver fatty acid-binding protein)\n\n•\n\n• NHE3 (sodium hydrogen exchanger 3)\n\n• NAG (N-acetyl glucosaminidase)\n\n• Alanine aminopeptidase\n\n• Osteopontin\n\n• ALP","t":"Acute Kidney Injury","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"Which intravenous fluid is preferred for the hydration of patients with pre-renal acute kidney injury?","o":["Hydroxyethyl starch","Dextran","0.9% NaCl","Ringer's lactate"],"a":2,"e":"Isotonic crystalloids like 0.9% NaCl are preferred for the correction of hypovolemia in pre-renal AKI.\n\nManagement of AKI:\n\n1. AKI care bundle:\n\n• Nephrotoxic drugs should be avoided and the dosage of other drugs must be adjusted according to kidney function\n\n• Creatinine levels and urine output must be closely monitored\n\n2. Hemodynamic management:\n\n• If hypovolemic - fluid replenishment using isotonic crystalloids (normal saline)\n\n• If euvolemic - fluid intake = urine output + additional ~500 mL\n\n• If hypervolemic - diuretics are administered. If the response is unsatisfactory, dialysis is required.\n\n3. Management of complications:\n\n• Pulmonary edema - Treated with diuretics, non-invasive ventilation, and dialysis if refractory\n\n• Metabolic acidosis - Treated when pH <7.2 with oral or IV sodium bicarbonate\n\n• Hyperkalemia - Treated by restriction of dietary potassium, administration of sodium polystyrene sulfonate, insulin, glucose, inhaled β-agonist, and calcium gluconate\n\nOptions A and B: Hydroxyethyl starch solutions and dextrans increase the risk of severe AKI and are contraindicated.\n\nOption D: Ringer's lactate is preferred only when a large volume of fluids is required.","t":"Acute Kidney Injury","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"Continuous renal replacement therapy is preferred in patients with which of the following features?","o":["Severe hypotension","Hyperkalemia","Metabolic acidosis","Uremic symptoms"],"a":0,"e":"Continuous renal replacement therapy (CRRT) is a modality of dialysis that involves hemodialysis and hemofiltration. It is the preferred option in AKI patients with severe hypotension.\n\nIt is preferred in the following cases:\n\n• Acute renal failure complicated by hemodynamic instability or heart failure\n\n• Severe volume overload unresponsive to diuretic therapy\n\n• Brain edema\n\n• Hypercatabolism state\n\n• Acute or chronic liver failure as a renal support measure\n\nIt is an extracorporeal blood purification therapy intended to substitute for impaired renal function over an extended period of time, aimed at being applied for 24 hours a day. There is no rapid shift of volume and osmolarity with this process as in intermittent hemodialysis.\n\nThe other types of dialysis are:\n\n• Intermittent hemodialysis - may be done in hemodynamically stable patients with AKI\n\n• Peritoneal dialysis - used when hemodialysis is not available.","t":"Acute Kidney Injury","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"Dialysis is not an absolute indication for patients who present with which of the following?","o":["Asterixis","Pericarditis","Uremic encephalopathy","Hyperkalemia"],"a":3,"e":"Dialysis is not indicated in patients who initially present with hyperkalemia. It is indicated in the case of refractory hyperkalemia that has failed to respond to other modalities of treatment.\n\nIndications for dialysis can be remembered using the mnemonic AEIOU:\n\n• Acidosis - metabolic acidosis that is refractory to medical treatment.\n\n• Electrolyte abnormality - refractory hyperkalemia and hypercalcemia.\n\n• Intoxication - barbiturates, alcohol, and lithium.\n\n• Overload - hypervolemia and pulmonary edema.\n\n• Uncontrolled hypertension (and)\n\n• Uremia - encephalopathy, pericarditis, asterixis, seizures, intractable nausea and vomiting, and bleeding diathesis.","t":"Renal Replacement Therapy","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is the most commonly used therapeutic modality for patients with end-stage renal disease?","o":["Hemodialysis","Renal transplant","Continuous ambulatory peritoneal dialysis","Continuous cycling peritoneal dialysis"],"a":0,"e":"The most common therapeutic modality for patients with end-stage renal disease (ESRD) is hemodialysis.\n\nThe various modalities of renal replacement therapy include the following:\n\n• Dialysis:\n\n• Acute intermittent hemodialysis\n\n• Continuous renal replacement therapy - includes hemodialysis and ultrafiltration techniques\n\n• Peritoneal dialysis\n\n• CAPD - Continuous ambulatory peritoneal dialysis\n\n• CCPD - Continuous cycling peritoneal dialysis\n\n• NIPD - Nocturnal intermittent peritoneal dialysis\n\n• Renal transplant","t":"Renal Replacement Therapy","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is incorrect about hemodialysis?","o":["Dialyzers are made up of cellulose membranes","Bicarbonate is commonly used as a buffer","Glutaraldehyde is used as a reprocessing agent","Urea is cleared more efficiently than creatinine"],"a":0,"e":"In hemodialysis, the dialyzer is made up of biocompatible membranes derived from polysulfone. Cellulose is bio-incompatible and is no longer used.\n\nHemodialysis is based on the principle of solute diffusion using an artificial semi-permeable membrane. Smaller molecules (urea) diffuse faster and are cleared more efficiently than larger molecules (creatinine). There are three components to this system:\n\n• Dialyzer - a chamber that contains compartments for blood and dialysate solution, separated by a semipermeable membrane.\n\n• Dialysate - a solution that helps in the removal of metabolic waste products. Bicarbonate is used as a buffer to correct acidosis in CKD patients.\n\n• Blood delivery system - consists of the vascular access and extracorporeal circuit.\n\nThe dialyzers used during a session of dialysis are reprocessed and reused for the next dialysis sessions. The reprocessors used for dialyzers are:\n\n• Glutaraldehyde\n\n• Formaldehyde\n\n• Peracetic acid-hydrogen peroxide\n\nThe following image depicts hemodialysis.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/53e103fc130b4abb9632f647983fc3a8x654x564.PNG"],"t":"Renal Replacement Therapy","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"A 50-year-old man with stage 5 chronic kidney disease is planned for hemodialysis. Which of the following is incorrect regarding the vascular access for this procedure?","o":["Tunneled catheters are associated with lower infection rates","Access of choice is radiocephalic arteriovenous fistula","Most common complication of arteriovenous grafts is infection","Fistulas have higher long term patency rates"],"a":2,"e":"The most common complication of arteriovenous (AV) grafts is stenosis, not infections.\n\nIn order to perform hemodialysis, vascular or hemodialysis access must first be established. The preferred site is the forearm. The following types of vascular access may be used for hemodialysis:\n\n• AV fistulas:\n\n• Fistula refers to the anastomosis of an artery and an adequately large vein (either side-to-side or end-to-side).\n\n• This causes arterialization of the vein, thereby causing venous wall thickening and increasing venous diameter.\n\n• Fistulas need 4-6 weeks to mature before they can be used.\n\n• The access of choice is radiocephalic AV fistula (Brescia-Cimino fistula).\n\n• Fistulas have higher long-term patency rates than grafts.\n\n• Thrombosis is a common complication.\n\nThe following image depicts a radiocephalic fistula.\n\n• AV grafts:\n\n• The artery and vein are connected by a prosthetic graft such as PTFE (polytetrafluoroethylene).\n\n• The advantage is that it can be performed with smaller veins.\n\n• Complications include stenosis (most common), due to intimal hyperplasia at anastomosis between graft and vein, and infections.\n\n• Central venous catheter:\n\n• Tunneled catheters are used for long-term IV access. As they tunnel through the skin before entering the vein, they have a lower risk of infection.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/498dc770b601482ab4fa70887fc30a22x960x720.PNG"],"t":"Renal Replacement Therapy","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"What is the rate of blood flow in the hemodialysis machine?","o":["150-250 mL/min","200-300 mL/min","250-450 mL/min","500-800 mL/min"],"a":2,"e":"During hemodialysis, heparinized blood flows through the dialyzer at a rate of 250–450 mL/min. On the other hand, the dialysate flows in the opposite counter-current direction at 500–800 mL/min.","t":"Renal Replacement Therapy","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"In a patient undergoing hemodialysis, which of the following can be used to monitor the adequacy of dialysis?","o":["Urea reduction ratio","Blood urea / creatinine ratio","Urinary protein / creatinine ratio","Creatinine reduction ratio"],"a":0,"e":"The adequacy of dialysis is determined by the urea reduction ratio. It is defined as the fractional reduction in blood urea nitrogen per hemodialysis session. It is calculated as (Co - C) / Co, where:\n\nCo - predialysis serum urea\n\nC - postdialysis serum urea\n\nThe target urea reduction ratio is 65–70%.","t":"Renal Replacement Therapy","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following has been most commonly implicated as a cause of dialysis-induced hypotension in patients?","o":["Bicarbonate","Aluminium","Acetate","Phosphate"],"a":2,"e":"Acetate is used as a buffer in the dialysate solution. It is the most common cause of dialysis-induced hypotension because of its vasodilatory and cardio-depressive effects.\n\nFor this reason, acetate is less commonly used these days and has been largely replaced by bicarbonate.","t":"Renal Replacement Therapy","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"What is the most common acute complication in patients who undergo hemodialysis?","o":["Anaphylaxis","Dialysis disquillibrium syndrome","Hypotension","Muscle cramps"],"a":2,"e":"Hypotension is the most common acute complication of hemodialysis, particularly among patients with diabetes mellitus.","t":"Renal Replacement Therapy","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"A 61-year-old man is brought to the casualty following a seizure episode. His wife says that he has been very drowsy and has not passed urine for the past 2 days. Investigations reveal serum urea of 71 mg/dl and creatinine of 5.6 mg/dl. He is referred for urgent hemodialysis. You would take all of the following precautions except:","o":["Warming the dialysate before dialysis","Removing more fluid during the beginning than the end of the procedure","Maintaining fluid removal rate below 13 mL/kg/hour","Careful evaluation of dry weight"],"a":0,"e":"Cooling, not warming, the dialysate before dialysis must be done to prevent hypotension during dialysis.\n\nHypotension is the most common acute complication of hemodialysis. The mechanism is not fully understood and occurs due to multiple factors. However, the following precautions have been shown to frequently prevent this complication:\n\n• Careful calculation of the dry weight and adjustment of the filtration rate accordingly.\n\n• Ultrafiltration modeling such that more fluid is removed during the beginning than the end of the procedure.\n\n• Avoiding rapid fluid removal and maintaining the rate < 13 mL/kg/hour.\n\n• Avoiding heavy meals during dialysis.\n\n• Cooling of the dialysate during the dialysis.","t":"Renal Replacement Therapy","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"A 67-year-old woman with CKD underwent her first session of hemodialysis. Towards the end of the session, she became drowsy and had a sudden seizure episode. What is the likely cause for this presentation?","o":["Hypotension","Aluminium toxicity","Anaphylaxis","Reverse urea effect"],"a":3,"e":"The given scenario is suggestive of the dialysis disequilibrium syndrome or the reverse urea effect. It is an acute complication that occurs especially in patients undergoing dialysis for the first time.\n\nDuring dialysis, urea in blood vessels is rapidly removed as compared to that in the interstitium. This decreases the osmolality of blood. This results in a fluid shift from the blood vessels into the interstitium and brain parenchyma, causing cerebral edema.\n\nIn order to prevent this, the rate of flow of dialysate must be adjusted to avoid the rapid removal of solutes.","t":"Renal Replacement Therapy","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is true about anaphylaxis associated with hemodialysis?","o":["Occur due to lack of cellulose in the membranes","Ethylene oxide is the cause of Type B reaction","Dialysis is discontinued in Type A reaction","Type A reaction associated with complement activation"],"a":2,"e":"Dialysis must be discontinued immediately in case of a type A reaction. It is an IgE-mediated immediate hypersensitivity reaction that can lead to shock and death.\n\nBio-incompatible cellulose-containing membranes are commonly associated with hemodialysis-associated anaphylaxis.\n\nThe reactions can be of two types:\n\nType A reaction\n Type B reaction\n\nIgE-mediated immediate hypersensitivity reaction in response to ethylene oxide. (used to sterilize dialyzers).\nDelayed reaction due to complement activation and cytokine release.\n\nOccur within minutes.\nOccur after hours.\n\nStop dialysis and give subcutaneous adrenaline or steroids.\n\n It is self-limiting and dialysis can be continued.","t":"Renal Replacement Therapy","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"A patient on hemodialysis may develop all of the following chronic complications except:","o":["Amyloidosis","Dementia","Bone disease","Hypovolemia"],"a":3,"e":"Hypovolemia is an acute complication of hemodialysis. The chronic complications of hemodialysis are:\n\n• Amyloidosis\n\n• Adynamic bone disease\n\n• Dementia (aluminum toxicity)","t":"Renal Replacement Therapy","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"A physician is evaluating a patient with carpal tunnel syndrome. He has been undergoing dialysis for the past 7 years. Deposition of which of the following is responsible for this condition?","o":["AA","Amyloid beta 1","Amyloid beta 2","Amyloid transthyretin"],"a":2,"e":"Carpal tunnel syndrome in the setting of chronic dialysis is suggestive of dialysis-associated amyloidosis. It occurs due to the deposition of amyloid ß-2 in tissues.\n\nThis condition is seen in patients undergoing long-term hemodialysis or continuous ambulatory peritoneal dialysis (CAPD). Amyloid deposition can lead to the following complications:\n\n• Destructive osteoarthropathies - carpal tunnel syndrome, flexor tenosynovitis\n\n• Subchondral bone cysts and pathologic fractures\n\n• Paraplegia - due to deposits along paravertebral ligaments and intervertebral discs\n\n• Visceral involvement - GI tract, heart, tongue\n\nManagement involves providing symptomatic relief by means of analgesics, anti-inflammatory drugs, physical and occupational therapy, and surgical procedures. Renal transplantation is the treatment of choice.","t":"Renal Replacement Therapy","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"A 57-year-old man is referred urgently for management of pulmonary edema. He was diagnosed with stage-5 CKD 3 weeks ago but was reluctant to start dialysis. On examination, he is confused and has a flapping tremor. Investigations are as follows. Which of his clinical features will not improve with dialysis?\n\nSerum urea - 99 mg/dl\n\nSerum creatinine - 5.1 mg/dl\n\nSerum calcium - 7.1 mg/dl\n\nArterial blood gas - Metabolic acidosis","o":["Pulmonary edema","Metabolic acidosis","Uremic encephalopathy","Renal osteodystrophy"],"a":3,"e":"Renal osteodystrophy does not show improvement with dialysis.\n\nFailure of renal function leads to decreased renal vitamin D production. The resulting hypocalcemia and hyperphosphatemia, in turn, lead to secondary hyperparathyroidism. This can be corrected only by vitamin D and calcium supplementation.\n\nOther manifestations of renal dysfunction and uremia show improvement with regular dialysis sessions.","t":"Renal Replacement Therapy","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"What is the most common cause of death in patients undergoing chronic dialysis?","o":["Cardiovascular causes","Electrolyte abnormality","Metabolic acidosis","Encephalopathy"],"a":0,"e":"Cardiovascular disease due to accelerated atherosclerosis is the major cause of death in patients with ESRD on chronic dialysis.\n\nCardiovascular mortality is higher in dialysis patients compared to patients after transplantation.","t":"Renal Replacement Therapy","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"You are educating a patient about performing peritoneal dialysis at home. During this process, how much fluid is infused into the peritoneum?","o":["1.5-3 L","2-4 L","3-5 L","0.5-1.5 L"],"a":0,"e":"In peritoneal dialysis, 1.5–3 L of dialysis fluid is infused into the peritoneal cavity.\n\nIn this process, the peritoneum itself acts as a semipermeable membrane. A peritoneal catheter is used to infuse the fluid into the cavity. It is allowed to dwell for a set period of time usually 2–4 hours to allow for the removal of metabolic waste products. The dialysis fluid contains osmotically active agents such as glucose or icodextrin.\n\nThe following image shows the process of peritoneal dialysis.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/203da591884e41c9a8aa8fd28a1db205x592x555.PNG"],"t":"Renal Replacement Therapy","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"A 56-year-old man presents with fever and severe abdominal pain for 2 days. He has been on peritoneal dialysis for the past 1 year. On examination, there is abdominal distension, guarding, and rebound tenderness. Which of the following is the most common cause of this condition?","o":["Gram-positive cocci","Gram-positive bacilli","Gram-negative cocci","Gram-negative bacilli"],"a":0,"e":"This clinical scenario in a patient undergoing peritoneal dialysis is suggestive of peritonitis. It is most commonly caused by gram-positive cocci such as Staphylococcus, which spread from the skin.","t":"Renal Replacement Therapy","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"A woman is started on continuous ambulatory peritoneal dialysis. Which of the following complications is she unlikely to develop?","o":["Residual uremia","Metabolic disturbances","Insulin resistance","Weight loss"],"a":3,"e":"Weight gain is a complication of peritoneal dialysis, not weight loss. The complications of peritoneal dialysis include:\n\n• Peritonitis\n\n• Catheter-associated non-peritonitis infections\n\n• Weight gain\n\n• Metabolic disturbances - hypoproteinemia and hyperglycemia\n\n• Residual uremia - especially among patients with no residual kidney function\n\n• Insulin resistance\n\n• Hypertriglyceridemia","t":"Renal Replacement Therapy","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is incorrect about renal transplants?","o":["ABO and HLA mismatch are relative contraindications","Contraindicated in overt AIDS and active hepatitis","Complications in donor include hypertension and focal segmental glomerulosclerosis","The most common cause of death after renal transplant is infections"],"a":3,"e":"The most common cause of death in renal transplant recipients is cardiovascular causes. It occurs in > 50% of patients.\n\nOther complications in recipients include:\n\n• Infections\n\n• Most common - CMV.\n\n• Most specific - BK (polyoma) virus\n\n• Most common fungal - pneumocystis.\n\n• Malignancy - most commonly of skin and lip\n\nDonors can develop long-term complications related to premature failure of the single remaining kidney including:\n\n• Hypertension\n\n• Proteinuria\n\n• Focal segmental glomerulosclerosis (FSGS)\n\nABO and HLA mismatch are relative, not absolute contraindications due to the availability of desensitization procedures. The absolute contraindications for transplantation are as follows:\n\nRecipient\nDonor\n\nAny active infections/ malignancy\nAny active infections (including HIV)/ malignancy\n\nShortened life expectancy due to other causes\nUncontrolled diabetes/ hypertension\n\nActive substance abuse/ uncontrolled psychiatric condition\nUncontrolled psychiatric condition or suicidality\n\nTreatment nonadherence\nAge < 18 or lack of mental capacity\n\nReversible renal failure","t":"Renal Replacement Therapy","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is incorrect about rejection reactions following a kidney transplant?","o":["Acute rejection can occur due to both type 4 and type 2 hypersensitivity reactions","Hyperacute rejection is a type 2 hypersensitivity reaction","Induction therapy for prophylaxis of rejection is cyclosporine, azathioprine, prednisolone.","Alemtuzumab is monoclonal antibody against CD52 used for immunosuppression"],"a":2,"e":"Cyclosporine, azathioprine, prednisolone constitute the prophylactic maintenance therapy used after renal transplant.\n\nImmunosuppressive therapy is given to reduce the risk of transplant rejection reactions. The following regimen is commonly followed:\n\n• Induction therapy:\n\n• Given at the time of transplant to reduce the risk of early acute rejection.\n\n• The preferred treatment is anti-lymphocyte serum and alemtuzumab.\n\n• Maintenance therapy:\n\n• Given to all kidney transplant patients except when the donor and recipient are identical twins.\n\n• The most common combination used is :\ncyclosporine, azathioprine, and prednisolone (also known as triple therapy).\n\n• Belatacept is a co-stimulatory blocking antibody approved by the FDA for maintenance therapy.\n\nRejection reactions can be hyperacute, acute, or chronic. Treatment of rejection is as follows:\n\n• First episode - IV prednisolone for 3 days\n\n• Failure to respond to prednisone or second episode - anti-thymocyte globulin","t":"Renal Replacement Therapy","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"A 45-year-old man with polycystic kidney disease underwent renal transplantation 3 months ago. Which of the following infections is he at the highest risk of?","o":["Candida","Pneumocystis","BK virus","Cytomegalovirus"],"a":3,"e":"The most common opportunistic infection occurring 1-6 months after renal transplant is Cytomegalovirus (CMV).\n\nOpportunistic infections after renal transplant\n\n< 1 month\n1-6 months\n> 6 months\n\nWound infections\n\nHerpesvirus\n\nOral candidiasis\n\nUrinary tract infection\n\nPneumocystis carinii\n\nCytomegalovirus\n\nLegionella\n\nListeria\n\nHepatitis B, C\n\nAspergillus\n\nNocardia\n\nBK virus\n\nHerpes zoster\n\nHepatitis B, C\n\nPneumocystis carinii is rare these days due to universal prophylaxis for 6-12 months following transplantation.","t":"Renal Replacement Therapy","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following lesions does not recur in the transplanted kidney?","o":["Diabetic nephropathy","Membranous glomerulonephritis","Lupus nephritis","Alport syndrome"],"a":3,"e":"Alport syndrome does not recur in the transplanted kidney. This is because it occurs due to an inherited defect in type 4 collagen in the glomerular basement membrane. This defect is absent in the transplanted kidney.\n\nDiabetic nephropathy, membranous glomerulonephritis, and lupus nephritis can recur in the transplanted kidney.","t":"Renal Replacement Therapy","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"Patients with which of the following conditions have the highest risk of disease recurrence after renal transplant?","o":["Lupus nephritis","Membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis","Diabetic nephropathy","Membranous glomerulonephritis"],"a":2,"e":"Patients with diabetic nephropathy have the highest risk of disease recurrence after renal transplant. Nearly 52% of transplant recipients have histological evidence of diabetic kidney disease/mesangial sclerosis at 10 years after transplantation.\n\nThe recurrence rates of the other conditions are as follows:\n\n• Membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis: 20-48%\n\n• Membranous glomerulonephritis: 10-45%\n\n• Lupus nephritis: 2-11%","t":"Renal Replacement Therapy","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"What is the average life expectancy in patients following a living-donor kidney transplant?","o":["10 years","14 years","18 years","20 years"],"a":1,"e":"The average life expectancy of a living-donor graft is around 14 years and that of a deceased-donor graft is around 10 years.","t":"Renal Replacement Therapy","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is true in patients with autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease?","o":["ADPKD progresses to end-stage renal disease in early adulthood","Calcium oxalate stones are the most common kidney stones","Renal cyst infections are rarely seen","Cardiovascular complications are a major cause of mortality"],"a":3,"e":"Cardiovascular complications are the most common cause of mortality in autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease (ADPKD). Infections are the second most common cause.\n\nADPKD is usually clinically silent. Many cases remain asymptomatic until the fifth decade of life and are diagnosed incidentally. PKD-1 mutations cause more severe disease than PKD-2 mutations. The following clinical features may be seen in symptomatic patients:\n\n• Abdominal mass\n\n• Back or flank pain\n\n• Recurrent UTIs - Renal cyst infections by Gram-negative bacteria are common\n\n• Nephrolithiasis - most commonly uric acid stones\n\n• Hematuria\n\n• Cardiac:\n\n• Hypertension - early feature\n\n• Left ventricular hypertrophy\n\n• Mitral valve prolapse, valvular insufficiency\n\n• Extra-renal:\n\n• Intracranial aneurysms\n\n• Hepatic cysts\n\nAs the cysts grow they compress on the surrounding normal tissue which leads to a progressive reduction in the GFR. Eventually, it leads to end-stage renal disease (ESRD) by the fifth decade of life.","t":"Cystic And Inherited Disorders Of The Kidney","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"A 48-year-old woman developed a sudden severe headache and vomiting. Her neighbor rushed her to the hospital where she was declared dead on arrival. She lived alone and her family history is unknown. An autopsy revealed a subarachnoid hemorrhage and the following findings in her kidneys. Which of the following is incorrect about this condition?","o":["Mutation of PKD-1 is responsible for 85% of cases","PKD-2 mutation has higher risk of end-stage renal disease than PKD-1","The PKD genes code for the polycystin protein","Cysts are formed only in 5% of the tubules of the kidney"],"a":1,"e":"This clinical scenario with a subarachnoid hemorrhage (SAH) and multiple renal cysts is suggestive of autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease (ADPKD). The PKD-1 mutation has a higher risk of end-stage renal disease (ESRD) than PKD-2.\n\nADPKD is a disorder which is characterized by progressive bilateral formation of renal cysts. It is caused by the following mutations:\n\n• PKD1 gene on chromosome 16 (85%) - codes for polycystin-1\n\n• PKD2 gene on chromosome 4 (15%) - codes for polycystin-2\n\nPolycystins are transmembrane proteins that are present on the primary cilia of the tubular cells. The mutated proteins in ADPKD alter the normal functioning and triggers cell growth and cyst formation.\n\nThough cysts are formed only in 5% of the tubules of the kidney, they compress surrounding renal tissue due to their enormous growth. This results in loss of renal function.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/884afe338d444a87befa49070ea41a2f.PNG"],"t":"Cystic And Inherited Disorders Of The Kidney","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"In patients with ADPKD, cysts are least likely to be present in which of the following sites?","o":["Arachnoid membranes","Lungs","Pancreas","Seminal vesicles"],"a":1,"e":"In patients with ADPKD, cysts in the lungs are very rare.\n\nThere are very few case reports with lung cysts in ADPKD patients, but the association between ADPKD and lung cysts has not been proved. However, there is a three-fold increase in the incidence of bronchiectasis in ADPKD patients.\n\nThe common extrarenal manifestations of ADPKD include:\n\n• Hepatic cysts (50-70%) - most common\n\n• Seminal vesicle cysts (40%) - can result in infertility\n\n• Arachnoid membrane cysts (8%) - usually asymptomatic, may increase the risk of subdural hematoma\n\n• Pancreatic cysts (7-36%)\n\n• Splenic cysts","t":"Cystic And Inherited Disorders Of The Kidney","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"A 60-year-old man presents with loss of weight and appetite over the last 3 months. He was diagnosed with autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease 15 years ago. A CT abdomen reveals bilateral, multicentric renal masses along with enlarged retroperitoneal lymph nodes. What is the likely diagnosis?","o":["Clear cell RCC","Papillary cell RCC","Chromophobe RCC","Sarcomatoid RCC"],"a":3,"e":"This clinical scenario is suggestive of renal cell carcinoma (RCC) with lymph node metastases. The sarcomatoid type of RCC is commonly associated with ADPKD.\n\nRenal cell carcinoma (RCC) is a relatively rare complication of ADPKD. There does not seem to be an increased frequency of RCC in these patients compared to the general population. However, when it occurs, it tends to be bilateral, multicentric and of sarcomatoid type.","t":"Cystic And Inherited Disorders Of The Kidney","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"A 25-year-old woman presented with persistent headaches over the last 1 month. Her father and paternal uncle suffered from kidney disease and both died in their 50's of a 'brain bleed'. Her blood pressure is 160/110 mm Hg and had also been elevated during a previous visit. Which of the following investigations should be avoided in this patient?","o":["Ultrasonography of the abdomen","Contrast enhanced CT of the abdomen","Non-contrast CT scan of the abdomen","MR angiography of the cerebral vasculature"],"a":1,"e":"This clinical scenario is suggestive of autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease (ADPKD). These patients usually have impaired renal function. Hence, contrast-enhanced CT (CECT) would usually be avoided due to the risk of contrast nephrotoxicity.\n\nThe following investigations are done in patients with ADPKD:\n\n• USG abdomen - for screening and diagnosis\n\n• Non-contrast CT abdomen - more sensitive and picks up cysts of smaller size\n\n• MR-angiography of brain - pre-symptomatic screening for intracerebral aneurysms","t":"Cystic And Inherited Disorders Of The Kidney","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"How many renal cysts on USG are required to diagnose a 45-year-old patient with autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease?","o":["At least 2 cysts in each kidney","At least 3 cysts in each kidney","At least 4 cysts in each kidney","Even a single cyst in each kidney"],"a":0,"e":"Between 40-59 years of age, the presence of ≥2 cysts in each kidney is needed to diagnose ADPKD.\n\nThis condition is diagnosed based on the USG findings using the Ravine’s criteria:\n\nAge (in years)\nImaging findings\n\n15-39\nTotal of ≥3 cysts, unilateral or bilateral\n\n40-59\n≥2 cysts in each kidney\n\n≥ 60\n≥ 4 cysts in each kidney","t":"Cystic And Inherited Disorders Of The Kidney","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"A 56-year-old woman on dialysis is being planned for a renal transplant with her son as an organ donor. He undergoes a USG scan as part of routine donor evaluation. The following findings are seen. Which of the following will you not recommend for the son?","o":["Increased fluid intake","Angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors","Surgical decompression of cysts","Tolvaptan"],"a":2,"e":"This clinical scenario and USG showing a cystic appearance of the kidney is suggestive of asymptomatic autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease (ADPKD). Surgical decompression is not a routine procedure performed in these patients. It may be required only in cases of very large cysts causing persistent pain and infected cysts.\n\nThe management includes the following:\n\n• Analgesics\n\n• Increased fluid intake\n\n• Prevents renal stone formation\n\n• Reduces growth of cysts by suppressing ADH release\n\n• ACE inhibitors and ARBs\n\n• Blood pressure control\n\n• Slowing of cyst growth\n\n• Tolvaptan (V2 receptor antagonist)\n\n• Reduce pain and slow the decline in renal function\n\n• Renal transplant\n\n• Definitive management\n\n• Pre-transplant nephrectomy may be required in patients with very large polycystic kidneys","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f38a100b1818472186a17e91e059b326.JPEG"],"t":"Cystic And Inherited Disorders Of The Kidney","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"A 24-year-old G2P1L0 female patient came for an antenatal check-up at 26 weeks gestation. On examination, her abdominal girth was less for gestational age and the fetal parts were easily palpable. Medical records from her previous pregnancy showed that the deceased infant had been positive for a PKHD1 mutation. Which of the following statements is correct regarding the most likely diagnosis of the fetus?","o":["It is commonly an asymptomatic and mild disease","In severe cases, it can be diagnosed antenatally on USG","Respiratory insufficiency is uncommon in newborns","It is commonly diagnosed at 5-10 years of age"],"a":1,"e":"A PKHD1 mutation in the deceased infant is suggestive of autosomal recessive polycystic kidney disease (ARPKD). As the woman has signs of oligohydramnios, it is likely that this fetus also has ARPKD. Severe cases of ARPKD can be diagnosed in-utero with a USG. Prenatal testing can also be done.\n\nARPKD is a hereditary renal disease of childhood caused by a mutation in the PKHD1 gene on chromosome 6. It codes for fibrocystin/ polyductin (FPC), a membrane protein present in the cells of the cortical and medullary collecting ducts.\n\nAffected fetuses show greatly enlarged kidneys and reduced fetal urine production. This leads to oligohydramnios and pulmonary hypoplasia. About 30% of neonates die soon after birth due to respiratory insufficiency. Most deaths occur < 1 month of life. Others develop chronic kidney disease and hypertension in early childhood.","t":"Cystic And Inherited Disorders Of The Kidney","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not a typical feature seen in patients with autosomal recessive polycystic kidney disease?","o":["Biliary dysgenesis","Portal hypertension","Caroli's disease","Hepatic cysts"],"a":3,"e":"Hepatic cysts are not a typical feature of autosomal recessive polycystic kidney disease (ARPKD). They occur only in rare cases.\n\nARPKD is usually diagnosed in the neonatal period. These patients commonly have the following findings:\n\n• Hypertension\n\n• Chronic renal insufficiency\n\n• Congenital hepatic fibrosis\n\n• Biliary dysgenesis with periportal fibrosis is seen\n\n• Can lead to portal hypertension\n\n• Caroli’s disease - dilatation of intrahepatic bile ducts\n\nNote: Caroli's disease is also associated with medullary sponge kidney.","t":"Cystic And Inherited Disorders Of The Kidney","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"Match the following:\n\n Disease\nGene\n\n1.\nMedullary cystic kidney disease-1 (MCKD-1)\na.\nUMOD\n\n2.\nMedullary cystic kidney disease-2 (MCKD 2)\nb.\nPKHD1\n\n3.\nAdolescent nephronophthisis\nc.\nMUC1\n\n4.\nAutosomal recessive polycystic kidney disease (ARPKD)\nd.\nNPHP2","o":["1-d, 2-b, 3-c, 4-a","1-b, 2-d, 3-a, 4-c","1-c, 2-a, 3-d, 4-b","1-a, 2-c, 3-b, 4-d"],"a":2,"e":"The gene mutations causing the following cystic disorders are:\n\n• Medullary cystic kidney disease-1 (MCKD-1) - MUC1\n\n• Medullary cystic kidney disease-2 (MCKD-2) - UMOD\n\n• Autosomal recessive polycystic kidney disease (ARPKD) - PKHD1\n\n• Adolescent nephronophthisis - NPHP 1-20\n\nMedullary cystic kidney disease (MCKD) is an autosomal dominant disorder. It presents with a late-onset, slowly progressive renal failure. Renal cysts are not common but can be seen occasionally. Medullary cystic kidney diseases are of two types, MCKD1 and MCKD2.\n\nMCKD1 is due to a defect in the mucin 1 (MUC1) gene. The mutated gene codes for a large protein fragment that has a toxic effect on the tubules. It is not associated with elevated uric acid levels and gout.\n\nMCKD2 is due to a defect in the UMOD gene, which codes for the protein uromodulin. It is associated with hyperuricemia and gout.","t":"Cystic And Inherited Disorders Of The Kidney","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is an autosomal recessive renal cystic disease?","o":["Tuberous sclerosis","Medullary cystic kidney","Nephronophthisis","Von Hippel-Lindau disease"],"a":2,"e":"Nephronophthisis is an autosomal recessive renal cystic disease.\n\nIt belongs to a category of conditions called ciliopathies. It occurs due to an underlying defect in the primary cilia of the tubular epithelial cells. The cilia play an important role in cell signaling and maintenance of cell growth, proliferation, and secretion.\n\nOther ciliopathies include autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease, autosomal recessive polycystic kidney disease, and medullary cystic kidney disease.","t":"Cystic And Inherited Disorders Of The Kidney","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"What is the most common inherited cause of kidney failure in children?","o":["Medullary sponge kidney","Nephronophthisis","Medullary cystic kidney disease","Autosomal recessive polycystic kidney disease"],"a":1,"e":"The most common inherited cause of kidney failure in childhood is nephronophthisis.\n\nNephronophthisis (NPHP) refers to a group of related, but genetically distinct, autosomal recessive disorders. Mutations are seen in the NPHP genes 1-20. These genes code for nephrocystins which are expressed on the kidney tubules.\n\nNPHP is classified into infantile, juvenile, and adolescent forms. The juvenile form is the most common form. Common features include tubulointerstitial fibrosis, renal cysts, and chronic kidney disease.","t":"Cystic And Inherited Disorders Of The Kidney","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"A child with Joubert syndrome will have which of the following conditions?","o":["Juvenile nephronophthisis","Medullary cystic kidney disease","Medullary sponge kidney","Autosomal recessive polycystic kidney disease"],"a":0,"e":"Joubert’s syndrome is defined by the presence of juvenile nephronophthisis (NPHP) and multiple neurologic findings.\n\nA few examples of multiorgan syndromes associated with nephronophthisis are:\n\n• Senior-Loken syndrome: NPHP with retinitis pigmentosa\n\n• Joubert syndrome: NPHP with multiple neurologic findings\n\n• Bardet-Biedl syndrome: NPHP with truncal obesity, cognitive impairment, retinal dystrophy, and polydactyly","t":"Cystic And Inherited Disorders Of The Kidney","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"The following finding on intravenous pyelography is seen in which of the following conditions?","o":["Medullary cystic kidney disease","Medullary sponge kidney","Senior loken syndrome","Infantile nephronophthisis"],"a":1,"e":"The given image shows a paintbrush or bouquet of flowers appearance on intravenous pyelography (IVP). It occurs in medullary sponge kidney (MSK).\n\nMSK is a sporadic benign condition that is often diagnosed incidentally. It is a developmental defect affecting the formation of collecting tubules and results in cystic dilatation of the medullary and papillary portions of collecting ducts. It is characterized by medullary cysts that are of variable size. It is associated with distal RTA which leads to hypercalciuria. The hypercalciuria leads to a tendency to form calcium oxalate and calcium phosphate stones. The patients can also have recurrent UTIs and hematuria.\n\nCT urography has largely replaced intravenous pyelography as the investigation of choice.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3706ea851a9c41e9969b4712651b6810.JPEG"],"t":"Cystic And Inherited Disorders Of The Kidney","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"What acid-base disturbance is seen in patients with renal tubular acidosis?","o":["Normal anion gap hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis.","High anion gap hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis","Normal anion gap hypochloremic metabolic acidosis","High anion gap hypochloremic metabolic acidosis."],"a":0,"e":"In renal tubular acidosis (RTA), there is normal anion gap hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis.\n\nRTA refers to a group of disorders in which there is impaired tubular transport of H+ and/or HCO3- despite relatively well-preserved renal function. There are 4 types:\n\n• Type I - impaired H+ secretion (distal), leading to decreased HCO3- production\n\n• Type II - impaired HCO3- reabsorption (proximal)\n\n• Type III - carbonic anhydrase deficiency causing defects in the transport of both H+ and HCO3- (mixed)\n\n• Type IV - hypoaldosteronism (hyperkalemic)\n\nThe overall deficiency of HCO3- in all the types is compensated by Cl- secretion. Hence, the serum anion gap remains normal.","t":"Renal Tubular Disorders","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"What is the most common type of renal tubular acidosis?","o":["Type 1","Type 2","Type 3","Type 4"],"a":3,"e":"The most common type of renal tubular acidosis (RTA) is the type 4 or hyperkalemic type.","t":"Renal Tubular Disorders","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is incorrect about type 4 renal tubular acidosis?","o":["The most common cause is diabetes mellitus","It can occurs in patients with interstitial renal diseases","It is always congenital","It is caused by hyporeninemic hypoaldosteronism"],"a":2,"e":"Type 4 renal tubular acidosis can be a congenital or acquired disorder.\n\nIt is caused by conditions that result in hypoaldosteronism or hyporeninemic hypoaldosteronism including:\n\n• Diabetic nephropathy - most common\n\n• Tubulointerstitial disease\n\n• Addison's disease\n\n• Obstructive uropathy\n\n• Drugs like NSAIDs, ACEI, ARBs can increase the risk\n\n• Psuedohypoaldosteronism type 1 (due to aldosterone resistance)\n\n• Psuedohypoaldosteronism type 2/ gordons syndrome (due to defects in Na-Cl cotransporter)\n\n• Congenital isolated hypoaldosteronism (due to enzyme deficiency)","t":"Renal Tubular Disorders","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"A 52-year-old man presented with dizziness and infrequent urination. He was diagnosed with stage 4 chronic kidney disease last month. Investigations reveal metabolic acidosis and hyperkalemia. What is the likely diagnosis?","o":["Type 1 RTA","Type 2 RTA","Type 3 RTA","Type 4 RTA"],"a":3,"e":"Hyperkalemia is a characteristic feature of type 4 RTA or generalized distal RTA. It commonly occurs in patients with chronic kidney disease (CKD).\n\nIn CKD patients, renal dysfunction results in hyperkalemia only when the GFR is severely impaired (stage 5). Hyperkalemia in the earlier stages of CKD is suggestive of type 4 RTA.\n\nIn CKD, volume overload leads to low levels of renin and, hence, aldosterone. This leads to Na+ loss and K+ retention in the distal tubule. The resultant hyperkalemia causes impaired NH3 synthesis in the proximal tubule. Hence, H+ cannot be excreted as NH4+ ions. Retained H+ ions lead to reduced HCO3- production and acidosis.","t":"Renal Tubular Disorders","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following would not be seen in patients with type 1 renal tubular acidosis?","o":["Reduced NH₄⁺ excretion","Serum potassium &lt; 3.5 mEq/L","Urinary pH is &lt; 5.5 after acid loading","Increased bone resorption"],"a":2,"e":"Urinary pH will be > 5.5 after acid loading in RTA type 1.\n\nIn this condition, there is a defect in H+ secretion by the distal tubules. Hence, there is a failure of urine acidification. H+ combines with NH3 to form NH₄⁺ and thus in RTA type 1, NH4+ excretion is reduced. In the acid loading test, acidosis is induced by the administration of NH4Cl (releases H+). In normal individuals, this results in increased excretion of H+, leading to acidic urine (pH < 5.5). However, this fails to occur in RTA type 1.\n\nImpaired secretion of H+ leads to potassium wasting and hypokalemia (via the H+/K+ATPase antiporter). The resulting metabolic acidosis causes bone demineralization and resorption.","t":"Renal Tubular Disorders","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"A 6-month-old infant is being evaluated for failure to thrive. Lab investigations are as follows. Hypokalemia is found to improve following alkalinization therapy. What type of renal stones is this infant at a higher risk of developing?\n\nInvestigation\nValue\n\nSerum sodium\n136 mEq/L\n\nSerum potassium\n3.1 mEq/L\n\nSerum pH\n7.1\n\nSerum anion gap\n11\n\nUrinary pH\n6.4","o":["Uric acid","Citrate","Cystine","Calcium phosphate"],"a":3,"e":"This clinical scenario is suggestive of type 1 RTA. In this condition, calcium phosphate and oxalate stones are formed in the distal convoluting tubules (DCT).\n\nIn this condition, there is a defect in H+ secretion by the distal tubules. This leads to:\n\n• Normal anion gap metabolic acidosis\n\n• Urinary pH > 5.5\n\n• Hypokalemia\n\n• Impaired H+ secretion causes K+ wasting\n\n• It improves following alkalinization therapy with NaHCO3\n\nAcidemia leads to hypercalcemia and then hypercalciuria. There is also hypocitraturia as acidemia induces increased cell citrate utilization. Normally, citrate in the urine binds to calcium and increases its solubility. In the absence of citrate in the urine, calcium begins to precipitate. This leads to nephrocalcinosis and calcium phosphate and oxalate stones.","t":"Renal Tubular Disorders","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following would you not expect in a patient with Lowe's syndrome?","o":["Glaucoma","Cataract","Hypertonia","Intellectual disability"],"a":2,"e":"Hypertonia is not a feature of Lowe's syndrome or oculo-cerebro-renal syndrome. The following clinical features are seen in this condition:\n\n• Hypotonia\n\n• Cataract\n\n• Glaucoma\n\n• Intellectual disability\n\n• Renal tubular acidosis (RTA) type 2","t":"Renal Tubular Disorders","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is unlikely to be seen in patients with Dent's disease?","o":["X-linked inheritance","Inactivating mutation of chloride channel","Recurrent kidney stones","Distal renal tubular defect"],"a":3,"e":"Dent's disease is an inherited cause of type 2 RTA. A proximal renal tubular defect is seen in this condition, not a distal tubular defect.\n\nIt is an X-linked recessive condition. It is caused by inactivating mutations in CLCN5, a chloride transporter expressed in endosomes of the proximal tubule. The resulting dysfunction of the proximal convoluted tubule and failure of reabsorption of substances leads to:\n\n• Hypercalciuria\n\n• Hypophosphatemia\n\n• Recurrent kidney stones","t":"Renal Tubular Disorders","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"Patients with which of the following are not at risk of developing type 2 renal tubular acidosis?","o":["Wilson's disease","Galactosemia","Hereditary fructose intolerance","Medullary sponge kidney"],"a":3,"e":"Medullary sponge kidney is a cause of RTA type 1, not type 2.\n\nThe causes of RTA type 2 include:\n\n• Amyloidosis\n\n• Wilson's disease\n\n• Galactosemia\n\n• Hereditary fructose intolerance\n\n• Cystinosis\n\n• Lowe syndrome\n\n• Drugs - tenofovir, acetazolamide, topiramate\n\nThe causes of RTA type 1 include:\n\n• Familial defects\n\n• Autoimmune conditions - SLE, Sjogren syndrome\n\n• Drugs - amphotericin, lithium, aminoglycosides, ifosfamide\n\n• Medullary sponge kidney","t":"Renal Tubular Disorders","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is incorrect about renal tubular acidosis type 2?","o":["It is associated with hypophosphatemia","It can be caused by Lowe's syndrome","Renal stones may occur in patients","Minimum urinary pH is &lt; 5.5"],"a":2,"e":"Renal stones are not a feature of type 2 or proximal RTA. This is because high levels of citrate in the urine bind with calcium and prevent stone formation.\n\nIn this condition, the cells of the proximal convoluted tubule (PCT) fail to reabsorb HCO3-. In the initial stages, this HCO3- loss leads to a urine pH > 5.5. However, as the body's HCO3- stores are depleted, the urinary pH falls to < 5.5 as the secretion of H+ in the DCT remains intact. Hence, the minimum urinary pH is < 5.5.\n\nIt can be caused by Lowe's syndrome but a majority of type 2 RTA cases are associated with Fanconi's syndrome. This syndrome is characterized by a generalized defect of reabsorption of various substances at the PCT. This leads to loss of HCO3-, glucose, amino acids, phosphate, citrate, and calcium in the urine. Loss of phosphate leads to vitamin-D resistant hypophosphatemic rickets /osteomalacia.\n\nNote: An exception is Dent's disease, a genetic cause of RTA type 2 in which renal stones are present.","t":"Renal Tubular Disorders","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"A 14-month-old baby was brought with complaints of refusal to walk after a minor fall while playing. Imaging revealed a left tibial fracture along with generalized sclerosis and a bone-in-bone appearance. Additionally, his developmental milestones were found to be delayed. Investigations are as follows. What is the likely renal tubular defect present in this patient?\n\nSerum sodium - 137 mEq/L\n\nSerum potassium - 2.9 mEq/L\n\nSerum pH - 7.1\n\nSerum bicarbonate - 15 mEq/L\n\nSerum anion gap - 9\n\nUrine pH - 5.7","o":["Type 1 RTA","Type 2 RTA","Type 3 RTA","Type 4 RTA"],"a":2,"e":"Pathological fractures and a bone-in-bone appearance point towards a diagnosis of osteopetrosis. The lab values are significant for hypokalemia with normal anion gap metabolic acidosis. These findings are suggestive of renal tubular acidosis (RTA) type 3.\n\nType 3 or mixed RTA is a rare condition that usually occurs in neonates and infants. It is due to an autosomal recessive, inherited carbonic anhydrase-2 (CA-2) deficiency. This results in features of both distal and proximal RTA with:\n\n• Failure to reabsorb HCO3 at PCT\n\n• Failure to excrete H+ at DCT\n\nThis results in the following findings:\n\n• Normal anion gap metabolic acidosis\n\n• Hypokalemia\n\n• Urinary pH > 5.5\n\n• Positive urine anion gap\n\nAs CA-2 is widely expressed, mutations of this gene result in a condition called marble brain disease or Guibaud-Vainsel syndrome. It presents with multiple clinical findings including:\n\n• Cerebral calcifications\n\n• Mental retardation\n\n• Mixed RTA (Type 3 RTA)\n\n• Osteopetrosis","t":"Renal Tubular Disorders","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"A 30-year-old man is being evaluated for high blood pressure noted on multiple occasions. Investigations reveal metabolic alkalosis, suppressed renin levels, and high urine chloride levels. What is the likely cause?","o":["Bartter syndrome","Liddle syndrome","Gitelman syndrome","Fanconi syndrome"],"a":1,"e":"The clinical scenario with hypertension and metabolic alkalosis is suggestive of Liddle's syndrome.\n\nIt is an autosomal dominant condition due to a gain of function mutation in the ENaC gene. This gene codes for the epithelial sodium channels present in the kidney. This leads to increased sodium retention and results in a state of pseudo-hyperaldosteronism. The following findings are seen:\n\n• Hypertension\n\n• Hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis\n\n• Normal-low aldosterone\n\n• Low renin\n\nTreatment is with diuretics that directly block the ENaC channels, like amiloride and triamterene. Spironolactone is not effective as it is an aldosterone-receptor antagonist.","t":"Renal Tubular Disorders","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"A young patient presented with polyuria and polydipsia. His BP is 130/80 mm Hg. Laboratory parameters revealed serum K+ of 3.2 mmol/L, blood pH of 7.46, and hypomagnesemia. Urinary calcium was elevated. He is not on any medications. This condition is likely due to a defect in which of the following?","o":["Proximal tubule","Distal convoluted tubules","Ascending loop of Henle","Epithelial sodium channels"],"a":2,"e":"This clinical scenario is suggestive of Bartter syndrome. It occurs due to a defect in the Na+/K+/Cl- channel in the thick ascending loop of Henle.\n\nA defect in the Na+/K+/Cl- channel impairs the transport functions of the thick ascending loop of Henle. It results in the following features:\n\n• Polyuria and polydipsia\n\n• Natriuresis with high aldosterone (due to RAAS activation)\n\n• Hypokalemia\n\n• Metabolic alkalosis\n\n• Hypomagnesemia\n\n• Hypocalcemia and hypercalciuria\n\nThe clinical features of this condition are similar to the effects of loop diuretics. These drugs also act on the Na+/K+/Cl- channel. Hence, response to loop diuretics may be blunted. Treatment is with potassium-sparing diuretics like spironolactone and amiloride. NSAID's like indomethacin are second line drugs however they can reduce renal function.\n\nOption A: Defect in the proximal tubule results in Fanconi's syndrome. These patients have acidosis, with glycosuria, generalized aminoaciduria, and phosphaturia.\n\nOption B: Defect in the Na+/Cl- cotransporter in distal convoluted tubule (DCT) results in Gitelman syndrome. These patients also present with hypokalemic alkalosis and hypomagnesemia. However, they also develop, hypocalciuria, and chondrocalcinosis.\n\nOption D: Increased activation of the epithelial sodium channels (ENaC) channels results in Liddle syndrome. This causes increased reabsorption of Na+ exchanged for K+ causing hypokalemia. Increased Na absorption leads to hypertension.","t":"Renal Tubular Disorders","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"A 17-year-old boy is being evaluated for fatigue and muscle weakness for 3 months. On examination, Chvostek's sign is positive and BP is 90/60 mm Hg. Investigations show hypokalemia and hypercalcemia with metabolic alkalosis. What is the likely diagnosis?","o":["Bartter syndrome","Gitelman syndrome","Liddle syndrome","Fanconi's syndrome"],"a":1,"e":"Chvostek's sign (spasm of facial muscles on tapping the facial nerve) indicates tetany. In this patient with hypercalcemia, it is likely due to hypomagnesemia. These clinical features are suggestive of Gitelman syndrome.\n\nThis condition occurs due to a mutation of Na+/Cl- cotransporter in the distal collecting tubule (DCT). The resulting impairment of the tubular transport leads to the following features:\n\n• Mild natriuresis with normal-low BP\n\n• Increased renin and aldosterone\n\n• Hypokalemia\n\n• Metabolic alkalosis\n\n• Hypercalcemia and hypocalciuria\n\n• Hypomagnesemia\n\nThe clinical features of this condition are similar to the effects of thiazide diuretics. These drugs also act on the Na+/Cl- cotransporter. Hence, response to thiazide diuretics may be blunted. Treatment is with potassium-sparing diuretics like spironolactone and amiloride.\n\nOption A: Bartter syndrome affects the Na+/K+/Cl- channels. It causes hypocalcemia and hypercalciuria.\n\nOption C: Liddle syndrome causes increased activation of ENaC channels. It results in hypokalemia and hypertension.\n\nOption D: Fanconi's syndrome occurs due to a defect in proximal tubular function. It results in metabolic acidosis.","t":"Renal Tubular Disorders","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is true regarding renal vein thrombosis?","o":["Right renal vein is more commonly involved","Most common cause in children is nephrotic syndrome","Investigation of choice is doppler ultrasound of renal vein","Most common cause in elderly is renal cell carcinoma"],"a":3,"e":"The most common cause of renal vein thrombosis in the elderly is renal cell carcinoma (RCC).\n\nThe most common causes of renal vein thrombosis in different age groups are as follows:\n\n• Children - Dehydration, followed by sepsis\n\n• Adults - Nephrotic syndrome (most commonly membranous glomerulonephritis)\n\n• Elderly - Renal cell carcinoma\n\nThe left renal vein is more commonly involved and 2/3rd of cases are bilateral. Doppler USG is used for screening but the investigation of choice is CT angiography.","t":"Vascular Diseases Of Kidney","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"A 10-year-old child develops hematuria after 4 days of severe diarrhea. Peripheral smear shows fragmented RBCs and thrombocytopenia. Ultrasound reveals the enlargement of both kidneys. What is the likely diagnosis?","o":["Renal vein thrombosis","Hemolytic uremic syndrome","Henoch-Schonlein purpura","Post-infectious glomerulonephritis"],"a":0,"e":"This clinical scenario is suggestive of renal vein thrombosis secondary to dehydration.\n\nHematuria in children following diarrhea can be due to the following:\n\nHemolytic uremic syndrome\nRenal vein thrombosis\n\nOnset\n5-10 days after diarrhea\nDepends on severity of dehydration\n\n Renal failure\nPresent\nPresent\n\nHematuria\n+\n+\n\nPeripheral smear fragmented RBCs\n+\n+\n\nPlatelets\nDecreased\nDecreased\n\nUSG kidney\nNormal\nEnlarged due to congestion by renal vein thrombosis (bilateral in 67% cases)\n\nHenoch-Schonlein purpura would present 1-3 weeks after an upper respiratory infection. It causes hematuria, non-blanching rash over the buttocks, and abdominal pain.","t":"Vascular Diseases Of Kidney","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"What is the most common cause of hemolytic uremic syndrome globally?","o":["Shigella dysenteriae type I","E. coli O157: H7","Streptococcus pneumoniae","Autoimmunity"],"a":1,"e":"The most common cause of hemolytic uremic syndrome (HUS) globally is Escherichia coli O157:H7.\n\nThe causes of HUS are as follows:\n\n• Infections: Usually occur 5-10 days after diarrheal diseases due to bacterial exotoxins.\n\n• E.coli O157:H7 - Shiga-like toxin (verotoxin)\n\n• Shigella dysenteriae type I - Shiga toxin\n\n• Streptococcus pneumoniae\n\n• Autoimmunity\n\n• Complement activation (in atypical HUS)","t":"Vascular Diseases Of Kidney","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is false about hemolytic uremic syndrome?","o":["Symptoms and signs of HUS develops as diarrhea begins to improve","Only supportive managment of acute renal failure is done in typical HUS","Eculizumab improves microangiopathic hemolytic anemia in atypical HUS","Coomb's positive hemolytic anemia is seen in HUS"],"a":3,"e":"The hemolytic anemia in hemolytic uremic syndrome (HUS) occurs as a result of mechanical RBC destruction caused by microthrombi. Hence, Coomb's test is negative.\n\nThere are two types of HUS, namely typical and atypical.\n\nTypical HUS\nAtypical HUS\n\n• 5-10 days after diarrheal illness\n\n• E.coli O157:H7 or Shigella dysenteriae type I\n\n• Mutation in genes encoding complement regulatory protein\n\n• Shiga-like toxins produced cause endothelial damage.\n\n• This leads to platelet activation and thrombi.\n\n• Uncontrolled activation of the complement system\n\n• No specific treatment\n\n• Supportive management of renal failure\n\n• Plasma exchange to replace the deficient proteins\n\n• Eculizumab, a C5 inhibitor","t":"Vascular Diseases Of Kidney","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"A 56-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department with sudden onset headache and photophobia. On examination, she is disoriented and febrile. The investigations and peripheral smear are given below. Which of the following statements is incorrect regarding this disease?\n\nHemoglobin - 11.2 g/dl\n\nPlatelet count - 32,000/uL\n\nSerum creatinine - 3.9 mg/dl\n\nBlood urea nitrogen - 38 mg/dl","o":["It is caused by ADAMTS13 deficiency","20% patients will have falsely positive RA factor","Arrhythmias are a frequent cause of death","Mainstay of treatment is blood transfusion"],"a":3,"e":"This clinical scenario and image showing schistocytes (fragmented RBCs) in a woman are suggestive of thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (TTP). Blood transfusion is not preferred in TTP as it can aggravate the condition.\n\nTTP is a type of thrombotic microangiopathy that occurs due to deficiency or autoantibodies against the ADAMTS13 metalloprotease, which is responsible for cleaving von Willebrand factor multimers. In the absence of this protein, vWF multimers aggregate and form thrombi. It presents with a classical pentad of clinical features:\n\n1. Fever\n\n2. Thrombocytopenia\n\n3. Microangiopathic hemolytic anemia\n\n4. Renal failure\n\n5. Neurological manifestations\n\nIt is treated with plasmapheresis and rituximab.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c6e98035e75c40da89cc66ad1426c39a.PNG"],"t":"Vascular Diseases Of Kidney","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"What is the most common cause of clinically significant renal artery stenosis?","o":["Hypertensive nephrosclerosis","Atherosclerosis","Fibromuscular dysplasia","Infrarenal aortic dissection"],"a":1,"e":"The most common cause of clinically significant renal artery stenosis is atherosclerosis.\n\nRenal artery stenosis is a relatively common finding in older patients with chronic essential hypertension. However, it is considered clinically significant only when renovascular hypertension develops. It occurs in the case of:\n\n• > 70% stenosis in both renal arteries\n\n• > 50% stenosis with post stenotic dilatation\n\nThe causes of renal artery stenosis are:\n\n• Atherosclerosis (most common) - common in older patients with cardiovascular risk factors\n\n• Fibromuscular dysplasia – more common in young, white females and is usually bilateral\n\n• Less common causes include:\n\n• Thromboemboli\n\n• External compression by tumors\n\n• Systemic vasculitis\n\n• Antiphospholipid antibody syndrome\n\n• Transplant renal artery stenosis\n\n• Infrarenal aortic aneurysms and dissections\n\nThe investigation of choice for renal artery stenosis is renal angiography.","t":"Vascular Diseases Of Kidney","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements is incorrect about fibromuscular dysplasia?","o":["String of beads appearance on angiography","Predominantly affects females","Most commonly affected peripheral artery is subclavian artery","Segmental, non-atheromatous dysplastic angiopathy"],"a":2,"e":"This clinical scenario and the string of beads appearance on CT angiography is characteristic of fibromuscular dysplasia. The most common peripheral (limb) arteries affected are the iliac arteries.\n\nFibromuscular dysplasia is a segmental, non-atheromatous, dysplastic type of angiopathy that predominantly affects females. The most common subtype is medial fibroplasia. It is one of the major causes of renovascular hypertension. The most common sites of involvement in decreasing order of frequency are as follows:\n\n1. Renal arteries\n\n2. Carotid arteries\n\n3. Peripheral (limb) arteries - most commonly iliac arteries\n\nOn angiography, a string of beads appearance is seen as shown below.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d4bec349e17a4253a66efe023d9dbf21x720x396.JPEG"],"t":"Vascular Diseases Of Kidney","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is unlikely in patients with atheroembolic kidney disease?","o":["Livedo reticularis","Aortic aneurysms","Transient eosinophilia","Normal complement levels"],"a":3,"e":"Atheroembolic kidney disease is associated with decreased complement levels.\n\nAtheroembolic kidney disease is more frequent in males with a history of diabetes, hypertension, and ischemic heart disease. Most clinical atheroembolic events follow angiographic procedures, often of the coronary vessels.\n\nIt is associated with aortic aneurysms and renal artery stenosis. Systemic embolic manifestations such as fever, abdominal pain, and weight loss are also present, although cutaneous manifestations are more common like livedo reticularis and localized toe gangrene.\n\nThe lab findings include:\n\n• Rising serum creatinine\n\n• Transient eosinophilia\n\n• Elevated erythrocyte sedimentation rate\n\n• Hypocomplementemia\n\nDefinitive diagnosis depends on kidney biopsy which demonstrates microvessel occlusion with cholesterol crystals that leave a cleft in the vessel as shown below.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f94586c7fa5242cfb5269b7961062b68x720x480.JPEG"],"t":"Vascular Diseases Of Kidney","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"A 50-year-old patient is being evaluated for uncontrolled hypertension despite being on 3 antihypertensive drugs. He has a history of myocardial infarction which was managed with percutaneous intervention last month. On examination, an abdominal bruit is noted. Lab values indicate elevated serum urea and creatinine. Which of the following investigations is the gold standard test to diagnose this condition?","o":["MR angiography","Renal arteriography","CT angiography","Duplex Doppler Ultrasound"],"a":1,"e":"This clinical scenario is suggestive of renal artery stenosis. Renal intra-arterial angiography is the gold standard for diagnosis.In a patient with hypertension and increased creatinine, an ultrasound of the abdomen must be performed first to visualize the renal size and morphology to look for a unilateral shrunken kidney and thereby renal artery stenosis.\n\nOption A: MR angiography is less preferred as gadolinium contrast can lead to nephrogenic fibrosis, particularly in patients with reduced kidney function.\n\nOption C: CT scan with image reconstruction provides an excellent image but also carries a risk of contrast nephropathy.\n\nOption D: Renal arterial doppler ultrasound measures renal artery velocities and can predict hemodynamically important lesions. However, it is not the gold standard test. Duplex ultrasonography is used as a screening investigation.","t":"Vascular Diseases Of Kidney","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following drugs must be avoided in patients with bilateral renal artery stenosis?","o":["ACE inhibitors","Loop diuretics","Beta blockers","Thiazide diuretics"],"a":0,"e":"ACE inhibitors must be avoided in patients with bilateral renal artery stenosis (RAS).\n\nIn renal artery stenosis, blood flow to the kidney is reduced, thereby reducing blood flow to the afferent arterioles. Hence, constriction of efferent arterioles is needed to maintain hydrostatic pressure within the glomerulus in order to maintain GFR.\n\nACEIs and ARBs inhibit efferent renal arteriolar vasoconstriction. In bilateral RAS, this causes a decline in the GFR within a few days of starting the drug.\n\nNote: Bilateral renal artery stenosis has been classically considered as an absolute contraindication to the use of ACE inhibitors. However, new studies show that they may be used in some cases with careful monitoring.","t":"Vascular Diseases Of Kidney","c":"Nephrology"},
 {"q":"The c wave seen in a normal JVP waveform represents?","o":["Bulging of tricuspid valve into the right atrium","Atrial relaxation during atrial diastole","Venous filling of the right atrium","Emptying of the right atrium into the right ventricle"],"a":0,"e":"The c-wave in a JVP waveform represents the bulging of the tricuspid valve into the right atrium during ventricular systole.\n\nJVP measured in the internal jugular vein is a reflection of the right atrial pressure.\n\nThe waveforms seen in a normal JVP are:\n\n• a wave corresponds to atrial contraction and occurs just after the P wave (on ECG) and just before the first heart sound.\n\n• c wave represents the bulging of the tricuspid valve into the right atrium during ventricular systole.\n\n• x descent corresponds to the atrial relaxation during atrial diastole.\n\n• v wave represents atrial filling during ventricular systole and peaks at the second heart sound.\n\n• y descent represents passive emptying of the right atrium into the right ventricle after tricuspid valve opening.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/eddc167e55494ad8a3d429b278359fa0x1280x1005.JPEG"],"t":"Diagnosis Of Cardiovascular Disorders","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"The following abnormality on a JVP waveform can be seen in which of the given conditions?","o":["Cardiac tamponade","Ventricular tachycardia","Tricuspid regurgitation","Atrial fibrillation"],"a":1,"e":"The given waveform shows a cannon ‘a’ wave seen in ventricular tachycardia. It occurs since atria contract against a closed tricuspid valve in ventricular tachycardia.\n\nJVP measured in the internal jugular vein is a reflection of the right atrial pressure.\n\nNote: Giant/prominent a-waves occur during every beat while cannon a-waves result from arrhythmias, therefore, are usually intermittent. The ECG is helpful to look for rhythm disturbances that are associated with cannon a-waves whereas echocardiography can be used to assess structural defects (of right atrium) that are associated with giant a-waves.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/05e9bad274c2480d9bff0f19ccb7452e.JPEG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/71da9a7e43614b29ab0d3c9000cdd94bx1280x958.JPEG"],"t":"Diagnosis Of Cardiovascular Disorders","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A 37-year-old male patient comes to the clinic with complaints of weakness, restlessness, and chest pain. On examination, his blood pressure is found to be 90/60 mmHg, his heart sounds are muffled, and his neck veins are distended. What is the typical JVP finding in this patient?","o":["Absent a wave","Prominent a wave","Rapid y descent","Absent y descent"],"a":3,"e":"Hypotension, soft or absent heart sounds, and jugular venous distention (Beck’s triad) are the three principal features of cardiac tamponade. The typical JVP finding here is absent y-descent.\n\nJVP shows a prominent x (early systolic) descent and an absent y (early diastolic) descent because of the ventricle's limited or absent early diastolic filling. JVP is almost always elevated in cardiac tamponade and may be associated with venous distention in the forehead and scalp.\n\nThere may be reductions in the amplitude of the QRS complexes, and electrical alternans of the P, QRS, or T waves in cardiac tamponade.\n\nNote: Alternation of QRS complexes, usually in a 2:1 ratio, on ECG is called electrical alternans. It is caused by the movement of the heart in the pericardial space. Electrical alternans is also seen in patients with myocardial ischemia, acute pulmonary embolism, and tachyarrhythmias.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8d6e618c142f40fbb8bd49d311bac2a5x1280x1518.JPEG"],"t":"Diagnosis Of Cardiovascular Disorders","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"The following four patients were examined on a tilt table for postural hypotension. The blood pressures before and immediately after performing head-up tilt are given below. Choose the correct option regarding the patients who suffer from orthostatic hypotension.\n\nBefore head-up tilt\nAfter head-up tilt\n\nSBP (mmHg)\nDBP (mmHg)\nSBP (mmHg)\nDBP (mmHg)\n\nPatient 1\n140\n90\n126\n76\n\nPatient 2\n132\n84\n110\n80\n\nPatient 3\n126\n78\n108\n70\n\nPatient 4\n136\n88\n114\n82","o":["2, 3","1, 2, 4","1, 4","2, 3, 4"],"a":1,"e":"Orthostatic hypotension is defined as a reduction in systolic blood pressure of at least 20 mmHg or diastolic blood pressure of at least 10 mmHg within 3 min of standing or head-up tilt on a tilt table.\n\nOn standing, blood is displaced to the lower extremities, causing a drop in the venous return to the heart. This drop leads to a decrease in cardiac output and stimulation of compensatory mechanisms which trigger a reflex increase in sympathetic (vasoconstrictor) outflow. Orthostatic hypotension is a manifestation of sympathetic (autonomic) failure.\n\nSymptoms include light-headedness, dizziness, and presyncope (near-faintness) in response to sudden postural change.","t":"Diagnosis Of Cardiovascular Disorders","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Pulsus paradoxus is seen in all of the following except:","o":["COPD","Tension pneumothorax","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Cardiac tamponade"],"a":2,"e":"Pulsus bisferiens is seen in hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, not pulsus paradoxus.\n\nPulsus paradoxus refers to the fall in systolic pressure by > 10 mmHg with inspiration. It is an exaggeration of the normal response. It is seen in the following conditions:\n\n• Cardiac tamponade\n\n• Massive pulmonary embolism\n\n• Hemorrhagic shock\n\n• Obstructive lung disease\n\n• Tension pneumothorax","t":"Diagnosis Of Cardiovascular Disorders","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Pulsus bisferiens is seen in all of the following conditions except:","o":["Advanced aortic regurgitation","AS with AR","Left ventricular dysfunction","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy"],"a":2,"e":"Pulsus alternans (characterized by alternating strong and weak beats) is seen in left ventricular dysfunction.\n\nPulsus bisferiens is a bifid pulse with two systolic peaks. It is seen in the following conditions:\n\n• Advanced aortic regurgitation (AR)\n\n• Aortic stenosis (AS) with AR\n\n• Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d2cd58f4c7674dae813b871e0d41baa1x1280x1617.JPEG"],"t":"Diagnosis Of Cardiovascular Disorders","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A loud S1 is associated with?","o":["Rheumatic mitral stenosis","Calcified mitral stenosis","Aortic regurgitation","All of the above"],"a":0,"e":"Rheumatic mitral stenosis is associated with loud S1.\n\nThe greater the distance the valve travels to close, the louder is the sound. Increased left atrial pressures in rheumatic mitral stenosis force the mobile portion of the mitral valve leaflets far apart such that they are forced closed from a relatively far distance, resulting in a loud S1. In calcific mitral stenosis, leaflet mobility is poor, and they are not separated by a large distance, resulting in a soft S1.\n\nLoud S1 is heard in the following:\n\n• Short PR intervals\n\n• Early stages of mitral stenosis as long as the valves are pliable\n\n• Atrial myxoma\n\n• High output states\n\n• Tachycardia\n\nSoft S1 is heard in the following:\n\n• Mitral regurgitation\n\n• Aortic regurgitation\n\n• Long PR intervals\n\n• Calcified Mitral stenosis\n\n• Left ventricular contractile dysfunction\n\n• Beta-receptor blockers\n\n• Pericardial effusion, obesity, COPD- due to decreased conduction through the chest wall","t":"Diagnosis Of Cardiovascular Disorders","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A hypertensive patient presented with chest pain and was found to have dilated cardiomyopathy on an echocardiogram. On auscultation, a reversed splitting of second heart sound was heard. Which of the following could most likely be responsible for the same?","o":["RBBB","ASD","LBBB","VSD"],"a":2,"e":"Reversed (or paradoxical) splitting refers to the situation when P2 is heard before A2. This is seen in the following:\n\n• LBBB\n\n• HOCM\n\n• Right ventricular pacing\n\n• Severe AS\n\n• Acute myocardial ischemia","t":"Diagnosis Of Cardiovascular Disorders","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"While examining a patient, you hear a widely split S2 on auscultation. Which of the following is not a possible diagnosis?","o":["Atrial septal defect","Mitral regurgitation","Pulmonary stenosis","Left bundle branch block"],"a":3,"e":"A wide split of S2 is not seen in the left bundle branch block (LBBB), however paradoxical splitting is seen.\n\nS2 is normally heard as A2-P2. This split is exaggerated in conditions that cause early aortic valve closure or delayed pulmonary valve closure.\n\nOption A: In atrial septal defect (ASD), left ventricle (LV) ejection time decreases due to the shunting of the blood from the left atrium (LA) to the right atrium leading to early aortic valve closure.\n\nOption B: In mitral regurgitation (MR), due to the regurgitation of blood from the LV to the LA, the LV ejection time decreases leading to early aortic valve closure.\n\nOption C: In pulmonary stenosis (PS), the delayed closure of pulmonary valves leads to the splitting of S2.\n\nWide splitting of S2 is also heard in RBBB and LV pacing.","t":"Diagnosis Of Cardiovascular Disorders","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following conditions is associated with a fixed wide splitting of S2?","o":["Mitral regurgitation","RBBB","Atrial septal defect","LV pacing"],"a":2,"e":"Fixed wide splitting of S2 is seen in atrial septal defect (ASD).\n\nThe ASD creates a left to right shunt that increases the blood flow to the right side of the heart. This causes the pulmonary valve to close later than the aortic valve independent of inspiration/expiration.","t":"Diagnosis Of Cardiovascular Disorders","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements is true?","o":["S3 and S4 are high pitched systolic sounds","S3 is always pathological","S4 is heard during the ventricular-filling phase","S3 occurs due to rapid filling of the ventricles during atrial systole"],"a":2,"e":"S4 is heard during the ventricular-filling phase.\n\nS3 and S4 are diastolic sounds that are low pitched, and best heard with the bell of the stethoscope at the cardiac apex in the left lateral decubitus position.\n\n S3 - Ventricular gallop\n S4 - Atrial gallop\n\n Occurs during early ventricular diastole\n\nOccurs near the end of ventricular diastole\n\nDuring the first rapid ventricular filling\n\n During active ventricular filling (filling by atrial contraction)\n\nDue to a sudden rush of blood entering a volume-depleted ventricle (normal ventricular compliance)\n\nDue to vibration of the ventricular wall during atrial contraction because of increased stiffness of ventricular walls (decreased ventricular compliance)\n\nIt can be physiological in children and young adults.\nAlways pathological\n\nIn older patients, it indicates systolic heart failure\nIndicates diastolic heart failure\n\nThe presence of an S3 and S4 heart sound is called a summation gallop and sounds like a galloping horse.","t":"Diagnosis Of Cardiovascular Disorders","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"An ECG of a patient who underwent pulmonary artery catheterization showed a right bundle branch block. On auscultation, a widely split S1 was observed. Which of the following is true regarding this auscultatory finding?","o":["The split increases with inspiration","The split increases with expiration","The split remains constant with inspiration and expiration","The change in split depends on the degree of bundle branch block"],"a":0,"e":"In a patient with a right bundle branch block (RBBB), there's a widely split S1 which increases with inspiration.\n\nS1 is normally heard as a single sound. Any delay in the closure of the tricuspid valve or early closure of the mitral valve produces a split in S1. During inspiration, venous return to the right atrium increases. Therefore, the atria take a longer time to empty into the ventricles delaying the closure of the tricuspid valve. This increases the split in S1 during inspiration. In a patient with an RBBB, the tricuspid valve closure is further delayed leading to a wide split in S1.","t":"Diagnosis Of Cardiovascular Disorders","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A 23-year-old woman presents with a history of occasional palpitations. She has no history of chest pain or dyspnea. On examination, blood pressure is 110/70 mmHg, the pulse is 82/min and regular. Joint hypermobility and thoracolumbar scoliosis are present. Gorlin's sign is positive. On auscultation, a click is heard after S1, followed by a late systolic murmur. What is the probable diagnosis?","o":["Ehlers-Danlos syndrome","Marfan's syndrome","Loeys-Dietz syndrome","Osteogenesis imperfecta"],"a":0,"e":"The probable diagnosis of the above scenario is Ehlers-Danlos syndrome (EDS).\n\nEDS is a group of clinically and genetically heterogeneous connective tissue disorders. Typical presenting features include joint hypermobility, skin hyperextensibility, and tissue fragility. EDS type 6 is also characterized by scoliosis. Gorlin’s sign refers to the ability of patients of Ehlers-Danlos syndrome to touch the tip of the nose with the tip of their tongue.\n\nMitral valve prolapse (MVP) is a frequent finding in patients with EDS. An early systolic click which may be followed by a late systolic murmur is the auscultatory finding heard in MVP.","t":"Diagnosis Of Cardiovascular Disorders","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not a diastolic sound?","o":["S3","S4","Opening snap","Ejection click"],"a":3,"e":"The ejection click is a systolic sound.\n\nEjection clicks are high-pitched sounds that occur at the moment of the maximal opening of the aortic or pulmonary valves. They are heard just after the first heart sound.\n\nEjection clicks occur in:\n\n• Non-calcific aortic or pulmonary stenosis\n\n• Aortic or pulmonary root dilatation\n\n• Congenital bicuspid aortic or pulmonary valves\n\nNonejection clicks, which occur after the onset of the carotid upstroke, are related to the mitral valve prolapse.","t":"Diagnosis Of Cardiovascular Disorders","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"On auscultation of a patient, you hear a murmur between S1 and S2. Which of the following conditions is not likely to be associated with this finding?","o":["Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Tricuspid regurgitation","Ventricular septal defect","Pulmonary regurgitation"],"a":3,"e":"A murmur occurring between S1 and S2 is a systolic murmur. Pulmonary regurgitation produces a diastolic murmur.\n\nThe causes of systolic murmurs are as follows:\n\n• Mid-systolic- aortic stenosis, pulmonic stenosis, ASD, HOCM.\n\n• Holosystolic/pansystolic- mitral regurgitation, tricuspid regurgitation, VSD.\n\n• Late systolic- mitral valve prolapse.\n\nThe causes of diastolic murmurs are as follows:\n\n• Early diastolic- aortic regurgitation, pulmonic regurgitation.\n\n• Mid/late diastolic- mitral stenosis, tricuspid stenosis, Austin-Flint.\n\nThe causes of continuous murmurs are as follows:\n\n• Patent ductus arteriosus (machinery or Gibson's murmur).\n\n• Venous hum\n\n• AV fistula\n\n• Ruptured sinus of Valsalva aneurysm","t":"Diagnosis Of Cardiovascular Disorders","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"While examining a patient, you hear a mid-diastolic murmur with presystolic accentuation on auscultation of the precordium. Which of the following condition is associated with this finding?","o":["Mitral regurgitation","MVP","Aortic stenosis","Mitral stenosis"],"a":3,"e":"A mid-diastolic murmur with presystolic accentuation and an opening snap is typically seen in mitral stenosis.\n\nThe presystolic accentuation is due to the atrial contraction forcing blood through the stenosed mitral valve. The presystolic accentuation disappears in atrial fibrillation.\n\nOption A: Mitral regurgitation presents with a holosystolic murmur, best heard at the apex with radiation to the left axilla.\n\nOption B: Mitral valve prolapse presents with an early systolic click followed by a late systolic murmur is heard.\n\nOption C: Aortic stenosis presents with a harsh crescendo-decrescendo ejection systolic murmur heard best at the right upper sternal border, with radiation of the murmur to the carotid arteries.","t":"Diagnosis Of Cardiovascular Disorders","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"You are a part of a health camp conducting check-ups for cardiovascular ailments. On auscultation of a patient's precordial area, you hear a systolic murmur. On asking the patient to perform the following maneuver, the intensity of the murmur increased. Which of the following conditions is associated with this finding?","o":["MR","TR","AS","HCM"],"a":3,"e":"The above image shows the Valsalva maneuver. The murmur of hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (HCM) increases in intensity while performing the Valsalva maneuver.\n\nThe murmurs produced by TR (tricuspid regurgitation), AS (aortic stenosis), and MR (mitral regurgitation) decrease in intensity while performing the Valsalva maneuver.\n\nNote: Valsalva maneuver decreases the intensity of all murmurs except MVP (mitral valve prolapse) and HCM.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/80a715e52b5f4dd3b7185306538bdf90.PNG"],"t":"Diagnosis Of Cardiovascular Disorders","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"While auscultating a patient's precordium, a murmur was heard that decreased in intensity on performing the following sustained maneuver. Which of the given condition is associated with this finding?","o":["Mitral regurgitation","Ventricular septal defect","Aortic regurgitation","Aortic stenosis"],"a":3,"e":"The above image shows a sustained handgrip maneuver. The murmur of aortic stenosis decreases in intensity during sustained handgrip.\n\nManeuvers like sustained handgrip, simultaneous inflation of blood pressure cuffs above the systolic in both upper limbs, and infusion of vasopressors increase the systemic vascular resistance (afterload). Since increasing afterload will prevent blood from flowing in a normal forward path, it will increase any murmurs that are due to backward flowing blood and decresase the ones due to forward blood flow.\n\nMurmurs that increase in intensity on sustained handgrip:\n\n• Mitral regurgitation\n\n• Ventricular septal defect\n\n• Aortic regurgitation\n\nMurmurs that decrease in intensity on sustained handgrip:\n\n• Aortic stenosis\n\n• Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/56062e05fec34e4bb4c6c74925931c67.JPEG"],"t":"Diagnosis Of Cardiovascular Disorders","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A child was being evaluated for an underlying cardiac defect. On auscultation, a murmur was heard which began in systole, enveloped the second heart sound, and continued through some portion of the diastole. Which of the following condition is not associated with this finding?","o":["Ventricular septal defect with aortic regurgitation","Patent ductus arteriosus","Coronary arteriovenous fistula","Venous hum"],"a":0,"e":"The murmur described above is a continuous murmur.\n\nA continuous murmur is produced when there is a pressure gradient between the cardiac chambers or the blood vessels throughout the systole and diastole. Continous murmurs are characterised by the flow of blood in the same direction and through the same channel during systole and diastole. These murmurs begin in systole, envelope the second heart sound (S2), and continue through some portion of diastole.\n\nConditions producing a continuous murmur:\n\n• Patent ductus arteriosus\n\n• Venous hum\n\n• AV fistula\n\n• Ruptured sinus of Valsalva aneurysm\n\nVentricular septal defect with aortic regurgitation does not produce a continuous murmur. It produces a systolic-diastolic murmur as the blood flows through different channels during systole and diastole. These murmurs do not envelope S2.","t":"Diagnosis Of Cardiovascular Disorders","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is true about Gallavardin effect?","o":["It is associated with a diastolic murmur","It is seen in aortic stenosis","It refers to the radiation of murmur to the carotids","The transmission makes the murmur low pitched"],"a":1,"e":"The Gallavardin effect refers to the phenomenon of the transmission of the mid-systolic murmur of aortic stenosis to the apex where it becomes higher pitched.","t":"Diagnosis Of Cardiovascular Disorders","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A patient presents with easy fatiguability and shortness of breath on moderate physical activity. On auscultation, a holosystolic murmur is heard over the lower left sternal border and radiating to the epigastrium. Which of the following condition is most likely to be associated with this finding?","o":["Mitral regurgitation","Tricuspid regurgitation","Ventricular septal defect","Atrial septal defect"],"a":1,"e":"A holosystolic murmur that is best heard over the lower left sternal border and radiating to the epigastrium most likely arises from tricuspid regurgitation.","t":"Diagnosis Of Cardiovascular Disorders","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is true about Still’s murmur?","o":["It is heard in mitral regurgitation","It is best heard in left lateral decubitus position","It is a mid-systolic murmur","It is of grade 4"],"a":2,"e":"Still’s murmur refers to the benign grade 2 mid-systolic murmur heard at mid or lower left sternal border in normal children and adolescents. Louder in hyperdynamic states(fever, anxiety).\n\nThe anatomic basis for the murmur has not been definitively determined. It is best heard in a supine position.","t":"Diagnosis Of Cardiovascular Disorders","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Graham Steell murmur is heard in?","o":["Aortic regurgitation","Pulmonary regurgitation","Mitral regurgitation","Tricuspid regurgitation"],"a":1,"e":"Graham Steell murmur is the early to mid-diastolic murmur heard in pulmonary regurgitation.\n\nIt is best heard in 2nd left intercostal space and radiates along left sternal border. It can increase with inspiration.","t":"Diagnosis Of Cardiovascular Disorders","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Carey Coombs murmur is heard in?","o":["Mitral valvulitis","Mitral regurgitation","Tricuspid stenosis","Tricuspid regurgitation"],"a":0,"e":"Carey Coombs murmur is heard in mitral valvulitis.\n\nIt is a mid-diastolic murmur heard during an episode of acute rheumatic fever causing mitral valvulitis.\n\nIt is produced due to the flow of blood through an edematous mitral valve and dissipates with the resolution of the acute attack.","t":"Diagnosis Of Cardiovascular Disorders","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Austin-Flint murmur is associated with which of the following conditions?","o":["Chronic AR","Severe MS","Pulmonary hypertension","Severe MR"],"a":0,"e":"Austin-Flint murmur is a low-pitched mid to late diastolic murmur of chronic severe AR.\n\nIt results from the turbulence at the mitral inflow area from the mixture of regurgitant and forward blood flow. It is best heard at the apex.\n\nAustin-Flint murmur is often confused with the murmur of mitral stenosis. This can be differentiated based on the following:\n\nFeatures\nChronic AR\nMS\n\nOpening snap\nAbsent\nPresent\n\nEffect of vasodilator administration\n\nMurmur becomes shorter and softer.\n\nMechanism- Fall in the diastolic pressure gradient between the aorta and left ventricle.\n\nMurmur remains constant or increases in intensity.\n\nMechanism- Reflex increase in cardiac output and mitral valve flow.","t":"Diagnosis Of Cardiovascular Disorders","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Which electrophysiological activity is responsible for the T wave in ECG?","o":["Ventricular repolarisation","Ventricular depolarisation","Atrial depolarisation","Atrial repolarisation"],"a":0,"e":"The T wave in ECG is due to ventricular repolarisation.","t":"Ecg Basics","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"The PR intervals obtained from the ECG recording of 4 patients are given below. Which of the following patients has a normal PR interval?\n\nPatient 1- 0.19s\n\nPatient 2- 0.3s\n\nPatient 3- 0.23s\n\nPatient 4- 0.1s","o":["Patient 1","Patient 2","Patient 3","Patient 4"],"a":0,"e":"The normal duration of the PR interval is 0.12-0.2 seconds (3 to 5 small boxes).\n\nPR interval is said to be prolonged if it is > 0.2 seconds and short if it is < 0.12 seconds.\n\nIt is measured from the beginning of the P wave to the beginning of the QRS complex. It represents the time from the start of atrial depolarization to the start of ventricular depolarization.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/957ea1a4db9e4409a387615c113af4a3x1280x1339.JPEG"],"t":"Ecg Basics","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"What is the normal QRS interval?","o":["&lt; 0.20 seconds","&lt;0.25 seconds","&lt; 0.11 seconds","&lt; 0.15 seconds"],"a":2,"e":"The normal duration of the QRS interval is normally 0.1-0.11 seconds (less than 3 small boxes).\n\nQRS interval represents the duration of ventricular depolarization. When the QRS complex is > 0.11 seconds, it is called a wide QRS complex. It can occur in:\n\n• Bundle branch blocks (RBBB or LBBB)\n\n• Depolarizations originating in the ventricles\n\n• Wolff-Parkinson-White syndrome\n\n• Hyperkalaemia","t":"Ecg Basics","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Which lead does the given ECG tracing represent in a normal ECG?","o":["aVL","aVR","V1","V6"],"a":1,"e":"As all the waves (P, QRS, T) are negative in the given ECG, it should be lead aVR. All the depolarisations waves (P, QRS, T) move away from lead aVR in the normal sinus.\n\nDepolarisation wave moving towards the positive pole of the lead (or active electrode) produces a positive deflection in the ECG and depolarization moving away from the positive pole of the lead produces a negative deflection in the ECG.\n\nThe lead aVR provides information from the right upper side of the heart.\n\nThe given image shows a normal 12 lead ECG:","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c1dc4ed5617f4786bbf2b98e827ef737.JPEG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0f77d513ca2a4b888208329cdfb90fc8x1280x1382.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b180f095ef484a1b862f718dcd8cb82ax1280x713.JPEG"],"t":"Ecg Basics","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"In a normal ECG obtained from a patient, a biphasic P wave may be seen in which of the following lead(s)?","o":["Leads II and V2","Leads II and V1","Lead V1","Lead V5"],"a":2,"e":"In a normal ECG, a biphasic P wave is seen in lead V1 only.\n\nLead V1 is a chest lead. So, it records potentials in the horizontal plane. The P wave in lead V1 consists of:\n\n• Positive component reflecting right atrial depolarization (due to anteriorly oriented vector)\n\n• Negative component reflecting left atrial depolarization (due to posteriorly oriented vector)","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d52351e328174a90a0c7f4bbcd2d5e97x1280x960.JPEG"],"t":"Ecg Basics","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following ECG findings are seen in a patient with hypokalemia?","o":["Prolonged PR interval with ST elevation","Decreased PR interval with ST depression","Decreased QT interval with tall T waves","Prolonged QT interval with flat T waves"],"a":3,"e":"Prolonged QT interval with flat T waves is seen in hypokalemia.\n\nHypokalemia prolongs ventricular repolarization. The ECG changes seen are as follows:\n\n• Flattening of T wave\n\n• ST-segment depression\n\n• Prolongation of QT interval\n\n• The appearance of U wave","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ec4e4533439146ed9de7afe36504c108x1280x2207.JPEG"],"t":"Ecg Basics","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A 45-year-old male patient with a long-standing history of rheumatic heart disease comes to the clinic with complaints of dyspnea. Interpret the ECG finding.","o":["Rabbit ear pattern","P mitrale","P pulmonale","Sawtooth pattern"],"a":1,"e":"The P waves in the given ECG are broad and notched. This is the P mitrale pattern which is seen in left atrial enlargement.\n\nThis patient has likely developed mitral stenosis (heard as a mid-diastolic murmur) secondary to his rheumatic heart disease. Mitral valve stenosis and atrial inflammation in rheumatic carditis lead to left atrial dilation.\n\nThe features of P mitrale are:\n\n• lead II: broad (>2.5mm) notched P waves\n\n• Lead V1: Biphasic wave with a prominent negative terminal component (representing delayed depolarization of the LA)\n\nP pulmonale is seen in right atrial enlargement.\n\nOption A: The rabbit ear pattern is seen in the right bundle branch block. The ECG of RBBB below shows the rSR' pattern of the QRS complex (rabbit ear) in leads V1-V2 and slurred S wave in lead V6.\n\nOption D: Sawtooth pattern in ECG is seen in atrial flutter (shown below).","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c7214fa40eb646af8b9f159df1b7682f.JPEG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1ba642933ad7476788755bf715738bf3x1280x960.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fa1307fb668745669ce788c0b1c52357x1280x933.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5f40a3dbbd734b679fab4cac748ddff0x1280x894.JPEG"],"t":"Ecg Basics","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A 67-year-old man with a history of COPD presented with worsening dyspnea for 2 weeks. On examination, pedal edema was present. An ECG was done and lead II showed the following pattern. Which of the following is responsible for this ECG finding?","o":["Right atrial overload","Left atrial overload","Right ventricular hypertrophy","Left ventricular hypertrophy"],"a":0,"e":"In the given ECG, a peaked P wave can be seen. This is called P pulmonale and it is seen in cases of right atrial enlargement. The patient is showing features of heart failure possibly as a consequence of dysfunction caused by the overload.\n\nSigns of heart failure in this patient:\n\n• Bilateral pitting edema in lower extremities (systemic congestion)\n\n• Worsening dyspnea due to increased PCWP causing pulmonary hypertension and pulmonary edema (pulmonary congestion)\n\nThe features of P pulmonale include:\n\n• The amplitude of the P wave is >2.5mm on Lead II\n\n• Prominent positive wave in the biphasic P wave of lead V1","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b341164248ca4b0e9e9ff6c0dcc183ae.JPEG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/43a27358d833450f897b84e29124f815x1280x960.JPEG"],"t":"Ecg Basics","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"What is the likely diagnosis of this patient based on the given ECG?","o":["Right ventricular hypertrophy","Left ventricular hypertrophy","Torsades de pointes","Atrial flutter"],"a":0,"e":"The given ECG shows deep S-waves in V6 and large R-waves in V1. This is suggestive of right ventricular hypertrophy (RVH).\n\nDiagnostic criteria for RVH:\n\n• Dominant R wave in V1 (R/S ratio > 1).\n\n• Dominant S wave in V5 or V6 (R/S ratio < 1).\n\n• Right ventricular strain pattern - ST depression/T wave inversion in the right precordial (V1-4) and inferior leads (II, III, aVF)\n\n• Right axis deviation of +110° or more.\n\nFor left ventricular hypertrophy, Sokolow Lyon criteria are commonly used.\n\nIt is based on the presence of tall left precordial R waves and deep right precordial S waves (S wave in V1 + [R wave in V5 or V6] >35 mm).","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/39d80382cbe54194b74cc073b771d107.PNG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/72785ac893614480b2bc71bf9fefcab3x1280x1334.JPEG"],"t":"Ecg Basics","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A 45-year-old man comes to the emergency room with complaints of sudden onset chest pain that radiates to the left arm and jaw. He is profusely sweating. An ECG was taken immediately. Interpret his ECG.","o":["Right ventricular hypertrophy","Right bundle-branch block","Left bundle-branch block","Ventricular tachycardia"],"a":2,"e":"The patient has a myocardial infarction (MI) with ECG changes of a left bundle-branch block (LBBB), which can be identified by the following classical features:\n\n• Wide QRS complex- QRS ≥120 ms\n\n• Predominantly negative (QS) complexes in lead V1 and entirely positive (R) complexes in lead V6.\n\n• M pattern seen in left chest leads (V5, V6)\n\nMI can have the following ECG changes:\n\n• ST-segment elevation- zone of injury\n\n• T wave depression\n\n• Deep Q waves- late sign to develop\n\n• New left bundle branch block (LBBB)\n\nThe ECG findings below are characteristic of LBBB: Mnemonic WILLIAM (W pattern in V1-V2; L-LBBB; M pattern in V3-V6)\n\nThe ECG findings below are characteristic of RBBB: Mnemonic MARROW (M pattern in V1-V2; R-RBBB; W pattern in V3-V6)","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/45ebf16ae3734bbfad303bbf66c8eff1.JPEG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fb930c11dc1548d6a183c5c6ab2ccf7ax1199x838.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0e6306d001434620bdba7c39df33bf02x1280x810.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/96faff1fdae6488e944b880304724ac9x528x329.PNG"],"t":"Ecg Basics","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A 50-year-old patient comes to the emergency room with a history of severe retrosternal pain radiating to the jaw. The patient initially felt some uneasiness while eating lunch after which he experienced pain. His ECG is as shown. Interpret his ECG.","o":["Atrial flutter","WPW syndrome","Myocardial infarction","Ventricular tachycardia"],"a":2,"e":"The given ECG shows ST-segment elevation in the anterior chest leads suggestive of myocardial infarction.\n\nFeatures of MI on ECG:\n\n•\n\nST-segment elevation: Zone of injury\n\n•\n\nT wave inversions\n\n•\n\nDeep Q waves: late sign to develop\n\n•\n\nNew left bundle branch block (LBBB)\n\nOption A: Atrial flutter shows a sawtooth wave pattern.\n\nOption B: WPW syndrome shows delta waves.\n\nOption D: Ventricular tachycardia has wide QRS complex and increased heart rate.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/63b772cc7c9a4a5eb0016b61f56d63fb.JPEG"],"t":"Ecg Basics","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A 72-year-old man comes to the emergency room complaining his heart is pounding and that he is going to die. The emergency physician orders an ECG. What is the diagnosis?","o":["Inferior wall MI","Extensive anterior wall MI","Anterolateral wall MI","Anteroseptal MI"],"a":3,"e":"The given ECG shows ST elevation in leads V1-V4 which is suggestive of acute anteroseptal myocardial infarction (MI). ST depression in the leads III and aVF signifies an old inferior wall infarction.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/edfec934c9dd4e948efdec9aa4fdee9e.JPEG"],"t":"Ecg Basics","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A 52-year-old diabetic man presents with retrosternal chest pain and profuse sweating and lightheadedness. Based on the ECG findings, what is the diagnosis?","o":["Extensive anterior wall MI","Anterolateral wall MI","Anteroseptal MI","Atrial flutter"],"a":0,"e":"The ECG shows ST elevations in leads I, aVL, and V1-V6. This is suggestive of extensive anterior wall MI.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2bacc8a01e8148ec9c31c2b01b458dd1.JPEG"],"t":"Ecg Basics","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"What is the diagnosis based on the given ECG?","o":["Anterior wall MI","Lateral wall MI","Inferior wall MI","Anteroseptal MI"],"a":1,"e":"The ECG is suggestive of lateral wall MI and possible posterior wall MI.\n\nST-elevation is present in the high lateral leads (I and aVL) and in V5 and V6.\n\nReciprocal ST depression is seen in the inferior leads (III and aVF) with associated ST depression in V1, V2, and V3 (which could represent posterior wall MI).\n\nThis pattern is consistent with an acute infarction localized to the superior portion of the lateral wall of the left ventricle (high lateral STEMI).","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/041742a394fc4e7dbe050ab420f9e296.JPEG"],"t":"Ecg Basics","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A 60-year-old patient presents with chest pain on the left side radiating to the arm and jaw on the same side. Cardiac troponins are found to be raised. What is the diagnosis based on this ECG?","o":["Anterior wall MI","Lateral wall MI","Inferior wall MI","Posterior wall MI"],"a":3,"e":"The given ECG is of posterior wall MI.\n\nThe ECG findings in posterior wall MI include,\n\n• Reciprocal ST depressions in leads V1 to V3. Posterior wall ischemia is indirectly recognized in this way\n\n• Tall and broad R waves (>30ms) in V2 and V3\n\n• Dominant R waves (R/S ratio > 1) in V2\n\n• Upright terminal portions of the T waves in V2 and V3","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/10157854c5054d5e9f440cbf1a5147da.JPEG"],"t":"Ecg Basics","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following ECG findings are unlikely to be present in a patient with hyperkalemia?","o":["Prolonged PR interval","Prolonged QT interval","Prolonged QRS interval","Loss of P waves"],"a":1,"e":"Prolonged QT interval is not a feature of hyperkalemia, it is a feature of hypokalemia.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b53611ee58544dd584f856ed4e0e764bx1280x1585.JPEG"],"t":"Ecg Basics","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A patient was brought to the ER with complaints of multiple episodes of vomiting, increased frequency of urination, and constipation for the past 5 days. He also complained of lethargy and muscle pain. He is a known case of SCC of the lung. What is likely to be seen in the ECG of this patient?","o":["ST segment depression","Decreased QT interval","Increased PR interval","Tall T waves"],"a":1,"e":"The above scenario is suggestive of hypercalcemia due to ectopic production of PTH-related peptides. It is mostly associated with squamous-cell lung cancer (SCC). ECG is likely to show a decreased QT interval.\n\nHypocalcemia shows increased QT interval.","t":"Ecg Basics","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"ECG of a patient revealed a QT interval of 0.55s. Which of the following will not be a differential diagnosis?","o":["Hypothermia","Digitalis toxicity","Hypocalcemia","Romano-Ward syndrome"],"a":1,"e":"QT interval of 0.55s suggests QT prolongation. It is not seen in digitalis toxicity. Rather, a QT shortening with characteristic “scooping” of the ST–T-wave complex, called a digitalis effect, is seen.\n\nThe ECG changes seen in digitalis toxicity are as follows:\n\n• Prolonged PR interval\n\n• ST-segment depression\n\n• Short QT interval\n\nQT interval represents the time taken for the ventricles to depolarize and repolarize. It is prolonged in the following:\n\n• Hypocalcemia\n\n• Hypokalemia\n\n• Congenital long QT syndromes such as Romano-Ward syndrome\n\n• Hypothermia\n\n• Drugs: Class IA and Class III antiarrhythmics\n\n• Intracranial bleeds: particularly SAH","t":"Ecg Basics","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"In a patient with a left-sided massive pneumothorax, all of the following ECG changes are seen except:","o":["Reduced R wave amplitude","Precordial T wave inversion","Decreased QRS amplitude","Left axis deviation"],"a":3,"e":"Right axis deviation is seen in left-sided pneumothorax.\n\nThe ECG changes seen in left-sided pneumothorax are as follows:\n\n• Reduced R wave amplitude\n\n• Inversion of T wave in precordial leads\n\n• Amplitude changes in QRS complex\n\n• Right axis deviation","t":"Ecg Basics","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A 52-year-old man has come to the emergency complaining of a burning type of chest pain. Comment on his ECG recording shown below.","o":["Sinus bradycardia","Sinus tachycardia","Normal sinus rhythm","Irregular rhythm"],"a":2,"e":"The above ECG strip shows normal sinus rhythm.\n\nThe features in this ECG that suggest this diagnosis are as follows:\n\n• The QRS complexes are narrow (<120 ms or 3 small squares) which implies that the impulse is supraventricular and is conducted via the normal conduction pathway.\n\n• Each QRS complex is preceded by a P wave. This means that all the atrial depolarizations that arise in the sinus node are conducted to the ventricles.\n\n• The PR interval is constant for all the impulses and is normal (<200 ms) in duration.\n\n• The RR interval is constant which implies a regular rhythm.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ac12bf3ea55644d4b5c63a854c41e773.JPEG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/251a17748fb242b0b115bb0876e4de25x537x130.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c3db622654d7465093f09972c262c87dx800x224.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fc5516f5be574d34a4d83ca6643b63cax800x224.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4baaf24d405f481b9956ca56d1ac06a7x800x224.JPEG"],"t":"Supraventricular Arrhythmias","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"All of the following are supraventricular tachycardias except:","o":["AV nodal reentry tachycardia","Sinus tachycardia","Junctional tachycardia","Premature ventricular beats"],"a":3,"e":"Premature ventricular beats are a type of ventricular tachycardias, not supraventricular tachycardias.\n\nTachycardia is defined as a heart rate of >100 beats/min. It can be classified based on the site of origin of the cardiac impulse as follows:\n\n1. Supraventricular tachycardias (SVTs) originate above the ventricles, that is, in the sinoatrial node, atria, or the atrioventricular node. Supraventricular tachycardias include:\n\n• Tachycardias originating from the atrium:\n\n• Sinus tachycardia- from the SA node\n\n• Focal atrial tachycardia\n\n• Multifocal atrial tachycardia\n\n• Atrial flutter\n\n• Atrial fibrillation\n\n• Tachycardias originating from the AV node:\n\n• Junctional tachycardia- automaticity within AV Node\n\n• AV nodal reentry tachycardia (AVNRT)\n\n• Tachycardia associated with accessory atrioventricular pathways: atrioventricular reentry tachycardia (AVRT)\n\n2. Ventricular tachycardias originate from the ectopic foci or the reentry pathways in the ventricles. Ventricular tachycardias include:\n\n• Premature ventricular beats\n\n• Ventricular tachycardia (VT)\n\n• Monomorphic VT- Very rapid monomorphic VT is also known as ventricular flutter\n\n• Polymorphic VT- associated with Torsades de pointes\n\n• Ventricular fibrillation","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/dfadd86771a643d6848dda9d6e6a85bbx1280x1903.JPEG"],"t":"Supraventricular Arrhythmias","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"ECG of a patient who presented with palpitations to the ER is shown below. Which of the following is a likely diagnosis?","o":["Sinus tachycardia","Ventricular tachycardia","Junctional tachycardia","Atrial flutter"],"a":1,"e":"The above ECG shows increased heart rate with wide QRS complexes (> 0.12s). This is suggestive of ventricular tachycardia (VT).\n\nTypes of ventricular tachycardia based on QRS morphology:\n\n• Monomorphic- a stable QRS morphology from beat to beat\n\n• Polymorphic- changing or multiform QRS variance from beat to beat. Torsades de pointes is an example of polymorphic ventricular tachycardia.\n\n• Bidirectional- has a beat-to-beat alternation in the QRS frontal plane axis. It is associated with digitalis toxicity or catecholaminergic polymorphic VT.\n\nThe most common cause of VT is ischemic heart disease.\n\nVentricular tachyarrhythmias occur due to impulses that originate within the ventricles. These impulses are conducted through the normal ventricular myocardial cells instead of the fast conducting Purkinje system. Hence, they present with wide QRS complexes ( >0.12 sec) on the ECG.\n\nSupraventricular tachyarrhythmias occur due to impulses originating above the ventricles. These impulses reach the ventricles through the proper fast conduction pathway resulting in a narrow QRS complex. SVTs are associated with wide QRS in few exceptional conditions like:\n\n• Conduction block in the left or right bundle branch\n\n• Activation of ventricles from an accessory pathway- antidromic AVRT\n\nThe given ECG shows wide stable QRS complexes in monomorphic ventricular tachycardia:","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e5bc29d2d1624ae48169c4e04b7e15b0.PNG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8037eec9a7e5488ea9f119a5d8ba3269x1280x720.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9989408335ef46bca7dabd1f13a8628cx1280x1903.JPEG"],"t":"Supraventricular Arrhythmias","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Which is the most common supraventricular tachyarrhythmia?","o":["Paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardia","Sinus tachycardia","Atrial fibrillation","Junctional tachycardia"],"a":1,"e":"The most common supraventricular arrhythmia is sinus tachycardia. It can either be physiological or pathological. Physiological sinus tachycardia is seen in exercise, fever, anemia, hyperthyroidism, etc.\n\nParoxysmal supraventricular tachycardias are those supraventricular tachycardias that occur with sudden onset and termination, they are non-sustained arrhythmias (lasts < 30 seconds). They include AVNRT, AVRT, and atrial tachycardias.\n\nThe most common sustained (lasts > 30 seconds) arrhythmia is atrial fibrillation.\n\nJunctional tachycardia originates from ectopic foci in the AV node. It is an uncommon tachycardia.","t":"Supraventricular Arrhythmias","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A 49-year-old woman was brought to the emergency with complaints of dizziness, shortness of breath, and palpitations. ECG showed tachyarrhythmia with narrow QRS complex and irregular RR intervals. What is the likely diagnosis?","o":["Atrioventricular re-entry tachycardia","AV nodal re-entry tachycardia","Atrial flutter","Atrial fibrillation"],"a":3,"e":"ECG showing tachyarrhythmia with a narrow QRS complex suggests a supraventricular tachycardia. An irregular RR interval is seen with the following supraventricular tachycardias:\n\n• Atrial fibrillation\n\n• Multifocal atrial tachycardia\n\n• Atrial flutter/ atrial tachycardia with variable block\n\nThe ECG below shows atrial fibrillation. Note the narrow QRS complexes and irregular RR intervals. Disappearacne of P-wave is another feature.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d04e4c8c008949509165142a549837d7x1280x631.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a716843e6e6a49e7b3baf959c07bbbf3x1280x1903.JPEG"],"t":"Supraventricular Arrhythmias","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A 65-year-old hypertensive patient presents with chest pain and dyspnea following an alcohol binge. His ECG is shown below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Ventricular tachycardia","Focal atrial tachycardia","Ventricular fibrillation","Atrial fibrillation"],"a":3,"e":"The ECG shows narrow QRS complexes with irregular RR intervals and no distinct P waves. This is suggestive of atrial fibrillation.\n\nAtrial fibrillation (AF) is a supraventricular tachycardia caused by ectopic foci or small reentry circuits within the atria. It has an atrial rate of 400-600/min and a ventricular rate of 100-160/min.\n\nRisk factors for developing AF include:\n\n• Age- usually above 60 years of age\n\n• Underlying heart disease\n\n• Hypertension\n\n• Diabetes mellitus\n\n• Obesity\n\n• Sleep apnea\n\n• Post-cardiac surgery\n\n• Inflammatory pericarditis\n\nAcute precipitating factors for AF include:\n\n• Hyperthyroidism\n\n• Acute alcohol intoxication\n\n• Acute illness like myocardial infarction and pulmonary embolism","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8aeacc19ffb44b44b9229247c55aede3.JPEG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d30f6e7195844df6a52b0fa6e84d940ax1280x1903.JPEG"],"t":"Supraventricular Arrhythmias","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A 50-year-old man was diagnosed with STEMI. He was admitted and given thrombolytic therapy. The next day he complained of palpitations and an ECG was taken. Which of the following drugs can be used to treat his condition?","o":["Amiodarone","Verapamil","Flecainide","Propafenone"],"a":0,"e":"The above ECG shows narrow QRS complex, irregular RR intervals, and absent P waves which are suggestive of atrial fibrillation. Among the given options, amiodarone can be administered in patients having atrial fibrillation with coronary or structural heart disease.\n\nDrugs for rhythm control in atrial fibrillation:\n\nIn patients without structural heart disease, class I agents are used. Class I agents act by blocking the Na+ channels leading to slower depolarization:\n\n• Flecainide\n\n• Propafenone\n\n• Disopyramide\n\nIn patients with coronary or structural heart disease, class III agents are used. Class III agents act by blocking the K+ channels leading to slower repolarization causing increased refractory period:\n\n• Amiodarone\n\n• Dofetilide\n\n• Sotalol\n\n• Ibutilide","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e6bc2c51d717431c88847282772b92d0.JPEG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0b4ad16cfb9b465dbf1fd2ca0f384ad0x1280x2567.JPEG"],"t":"Supraventricular Arrhythmias","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A 60-year-old man who is on treatment for chronic AF presents with nausea, dizziness, and anorexia. He has a regular heart rate of 50/min. The most likely cause is:","o":["Thromboembolic stroke","Myocardial infarction","Non compliance to treatment","Digitalis toxicity"],"a":3,"e":"A patient of atrial fibrillation usually has an irregular heart rate of >100/ min but this patient presents with a regular slow heart rate. The most likely cause of slow regular heart rate in chronic AF is digitalis toxicity. Symptoms such as nausea, anorexia are also suggestive of digitalis toxicity.\n\nDigitalis lowers the AV nodal conduction by stimulating the vagal nerve endings leading to increased vagal tone. Hence digitalis toxicity can cause AV nodal block. This leads to a junctional escape rhythm taking over the ventricles causing slow and regular heart rate.\n\nDigitalis toxicity can also lead to a variety of arrhythmias as it increases the intracellular calcium levels increasing the risk of tachyarrhythmias.","t":"Supraventricular Arrhythmias","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"ECG of a patient who was brought to the casualty due to severe chest pain followed by syncope is given below. What is the likely diagnosis?","o":["Atrial fibrillation","Atrial flutter","Ventricular tachycardia","Ventricular fibrillation"],"a":1,"e":"The given ECG is suggestive of atrial flutter.\n\nThe given ECG shows narrow QRS complexes, hence it is supraventricular tachycardia. The RR intervals are regular and the normal P waves are replaced by sawtooth-shaped waves, known as flutter waves.\n\nIn atrial flutter, the ventricular rate is determined by the AV conduction ratio. The commonest AV ratio is 2:1. In the above ECG, the rhythm is atrial flutter 4:1 block with an atrial rate of about 270 and a ventricular rate of 68/min.\n\nThe image below shows sawtooth shaped waves in atrial flutter.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2aefa186cd024c86a568950289a398e6.PNG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/30021f635ca846c982f297ca4a708790x1280x894.JPEG"],"t":"Supraventricular Arrhythmias","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardias do not include:","o":["Atrial fibrillation","Atrial tachycardia","AV nodal reentry tachycardia","AV reentry tachycardia"],"a":0,"e":"Paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardias do not include atrial fibrillation.\n\nParoxysmal supraventricular tachycardias are tachycardias that occur with sudden onset and termination, they are not sustained arrhythmias. They include\n\n1. AVNRT\n\n2. AVRT\n\n3. Atrial tachycardia\n\nWhereas, atrial fibrillation is the most common sustained arrhythmia among supraventricular tachycardias.\n\nFeatures suggestive of PSVT:\n\n• Narrow complex tachycardia at 150 bpm\n\n• No visible P waves\n\n• Pseudo R’ waves due to retrograde atrial activation\n\nThe given ECG shows pseudo R' waves:","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/87a6b1840ca4407cade4b583d8fde458x1280x685.JPEG"],"t":"Supraventricular Arrhythmias","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A 32-year-old man is brought to the casualty after he developed sudden onset dyspnea, chest pain, and palpitations at a party. His blood pressure is 100/62 mmHg. His ECG is shown below. What is the drug of choice to treat his condition?","o":["Procainamide","Adenosine","Verapamil","Propranolol"],"a":1,"e":"The ECG is suggestive of paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardia (PSVT) as it shows:\n\n• Narrow QRS complexes\n\n• No visible p waves\n\n• Pseudo R' waves in V1 lead\n\nAdenosine is the preferred drug in stable paroxysmal supraventricular tachycardia (PSVT).\n\nTreatment of PSVT:\n\nIn hemodynamically unstable patients, QRS-synchronous DC cardioversion should be done.\n\nIn hemodynamically stable patients,\n\n1. Mechanical measures: These maneuvers result in an acute increase in the vagal tone which decreases AV conduction and terminates the arrhythmia. They include the following:\n\n• Valsalva maneuver\n\n• Lowering the head between the knees\n\n• Coughing\n\n• Splashing cold water on the face\n\n• Breath-holding\n\n• Carotid sinus massage\n\n2. Pharmacologic therapy:\n\n• Adenosine- it is the first-line agent. It blocks conduction across the AV node and terminates the SVT. It is given as a 6-18 mg bolus\n\n• Calcium channel blockers- verapamil and diltiazem\n\n• Beta-blockers- esmolol, propranolol, and metoprolol\n\n• IV Procainamide- it is administered if the tachycardia is being mediated through an accessory pathway. It facilitates the AV conduction and therefore causes an initial increase in rate. Therefore, it is given after administering calcium channel blockers or beta-blockers.\n\nThe given ECG shows pseudo R' waves:","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d87a30aafdb14f189d8ccfeb2af1170a.JPEG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/df8356f046484034b645cc2d6435418fx1280x685.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e38208191fe34a4d96e6d6b9f2e981a8x1280x1845.JPEG"],"t":"Supraventricular Arrhythmias","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is false about accessory pathways?","o":["Bundle of Kent has antegrade conduction","Concealed pathways have retrograde conduction","Antegrade conduction causes preexcitation","Retrograde conduction causes preexcitation"],"a":3,"e":"Retrograde conduction via accessory pathways does not cause preexcitation.\n\nAccessory pathways (APs) are abnormal conduction pathways connecting the atrium and ventricle. They can conduct impulses in the following ways:\n\nAntegrade conduction:\n\n• Occurs from the atria to the ventricle\n\n• The impulses from atria reach the ventricles faster via the accessory pathway as compared to the normal AV nodal pathway as they bypass the AV nodal delay.\n\n• This leads to an earlier excitation of the ventricles known as the preexcitation of the ventricles and thus a wide QRS complex.\n\n• Examples- WPW syndrome and antidromic AVRT\n\nRetrograde conduction:\n\n• Occurs from the ventricle to the atria.\n\n• The impulses reach the ventricles via the normal AV nodal pathway but re-enter the atria through the accessory pathway.\n\n• Ventricular depolarization is normal, there is no preexcitation and thus a narrow QRS complex. It causes supraventricular tachycardia as the impulses re-enter the atria via the accessory pathway.\n\n• Examples- concealed accessory pathways (can cause SVTs) and orthodromic AVRT.","t":"Supraventricular Arrhythmias","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is the most common reentrant tachycardia caused by an accessory pathway?","o":["Orthodromic AV reentry","Antidromic AV reentry","WPW syndrome","AV nodal reentry"],"a":0,"e":"The most common reentrant tachycardia caused by an accessory pathway is orthodromic AV reentry.\n\nAccessory pathways (APs) are abnormal conduction pathways connecting the atrium and ventricle. They can conduct impulses in the following ways:\n\nAntegrade conduction:\n\n• Occurs from the atria to the ventricle\n\n• The impulses from atria reach the ventricles faster via the accessory pathway as compared to the normal AV nodal pathway as they bypass the AV nodal delay.\n\n• This leads to an earlier excitation of the ventricles known as the preexcitation of the ventricles and thus a wide QRS complex.\n\n• Examples- WPW syndrome and antidromic AVRT\n\nRetrograde conduction:\n\n• Occurs from the ventricle to the atria.\n\n• The impulses reach the ventricles via the normal AV nodal pathway but re-enter the atria through the accessory pathway.\n\n• Ventricular depolarization is normal, there is no preexcitation and thus a narrow QRS complex. It causes supraventricular tachycardia as the impulses re-enter the atria via the accessory pathway.\n\n• Examples- concealed accessory pathways (can cause SVTs) and orthodromic AVRT.\n\nAV nodal reentry tachycardia (AVNRT) is not caused by an accessory pathway. It involves tissues with different conduction speeds within the AV node or just adjacent to the AV node, known as the fast pathway and the slow pathway. This is known as dual AV nodal physiology.","t":"Supraventricular Arrhythmias","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A patient's ECG shows a short PR interval and delta wave. Additional findings include atrial fibrillation and rapid ventricular rate. He is hemodynamically stable. All of the following drugs can be used in the management except:","o":["Amiodarone","Ibutilide","Procainamide","Verapamil"],"a":3,"e":"The given clinical scenario describes a patient with WPW syndrome with atrial fibrillation.\n\nRate-slowing drugs such as non-dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers (eg, verapamil, diltiazem), beta-blockers, and digoxin are contraindicated in these patients. They increase the refractoriness of the AV node with minimal or no effect on the accessory pathway. This can lead to faster ventricular rates, increasing the risk of ventricular fibrillation.\n\n The ECG below shows a short PR interval (<120 ms) and delta waves in WPW syndrome.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e995440e11774983af8d62b5c045aadfx1280x707.JPEG"],"t":"Supraventricular Arrhythmias","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"The most common location of the accessory pathway in WPW syndrome is:","o":["Left free wall","Posteroseptal","Right free wall","Anteroseptal"],"a":0,"e":"The most common location of an accessory pathway in WPW syndrome is on the left free wall. It is present between left atrium and free wall of left ventricle.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/31cf21977756492a9f510532fe907b41x529x368.JPEG"],"t":"Supraventricular Arrhythmias","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"All are true about the condition that is diagnosed by the given ECG except:","o":["It is a congenital condition","Can be seen in asymptomatic individuals","Prevalence increases with age","Caused by an abnormal accessory pathway"],"a":2,"e":"The given ECG is suggestive of Wolff-Parkinson-White syndrome (WPW). Its prevalence decreases with age.\n\nThe classic ECG features in WPW are as follows:\n\n•\n\nA shortened PR interval (typically <120 ms)\n\n•\n\nA slurring and the slow rise of the initial upstroke of the QRS complex (delta wave)\n\n•\n\nA widened QRS complex (total duration >0.12 seconds)\n\n•\n\nST-segment–T wave (repolarization) changes, generally directed opposite the major delta wave and QRS complex, reflecting altered depolarization","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/bf7cc55fc53b4aa59a2a3e7d29495a66.PNG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/417eb888db05485f932f2cbda6d267f6x571x439.PNG"],"t":"Supraventricular Arrhythmias","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A 30-year-old woman comes to the clinic with complaints of recurrent palpitation and lightheadedness. An ECG was taken and is as shown below. What is the best step in management?","o":["DC cardioversion","Carotid sinus massage","Synchronised cardioversion","Catheter ablation"],"a":3,"e":"The ECG shows the presence of delta waves (preexcitation wave), wide QRS, and short PR interval which are classically seen in Wolff Parkinson White (WPW) syndrome. In this patient, catheter ablation is the best for long-term management.\n\nPatients with preexcitation who have symptoms of arrhythmia are at risk for developing AF and sudden death if they have an accessory pathway that allows rapid antegrade conduction (preexcitation & tachyarrhythmias are features of WPW syndrome due to the abnormal accessory pathway).\n\nComplications of catheter ablation are rare but include AV block, cardiac tamponade, thromboembolic events, and hematoma at the access site.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ceea5122e7a248ac85308dd2f6c06b26.JPEG"],"t":"Supraventricular Arrhythmias","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is true regarding sick sinus syndrome?","o":["Most patients are symptomatic in the early stages","Most commonly in young patients","Frequently seen in patients with atrial fibrillation","Treated using beta-blockers"],"a":2,"e":"Sick sinus syndrome is frequently seen in patients with concomitant atrial fibrillation.\n\nSick sinus syndrome (or sinus node dysfunction) is a group of abnormal heart rhythms. It is a disorder of the sinoatrial node caused by impaired pacemaker function and impulse transmission. This leads to a combination of abnormal rhythms, including atrial bradyarrhythmias, atrial tachyarrhythmias, and sometimes a pattern of alternating bradycardia and tachycardia, known as tachy-brady syndrome. These irregular rhythms can cause symptoms such as palpitations, decreased tissue perfusion, subsequent fatigue, lightheadedness, pre-syncope, and syncope.\n\nThe management of sick sinus syndrome includes identifying and correcting reversible factors, as an initial step.\n\nSome examples of reversible causes of Sinus Node Dysfunction include,\n\n• Hypothyroidism\n\n• Sleep apnea\n\n• Anti-hypertensive medications eg. Beta-blockers\n\n• Anti-arrhythmic medications eg. Amiodarone, Procainamide\n\nAmbulatory ECG monitoring is required to diagnose as the symptoms are nonspecific.\n\nTreatment involves permanent pacing.\n\nOther options:\n\nOption A: The course of sinus node dysfunction is usually a slow progression over decades. In the early stages, most patients are asymptomatic.\n\nOption B: It occurs most commonly in elderly patients due to the gradual loss of SA node function.\n\nOption D: Beta-blockers are contraindicated in this condition.","t":"Supraventricular Arrhythmias","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A hypertensive patient was brought to the emergency due to complaints of palpitations and breathing difficulty. Comment on his ECG.","o":["Multifocal PVC","Unifocal PVC","Ventricular quadrigeminy","Ventricular bigeminy"],"a":3,"e":"The above ECG shows a pattern of premature ventricular contractions, one premature beat after one normal beat. This is seen in ventricular bigeminy.\n\nPremature ventricular contractions/beats (PVC) are single ventricular beats that occur earlier than the next normal supraventricular beat. They originate from abnormal foci in the ventricular tissue:\n\n• Unifocal PVCs arise from a single focus and hence have the same QRS morphology\n\n• Multifocal PVCs arise from multiple foci and therefore have different QRS morphologies\n\nThey are not preceded by a P wave as they do not originate in the atria. They show wide QRS complexes (>0.12 ms) on the ECG because they are conducted via normal myocardial tissue instead of the Purkinje system.\n\nVentricular bigeminy refers to a pair of normal and premature ventricular contractions, whereas ventricular trigeminy refers to a premature beat that occurs after two normal beats. Quadrigeminy is when a premature beat follows three normal beats.\n\nFew other terms associated with premature ventricular contractions:\n\n• Two successive PVCs are known as a couplet\n\n• Three successive PVCs are known as a triplet.\n\nThe given ECG shows ventricular bigeminy with premature beats circled in red and normal ventricular contractions circled in green.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/56d3f3941c6d447d9e74e846087b52a0.PNG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e2b6162528124a3ba471869ec72fd6b5x1280x774.JPEG"],"t":"Ventricular Arrhythmias And Heart Blocks","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Identify the marked ECG feature in a patient diagnosed with ventricular tachycardia.","o":["Fusion beat","Capture beat","Polymorphic QRS","Triphasic QRS"],"a":0,"e":"The ECG feature of ventricular tachycardia seen in the given image is a fusion beat. It occurs when a sinus and ventricular beat coincide to produce a hybrid complex.\n\nVentricular tachycardia (VT) is defined as ≥ 3 consecutive premature ventricular contractions at a rate of >100/min. It is characterized by:\n\n• Wide QRS complex\n\n• AV dissociation- Atrial activity independent of ventricular activity, seen on the ECG as every p wave not followed by QRS complex\n\n• Fusion beats- occur when a sinus and ventricular beat coincide to produce a hybrid complex\n\n• Capture beats occur when the SA node transiently ‘captures’ the ventricles, that is, ventricles are depolarized by the impulses from the SA node producing a QRS complex of normal duration.\n\nThe given ECG shows a fusion beat (red arrow), and a capture beat (green arrow) can also be seen:\n\nThe given image shows capture beats in VT:\n\nOption C: Polymorphic QRS, that is, continuously changing QRS morphology is seen in polymorphic ventricular tachycardia.\n\nOption D: Triphasic QRS (eg. RSR′ pattern on V1) is seen in supraventricular tachycardias with the wide QRS complex.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/eae88e12ae174243bfc7833c17211031.JPEG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/840243c451624f6f87a76dc22f4929c2x1280x859.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ee4d7b7344274b0985360d4b8308dcc9x1280x780.JPEG"],"t":"Ventricular Arrhythmias And Heart Blocks","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"All of the following are effective in the management of patients with ventricular tachycardia except:","o":["Procainamide","Amiodarone","DC cardioversion","Carotid sinus massage"],"a":3,"e":"Carotid sinus massage will be effective only in supraventricular tachycardias but not in ventricular tachycardias. This is because it acts by increasing the AV node block which does not play any role in the pathogenesis of ventricular tachycardia.\n\nHence, AV node blocking maneuvers (carotid sinus massage, valsalva) and drugs (adenosine) are not used in the management of VT.","t":"Ventricular Arrhythmias And Heart Blocks","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Accelerated idioventricular rhythm is the most common arrhythmia associated with:","o":["Myocardial reperfusion","Pericarditis","Cocaine intoxication","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy"],"a":0,"e":"Accelerated idioventricular rhythm is the most common arrhythmia associated with myocardial reperfusion, most commonly in the first 2 days after presentation.\n\nIdioventricular rhythm is defined as ≥ 3 consecutive premature ventricular contractions with a heart rate of <50 bpm. If the heart rate is 60-100 bpm, it is known as accelerated idioventricular rhythm, and if it is more than 100 bpm it is known as ventricular tachycardia. It may be the result of an abnormal focus in the ventricle that generates an impulse faster than the sinus node and therefore assumes control.\n\nOther causes of AIVR include:\n\n• Buerger disease\n\n• Congenital heart disease\n\n• Dilated cardiomyopathy\n\n• Myocarditis\n\n• Drugs: Digoxin toxicity, cocaine toxicity, and various anesthesia agents\n\n• Electrolyte abnormality\n\n• Postresuscitation\n\nEpisodes of AIVR have not been found to affect prognosis in acute MI and usually do not require intervention. If AIVR is accompanied by hemodynamic compromise, treatment with atropine or atrial pacing will often suppress the rhythm.","t":"Ventricular Arrhythmias And Heart Blocks","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Automatic implantable cardioverter-defibrillator (AICD) implantation is done for all of the following conditions except:","o":["Brugada syndrome","Ventricular fibrillation","Acute coronary syndrome with low ejection fraction","Acute myocardial infarction"],"a":3,"e":"An automated implantable cardioverter-defibrillator (AICD) is a device placed in the chest or abdomen to detect arrhythmias and perform cardioversion, defibrillation, and pace the heart.\n\nIt is contraindicated in patients experiencing tachyarrhythmias with reversible or transient causes such as acute MI.\n\nIt is used in:\n\n• Brugada syndrome- refers to a sodium channelopathy with a high risk of arrhythmias that can lead to sudden cardiac death.\n\n• Ventricular fibrillation and ventricular tachycardia.\n\n• Cardiac resynchronization therapy for prevention of sudden cardiac death in heart failure.\n\n• High-risk situations of sudden cardiac death in hypertrophic cardiomyopathy.","t":"Ventricular Arrhythmias And Heart Blocks","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Sudden cardiac death is defined as unexpected nontraumatic death in stable patients within:","o":["1 hour after onset of symptoms","3 hours after onset of symptoms","120 mins after onset of symptoms","90 mins after onset of symptoms"],"a":0,"e":"Sudden cardiac death is defined as unexpected non-traumatic death in clinically well or stable patients who die within 1 hour after onset of symptoms.\n\nThe causative rhythm in most cases is ventricular fibrillation, which is usually preceded by ventricular tachycardia except in the setting of acute ischemia or infarction.","t":"Ventricular Arrhythmias And Heart Blocks","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A 60-year-old man with a history of ischemic heart disease is brought to the emergency in an unconscious state. He has been neglecting symptoms of dizziness and palpitations for the past few days. His ECG is given below. The most possible diagnosis is:","o":["Monomorphic VT","Polymorphic VT","Ventricular fibrillation","Torsades de pointes"],"a":2,"e":"The given ECG is suggestive of ventricular fibrillation.\n\nThe features in the above ECG that support the diagnosis are:\n\n• Disordered electrical ventricular activation with no identifiable QRS complexes\n\n• No identifiable P-waves, no PR interval\n\nPossible mechanisms of ventricular fibrillation include spiral wave reentry and multiple circulating reentry wavefronts.\n\nThe ECG features suggestive of ventricular tachycardia are as follows:\n\n• Wide QRS complex with abnormal morphology (marked with red arrows in image below)\n\n• Absence of the P waves\n\n• Rate >100/min- in the ECG below, it is ~180 beats/min (1500/no. of small boxes between two R waves)","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/33b712ef95914775aedb42cea4ec91dc.JPEG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4b491422ab4c4ed3b1715d7a5627af64x854x131.PNG"],"t":"Ventricular Arrhythmias And Heart Blocks","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A patient who is a known case of Crohn's disease was brought to the emergency. He complained of sudden onset cold sweats and palpitations. Which among the following is the likely cause of his ECG finding shown in the given image?","o":["Hypomagnesemia","Hypophosphatemia","Hyperkalemia","Hypercalcemia"],"a":0,"e":"The given ECG finding shows torsades de pointes, which can occur due to hypomagnesemia (Crohn's disease causes chronic diarrhea which can cause excess GI loss of magnesium).\n\nTorsades de pointes means twisting of points, it implies twisting of the QRS complexes around the isoelectric line of the ECG. It is a form of polymorphic ventricular tachycardia that is seen in patients with a prolonged QT interval.\n\nA prolonged QT interval usually results from prolonged ventricular repolarization (lengthening of T wave). A premature ventricular contraction falling in the elongated T wave can initiate torsades de pointes.\n\nThe features of torsades de pointes are:\n\n• Polymorphic QRS complexes that change in amplitude\n\n• Cycle length giving an appearance of oscillations around the baseline\n\n• QT prolongation","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f0a2ae1bbefa4b058e14446d0f511207.PNG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c7e91f991605450a889ac12a01c9ce85x596x659.PNG"],"t":"Ventricular Arrhythmias And Heart Blocks","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is seen in patients with Brugada syndrome?","o":["ST segment elevation in V1-V3","ST segment elevation in V4-V6","ST segment elevation in 1, V5, AvL","ST segment elevation in II , III AvF"],"a":0,"e":"The major feature of Brugada syndrome is transient or concealed ST-segment elevation in V1 to V3.\n\nBrugada syndrome is a rare syndrome characterized by ST-segment elevation and negative T waves in more than 1 anterior precordial leads ( V1- V3 ). It is associated with mutations involving cardiac sodium channels. The patients have episodes of polymorphic VT which may lead to syncope or cardiac arrest.\n\nIt shows an autosomal dominant inheritance pattern and affects males more than females. The episodes of VT may occur either in sleep or maybe provoked by febrile illness or sodium channel-blocking drugs (Class I antiarrhythmics) like ajmaline, flecainide, and procainamide.\n\nThe episodes of VT can be suppressed using quinidine or catheter ablation. ICD can be used in individuals who have been resuscitated from cardiac arrest.","t":"Ventricular Arrhythmias And Heart Blocks","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Brugada syndrome is associated with:","o":["Left bundle branch block","Left anterior fascicular block","Left posterior fascicular block","Right bundle branch block"],"a":3,"e":"Brugada syndrome is associated with right bundle branch block. It is an inherited disease that predisposes patients to potentially life threatening arrythmias.\n\nThe ECG below is depictive of Brugada syndrome with concave ST segments and negative T waves (to be seen in more than one anterior precordial leads, i.e., V1-V3)","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/be306bd6f6fd4cc08068642c7b672f3dx1159x572.PNG"],"t":"Ventricular Arrhythmias And Heart Blocks","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Romano-Ward syndrome is:","o":["Congenital short PR associated with deafness","Congenital long QT without deafness","Congenital long QT associated with cataract","Congenital short PR without cataract"],"a":1,"e":"Romano-Ward syndrome is a congenital long QT syndrome without deafness.\n\nRomano-Ward syndrome is associated with specific genetic mutations affecting membrane potassium and sodium channels. The inheritance pattern may be autosomal recessive or autosomal dominant.\n\nThe preferred drugs for managing this condition are the beta-blockers. Implantable cardioverter-defibrillator (ICD) implantation can also be done.\n\nJervell-Lange-Nielsen syndrome is congenital long QT with deafness.\n\nDISORDER\nGene and its Function\n\nLong QT\nsyndrome\n\nKCNQ1\nK+ channel (LOF)\n\nKCNH2\nK+ channel (LOF)\n\nSCN5A\nNa+ channel (GOF)\n\nCAV3\nCaveolin, Na+ current (GOF)\n\nShort QT\nsyndrome\n\nKCNQ1\nK+ channel (GOF)\n\nKCNH2\nK+ channel (GOF)\n\nBrugada\nsyndrome\n\nSCN5A\nNa+ channel (LOF)\n\nCACNB2b\nCa++ channel (LOF)\n\nSCN1b\nNa+ channel (LOF)\n\nCPVT\nsyndrome\n\nRYR2\nDiastolic Ca++ release (GOF)\n\nCASQ2\nDiastolic Ca++ release (LOF)\n\nLOF= Loss of Function\n\nGOF= Gain of Function","t":"Ventricular Arrhythmias And Heart Blocks","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"The ECG of a patient as given below is suggestive of:","o":["1st degree AV Block","Mobitz type I","Mobitz type II","Complete AV Block"],"a":0,"e":"The above ECG rhythm strip shows 1st degree AV Block.\n\nThe following features of this ECG support the diagnosis:\n\n• The P wave precedes each and every QRS complex\n\n• PR interval is prolonged\n\n• The PR interval is constant in every beat","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/dfd8f3d79be14548ad01b952dda23e85.JPEG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a9c4147a0a6746e892b07e953a82cf37x1280x402.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/df5ebffa689c4c46b5ca7c1e20380017x1280x402.JPEG"],"t":"Ventricular Arrhythmias And Heart Blocks","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A concerned wife brought her 43-year-old husband to the clinic after an episode of light-headedness and palpitations. An ECG was taken. What is the diagnosis?","o":["Mobitz type II block","Mobitz type I block","Complete Heart block","1st Degree AV block"],"a":1,"e":"This patient has symptoms and ECG findings suggestive of Mobitz type I or Wenckebach block.\n\nIn the given ECG PR segment progressively increases in each successive cycle, until a P wave of the series fails to produce a QRS complex (dropped QRS). In this example, for every 5 'P' waves, there are only 4 'QRS' complexes; hence, this is a 5:4 Wenckebach block.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/984406bb7c55446aa29563339e422f4b.PNG"],"t":"Ventricular Arrhythmias And Heart Blocks","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"All of the following may be seen in Mobitz type II heart block except:","o":["Block in bundle of His","Atrial rate more than the ventricular rate","Constant PR interval","P-QRS dissociation"],"a":3,"e":"The absence of a constant relationship between the P wave and the QRS complex (P-QRS dissociation) is a feature of complete heart block. It is not seen in Mobitz type II second-degree heart block.\n\nThe PR interval remains constant in Mobitz type II second-degree heart block but few p waves are not followed by QRS complex. It occurs due to a block in the bundle of His. The atrial rate is more than the ventricular rate.","t":"Ventricular Arrhythmias And Heart Blocks","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A 56-year-old woman was brought to the emergency room after she lost consciousness briefly during a speech. An ECG was taken immediately. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Mobitz type II block","Mobitz type I block","Complete Heart block","1st Degree AV block"],"a":0,"e":"The given ECG shows a constant PR interval in each successive cycle followed by a dropped QRS (P wave fails to produce a QRS complex). This pattern is suggestive of Mobitz type II block.\n\nMobitz type II block is characterized by an intermittent failure of conduction of the p wave without any change in the preceding impulses.\n\nThe dropped QRS is marked in the image below:\n\nNote: Bradyarrhythmias that cause syncope include those due to severe sinus node dysfunction and AV block( Mobitz type II block, complete AV block). Syncope due to bradycardia or asystole is referred to as Stokes-Adams attack.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/180d8d67ced845ae98a7e352a9d98dee.JPEG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/93a4a2eb43154cf4af9520d5163d584bx800x145.JPEG"],"t":"Ventricular Arrhythmias And Heart Blocks","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Interpret the given ECG:","o":["Wenckebach block","Mobitz block","Complete heart block","1st Degree heart block"],"a":2,"e":"The above ECG shows a complete heart block.\n\nFeatures in the ECG supporting the diagnosis of complete heart block:\n\n• PP interval is constant\n\n• RR interval is constant\n\n• P waves more in number than QRS complexes\n\n• There is no relation between P waves and the QRS complexes.\n\nFor a second-degree heart block, the QRS complex should be preceded by the P wave, whereas in a third-degree heart block, the QRS complex is independent of the P wave.\n\nThe ECG features are marked in the image below:","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/af8526277f0441f4a2c819c8cf565d6c.JPEG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2cda5e1e69cc4437b5481b5bc60d6f25x720x178.JPEG"],"t":"Ventricular Arrhythmias And Heart Blocks","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"You observe that an ECG recording revealed a QRS complex &gt; 0.12 seconds. Which of the following would you not include in the differential diagnosis?","o":["Ventricular tachycardia","Wolff-Parkinson-White syndrome","Left anterior fascicular block","Right bundle branch block"],"a":2,"e":"QRS > 0.12s points towards a wide QRS complex. The left anterior fascicular block does not cause a wide QRS complex.\n\nFascicular blocks do not prolong the QRS interval. They only lead to axis deviation.\n\n• Left anterior fascicular block: left axis deviation\n\n• Left posterior fascicular block: right axis deviation\n\nWide QRS complexes (≥ 0.12 seconds) are seen in the following:\n\n• Ventricular tachycardias\n\n• Ventricular premature contractions\n\n• Wolff-Parkinson-White syndrome\n\n• Bundle branch blocks (complete blocks- QRS interval ≥ 0.12 seconds, incomplete blocks- QRS interval between 0.10–0.12 seconds)\n\n• Hyperkalemia","t":"Ventricular Arrhythmias And Heart Blocks","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"What is the most common cause of valvular heart disease in developing countries?","o":["Rheumatic fever","Infective endocarditis","Congenital","Idiopathic"],"a":0,"e":"The most common cause of valvular heart disease in developing countries is rheumatic fever. This is mainly due to the repeated infections with rheumatogenic streptococci and inadequate coverage of antibiotics. It mainly affects the mitral valve.\n\nIn developed countries, degenerative or inflammatory processes are the most common cause of valvular heart diseases.","t":"Valvular Heart Diseases","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"What is the normal range for the mitral valve area in an adult?","o":["4-6 sq mm","4-6 sq cm","6-8 sq mm","6-8 sq cm"],"a":1,"e":"The size of the mitral orifice in a normal adult is 4-6 sq cm.\n\nDepending on the area of mitral valve opening (MVA), mitral stenosis can be classified as:\n\n• Progressive MS - MVA > 1.5 sq cm\n\n• Severe MS - MVA ≤ 1.5 sq cm\n\n• Very severe MS - ≤ 1 sq cm","t":"Valvular Heart Diseases","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A 45-year-old woman is diagnosed with mitral stenosis. Which of the following features is least likely to be seen in this patient?","o":["Pulmonary artery hypertension","Tricuspid regurgitation","Right ventricular hypertrophy","Left ventricular hypertrophy"],"a":3,"e":"Left ventricular hypertrophy is not seen in mitral stenosis. On the contrary, right ventricle hypertrophy is seen.\n\nMechanism of mitral stenosis (MS):\n\nIn a normal mitral valve, there is no obstruction to the flow of blood from the left atrium(LA) to the left ventricle. Therefore a small LA pressure is sufficient to empty the blood into the ventricle.\n\nWhen the MVA is <~2 sq cm, blood flows from the left atrium to the left ventricle, propelled by a mildly elevated atrial pressure. Hence, patients are usually asymptomatic and may develop dyspnea only when the heart rate increases, as seen in exertion, excitement, fever, etc. All these conditions:\n\n• Increase the rate of blood flow across the mitral orifice, resulting in further elevation of the LA pressure\n\n• Decrease the diastolic filling time, resulting in a reduction in cardiac output\n\nThe image below explains the mechanism of mitral stenosis.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/352c332d48124852aa00a568747835a7x1280x1782.JPEG"],"t":"Valvular Heart Diseases","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A 45-year-old male patient comes to the clinic with 3 months history of breathlessness. After Evaluation, the patient is diagnosed with mitral stenosis. Which of the following is least likely to be heard on auscultation?","o":["Diastolic murmur","Mid systolic click","Opening snap","Pansystolic murmur"],"a":1,"e":"Mid-systolic click is least likely to be heard in a patient with mitral stenosis. It is seen in a patient with mitral valve prolapse.\n\nImportant clinical features of Mitral stenosis\n\nFindings\nCause\n\nInspection\n\nMalar flush, pinched and blue facies\nDecreased cardiac output and increased pulmonary artery hypertension leads to retention of CO2 and vasodilation\n\nPrecordial bulge\n\nPalpation\n\nParasternal lift\nEnlarged right ventricle\n\nPalpable P2\nPulmonary hypertension\n\nDiastolic thrill\nIncreased intensity of diastolic murmur\n\nAuscultation\n\nLoud S1\nIt is seen in initial stages when the mitral valve is flexible.\n\nOpening snap (OS)\n\nIt is caused due to the sudden opening of thickened leaflets.\n\nIt follows A2. As the disease progresses, the duration between A2 and OS decreases.\n\nIt is best audible at the apex and with the diaphragm of the stethoscope\n\nDiastolic murmur\n\nLow pitched rumbling murmur heard with the bell of the stethoscope.\n\nIt follows OS and best heard at apex with left lateral recumbent position. When it is loud, may radiate to left axilla.\n\nThe duration of murmur correlates with the severity of stenosis\n\nLoud P2\nPulmonary artery hypertension\n\nOther Auscultatory Findings\n\nPansystolic murmur\nDue to functional tricuspid regurgitation in severe pulmonary hypertension\n\nGraham Steell murmur\n\nIt is a diastolic murmur due to pulmonary regurgitation\n\nCaused due to the dilation of the pulmonary valve ring in severe pulmonary hypertension\n\nJVP\n\nProminent a waves\nAtrial systole\n\nECG\n\nTall and peaked P waves\nRight atrial enlargement\n\nRight axis deviation\nRight ventricular hypertrophy","t":"Valvular Heart Diseases","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A 48-year-old female patient was admitted to the hospital in view of severe dyspnea. History revealed that the dyspnea was exertional initially and later worsened to breathlessness during normal daily activities. She died on the 3rd day following admission. The image of the autopsy of the heart is shown below. Identify the condition.","o":["Mitral stenosis","Mitral regurgitation","Tricuspid stenosis","Tricuspid regurgitation"],"a":0,"e":"The given image is of mitral stenosis showing fish mouth or buttonhole appearance.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/605ccf70079548dc8de82f986d56f830.JPEG"],"t":"Valvular Heart Diseases","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A 62-year-old woman presents with breathlessness for 5 months. She is unable to carry out her daily activities and has difficulty sleeping. On examination, she has a parasternal heave. On auscultation, opening snap (OS) is heard followed by a low pitched rumbling diastolic murmur. Which of the following findings is suggestive of increased severity of the given condition?","o":["Loud S1","Soft P2","Decreased A2-OS interval","Increased A2-OS interval"],"a":2,"e":"The above clinical scenario is suggestive of mitral stenosis. The more severe the thickening, the earlier the opening snap occurs in diastole. Hence, the decreased interval between A2 and opening snap (OS) indicates increased severity of the stenosis.\n\nThe auscultatory findings which are suggestive of increased severity of murmur are:\n\n• Decreased interval between A2 and OS\n\n• Increased duration of the diastolic murmur\n\nLoud S1 is seen in the initial stages of MS when the mitral leaflets are flexible. When there is further thickening and calcification of the leaflets, they become immobile, and the amplitude of S1 decreases.\n\nIncreased severity of the stenosis increases the left atrial pressure. This increase in left atrial pressure can increase pulmonary artery pressure which leads to loud P2 on auscultation.","t":"Valvular Heart Diseases","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A patient with mitral stenosis presented with worsening dyspnea and fatigue which are now present at rest. He also complained of a feeling of heavy pounding in the chest with recurrent chest pain. You decided to evaluate the patient using echocardiography. When would you advise the patient to undergo a valvotomy?","o":["Mitral orifice is &lt; 0.8 sq cm","Mitral orifice is &lt; 1 sq cm","Mitral orifice is &lt; 1.5 sq cm","In all patients who are symptomatic"],"a":2,"e":"Mitral valvotomy is indicated in symptomatic (NYHA Functional Class II-IV) patients with mitral stenosis (MS) with orifice < 1.5 cm2 in normal-sized adults or <1 cm2/m2 body surface area.\n\nTreatment of mitral stenosis:\n\n• Administration of warfarin in patients with MS who develop atrial fibrillation.\n\n• Surgical treatment includes:\n\n• Mitral valvotomy: It is done in symptomatic patients whose mitral orifice is less than 1.5 cm2. This can be either percutaneous mitral valvotomy or open surgical valvotomy.\n\n• Mitral valve replacement: This is done in patients whose mitral valve is severely distorted.","t":"Valvular Heart Diseases","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A 26-year-old woman undergoing evaluation for panic disorder was referred to the physician for complaints of palpitations and breathlessness. Detailed history revealed the presence of chest pain, which was substernal and not related to exercise. On auscultation, mid-systolic clicks were audible. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Unstable angina","Aortic stenosis","Mitral valve prolapse","Mitral regurgitation"],"a":2,"e":"The above clinical scenario is suggestive of mitral valve prolapse.\n\nIt is caused due to the myxomatous degeneration of the mitral valve. It may be associated with Marfan's syndrome, osteogenesis imperfecta, and Ehlers-Danlos syndrome. It is more common in females of the age group of 15- 30 years.\n\nMost patients are usually asymptomatic. However, some patients may present with symptoms such as palpitations, syncope due to arrhythmias (premature ventricular contractions, ventricular tachycardia, and atrial fibrillation), non-specific chest pain, and dyspnea\n\nAuscultation reveals mid-systolic clicks. If there is associated mitral regurgitation, a systolic murmur might also be heard. The duration of the murmur of MVP decreases on squatting and increases on standing. Whereas the duration of the murmur of MR increases on squatting and decreases on standing.","t":"Valvular Heart Diseases","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Carvallo’s sign is associated with the murmur of which valvulopathy?","o":["Mitral stenosis","Tricuspid regurgitation","Pulmonary regurgitation","Aortic stenosis"],"a":1,"e":"Carvallo’s sign refers to the increase in the intensity of systolic murmur of tricuspid regurgitation with inspiration.\n\nDuring inspiration, the venous return to the heart increases due to the negative intrathoracic pressure. This increases the leak from the right ventricle to the right atrium making the murmur louder.\n\nIn general, the right-sided heart murmurs increase in intensity with inspiration and decrease in intensity with expiration, while the opposite is true for left-sided heart murmurs. Pulmonic ejection sound is the only right-sided acoustic event that decreases in intensity with inspiration.","t":"Valvular Heart Diseases","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"The severity of mitral regurgitation can be assessed by:","o":["Intensity of murmur","Ejection click","Wide split of S2","Loud S1"],"a":2,"e":"The severity of mitral regurgitation (MR) can be assessed by the wide splitting of the S2. Due to severe regurgitation of blood, the pressure in the left ventricle falls rapidly, leading to the early closure of the aortic valve.\n\nClinical findings that indicate the severity of MR is:\n\n• Reduced cardiac output\n\n• Palpable systolic thrill\n\n• Palpable S3\n\n• Wide splitting of S2\n\nNote: The intensity of murmur does not always correlate with the severity of the murmur. Intensity may be diminished in severe MR caused by LV dysfunction, acute MI and periprosthetic valve regugitation.","t":"Valvular Heart Diseases","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A 65-year-old male patient presented with chest pain, exertional dyspnea and syncope. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Tricuspid regurgitation","Aortic regurgitation","Mitral stenosis","Aortic stenosis"],"a":3,"e":"The most likely diagnosis is aortic stenosis.\n\nAortic stenosis is the narrowing of the aortic valve. It may be caused due to the calcification of the normal aortic valve, calcification of congenital bicuspid valve, and rheumatic heart disease.\n\nThree cardinal clinical features of aortic stenosis are:\n\n1. Exertional dyspnea - this occurs due to the left ventricular (LV) diastolic dysfunction. Raised end-diastolic LV volume and reduced LV compliance lead to increased LV pressure, leading to elevation of pulmonary capillary wedge pressure.\n\n2. Angina pectoris - it occurs due to the combination of the increased O2 needs of hypertrophied myocardium and reduction of O2 delivery.\n\n3. Syncope - it occurs due to the combination of decreased cardiac output and systemic arterial dilation which lead to a reduction of cerebral perfusion.","t":"Valvular Heart Diseases","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"On auscultation of a patient, a murmur was heard which is depicted by the following phonocardiogram. Which of the given conditions is most likely to produce this murmur?","o":["Mitral stenosis","Mitral regurgitation","Aortic stenosis","Aortic regurgitation"],"a":2,"e":"The phonocardiogram given above is of an ejection systolic murmur, which is seen in aortic stenosis. It is also heard in pulmonary stenosis. They are differentiated based on the difference in symptoms, site of murmur, etc.\n\n Clinical findings in aortic stenosis:\n\n• Pulse - slow rising and late peaking pulse which is known as pulsus parvus et tardus. A carotid thrill may also be palpated over these arteries.\n\n• Apical impulse - A double apical impulse may be recognized, and in the later stages, it may be displaced laterally.\n\nAuscultation findings in aortic stenosis:\n\n• Early systolic ejection sound - heard in early stages, disappears when the valve becomes calcified and rigid\n\n• Paradoxical splitting of S2 - With worsening of stenosis, delayed closure of the aortic valve occurs. This leads to narrowing of S2 sound, and in severe cases, leads to paradoxical splitting of S2.\n\n• S4 and S3 - heard in severe cases\n\n• Ejection systolic murmur - starts after S1, increases in intensity to reach the peak, and ends before A2. It radiates to the neck and base of the heart. It is augmented by squatting and reduced by the strain of the Valsalva maneuver.\n\nThe image below shows phonocardiograms of normal and abnormal heart sounds.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ee74341748944fe399a382cef7e13e93.JPEG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cf3af64542444f4b88acecf816f28bc3x1280x2348.JPEG"],"t":"Valvular Heart Diseases","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A patient presented with breathlessness on exertion and chest pain. Auscultation revealed an ejection systolic murmur heard best at the base of the heart. Echocardiography showed reduced left ventricular ejection fraction and left ventricular hypertrophy. Which of the following features does not signify a severe form of this disease?","o":["Pulsus parvus et tardus","Single S2","Late peaking of murmur","Wide splitting of S2"],"a":3,"e":"The above features point to a diagnosis of aortic stenosis. Wide splitting of S2 is not a feature of severe aortic stenosis. On the contrary, there might be single S2 or paradoxical splitting of S2.\n\nFeatures of severe aortic stenosis are:\n\n• Pulsus parvus et tardus\n\n• Palpable carotid thrill\n\n• Louder and late peaking of systolic murmur\n\n• Single S2 or paradoxical splitting of S2\n\n• Presence of S3 and S4 heart sounds\n\n• Signs of right and left ventricular failure\n\n• Left ventricular hypertrophy","t":"Valvular Heart Diseases","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following conditions is not associated with aortic regurgitation?","o":["Ankylosing spondylitis","Marfan syndrome","Acute myocardial infarction","Bicuspid aortic valve"],"a":2,"e":"Acute myocardial infarction is not associated with aortic regurgitation.\n\nAortic regurgitation is the backflow of the blood from the aorta to the left ventricle.\n\nIt may be caused either due to the defect in the valve or defect in the aortic root (dilation of the aortic annulus).\n\nConditions that affect valves are:\n\n• Congenital\n\n• Endocarditis\n\n• Rheumatic fever\n\n• Myxomatous (prolapse)\n\n• Traumatic\n\n• Syphilis\n\n• Ankylosing spondylitis\n\nConditions that affect aortic root are:\n\n• Aortic dissection\n\n• Cystic medial degeneration\n\n• Marfan syndrome\n\n• Bicuspid aortic valve\n\n• Hypertension","t":"Valvular Heart Diseases","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A 55-year-old male patient presents to the hospital with palpitations. On examination, a diastolic murmur is heard in the left third intercostal space and pistol shot sound is heard over the femoral arteries. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Aortic regurgitation","Aortic stenosis","Tricuspid regurgitation","Mitral regurgitation"],"a":0,"e":"The presence of a diastolic murmur along with the pistol shot sound over the femoral is most likely to be due to aortic regurgitation.\n\nThe image below explains the mechanism of aortic regurgitation.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2963fd3672f3474aa841f68362712e0ax1280x1895.JPEG"],"t":"Valvular Heart Diseases","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A 58-year-old man presented with exertional dyspnea and angina. On examination, the pulse was characterized by abrupt distention and quick collapse. Auscultation revealed an early diastolic murmur. Which of the following is a feature of this murmur with respect to the likely diagnosis in this case?","o":["Crescendo-decrescendo murmur","Best heard in inspiration","High pitched murmur","Severity correlates with intensity of murmur"],"a":2,"e":"The given clinical features are suggestive of aortic regurgitation. An early diastolic murmur is a high-pitched decrescendo diastolic murmur.\n\nAuscultatory findings in AR are:\n\n• Soft S1 - due to the early closure of the mitral valve\n\n• Diminished or absent A2\n\n• S3 - due to increased left ventricular (LV) end-diastolic volume. The presence of S3 may be a sign of impaired LV function.\n\n• Early diastolic murmur -\n\n• High-pitched, blowing, decrescendo, early diastolic murmur\n\n• Best heard in the left third intercostal space\n\n• Heard with the patient sitting up and leaning forward, with breath held in forced expiration\n\n• Duration of the murmur best correlates with the severity\n\n• Systolic ejection murmur - It is heard due to increased LV stroke volume and ejection rate.\n\n• Austin Flint murmur - a low-pitched, mid to late diastolic murmur. It is caused due to the large volume of regurgitant blood, hitting the anterior leaflet of the mitral valve or LV wall.","t":"Valvular Heart Diseases","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is the criteria for severe aortic regurgitation, in transthoracic doppler echocardiography?","o":["Effective regurgitant orifice &lt;0.3 sq. cm","Regurgitant volume &gt;60mL/ beat","Regurgitant fraction more than 60%","Vena contracta &lt;0.6 cm"],"a":1,"e":"Regurgitant volume >60mL/ beat is the criteria for severe aortic regurgitation, in transthoracic doppler echocardiography (TTE).\n\nDoppler echocardiography can be used to assess the severity of aortic regurgitation. According to this, AR is considered to be severe when:\n\n• Central jet width is more than 65% of left ventricular outflow tract\n\n• Vena contracta width >6mm\n\n• Regurgitant fraction >50%\n\n• Regurgitant volume >60mL/beat\n\n• Effective regurgitant orifice >0.3cm2","t":"Valvular Heart Diseases","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"What is the treatment of choice in symptomatic acute severe aortic regurgitation?","o":["Beta blockers","Aortic valve replacement","Periodic monitoring","Diuretics"],"a":1,"e":"The treatment of choice in symptomatic acute severe AR is aortic valve replacement (AVR).\n\nTreatment of aortic regurgitation:\n\n• In non-severe AR, periodic monitoring can be done. The symptoms of dyspnea and decreased cardiac output can be managed by diuretics and vasodilators.\n\n• In severe AR, surgery (AVR) is indicated in the following conditions:\n\n• The patient is symptomatic.\n\n• The patient is asymptomatic, but the LV ejection fraction is less than 50% or LV end-diastolic diameter is more than 65mm.\n\n• The patient is asymptomatic and undergoing other cardiac surgeries.","t":"Valvular Heart Diseases","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A patient has enlarged neck veins, hepatomegaly and a holosystolic murmur along the lower left sternal margin. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Tricuspid regurgitation","Mitral regurgitation","Left heart failure","Right heart failure"],"a":0,"e":"The most likely diagnosis in the above clinical scenario is tricuspid regurgitation.\n\nClinical features of tricuspid regurgitation are:\n\n• Distended neck veins with prominent c-v waves\n\n• Hepatomegaly with systolic pulsations\n\n• Positive hepatojugular reflex - the distension of the jugular vein when firm pressure is applied over the liver\n\n• Blowing holosystolic murmur along the lower left sternal margin","t":"Valvular Heart Diseases","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"On auscultation of a patient, a murmur was heard which is depicted by the following phonocardiogram. Which of the given conditions is most likely to produce this murmur?\n\n1. Mitral regurgitation\n\n2. Tricuspid stenosis\n\n3. Mitral stenosis\n\n4. Tricuspid regurgitation\n\n5. Atrial septal defect","o":["2,5","2,3","1,4","3,5"],"a":2,"e":"The given phonocardiogram is of a pansystolic/holosystolic murmur. It is heard in mitral regurgitation (MR), tricuspid regurgitation (TR), and ventricular septal defect (VSD).","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2d8973c45052468498058ef4ae75c611.JPEG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5c7adc6b1a1644a59d50083ff2d2fec4x1280x2348.JPEG"],"t":"Valvular Heart Diseases","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Barlow’s syndrome refers to which of the following?","o":["Carcinoid of heart","Mitral valve prolapse","Aortic regurgitation","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy"],"a":1,"e":"Barlow’s syndrome refers to mitral valve prolapse.\n\nMitral valve prolapse syndrome is also called as systolic click-murmur syndrome, Barlow’s syndrome or floppy valve syndrome. It is the most common abnormality leading to primary mitral regurgitation.\n\nNote: Infantile scurvy is referred to as Barlow's disease.","t":"Valvular Heart Diseases","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"In patients with infective endocarditis, the vegetations are most commonly present on:","o":["Aortic valve","Pulmonary valve","Mitral valve","Tricuspid valve"],"a":2,"e":"The mitral valve is the most common valve affected in infective endocarditis.\n\nInfective endocarditis is the infection of the endocardial layer of the heart. It may involve the endocardial layer over the heart valves, mural endocardium, or septum. However, it can also affect intracardiac devices.\n\nNote: Infection of the arteriovenous shunts and the arterio-arterial shunt is called infective endarteritis and not infective endocarditis.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f9351c06f3ee4594b907382bf0c24802x795x1052.JPEG"],"t":"Infective Endocarditis And Acute Rheumatic Fever","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not a cardiac manifestation of infective endocarditis?","o":["Impaired conduction system","Heart failure","Perivalvular abscess","Roth's spots"],"a":3,"e":"Roth's spots are not the cardiac manifestation of infective endocarditis (IE). They are the immunological phenomenon seen in patients with infective endocarditis.\n\nSymptoms of infective endocarditis can be broadly divided into cardiac and non-cardiac manifestations. Non-cardiac manifestations of infective endocarditis are further divided into immunologic and vascular phenomena.\n\n• Cardiac manifestations of IE:\n\n• Presence of new regurgitant murmurs\n\n• Congestive heart failure due to valve dysfunction\n\n• Perivalvular abscesses and intracardiac fistulae- caused due to the extension of infection beyond the valves and into the adjacent myocardial tissue\n\n• Conduction system defects- due to the damage of the interventricular septum by the abscess.\n\n• Embolization of coronary arteries\n\n• Non-cardiac manifestations of IE:\n\n• Immunological phenomena (mnemonic ROGeR):\n\n• Osler's nodes- small, tender, red papules that are present on the distal finger and toe pads.\n\n• Roth's spots- white-centered retinal hemorrhages\n\n• Immune-complex mediated glomerulonephritis\n\n• Positivity for rheumatoid factor\n\n• Vascular phenomena:\n\n• Janeway lesions- these are faint, red, macular, painless lesions, commonly present on the thenar and hypothenar eminences.\n\n• Major arterial emboli\n\n• Septic pulmonary infarct\n\n• Mycotic aneurysm\n\n• Intracranial hemorrhages\n\n• Conjunctival hemorrhages\n\n• Splenic abscess\n\n• Cerebral abscess\n\nThe image below shows Roth spot on the retina.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f2beaccebfbf4c2e8f1e534047c19065x610x607.PNG"],"t":"Infective Endocarditis And Acute Rheumatic Fever","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not included in the minor criteria of modified Duke criteria for clinical diagnosis of infective endocarditis?","o":["History of injection drug use","Immunological evidence of glomerulonephritis","Positive blood culture but not meeting the major criterion","Positive echocardiogram not meeting major criteria"],"a":3,"e":"Positive echocardiogram not meeting major criteria was earlier a minor criterion but is now removed.","t":"Infective Endocarditis And Acute Rheumatic Fever","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"According to the modified Duke criteria, a single positive blood culture is included in the major criteria for which organism?","o":["Coagulase negative staphylococcus aureus","Coxiella burnetii","Viridans streptococci","HACEK group"],"a":1,"e":"According to modified Duke criteria for the diagnosis of infective endocarditis, single positive blood culture for Coxiella burnetii or phase I IgG antibody titre > 1:800 is included in the major criteria.","t":"Infective Endocarditis And Acute Rheumatic Fever","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A known IV drug abuser is referred to medicine OPD from the de-addiction wing due to prolonged fever and new-onset seizures. His fever has been present for 2 weeks and did not come down with antibiotics. An early diastolic murmur is heard on auscultation. Multiple painless skin lesions are present on the hands. What are these lesions?","o":["Osler’s nodes","Janeway lesions","Roth's spots","Pink rim rashes"],"a":1,"e":"The above scenario is suggestive of infective endocarditis. The skin lesions shown in the image are Janeway lesions. They are non-tender, erythematous, or hemorrhagic macular or nodular lesions, which are present on the palms or soles.\n\nAnother skin finding that can be found in patients with infective endocarditis is Osler's nodes. In contrast to Janeway lesions, these are small, tender, red papules which are present on the distal finger and toe pads.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/49118a8b752b4297be2ffedefe4beec0.JPEG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c3d63edfc18c4f9f94efcc959cf489eex600x468.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6cf5ec365ee3472488fbff96ed832bf2x768x1024.JPEG"],"t":"Infective Endocarditis And Acute Rheumatic Fever","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is true about the vegetations seen in infective endocarditis?","o":["Present mainly along the lines of closure","Present on both side of valve leaflets","Extend into the chordae","Small and warty"],"a":2,"e":"Vegetations of infective endocarditis can extend onto chordae and are:\n\n• Large and irregular\n\n• Mainly present on valve cusps\n\n• Prone to embolization","t":"Infective Endocarditis And Acute Rheumatic Fever","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"You are asked to collect blood samples for culture from a patient suspected to have infective endocarditis by a typical pathogen. Choose the correct statement for the same.","o":["3 separate culture sets - two aerobic and one anaerobic","3 separate culture sets - two anaerobic and one aerobic","2 separate culture sets - two aerobic","2 separate culture sets - one aerobic and one anaerobic"],"a":3,"e":"Option D. According to the Modified Duke 2023 Criteria for infective endocarditis, when a typical organism is suspected, two separate culture sets are required, one aerobic and one anaerobic.\nIf an atypical organism is suspected, three or more separate culture sets are used.\n\nAccording to the new Duke’s criteria, there is no specific timing to draw blood for blood culture.","t":"Infective Endocarditis And Acute Rheumatic Fever","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A 38-year-old patient presented with malaise, fatigue, and rigors for 1 week. Multiple puncture sites were present on the forearm along with sclerosed veins. His temperature on admission was 38.5 °C. Examination revealed a pan-systolic murmur which was loudest at the left sternal edge. Three sets of blood cultures were taken. Which organism is most likely to be found in his blood cultures?","o":["Streptococcus sanguis","Enterococcus faecium","Coxiella burnetii","Staphylococcus aureus"],"a":3,"e":"The above clinical scenario is suggestive of infective endocarditis. Staphylococcus aureus is most likely to be positive in cultures as it is the most common cause of infective endocarditis in IV drug abusers.\n\nEndocarditis in injection drug users is mostly right-sided and involves the tricuspid valve.","t":"Infective Endocarditis And Acute Rheumatic Fever","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"What is the most common cause of culture-negative infective endocarditis?","o":["Coxiella burnetii","HACEK group of organisms","Fungal endocarditis","Prior antibiotic use"],"a":3,"e":"The most common cause of culture-negative infective endocarditis is the prior use of antibiotics.\n\nSome proportion of patients with endocarditis have negative blood cultures. The important causes are:\n\n• Prior antibiotic use (most common cause)\n\n• IE due to fastidious organisms or unusual pathogens or fungi such as:\n\n• Streptococci (Granulicatella and Abiotrophia species)\n\n• HACEK organisms\n\n• Coxiella burnetii\n\n• Bartonella species\n\n• Brucella\n\n• Tropheryma whipplei","t":"Infective Endocarditis And Acute Rheumatic Fever","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"An 80-year-old woman with a history of moderate aortic stenosis presented with fatigue, weight loss, and night sweats for 2 weeks. Blood tests showed the following. Echocardiography revealed an intracardiac mass on the mitral valve. Blood cultures showed growth of Streptococcus gallolyticus. What additional investigation should be performed?\n\nHb- 9.9 g/dL\n\nWBC count- 16.0 × 10^9 /L\n\nCRP- 187 mg/L","o":["Hysteroscopy","Cystoscopy","Colonoscopy","Bronchoscopy"],"a":2,"e":"The given clinical features and laboratory investigations are suggestive of infective endocarditis caused due to Streptococcus gallolyticus. In this condition, a colonoscopy should be performed.\n\nStreptococcus gallolyticus originates from the gastrointestinal tract and is associated with polyps and colon cancer. Hence, the presence of this organism in the vegetation of infective endocarditis should warrant a gastrointestinal investigation.","t":"Infective Endocarditis And Acute Rheumatic Fever","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Transesophageal echocardiography is preferred over transthoracic echocardiography as initial investigation in all of the following patients, except:","o":["Patient with suspected prosthetic valve endocarditis","Patient with suspected IE presenting with fever","Patient with suspected IE with a new onset regurgitant murmur","Patient with suspected IE who has previous history of IE"],"a":1,"e":"For a patient with low suspicion of IE, transthoracic echocardiography (TTE) will be done as an initial investigation.\n\nTransesophageal echocardiography (TEE) is preferred as the initial investigation in all high-risk patients and/or in patients where a high clinical suspicion of infective endocarditis (IE) exists.\n\nTEE is also preferred over TTE for evaluating:\n\n• Vegetations < 2mm in diameter\n\n• Prosthetic valve endocarditis (PVE)\n\n• Detection of myocardial abscess\n\n• Detection of valve perforation\n\n• Detection of intracardiac fistulae\n\n• Detection of vegetations in a patient with a cardiovascular implantable electronic device (CIED)","t":"Infective Endocarditis And Acute Rheumatic Fever","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Patients with which of the following conditions have the lowest risk of developing infective endocarditis?","o":["Atrial septal defect","Ventricular septal defect","Patent ductus arteriosus","Tetralogy of Fallot"],"a":0,"e":"Atrial septal defect (ASD) is associated with a very low incidence of IE. In ASD, the velocity of the shunt blood is low due to the decreased pressure gradient between the two atria. This low velocity of the blood does not usually damage the lining endocardium.\n\nUnrepaired VSD is the most frequent congenital heart disease lesion associated with IE, followed by ventricular outflow tract obstructive lesions, such as with tetralogy of Fallot.\n\nRisk factors for infective endocarditis\n\nHigh risk\nModerate risk\nLow risk\n\nProsthetic cardiac valves\nMitral valve prolapse with regurgitation\nMitral valve prolapse without regurgitation\n\nPrevious bacterial endocarditis\nIsolated mitral stenosis\nAtrial septal defect\n\nCyanotic congenital heart diseases\nSurgically corrected left to right shunt with no residual shunt\n\nMitral regurgitation\n\nVentricular septal defect\n\nUncorrected left to right shunt (except ASD)","t":"Infective Endocarditis And Acute Rheumatic Fever","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A 50-year-old woman underwent mitral valve replacement for severe MR, 5 years ago. She is now posted for an elective colonoscopy, for evaluation of altered bowel habits. What is the recommendation regarding antibiotic prophylaxis for this patient?","o":["Amoxicillin, 2g orally, taken 30 to 60 minutes before the procedure","Clindamycin 600mg orally, taken 30 minutes before the procedure","Ampicillin, 2g IM or IV 60 minutes before the procedure","No antibiotic prophylaxis required"],"a":3,"e":"Antibiotic prophylaxis is not required in patients undergoing genitourinary or gastrointestinal procedures even if they have a high-risk cardiac lesion for developing infecting endocarditis (e.g., prosthetic valve).\n\nAntibiotic prophylaxis is recommended for patients who are undergoing dental procedures, invasive respiratory tract procedures, and procedures on the infected skin.","t":"Infective Endocarditis And Acute Rheumatic Fever","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Antibiotic prophylaxis is recommended for each of the following patients undergoing dental procedures, except:","o":["A patient with palliative shunt for cyanotic CHD","A patient with previous CABG surgery","Patient with valvulopathy post cardiac transplantation","A patient with prosthetic heart valve"],"a":1,"e":"Routine antibiotic prophylaxis for infective endocarditis is not recommended for patients with previous coronary artery bypass graft surgery. They do not have a long-term risk of infection.\n\nHigh-risk cardiac lesions, for which endocarditis prophylaxis is advised before dental procedures are:\n\n• Prosthetic heart valves\n\n• Prior endocarditis\n\n• Unrepaired cyanotic congenital heart disease, including palliative shunts or conduits\n\n• Completely repaired congenital heart defects during the 6 months after repair\n\n• Incompletely repaired congenital heart disease with residual defects adjacent to prosthetic material\n\n• Valvulopathy developing after cardiac transplantation\n\nAntibiotic prophylaxis is not routinely recommended for patients with acquired valvular heart diseases.\n\nStandard regimen: Amoxicillin 2g per orally is given 1-hour prior to surgery. In patients with penicillin allergy, clarithromycin, and azithromycin 500mg per orally can be given.","t":"Infective Endocarditis And Acute Rheumatic Fever","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"The blood culture of a patient with suspected infective endocarditis is positive for gram-positive cocci in chains. What is the treatment of choice?","o":["Ampicillin with gentamicin for 2 weeks","Penicillin with gentamicin for 2 weeks","Vancomycin for 2 weeks","Ceftriaxone for 2 weeks"],"a":1,"e":"The treatment for infective endocarditis with streptococci consists of penicillin with gentamicin for 2 weeks.\n\nPenicillin G (2-3mU IV Q4h) or Ceftriaxone (2g single dose daily) can be given as a monotherapy for 4 weeks.\n\nTreatment of infective endocarditis\n\nStreptococci\nPenicillin + Gentamicin\n\nStaphylococcus aureus infecting native valve\nVancomycin\n\nStaphylococcus aureus infecting prosthetic valves\nVancomycin + Gentamicin + Rifampin\n\nEnterococci\nAmpicillin + Gentamicin\n\nHACEK group\nCeftriaxone or Ampicillin + Sulbactam\n\nCoxiella burnetti\nDoxycycline + Hydroxychloroquine\n\nBartonella\nDoxycycline + Gentamicin","t":"Infective Endocarditis And Acute Rheumatic Fever","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"What is the causative organism of acute rheumatic fever?","o":["Group A streptococcus","Group B streptococcus","Group C streptococcus","Group D streptococcus"],"a":0,"e":"Acute rheumatic fever is caused due to infection with group A streptococcus (GAS), which is Streptococcus pyogenes. It usually follows a streptococcal sore throat or scarlet fever. Other groups of streptococci do not cause acute rheumatic fever.\n\nThe pathogenesis of the development of ARF is based on the concept of molecular mimicry. The antibodies which are produced against M protein and N-acetyl glucosamine of group A streptococci, bind to and damage the human endothelial cells.\n\nIt mainly affects children between 5-14 years of age. Patients have various rheumatologic, cardiac, and neurological manifestations. Modified Jone's criteria is used for the diagnosis of acute rheumatic fever.\n\nThe main aim of treatment of rheumatic fever is to relieve symptoms, prevent inflammation and prevent further infections by group A streptoccus by the use of penicillins.","t":"Infective Endocarditis And Acute Rheumatic Fever","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"What is the most common clinical feature seen in patients with acute rheumatic fever?","o":["carditis","Polyarthritis","Chorea","Erythema marginatum"],"a":0,"e":"The most common clinical feature of acute rheumatic fever is carditis followed by polyarthritis.","t":"Infective Endocarditis And Acute Rheumatic Fever","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is a feature of Sydenham’s chorea?","o":["Always associated with arthritis","Affects mainly the head and upper limbs","Occurs within 3 weeks of streptococcal infection","Only restricted to one half of the body"],"a":1,"e":"Choreiform movements in Sydenham’s chorea is mainly seen in the head & upper limbs.\n\nSydenham chorea is characterized by the presence of involuntary, purposeless, jerky movements of the hands, arms, shoulders, feet, legs, face, and trunk. The movements may be generalized or restricted to one side of the body.\n\nIt follows a long latent period (more than 6 months) and therefore can occur alone without any other clinical manifestations.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ea25fdbaa4aa4f1e9d86c7d59a3e1f91x510x451.GIF"],"t":"Infective Endocarditis And Acute Rheumatic Fever","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is a feature of subcutaneous nodules palpated in a patient with acute rheumatic fever?","o":["Painful","Early manifestation","Common in hands and feet","Fixed to underlying structure"],"a":2,"e":"Subcutaneous nodules are commonly present in hands, feet, elbows, and occiput.\n\nThey are firm, painless, and freely movable subcutaneous nodules, present over the bony prominences of hands, feet, elbow, and occiput. They are a delayed manifestation of rheumatic fever and are usually associated with carditis.","t":"Infective Endocarditis And Acute Rheumatic Fever","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is a minor criterion according to modified Jones criteria for diagnosis of rheumatic fever in low risk population?\n\n1. Reduced PR interval\n\n2. Subcutaneous nodules\n\n3. ESR ≥60 mm\n\n4. Erythema marginatum\n\n5. Polyarthalgia","o":["1,5","2,4","2,3","3,5"],"a":3,"e":"ESR ≥60 mm and polyarthralgia are a part of minor criteria for the low-risk population among the given options.\n\nThe major and minor criteria for arthritis differ in low risk and medium to high-risk populations.\n\n• For low-risk populations, polyarthritis is a major criterion and polyarthralgia is a minor criterion.\n\n• In the medium to high-risk populations, polyarthritis, polyarthralgia, monoarthritis are major criteria and monoarthralgia is a minor criterion.","t":"Infective Endocarditis And Acute Rheumatic Fever","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Which among the following is not a feature of joint pain seen in patients with rheumatic fever?","o":["Migratory arthritis","Mainly involves large joints","Symmetric involvement","Good response to salicylates"],"a":2,"e":"In rheumatic fever, joint pain is usually asymmetric.\n\nCharacteristic features of arthritis of acute rheumatic fever are:\n\n• Migratory- the pain moves from one joint to another over a period of hours\n\n• Involvement of large joints – most commonly knees, ankle, hips & elbows\n\n• Asymmetric\n\n• Highly responsive to salicylates","t":"Infective Endocarditis And Acute Rheumatic Fever","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A 30-year-old patient presented to the clinic with skin lesions as shown in the image. On examination, body temperature is 38.5 °C, ESR and ASLO titers are elevated. What is true about the skin finding?","o":["Pruritic","It is called erythema migrans","Disappears after hot shower","Generally occurs in patients with carditis"],"a":3,"e":"The above clinical features are suggestive of rheumatic fever and the skin finding shown in the image is erythema marginatum. It generally occurs in patients with carditis.\n\nErythema marginatum is a pink, non-pruritic rash that generally occurs on the upper part of the arms or trunk but not on the face. It extends from the centre with an irregular serpiginous border. As the size of the rash increases, the center of the rash returns to normal, while the borders remain pink. Hence it is named erythema marginatum.\n\nThe rash may also become more prominent after a hot shower.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0a34819b2d2c4be0bbc94e59f285fafb.JPEG"],"t":"Infective Endocarditis And Acute Rheumatic Fever","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Evidence of preceding streptococcal infection is not essential for making a diagnosis of acute rheumatic fever in patients presenting with:","o":["Polyarthritis","Chorea","Subcutaenous nodules","Erythema marginatum"],"a":1,"e":"Evidence of preceding streptococcal infection for making diagnosis of acute rheumatic fever (ARF) is not required in the presence of chorea.\n\nChorea is frequently a late finding of ARF. When chorea appears, major findings, minor findings and findings of preceding streptococcal infection would have been disappeared.","t":"Infective Endocarditis And Acute Rheumatic Fever","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A patient diagnosed with acute rheumatic fever develops a new-onset murmur. His echocardiography is likely to reveal:","o":["Mitral stenosis","Aortic stenosis","Mitral regurgitation","Aortic regurgitation"],"a":2,"e":"Characteristic initial valvular manifestation in acute rheumatic fever (ARF) is mitral regurgitation.\n\nDamage of the valves leads to mitral regurgitation in the early stages. With repeated episodes of rheumatic fever, there is thickening, calcification, and stenosis of the affected valve.\n\nMitral regurgitation is the most common cardiac lesion in ARF, while mitral stenosis is the most common lesion in rheumatic heart disease.\n\nOther cardiac manifestations of acute rheumatic fever include myocardial inflammation that can also affect electrical conduction pathways, leading to P-R interval prolongation (first-degree atrioventricular block or rarely higher-level block) and softening of the first heart sound.","t":"Infective Endocarditis And Acute Rheumatic Fever","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"The prevalence of rheumatic valvular heart disease peaks between:","o":["5-15 years","15-25 years","25-40 years","&gt;40 years"],"a":2,"e":"The prevalence of rheumatic valvular heart disease peaks between 25-40 years.\n\nAcute rheumatic fever is mainly a disease of children and is common between 5–14 years of age and rare in a person aged more 30 years. In contrast to this, rheumatic valvular heart disease is common between 25-40 years.","t":"Infective Endocarditis And Acute Rheumatic Fever","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"The drug used for refractory intractable rheumatic chorea is:","o":["Probenecid","Diazepam","Haloperidol","Sodium valproate"],"a":3,"e":"The drug used for refractory intractable rheumatic chorea is sodium valproate.\n\nSydenham's chorea is characterized by involuntary, purposeless, jerky movements which mainly affect the head and upper limbs. In the tongue, it is visible as the characteristic darting movements.\n\nMedications used to treat Sydenham chorea are:\n\n• Mild cases: managed by providing a calm environment\n\n• Severe cases: dopamine antagonists like haloperidol, pimozide, or antiepileptic medications like sodium valproate (preferred due to no extrapyramidal side effects) and carbamazepine\n\n• Since Sydenham chorea is thought to be an immune-mediated disorder, steroids are effective in the treatment of severe or refractory cases.","t":"Infective Endocarditis And Acute Rheumatic Fever","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A 12-year-old boy is admitted with fever and polyarthritis. His ESR on admission is 80mm/hr. There is no evidence of carditis. A diagnosis of acute rheumatic fever is made and treatment is initiated. How long will you put the patient on secondary prophylaxis?","o":["Till 21 years of age","Till 17 years of age","Till 22 years of age","Lifelong"],"a":0,"e":"This patient has a rheumatic fever without carditis. He should be given prophylaxis till 21 years of age as the duration of secondary prophylaxis is 5 years or until 21 years of age (whichever is longer).\n\nSecondary prophylaxis is given for patients with acute rheumatic fever (ARF). These patients are at a higher risk of developing a further episode of ARF after a group A streptococcal infection. Hence they should receive long-term penicillin prophylaxis to prevent recurrent infections.\n\nThe antibiotics that can be given for secondary prophylaxis are:\n\n• Benzathine penicillin G (1.2 million units) given every 4 weeks (Best antibiotic)\n\n• Oral penicillin V (250 mg) given twice daily (less effective than benzathine penicillin G)\n\n• Erythromycin (250 mg) twice daily (For patients allergic to penicillin)","t":"Infective Endocarditis And Acute Rheumatic Fever","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is false with respect to lifestyle modifications for managing essential hypertension as recommended by AHA 2019 guidelines?","o":["Low potassium diet","Sodium intake &lt; 1.5g /day","DASH diet","Limit alcohol consumption"],"a":0,"e":"Low potassium increases blood pressure and hence is not recommended in the dietary modifications. On the contrary, potassium supplementation lowers the BP significantly in hypertensives. It is given preferably by dietary modifications unless contraindicated.","t":"Hypertensive Vascular Disease","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A 57-year-old male patient presented to the clinic for a routine clinical check-up. On examination, his BP was found to be 136/88 mmHg. During his subsequent visits, the systolic blood pressure recorded was found to be within the range of 130-138 mmHg and diastolic blood pressure was within the range of 86-88mmHg. According to the European guidelines for hypertension 2018, his blood pressure would be classified as ____________","o":["Normal","High normal","Grade 1 hypertension","Isolated systolic hypertension"],"a":1,"e":"According to European guidelines for hypertension 2018, systolic blood pressure between 130-139 mmHg and diastolic blood pressure between 85-89 mmHg is classified as high normal.","t":"Hypertensive Vascular Disease","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"An 80-year-old male patient complains of headache and dizziness. His ambulatory BP was recorded to be in the range of 144-152/80-86 mmHg. What is the likely diagnosis?","o":["Grade 1 hypertension","White coat hypertension","Secondary hypertension","Isolated systolic hypertension"],"a":3,"e":"SBP ≥140 mm Hg and DBP <90 mmHg is called isolated systolic hypertension.\n\nWhite-coat hypertension is characterized by:\n\n• The office blood pressure is ≥140/90 mm Hg on ≥3 separate office visits\n\n• ≥2 blood pressure measurements taken outside the office are <140/90 mm Hg, frequently using home blood pressure monitoring; and\n\n• There is no evidence of hypertensive target organ damage.","t":"Hypertensive Vascular Disease","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is the gold standard investigation to measure vessel wall stiffness in patients suspected to have hypertension mediated end-organ damage (HMOD)?","o":["Ankle brachial index","Carotid Angiography","Pulse wave velocity","Femoral artery doppler"],"a":2,"e":"Carotid-femoral pulse wave velocity (PWV) is the gold standard for measuring large artery stiffness due to hypertension.\n\nLong-standing hypertension can lead to structural and functional changes in arteries and target organs like the heart, brain, arteries, and kidneys. This is called hypertension mediated organ damage (HMOD).\n\nOrgan\nConsequences\nInvestigations\n\nHeart\n\nLeft ventricular hypertrophy\n\nDecreased diastolic function\n\nECG\n\nEchocardiography\n\nBlood vessels\n\nStiffening\n\nPulse wave velocity of carotid or femoral artery (gold standard)\n\nCarotid ultrasound\n\nAnkle-brachial pressure index\n\nKidney\n\nHyaline arteriosclerosis (benign hypertension)\n\nHyperplastic arteriosclerosis (severe hypertension)\n\nRenal function tests\n\nGFR\n\nRetina\nHemorrhages and cotton wool spots\nFundoscopy\n\nBrain\nStroke, impaired cognition\nMRI","t":"Hypertensive Vascular Disease","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A 60-year-old male patient has grade 2 hypertension. He is also a known case of type 2 diabetes for 9 years. What is the desired goal for blood pressure range in this patient?","o":["120-130/80-89 mmHg","130-140/80-89 mmHg","120-130/70-79 mmHg","130-140/70-79 mmHg"],"a":2,"e":"In a patient (18-65 years) with hypertension and coexisting diabetes, the desired goal for systolic blood pressure is between 120-130 mmHg and diastolic blood pressure is between 70-79 mmHg. In patients above 65 years of age, the desired goal of systolic blood pressure is between 130-139 while the desired diastolic range remains the same.\n\nThis goal is in accordance with the European Society of Cardiology guidelines (2018).","t":"Hypertensive Vascular Disease","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"While treating a 45 year old hypertensive patient with underlying coronary artery disease, what target blood pressure should you titrate for, as per the ESC 2018 guidelines?","o":["140-150 mmHg","110-120 mmHg","130-140mmHg","120-130 mmHg"],"a":3,"e":"The target systolic blood pressure for hypertensive patients in the age group 18-65 years with coronary artery disease is between 120-130 mmHg.","t":"Hypertensive Vascular Disease","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A 72-year-old man who is a known hypertensive for 12 years comes for a regular health check-up. On examination, his blood pressure is 172/100 mmHg. His serum creatinine is 1.5mg/dL, urinary albumin to creatinine ratio (UACR) is 40mg/g, and GFR is 49mL/min. What is the preferred antihypertensive treatment in this scenario?","o":["Captopril with amlodipine","Captopril with atenolol","Telmisartan with atenolol","Captopril with telmisartan"],"a":0,"e":"In the given clinical scenario, the patient is hypertensive along with chronic kidney disease. The preferred initial antihypertensive treatment is captopril with amlodipine.\n\nThe preferred treatment in hypertensive patients with chronic kidney disease is a combination of angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors (ACEi) or angiotensin II receptor blockers (ARBs) with calcium channel blockers (CCBs) or diuretics (ACEi/ARBs + CCBs/diuretics).","t":"Hypertensive Vascular Disease","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A hypertensive patient underwent coronary angiography after he presented with chest pain. His angiographic image is as below. What is the preferred drug for the management of hypertension in this patient?","o":["Spironolactone","Atenolol","Tamsulosin","Hydralazine"],"a":1,"e":"The above coronary angiogram shows partial occlusion of the left circumflex coronary artery. Beta-blockers like atenolol in combination with ACE inhibitors or ARBs or calcium channel blockers or diuretics are preferred for the management of hypertension in patients with coronary artery disease.\n\nOther indications for beta-blockers are:\n\n• Congestive heart failure due to systolic dysfunction\n\n• Post myocardial infarction\n\n• Sinus tachycardia\n\n• Ventricular arrhythmias\n\nThe image below shows partial occlusion of left circumflex coronary artery.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/abd7b993a7e24d4d91b065b98f3dc8a0.PNG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4ddd1e622e36420fa3c560adb8b7af52x470x446.JPEG"],"t":"Hypertensive Vascular Disease","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A 30-year-old man presents with sudden onset of a headache, palpitation, and profuse sweating. He had several such episodes in the past. During these episodes, his BP is found to be high. A 24-hour urinary metanephrine is elevated. What is the most likely etiology?","o":["Fibromuscular dysplasia","Renal artery stenosis","Pheochromocytoma","Hyperthyroidism"],"a":2,"e":"The most likely etiology is pheochromocytoma. Patients commonly present with a classical triad of palpitation, headache and profuse sweating. The presence of this triad in association with hypertension and elevated 24-hour urinary metanephrine makes pheochromocytoma a likely diagnosis.","t":"Hypertensive Vascular Disease","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements are true with respect to hypertensive emergency as per ESC 2018 guidelines?\n\n1) BP >180/110mm Hg\n2) Acute end-organ damage is usually not present\n3) The initial goal in hypertensive emergency presenting as encephalopathy is to bring down the SBP no more than 30% within 2 hours\n4) Should be treated using parenteral drugs","o":["1,3,4","1,4","1,2,4","1,2,3,4"],"a":1,"e":"Hypertensive emergencies are situations in which grade 3 hypertension (BP>180/110 mmHg) is associated with acute hypertension-mediated organ damage (HMOD).\n\nTreatment of hypertensive emergency depends on the type of end-organ damage present (as shown in the table below). It is treated with rapidly acting intravenous antihypertensive agents.\n\nIn hypertensive encephalopathy, the initial goal of therapy is to immediately reduce mean arterial blood (MAP) pressure by no more than 25%. Rapid reduction of blood pressure may lead to reduced cerebral blood flow and result in cerebral infarction.\n\nThe table below shows the treatment of various hypertensive emergencies with intravenous drug therapy.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/58a26c44b82e40bd9ee6dccde9a3edddx1280x1817.JPEG"],"t":"Hypertensive Vascular Disease","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following patients would you diagnose as having hypertensive urgency?\n\n1. A 50-year-old man with a BP of 162/104 mm Hg with no other complaints\n\n2. A 47-year-old woman with alteration in mental status, nausea, headache, and a BP of 190/120 mm\n\n3. A 60-year-old man with a BP of 188/120 mm Hg without any target organ damage\n\n4. A 55-year-old woman with a BP of 200/140 mm Hg along with papilloedema","o":["1","2","3","4"],"a":2,"e":"Patient 3 suffers from hypertensive urgency as BP > 180/110 mmHg and end-organ damage is not seen.\n\nTarget organ damage is absent in hypertensive urgency but is present in a hypertensive emergency.\n\nSevere hypertension (grade 3) is generally defined as blood pressure ≥180/110 mmHg. Hypertensive emergency, hypertensive urgency, and malignant hypertension are all forms of severe hypertension.\n\nOption A: Patient 1 suffers from hypertension. Since BP <180/110 mmHg it is not a hypertensive crisis (urgency or emergency).\n\nOption B: Patient 2 has a BP > 180/110 mmHg along with symptoms of hypertensive encephalopathy. This is an example of a hypertensive emergency as there is severe hypertension with signs or symptoms of hypertensive-mediated organ damage (HMOD).\n\nOption D: Patient 4 has a BP > 180/110 mm Hg along with papilledema. This is a case of severe hypertension associated with fundoscopic changes (flame hemorrhages, papilloedema) which is suggestive of malignant hypertension. Microangiopathy, DIC, and encephalopathy may also be present. Fibrinoid necrosis of arterioles and small arteries is seen. The prognosis is very poor if untreated.\n\nPosterior reversible encephalopathy syndrome (PRES) or acute hypertensive encephalopathy is a rare complication in patients with an acute rise in blood pressure. Due to the acute rise, there is increased cerebral perfusion and edema of the brain. Clinically it is recognized by alteration in mental status, nausea, headache, visual disturbance, seizure, or development of focal neurological deficits.","t":"Hypertensive Vascular Disease","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A 55-year-old female patient who is a known case of hypertension, diabetes, and hyperlipidemia presents to the emergency department with a severe headache and vomiting. The patient is agitated and her blood pressure is 220/120 mmHg. Ophthalmoscopy reveals the presence of papilledema. Which of the following antihypertensive is least useful in the management of this patient?","o":["Furosemide","Nicardipine","Labetolol","Enalaprilat"],"a":0,"e":"The above scenario points towards the diagnosis of malignant hypertension. The drug which is least useful in the management is furosemide(diuretic).\n\nVolume depletion is common in patients with malignant hypertension, and the administration of a diuretic together with a hypertensive agent can lead to an extreme drop in blood pressure.\n\nDiuretics are generally not recommended for the rapid reduction of blood pressure in patients with hypertensive crises. They are used only in conditions of volume overload such as acute pulmonary edema.","t":"Hypertensive Vascular Disease","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is the most important risk factor for the development of hypertension?","o":["High salt intake","Age","Obesity","Genetic factors"],"a":2,"e":"The most important risk factor for the development of hypertension is obesity.\n\nHypertension is defined as repeated office systolic blood pressure of ≥ 140 mm Hg and/or diastolic blood pressure of ≥ 90 mm Hg.\n\nPrimary/essential hypertension is the leading cause of hypertension in adults. It is caused due to the interaction of genetic factors with various other factors such as diet, sedentary lifestyle, etc. In some people, hypertension may result from renal, endocrine, or adrenal disorders (secondary hypertension).\n\nRisk factors of hypertension can be divided into modifiable and non-modifiable:\n\nNon-modifiable risk factors are:\n\n1. Age- risk increases with age\n\n2. Sex- in young and middle-aged adults, the risk is higher in males\n\n3. Genetic factors- mutation in the genes which encode the components of the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system, atrial natriuretic peptide, and alpha adducin\n\n4. Ethnicity\n\nModifiable risk factors are:\n\n1. Obesity (most important)\n\n2. High alcohol intake\n\n3. High salt intake\n\n4. Sedentary lifestyle\n\n5. Stress\n\n6. Dyslipidemia\n\n7. Diet low in potassium and calcium","t":"Hypertensive Vascular Disease","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following arteries is most commonly involved in patients with coronary artery disease?","o":["Right coronary artery","Left anterior descending coronary artery","Left circumflex coronary artery","Left main coronary artery"],"a":1,"e":"The left anterior descending (anterior interventricular) coronary artery is most commonly involved in coronary artery disease (CAD) or coronary heart disease or ischemic heart disease.\n\nThe regions affected are the anterior wall of the left ventricle near the apex and the anterior portion of the ventricular septum.\n\nCAD is caused by plaque buildup in the walls of the coronary arteries that narrow over time and can partially or totally block the blood flow causing myocardial ischemia and subsequently infarction.\n\nThe image below shows coronary circulation.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f161dceb366041b484881ba69aa07ac9x1280x1757.JPEG"],"t":"Ischemic Heart Disease   Presentation And Diagnosis","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Which area of the myocardium is most vulnerable to damage in patients with ischemic heart disease?","o":["Subepicardium","Subendocardium","Pericardium","Midmyocardium"],"a":1,"e":"Subendocardium is the region of the myocardium most vulnerable to ischemic damage.\n\nThe myocardium is perfused from the epicardium to the endocardium. This results in the subendocardium being the least perfused region making it most vulnerable to ischemic damage.\n\nA narrow zone of the myocardium immediately beneath the endocardium is spared from necrosis because it can be oxygenated by diffusion from the ventricle.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ae8efadea5c945288ec0f801b131133ax600x1787.JPEG"],"t":"Ischemic Heart Disease   Presentation And Diagnosis","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A patient presented to the emergency with symptoms of unstable angina. ECG done in pain-free interval showed biphasic T waves in precordial leads without ST changes. Cardiac troponins were normal. Coronary angiography revealed stenosis of the proximal left anterior descending artery. What is the likely diagnosis?","o":["Broken heart syndrome","Wellens syndrome","Barlow syndrome","Brugada syndrome"],"a":1,"e":"The above scenario is suggestive of Wellens syndrome. It is alternatively known as anterior, descending, T-wave syndrome. The ECG pattern of Wellens syndrome is relatively common in patients who exhibit symptoms consistent with unstable angina.\n\nECG in the pain-free interval is characterized by deeply inverted or biphasic T waves in precordial leads and with minimal or no ST elevations. Cardiac enzymes are normal or mildly elevated.\n\nThese patients are at high risk for impending large anterior wall acute myocardial infarction.\n\nThe ECG below shows biphasic T waves in leads V2 and V3 and inverted T waves in leads V4 and V5 in Wellens syndrome.\n\nOption A: Broken heart syndrome is another name for Takotsubo cardiomyopathy.\n\nOption C: Barlow syndrome is associated with mitral valve prolapse.\n\nOption D: Brugada syndrome is a genetic cardiac rhythm disorder.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ab34cfb7ac014017bb5a64f2edb92c38x1280x623.JPEG"],"t":"Ischemic Heart Disease   Presentation And Diagnosis","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"In a patient with suspected stable angina, which of the following findings are likely to be present?","o":["CK-MB is elevated ; Troponin T is normal","Troponin I is elevated ; Myoglobin is normal","Myoglobin is elevated ; CK-MB is normal","Troponin I is normal ; CK-MB is normal"],"a":3,"e":"The levels of cardiac markers remain unchanged in stable and unstable angina as there is no necrosis of cardiac myocytes.\n\nThe clinical presentations of ischemic heart disease can be broadly classified into:\n\n• Chronic coronary syndrome (CCS) - this includes\n\n• Stable angina - this is an episodic angina that occurs with predictable severity, frequency, and threshold of precipitation by stressors, and is relieved by rest and nitrates\n\n• Acute coronary syndrome (ACS) - this includes\n\n• Unstable angina - this is diagnosed in patients with symptoms suggestive of an ACS with no elevation of biomarkers\n\n• Myocardial infarction - it is the necrosis and death of a portion of the myocardium due to prolonged ischemia. The cardiac biomarkers are elevated in this condition","t":"Ischemic Heart Disease   Presentation And Diagnosis","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"While evaluating a patient with chest pain in the emergency, you ask him to localize the sensation. He places a hand over the sternum with a clenched fist. What is this sign called?","o":["Mayne’s sign","Levine’s sign","Frank’s sign","Freidriech’s sign"],"a":1,"e":"When a patient with angina is asked to localize the sensation, he or she typically places a hand over the sternum, sometimes with a clenched fist, to indicate a squeezing, central, substernal discomfort. This is called Levine’s sign.\n\nOption A: Mayne's sign- Drop of at least 15 mmHg in the diastolic blood pressure on raising the arm. It occurs in patients with aortic regurgitation (not reliable).\n\nOption C: Frank’s sign- Diagonal crease in the ear lobe indicative of cardiovascular diseases and/or diabetes.\n\nOption D: Friedreich's sign- Exaggerated drop in JVP seen in constrictive pericarditis.","t":"Ischemic Heart Disease   Presentation And Diagnosis","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A 60-year-old woman presents with chest discomfort, associated with a squeezing sensation in the substernal area. She added that it was precipitated by physical activity and relieved on taking rest. Which of the following is a feature of this condition?","o":["Pain radiating to the trapezius","Pain lasting more than 20 minutes","Pain localized above the mandible","Crescendo decrescendo nature of pain"],"a":3,"e":"The above scenario is suggestive of stable angina in which the pain is usually crescendo-decrescendo in nature.\n\nCharacteristic features of pain in stable angina:\n\n• Squeezing, central, substernal discomfort\n\n• Crescendo-decrescendo in nature\n\n• Typically lasts 2 to 5 min\n\n• Can radiate occasionally to neck, jaw, epigastrium, shoulders, left arm.\n\nAngina is rarely localized below the umbilicus or above the mandible. Myocardial ischemic discomfort does not radiate to the trapezius muscles, that radiation pattern is more typical of pericarditis.\n\nFeatures\nStable angina\nUnstable angina\n\nPain\n\n• Squeezing, central, substernal discomfort.\n\n• Crescendo-decrescendo pattern.\n\n• Precipitated by exercise, cold weather, or emotional stress duration of 2-5 min.\n\n• Same as stable angina but may be more severe.\n\n• Crescendo pattern.\n\n• Pain occurs at rest and may last up to 20 min.\n\nPathology\n\nIschemia due to fixed stenosis\n\nUnstable plaques causing dynamic obstruction\n\nPredictability\n\nPredictable\n\nUnpredictable\n\nDiagnosis\n\n• ECG during exercise\n\n• ECG during exercise contraindicated\n\n• Coronary angiography recommended\n\nECG\n\n• ECG at rest: normal\n\n• ECG in exercise: ST-segment depression and ischemic T-waves\n\n• ECG at rest: ST-segment depression and ischemic T-waves","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e3a547b20e784f779102e5b75a51dc8bx1280x1867.JPEG"],"t":"Ischemic Heart Disease   Presentation And Diagnosis","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A patient presented with angina and ECG showed ST-segment elevation. The pulse rate was found to be 54 beats/min and blood pressure was 98/56 mm Hg. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Anterior wall MI","Posterior wall MI","Inferior wall MI","Anteroseptal wall MI"],"a":2,"e":"The above scenario is suggestive of STEMI with bradycardia which is a feature of inferior wall myocardial infarction. Up to one-half with inferior infarction show evidence of parasympathetic hyperactivity (bradycardia and/or hypotension).\n\nMany patients have a normal pulse rate and blood pressure within the first hour of STEMI.\n\nAbout one-fourth of patients with anterior infarction have manifestations of sympathetic nervous system hyperactivity (tachycardia and/or hypertension).","t":"Ischemic Heart Disease   Presentation And Diagnosis","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A patient was admitted with acute MI. During a daily examination, a transient murmur was heard. What is the most likely cause for this finding?","o":["Mitral stenosis","Mitral regurgitation","Aortic stenosis","Aortic regurgitation"],"a":1,"e":"A transient midsystolic or late systolic apical murmur due to mitral regurgitation secondary to dysfunction of the mitral valve apparatus (papillary muscle dysfunction, LV dilation) may be present in STEMI.","t":"Ischemic Heart Disease   Presentation And Diagnosis","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following cardiac biomarkers is the earliest to rise after acute myocardial infarction?","o":["Creatine kinase","Myoglobin","Troponin T","Troponin I"],"a":1,"e":"Myoglobin is the earliest to rise in acute MI followed by troponin, CK-MB and then LDH.\n\nSerum myoglobin and heart-type fatty acid-binding protein are smaller molecules that diffuse through interstitial ﬂuids more rapidly after cell death than the larger CK and troponin molecules. They become abnormal as early as 30 minutes after myocardial injury. Neither molecule is specific to myocardial tissue. Myoglobin levels are not tested in routine practice.","t":"Ischemic Heart Disease   Presentation And Diagnosis","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A patient is under evaluation for acute MI. Which of the following biomarkers would you prefer to test for?","o":["CK-MB","Myoglobin","Cardiac troponin-T","Cardiac troponin-I"],"a":3,"e":"The most preferred cardiac biomarker for myocardial injury/acute MI amongst the given options is cardiac troponin-I.\n\nCardiac troponin-I, CK-MB, and myoglobin are significantly more specific for acute-MI than cardiac troponin-T.\n\nThe cardiac troponins are particularly valuable when there is clinical suspicion of a small MI that may be below the detection limit for CK-MB measurement. They have replaced CK-MB as the biomarker of choice for diagnosing myocardial infarction.\n\nCardiac troponins:\n\n• Most specific cardiac biomarker for myocardial injury/acute MI\n\n• Elevations are useful for short & long-term prognoses in MI\n\n• Elevations correlate with the estimation of infarct size and risk of death\n\n• Newer highly sensitive assays for troponins become positive within the first 3 hours after onset of chest pain in MI","t":"Ischemic Heart Disease   Presentation And Diagnosis","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Elevated cardiac troponins may not be seen in which of the following patients?","o":["A 24-year-old male with myocarditis","A 65-year-old woman suffering from Takotsubo cardiomyopathy","A patient who underwent cardioversion for VT in the emergency room","A 48-year-old man diagnosed with angina"],"a":3,"e":"Troponins are not elevated in patients suffering from angina. Troponins are elevated in the case of myocardial necrosis (seen in MI) whereas angina is associated with myocardial insufficiency/ischemia.\n\nConditions associated with an elevation in cardiac troponins:\n\n• MI\n\n• Myocarditis\n\n• Takotsubo cardiomyopathy\n\n• Myocardial contusion\n\n• Cardioversion/defibrillation\n\n• LV strain from CHF\n\n• Hypertensive crisis\n\n• RV strain from PE\n\n• Renal disease\n\n• Sepsis","t":"Ischemic Heart Disease   Presentation And Diagnosis","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following biomarkers is preferred for diagnosing reinfarction in a patient post-NSTEMI?","o":["Troponins","Myoglobin","LDH","CK-MB"],"a":0,"e":"Troponin(serial) is the preferred biomarker for diagnosing reinfarction.\n\nSerial troponin levels are used to detect reinfarction, an acute MI that occurs within 28 days of an incident or recurrent MI.\n\nIn case of suspected reinfarction, an immediate measurement of cardiac troponin followed by a second sample after 3-6 hours. ≥20 percent rise in the second sample suggests reinfarction.","t":"Ischemic Heart Disease   Presentation And Diagnosis","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is likely to be seen in a patient diagnosed with acute myocardial infarction?","o":["Ratio of LDH 1 / LDH 2 &lt; 1 in plasma","Ratio of LDH 1 / LDH 2 &gt; 1 in plasma","Ratio of LDH 1 / LDH 4 &lt; 1 in plasma","Ratio of LDH 2 / LDH 1 &gt; 1 in plasma"],"a":1,"e":"In myocardial injury, the LDH-1 fraction increases, and the ratio of LDH-1 to LDH-2 becomes >1 in the plasma. This is called flipped LDH pattern.\n\nNormally, the LDH-2 fraction is greater than the LDH-1 fraction in the plasma, and therefore, the ratio of LDH-1 to LDH-2 is normally <1.","t":"Ischemic Heart Disease   Presentation And Diagnosis","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"What is the maximum predicted heart rate in a 50-year-old gentleman on exercise tolerance test?","o":["150 bpm","170 bpm","200 bpm","160 bpm"],"a":1,"e":"Maximum predicted heart rate on an exercise tolerance test (treadmill test) is given by: HRmax = 220 – Age\n\nSo in a 50-year-old male, the maximum predicted heart rate is 220 - 50 = 170 bpm.","t":"Ischemic Heart Disease   Presentation And Diagnosis","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not a contraindication for performing an exercise tolerance test?","o":["Unstable angina","Symptomatic aortic stenosis","Active endocarditis","AAA with transverse diameter of 5.5 cm"],"a":3,"e":"Treadmill exercise testing (treadmill test) may be safely performed in patients with an abdominal aortic aneurysm.\n\nAbsolute contraindications to exercise testing:\n\n• Acute myocardial infarction (within 2 days)\n\n• High-risk unstable angina\n\n• Uncontrolled cardiac arrhythmia with hemodynamic compromise\n\n• Active endocarditis\n\n• Symptomatic severe aortic stenosis\n\n• Decompensated heart failure\n\n• Acute pulmonary embolism or pulmonary infarction\n\n• Acute myocarditis or pericarditis\n\n• Severe pulmonary hypertension\n\n• Aortic dissection\n\nRelative Contraindications to exercise testing:\n\n• Known left main coronary artery stenosis\n\n• Moderate aortic stenosis with uncertain relation to symptoms\n\n• Tachyarrhythmias with uncontrolled ventricular rates\n\n• Acquired complete heart block\n\n• Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy with severe resting gradient\n\n• Mental impairment with limited ability to cooperate","t":"Ischemic Heart Disease   Presentation And Diagnosis","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following drugs cannot be used for a patient undergoing stress echocardiography?","o":["Dobutamine","Dipyridamole","Regadenoson","Phenylephrine"],"a":3,"e":"Phenylephrine, an alpha-receptor agonist, cannot be used for a patient undergoing stress echocardiography as it causes vasoconstriction.\n\nDobutamine (preferred agent) and vasodilators (dipyridamole, adenosine, regadenoson) are used for pharmacological stress echocardiography.\n\nPharmacologic stress testing is used in those who cannot exercise because of pulmonary veno-occlusive disease, musculoskeletal disease, or exertional dyspnea.\n\nStress echocardiography can be done using either exercise (e.g.: treadmill test or bicycle test) or pharmacologic agents.","t":"Ischemic Heart Disease   Presentation And Diagnosis","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following findings during an exercise tolerance test is not associated with multivessel coronary artery disease?","o":["Early onset of ST depression","Persistence of ST segment changes late into recovery phase","Failure to increase systolic BP by at least 20mmHg","ST segment elevation in aVR"],"a":2,"e":"A failure to increase systolic blood pressure by 10 mm Hg, or a sustained decrease in systolic blood pressure of 10 mm Hg or more, is suggestive of multivessel (or left main) CAD.\n\nOther abnormalities on exercise testing that are suggestive of multivessel CAD include:\n\n• Early-onset of ischemic ST-segment depression\n\n• ST-segment depression of ≥2 mm (0.2mV) involving 5 or more leads, or persisting ≥5 minutes into recovery\n\n• Exercise-induced ST-segment elevation is also consistent with multivessel CAD\n\n• Recent evidence suggests that ST-segment elevation of ≥1 mm in lead aVR may be a significant predictor of left main coronary artery disease, proximal left anterior descending (LAD) artery disease, or at least multivessel CAD\n\nAn exercise tolerance/treadmill test involves walking in place on a treadmill while monitoring the electrical activity of your heart. Throughout the test, the speed and incline of the treadmill increase. The results show how well your heart responds to the stress of different levels of exercise.","t":"Ischemic Heart Disease   Presentation And Diagnosis","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following compounds is used for myocardial perfusion imaging?","o":["Iodine-123","Iridium-192","Thallium-201","Gallium-67"],"a":2,"e":"Myocardial perfusion imaging is done with either thallium-201 or technetium-99m labeled compounds (e.g., technetium-99m sestamibi).","t":"Ischemic Heart Disease   Presentation And Diagnosis","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A patient is being considered for CABG in order to achieve coronary revascularization. Which of the following test will not be useful to assess myocardial viability for predicting the risk of perioperative morbidity?","o":["Thallium 201 imaging","Cardiac MRI","PET","CT"],"a":3,"e":"Modalities for the determination of myocardial viability include:\n\n• Thallium-201 imaging\n\n• MRI\n\n• Dobutamine echocardiography\n\n• PET scan (investigation of choice)\n\nThe investigation of choice for assessing the ventricular function is cardiac MRI.","t":"Ischemic Heart Disease   Presentation And Diagnosis","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Which questionnaire is used for the assessment of a patient who presented with symptoms of angina?","o":["ROSE questionnaire","IPSS questionnaire","CAGE questionnaire","YTS questionnaire"],"a":0,"e":"WHO Rose questionnaire is used for the assessment of angina.\n\nOption B: International prostate symptom score (IPSS) questionnaire is used for BPH.\n\nOption C: The CAGE questionnaire is used to check for signs of possible alcohol dependency.\n\nOption D: The youth tobacco survey (YTS) questionnaire is used to study the prevalence of use of tobacco products amongst youth.","t":"Ischemic Heart Disease   Presentation And Diagnosis","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following ECG findings is first to appear in a patient suspected of acute myocardial infarction?","o":["Elevation of S wave","Prolonged QT interval","Tall T waves","Prolonged PR interval"],"a":2,"e":"During acute myocardial infarction, the ECG evolves through 3 stages:\n\n1. T-wave peaking and T-wave inversion:\n\n• Peaked T-wave aka hyperacute T-wave- first ECG change (occurs within minutes)\n\n• If the blood flow is restored or the oxygen demands of the heart are met, the T waves will revert to normal\n\n• If myocardial cell death has occurred, T wave inversions appear (over days) and persist for months to years.\n\n• A nonspecific finding and is indicative only of ischemia and not diagnostic of infarction\n\n2. ST-segment elevation occurs over minutes to hours (after T-wave peaking)\n\n3. The appearance of new pathologic Q-waves:\n\n• Occurs over hours to days (after ST-segment elevation) and is indicative of an evolving MI\n\n• The persistence of pathological Q-waves indicates irreversible myocardial cell death","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a44fb1227fbd427cabbc10861da7405dx600x1321.JPEG"],"t":"Ischemic Heart Disease   Presentation And Diagnosis","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following conditions is not associated with an ST-segment elevation?","o":["Prinzmetal’s angina","Pericarditis","Ventricular aneurysm","Unstable angina"],"a":3,"e":"There is no ST-segment elevation in unstable angina.\n\nDifferential diagnosis of ST-segment elevation (Mnemonic-ELEVATION):\n\n• Electrolytes(Hypercalcemia, Hyperkalemia)\n\n• Left bundle branch block\n\n• Early(Benign) repolarization, Embolism(Acute pulmonary)\n\n• Ventricular hypertrophy\n\n• Arrhythmia(Brugada syndrome), Aneurysm of LV, Aortic dissection, Antiarrhythmic drugs(Class 1c)\n\n• Tako-tsubo syndrome, Tumor invading left ventricle, Trauma to ventricles\n\n• Infarction(MI), Inflammation(Myocarditis, Pericarditis)\n\n• Osborn J waves (Hypothermia or hypercalcemia)\n\n• Non-atherosclerotic vasospasm(Prinzmetal's angina)","t":"Ischemic Heart Disease   Presentation And Diagnosis","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A 38-year-old woman presented with chest pain that was unrelated to physical exertion. ECG showed ST-segment elevation. Intracoronary acetylcholine provocation testing revealed a transient spasm of the right coronary artery. What is the drug of choice in this condition?","o":["Dopamine","Diltiazem","Propranolol","Digoxin"],"a":1,"e":"The above scenario in which rest pain is associated with ST-segment elevation and transient coronary vasospasm is suggestive of Prinzmetal's angina. The drug of choice is a calcium channel blocker.\n\nPrinzmetal variant angina (PVA) is a clinical syndrome in which chest pain occurs without the usual precipitating factors. It usually results from coronary vasoconstriction (vasospasm) and tends to involve the right coronary artery.\n\nIt is a supply ischemia and usually causes transmural ischemia (subendocardial in classic angina pectoris).\n\nIt often affects women under 50 years of age. It characteristically occurs in the early morning, awakening patients from sleep, and may be associated with arrhythmias or conduction defects.\n\nThe clinical diagnosis of PVA is made by the detection of transient ST-segment elevation with rest pain. Coronary angiography demonstrates transient coronary spasm which is the diagnostic hallmark of PVA.\n\nNitrates and calcium channel blockers both can be used in alleviating symptoms in Prinzmetal's angina. However due to the development of nitrate tolerance in some individuals, they are less preferred and calcium channel blockers such as verapamil, diltiazem are used as first-line agents.","t":"Ischemic Heart Disease   Complications And Management","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A patient who presents to the ER with chest pain and dyspnea is diagnosed with STEMI. Swift revascularization by thrombolysis is planned for her. What is the dose of streptokinase to be used?","o":["0.5 million units IV over 1 hour","1.5 million units IV over 1 hour","2 million units IV over 1 hour","2.5 million units IV over 1 hour"],"a":1,"e":"Streptokinase is given in a dose of 1.5 million units IV given over 1 hour for treatment of MI.\n\nFibrinolytic therapy should ideally be initiated within 30 min of presentation. The fibrinolytic agents that have been approved for IV use in patients with STEMI are:\n\n• Tissue plasminogen activator (tPA)\n\n• Streptokinase\n\n• Tenecteplase (TNK)\n\n• Reteplase (rPA)\n\nAdverse effects of streptokinase:\n\n• Transient hypotension\n\n• Allergic reactions\n\n• Hemorrhage","t":"Ischemic Heart Disease   Complications And Management","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Which of the given fibrinolytic agents is least preferred for thrombolysis in a patient with MI?","o":["Streptokinase","Alteplase","Tenecteplase","Reteplase"],"a":0,"e":"Streptokinase has the lowest clot selectivity among the fibrinolytic agents.\n\nFibrinolytic agent with:\n\n• Least fibrin specificity– streptokinase\n\n• Most fibrin specificity – tenecteplase","t":"Ischemic Heart Disease   Complications And Management","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"While planning thrombolysis for a patient with MI, use of which of the following agents will reduce the chances of a dosing error?","o":["Streptokinase","Tissue plasminogen activator","Alteplase","Tenecteplase"],"a":3,"e":"Tenecteplase is given as a single bolus dose (based on body weight) which reduces the rate of medication errors (common with IV infusions).\n\nTenecteplase and reteplase are referred to as bolus fibrinolytics as their administration does not require prolonged intravenous infusion.","t":"Ischemic Heart Disease   Complications And Management","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not an absolute contraindication for the use of a fibrinolytic agent in patients with an acute MI?","o":["Non-haemorrhagic stroke within 1 year","Marked hypertension","Prolonged cardiopulmonary resuscitation","Suspicion of aortic dissection"],"a":2,"e":"Prolonged cardiopulmonary resuscitation is a relative contraindication for fibrinolytic therapy.\n\nContraindications for the use of fibrinolytic agents\n\nAbsolute contraindications\n\nRelative contraindications\n\nHistory of hemorrhagic stroke at any time\n\nCurrent use of anticoagulants (INR ≥2)\n\nNon-hemorrhagic stroke or other cerebrovascular events within the past year\n\nRecent (<2 weeks) invasive or surgical procedure\n\nMarked hypertension (SBP>180mmHg and/or a DBP>110 mmHg) at any time during the acute presentation\n\nProlonged (>10 min) cardiopulmonary resuscitation\n\nSuspicion of aortic dissection\n\nKnown bleeding diathesis\n\nActive internal bleeding (excluding menses)\n\nPregnancy\n\nHemorrhagic ophthalmic condition (e.g., hemorrhagic diabetic retinopathy)\n\nActive peptic ulcer disease\n\nHistory of severe hypertension that is currently adequately controlled","t":"Ischemic Heart Disease   Complications And Management","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"All of the following drugs should be avoided in a patient with angina except:","o":["Thyroxine","Sumatriptan","Dipyridamole","Trimetazidine"],"a":3,"e":"Trimetazidine is an antianginal drug.\n\nIt is a PFox (partial fatty acid oxidation) inhibitor. It inhibits the beta-oxidation of fatty acids and thus enhances glucose oxidation. This results in decreased oxygen consumption in ATP synthesis as beta-oxidation consumes more oxygen.\n\nOption A: Thyroxine increases the heart rate, which can increase the myocardial oxygen demand and can precipitate angina.\n\nOption B: Sumatriptan and other triptans cause coronary vasospasm. They are therefore contraindicated in patients with CAD.\n\nOption C: Dipyridamole can produce coronary steal phenomenon and can worsen angina.","t":"Ischemic Heart Disease   Complications And Management","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following combinations should be avoided while treating a patient with angina?","o":["Nitrates and β-blocker","Nitrates and Verapamil","Verapamil and β-blocker","Amlodipine and β-blocker"],"a":2,"e":"Verapamil and β-blockers have additive effects on inhibiting SA node and AV conduction, the combination should be avoided to prevent severe bradycardia and conduction block. These agents can also have additive effects on decreasing left ventricular function which could seriously impair cardiac output in a patient with heart failure.\n\nDiltiazem can be combined with beta-blockers in patients with normal ventricular function and no conduction disturbances.\n\nAmlodipine and β-blockers have complementary actions on coronary blood supply and myocardial oxygen demands. Whereas the former decreases blood pressure and dilates coronary arteries, the latter slows the heart rate and decreases contractility.","t":"Ischemic Heart Disease   Complications And Management","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"What is the mechanism of action of ranolazine?","o":["Inhibits adenosine reuptake","Inhibits late inward Na current","Potassium channel opener","Rho-kinase inhibitor"],"a":1,"e":"The predominant mechanism of action of ranolazine is inhibition of the late sodium current in myocardial cells.\n\nRanolazine is useful for patients with chronic angina despite standard medical therapy. It is administered at a dose of 500-1000 mg twice daily.\n\nContraindications:\n\n• Hepatic impairment\n\n• QT prolongation\n\n• Drugs inhibiting CYP3A metabolic system- ketoconazole, diltiazem, verapamil, macrolide antibiotics, HIV protease inhibitors, and large quantities of grapefruit juice","t":"Ischemic Heart Disease   Complications And Management","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A patient on clopidogrel is scheduled to undergo an elective operation. What is your advice regarding the drug?","o":["Stop clopidogrel 12-24 hours before operation","Continue clopidogrel","Stop clopidogrel 7-10 days before operation","Stop clopidogrel 5-7 days before operation"],"a":3,"e":"Clopidogrel should be stopped 5-7 days prior to surgery to avoid the risk of increased surgical bleeding.","t":"Ischemic Heart Disease   Complications And Management","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following drugs would you prescribe to prolong life in a post-MI patient?","o":["Beta blockers","Calcium channel blockers","Nitrates","Aspirin"],"a":0,"e":"Beta-blockers are the only antianginal agents that have been demonstrated to prolong life in patients with coronary disease (post-myocardial infarction).\n\nNote: B-blockers and spironolactone have been demonstrated to improve survival in individuals with heart failure.","t":"Ischemic Heart Disease   Complications And Management","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A patient presents with an intense exertional chest pain radiating to the neck along with shortness of breath for 30mins. ECG reveals ST-segment depression. Cardiac troponins are elevated. Which of the following is incorrect regarding the management of this condition?","o":["Aspirin loading dose should be commenced immediately","ACC/ AHA guidelines call a P2Y12 inhibitors as a class I recommendation","Glycoprotein IIB/IIIA inhibitors are useful adjuncts","Fibrinolytic therapy is useful in this condition"],"a":3,"e":"The above scenario is suggestive of NSTEMI. Antiplatelet and anticoagulation therapies and coronary intervention are mainstays of treatment.\n\nA distinction in acute coronary syndrome between patients with and without ST-segment elevation at presentation is essential to determine the need for reperfusion therapy. Fibrinolytic therapy is harmful in acute coronary syndrome without ST-segment elevation, unlike with ST-segment elevation, where acute reperfusion saves lives.","t":"Ischemic Heart Disease   Complications And Management","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A patient is diagnosed with STEMI on presentation. Coronary angiography showed complete occlusion of the infarct-related artery. Immediate primary PCI with a drug-eluting stent is planned. Which of the following drug-eluting stents will have a relatively higher risk of restenosis and late stent thrombosis?","o":["Zotarolimus","Biolimus","Everolimus","Sirolimus"],"a":3,"e":"Sirolimus and paclitaxel are a part of first-generation drug-eluting stents that carry a relatively higher risk of restenosis and late stent thrombosis than second-generation drug-eluting stents.\n\nSecond-generation drug-eluting stents use newer agents such as everolimus, biolimus, and zotarolimus. These second-generation drug-eluting stents are more effective with fewer complications like stent thrombosis than first-generation stents.\n\nAn antiproliferative agent is attached to the stent by the use of a thin polymer coating. This antiproliferative drug elutes from the stent over a 1 to 3 month period after implantation.","t":"Ischemic Heart Disease   Complications And Management","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A 40-year-old male patient presents to the emergency department with chest pain for 2 hours. The ECG shows ST-segment depression and cardiac troponins are elevated. The patient has a history of PCI 3 months back. He is given aspirin, clopidogrel, nitrates, and unfractionated heparin and shifted to the coronary care unit. What is the next best course of action?","o":["Continue conservative management and monitoring of cardiac enzymes and ECG","Continue conservative management and plan for delayed revascularization procedure after the patient is discharged","Immediate revascularization with thrombolytics","Revascularization with PCI"],"a":3,"e":"The patient described above is categorized as a high-risk patient due to his history of PCI 3 months ago. The next best course of action for this patient is immediate revascularization with PCI.\n\nRevascularization with PCI (percutaneous coronary intervention) is indicated in high-risk patients who have one or more of the following:\n\n• New ST-segment depression\n\n• Diabetes mellitus\n\n• Renal dysfunction - eGFR <60mL/min per 1.73m2\n\n• Ejection fraction <40%\n\n• Early postinfarction angina\n\n• PCI within the past 6 months\n\n• Prior CABG\n\n• Refractory angina\n\n• Symptoms of congestive heart failure\n\n• Hemodynamic instability\n\n• Recurrent angina at rest/low-level activity despite treatment\n\n• Elevated troponin T or troponin I\n\n• Sustained VT or VF","t":"Ischemic Heart Disease   Complications And Management","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"For an acute STEMI patient with cardiogenic shock, what would be the treatment of choice?","o":["Intra-aortic balloon pump with PCI","Revascularization with PCI","Thrombolysis","Intra-aortic balloon pump"],"a":1,"e":"The treatment of choice in patients with acute STEMI with cardiogenic shock is revascularization with PCI.\n\nThe 2013 ACCF/AHA guidelines for the treatment of cardiogenic shock in patients with acute MI are:\n\n• Emergency revascularization with PCI or CABG is recommended in suitable patients irrespective of the time delay from MI onset.\n\n• In the absence of contraindications, fibrinolytic therapy should be administered to patients with STEMI and cardiogenic shock who are unsuitable candidates for either PCI or CABG.\n\nRoutine use of Intra-Aortic Balloon Pump counterpulsation (IABP) is not recommended for cardiogenic shock (based on IABP-SHOCK II trial). IABP can be useful for those patients with cardiogenic shock after STEMI who do not quickly stabilize with pharmacological therapy or have mechanical complications (eg, acute mitral regurgitation or rupture of the ventricular septum).\n\nAlternative left ventricular assist devices for circulatory support may be considered in patients with refractory cardiogenic shock.","t":"Ischemic Heart Disease   Complications And Management","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A 40-year-old chronic smoker comes with acute epigastric discomfort for the past one hour. ECG shows ST-segment depression. What is the immediate intervention?","o":["Aspirin","Thrombolytic therapy","IV pantoprazole","PCI"],"a":0,"e":"The above scenario is suggestive of a non-ST-elevation acute coronary syndrome (NSTE-ACS). Immediate intervention is the administration of aspirin.\n\nAspirin is the first drug given in acute coronary syndromes. An initial dose of 325 mg to be chewed and swallowed is given. If oral administration is not feasible, it should be given as a rectal suppository.\n\nThe following protocol must be followed in the management of patients with acute coronary syndrome:\n\n1. Stabilization of airway, breathing, and circulation\n\n2. Administration of O2\n\n3. Treatment of sustained ventricular arrhythmia rapidly according to ACLS protocols\n\n4. Give aspirin 325 mg\n\n5. History and examination, assess cardiac biomarkers\n\n6. Give three sublingual nitroglycerin tablets each 5 min apart. Add IV nitroglycerin for the persistence of symptoms.\n\n7. Treat left heart failure if present with an after-load reducing agent or loop diuretic\n\n8. Give beta-blockers\n\n9. Give morphine for the persistence of anxiety and discomfort related to MI\n\n10. In patients with STEMI - Reperfusion with PCI or fibrinolysis (if PCI is unavailable within 120 min) followed by antiplatelet and anticoagulant therapy. In patients with NSTEMI - antiplatelet and anticoagulant therapy are indicated.","t":"Ischemic Heart Disease   Complications And Management","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A previously healthy 58-year-old man is admitted to the hospital because of an acute MI. ECG is shown below. Within several hours, he becomes oliguric and hypotensive. On auscultation, no added sounds were present. What is the most likely reason for this patient being in shock?","o":["Mitral regurgitation","Infarction causing septal defect","Right ventricular infarction","Decreased ejection fraction from the left ventricle"],"a":2,"e":"The above ECG shows ST-segment elevation in leads II, III, and aVF suggestive of an inferior-wall MI. In the given scenario, the most likely reason for the patient to have shock is right ventricular infarction.\n\nThe diagnosis of right ventricular MI is suspected in patients with inferior-wall MI, clear lung fields (no added sounds on auscultation), and shock. It can also be confirmed by the use of right-sided electrocardiogram (ECG) leads.\n\nIn patients with presumed right ventricular shock, intravenous (IV) fluid replacement is indicated if jugular (central) venous pressure is not elevated.\n\nOptions A, B, and D: No added sounds on auscultation clearly rule out mitral regurgitation, septal defect (pansystolic murmur is heard), and decreased ejection fraction from the left ventricle (leads to pulmonary congestion and added sounds), as the cause of shock in this patient.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/0f48e03fa1a844e4bac85051716441a2.JPEG"],"t":"Ischemic Heart Disease   Complications And Management","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A patient presented with sudden circulatory collapse and engorged neck veins 3 days post-MI. A bedside transthoracic echocardiogram revealed the following. Which of the following is false regarding the cause of this patient's findings?","o":["It usually results in immediate death","Immediate pericardiocentesis should be done","It usually involves inferior wall","Definitive management is surgical repair"],"a":2,"e":"The echocardiogram shows pericardial effusion with cardiac tamponade (swinging heart sign). The likely diagnosis in the above scenario is myocardial rupture post-MI. It usually occurs 3-5 days postinfarction and commonly involves the anterior wall.\n\nThis condition usually results in immediate death. It is common in older women.\n\nIncomplete or gradual rupture may be sealed off by the pericardium creating a pseudoaneurysm.\n\nImmediate pericardiocentesis should be done to relieve the symptoms of shock due to tamponade. Early surgical repair is the definitive treatment.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/76f89c9e83e44831b877fd857bc82bb2.GIF"],"t":"Ischemic Heart Disease   Complications And Management","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements about Dressler syndrome is false?","o":["It presents as pericarditis","It is an autoimmune phenomenon","First line therapy consists of aspirin","It occurs 2-7 days after MI"],"a":3,"e":"Dressler syndrome commonly occurs 1-12 weeks after MI, not 2-7 days. However, it can recur over months.\n\nDressler syndrome is a post-myocardial infarction syndrome that presents as pleuritic chest pain, fever, and sometimes a pleuritic rub and effusion. The ECG findings include PR segment depression or ST-segment elevation. It is an autoimmune phenomenon occurring due to immune-complex deposition in the pericardium and lung pleura.\n\nThe first-line therapy consists of aspirin.\n\nNote: NSAIDs and corticosteroids impair the infarct healing and predispose the patients to myocardial rupture. Therefore these drugs must be avoided in the early postinfarction period.","t":"Ischemic Heart Disease   Complications And Management","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Left ventriculography with contrast was performed in a post-MI patient and is shown below. Infarct involving which portion of the myocardium is most likely to cause this condition?","o":["Subendocardial","Anterior transmural","Posterior transmural","Inferior wall"],"a":1,"e":"The above image showing left ventriculography with contrast is suggestive of a left ventricular aneurysm. Anterior transmural infarcts are involved in causing aneurysms as a post-MI complication.\n\nPatients with anterior transmural infarcts are at the greatest risk for:\n\n• Free-wall rupture\n\n• Expansion\n\n• Mural thrombi\n\n• Aneurysm\n\nPosterior transmural infarcts are more likely to be complicated by:\n\n• Conduction blocks\n\n• Right ventricular involvement, or both","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/43d06faf636741cc99245724bfdf2c06.PNG"],"t":"Ischemic Heart Disease   Complications And Management","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A 50-year-old man started experiencing chest pain and was rushed to a primary care facility at 10 AM. A diagnosis of acute severe MI was made. Due to the unavailability of PCI, he was taken to a tertiary center by 10:30 AM. By what time should PCI be performed in this patient at the latest?","o":["11 AM","12 PM","11:30 AM","12:30 PM"],"a":1,"e":"The door-to-balloon time for a patient arriving in a hospital that is not equipped to perform a PCI and is then transported to a hospital where PCI can be performed is 120 min.\n\nThe door-to-balloon time is the time interval between the first medical contact and PCI.\n\nIt is 90 min or less for a patient arriving in a hospital equipped with PCI (ACC/AHA guidelines and MoHFW, India guidelines).\n\nIn patients who cannot receive PCI within 120 min of first medical contact, fibrinolysis is given. Fibrinolysis is only considered if the patient presents within 12 hours of symptom onset.\n\nNote: The European society of cardiology (ESC) recommends 60 min target time for patients presenting in a PCI-centre and 90 min maximum target time for PCI-mediated reperfusion.","t":"Ischemic Heart Disease   Complications And Management","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following defines PCI related MI ?","o":["Elevation of cTn &gt; 5 times normal in the absence of any other features","Elevation of cTn &gt; 5 times normal in the presence of symptoms of MI, ECG changes, angiographic findings or imaging abnormality","Elevation of cTn &gt; 3 times normal in the absence of any other features","Elevation of cTn &gt; 3 times normal in the presence of symptoms of MI, ECG changes, angiographic findings or imaging abnormality"],"a":1,"e":"PCI related MI is defined as elevation of cTn > 5 times normal in the presence of symptoms of MI, ECG changes, angiographic findings or imaging abnormality.\n\nThe imaging abnormality could be a new loss of viable myocardium or new regional wall motion abnormality. If the baseline cTn values are elevated but stable or falling, a rise in cTn >20 % defined PCI related MI.\n\nCABG related MI is defined as elevation of cTn > 10 times the normal baseline values in the presence of new pathologic Q waves, angiographic findings or imaging abnormality.","t":"Ischemic Heart Disease   Complications And Management","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is the most common protein mutation seen in hypertrophic cardiomyopathy?","o":["Myosin heavy chain","Alpha-tropomyosin","Cardiac troponin C","Titin"],"a":0,"e":"The most common mutations in HCM are those affecting the myosin heavy chain.\n\nHypertrophic cardiomyopathy (HCM) is an autosomal dominant disease. In the majority of cases it is due to missense mutations in the following two sarcolemmal protein genes:\n\n• Myosin binding protein C (MYBC)\n\n• Beta myosin heavy chain (MYH7)\n\nOption B: Alpha-tropomyosin mutation is seen in HCM but is not common.\n\nOptions C and D: Mutations in cardiac troponin C and titin are associated with dilated cardiomyopathy (DCM).","t":"Cardiomyopathy And Myocarditis","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A 19-year-old basketball player collapses during a match and regains consciousness several minutes later. He reveals that he has had similar episodes before. His older brother, a soccer player, had died suddenly at the age of 22 years during a soccer game due to a heart ailment. Which of the following is least likely to be found on echocardiography in this patient?","o":["Dynamic left ventricular outflow tract obstruction","Systolic anterior motion of mitral valve","Mitral regurgitation with anteriorly directed regurgitant jet","Predominant diastolic dysfunction"],"a":2,"e":"The given clinical scenario of exercise intolerance (syncope) in the patient and family history of sudden cardiac death in his elder brother at a young age is suggestive of hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (HCM). Mitral regurgitation with posteriorly directed regurgitant jet is usually seen on echocardiogram in HCM. ECG and echocardiogram are used to confirm the diagnosis of HCM.\n\nHCM is characterized by myofiber disarray in the heart, resulting in a hypertrophic myocardium. The hypertrophy leads to the following pathophysiological changes in HCM:\n\n• Diastolic dysfunction - presenting as exercise intolerance\n\n• Systolic anterior motion (SAM) of the mitral valve\n\n• The hypertrophied muscles result in a narrow outflow tract\n\n• This leads to the production of a high-velocity jet pulling the mitral leaflets towards the septum posteriorly\n\n• SAM leads to mitral regurgitation\n\n• Dynamic left ventricular outflow tract (LVOT) obstruction\n\nEchocardiography features of HCM are as follows:\n\n• Left ventricular wall thickness >15 mm\n\n• Hypertrophied heart more confined to the interventricular septum resulting in banana-shaped ventricular cavity","t":"Cardiomyopathy And Myocarditis","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"While evaluating a young man for hypertrophic cardiomyopathy you hear a harsh and late peaking systolic ejection murmur at the lower left sternal border. On performing which of the following maneuvers, the intensity of this murmur is expected to decrease?","o":["Valsalva","Squatting","Standing posture","Exercise"],"a":1,"e":"Squatting increases the preload and afterload thereby decreasing the outflow obstruction and intensity of murmur in HCM.\n\nIn HCM left ventricular size is small, thus the approximation of the interventricular septum with the mitral valve increases. The following mechanisms further increase this approximation and increase blood outflow obstruction and intensity of murmur:\n\n• A decrease in left ventricular cavity volume or pressure (decreased preload)\n\n• A decrease in systemic vascular resistance and/or arterial pressure (decreased afterload)\n\n• Increase in left ventricular contractility\n\nThe image below shows a cross-section of a heart with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy.\n\nParameters\n Murmur intensity increased\nMurmur intensity decreased\n\nPreload\n\nValsalva\n\nStanding\n\n(↓preload = ↑obstruction)\n\nSquatting\n\nPassive leg raising\n\n(↑preload = ↓obstruction)\n\nForce of contraction\n\nExercise\n\n(↑force = ↑obstruction)\n\nAfterload\n\nVasodilators\n\n(↓afterload = ↑obstruction)\n\nIsotonic handgrip\n\nSquatting\n\n(↑afterload = ↓obstruction)\n\nNote: All murmurs increase in intensity on squatting except that of HOCM and MVP.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/703f2e61cc0f42d89fa1d70ed7380ab4x1280x2419.JPEG"],"t":"Cardiomyopathy And Myocarditis","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A 20-year-old patient has been diagnosed with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy. Which of the following is not a major risk factor for sudden death in this condition?","o":["Spontaneous non sustained ventricular tachycardia","Spontaneous sustained ventricular tachycardia","Family history of sudden cardiac death","Atrial fibrillation"],"a":3,"e":"Atrial fibrillation in HCM is not associated with increased HCM-related mortality. It frequently accounts for unexpected hospital admissions, lost productivity, and impaired quality of life.\n\nAmong HCM patients, the greatest risk for sudden cardiac death is seen in patients who have sustained ventricular tachycardia (VT). These patients must undergo placement of an implantable cardioverter-defibrillator (ICD) for secondary prevention.","t":"Cardiomyopathy And Myocarditis","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A 17-year-old boy presents with complaints of syncope. ECG is suggestive of LV hypertrophy. A doppler echocardiography revealed abnormal systolic anterior leaflet motion of the mitral valve. Which of the following drugs should be avoided in this patient?","o":["Digoxin","Beta blockers","Verapamil","Disopyramide"],"a":0,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of hypertrophic cardiomyopathy. Digoxin should be avoided as it may increase contractility and worsen obstruction.\n\nDrugs that can lead to possible aggravation of outflow tract obstruction and thus contraindicated in HCM are:\n\n• Digoxin\n\n• Inotropes\n\n• Nitrates\n\n• ACE inhibitors\n\n• Dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers (eg- nifedipine, amlodipine)\n\nBeta-blockers and verapamil are first-line agents in the treatment of HCM. Disopyramide and amiodarone are the preferred antiarrhythmic agents.","t":"Cardiomyopathy And Myocarditis","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Which is the most common sustained arrhythmia in hypertrophic cardiomyopathy?","o":["Atrial fibrillation","Atrial flutter","Multifocal atrial tachycardia","Ventricular tachycardia"],"a":0,"e":"Symptomatic atrial fibrillation (AF) is the most common sustained arrhythmia in hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (HCM). It frequently accounts for unexpected hospital admissions, lost productivity, and impaired quality of life.\n\nTreatment of atrial fibrillation in HCM:\n\n• Amiodarone - drug of choice\n\n• Disopyramide\n\n• Radiofrequency ablation for medically refractory cases\n\n• Anticoagulation to prevent embolic events\n\nNote: A sustained arrhythmia is one that lasts for >30 seconds.","t":"Cardiomyopathy And Myocarditis","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"You are counseling the parents of a 15-year-old boy who was recently diagnosed with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy. What is the recommendation regarding his participation in sports?","o":["Participation in competitive sports is not recommended","Only non-contact competitive sports are allowed","Non-contact competitive sports with beta-blocker therapy is allowed","High-intensity exercise is acceptable with verapamil therapy"],"a":0,"e":"Participation in competitive sports is not recommended for patients with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy.\n\nThe following general measures are advised for patients with HCM:\n\n• Patients are advised against engaging in competitive athletics or vigorous physical activities\n\n• Maintaining low to moderate levels of exercise as a part of a healthy lifestyle has been proven to be beneficial\n\n• Adequate hydration at all times\n\nNote: Non-contact sports are those sports wherein the participants are physically separated with almost no physical contact (ex: tennis, badminton) between them. Contact sports involve some form of physical contact between the participants (ex: wrestling, boxing)","t":"Cardiomyopathy And Myocarditis","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A 65-year-old Japanese woman was brought to the emergency department with worsening angina and palpitations. Her physical examination was normal. ECG showed giant T wave inversions and coronary angiography was normal. Left ventriculography showed spade-like deformity. What is the most probable diagnosis?","o":["Dilated cardiomyopathy","Restrictive cardiomyopathy","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Apical hypertrophic cardiomyopathy"],"a":3,"e":"The presence of giant T wave inversions and spade-like deformity on left ventriculography points to a diagnosis of apical hypertrophic cardiomyopathy.\n\nApical hypertrophic cardiomyopathy is also called Yamaguchi syndrome. It is characterized by hypertrophy of the myocardium, predominantly in the left ventricular apex. It was first described in the Japanese population and is uncommon in other populations.\n\nThe symptoms are milder and the risk of sudden cardiac death is lower than in the traditional septal form of HCM.\n\nThe image below shows the ace of spades appearance on left ventriculography:\n\nThe image below shows the ace of spades appearance on echocardiography with massive hypertrophy of the apex and relative sparing of the base:","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c416b7d96a8b4473a0f61b4338cc32cex510x494.GIF","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/461c32594a3844bc92a901bab8ae9608x510x513.GIF"],"t":"Cardiomyopathy And Myocarditis","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A 17-year-old Chinese boy presented with prolonged fatigue, shortness of breath, and swelling in the legs. His echocardiography was suggestive of dilated cardiomyopathy. Which of the following nutritional deficiencies can explain his condition?","o":["Molybdenum","Manganese","Zinc","Selenium"],"a":3,"e":"The patient is probably suffering from Keshan's disease, which is due to selenium deficiency. It is endemic dilated cardiomyopathy that affects children and women of childbearing age in some areas in China.\n\nThe nutritional deficiencies that can lead to cardiomyopathy are:\n\n• Thiamine\n\n• Selenium\n\n• Carnitine\n\n• Calcium\n\n• Phosphate\n\n• Magnesium","t":"Cardiomyopathy And Myocarditis","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Endomyocardial biopsy of a patient shows vacuolar degeneration and myofibrillar loss. Which of the following drugs may be responsible for this finding?","o":["Alcohol","Doxorubicin","Cyclophosphamide","Trastuzumab"],"a":1,"e":"Endomyocardial biopsy showing vacuolar degeneration and myofibrillar loss is seen in cardiotoxicity due to anthracyclines (doxorubicin).\n\nDifference between cardiotoxicity due to anthracyclins and trastuzumab:\n\nAnthracyclins\nTrastuzumab\n\nIrreversible\nReversible\n\nDose-dependent\nNot dose-dependent\n\nBiopsy shows myocyte destruction\nBiopsy does not show myocyte destruction","t":"Cardiomyopathy And Myocarditis","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"In which of the following patients, the risk of developing peripartum cardiomyopathy is less?","o":["A 26-year-old pregnant woman with an obstetric score of G2P1","A 39-year-old pregnant woman","A 22-year-old pregnant woman with pre-eclampsia","A primigravida with twin pregnancy"],"a":0,"e":"Low parity is not a risk factor for peripartum cardiomyopathy. Increased parity is a risk factor.\n\nPeripartum cardiomyopathy (PPCM) is a type of dilated cardiomyopathy. It develops during the last month of pregnancy or within the first 5 months following delivery. The risk factors are:\n\n• Increased maternal age\n\n• Increased parity\n\n• Twin pregnancy\n\n• Malnutrition\n\n• Use of tocolytic therapy for premature labor\n\n• Preeclampsia or toxemia of pregnancy","t":"Cardiomyopathy And Myocarditis","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A young male patient presents with progressive dyspnea and weakness. He gives a history of fever, myalgia, and sore throat one week ago. To further evaluate him as per Dallas criteria, which of the following tests need to be performed?","o":["ECG","Echocardiography","Endomyocardial biopsy","CT-angiography"],"a":2,"e":"The above clinical scenario is suggestive of infective myocarditis. The Dallas criteria used for the diagnosis of myocarditis is based on endomyocardial biopsy. It is used to define the disease.\n\nActive myocarditis is defined as an inflammatory infiltrate of the myocardium with necrosis and/or degeneration of adjacent myocytes not typical of the ischemic damage associated with coronary artery disease.\n\nBorderline myocarditis is the term used when the inflammatory infiltrate is too sparse or myocyte injury is not demonstrated.","t":"Cardiomyopathy And Myocarditis","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Which is the most common infective cause of myocarditis?","o":["Borrelia burgdorferi","Trypanosoma cruzi","Trichinella","Toxoplasma gondii"],"a":1,"e":"The most common infective cause of myocarditis is Trypanosoma cruzi causing Chagas disease.\n\nInfective myocarditis is most commonly associated with viruses (adenovirus, coxsackie B virus, HHV-6) and the protozoan Trypanosoma cruzi.\n\nThe most common noninfective cause of myocarditis is sarcoidosis.","t":"Cardiomyopathy And Myocarditis","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A 48-year-old man presents with tiredness and palpitations. He also complains of abdominal pain, increased frequency of urination at night, and unexplained weight gain. On examination, the pulse is irregular at 118 beats/min. Bilateral pitting pedal edema is present. ECG is suggestive of right axis deviation. Which of the following feature is unlikely to be present in this patient?","o":["Pulsatile liver","Raised right atrial pressure","Positive hepatojugular reflux","Increased PCWP"],"a":3,"e":"The above scenario is suggestive of right-sided heart failure. Increased PCWP is not a sign of right-sided heart failure. PCWP is an indirect measurement of the left atrial pressure. Hence, it is a sign of left-sided heart failure.\n\nThe signs of heart failure are:\n\nRight heart failure\nLeft heart failure\n\nDue to congestion in the systemic circulation\nDue to congestion in the lungs.\n\nSymptoms and signs:\n\n• Raised JVP\n\n• Congestive hepatomegaly\n\n• Positive hepatojugular reflux\n\n• Ascites\n\n• Bilateral pitting edema\n\n• Weight gain- due to edema\n\n• Fatigue- due to ↓pumping of blood to lungs leading to ↓oxygenation of the blood\n\nSymptoms and signs:\n\n• Dyspnea on exertion\n\n• Orthopnea\n\n• Paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea (characteristic of LVF)\n\n• Pulmonary rales\n\n• Increased PCWP\n\n• Fatigue- due to ↓cardiac output","t":"Heart Failure","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A patient who is being evaluated for heart failure undergoes echocardiography for assessment of ejection fraction (EF). When will you categorize his condition as heart failure with reduced ejection fraction based on the results?","o":["EF &lt; 30%","EF &lt; 40%","EF &lt; 50%","EF &lt; 60%"],"a":1,"e":"Heart failure with reduced ejection fraction is ejection fraction <40%.\n\nAccording to the European society of cardiology (ESC) heart failure guidelines 2016 -\n\n• Heart failure with reduced ejection fraction (HFrEF)- ejection fraction <40%\n\n• Heart failure with mid-range ejection Fraction (HFmrEF)- ejection fraction 40-50%\n\n• Heart failure with preserved ejection fraction (HFpEF)- ejection fraction >50%","t":"Heart Failure","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A 65-year-old male patient came with complaints of episodes of shortness of breath and coughing at night which awakens him from sleep. On examination, distended neck veins were observed and S3 was heard on auscultation. Which of the following biomarkers can be used for the diagnosis in this patient?","o":["Brain natriuretic peptide (BNP)","Atrial natriuretic peptide (ANP)","Endothelin-1 (ET-1)","Lipocalin"],"a":0,"e":"The given clinical scenario suggests a diagnosis of heart failure. The brain natriuretic peptide (BNP) can be used to confirm this.\n\nNatriuretic peptides are protein molecules that are secreted by the ventricular myocytes in response to volume or pressure overload. Ventricular musculature releases pre-proBNP which is cleaved into active BNP and inert NT-proBNP.\n\nBNP causes natriuresis, vasodilatation, and diuresis that leads to improved myocardial relaxation. These peptides also counteract the actions of the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system. Measurement of both BNP and inert NT-proBNP levels can be used in the diagnosis of heart failure and to determine the prognosis.","t":"Heart Failure","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Falsely low values of BNP can be seen in which of the following?","o":["Advanced age","Female patients","Obesity","Renal impairment"],"a":2,"e":"Falsely low values of BNP can be seen in obese patients.\n\nIt is also falsely low in:\n\n• Flash pulmonary edema\n\n• Heart failure due to- mitral regurgitation, mitral stenosis, constrictive pericarditis, restrictive cardiomyopathy\n\nIt falsely increases in:\n\n• Advanced age\n\n• Renal impairment\n\n• Female patients","t":"Heart Failure","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is a newer biomarker to assess the prognosis in a patient with heart failure?","o":["Soluble ST-2","Lipocalin","Endothelin-1 (ET-1)","Cystatin C"],"a":0,"e":"Soluble ST-2 is a newer biomarker that can be used for determining the prognosis of patients with heart failure. Other newer biomarkers used are galectin-3, GDF 15.\n\nOptions B and D: Lipocalin, also known as neutrophil gelatinase-associated lipocalin (NGAL) or siderocalin, cystatin C, and IL-18 are biomarkers of acute kidney injury (AKI).\n\nOption C: Endothelin-1 is a useful biomarker for early detection of bronchiolitis obliterans in lung transplant recipients.","t":"Heart Failure","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A patient is diagnosed with heart failure with an ejection fraction &lt; 40%. What is the treatment of choice in this patient after the euvolemic status has been achieved?","o":["ARBs + Diuretics","ACE inhibitors + Beta-blockers","CCBs + ARBs","Diuretics + CCBs"],"a":1,"e":"ACE inhibitors + beta-blockers are the treatment of choice in patients with heart failure with an ejection fraction of <40% after euvolemic status is achieved using diuretics.\n\nThe first-line drugs used in congestive heart failure with reduced ejection fraction are ACE inhibitors + diuretics. Once the euvolemic status is achieved, ACE inhibitors + beta-blockers are the drugs of choice.\n\nBeta-blockers that improve survival in heart failure with reduced ejection fraction are carvedilol, metoprolol, and bisoprolol.","t":"Heart Failure","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"The preferred drug for a patient with chronic symptomatic heart failure with reduced ejection fraction is:","o":["Bosentan","Sacubitril/Valsartan","Omapatrilat","Moxonidine"],"a":1,"e":"Sacubitril/Valsartan, an angiotensin receptor neprilysin inhibitor (ARNI), is used in patients with chronic symptomatic HFrEF to reduce morbidity and mortality (class I recommendation).\n\nSacubitril (neprilysin inhibitor) is approved in combination with valsartan (ARB), in a ratio of 1:1. The combination is approved for use in chronic heart failure.\n\nSacubitril is a prodrug that is activated to sacubitrilat. It inhibits the enzyme neprilysin (neutral endopeptidase), which is responsible for the degradation of atrial and brain natriuretic peptides (reduction of blood volume). Thus, it increases the levels of atrial and brain natriuretic peptides and lowers blood pressure.","t":"Heart Failure","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following drugs is a selective myosin activator?","o":["Istaroxime","Omecamtiv mecarbil","Carperitide","Milrinone"],"a":1,"e":"Omecamtiv mecarbil is the selective myosin activator. It activates cardiac myosin and prolongs systole without increasing oxygen consumption of the heart.","t":"Heart Failure","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following drugs used in heart failure is an inhibitor of I(f) current in the sinoatrial node ?","o":["Omapatrilat","Ivabradine","Nesiritide","Levosimendan"],"a":1,"e":"Ivabradine is an inhibitor of If current in the sinoatrial node. It reduces the heart rate without decreasing the force of contraction.\n\nIt is used in patients with chronic HFrEF with a sinus rhythm and a resting heart rate ≥70 bpm who are either on a maximum tolerated beta-blocker dose or have a contraindication for beta-blockers. The concurrent treatment should include ACE inhibitor ( or ARB ) and aldosterone receptor antagonist.\n\nOption A: Omapatrilat is a dual neprilysin inhibitor+ ACE inhibitor.\n\nOption C: Nesiritide belongs to the intravenous vasodilators class and is a recombinant human brain natriuretic peptide (BNP).\n\nOption D: Levosimendan belongs to the positive inotropes class and is a combined calcium sensitizer and PDE3 inhibitor.","t":"Heart Failure","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"In a patient with heart failure, which of the following is not used as an inotropic agent?","o":["Levosimendan","Milrinone","Omecamtiv Mecarbil","Nesiritide"],"a":3,"e":"Nesiritide is not used as an inotropic agent. It belongs to the intravenous vasodilator class and is a recombinant brain natriuretic peptide (BNP)\n\nOption A: Levosimendan belongs to the positive inotropes class and is a combined calcium sensitizer and PDE3 inhibitor.\n\nOption B: Milrinone belongs to the positive inotrope class and is a PDE-3/4 inhibitor.\n\nOption C: Omecamtiv mecarbil is a selective myosin activator that increases myocardial contractility without increasing the myocardial oxygen demand.","t":"Heart Failure","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following beta-blockers would you prefer to use in a patient with heart failure with reduced ejection fraction?","o":["Atenolol","Nebivolol","Esmolol","Bisoprolol"],"a":3,"e":"Bisoprolol is preferred for use in patients having heart failure with reduced ejection fraction (HFrEF).\n\nThe only beta-blockers that have been proved to improve the survival of patients with HFrEF or chronic HF are -\n\n• Bisoprolol\n\n• Metoprolol\n\n• Carvedilol\n\nNote: Nebivolol is not approved by FDA for the treatment of heart failure.","t":"Heart Failure","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following drugs does not reduce mortality in a patient with heart failure with reduced ejection fraction?","o":["Digoxin","Beta-blockers","ACE inhibitors","Hydralazine + Isosorbide dinitrate"],"a":0,"e":"Digoxin does not reduce mortality in patients with HFrEF.\n\nThe drugs which reduce mortality in patients with HFrEF are :\n\n• ACE inhibitors\n\n• Angiotensin receptor blockers (ARBs)\n\n• Angiotensin receptor neprilysin inhibitor (ARNI)\n\n• Beta-blockers\n\n• Aldosterone antagonists\n\n• Spironolactone\n\n• Eplerenone\n\n• Hydralazine+Nitrates","t":"Heart Failure","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A patient was brought to the emergency with dyspnea, cough, and chest pain. His blood pressure was 180/100 mmHg and SpO2 was 90%. Chest X-ray is shown below. Echocardiography revealed reduced LV ejection fraction (LVEF). Which of the following will you not use in the management of this patient?","o":["Digoxin","Morphine","Furosemide","Positive pressure ventilation"],"a":0,"e":"The above X-ray showing batwing appearance along with the given symptoms and reduced LVEF is suggestive of acute decompensated heart failure with pulmonary edema. Digoxin has no role in the management of this condition.\n\nThe management of acute cardiogenic pulmonary edema consists of:\n\n• Oxygen therapy and positive pressure ventilation\n\n• Reduction of preload:\n\n• Diuretics: Furosemide is the diuretic of choice\n\n• Nitrates: sublingual nitroglycerin is the first-line therapy. If pulmonary edema persists, IV nitroglycerin is administered.\n\n• Morphine: It is a transient venodilator that reduces preload, thereby reducing dyspnea and anxiety.\n\n• ACE inhibitors: They reduce both preload and afterload and are recommended for hypertensive patients.\n\n• Nesiritide: It is an IV recombinant BNP which is a potent vasodilator with diuretic properties.\n\n• Inotropic and inodilators:\n\n• Dopamine, dobutamine-inotropic agents.\n\n• Milrinone- stimulates myocardial contractility and promotes peripheral vasodilatation.\n\n• Intra-aortic balloon counterpulsation- or LV assist devices are used in cardiogenic shock and end-organ failure.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c24e386a910e45c4b62dd6d210fb536e.JPEG"],"t":"Heart Failure","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A patient presents with abrupt worsening of renal function leading to acute cardiac disorder. Which type of cardiorenal syndrome would this be?","o":["Type I","Type II","Type III","Type V"],"a":2,"e":"Type III cardiorenal syndrome is an abrupt worsening of renal function leading to acute cardiac disorder.\n\nCardiorenal Syndrome (CRS): A pathophysiologic disorder of the heart and kidneys whereby acute or chronic dysfunction in one organ may induce acute or chronic dysfunction in the other organ.\n\nTypes of Cardiorenal Syndromes\n\nType\n\nPrecipitating event\n\nResulting in\n\nCRS Type I\n\n(Acute Cardiorenal Syndrome)\n\nAbrupt worsening of\ncardiac function:\n\n• Acute cardiogenic\nshock\n\n• Decompensated\ncongestive heart failure\n\nAcute kidney injury\n\nCRS Type II\n\n(Chronic Cardiorenal Syndrome)\n\nChronic abnormalities in\ncardiac function:\n\n• Chronic congestive heart failure\n\nChronic kidney disease\n\nCRS Type III\n\n(Acute Renocardiac Syndrome)\n\nAcute renal dysfunction:\n\n• Renal ischemia or\nglomerulonephritis\n\nAcute cardiac disorder\n\nCRS Type IV\n\n(Chronic Renocardiac Syndrome)\n\nChronic kidney disease:\n\n• Glomerular disease\n\nDecreased cardiac\nfunction\n\nCardiac hypertrophy\n\nHigh risk of adverse\ncardiovascular events\n\nCRS Type V\n\n(Secondary Cardiorenal Syndrome)\n\nSystemic conditions:\n\n• Diabetes\n\n• Sepsis\n\nCardiac and renal dysfunction","t":"Heart Failure","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"What is the defined duration for the diagnosis of acute pericarditis?","o":["&lt;2 weeks","&lt;4 weeks","&lt;6 weeks","&lt;1 week"],"a":2,"e":"Clinically, acute pericarditis is of <6 weeks duration.\n\nPericarditis is the inflammation of the pericardial sac. This inflammation can lead to excessive secretion of the pericardial fluid leading to pericardial effusion. When the accumulation of fluid causes obstruction to the inflow of blood into the ventricles, it is called cardiac tamponade.\n\nClinically, pericarditis is classified as:\n\n• Acute - < 6 weeks\n\n• Subacute - 6 weeks to 6 months\n\n• Chronic - > 6 months","t":"Pericardial Diseases","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A 30-year-old man presented with low-grade fever and severe retrosternal chest pain for 1 week. The pain got exaggerated on lying down and relieved on sitting up. On auscultation of the precordium, a grating sound was audible. Which of the given statements is false regarding this auscultatory finding?","o":["Pathognomonic of acute pericarditis","A very high pitched sound","Best heard with the patient in left lateral position","Loudest at the lower left sternal border"],"a":2,"e":"In this patient, Low-grade fever, chest pain that got relieved on leaning forward, and characteristic auscultatory findings are suggestive of acute pericarditis. The grating sound heard on auscultation is a pericardial friction rub.\n\nFeatures of the pericardial rub:\n\n• Superficial scratchy/leathery/grating sound caused due to friction between the visceral and parietal pericardium\n\n• Best heard at end-expiration with the patient sitting and leaning forward\n\n• Very high-pitched sound best heard with the diaphragm of the stethoscope, at the lower left sternal border\n\n• Consists of three components corresponding to atrial systole, ventricular systole, and early ventricular diastole\n\n• Pathognomonic of acute pericarditis","t":"Pericardial Diseases","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A young man presents to the medicine OPD with dyspnea, cough, and severe chest pain for 1 week. Chest pain is pleuritic in nature. On examination, the apical impulse is not palpable and breath sounds are normal. Which of the following is likely to be seen in the ECG of this patient?","o":["ST-segment elevations with a convex upward configuration","PR-segment depressions early in the disease progression","Reciprocal ST-segment depressions in all corresponding leads","T-wave inversions developing concurrently with ST-segment elevations"],"a":1,"e":"The above scenario is suggestive of acute pericarditis. PR-segment depressions are seen early in acute pericarditis.\n\nThe ECG changes in acute pericarditis evolve through 4 stages -\n\n• Stage 1 - Generalized ST-segment elevation with upward concavity with reciprocal depressions only in aVR and V1 and PR segment depression.\n\n• Stage 2 - The ST segments return to normal\n\n• Stage 3 - Diffuse T-wave inversion\n\n• Stage 4 - ECG returns to normal in weeks or months\n\nECG in acute pericarditis vs acute MI:\n\nAcute pericarditis\n\nAcute MI\n\nDistribution of ST-segment elevations\n\nMore generalized\n\nLimited to anterior or inferior leads because of the localized area of infarct\n\nMorphology of ST-segment elevations\n\nConcave upwards\n\nConvex upwards\n\nReciprocal ST-segment depressions in the opposite leads\n\nNot present\n\nTypically present\n\nT-wave inversions\n\nOnly after ST-segment returns to baseline\n\nT wave inversions can be seen even before the ST-segment returns to baseline\n\nPR-segment\n\nDepressed (or elevated in lead aVR) in 75% to 80% of patients\n\nUsually not depressed (can be depressed only if concomitant atrial infarction is present).\n\nPathological Q-waves\n\nNot seen\n\nMay be seen\n\nCardiac biomarkers are grossly elevated in acute-MI, whereas in acute pericarditis the elevation if present is only mild and indicative of epi-myocarditis causing myocyte necrosis.\n\nThe ECG below shows acute pericarditis causing diffuse ST-segment elevations in leads I, II, aVF, and V2 to V6, with reciprocal ST depressions in lead aVR.\n\nThe image below shows ECG with ST-segment elevation in Leads II, III, and aVF for an inferior acute myocardial infarction (AMI).","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/db0b2df12dab4df3b27714c035ad8ad7x600x600.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6a9a7602309d4ab49a10346bb2522ce6x1280x689.JPEG"],"t":"Pericardial Diseases","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Auscultation of a patient with pleuritic chest pain revealed a patch of dullness with increased vocal fremitus beneath the angle of the left scapula. What is this sign called?","o":["Auenbrugger’s sign","Moschcowitz’s sign","Dressler’s sign","Ewart’s sign"],"a":3,"e":"A patch of dullness with increased vocal fremitus beneath the angle of the left scapula due to the compression of the base of the lung by the pericardial fluid is called Ewart’s sign. It is seen in pericardial effusion.\n\nAcute pericarditis is frequently associated with pericardial effusion.\n\nOption A: Auenbrugger’s sign is the bulging of the epigastrium in severe pericardial effusion.\n\nOption B: Moschcowitz’s sign is the abrupt transition in percussion from the lungs to the right heart border seen in pericardial effusion.\n\nOption C: Dressler’s sign is the dullness to percussion of the lower half of the sternum seen in pericardial effusion.","t":"Pericardial Diseases","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not a component of Beck’s triad?","o":["Pulsus paradoxus","Hypotension","Muffled heart sounds","Raised JVP"],"a":0,"e":"Pulsus paradoxus is not part of Beck’s triad. Pulsus paradoxus is seen in cardiac tamponade. However, it is not a part of Beck's triad.\n\nBeck’s triad is seen in cardiac tamponade and it includes:\n\n• Hypotension\n\n• Soft or absent heart sounds\n\n• Jugular venous distension\n\nCardiac tamponade is the accumulation of fluid in the pericardial space significant enough to cause an obstruction to the inflow of blood to the ventricles. This reduces cardiac output and arterial pressure causing hypotension.\n\nThis also causes a greater than the normal reduction in the systolic arterial blood pressure producing pulsus paradoxus. Pulsus paradoxus refers to the fall in systolic blood pressure >10 mmHg with inspiration.\n\nOther causes of pulsus paradoxus include:\n\n• Massive pulmonary embolism\n\n• Tension pneumothorax\n\n• Severe obstructive lung disease\n\n• Hemorrhagic shock\n\nThe echocardiography clip below shows the swinging heart sign, which is characteristic of cardiac tamponade.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1d5f18fba5084819a91ce8c67bebfeb8x379x339.GIF"],"t":"Pericardial Diseases","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"An RTA victim is brought with breathing difficulty. On examination, the following is noted. Which of the following JVP findings is most likely to be seen in this patient?\n\n• BP- 80/58mmHg\n\n• Pulse- feeble\n\n• JVP- elevated\n\n• Heart sounds- muffled","o":["Absent x and prominent y descent","Prominent x and absent y descent","Prominent x and y descents","Absent x and y descents"],"a":1,"e":"The given clinical scenario with hypotension, muffled heart sounds, and elevated JVP (Beck's triad) suggest a diagnosis of cardiac tamponade. The JVP finding most likely to be seen is prominent x descent and absent y descent.\n\nNote: JVP in constrictive pericarditis is characterized by prominent x and y descents.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5cc0b1cac4434af9b54964b55146d5c8x603x708.PNG"],"t":"Pericardial Diseases","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A patient with dizziness and breathing difficulty came to the emergency department. The ECG is given below. What is the most probable diagnosis?","o":["Acute pericarditis","Constrictive pericarditis","Cardiac tamponade","Restrictive cardiomyopathy"],"a":2,"e":"The ECG shows electrical alternans with sinus tachycardia. This along with the patient's symptoms suggests a likely diagnosis of cardiac tamponade.\n\nElectrical alternans is the beat-to-beat alternation in the ECG waveform. In cardiac tamponade, this is caused by the periodic swinging motion of the heart in a large pericardial effusion.\n\nFeature\nCardiac tamponade\nConstrictive pericarditis\nRestrictive cardiomyopathy\n\n Pulsus paradoxus\n+++\n+\n+/- (Rare)\n\nJVP\n\nProminent x descent\n\n+++\n+\n+++\n\nProminent y descent\n-\n++\n+\n\nKussmaul's sign\n-\n+\n+/- (Rare)\n\nPericardial knock\n-\n++\n-\n\nThird heart sound\n-\n-\n+\n\nElectrical alternans\n++\n-\n-\n\nThickened and calcified pericardium\n-\n+++\n-","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/977add71bc7e47329d0737fde52a71dd.JPEG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1c6c060940e3403eb77355813df0c89ax1280x1090.JPEG"],"t":"Pericardial Diseases","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A 25-year-old road traffic accident victim presents with chest pain and breathlessness. On examination, his BP was 80/60mmHg.On inspection, his neck veins were distended and on auscultation, his heart sounds were muffled. Which of the following clinical features is likely to be seen in this patient?","o":["Pericardial knock","Kussmaul's sign","Third heart sound","Pulsus paradoxus"],"a":3,"e":"The symptoms and signs suggest a diagnosis of cardiac tamponade. Out of the given features, pulsus paradoxus is seen in cardiac tamponade.\n\nFeature\nCardiac tamponade\nConstrictive pericarditis\nRestrictive cardiomyopathy\n\n Pulsus paradoxus\n+++\n+\n+/- (Rare)\n\nJVP\n\nProminent x descent\n\n+++\n++\n+++\n\nProminent y descent\n-\n++\n+\n\nKussmaul's sign\n-\n+++\n+/- (Rare)\n\nPericardial knock\n-\n++\n-\n\nThird heart sound\n-\n-\n+\n\nElectrical alternans\n++\n-\n-\n\nThickened and calcified pericardium\n-\n+++\n-","t":"Pericardial Diseases","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A young motorist sustained injuries in a road traffic accident. He was found to have fractures of multiple ribs anteriorly on both sides. Radial and femoral fractures were also present. On examination, the following was noted. What should be the immediate intervention?\n\n• Blood pressure- 80/60 mmHg\n\n• Heart rate- 140/min\n\n• Respiratory rate- tachypnea\n\n• General state: agitated and restless\n\n• Jugular veins- distended\n\n• Respiratory system- B/L equal and adequate air entry\n\n• CVS- heart sounds barely audible. Femoral pulse weakly palpable. Distal pulses not felt","o":["Rapid blood transfusion","Urgent pericardial tap","Intercostal tube drainage on both sides","Immediate repair of femoral artery"],"a":1,"e":"The presence of hypotension, distended jugular veins, and absence of heart sounds (Beck's triad) are suggestive of cardiac tamponade. An urgent pericardial tap needs to be done to relieve the tension on the heart chambers.\n\nAlthough these features could be seen in tension pneumothorax as well, the presence of adequate bilateral air entry excludes this diagnosis.\n\nPericardiocentesis, also known as pericardial tap, can be performed with sub-xiphoid, parasternal, or apical approaches. The most common approach is a sub-xiphoid approach under echocardiographic guidance.\n\nOption A: Rapid blood transfusion is needed in massive blood loss indicated by hypotension. Although this patient presents with hypotension, it is most likely a result of cardiac tamponade. Although blood transfusion is indicated, it will be effective only when the pericardial tap decompresses the pericardium.\n\nOption C: Intercostal tube drainage is done in tension pneumothorax. The presence of adequate air entry bilaterally excludes this diagnosis.\n\nOption D: The fixation of femur fracture and femoral artery is not a priority here. The inability to palpate pulses distal to the fracture could be a result of hypotension. This needs to be explored only if the patient fails to improve after the pericardial tap and blood transfusion.","t":"Pericardial Diseases","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"You're an intern examining a patient in the ICU. While assessing the JVP, you notice a lack of fall during inspiration. Which of the following conditions would you not consider as a differential diagnosis?","o":["Restrictive cardiomyopathy","Cardiac tamponade","Constrictive pericarditis","Right ventricular infarct"],"a":1,"e":"A rise or a lack of fall in JVP with inspiration is known as Kussmaul's sign. It is not seen in cardiac tamponade.\n\nNormally, in inspiration due to the negative intrathoracic pressure and increased intrathoracic volume, the capacity of the right heart increases and the JVP falls. In conditions where the myocardium or pericardium is poorly compliant, it does not allow the right heart chambers to accommodate more blood and this fall is not seen. In fact, due to an increase in venous return during inspiration, the JVP might rise. This is called Kussmaul's sign.\n\nThis is seen in:\n\n• Restrictive cardiomyopathy\n\n• Constrictive pericarditis\n\n• Massive pulmonary embolism\n\n• Right ventricular infarcts\n\n• Advanced cases of left ventricular systolic failure\n\nKussmaul’s sign is not seen in patients with cardiac tamponade because even though the increase in pericardial pressure exerts an inward force compressing the entire heart during inspiration, the increase in negative intrathoracic pressure is still transmitted to the right side of the heart. This allows for an increase in blood flow to the right atrium. Therefore, the JVP falls as seen in normal conditions.\n\nFeature\nCardiac tamponade\nConstrictive pericarditis\nRestrictive cardiomyopathy\n\n Pulsus paradoxus\n+++\n+\n+/- (Rare)\n\nJVP\n\nProminent x descent\n\n+++\n++\n+++\n\nProminent y descent\n-\n++\n+\n\nKussmaul's sign\n-\n+++\n +/- (Rare)\n\nPericardial knock\n-\n++\n-\n\nThird heart sound\n-\n-\n+\n\nElectrical alternans\n++\n-\n-\n\nThickened and calcified pericardium\n-\n+++\n-","t":"Pericardial Diseases","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A patient with a history of frequent exacerbations of COPD is undergoing right heart catheterization for evaluation of pulmonary artery pressure. What is the mean pulmonary arterial pressure above which the diagnosis of pulmonary hypertension can be made?","o":["&gt;20 mmHg","&gt;25 mmHg","&gt;32 mmHg","&gt; 30 mmHg"],"a":0,"e":"Pulmonary hypertension is a pathological hemodynamic condition characterized by a mean pulmonary arterial pressure (mPAP) >20mmHg assessed by right heart catheterization.\n\nPulmonary hypertension could be a complication of cardiac or pulmonary disease or a primary disorder of small pulmonary arteries. Right heart catheterization is the gold standard investigation in diagnosing pulmonary hypertension.\n\nNote: The threshold for diagnosing pulmonary hypertension has been revised from >25 mmHg to >20 mmHg to enable earlier detection.","t":"Pulmonary Hypertension","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following genes is the most commonly implicated in hereditary pulmonary arterial hypertension?","o":["Endothelin","PAX – 11","Bone morphogenetic protein receptor 2","Homebox gene"],"a":2,"e":"Bone morphogenetic protein receptor 2 (BMPR2) mutation is the most commonly implicated gene in hereditary pulmonary arterial hypertension (PAH).\n\nThe BMPR2 receptor is involved in the regulation of growth, differentiation, and apoptosis of pulmonary artery endothelial and smooth muscle cells. Due to the mutation, there is enhanced proliferation and reduced apoptosis of vascular endothelial and smooth muscle cells leading to pulmonary hypertension.","t":"Pulmonary Hypertension","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Which among the following is not a feature of pulmonary arterial hypertension?","o":["Mean pulmonary arterial pressure &gt;20 mm Hg","Pulmonary capillary wedge pressure ≤ 25 mm Hg","Left atrial pressure ≤ 15 mm Hg","Pulmonary vascular resistance ≥ 3 Wood units"],"a":1,"e":"Pulmonary capillary wedge pressure (PCWP) ≤ 15 mm Hg is a feature of pulmonary arterial hypertension (PAH).\n\nThe table below shows the hemodynamic classification of pulmonary hypertension. PAH belongs to WHO group 1.\n\nParameters\n\nPre-capillary\n\nWHO groups 1,3,4,5\n\nPost-capillary\n\nWHO group 2\n\nCombined pre and post-capillary\n\nWHO groups 2,5\n\nmPAP (mmHg)\n>20\n>20\n>20\n\nPCWP (mmHg)\n≤15\n>15\n>15\n\nPVR (wood units)\n≥3\n<3\n≥3\n\nmPAP- Mean pulmonary artery pressure\n\nPCWP-Pulmonary capillary wedge pressure\n\nPVR- Pulmonary vascular resistance","t":"Pulmonary Hypertension","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"In a patient diagnosed with pulmonary hypertension, which of the following is an unlikely finding on a chest X-ray?","o":["Enlarged central pulmonary artery","Pruning of distal pulmonary vasculature","Right ventricular enlargement","Focal area of oligemia"],"a":3,"e":"A focal area of oligemia or Westermark's sign is seen in pulmonary embolism.\n\nThe signs seen on chest radiograph in pulmonary hypertension are:\n\n• Enlargement of central pulmonary arteries\n\n• Rapid tapering of vessels known as vascular pruning\n\n• Right ventricular enlargement\n\nThe ECG shows features of RV strain in patients with PH. The pulmonary function tests show a reduction in DLCO in PAH.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/5a3eaa1db3454c32a80dd6db20bf8c1bx1280x1298.JPEG"],"t":"Pulmonary Hypertension","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Echocardiography was done for a patient who presented with severe exertional dyspnea, chest pain, and occasional fainting spells. It revealed a mitral valve area of 1 sq cm, elevated LA pressure, and significant RV hypertrophy with TR. Which is the next best step in the evaluation of this patient?","o":["Cardiac MRI","CT pulmonary angiography","Right heart catheterization","Ventilation / perfusion scan"],"a":2,"e":"In the given scenario, a mitral valve area of 1 sq cm suggests severe mitral stenosis. This leads to elevated LA pressure and subsequently pulmonary hypertension. Due to pulmonary hypertension, there is compensatory RV hypertrophy (right ventricular-pulmonary hypertension coupling) and secondary TR. The next step should be to confirm pulmonary hypertension. Right heart catheterization is the gold standard test for diagnosing pulmonary hypertension.\n\nOption A: Cardiac MRI is considered the gold standard for assessing RV function. In the case of pulmonary hypertension, it is used for serial follow-ups due to its non-invasive nature.\n\nOptions B and D: CT angiography and ventilation/perfusion scan are done when pulmonary hypertension is suspected to be due to pulmonary embolism, coagulation disorders, or past history of thrombosis (chronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension) which should be followed by right heart catheterization.","t":"Pulmonary Hypertension","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A 32-year-old female patient presents with gradually worsening shortness of breath, fatigue, and exertional chest pain. On auscultation, a loud P2 is heard. Pulmonary function tests reveal an isolated reduction in DLCO. Her family history is positive for similar findings in other members and genetic testing has revealed a mutation in the BMPR2 gene. Based on suspicion, which of the following therapies is this patient most likely to benefit from?","o":["Bronchodilator","Vasodilator","Nitric oxide inhibitor","Immunosuppressants"],"a":1,"e":"The given clinical scenario suggests a diagnosis of pulmonary arterial hypertension. Vasodilator therapy would be beneficial in this patient. Endothelin receptor antagonists, PDE-5 inhibitors, and prostacyclins are examples of vasodilators.\n\nOption A: Since pulmonary arterial hypertension is a vascular disorder, bronchodilation is not beneficial.\n\nOption C: Nitric oxide is a vasodilator and inhibitors of nitric oxide would worsen the disease in pulmonary arterial hypertension.\n\nOption D: Immunosuppressants are not useful in pulmonary arterial hypertension.","t":"Pulmonary Hypertension","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following group of drugs can not be used in the management of a patient with pulmonary arterial hypertension?","o":["Phosphodiesterase type-5 inhibitors","Prostacyclin derivatives","ACE inhibitors","Endothelin receptor antagonists"],"a":2,"e":"ACE inhibitors are not used in pulmonary arterial hypertension.","t":"Pulmonary Hypertension","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Aortic aneurysms caused by spondyloarthropathies are most commonly seen in:","o":["Arch of aorta","Abdominal aorta","Ascending thoracic aorta","Descending thoracic aorta"],"a":2,"e":"Aortic aneurysms caused by spondyloarthropathies are most commonly seen in the ascending aorta.\n\nAn aneurysm is a pathologic dilation of a segment of a blood vessel. An aortic aneurysm occurs when the structural components of the aortic wall, that is, elastin and collagen, are damaged. This may occur either due to degradation or abnormal synthesis of these components.\n\nThe causes of aneurysm are as follows:\n\nEtiology\nMost common site\n\nDegenerative process-oxidative and biomechanical stress\n\n• Atherosclerosis\n\n• Aging\n\n• Cigarette smoking\n\n• Hypertension\n\nDescending thoracic aorta, abdominal aorta\n\nMedial degeneration/Cystic medial necrosis- degenerative changes in the tunica media of the aorta\n\n• Hypertension\n\n• Genetic syndromes like Marfan’s syndrome, Loeys-Dietz syndrome, etc.\n\nAscending aorta\n\nInfectious\n\nCauses\n\nSyphilitic aneurysms-periaortitis and mesoaortitis damage the elastic fibers\n\nAscending aorta/aortic arch\n\nTuberculous aneurysms-granulomatous destruction of the media\n\nThoracic aorta\n\nMycotic aneurysms- bacterial or fungal infections of the aorta\n\nAorta\n\nAortitis\n\nTakayasu’s arteritis & Giant cell arteritis (vasculitides)\nAortic arch, descending thoracic aorta\n\nSpondyloarthropathies-ankylosing spondylitis, rheumatoid arthritis, etc.\n\nAscending aorta\n\nTraumatic- chest trauma\n\nDescending thoracic aorta","t":"Diseases Of Aorta And Peripheral Arteries","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"What is the most common pathology associated with ascending aortic aneurysm?","o":["Medial degeneration","Vasculitis","Syphilis","Atherosclerosis"],"a":0,"e":"The most common pathology associated with ascending aortic aneurysm is medial degeneration.\n\nMedial degeneration (cystic medial necrosis) describes the degenerative process of the tunica media of the aorta. There is degeneration of collagen and elastic fibers in the media and loss of medial cells that are replaced by multiple clefts of mucoid material, such as proteoglycans.\n\nIt is seen in the following conditions and is associated with the corresponding mutations:\n\n• Marfan’s syndrome- fibrillin 1\n\n• Loeys-Dietz syndrome- TGFBR1, TGFBR2\n\n• Ehlers-Danlos syndrome type IV - type III procollagen\n\n• Familial thoracic aortic aneurysm syndromes- genes associated with actin and myosin eg. ACTA2, MHC11\n\n• Congenital bicuspid aortic valves\n\n• Turner’s syndrome\n\n• Hypertension","t":"Diseases Of Aorta And Peripheral Arteries","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A 40-year-old man who is a known case of Marfan's syndrome has come for his regular check-up. His chest X-ray is shown below. Following this, he is being taken up for echocardiography to evaluate the given finding further. Which of the following is true regarding the management of this patient?","o":["Operative repair if diameter is &gt; 5 cm","Operative repair if diameter is &gt; 6 cm","Operative repair if diameter is &gt; 5.5 cm","Operative repair if diameter is &gt; 4 cm"],"a":3,"e":"The X-ray shows a widening of the mediastinum with trachea displaced to the right and dilatation of the ascending thoracic aorta (red arrow) i.e. ascending thoracic aortic aneurysm. In the case of Marfan's syndrome, ascending aortic aneurysm of 4-5 cm is taken up for operative repair. This is because of a higher rate of expansion of the aneurysm in these patients.\n\nManagement of thoracic aortic aneurysm:\n\n• Beta-blockers and angiotensin receptor antagonists reduce the rate of expansion, especially in Marfan's syndrome.\n\n• Operative treatment is indicated in:\n\n• All symptomatic cases\n\n• Ascending aortic aneurysm - ≥ 5.5 cm in diameter or growth rate of >0.5 cm/year\n\n• Descending aortic aneurysm - > 6 cm in diameter or growth rate of >1 cm/year\n\n• Marfan's syndrome - 4-5 cm in diameter","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9380d05bdecf449f975b085ff2e7b092.PNG"],"t":"Diseases Of Aorta And Peripheral Arteries","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A 50-year-old man came with complaints of irregular bowel movements. While undergoing an abdominal ultrasound examination, an abdominal aortic aneurysm of 5.8 cm diameter was noted. There is a history of hyperlipidemia for the past 10 years. What is the next best step in the management of AAA?","o":["Medical management with beta blockers","Medical management with statins","Operative repair of the aneurysm","Follow up with serial abdominal ultrasound"],"a":2,"e":"Operative repair of the aneurysm is the next best step in management in this case. Operative repair is indicated for both symptomatic or asymptomatic abdominal aortic aneurysms if they are ≥ 5.5 cm in diameter.\n\nAbdominal aortic aneurysms are mostly asymptomatic and are detected as incidental findings on investigations for other complaints. Sometimes, they present with pain and pulsatile abdominal mass. Rupture of aneurysm presents with acute pain and hypotension.\n\nManagement of abdominal aortic aneurysms:\n\n• Statins- atherosclerosis is the most common pathology associated with abdominal aortic aneurysms. Hence, statins are prescribed to reduce the risk of cardiovascular events.\n\n• Asymptomatic aneurysms <5.5 cm in diameter- serial abdominal ultrasound to monitor the rate of expansion of the aneurysm.\n\n• Operative repair is indicated for:\n\n• Aneurysm of any size associated with symptoms\n\n• Rapidly growing aneurysms\n\n• Aneurysms ≥5.5 cm in diameter\n\nNote: Medical management with beta-blockers though helps in BP control, it is not effective in reducing the rate of growth of abdominal aortic aneurysms.\n\nThe given image shows an abdominal aortic aneurysm on CT:","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bc63864be699443db27a6ea30ce078f5x1007x844.JPEG"],"t":"Diseases Of Aorta And Peripheral Arteries","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A 55-year-old patient came to the OPD with a complaint of abdominal pain. On further investigations, he was found to have an abdominal aortic aneurysm. Which of the following investigations may indicate the etiology of the aneurysm?","o":["Abdominal radiography","Abdominal ultrasound","MRI","Blood cultures"],"a":3,"e":"Blood cultures may indicate the etiology of the aneurysm. They are often positive in patients with mycotic aneurysms.\n\nA mycotic aneurysm is a rare condition that develops as a result of staphylococcal, streptococcal, salmonella or other bacterial or fungal infections of the aorta. The term mycotic aneurysm is usually a misnomer as the causative pathogen is usually bacteria rather than fungi.\n\nThese aneurysms are usually saccular. These organisms may involve every major artery, but aortic involvement predominates.","t":"Diseases Of Aorta And Peripheral Arteries","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"All of the following are predisposing factors for aortic dissection, except:","o":["Takayasu's arteritis","Ehlers Danlos syndrome","Second trimester of pregnancy","Coarctation of aorta"],"a":2,"e":"The third trimester of pregnancy is a predisposing factor for aortic dissection, not second.\n\nPredisposition to aortic dissection increases when there are factors associated with:\n\n• Medial degeneration- seen in:\n\n• Syndromes affecting the connective tissue- Marfan's, Ehlers Danlos syndrome\n\n• Vasculitis- Takayasu's arteritis, giant cell arteritis\n\n• Increased stress on the aortic wall- seen in:\n\n• Hypertension\n\n• Third trimester of pregnancy\n\n• Other conditions with increased incidence include:\n\n• Bicuspid aortic valve\n\n• Coarctation of aorta\n\n• Aortic trauma\n\n• Cocaine use","t":"Diseases Of Aorta And Peripheral Arteries","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A 65-year-old man presents with acute pain in the chest which radiates to the interscapular region of the back. He is a known hypertensive for the past 10 years. His CT is shown below. Which of the following drug is contraindicated in this patient?","o":["Hydralazine","Esmolol","Labetalol","Verapamil"],"a":0,"e":"The given clinical scenario and the CT image showing double-lumen of the aorta are suggestive of aortic dissection. Isolated use of a direct vasodilator such as hydralazine is contraindicated in acute dissection.\n\nIt is because these agents can increase hydraulic shear and worsen the dissection by causing vasodilation without reducing the left ventricular ejection force that continues to weaken the arterial wall.\n\nMedical therapy for aortic dissection includes beta-blockers and sodium nitroprusside to reduce cardiac contractility and blood pressure. The heart rate is reduced to around 60 bpm and the SBP is lowered to ≤ 120mmHg.\n\nCCBs like verapamil and diltiazem may be used IV if nitroprusside or beta-blockers cannot be employed. Parenteral angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitor such as enalaprilat may be considered.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/04f3c05f113a44a3bf566233aa1b45c2.JPEG"],"t":"Diseases Of Aorta And Peripheral Arteries","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"The image depicting Stanford type A aortic dissection is?","o":["1","2","3","4"],"a":3,"e":"Image D depicts Stanford type A aortic dissection.\n\nThe classification schemes for aortic dissection are:\n\n• Stanford classification\n\n• Type A - dissection involves the ascending aorta with or without the involvement of other parts of the aorta\n\n• Type B - dissection is limited to the arch and/or descending aorta without the involvement of ascending aorta\n\n• DeBakey classification\n\n• Type I - dissection involves ascending to descending aorta\n\n• Type II - dissection is limited to the ascending aorta or transverse aorta without descending aorta\n\n• Type III - intimal tear is located in the descending aorta with distal propagation of the dissection\n\nHence, if the ascending aorta is involved, it is Stanford type A and DeBakey type I or II. If only the aortic arch or the descending aorta is involved, it is Stanford type B and DeBakey type III.\n\nFrom a management standpoint, the classification of aortic dissections into type A or B is more practical and useful, since DeBakey types I and II are managed in a similar manner.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2e4ad9dd43b147f19d9aac05eac88477.JPEG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9bbea561204143d7aa2f6c7353fdeadax1280x1582.JPEG"],"t":"Diseases Of Aorta And Peripheral Arteries","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A 50-year-old man presents with a history of severe pain and numbness in his calf muscles following morning walks. The symptoms subside on taking rest. On examination, thickened toenails, decreased pulses, and reduced temperature of the legs are noted. He has a history of smoking for the past 20 years. Which of the following is the most appropriate advice for this patient?","o":["Antiplatelet therapy with aspirin","Antiplatelet therapy and warfarin","Intravenous heparin","Percutaneous transluminal angioplasty"],"a":0,"e":"The given clinical scenario describes intermittent claudication. It is the most common symptom of peripheral arterial disease (PAD). The most appropriate advice for this patient is antiplatelet therapy with aspirin.\n\nPAD occurs due to atherosclerotic plaques causing stenosis and occlusion of blood flow. The symptoms of inadequate blood flow exacerbate during increased physical activity.\n\nThe following advice is given to patients with PAD:\n\n• Lifestyle modification- regular exercise, discontinue smoking\n\n• Statins to treat hypercholesterolemia\n\n• Reducing the risk of cardiovascular events:\n\n• Controlling hypertension- angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors, angiotensin receptor blockers, beta-blockers\n\n• Antiplatelet therapy- aspirin, clopidogrel\n\n• Revascularization procedures (catheter-based or surgical)- For patients not improving with medical therapy\n\n• Endovascular interventions- percutaneous transluminal balloon angioplasty (PTA), stent placement, atherectomy\n\n• Surgical- aortobifemoral bypass using Dacron grafts, axillofemoral bypass, femoro-femoral bypass\n\nAntiplatelet therapy and warfarin is not preferred because of the high risk of bleeding.\n\nIntravenous heparin is used to treat acute limb ischemia which most often occurs due to thromboembolic events. Acute limb ischemia presents with loss of pulses distal to the occlusion, cyanosis, or pallor. These symptoms do not subside on taking rest. Hence, this patient has intermittent claudication and not acute limb ischemia.","t":"Diseases Of Aorta And Peripheral Arteries","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following collagen vascular diseases has the strongest association with Raynaud’s phenomenon?","o":["Systemic lupus erythematosus","Systemic sclerosis","Rheumatoid arthritis","Dermatomyositis"],"a":1,"e":"Among the given collagen vascular diseases, systemic sclerosis has the strongest association with Raynaud’s phenomenon.\n\nRaynaud’s phenomenon occurs in:\n\n• 80–90% of patients with systemic sclerosis (scleroderma).\n\n• 20% of patients with systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE).\n\n• 30% of patients with dermatomyositis or polymyositis.","t":"Diseases Of Aorta And Peripheral Arteries","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A 45-year-old woman complains of bluish discoloration of her fingertips on exposure to cold. Which of the following drugs should be avoided in this patient?","o":["Clonidine","Prazosin","Diltiazem","Isradipine"],"a":0,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of Raynaud’s phenomenon. Clonidine is avoided in Raynaud’s phenomenon as it worsens the condition.\n\nClonidine acts on central alpha-2 adrenoreceptor in CNS and reduces sympathetic outflow. This causes decreased blood pressure and perfusion that can exacerbate Raynaud's phenomenon or any PVD.\n\nDrugs that can be used for the treatment of Raynaud’s phenomenon:\n\nPrimary Raynaud's:\n\n• Dihydropyridine CCBs- nifedipine, isradipine, felodipine, and amlodipine\n\n• Diltiazem\n\n• Prazosin, doxazosin, and terazosin\n\nSecondary Raynaud's:\n\n• Phosphodiesterase type 5 inhibitors- sildenafil and tadalafil\n\nDigital sympathectomy is helpful in some patients who are unresponsive to medical therapy.","t":"Diseases Of Aorta And Peripheral Arteries","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A patient was brought to the emergency with complaints of chest pain, hemoptysis, and severe dyspnea. CT scan showed vessel sign suggestive of pulmonary infarction. Which among the following is typically responsible for this?","o":["Small pulmonary embolism","Submassive pulmonary embolism","Massive pulmonary embolism","Saddle embolism"],"a":0,"e":"Pulmonary infarction as suggested by CT is typically due to a small pulmonary embolism.\n\nPulmonary infarction occurs when small thrombi lodge in the segmental and subsegmental vessels. These patients most often have pleuritic chest pain as the site where the thrombus lodges are near the innervation of pleural nerves.\n\nMassive pulmonary emboli and saddle emboli that occlude the main pulmonary artery cause hypotension and shock as opposed to infarction. These are caused by larger-sized emboli that lodge more proximally in the pulmonary circulation.\n\nPulmonary embolism is classified based on the degree of reduction of pulmonary vascular blood flow and associated hemodynamic disturbances:\n\n• Massive: Hypotension lasting for >15 minutes or BP maintained with inotropes.\n\n• Submassive: No hypotension, but features of cardiac injury present.\n\n• Non-massive: No hypotension or features of cardiac injury.\n\nCT findings associated with pulmonary infarction include a feeding vessel or vessel sign as shown below. It depicts a distinct vessel feeding directly to a lesion, usually of hematogenous origin. However, it is not specific for pulmonary infarction.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/aebc617bd39743029953f67c33f9c404x1280x1278.JPEG"],"t":"Dvt And Pulmonary Embolism","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A patient with a suspected pulmonary embolism underwent a chest X-ray. It showed an enlarged right descending pulmonary artery. What is this sign called?","o":["Westermark’s sign","Hampton’s hump","Palla’s sign","Black pleura sign"],"a":2,"e":"Enlarged right descending pulmonary artery on a chest X-ray in pulmonary embolism is called Palla’s sign.\n\nThe black pleura sign is seen in alveolar pulmonary microlithiasis. It refers to the shadow of the pleura between the extensive alveolar calcification and the adjacent rib cage.","t":"Dvt And Pulmonary Embolism","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Echocardiography in a patient with pulmonary embolism revealed akinesia of the right ventricular free wall with normal motion at the right ventricular apex. What is this sign called?","o":["Moses’ sign","McConnell’s sign","Homan’s sign","Lowenberg’s sign"],"a":1,"e":"The sign described is called McConnell’s sign.\n\nIt demonstrates right ventricular dysfunction as akinesia of the right ventricular free wall with normal motion at the right ventricular apex. It is the best-known indirect sign of pulmonary embolism on transthoracic echocardiography.\n\nMoses' (Bancroft's), Homan’s, and Lowenberg’s signs are seen in venous thrombosis. They're not specific to pulmonary embolism.","t":"Dvt And Pulmonary Embolism","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following biomarker is least likely to be elevated in a patient with pulmonary embolism?","o":["Troponins","BNP","Heart-type fatty acid–binding protein","NGAL"],"a":3,"e":"NGAL (Neutrophil gelatinase-associated lipocalin) is a kidney injury marker and not a cardiac injury marker.\n\nThe following are biomarkers of cardiac injury in pulmonary embolism:\n\n• Plasma brain natriuretic peptide (BNP)\n\n• Heart-type fatty acid binding protein\n\n• Troponins.\n\nSerum troponin and plasma heart-type fatty acid-binding protein levels increase because of right ventricular microinfarction. Myocardial stretch causes the release of brain natriuretic peptide (BNP) or NT-proBNP.","t":"Dvt And Pulmonary Embolism","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following conditions is likely to show increased D-dimer values?\n\n1. Myocardial infarction\n\n2. Pneumonia\n\n3. Pregnancy\n\n4. Sepsis","o":["1,2","1,3,4","2,3","1,2,3,4"],"a":3,"e":"All of the conditions mentioned above have raised D-dimer levels.\n\nD-dimer is a fibrin degradation product.\n\nPlasma D-dimer levels commonly rise in DVT and pulmonary embolism due to the breakdown of fibrin by plasmin. D-dimer is more sensitive to pulmonary embolism than DVT. The sensitivity of D-dimer levels in the diagnosis of pulmonary embolism is > 95% but it is is not specific for pulmonary embolism.\n\nNormal D-dimer rules out pulmonary embolism in low and intermediate-risk patients. Patients with a high risk of PE should undergo a CT pulmonary angiogram to effectively rule out PE.\n\nConditions which have raised D-dimer levels include:\n\n• Myocardial infarction\n\n• Pneumonia\n\n• Sepsis\n\n• Cancer\n\n• Postoperative state\n\n• Pregnancy- second and third trimester","t":"Dvt And Pulmonary Embolism","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A middle-aged patient presents to the emergency department with a sudden onset pain in the right leg. The pain is associated with swelling, redness, and tenderness. Which of the following is false regarding the management of this condition?","o":["Recurrence of this condition despite intensive anticoagulation is an indication for IVC filter","IVC filters can prevent recurrent PE in patients of right heart failure","IVC filters reduce the rate of recurrence of this condition","Pulmonary thromboendarterectomy is done in patients with chronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension"],"a":2,"e":"The above scenario is suggestive of deep vein thrombosis (DVT). IVC filters increase the rate of recurrence of DVT (by providing a nidus for clot formation) even though they usually prevent pulmonary embolism (over the short term).\n\nThe two principal indications for insertion of an IVC filter are:\n\n1. Active bleeding (prevents the use of anticoagulation)\n\n2. Recurrent venous thrombosis despite intensive anticoagulation\n\nPrevention of recurrent PE in patients with right heart failure who are not candidates for fibrinolysis and prophylaxis of extremely high-risk patients are some other indications for filter placement.\n\nPatients impaired by dyspnea due to chronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension should be considered for pulmonary thromboendarterectomy.","t":"Dvt And Pulmonary Embolism","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A resident is evaluating a patient, who presented with acute onset of chest pain and dyspnea, for pulmonary embolism. Which of the following imaging test should be performed to confirm this diagnosis?","o":["Doppler echocardiography","CT pulmonary angiography","Ventilation perfusion scan of lungs","MR pulmonary angiography"],"a":1,"e":"CT pulmonary angiography should be performed as it is the principal imaging test for the diagnosis of pulmonary embolism (PE).\n\nOption A: Most patients with acute PE have normal echocardiograms. However, echocardiography is a very useful diagnostic tool for detecting conditions that may mimic PE, such as acute myocardial infarction, pericardial tamponade, and aortic dissection.\n\nOption C: Ventilation-perfusion (V/Q) scanning has become a second-line diagnostic test for PE, used mostly for patients who cannot tolerate intravenous contrast or in whom the CT results are inconclusive.\n\nOption D: MR pulmonary angiography may detect large proximal PE but is not reliable for smaller segmental and subsegmental PE. It is used when neither CT angiography nor ventilation-perfusion scan can be done.\n\nThe images below demonstrate a pulmonary embolus (marked by arrows). The image of the gross specimen shows saddle thromboembolism.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3deedeebeb6c408483be4651e755d530x1072x831.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/faa4e0ce5d3c4987a19a999a4fa1ec57x800x570.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3dccaa20c5184acdbff5d98c7db3369dx800x542.JPEG"],"t":"Dvt And Pulmonary Embolism","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A 50-year-old patient is diagnosed with deep vein thrombosis in his right limb. Which of the following parameters is a part of Well’s criteria that assess the clinical likelihood of pulmonary embolism in this patient?","o":["Dyspnea","Orthopnea","Chest pain","Hemoptysis"],"a":3,"e":"Hemoptysis is a parameter in Well’s criteria used to assess the likelihood of pulmonary embolism.","t":"Dvt And Pulmonary Embolism","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"In a patient with a Well's score of 1.5, which of the following should be the initial investigation?","o":["D-dimer levels","CT pulmonary angiography","V/Q lung scan","Doppler ultrasonography"],"a":0,"e":"A patient with Well's score of 1.5 has a low probability of pulmonary embolism. The initial investigation should be the measurement of D-dimer levels.\n\nIn a patient with a high probability of pulmonary embolism, the initial investigation is CT pulmonary angiography.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/27f85ad8831c4dd4ad111da9dd5b274ex1280x1408.JPEG"],"t":"Dvt And Pulmonary Embolism","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is an indication for fibrinolytic therapy in a patient diagnosed with massive pulmonary embolism?","o":["Persistent hypotension","RV dysfunction","RV enlargement on chest CT","Benign intracranial neoplasm"],"a":0,"e":"For fibrinolysis (thrombolytic therapy) in massive pulmonary embolism, the only widely accepted indication is persistent hypotension or shock. It is defined as SBP <90mmHg or a decrease in SBP by ≥40 mmHg from baseline for >15 minutes.\n\nAlteplase or recombinant tissue plasminogen activator (tPA), streptokinase, and urokinase are approved for the treatment of PE. tPA is the most commonly used fibrinolytic, but it is not superior to other agents.\n\nPulmonary embolism is classified based on the degree of reduction of pulmonary vascular blood flow and associated hemodynamic disturbances:\n\n• Massive: Hypotension lasting for >15 minutes or BP maintained with inotropes.\n\n• Submassive: No hypotension, but features of cardiac injury present.\n\n• Non-massive: No hypotension or features of cardiac injury.\n\nContraindications for thrombolysis:\n\n• Haemorrhagic stroke\n\n• Ischemic stroke in the last 6 months\n\n• CNS tumours\n\n• Major surgery/trauma in the last 3 weeks\n\n• Active bleeding\n\n• Bleeding diathesis.","t":"Dvt And Pulmonary Embolism","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A 32-year-old woman with SLE, secondary anti-phospholipid antibody syndrome develops pulmonary embolism. After admission, she is started on anticoagulants. For how long should the anticoagulation therapy be continued?","o":["12-24 months","3-5 years","6-12 months","Indefinitely"],"a":3,"e":"Duration of anticoagulation for DVT and venous thromboembolism secondary to antiphospholipid antibody syndrome (APLA) is lifelong.\n\nDuration of anticoagulation for DVT and venous thromboembolism:\n\nClinical Scenario\nDuration of Anticoagulation\n\nDVT isolated to an upper extremity or calf that has been provoked by surgery, trauma, estrogen, or an indwelling central venous catheter or pacemaker\n3 months\n\nProvoked proximal leg DVT or PE\n3 to 6 months\n\nFor patients with cancer and VTE\n\nPrescribe LMW heparin\n\nContinue anticoagulation indefinitely unless the patient is rendered cancer-free\n\nIdiopathic unprovoked VTE\nIndefinite\n\nAntiphospholipid antibody syndrome\nIndefinite","t":"Dvt And Pulmonary Embolism","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following drug was recently approved by FDA for the extended prophylaxis of venous thromboembolism in hospitalized medically ill patients?","o":["Unfractionated heparin","Apixaban","Enoxaparin","Betrixaban"],"a":3,"e":"Betrixaban is a new oral, once-daily factor Xa inhibitor approved by the United States Food and Drug Administration (FDA) for extended-duration prophylaxis of VTE in acute medically ill patients.\n\nIn patients with no obvious risk factors for VTE, early ambulation with or without mechanical methods (pneumatic compression stockings or venous foot pump) should be advised.\n\nIn patients with one risk factor for VTE with no risk of bleeding, LMWH is preferred. In presence of renal failure/cost issues, unfractionated heparin is used. In the case of heparin-induced thrombocytopenia, fondaparinux is preferred.\n\nIn patients with multiple risk factors, increasing the dose of anticoagulants or using a mechanical device is advised. For patients with risk factors and a high risk of bleeding or contraindication for anticoagulation, mechanical methods are recommended.","t":"Dvt And Pulmonary Embolism","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"What is May-Thurner syndrome?","o":["Compression of right iliac vein by right iliac artery","Compression of right iliac vein by left iliac artery","Compression of left iliac vein by right iliac artery","Compression of left iliac vein by left iliac artery"],"a":2,"e":"May-Thurner syndrome (MTS) is caused when the left iliac vein is compressed by the right iliac artery against the fifth lumbar vertebra. It is also called iliocaval venous compression syndrome, iliac vein compression syndrome, Cockett's syndrome, and venous spur.\n\nMost patients have a partial obstruction and are asymptomatic. It can however progress to symptomatic extensive deep vein thrombosis (DVT). It is a cause of recurrent DVT affecting the left leg.","t":"Dvt And Pulmonary Embolism","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"You are evaluating a patient with a cardio-respiratory compromise. Chest X-ray shows a focal area of oligemia. The ECG of the patient is shown below. Choose the false statement regarding this condition.","o":["Most common symptom is dyspnea","Most common sign is tachycardia","Most common ECG abnormality is sinus tachycardia","Most preferred preliminary investigation is D-dimer"],"a":1,"e":"The above scenario is suggestive of pulmonary embolism. ECG shows the S1Q3T3 sign (i.e, an S wave in lead I, a Q wave in lead III, and an inverted T wave in lead III) that is relatively specific but insensitive. The most common sign of pulmonary embolism is tachypnea.\n\nMost common symptom of pulmonary embolism is dyspnea. Most common ECG abnormality in pulmonary embolism is sinus tachycardia.Most common preliminary investigation used is D-dimer.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e0b61b3332b14758a20b2e04ef0862ed.JPEG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c65ce46408fd45f9815585362faea2c9x1280x1255.JPEG"],"t":"Dvt And Pulmonary Embolism","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A 45-year-old woman underwent bariatric surgery. On postoperative day 4, she developed pulmonary embolism as confirmed by a CT pulmonary angiography. Her blood pressure was 70/44 mmHg with poor urine output. IV fluids were administered with dobutamine after which the blood pressure increased to 82/54 mmHg. What is the most appropriate next step to manage this patient?","o":["Low molecular weight heparin","IVC filter","Surgical pulmonary embolectomy","Thrombolysis with tPA"],"a":2,"e":"Surgical pulmonary embolectomy is the most appropriate next step in the management of this patient.\n\nThis patient is suffering from a massive pulmonary embolism. In such cases, thrombolytic therapy is given once diagnosis is made using CT pulmonary angiography or bedside ECHO.\n\nBut, since this patient underwent a large open abdominal operation (bariatric surgery), thrombolysis is contraindicated due to the increased risk of life-threatening bleeding. Therefore, surgical embolectomy must be done. The main pulmonary arteries are incised, and fresh clots are removed.\n\nContraindications for thrombolysis:\n\n• Haemorrhagic stroke\n\n• Ischemic stroke in the last 6 months\n\n• CNS tumours\n\n• Major surgery/trauma in the last 3 weeks\n\n• Active bleeding\n\n• Bleeding diathesis.","t":"Dvt And Pulmonary Embolism","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A 42-year-old lady was admitted with unprovoked pulmonary embolism from left distal leg DVT. On admission, her coagulation studies were normal. She was started on unfractionated heparin with the first dose of warfarin on the same day.\n\nAfter 48 hours of starting warfarin: INR = 3.5\n\nHeparin was discontinued, and warfarin was continued at a reduced dose.\n\nOn day 4:\n\n• Extensive left leg proximal DVT progressed from the previous scan.\n\n• INR = 4.2\n\nHow will you manage her?","o":["Discontinue warfarin and start long term low molecular weight heparin","Reinstitute heparin and continue warfarin","Send HIT tests and start argatroban or lepirudin","Continue warfarin"],"a":1,"e":"This patient had suboptimal anticoagulation management. The best approach would be to reinstitute heparin and continue warfarin.\n\nThe patient had unprovoked PE from lower limb DVT. She was started on warfarin bridged with heparin initially.\n\nWarfarin & parenteral anticoagulation need to be overlapped for at least 5 days, independent of the INR achieved during these 5 days in patients with acute venous thromboembolism.\n\nWarfarin inhibits protein C, protein S (which is part of the fibrinolytic pathway) in addition to factors II, VII, IX, X. Protein C has a short half-life (4-6 hours) and thus decreases early. Since protein C is antithrombotic, decreased levels of protein C by warfarin renders the patient hypercoagulable during the first few days of treatment before factor II with a long half-life (36 hours) decreases and protects the patient from thrombosis.\n\nThus warfarin creates a prothrombotic state in the first 5 days putting the patient at risk of progression of thrombosis unless a parenteral anticoagulant is given overlapping warfarin for these days.","t":"Dvt And Pulmonary Embolism","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A 35-year-old lady is admitted with unprovoked pulmonary embolism. On admission, her PT was 13.6 seconds (normal 9.6-13.4 seconds) and aPTT was 62 seconds (normal 28.2-34.6 seconds). She has no history of bleeding. Thrombophilia work up was done and she was found to have a strong lupus anticoagulant. How will you manage the patient’s initial anticoagulation?","o":["Start intravenous unfractionated heparin with aPTT goal in the upper therapeutic range","Start intravenous unfractionated heparin and monitor with anti Xa tests","Start warfarin without heparin bridge because aPTT is already in the therapeutic range","Start intravenous argatroban and monitor by aPTT"],"a":1,"e":"This patient is most probably suffering from antiphospholipid antibody syndrome (APS) as her lupus anticoagulant is strongly positive. In such patients, the initial anticoagulation is with unfractionated heparin and is monitored by anti-Xa tests.\n\nDue to the presence of lupus anticoagulant, the lab measurement of aPTT will show erroneously prolonged aPTT. Therefore, even though there is an apparently prolonged aPTT, the patient is at increased risk of thrombosis and not bleeding, and hence patients with APS should be anticoagulated.\n\nAnti Xa tests are not influenced by APS. So, the patient can be monitored for the adequacy of anticoagulation with UFH by monitoring anti-Xa.\n\nOption A: The patient’s aPTT is already prolonged due to lupus anticoagulant, so we cannot monitor the patient with aPTT after starting unfractionated heparin.\n\nOption C: Warfarin in the first few days of therapy, causes a decrease in Protein C & S levels, causing a prothrombotic state. Hence, a heparin bridge is necessary for APS which is already a pro-thrombotic state.\n\nOption D: Argatroban is a direct thrombin inhibitor used in heparin-induced thrombocytopenia(HIT) to prevent thrombosis and also to increase platelet levels. Therapy is monitored with aPTT.\n\nNote: Lupus anticoagulant may function as an inhibitor in vitro by binding to the reagent phospholipid and blocking the phospholipid-dependent reactions of the intrinsic pathway. The net effect is a prolongation of the aPTT.","t":"Dvt And Pulmonary Embolism","c":"Cardiology"},
 {"q":"A 60-year-old man with hemiplegia is brought to the OPD for a follow-up visit. He suffered from a stroke 2 months ago. On examination, he was found to have slurred speech and a positive Babinski sign. Which other examination findings are expected to be seen in this patient?","o":["Predominant proximal muscles involvement","Hypotonia","Fasiculations","Exaggerated deep tendon reflexes"],"a":3,"e":"The given patient has slurred speech and a positive Babinski sign, which is suggestive of an upper motor neuron lesion (UMN lesion). Exaggerated deep tendon reflexes are a feature of UMN lesions.\n\nThe distal muscle groups are affected more than the proximal muscles and the axial muscles are spared (as the anterior corticospinal tracts supplying the axial muscles have bilateral representation) in UMN lesions. Axial muscles are involved when the lesion is severe or bilateral.\n\nHypertonia in the form of spasticity is seen in the upper motor neuron lesions. Fasciculations and hypotonia are features of lower motor neuron lesions.","t":"General Neurology, Brain Death, And Gcs","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements about hypertonia is false?","o":["Spasticity is velocity dependent","Rigidity is present throughout the range of motion","Spasticity predominantly affects the extensors of upper limb","Rigidity affects flexors and extensors equally"],"a":2,"e":"Spasticity predominantly affects the antigravity muscles - flexors in the upper limb and extensors in the lower limb.\n\nSpasticity\nRigidity\n\nSeen in lesions of pyramidal tracts\nSeen in lesions of the extrapyramidal tracts such as rubrospinal and vestibulospinal tracts\n\nVelocity dependent - More noticeable with fast movements\nPresent throughout range of motion without change in intensity\n\nClasp-knife phenomenon - sudden release after reaching maximum\nLead pipe or plastic stiffness\n\nPredominantly affects antigravity muscles\nAffects flexors and extensors equally","t":"General Neurology, Brain Death, And Gcs","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following features would you not expect to see in a patient with pseudobulbar palsy?","o":["Dysarthria","Dysphagia","Depressed jaw jerk","Dysphonia"],"a":2,"e":"Jaw jerk is brisk in pseudobulbar palsy.\n\nBilateral corticobulbar lesions produce pseudobulbar palsy. This condition is characterized by dysarthria, dysphagia, dysphonia, emotional lability, bilateral facial weakness, and brisk jaw jerk.\n\nDamage to lower motor neurons of the cranial nerves in the brainstem is called bulbar palsy. Depressed jaw jerk is seen in bulbar palsy.\n\nPseudobulbar palsy is analogous to UMN lesions exhibiting hyperreflexia. Bulbar palsy is analogous to LMN lesions exhibiting hyporeflexia.","t":"General Neurology, Brain Death, And Gcs","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Match the following pairs of reflexes and their root values:\n\nReflex\nRoot value\n\n1. Biceps\na) L1, L2\n\n2. Cremasteric\nb) S1, S2\n\n3. Knee\nc) L5-S2\n\n4. Plantar\nd) L3, L4\n\n5. Ankle\ne) C5, C6","o":["1-a, 2-b, 3-e, 4-c, 5-d","1-e, 2-a, 3-d, 4-c, 5-b","1-a, 2-e, 3-b, 4-c, 5-d","1-e, 2-c, 3-d, 4-a, 5-b"],"a":1,"e":"The correct match is:\n\nReflex\nRoot value\n\nBiceps\nC5, C6\n\nCremasteric\nL1, L2\n\nKnee\nL3, L4\n\nPlantar\nL5 - S2\n\nAnkle\nS1, S2","t":"General Neurology, Brain Death, And Gcs","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"All of the following drugs have to be stopped before a lumbar puncture, except:","o":["Abciximab","Eptifibatide","Aspirin","Rivaroxaban"],"a":2,"e":"Aspirin and other NSAIDs can be continued in a patient who is scheduled to undergo a lumbar puncture (LP). These drugs have not shown an increased risk of developing spinal hematomas. Hence an LP can be performed in patients receiving these drugs.\n\nOptions A and B: Abciximab and eptifibatide are Gp IIb/ IIIa inhibitors. Platelet aggregation remains abnormal following the discontinuation of these drugs. LP, spinal and epidural procedures must be avoided until the platelet function is normal.\n\nOption D: Rivaroxaban is an oral factor Xa inhibitor. LP should be avoided in patients receiving this drug due to the risk of developing epidural or spinal hematomas. Other factor Xa inhibitors, namely, fondaparinux and apixaban must also be held prior to LP, spinal and epidural procedures.","t":"General Neurology, Brain Death, And Gcs","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A pathologist received a blood-tinged CSF sample for evaluation. The sample was subjected to centrifugation and the following finding was observed. What is the least likely differential diagnosis?","o":["Traumatic tap","Subarachnoid hemorrhage","Liver disease","Elevated protein levels"],"a":0,"e":"The given image shows xanthochromatic CSF on centrifugation. This is unlikely with a traumatic tap. A traumatic tap has clear supernatant following CSF centrifugation.\n\nXanthochromia refers to the yellowish appearance of CSF. In a lumbar puncture, a traumatic tap can be confused with subarachnoid hemorrhage (SAH) as both show blood in the drawn CSF sample. In such situations, centrifugation of the sample distinguishes the two.\n\n• Clear supernatant following CSF centrifugation supports a traumatic tap.\n\n• Xanthochromatic supernatant suggests SAH.\n\nXanthochromatic supernatant may be present in the following conditions:\n\n• In Liver disease, due to elevated bilirubin levels.\n\n• When CSF protein concentration is elevated.\n\n• In subarachnoid hemorrhage (SAH), RBCs are present in the CSF long enough for them to undergo lysis. This releases hemoglobin which is converted to bilirubin, thus staining the CSF yellow.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d2c9d467b91449539d691a616a9692e3.JPEG"],"t":"General Neurology, Brain Death, And Gcs","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A 23-year-old woman presents with nausea, fever, and neck rigidity. A lumbar puncture is ordered. All of the following are effective strategies to prevent post-lumbar puncture headache in this patient, except:","o":["Use of atraumatic needle","Insertion of a needle with bevel pointed upwards","Replacement of stylet prior to removal of the needle","Use of 20 gauge needle"],"a":3,"e":"The use of a 22 gauge needle (or smaller) can reduce the incidence of post-dural headache after lumbar puncture.\n\nEffective strategies that reduce the incidence of PDPH include:\n\n• Use of small-diameter needle (22-gauge or smaller)\n\n• Use of atraumatic needle (Sprotte and others)\n\n• Replacement of stylet prior to removal of the needle\n\n• Insertion of a needle with bevel oriented in a cephalad to caudad direction (when using a stranded needle)\n\nIneffective strategies:\n\n• Bed rest (up to 4h) following LP\n\n• Supplemental fluids\n\n• Minimizing the volume of spinal fluid removed\n\n• Immediate mobilization following LP\n\nThe customary practice of remaining in a recumbent position post-LP is unnecessary. Studies have shown no benefit in terms of reducing the incidence of headaches.","t":"General Neurology, Brain Death, And Gcs","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A 60-year-old man presents to the OPD with sudden onset speech difficulty, tremors, and difficulty in walking. He is noted to have a wide stepping gait. He is a known hypertensive but is not adherent to treatment. Where is the lesion most likely to be seen on neuroimaging?","o":["Frontal lobe","Cerebellum","Basal ganglia","Internal capsule"],"a":1,"e":"The given clinical features are suggestive of cerebellar ataxia, hence the lesion is most likely to be seen in the cerebellum. Cerebellar ataxia can be caused due to an acute vascular event such as infarction or hemorrhage.\n\nWide stepping gait, a broad-based unstable gait is indicative of cerebellar dysfunction. Few other notable gait abnormalities are:\n\n• Short stepped gait- parkinsonism\n\n• Scissoring gait- spasticity i.e. UMN lesion\n\n• High-stepped, slapping gait- posterior column or peripheral nerve disease\n\n• Waddling gait- proximal muscle weakness\n\nA few of the cerebellar signs include:\n\n• Dysdiadokinesia / dysmetria\n\n• Ataxia\n\n• Nystagmus\n\n• Intention tremor\n\n• Speech - slurred or scanning\n\n• Hypotonia","t":"General Neurology, Brain Death, And Gcs","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A 50-year-old man is brought to the emergency ward following an acute episode of inability to move his lower limbs. Examination findings are given below. Which of the following investigations is most appropriate for this patient?\n\n• Lower Limbs:\n\n• Reduced power\n\n• Reduced tone\n\n• Absent reflexes\n\n• Negative Babinski reflex\n\n• Upper Limbs:\n\n• Normal power\n\n• Normal tone\n\n• 2+ Reflexes","o":["Brain MRI","Spinal MRI","Electromyography","Nerve conduction studies"],"a":1,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of acute paraparesis. This is caused most commonly by an intraspinal lesion. A negative Babinski reflex and flaccidity are suggestive of a lower motor neuron lesion. Hence, the most appropriate among the given investigations for acute paraparesis with LMN signs is a spinal MRI.\n\nSpinal causes of acute onset paraparesis include:\n\n• Injury to the spinal cord\n\n• Thrombosis of anterior vertebral artery\n\n• Hematomyelia\n\n• Prolapsed intervertebral disc","t":"General Neurology, Brain Death, And Gcs","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is the most common cause of autonomic storm?","o":["Botulinum toxin","Brain injury","Spinal cord injury","Guillain-Barré syndrome"],"a":1,"e":"Brain injury is the most common cause of autonomic storm and typically follows severe head trauma and postresuscitation anoxic-ischemic brain injury.\n\nAn autonomic storm is an acute state of sustained sympathetic surge which can cause alterations in BP and heart rate, body temperature, respiration, and sweating.\n\nOther causes include:\n\n• Spinal cord injury\n\n• Drugs and toxins (phenylpropanolamine, cocaine, amphetamines, and TCA's; tetanus; and less often, botulinum toxin)\n\n• Autonomic neuropathy\n\n• Chemodectomas (e.g., pheochromocytoma).\n\n• Guillain-Barré syndrome can produce a moderate autonomic storm.","t":"General Neurology, Brain Death, And Gcs","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"What is the minimum Glasgow coma scale-pupils score for a head injury?","o":["0","3","4","1"],"a":3,"e":"The GCS-P score allows a minimum score of 1.\n\nThe Glasgow coma scale – pupils score (GCS-P) was described in response to the wish for a single index combining the coma scale with pupillary reactivity as a reflection of brainstem function. Its possible values range from 1 to 15, reflecting an extended range of severity, and may be particularly useful in relating to prognosis.\n\nThe GCS-P is calculated by subtracting the pupil reactivity score (PRS) from the Glasgow coma scale (GCS) total score:\n\nGCS-P = GCS - PRS\n\nThe minimum score in GCS is 3.","t":"General Neurology, Brain Death, And Gcs","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A 32-year-old man is brought to the emergency department following a road traffic accident while driving a motorcycle. During the evaluation, the patient opens his eyes in response to voice and says inappropriate words. He is unable to move his right arm but extends his left arm when a painful stimulus is applied to his right arm. What is the GCS score of this patient?","o":["7","8","9","10"],"a":1,"e":"The GCS score of the patient described is 8 (E3 V3 M2).\n\nIn the case elaborated above, the score allotted is :\n\n• Eye-opening to speech—3\n\n• Inappropriate words—3\n\n• Extensor response of the upper limb in response to painful stimuli—2\n\nPatients scoring a GCS of 3 or 4 have an 85% chance of dying or remaining vegetative.\n\nHowever, scores >11 indicate only a 5–10% likelihood of death or vegetative state and an 85% chance of moderate disability or good recovery.","t":"General Neurology, Brain Death, And Gcs","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"You are evaluating a patient post a neurosurgical procedure. The patient opens her eyes in response to a loud voice, localizes pain, and is confused and disoriented. What is the GCS score of this patient?","o":["E3M5V4","E3M6V5","E4M4V5","E3M5V5"],"a":0,"e":"In this patient, GCS is E3M5V4\n\n• Eye-opening to loud voice - 3\n\n• Motor response localizes pain - 5\n\n• Verbal response is confused and disoriented - 4","t":"General Neurology, Brain Death, And Gcs","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A patient is brought to the emergency room after he was brutally beaten during a mugging incident. He is unresponsive even to painful stimuli and is apneic; however, he does have a pulse. Which of the following clinical findings would exclude a diagnosis of brain death?","o":["Absence of oculovestibular reflex","Constricted pupils","Invariant pulse rate","Positive deep tendon reflexes"],"a":1,"e":"Constricted pupils would rule out brain death.\n\nBrain death is the irreversible cessation of all cerebral function with the preservation of cardiac activity and maintenance of respiratory and somatic function by artificial means.\n\nIn brain death, the pupils are usually midsized or maybe dilated. However, constricted pupils are not seen.\n\nThe diagnosis of brain death should be confirmed with the following clinical findings:\n\n• Absent brain-originating motor response, including response to pain stimulus above the neck or other brain-originating movements (eg, seizures, decerebrate or decorticate posturing)\n\n• Unresponsiveness to any stimuli\n\n• Brainstem damage, as evidenced by dilated or mid-sized pupils without light reaction\n\n• Absent corneal, gag, and oculovestibular reflexes\n\n• Absent sucking or rooting reflexes\n\n• Absent cough with tracheal suctioning\n\n• Apnea, indicating medullary destruction\n\n• The heart rate should be invariant and does not accelerate to atropine","t":"General Neurology, Brain Death, And Gcs","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"The family of an ICU patient who is in a coma has given consent for organ donation. An apnea test is performed. Lack of respiratory effort at what arterial pCO2 level will aid in confirming brain death?","o":["40 mmHg","50 mmHg","60 mmHg","70 mmHg"],"a":2,"e":"To declare brain death, apnea is confirmed if no respiratory effort has been observed in the presence of arterial pCO2 levels of ≥60 mmHg (apnea test is positive).\n\nDestruction of the medulla leads to complete and irreversible apnea. Demonstration that apnea is due to medullary damage requires that the PCO2 be high enough to stimulate respiration during a test of spontaneous breathing.\n\nAn apnea test is conducted to confirm that there is no respiratory effort in the presence of a sufficiently elevated arterial PCO2. Apnea testing can be done safely by the use of preoxygenation with 100% oxygen prior to and following removal of the ventilator. CO2 tension increases ~0.3–0.4 kPa/min (2–3 mmHg/min) during apnea.","t":"General Neurology, Brain Death, And Gcs","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Clinical criteria for brain death include all except:","o":["Coma","Absent brain stem reflexes","Absent spinal cord reflexes","Absent motor activity"],"a":2,"e":"Clinical criteria for brain death does not include absent spinal cord reflexes. Deep tendon reflexes/spinal cord reflexes may be present in brain death as the spinal cord is intact.\n\nCriteria for brain death:\n\n• Coma - Deep, unresponsiveness to noxious stimuli\n\n• Absence of brainstem reflexes - Pupillary, oculocephalic, corneal, gag reflex, etc.\n\n• Apnea - Absence of spontaneous respiration at arterial pCO2 tension of at least 50–60mm Hg","t":"General Neurology, Brain Death, And Gcs","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A 30-year-old patient is undergoing EEG with his eyes open. What is the expected frequency of the waves being recorded?","o":["&lt; 4 Hz","4- 7 Hz","8- 13 Hz","&gt;13 Hz"],"a":3,"e":"Beta waves are seen on the EEG of an adult recorded with eyes open. The frequency of these waves on an EEG is >13 Hz.","t":"Seizures And Epilepsy","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A middle-aged obese man is being evaluated at the sleep clinic. During his EEG recording, he is awake with his eyes closed. Which of the following waves will be predominantly seen?","o":["Predominant alpha waves with intermixed beta activity","Predominant beta waves with intermixed alpha activity","Predominant theta waves with intermixed delta activity","Predominant delta waves with intermixed theta activity"],"a":0,"e":"In a normal adult awake patient lying quietly with his eyes closed, alpha waves are predominantly seen intermixed with a variable generalized beta activity.\n\nThis alpha rhythm is attenuated when the eyes are opened.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/169a115416014488a724910b26ea5376x800x349.PNG"],"t":"Seizures And Epilepsy","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not a type of generalized epilepsy?","o":["Childhood absence","Juvenile absence","Juvenile myoclonic","Jacksonian march"],"a":3,"e":"Jacksonian march is a feature seen in focal seizures, not generalized epilepsy.\n\nIn Jacksonian march, the abnormal motor movements begin in a very restricted region such as the fingers and gradually progress to include a larger portion of the extremity. It represents the spread of the seizure activity over a progressively larger region of the motor cortex.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d304f7ff93bb403eb7285a1fd078d330x1200x1368.JPEG"],"t":"Seizures And Epilepsy","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A 13-year-old boy presents with involuntary, sudden jerking movements of both upper limbs. They occur most frequently in the morning after awakening and are provoked by lack of sleep. This type of seizure is classified as ________.","o":["Unknown onset","Focal and generalized onset","Focal onset","Generalised onset"],"a":1,"e":"The given scenario is suggestive of juvenile myoclonic epilepsy (JME). Myoclonic seizures are the predominant feature of JME and are now classified under both focal onset and generalized onset seizures.\n\nGeneralized seizures arise at some point in the brain but immediately and rapidly engage neuronal networks in both cerebral hemispheres.\n\nFocal seizures arise from a neuronal network either discretely localized within one brain region or more broadly distributed but still within a cerebral hemisphere.\n\nMyoclonic seizures are characterized by:\n\n• Preserved consciousness\n\n• Preserved postural control\n\n• Lack of postictal confusion\n\nJME is an epilepsy syndrome that has familial inheritance and begins in adolescence. It most commonly occurs in the morning and is provoked by sleep deprivation. JME is usually associated with GTCS and sometimes with absence seizures. EEG shows a typical 4-6 Hz polyspike and wave pattern. Treatment includes administration of sodium valproate.\n\n2017 ILAE-International League Against Epilepsy Classification:","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8b20cc25a8f44d2980c78fd543cb7d44x1200x1368.JPEG"],"t":"Seizures And Epilepsy","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not seen in focal seizures?","o":["Jacksonian march","Todd's paralysis","Epilepsia partialis continua","Ictal cry"],"a":3,"e":"The ictal cry is seen in generalized, tonic-clonic seizures (GTCS). Jacksonian march, Todd's paralysis, and Epilepsia partialis continua are features of focal seizures.\n\nJacksonian march is a feature seen in focal seizures wherein the abnormal motor movements begin in a very restricted region such as the fingers and gradually progress to include a larger portion of the extremity. It represents the spread of seizure activity over a progressively larger region of the motor cortex.\n\nTodd’s paralysis is a transient focal temporary paralysis usually persisting for a few minutes to hours (maximum up to 48 hours) in the postictal phase.\n\nEpilepsia partialis continua refer to continued focal seizures for hours or days. It is often refractory to medical therapy.\n\nTonic contraction of the muscles of expiration and the larynx at the onset of a generalized tonic-clonic seizure will produce a loud moan or ictal cry.","t":"Seizures And Epilepsy","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A 10-year-old girl is brought to the OPD with complaints of frequent fainting and sudden falls while playing. She sometimes falls off while sitting in the classroom hitting her head to the desk. She regains consciousness immediately and resumes her activity, and has normal behavior after such episodes. The girl is otherwise healthy. What is the likely diagnosis?","o":["Typical absence seizures","Atonic seizures","Syncope","Atypical absence seizures"],"a":1,"e":"The given history is most consistent with atonic seizures. Atonic seizures are characterized by a sudden loss of postural control and there is no postictal confusion.\n\nOptions A and D: Absence seizures usually present as blank stares or unresponsiveness for a few seconds. Postural control is not lost during absence seizures.\n\nOption C: The girl regains consciousness immediately and has no associated complaints like sweating, fatigue, or tachycardia. She also resumes her activity after the episodes. Also, there is no precipitating factor that points towards syncope. Hence it is unlikely to be syncope.","t":"Seizures And Epilepsy","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A 6-year-old boy is brought to the hospital. He is found to have a sudden brief lapse of consciousness with preserved postural control. No confusion is reported after regaining consciousness. What is the diagnosis?","o":["Atonic seizures","Myoclonic seizures","Absence seizures","Generalized tonic clonic seizures"],"a":2,"e":"Sudden, brief lapse of consciousness with preserved postural control and no post-ictal confusion is seen in absence seizures. Usually, the seizure lasts only a few seconds.\n\nEEG shows generalized symmetric 3 Hz spike and wave pattern (as shown below). Hyperventilation provokes these EEG discharges and seizures.\n\nOption A: Atonic seizures are characterized by a sudden loss of postural control.\n\nOption B: Myoclonic seizures have preserved consciousness. They involve a sudden brief jerking movement involving the entire body or a part of it.\n\nOption D: Generalised tonic-clonic seizures are characterized by post-ictal confusion.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3bab740325924cb58492e3b1a4e8a822x1279x1242.JPEG"],"t":"Seizures And Epilepsy","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"An unconscious patient with a history of seizures is brought to the ED. He then develops muscular flaccidity and excessive salivation. After a few minutes, he begins to regain consciousness and is in a confused state. This phenomenon is associated with which of the following types of seizures?","o":["GTCS","Absence seizures","Atonic seizures","Myoclonic seizures"],"a":0,"e":"The scenario is suggestive of post-ictal confusion which is seen in generalized tonic-clonic seizures.\n\nIt is not seen in absence seizures, atonic seizures or myoclonic seizures.","t":"Seizures And Epilepsy","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A 15-year-old boy presents to the clinic with involuntary, sudden jerking movements of both upper limbs. They occur most frequently in the morning after awakening and are provoked by a lack of sleep. EEG demonstrates bilaterally synchronous spike-and-slow- wave discharges. Which of the following features is not seen in this type of seizure?","o":["Post-ictal paralysis","Preserved postural control","Lack of postictal confusion","Preserved consciousness"],"a":0,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of myoclonic seizures. Post-ictal paralysis is not seen in these seizures.\n\nMyoclonic seizures are characterized by preserved consciousness (rarely very short loss of consciousness), preserved postural control, Lack of postictal confusion.\n\nMyoclonic seizures usually coexist with other forms of generalized seizures but are the predominant feature of juvenile myoclonic epilepsy (JME). The EEG shows bilaterally synchronous spike-and-slow wave discharges.\n\nJME or Janz's syndrome is an epilepsy syndrome that has familial inheritance and begins in adolescence. It most commonly occurs in the morning and is provoked by sleep deprivation. JME is usually associated with GTCS and sometimes with absence seizures. EEG shows a typical 4-6 Hz polyspike and wave pattern. Treatment includes administration of sodium valproate.","t":"Seizures And Epilepsy","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"The following EEG is that of a 6-year-old girl who is easily distracted in class and exhibits poor scholastic performance. Seizures are precipitated by hyperventilation. What is the diagnosis ?","o":["Myoclonic seizures","Atonic seizures","Absence seizures","Clonic seizures"],"a":2,"e":"EEG shows generalized symmetric 3 Hz spike and wave pattern. This is seen in absence seizures.\n\nAbsence seizures are characterized by a sudden brief lapse of consciousness with preserved postural control that lasts only a few seconds. However, there is no post-ictal confusion.\n\nHyperventilation provokes these EEG discharges and also the seizures.\n\nEthosuximide is the drug of choice in typical absence seizures.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/fe1856fa68ca48789a3444be078c5a7c.PNG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b95a435b09ec4564987dbfbf992d6140x1280x1180.JPEG"],"t":"Seizures And Epilepsy","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"What is the first-line agent for treating a patient with generalized tonic-clonic seizures?","o":["Phenytoin","Carbamazepine","Ethosuximide","Valproate"],"a":3,"e":"The first-line agents in the treatment of generalized tonic-clonic seizures (GTCS) are Valproate, levetiracetam, and lamotrigine.","t":"Seizures And Epilepsy","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A 16-year-old boy developed abnormal jerking movements that started in his left hand and gradually involved the entire arm. Following this, the patient experienced weakness and difficulty in moving the left arm for about an hour. Two similar episodes have also occurred in the past. Which of the following is the first-line drug for this condition?","o":["Carbamazepine","Valproate","Ethosuximide","Topiramate"],"a":0,"e":"The given clinical scenario describes Jacksonian march followed by Todd’s palsy. These are characteristics of a focal seizure. Among the given drugs, the first-line drug for focal seizures is carbamazepine.","t":"Seizures And Epilepsy","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"All of the following can be used as first-line agents in patients with typical absence seizures, except:","o":["Levetiracetam","Ethosuximide","Lamotrigine","Valproate"],"a":0,"e":"Levetiracetam is not the first-line agent for the treatment of typical absence seizures.\n\nFirst-line drugs for:\n\n• Typical absence seizures: Ethosuximide (drug of choice), valproate, and lamotrigine\n\n• Atypical absence seizures: Valproate","t":"Seizures And Epilepsy","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is used as the first-line antiepileptic drug in the treatment of both generalized as well as focal seizures?","o":["Valproate","Carbamazepine","Lamotrigine","Phenytoin"],"a":2,"e":"Lamotrigine is a first-line drug in the treatment of both generalized tonic-clonic and focal seizures.","t":"Seizures And Epilepsy","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is the drug of choice for a patient with generalized epilepsy syndrome having mixed seizures?","o":["Ethosuximide","Carbamazepine","Phenytoin","Valproate"],"a":3,"e":"Valproate is the drug of choice in generalized epilepsy syndrome having mixed seizures. It is particularly effective in absence, myoclonic and atonic seizures.\n\nValproate is usually first given alone (monotherapy) and if ineffective, another drug such as lamotrigine may be added.\n\nIn generalized epilepsy, drugs such as phenytoin, carbamazepine, and oxcarbazepine can increase the number of seizures. They can also provoke new seizure types - myoclonic or absence seizures. Hence, they are avoided.\n\nEthosuximide is the drug of choice in typical absence seizures and is not used for GTCS.","t":"Seizures And Epilepsy","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A 34-year-old man is brought to the emergency department with violent shaking of arms and legs for the past 1 hour. He is a chronic alcoholic whose last intake was 2 days ago. IV lorazepam and phenytoin are administered to this patient. After 5 minutes, the seizures have not stopped. What is the next best step in the management of this patient?","o":["Phenobarbitone","Lorazepam","Propofol","Thiamine infusion"],"a":1,"e":"With seizures occurring for 1 hour, this patient is suffering from status epilepticus. A second dose of lorazepam can be given to control this patient's seizures if there is no response after 5 minutes of administration of the first dose.\n\nStatus epilepticus refers to continuous seizures or repetitive, discrete seizures with impaired consciousness in the inter-ictal period. For practical purposes, it is defined as any one of the following:\n\n• Continuing seizures >5 minutes or\n\n• No regaining of consciousness between 2 seizures.\n\nManagement of status epilepticus:","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b2c4d09f0aae43149167777293a4c3c6x1280x1788.JPEG"],"t":"Seizures And Epilepsy","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A 30-year-old man on antiepileptic therapy for GTCS presents with weight gain, alopecia and thrombocytopenia. Which of the following antiepileptic drugs is the most likely cause for this presentation?","o":["Phenytoin","Carbamazepine","Lamotrigine","Valproate"],"a":3,"e":"Weight gain, alopecia, and thrombocytopenia are the side effects of valproate use.\n\nAdverse effects of Sodium Valproate (mnemonic: VALPROATE)\n\n• Vomiting\n\n• Alopecia\n\n• Liver toxicity\n\n• Pancreatitis\n\n• Rash\n\n• Obesity\n\n• Anorexia\n\n• Teratogenicity (most common: spina bifida), thrombocytopenia, tremors\n\n• Edema\n\nThe first-line agents for treating GTCS are valproate, leviteracetam, and lamotrigine.","t":"Seizures And Epilepsy","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following features would you not expect to see in a patient with West syndrome?","o":["Infantile spasms","Abnormal EEG","Developmental regression","Café au lait"],"a":3,"e":"Café au lait is a neurocutaneous marker seen in neurofibromatosis, not a component of West syndrome.\n\nThe triad of West syndrome is:\n\n• Infantile spasms (also called Salaam spells)\n\n• Developmental regression\n\n• Hypsarrhythmia (abnormal EEG)\n\nWest syndrome is treated with:\n\n• ACTH injections\n\n• Vigabatrin if associated with tuberous sclerosis","t":"Seizures And Epilepsy","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A 4-month-old child was brought with repeated epileptic spasms. The child had reached his normal milestone at the age of 2 months. But the parents noticed that he lost the ability to hold his head steadily and he recently couldn't track light or sound. The EEG findings are shown below. What is the drug of choice for the treatment of this condition?","o":["ACTH","Vigabatrin","Gabapentin","Pregabalin"],"a":0,"e":"The given scenario is of infantile spasms, developmental regression and the EEG showing hypsarrhythmia is suggestive of West syndrome. The drug of choice for this condition is ACTH.\n\nWest syndrome is a rare type of epilepsy syndrome that begins in infancy. It is characterized by a triad of\n\n• Infantile spasm (also called salaam spells) is a convulsive disease characterized by brief, symmetric axial muscle contraction (neck, trunk, and/or extremities).\n\n• Developmental regression\n\n• Hypsarrhythmia (Himalayan wave pattern) on EEG: Characterized by a high-voltage, slow, chaotic background with multifocal spikes\n\nTreatment of West syndrome includes:\n\n• ACTH\n\n• Vigabatrin if associated with tuberous sclerosis","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d6d904bf59764876ac6c40c43d33cab9.PNG"],"t":"Seizures And Epilepsy","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following drugs is effective in controlling infantile spasms in a patient who has the neurocutaneous marker as shown in image?","o":["Prednisolone","ACTH","Vigabatrin","Gabapentin"],"a":2,"e":"The given image shows a Shagreen patch, which is a typical feature of tuberous sclerosis. Vigabatrin is effective in the control of seizures in patients with tuberous sclerosis.\n\nTuberous sclerosis (Bourneville disease) is an autosomal dominant disease caused by mutations in either the TSC1 or the TSC2 gene. The clinical features seen are as follows:\n\n• Seizures\n\n• Intellectual disability\n\n• Adenoma sebaceum (facial angiofibromas)\n\n• Shagreen patch\n\n• Hypomelanotic macules\n\n• Periungual fibromas\n\n• Renal angiomyolipomas\n\n• Cardiac rhabdomyomas.\n\nA Shagreen patch is an irregularly thickened, slightly elevated, soft, skin-colored plaque, usually in the lumbosacral region.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/67b3cdb78be5479e8b6cc6bd725f3921.JPEG"],"t":"Seizures And Epilepsy","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A 14-year-old boy develops an episode of involuntary jerky movements of the left hand accompanied by a repetitive chewing movement. He had a blank look on his face and was unresponsive during this episode. He also has no memory of the episode. EEG was normal and MRI showed hippocampal sclerosis. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Juvenile myoclonic epilepsy","Lennox-Gastaut syndrome","Gelastic epilepsy","Mesial temporal lobe epilepsy"],"a":3,"e":"The given clinical scenario of focal seizure with impaired consciousness which along with hippocampal sclerosis is suggestive of mesial temporal lobe epilepsy.\n\nThe following image shows left hippocampal sclerosis:","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/52adefba5adc43cdac6b7b288d8751b7x1280x687.JPEG"],"t":"Seizures And Epilepsy","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements is false regarding mesial temporal lobe epilepsy ?","o":["It results in focal seizures with dyscognitive features","The person experiences an aura before the seizure","It is associated with a family history of epilepsy","Seizures can be controlled with anticonvulsants such as carbamazepine"],"a":3,"e":"Seizures of mesial temporal lobe epilepsy are not controlled with carbamazepine. The condition is refractory to anticonvulsant therapy but responds well to surgical intervention.","t":"Seizures And Epilepsy","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following drugs must be avoided in myoclonic seizures?","o":["Valproate","Levatriacetam","Ethosuxamide","Oxcarbazepine"],"a":3,"e":"Oxcarbazepine is known to worsen myoclonic seizures and is therefore contraindicated in it.\n\nDrugs like carbamazepine, oxcarbazepine and phenytoin can worsen the myoclonic, absence, tonic and atonic seizures and are therefore avoided.","t":"Seizures And Epilepsy","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is seen in patients with Lennox-Gastaut syndrome?","o":["Atypical febrile seizures","Single type of seizures","EEG showing less than 3Hz Spike and wave discharge","Good prognosis with adequate control"],"a":2,"e":"EEG showing <3Hz spike and wave discharge is a characteristic feature of Lennox-Gastaut syndrome.\n\nLennox-Gastaut syndrome is a rare type of epilepsy syndrome that begins in childhood. It may occur due to various reasons such as developmental anomalies, mutations, trauma, infections, etc. It is characterized by a typical triad of\n\n• Multiple seizure types\n\n• Intellectual disability\n\n• Typical <3 Hz spike and wave pattern on EEG\n\nTreatment includes administration of anti-epileptic drugs such as lamotrigine and sodium valproate. It is frequently refractory to medical therapy and usually has a poor prognosis.\n\nNote: EEG with symmetric 3 Hz spike and wave pattern is seen in typical absence seizure.","t":"Seizures And Epilepsy","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is false regarding Lafora’s disease?","o":["Myoclonus is seen","Autosomal recessive","Diagnosis by DNA sequencing","Treatment with glucocerebrosidase infusions"],"a":3,"e":"Glucocerebrosidase infusions are not the treatment for Lafora's disease. There is no approved treatment for Lafora body disease.\n\nThe mainstay of management is anti-epileptic drugs, valproate in particular; however, other AEDs such as topiramate, ethosuximide, and phenobarbital can be used to control seizures. Most recently, perampanel, a new AMPA receptor antagonist AED, is shown to be effective.\n\nLafora body disease is an autosomal recessive neurodegenerative disease and has an adolescent onset. It is associated with polyglucosan intracellular inclusion bodies. It is characterized by:\n\n• Progressive myoclonus\n\n• Epileptic seizures\n\n• Dementia\n\n• Ataxia.\n\nDiagnosis is by DNA sequencing. Death usually occurs within 10 years of the onset of the disease.","t":"Seizures And Epilepsy","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"You are at a criminal court to provide medical testimony. The trial ends with the accused being sentenced to life imprisonment. Abruptly, the convict breaks into uncontrollable laughter. Within minutes, he collapses and starts to have a seizure. His lawyer reports that he has a childhood seizure disorder. What is the likely diagnosis?","o":["Gelastic seizures","Kluver Bucy syndrome","Witzelsucht","Pseudobulbar affect"],"a":0,"e":"The given scenario is suggestive of a gelastic seizure.\n\nThis condition is also known as gelastic epilepsy, and is a rare type of seizure that involves a sudden burst of energy, usually in the form of laughing or crying. It is seen when the seizure focus is within the left prefrontal cortex. Gelastic seizures have been associated classically with hypothalamic hamartomas.\n\nOption A: Kluver Bucy syndrome is characterized by hypersexuality, placidity, a tendency to explore the environment with the mouth, inability to recognize the emotional significance of visual stimuli, and constantly shifting attention, called hypermetamorphosis.\n\nOption B: Witzelsucht is a tendency to joke and make puns.\n\nOption C: Pseudobulbar affect is defined by involun­tary, sudden, and frequent episodes of laughing or crying that are generally out of proportion or inappropriate to the situation.","t":"Seizures And Epilepsy","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A 10-year-old girl is brought to the OPD following a generalized tonic-clonic seizure. She had previous episodes of sudden falls at school and also sometimes stares with a blank face. She is also found to be having poor scholastic performance. Her EEG reveals a slow spike and wave pattern. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Focal seizures progressing to generalized seizures","Myoclonic epilepsy progressing to generalized seizures","Lenox-Gastaut syndrome","Atypical absence seizures"],"a":2,"e":"The given clinical scenario reveals multiple types of seizures such as atonic seizures (sudden falls), absence seizures (blank stares and unresponsive), and generalized tonic-clonic seizures in this patient. This is a characteristic feature of Lenox-Gastaut syndrome.\n\nLennox-Gastaut syndrome is a rare type of epilepsy syndrome that begins in childhood. It may occur due to various reasons such as developmental anomalies, mutations, trauma, infections, etc. It is characterized by a typical triad of\n\n• Multiple seizure types\n\n• Intellectual disability\n\n• Typical <3 Hz spike and wave pattern on EEG\n\nTreatment includes administration of anti-epileptic drugs such as lamotrigine and sodium valproate. It is frequently refractory to medical therapy and usually has a poor prognosis.","t":"Seizures And Epilepsy","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Ischemia occurs in the brain when the cerebral blood flow falls below _____.","o":["40 mL/100 g/min","20 mL/100 g/min","30 mL/100 g/min","50 mL/100 g/min"],"a":1,"e":"A decrease in the cerebral blood flow below 20 mL/100 g/min causes reversible ischemia.\n\nIf this state is prolonged for several hours to days, it can lead to infarction. A decrease in CBF to 0 causes the death of the brain tissue within 4–10 min.\n\nNormal cerebral blood flow (CBF) in an adult brain at rest is 50–55 mL/100 g/min.","t":"Ischemic Stroke","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A patient presented with acute onset of weakness of the left arm which resolved completely in 10 days. He is known to have mitral stenosis, hypertension and dyslipidemia. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Transient ischemic attack","Embolic stroke","Hemorrhagic stroke","Thrombotic stroke"],"a":1,"e":"This patient with mitral stenosis and acute onset weakness of the left arm is most likely to have had an embolic stroke, which is a type of ischemic stroke. Embolic strokes tend to occur suddenly with maximum neurologic deficit present at onset.\n\nMitral stenosis is known to cause atrial fibrillation and is a common cause of cardioembolic stroke.\n\nTransient ischemic attack (TIA) is a temporary, focal neurological deficit of sudden onset (stroke-like symptoms) lasting for less than 24 hours. This occurs due to focal ischemia of the brain, retina or cochlea. Most TIAs last for less than 1 hour to a few minutes.\n\nThere is a high risk of developing an ischemic stroke after a TIA, especially for the first 3 months following a TIA.","t":"Ischemic Stroke","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A patient presented with right-sided hemiparesis that completely resolved in 4-6 hours. What score will you use to determine the risk of a future stroke in this patient?","o":["CHADS2-VASc","CURB-65","ABCD2","APACHE II"],"a":2,"e":"This patient's clinical features are suggestive of a transient ischemic attack. The risk of stroke following TIAs is determined by the ABCD2 score.\n\nCHADS2-VASc score is used to determine the risk of stroke in a patient with atrial fibrillation.\n\nCURB-65 is a scoring system used to predict mortality in patients with community-acquired pneumonia.\n\nAPACHE II is a scoring system used to predict ICU mortality.","t":"Ischemic Stroke","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A 67-year-old patient with hypertension, diabetes, and asthma is found to have an irregularly irregular pulse during a routine physical examination. ECG findings are shown below. How is the risk of stroke determined in this patient?","o":["CHA2DS2-VASC","NIH Stroke Score","ABCD2","Modified GCS"],"a":0,"e":"The given scenario along with the ECG findings are suggestive of atrial fibrillation. The risk of stroke in this condition is determined by CHA2DS2-VASC score.\n\nThe ECG show irregular QRS complexes of normal morphology and absent P waves.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2f4640dee0ed46a08a4f3b808b55a1c7.JPEG"],"t":"Ischemic Stroke","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A 73-year-old woman who was brought to the ER with hemiparesis was found to have a lacunar infarction. What causes this condition?","o":["Emboli in small vessels","Thrombosis in large vessels","Lipohyalinosis of penetrating arteries","Hemorrhagic stroke"],"a":2,"e":"Lacunar infarcts, also called small vessel stroke, refer to infarction following atherothrombotic or lipohyalinotic occlusion of small penetrating arteries in the brain. These penetrating arteries arise from the stem of the middle cerebral artery, circle of Willis, and basilar and vertebral arteries.\n\nThe principal risk factors are hypertension and old age.\n\nThe image below shows two lacunar infarctions on a CT.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/95785abcfd0640b980cba1bd6173502cx446x539.JPEG"],"t":"Ischemic Stroke","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not caused by a lacunar stroke?","o":["Pure motor hemiparesis","Pure sensory stroke","Ataxic hemiparesis","Crural monoplegia with urinary incontinence"],"a":3,"e":"Crural monoplegia with urinary incontinence is not caused by a lacunar stroke. It is caused by the occlusion of A2 segment of the anterior cerebral artery. Crural monoplegia refers to a monoplegia of the lower limbs.\n\nThe types of lacunar syndromes are as follows:\n\n1. Pure motor hemiparesis - infarct in the posterior limb of the internal capsule\n\n2. Pure sensory stroke - infarct in the thalamus\n\n3. Ataxic hemiparesis - infarct in the pons or internal capsule\n\n4. Dysarthria and clumsy hand syndrome - infarction in the pons or internal capsule.\n\nThe ACA is divided into two segments:\n\n• The pre-communal (A1) circle of Willis, or stem, which connects the internal carotid artery to the anterior communicating artery\n\n• The post-communal (A2) segment distal to the anterior communicating artery","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b126036efc8344fda3964b90cc21af3ex1280x1284.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a242be93074f44e487601de68714f028x1280x1564.JPEG"],"t":"Ischemic Stroke","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A patient is diagnosed with an ischaemic stroke. His son says that he holds the comb incorrectly while combing his hair. During the evaluation, he was unable to button his shirt when requested. The lesion is most likely to be in which lobe?","o":["Dominant frontal lobe","Dominant parietal lobe","Non-dominant frontal lobe","Non-dominant parietal lobe"],"a":1,"e":"The given scenario where the patient is unable to perform learned skilled movements is suggestive of apraxia. The lesion is likely to be in the dominant parietal lobe as apraxia is a feature of a lesion in this area.\n\nThe features of dominant parietal lobe lesions are as follows:\n\n• Apraxia - inability to perform learned skilled movements\n\n• Acalculia - difficulty performing simple mathematical tasks\n\n• Alexia - inability to recognize or read written words or letters\n\n• Agnosia - inability to recognize and identify objects, persons, or sounds\n\n• Dysphasia - language disorder marked by deficiency in the generation of speech and its comprehension\n\n• Contralateral inferior homonymous quadrantanopia\n\n• Gerstmann syndrome (acalculia, dysgraphia, finger agnosia, right-left confusion)\n\nFeatures of non-dominant parietal lobe lesions include:\n\n• Spatial disorientation\n\n• Constructional apraxia\n\n• Contralateral inferior homonymous quadrantanopia\n\n• Contralateral hemineglect.","t":"Ischemic Stroke","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following features is not likely to be seen in a patient with a temporal lobe lesion?","o":["Neglect","Superior quadrantanopia","Auditory hallucinations","Poor memory"],"a":0,"e":"Neglect is not a feature of temporal lobe lesion. It is a feature of a non-dominant parietal lobe lesion.\n\nFeatures of dominant temporal lobe lesions:\n\n• Complex hallucinations - visual, auditory or olfactory\n\n• Memory impairment\n\n• Complex partial seizures\n\n• Wernicke’s aphasia\n\n• Contralateral superior quadrantanopia\n\nFeatures of non-dominant temporal lobe lesions:\n\n• Poor non-verbal memory\n\n• Loss of musical skills\n\n• Complex hallucinations\n\n• Contralateral superior quadrantanopia.","t":"Ischemic Stroke","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A 43-year-old man is brought to the hospital due to extremely aggressive behaviour towards his family. His family members also say that he has become forgetful and has developed urinary incontinence. Which of the following lobes is likely to be involved in his disease process?","o":["Frontal lobe","Dominant parietal lobe","Temporal lobe","Non-dominant parietal lobe"],"a":0,"e":"Antisocial behavior, urinary incontinence, and impaired memory in this patient are suggestive of frontal lobe involvement.\n\nFeatures of frontal lobe lesions:\n\n• Disinhibition\n\n• Lack of initiative\n\n• Antisocial behavior\n\n• Impaired memory\n\n• Incontinence\n\n• Grasp reflexes\n\n• Anosmia.","t":"Ischemic Stroke","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A 60-year-old patient with atrial fibrillation complains of sudden onset weakness of the right lower limb, with urinary and faecal incontinence. MRI shows an ischaemic stroke involving the left anterior cerebral artery. Which of the following features would you not expect to see in this patient?","o":["Ataxia","Prosopagnosia","Abulia","Paratonic rigidity"],"a":1,"e":"Prosopagnosia, also called face blindness, is the inability to recognize faces including one's own image. This occurs due to occipitotemporal lobe damage, seen in posterior cerebral artery (PCA) strokes involving cortical branch occlusion.\nIt is not seen in anterior cerebral artery strokes.\n\nThe anterior cerebral artery (ACA) supplies parts of the frontal lobe, corpus callosum, paracentral lobule, and other deeper structures.\n\nSigns and symptoms of ACA occlusion and structures involved:\n\n• Urinary incontinence, fecal incontinence\n\n• Paratonic rigidity\n\n• Abulia (Akinetic mutism), lack of spontaneity, slowness\n\n• Gait impairment\n\n• Paralysis of contralateral foot and leg\n\n• Paresis of the contralateral arm\n\n• Cortical sensory loss over the contralateral lower limb\n\n• Dyspraxia of left limbs, tactile aphasia in left limbs.","t":"Ischemic Stroke","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Infarction of area of distribution of which of the following arteries is most likely to be seen in a patient with hemiplegia?","o":["Vertebral artery","Middle cerebral artery","Anterior spinal artery","Posterior cerebral artery"],"a":1,"e":"Hemiplegia is most commonly associated with infarction of the area of distribution of middle cerebral artery.\n\nMiddle cerebral artery supplies the motor cortex responsible for sending the output to the motor neurons. Infarction in this territory leads to hemiplegia.\n\nInfarction of area of distribution of vertebral, anterior spinal and posterior cerebral arteries can also lead to hemiplegia.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b63f43cf3df54aef8cfda110e6eed161x1024x999.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a5a9740ac933421b956062949550cfefx1024x999.JPEG"],"t":"Ischemic Stroke","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following features is not expected to be seen in a patient with a right middle cerebral artery infarct?","o":["Aphasia","Hemiparesis","Facial weakness","Dysarthria"],"a":0,"e":"Aphasia is not a feature of right middle cerebral artery infarct (MCA).\n\nAphasia occurs when there is damage to the dominant hemisphere, i.e., left hemisphere. The dominant hemisphere contains the language network which includes Broca’s area, Wernicke’s area, their interconnections and connections with frontal, parietal and temporal areas.\n\nThe MCA originates from the internal carotid artery. The stem of the MCA is referred to as the M1 segment. It gives rise to cortical and penetrating/lenticulostriate branches.\n\nMCA stem occlusion leads to :\n\n• Contralateral hemiplegia\n\n• Hemianesthesia\n\n• Homonymous hemianopia\n\n• Global aphasia (dominant hemisphere)\n\n• Anosognosia, constructional apraxia, neglect (non-dominant hemisphere)\n\nOcclusion of the lenticulostriate branches of MCA (lacunar stroke) may affect the genu of the internal capsule. This can lead to dysarthria with contralateral clumsy hand (fine motor weakness).","t":"Ischemic Stroke","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Visual field examination of a 52-year-old stroke patient reveals the finding shown in the image. During examination, you notice that he is not able to comprehend your instructions and sometimes gives irrelevant replies. What is the likely site of arterial occlusion?","o":["Left MCA stem","Right M1 segment of MCA","Left M2 segment of MCA – inferior division","Right M2 segment of MCA – superior division"],"a":2,"e":"The given scenario is suggestive of homonymous right superior quadrantanopia (pie in the sky appearance) and sensory aphasia. These are seen in occlusion of the inferior division of the opposite side middle cerebral artery (MCA).\n\nParts of the middle cerebral artery:\n\n• The MCA originates from the internal carotid artery. The stem of the MCA is referred to as the M1 segment.\n\n• In the Sylvian fissure, the MCA divides into superior and inferior divisions. These branches are referred to as the M2 segments of the MCA.\n\nOcclusion of the following sites and their effects are given below:\n\n• MCA stem (M1) - leads to contralateral hemiplegia, hemianesthesia and homonymous hemianopia amongst other features\n\n• Superior division of MCA (M2) on the dominant side - leads to motor aphasia (Broca's) and facio-brachial weakness\n\n• Inferior division of MCA (M2) on the dominant side - leads to sensory aphasia (Wernicke's) and contralateral superior quadrantanopia\n\nThe following image of coronal section of cerebral hemisphere shows the segments of MCA:","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/dbb8e1346c494dc4bd451fb94e494330.JPEG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cc07443b750e427d88b54f64f1128e4bx1280x1649.JPEG"],"t":"Ischemic Stroke","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A patient is found to have Wernicke’s aphasia without motor involvement. Where is the infarct likely to be?","o":["ACA territory of the right hemisphere","ACA territory of the left hemisphere","MCA territory of the right hemisphere","MCA territory of the left hemisphere"],"a":3,"e":"The presence of Wernicke’s aphasia without motor involvement suggests an infarct in the MCA territory of the left (dominant) hemisphere.\n\nWernicke’s area is located in the superior temporal gyrus in the dominant hemisphere (mostly the left side). This area is supplied by the inferior division of MCA.\n\nMCA divides into superior and inferior divisions in the Sylvian fissure. These branches are referred to as the M2 segments of MCA.\n\n• The superior division - supplies the frontal and the superior parietal cortex.\n\n• The inferior division - supplies the inferior parietal and temporal cortex.\n\nAn infarct in the territory of the inferior division of MCA in the dominant hemisphere causes Wernicke’s aphasia without weakness. As the frontal cortex (containing the motor area) is supplied by the superior division and is not affected, weakness is not observed.\n\nAn infarct in the inferior division of MCA of the non-dominant hemisphere (mostly the right side) causes hemineglect without weakness.","t":"Ischemic Stroke","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not a feature of an anterior choroidal artery lesion?","o":["Aphasia","Contralateral hemiplegia","Hemianesthesia","Homonymous hemianopia"],"a":0,"e":"Aphasia (dominant hemisphere), constructional apraxia and neglect (non-dominant hemisphere) are seen in MCA stem occlusion. These features are absent in anterior choroidal artery occlusion.\n\nFeatures common to both MCA and anterior choroidal artery occlusion include:\n\n• Contralateral hemiplegia\n\n• Hemianaesthesia\n\n• Homonymous hemianopia.","t":"Ischemic Stroke","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A man is rushed to the ER after losing control of his car and crashing into a tree. Examination findings are given below. What is the most likely cause for his presentation?\n\nBP - 190/100 mmHg\n\nRespiratory rate - 22/minute\n\nGCS - 9/15\n\nTemperature - 106.8 °F\n\nHead and neck examination - Minor abrasions over the face\n\nCNS - Quadriparesis. Pupils are 2 mm and unreactive.","o":["Spinal cord trauma","Morphine poisoning","Pontine hemorrhage","Ponto-medullary laceration"],"a":2,"e":"The given scenario is suggestive of pontine haemorrhage. Clinical features of pontine haemorrhage are:\n\n• Deep coma\n\n• Quadriparesis and multiple cranial nerve dysfunction\n\n• Decerebrate rigidity\n\n• Pin-point pupils\n\n• Hyperpnea, hyperpyrexia, severe hypertension, and hyperhidrosis\n\nOption A: The presence of hyperpyrexia and pin-point pupils rule out the possibility of spinal cord trauma.\n\nOption B: Morphine poisoning is a differential diagnosis for pin-point pupils. But it leads to slower respiratory rate, not tachypnea.\n\nOption D: In ponto-medullary laceration, medullary involvement would cause respiratory depression, leading to apnea. But here tachypnea is noted.","t":"Ischemic Stroke","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Thrombolysis is contraindicated in which of the following patients with ischaemic stroke?\n\n1. A patient brought to the ER within half an hour of onset of symptoms\n\n2. A 57-year-old man brought to the ER within 3 hours of onset of symptoms and having platelet count of 180,000 / µL\n\n3. A patient with a sustained BP of 190/120 mm of Hg\n\n4. A patient with a history of gastrointestinal bleeding about a month ago\n\n5. A patient who has recent history of myocardial infarction","o":["Patients 1, 2 and 4","Patient 2 only","Patients 3 and 5","Patient 3 and 4"],"a":2,"e":"A sustained blood pressure > 185/110 mmHg and recent myocardial infarction are contraindications for initiating thrombolysis in ischemic stroke.","t":"Ischemic Stroke","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A patient with acute ischemic stroke requires IV thrombolysis. His symptoms had begun at 6:30 PM and he was brought to the hospital by 7:00 PM. For this treatment to be beneficial for the patient, it should be administered within what time?","o":["7:30 PM","9:00 PM","10:30 PM","11:00 PM"],"a":3,"e":"IV thrombolysis in acute ischemic stroke has been proven to be beneficial up to 4.5 hours after the onset of symptoms according to European Cooperative Acute Stroke Study (ECASS) III.\n\nAccording to MoHFW (India), thrombolysis is to be carried out within 4.5 hours of symptom onset.\n\nIV tPA should be administered to all eligible acute stroke patients within 3 hours of last known normal and to a more selective group of eligible acute stroke patients (based on ECASS III exclusion criteria) within 4.5 hours of last known normal.","t":"Ischemic Stroke","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A woman on oral contraceptive pills presents with a headache for 2 days and paraparesis. What could be the most likely cause?","o":["Posterior reversible encephalopathy syndrome","Cerebral venous thrombosis","Complicated migraine","Subarachnoid hemorrhage"],"a":1,"e":"The presence of headache and paraparesis in this patient with a history of oral contraceptive use is suggestive of cerebral venous thrombosis.\n\nCerebral venous thrombosis is commonly seen in hypercoagulable states such as:\n\n• Oral contraceptive pill use\n\n• Pregnancy and puerperium\n\n• Inflammatory bowel disease\n\n• Dehydration\n\n• Intracranial infections.\n\nIt presents with:\n\n• Headache (most common)\n\n• Seizures\n\n• Intracranial Hypertension\n\n• Focal neurological Deficits\n\n• Altered Mental status\n\nMR venography is used for diagnosis.\n\nThe below image shows the empty delta sign seen on CECT or MRI with contrast in patients with sagittal sinus thrombosis.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f4365e3dc86d42f1b7955a90873634cex743x900.JPEG"],"t":"Ischemic Stroke","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"What is the treatment of choice for a patient diagnosed with sagittal sinus thrombosis?","o":["Aspirin","Heparin","Warfarin","rtPA"],"a":1,"e":"IV heparin is the drug of choice in cerebral venous thrombosis. It prevents further thrombosis and reduces venous hypertension and ischemia.\n\nAnticoagulation is often continued indefinitely if thrombophilia is diagnosed.\n\nIf there is no underlying hypercoagulable state, treatment with vitamin K antagonists (VKAs) for 3–6 months may be initiated and then converted to aspirin, depending on the degree of resolution of the venous sinus thrombus.\n\nTreatment of CVT:\n\n• Acute treatment - IV Heparin or LMWH\n\n• Maintenance therapy:\n\n• Warfarin for at least 3 months\n\n• In pregnant women with a history of CVT - anticoagulation with LMWH throughout pregnancy and till 8 weeks postpartum.","t":"Ischemic Stroke","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A 40-year-old patient presents with headache and weakness of the right side of the body. He has difficulty in speaking also. Angiography shows a puff of smoke appearance. Which of the following statements regarding this disease is false?","o":["It is an occlusive disease involving large intracranial arteries","Vascular inflammation is absent","Dawson’s fingers are the characteristic imaging finding","Rich collateral circulation is present"],"a":2,"e":"This patient's clinical features are suggestive of Moyamoya disease and Dawson's fingers is not a finding in this condition.\n\nDawson fingers are a radiographic feature typically seen in multiple sclerosis (MS)\n\nThe picture below demonstrates Dawson fingers.\n\nMoyamoya disease is an occlusive disease involving large intracranial arteries. It has a bimodal age distribution and is seen in the first and fourth decades:\n\n• Childhood moyamoya is characterized by ischemic manifestations\n\n• Adult moyamoya is characterized by hemorrhagic manifestations\n\nThe first symptom is a stroke or recurrent TIA.\n\nTreatment is by surgical bypass of extracranial carotid arteries to the dura or middle cerebral artery. This may prevent stroke and haemorrhage.\n\nThe below image shows the MR angiography findings in a patient with Moyamoya disease.\n\nNote: As this is a chronic condition, the lenticulostriate arteries develop a rich collateral circulation around the occlusive lesion. This is seen as a 'puff of smoke' (moyamoya in Japanese) on X-ray angiography.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e5e0a94a4dd44e1c97e0fcc91bb61c7ex1279x1211.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1c4c082b8ead4cab8f5bb3df228a844dx392x485.JPEG"],"t":"Ischemic Stroke","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which among the following is not a component of CHA2DS2-VASc score?","o":["Age &gt; 60 years","Hypertension","Diabetes","Transient ischemic attack"],"a":0,"e":"Age > 60 years is not a component of the CHADS2-VASc score. Age > 75years and age group 65-75 yrs are the two age criteria considered in the score.\n\nThe CHA2DS2-VASc score is used to assess the risk of stroke in patients who have atrial fibrillation. Anticoagulation therapy is given when the score is ≥2. Patients with a score of 1 may also be considered for anticoagulation therapy.\n\nAnticoagulation therapy is given for all the patients of AF having mitral stenosis, hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, or a history of stroke.","t":"Ischemic Stroke","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A patient has developed confusion and keeps bumping into objects. He can speak fluently. But on examination, he cannot differentiate between fingers and cannot write. He also gets confused with the examiner's right and left sides. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Gerstmann syndrome","Anton syndrome","Millard Gubler syndrome","Dejerine Roussy syndrome"],"a":0,"e":"The most likely diagnosis for this patient is Gerstmann syndrome. It is commonly associated with damage to the left (dominant) inferior parietal lobule.\n\nGerstmann syndrome consists of:\n\n• Acalculia - impairment of simple arithmetic\n\n• Dysgraphia - impaired writing\n\n• Finger anomia - an inability to name individual fingers\n\n• Right-left confusion - an inability to tell whether a hand, foot, or arm of the patient or examiner is on the right or left side of the body\n\nOption B: Anton syndrome also known as visual anosognosia occurs due to bilateral infarction in the distal PCAs. It produces cortical blindness and the patients are unaware of the blindness or may even deny it.\n\nOption C: Millard-Gubler syndrome occurs due to ventral pontine injury. It causes functional loss of the 6th and 7th cranial nerves and corticospinal tract. This presents as diplopia, ipsilateral facial palsy, ipsilateral loss of corneal reflex, contralateral hemiparesis.\n\nOption D: Dejerine-Roussy syndrome is associated with hemisensory loss and severe burning pain in the affected region. It is also known as thalamic pain syndrome.","t":"Brainstem Syndromes And Hemorrhagic Stroke","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A 78-year-old woman presents with right hemisensory loss followed by agonizing, burning pain in the affected regions. The pain responds poorly to analgesics. These features are classically seen in which of the following conditions?","o":["Weber’s syndrome","Parinaud’s syndrome","Dejerine-Roussy syndrome","Millard-Gubler syndrome"],"a":2,"e":"Hemisensory loss and severe burning pain in the affected regions is classically seen in Dejerine-Roussy syndrome, also known as thalamic pain syndrome.\n\nDejerine- Roussy syndrome occurs due to occlusion of the thalamic and thalamogeniculate branches arising from the P1 segment resulting in contralateral hemisensory loss and severe burning pain in the affected regions.\n\nThe P1 segment of the posterior cerebral artery extends from the basilar bifurcation to the junction with the posterior communicating artery.\n\nThis pain responds poorly to analgesics. Anticonvulsants such as carbamazepine or gabapentin or tricyclic antidepressants may be beneficial.","t":"Brainstem Syndromes And Hemorrhagic Stroke","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A 73-year-old woman was brought to the OPD since she has been tripping over things at home. On entering, she complimented the doctor’s tie even though he was not wearing one and continued to insist that she could see normally. On examination, bilateral blindness was present but pupillary light reflex was normal. What is the likely diagnosis?","o":["Anton's syndrome","Balint’s syndrome","Parinaud’s syndrome","Riddoch phenomenon"],"a":0,"e":"The given scenario is suggestive of Anton's syndrome. It occurs due to bilateral infarction in the distal posterior cerebral artery (P2 segment) and is associated with the following features:\n\n• Cortical blindness with preserved pupillary light reaction\n\n• Visual anosognosia - denial of loss of vision\n\n• Confabulation in the setting of obvious visual loss\n\nOption D: Riddoch phenomenon is a condition where cortical blindness is associated with the perception of only moving targets.","t":"Brainstem Syndromes And Hemorrhagic Stroke","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A patient with a history of ischemic heart disease now comes to the hospital due to problems with vision. She complains that while turning her head, the image of the previous target still persists for a few seconds before she can see the new target. This phenomenon is seen in which of the following conditions?","o":["Anton's syndrome","Balint's syndrome","Parinaud's syndrome","Claude's syndrome"],"a":1,"e":"The persistence of visual image even after shifting gaze is called palinopsia. It is seen in Balint’s syndrome.\n\nBalint’s syndrome results due to low flow in the watershed areas between distal PCA and MCA territories. It is characterized by the following:\n\n• Palinopsia - persistence of a visual image despite gazing at another scene\n\n• Asimultagnosia - difficulty to focus on more than one item in the visual field\n\n• Optic ataxia - Inability to reach accurately under visual guidance\n\n• Ocular apraxia - Inability to direct gaze accurately to a new target\n\nClaude's syndrome is a distinctive brainstem syndrome characterized by ipsilateral third nerve palsy with contralateral hemiataxia. It is due to an intrinsic or extrinsic lesion in the midbrain.\n\nNote: While some authors use the term simultanagnosia, others use asimultanagnosia instead. A few authors also use these two terminologies interchangeably.","t":"Brainstem Syndromes And Hemorrhagic Stroke","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following features do you not expect to see in a patient with Balint's syndrome?","o":["Opsoclonus","Optic ataxia","Simultanagnosia","Ocular apraxia"],"a":0,"e":"Opsoclonus is not seen in Balint's syndrome.\n\nThe features of Balint’s syndrome are:\n\n• Optic ataxia - the inability to guide the arm to reach a certain object in the visual field\n\n• Simultanagnosia - the inability to perceive more than one object at a time. In other words, it is the inability to recognize multiple elements in a visual presentation, one object or some elements of a scene can be appreciated but not the display as a whole.\n\n• Ocular apraxia - the inability to control voluntary purposeful eye movements\n\n• Palinopsia - the persistence of a visual image even after the stimulus has been removed\n\nNote: While some authors use the term simultanagnosia, others use asimultanagnosia instead. A few authors also use these two terminologies interchangeably.","t":"Brainstem Syndromes And Hemorrhagic Stroke","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A patient presents with ipsilateral oculomotor palsy and contralateral hemiplegia. What is the diagnosis?","o":["Anton’s syndrome","Benedikt syndrome","Claude's syndrome","Weber's syndrome"],"a":3,"e":"Ipsilateral oculomotor palsy and contralateral hemiplegia is seen in Weber’s syndrome.\n\nWeber’s syndrome occurs due to infarction in the distribution of the penetrating branches of the PCA. It affects the cerebral peduncle, especially medially, and damages III cranial nerve and the pyramidal fibers. It results in contralateral hemiplegia and ipsilateral oculomotor palsy.\n\nClaude's syndrome is a distinctive brainstem syndrome characterized by ipsilateral third cranial nerve palsy with contralateral hemiataxia. This is due to an intrinsic or extrinsic lesion in the midbrain.","t":"Brainstem Syndromes And Hemorrhagic Stroke","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A patient presented with loss of upgaze and convergence-retraction nystagmus. What is the likely diagnosis?","o":["Nothnagel's syndrome","Balint’s syndrome","Weber’s syndrome","Parinaud’s syndrome"],"a":3,"e":"The presence of convergence-retraction nystagmus and loss of upgaze is seen in Parinaud's syndrome. It is caused by a lesion in the dorsal midbrain.\n\nThe following tetrad of symptoms are seen:\n\n1. Loss of upgaze\n\n2. Light-near dissociation\n\n3. Convergence-retraction nystagmus\n\n4. Lid retraction\n\nConvergence‐retraction nystagmus is irregular, jerky nystagmus in which both eyeballs rhythmically converge and retract into the orbit, particularly on attempting an upward gaze.\n\nNothnagel syndrome is associated with oculomotor palsy and contralateral limb ataxia. Underlying structures involved are the superior cerebellar peduncle and tectum of the midbrain.","t":"Brainstem Syndromes And Hemorrhagic Stroke","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A patient presented with left-sided hemiplegia and examination revealed right-sided abducens nerve palsy and facial nerve palsy. Further investigation revealed a right-sided pontine haemorrhage. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Marie-Foix syndrome","Raymond’s syndrome","Millard–Gubler syndrome","Foville's syndrome"],"a":2,"e":"Ipsilateral abducens and facial nerve palsy with contralateral hemiplegia are seen in Millard–Gubler syndrome.\n\nIn this syndrome, there is a ventral pontine injury (lesion in abducens fascicle) that causes lateral rectus palsy.\n\nCNS lesion\n\n Effects\n\nMillard–Gubler Syndrome\n\n• Ipsilateral abducens nerve weakness\n\n• Ipsilateral facial nerve palsy\n\n• Contralateral hemiplegia\n\nRaymond’s Syndrome\n\n• Ipsilateral abducens nerve palsy\n\n• Contralateral hemiplegia\n\nFoville’s Syndrome\n\n• Lateral gaze palsy\n\n• Ipsilateral facial nerve palsy\n\n• Contralateral hemiplegia\n\nBalint's syndrome\n\n• Optic ataxia\n\n• Ocular apraxia\n\n• Visuospatial disorientation\n\nLateral pontine syndrome (Marie-Foix syndrome)\n\n• Contralateral hemiparesis\n\n• Contralateral loss of proprioception & vibration\n\n• Ipsilateral limb ataxia\n\n• Ipsilateral facial palsy\n\n• Lateral hearing loss\n\n• Vertigo\n\n• Nystagmus","t":"Brainstem Syndromes And Hemorrhagic Stroke","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following differentiates Foville’s syndrome from Millard-Gubler syndrome?","o":["Contralateral hemiplegia","Ipsilateral hemiplegia","Ipsilateral facial palsy","Lateral gaze palsy"],"a":3,"e":"Lateral gaze palsy is a feature of Foville’s Syndrome which is a result of dorsal pontine injury.\n\nMillard-Gubler syndrome occurs due to ventral pontine injury. Lateral gaze palsy does not occur in this condition because the abducens fascicle is injured rather than the nucleus.\n\nCNS lesion\n\n Effects\n\nMillard–Gubler Syndrome\n\n• Ipsilateral abducens nerve weakness\n\n• Ipsilateral facial nerve palsy\n\n• Contralateral hemiplegia\n\nRaymond’s Syndrome\n\n• Ipsilateral abducens nerve palsy\n\n• Contralateral hemiplegia\n\nFoville’s Syndrome\n\n• Lateral gaze palsy\n\n• Ipsilateral facial nerve palsy\n\n• Contralateral hemiplegia\n\nBalint's syndrome\n\n• Optic ataxia\n\n• Ocular apraxia\n\n• Visuospatial disorientation\n\nLateral pontine syndrome (Marie-Foix syndrome)\n\n• Contralateral hemiparesis\n\n• Contralateral loss of proprioception & vibration\n\n• Ipsilateral limb ataxia\n\n• Ipsilateral facial palsy\n\n• Lateral hearing loss\n\n• Vertigo\n\n• Nystagmus\n\n• Contralateral loss of pain and temperature","t":"Brainstem Syndromes And Hemorrhagic Stroke","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Wallenberg syndrome is most commonly caused by thrombosis of which of the following arteries?","o":["Vertebral artery","Posterior inferior cerebellar artery","Anterior inferior cerebellar artery","Basilar artery"],"a":0,"e":"Thrombosis of the vertebral artery is most commonly implicated in Wallenberg syndrome.\n\nWallenberg syndrome, also called lateral medullary syndrome, is due to occlusion of the ipsilateral vertebral artery in most cases. In the remainder, posterior inferior cerebellar artery occlusion is responsible.\n\nWallenberg / lateral medullary syndrome affects the following structures:\n\n• Descending tract and nucleus of cranial nerve V - Ipsilateral impaired sensation over half of the face\n\n• IX and X cranial nerves - Ipsilateral paralysis of palate, vocal cord, diminished gag reflex\n\n• Nucleus of tractus solitarius - Ipsilateral loss of taste\n\n• Vestibular nucleus - Nystagmus, diplopia, vertigo, nausea\n\n• Spinocerebellar tract - Ipsilateral ataxia\n\n• Descending sympathetic tract - Ipsilateral Horner’s syndrome\n\n• Lateral spinothalamic tract - Contralateral impaired pain and thermal sensation over half of the body.","t":"Brainstem Syndromes And Hemorrhagic Stroke","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following features do you not expect to see in a patient with Wallenberg syndrome?","o":["Ipsilateral ataxia","Contralateral hemiplegia","Vertigo","Impaired sensation on ipsilateral half of face"],"a":1,"e":"The pyramidal tracts are not affected in Wallenberg syndrome and therefore hemiplegia is not seen.\n\nWallenberg / lateral medullary syndrome affects the following structures:\n\n• Descending tract and nucleus of CN V - I/L impaired sensation over half of face\n\n• IX and X CN - I/L paralysis of palate, vocal cord, diminished gag reflex\n\n• Nucleus of Tractus Solitarius – I/L loss of taste\n\n• Vestibular nucleus - Nystagmus, diplopia, vertigo, nausea\n\n• Spinocerebellar tract – I/L ataxia\n\n• Descending sympathetic tract – I/L Horner’s syndrome\n\n• Lateral spinothalamic tract – C/L impaired pain and thermal sensation over half of the body.","t":"Brainstem Syndromes And Hemorrhagic Stroke","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following features would you not expect to see in a patient with medial medullary syndrome?","o":["Contralateral hemiparesis","Ipsilateral paralysis of tongue","Contralateral loss of proprioception","Horner's sydrome"],"a":3,"e":"Horner’s syndrome is not a feature of medial medullary syndrome.\n\nMedial medullary syndrome occurs due to the occlusion of the vertebral artery or a branch of the vertebral or basilar artery. The features of medial medullary syndrome are\n\n• CN XII - Ipsilateral paralysis of the tongue\n\n• Pyramidal tract - Contralateral hemiplegia\n\n• Medial lemniscus - Impaired tactile and proprioception over the contralateral half of the body\n\nHorner’s syndrome occurs due to a lesion involving the sympathetic fibres. It is seen in lateral medullary syndrome.","t":"Brainstem Syndromes And Hemorrhagic Stroke","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which is the most common site of bleed in patients with intracerebral hypertensive haemorrhage?","o":["Thalamus","Subcortical white matter","Pons","Putamen"],"a":3,"e":"The most common site for intracerebral hypertensive hemorrhage is the putamen.\n\nThe most common type of intracranial haemorrhage is an intraparenchymal i.e., intracerebral haemorrhage. It is most often caused by a hypertensive bleed.\n\nMost common sites of intracerebral hypertensive bleed:\n\n• Putamen (basal ganglia)\n\n• Thalamus\n\n• Cerebellum\n\n• Pons.","t":"Brainstem Syndromes And Hemorrhagic Stroke","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Raised intracranial tension is not associated with which of the following manifestations?","o":["Seizures","Blurring of vision","Paraparesis","Abducent paralysis"],"a":2,"e":"Raised ICT is not associated with paraparesis.\n\nManifestations of raised ICT include:\n\n• Hypertension\n\n• Bradycardia\n\n• Respiratory depression\n\n• Headache, visual blurring due to papilloedema and abducent nerve palsy\n\n• Seizures\n\nParaparesis is usually caused by a spinal lesion such as thoracic myelopathy and neoplasms causing compression of the spinal cord.\n\nRare intracranial causes of paraparesis are:\n\n• Parasagittal meningioma\n\n• Occlusion of unpaired anterior cerebral artery\n\n• Cerebral venous thrombosis affecting the sagittal sinus.","t":"Brainstem Syndromes And Hemorrhagic Stroke","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is a component of Cushing’s triad?","o":["Bradycardia","Hypotension","Loss of consciousness","Papilledema"],"a":0,"e":"Bradycardia is a component of Cushing's triad.\n\nCushing’s triad is characteristically seen in raised ICT. Its components are as follows:\n\n• Hypertension\n\n• Bradycardia\n\n• Respiratory depression.","t":"Brainstem Syndromes And Hemorrhagic Stroke","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A 62-year-old man presents to the casualty with a severe headache, fever, and vomiting for the past 5 hours. There is no history of trauma. CT head is shown below. What is the most common cause for his presentation?","o":["Saccular aneurysms","Hypertension","Microaneurysms","Atherosclerosis"],"a":0,"e":"The given scenario is suggestive of non-traumatic subarachnoid haemorrhage. The most common cause for this condition is the rupture of a berry aneurysm, also known as a saccular aneurysm.\n\nCT shows hyperattenuating material filling the subarachnoid space, which is suggestive of SAH. Most commonly, it occurs around the circle of Willis due to the rupture of a berry aneurysm at this location.\n\nMost unruptured intracranial aneurysms are completely asymptomatic. Prodromal symptoms occasionally suggest the site of the enlarging unruptured aneurysm. For example, cranial nerve III palsy with loss of ipsilateral light reflex and focal pain above or behind the eye suggests an expanding aneurysm at the junction of the posterior communicating artery and internal carotid artery.\n\nRuptured aneurysms cause an excruciating thunderclap headache followed by a transient loss of consciousness.\n\nNon-contrast CT scan is the investigation of choice for SAH. LP shows xanthochromatic CSF.\n\nTreatment involves clipping or coiling the aneurysm to eliminate the risk of rebleeding.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7c6a04685bcb4f1d96afbb6526dff508.PNG"],"t":"Brainstem Syndromes And Hemorrhagic Stroke","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following structures is most likely to be compressed by an aneurysm of the posterior communicating artery?","o":["Oculomotor nerve","Optic nerve","Trochlear nerve","Abducens nerve"],"a":0,"e":"Aneurysm of the posterior communicating artery will most likely compress the oculomotor nerve. This is because the posterior communicating artery is anatomically located very close to the oculomotor nerve.\n\nPatients with an aneurysm of the posterior communicating artery can present with symptoms of oculomotor nerve palsy such as:\n\n• Unilateral ptosis (LPS)\n\n• Outward eye deviation (MR)\n\n• Dilated pupil.\n\nThe nerves controlling the pupils are located very superficially and these are always affected due to the mass effect of the aneurysm. Therefore the pupil sparing third nerve palsies are unlikely to be due to an aneurysm.\n\nPupil-sparing oculomotor nerve palsy is often assumed to be caused by ischemic injuries such as hypertension and diabetes mellitus.\n\nExpanding unruptured aneurysms can occasionally lead to prodromal symptoms which suggest the location of the aneurysm.","t":"Brainstem Syndromes And Hemorrhagic Stroke","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"At which of the following locations does a Berry aneurysm have the highest risk of rupture?","o":["Origin of posterior communicating artery","Origin of anterior cerebral artery","Middle cerebral artery","Anterior communicating artery"],"a":0,"e":"The size and site of aneurysm are important in predicting the risk of rupture. Aneurysms with the following features have a greater risk of rupture:\n\n• Size >7 mm in diameter\n\n• Located at the top of the basilar artery (i.e origin of the posterior cerebral artery)\n\n• Located near the origin of the posterior communicating artery.\n\nThe most common location of Berry aneurysm is the anterior communicating artery.","t":"Brainstem Syndromes And Hemorrhagic Stroke","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"The CT finding of a patient is shown below. What is the most common cause for this condition?","o":["Rupture of berry aneurysm","Injury to bridging dural veins","Rupture of cerebral AV malformation","Injury to middle meningeal artery"],"a":1,"e":"The given image is suggestive of a subdural hematoma and it most commonly results from injury to the bridging dural veins.\n\nSubdural hematoma refers to the haemorrhage between the dura and arachnoid mater. The image of the CT scan reveals a hyperdense crescentic lesion. Mass effect with midline displacement can also be seen.\n\nCauses of SDH are:\n\n• Trauma\n\n• Anticoagulants\n\n• Antiplatelets\n\n• Alcohol abuse\n\n• Shaken baby syndrome.\n\nNon-contrast CT is the investigation of choice. Radiographic features of subdural hemorrhage are:\n\n• Typically unilateral\n\n• Crescent-like distribution (concavo-convex) around the periphery of the brain\n\n• Not limited by sutures and can fill dural reflections\n\n• Chronic subdural hemorrhage: Crescent-shaped hypodense lesion on axial CT\n\n• Acute subdural hemorrhage: Hyperdense collections on CT","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3cf52742ee2047eb97da4bd2ea6397ea.PNG"],"t":"Brainstem Syndromes And Hemorrhagic Stroke","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"The CT scan of a patient who was brought to the casualty after a road traffic accident is shown below. Between what structures does blood accumulate in this condition?","o":["Outer table and inner table of the skull","Outer table and dura mater","Inner table and dura mater","Periosteum and aponeurotic layer of scalp"],"a":2,"e":"The given CT image is suggestive of extradural haemorrhage (EDH). In this condition, blood accumulates between the inner table of the skull and the dura mater.\n\nThe typical presentation is an initial loss of consciousness following trauma, a complete transient recovery (lucid interval), and a rapid progression of neurological deterioration.\n\nThis condition is treated by rapid surgical evacuation and ligation or cautery of the damaged vessel.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6eb53136919445c19fed5d467bbd074e.JPEG"],"t":"Brainstem Syndromes And Hemorrhagic Stroke","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements is false regarding extradural hemorrhage?","o":["CT shows crescentic opacity","Lucid interval is characteristic","Middle meningeal artery bleed is the most common source","Most common cause is trauma"],"a":0,"e":"In extradural hemorrhage (EDH), CT brain does not show a crescentric opacity. It shows a lentiform opacity.","t":"Brainstem Syndromes And Hemorrhagic Stroke","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A 50-year-old hypertensive man presented with an acute-onset headache for the past 2 hours, neck stiffness, nausea, and vomiting. He claims it is the worst headache of his life. There are no focal neurological deficits and his CT scan shows blood in the Sylvian fissure. What is the most probable diagnosis?","o":["Extradural hemorrhage","Subarachnoid hemorrhage","Subdural haemorrhage","Hypertensive bleed"],"a":1,"e":"This patient's clinical features of sudden-onset headache with neck stiffness, nausea, and vomiting with no neurological deficits is suggestive of a diagnosis of subarachnoid haemorrhage. The presence of blood in the Sylvian fissure confirms this diagnosis.\n\nTrauma is the most common cause. However, spontaneous sub-arachnoid bleeding occurs most often due to the rupture of a berry aneurysm. Most of them are found in the anterior circulation, mostly on the circle of Willis. The three most common locations are:\n\n• Terminal ICA\n\n• MCA bifurcation\n\n• Top of the basilar artery.\n\nA non-contrast CT scan is the investigation of choice. LP shows xanthochromatic CSF.\n\nTreatment involves clipping or coiling the aneurysm to eliminate the risk of rebleeding.","t":"Brainstem Syndromes And Hemorrhagic Stroke","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A 62-year-old man presents to the ER with headache, fever, and vomiting for the past 5 hours. A CT image is shown below. Physical examination shows positive Kernig’s and Brudzinski’s signs. Vitals: Temp - 101 F, BP - 120/88 mmHg, PR - 110 beats/min, pupils reactive. What is the next best step?","o":["Administer IV ceftriaxone","Administer IV tenecteplase","Craniotomy","Surgical clipping"],"a":3,"e":"The scenario is suggestive of subarachnoid hemorrhage (SAH) and the treatment of choice for this condition is surgical clipping.\n\nCT shows hyperattenuating material filling the subarachnoid space, which is suggestive of SAH. Most commonly, it occurs around the circle of Willis due to the rupture of a berry aneurysm at this location.\n\nSAH can present with fever, positive Kernig’s sign, and Brudzinski’s sign, as well as mimic meningitis or encephalitis.\n\nTreatment with the calcium channel antagonist nimodipine (60 mg PO every 4 h) improves outcome, by preventing ischemic injury.\n\nOption A: Vancomycin + ceftriaxone is the recommended therapy for bacterial meningitis. Meningitis does not show hyper-attenuated subarachnoid space.\n\nOption B: IV Tenecteplase is used for ischemic stroke without bleeding complications.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/37ddcc745fff48979226f4b960c281e9.PNG"],"t":"Brainstem Syndromes And Hemorrhagic Stroke","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following functions are likely to be impaired in patients with peripheral neuropathy?","o":["Sensory or motor function only","Sensory or autonomic function only","Motor or autonomic function only","Sensory, motor or autonomic function"],"a":3,"e":"Peripheral neuropathies can impair sensory, motor, or autonomic function, either singly or in combination.\n\nPeripheral nerves are composed of sensory, motor, and autonomic elements.\n\nPeripheral neuropathies are classified into those that primarily affect the\n\n• Cell body - neuronopathy or ganglionopathy\n\n• Myelin - myelinopathy\n\n• Axon - axonopathy","t":"Peripheral Neuropathy","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Disorders of autonomic function should be considered in patients with:\n\n1. Unexplained orthostatic hypotension\n\n2. Dysuria\n\n3. Hyperhidrosis\n\n4. Urinary incontinence\n\n5. Dementia","o":["2 and 4","3 and 5","1, 2 and 5","1, 3, and 4"],"a":3,"e":"Disorders of autonomic function (dysautonomia) is usually considered in patients with:\n\n• Unexplained orthostatic hypotension (OH)\n\n• Syncope\n\n• Sleep dysfunction\n\n• Altered sweating (hyperhidrosis or hypohidrosis)\n\n• Impotence\n\n• Constipation or other GI symptoms like bloating, nausea, vomiting of old food, diarrhea.\n\n• Bladder disorders such as urinary frequency, hesitancy, or incontinence.","t":"Peripheral Neuropathy","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A 50-year-old woman complains of numbness of her hands, generalized weakness, and easy fatiguability. She is a vegetarian. She looks very pale and her peripheral smear reveals macrocytic hypochromic anemia. If untreated, she is at risk of developing which of the following conditions?","o":["Proprioceptive loss","Stabbing pain","Temperature loss","Burning pain"],"a":0,"e":"The given clinical scenario of macrocytic anemia and a vegetarian diet is suggestive of vitamin B12 deficiency. This typically leads to large fiber neuropathy which presents with loss of proprioception and vibration.\n\nSmaller-diameter myelinated and unmyelinated fibers transmit pain and temperature sensation. Hence, small fiber neuropathies present as temperature loss or burning or stabbing pain.","t":"Peripheral Neuropathy","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Small fiber neuropathies can be seen in patients with all of the following conditions, except:","o":["Sjogren’s syndrome","HIV","Hansen's disease","Amyloidosis"],"a":0,"e":"Sjogren’s syndrome does not cause small fiber neuropathy. It causes preferential large fiber sensory neuropathy.","t":"Peripheral Neuropathy","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A 30-year-old man presents with weakness of both the lower limbs. On examination, there is a sensory loss in the lower limbs and both the proximal and distal leg muscles have reduced power. This presentation is characteristic of which of the following conditions?","o":["Acquired immune demyelinating polyneuropathies","Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis","Mononeuropathy multiplex","Diabetic plexopathy"],"a":0,"e":"The given clinical scenario suggests symmetric proximal and distal weakness with sensory loss. This pattern is characteristic of acquired immune demyelinating polyneuropathies.\n\nIt consists of:\n\n• Acute form - Acute inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy [AIDP] also known as Guillain-Barré syndrome [GBS]\n\n• Chronic form - Chronic inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy [CIDP]\n\nOption B: Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS) is a multiple degenerative motor neuron disease. It leads to muscle weakness and disability in the absence of sensory symptoms and signs.\n\nOption C: Mononeuropathy multiplex refers to multiple compressive mononeuropathies. It presents as asymmetric subacute or acute sensory and motor symptoms and signs.\n\nOption D: Polyradiculopathy or plexopathy due to diabetes mellitus presents with asymmetric weakness.","t":"Peripheral Neuropathy","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A patient presented with peripheral neuropathy with asymmetric distal weakness without sensory loss. The differential diagnosis for this condition would include all except:","o":["Motor neuron disease","Juvenile monomelic amyotrophy","Hereditary motor neuropathy","Multifocal motor neuropathy"],"a":2,"e":"Hereditary motor neuropathy does not present with asymmetric distal weakness without sensory loss. It presents with symmetric distal weakness without sensory loss.","t":"Peripheral Neuropathy","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"What is the most common type of hereditary neuropathy?","o":["Parsonage-Turner syndrome","Friedreich’s ataxia","Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease","Familial amyloid polyneuropathy"],"a":2,"e":"Charcot-Marie-Tooth (CMT) disease is the most common type of hereditary neuropathy.\n\nOther examples of rare hereditary neuropathies are Friedreich’s ataxia, familial amyloid polyneuropathy and Tangier disease.","t":"Peripheral Neuropathy","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"All of the following are associated with autonomic neuropathies, except:","o":["Diabetes","Acquired amyloidosis","Hyperthyroidism","Porphyria"],"a":2,"e":"In hyperthyroidism, excessive thyroxine causes sympathetic autonomic overactivity, not neuropathy.\n\nHypothyroidism can be associated with peripheral neuropathy, most typically carpal tunnel syndrome. Treatment is the correction of hypothyroidism.","t":"Peripheral Neuropathy","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A young woman presents with complaints of insomnia due to her feet becoming very hot and painful at night. On examination, she has multiple superficial foot ulcers, and she is unable to feel light touch and crude touch. What is your diagnosis?","o":["HSAN I","Dejerine-Roussy syndrome","Riley-Day syndrome","Charcot Marie Tooth disease"],"a":0,"e":"The given case scenario is suggestive of burning feet syndrome, seen in HSAN 1.\n\nThere are five types of hereditary sensory and autonomic neuropathies (HSAN I–V), of which the most important are HSAN I and HSAN III.\n\nHSAN I has an autosomal dominant inheritance. It presents in adolescence or adulthood as a distal small-fiber neuropathy. It causes burning feet syndrome in which patients present with:\n\n• Burning sensation of feet, especially at night\n\n• Sensory loss\n\n• Foot ulcers.\n\nOption B: Dejerine Roussy syndrome, also known as thalamic pain syndrome usually occurs after a stroke. The patient experiences burning pain over affected areas of the body.\n\nOption C: HSAN III or Riley Day syndrome presents in childhood. It presents with labile BP, decreased tearing, hyperhidrosis, reduced sensitivity to pain, and areflexia.\n\nOption D: Charcot Marie Tooth disease is the most common type of hereditary neuropathy. It is a hereditary motor and sensory neuropathy (HMSN) which presents with motor weakness and skeletal deformities.","t":"Peripheral Neuropathy","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is the most common type of neuropathy seen in diabetes?","o":["Cryptogenic sensory polyneuropathy","Mononeuropathies","Autonomic neuropathy","Distal Symmetric Sensory and Sensorimotor Polyneuropathy"],"a":3,"e":"Distal symmetric sensory and sensorimotor polyneuropathy (DSPN) is the most common form of diabetic neuropathy and is seen in approximately one-third of patients with DM.\n\nOther neuropathies seen in diabetes mellitus are:\n\n• The most common mononeuropathies are median neuropathy at the wrist and ulnar neuropathy at the elbow\n\n• Diabetic autonomic neuropathy is typically seen in combination with DSPN\n\n• Cranial neuropathy (third nerve palsy is the most common) and diabetic neuropathic cachexia\n\nThe Michigan diabetic neuropathy score is a screening tool used to assess diabetic neuropathy.","t":"Peripheral Neuropathy","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following can be prescribed as a first-line drug for a patient with painful diabetic neuropathy?","o":["Carbamazepine","Tramadol","Duloxetine","Venlafaxine"],"a":2,"e":"Duloxetine is a selective serotonin-norepinephrine reuptake inhibitor (SSNRI) and is one of the first-line drugs used in the treatment of painful diabetic neuropathy.\n\nTreatment of painful sensory neuropathies:\n\n• First-line:\n\n• Lidoderm 5% patch\n\n• Tricyclic antidepressants (e.g., amitriptyline, nortriptyline)\n\n• Gabapentin, Pregabalin\n\n• Duloxetine\n\n• Second-line:\n\n• Carbamazepine\n\n• Phenytoin\n\n• Venlafaxine\n\n• Tramadol\n\n• Third-line:\n\n• Mexiletine\n\nOptimal glucose control is considered the cornerstone for the treatment of diabetes and its complications. Intensive glucose control has been shown to prevent the development of peripheral neuropathy.","t":"Peripheral Neuropathy","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Sensorimotor neuropathy may be caused by all of the following except:","o":["Amiodarone","Lead poisoning","Arsenic poisoning","Isoniazid"],"a":1,"e":"Lead poisoning does not cause sensorimotor neuropathy. It causes symptoms and signs of primarily motor neuropathy. The most common presentation of lead poisoning is encephalopathy.\n\nThe sensation is generally preserved but the autonomic nervous system can be affected in lead poisoning.","t":"Peripheral Neuropathy","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following vitamins will you prescribe for a patient on antitubercular therapy?","o":["B9","B1","B12","B6"],"a":3,"e":"Isoniazid (INH), which is a first-line anti-tuberculosis drug, inhibits pyridoxal phosphokinase, resulting in pyridoxine deficiency and neuropathy. Prophylactic administration of vitamin B6 (pyridoxine) 10 mg/day can prevent neuropathy from developing.\n\n10 mg daily suffices for prophylaxis but patients with evidence of vitamin B6 deficiency may require up to 50 mg daily.\n\nCurrent WHO guidelines recommend routine substitution of pyridoxine to patients who are at risk of peripheral neuropathy, e.g., malnutrition, chronic alcohol dependence, diabetics, HIV.\n\nHowever, pyridoxine excess (acute intoxication or long-term use of large doses) is also found to cause peripheral neuropathy.","t":"Peripheral Neuropathy","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"During a regular health checkup, you notice the following nail finding in a 64-year-old man. He lives near a smelting industry on the banks of a river. Which of the following conditions can be seen in this patient?","o":["Pure motor neuropathy","Pure sensory neuropathy","Predominant sensory neuropathy","Sensorimotor neuropathy"],"a":3,"e":"The image shows Mee’s lines, transverse white striae of the fingernails. This along with history is suggestive of chronic arsenic poisoning probably due to a contaminated water source. Arsenic poisoning causes toxic sensorimotor polyneuropathy.\n\nThe neuropathy manifests 5–10 days after ingestion of arsenic and progresses for several weeks, mimicking Guillain–Barre's Syndrome.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a36d1acdbfb24f6fada896c347f7c18a.JPEG"],"t":"Peripheral Neuropathy","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is the most common antecedent infectious cause of Guillain-Barre syndrome?","o":["Cytomegalovirus","Mycoplasma pneumoniae","Campylobacter jejuni","Epstein Barr virus"],"a":2,"e":"Campylobacter jejuni is one of the most common risk factors for GBS.\n\nInfections that may trigger GBS are:\n\n• Campylobacter jejuni\n\n• Cytomegalovirus (CMV)\n\n• Epstein Barr virus\n\n• Mycoplasma\n\n• Varicella zoster virus\n\n• Human immunodeficiency virus (HIV), dengue, or influenza, Zika, hepatitis\n\nLess commonly vaccinations, surgical procedures, and trauma have been reported to trigger the development of GBS.","t":"Guillain Barre Syndrome And Other Immune Mediated Neuropathies","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following autoantibody isotype is involved in the pathogenesis of Guillian-Barre syndrome?","o":["IgA","IgG","IgM","IgE"],"a":1,"e":"IgG isotype of autoantibody is involved in the pathogenesis of GBS.\n\nIgM isotype autoantibodies are known to play a role in some types of chronic immune neuropathies.","t":"Guillain Barre Syndrome And Other Immune Mediated Neuropathies","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"In Guillain-Barre syndrome due to antecedent infection with Campylobacter jejuni, the autoantibodies are most commonly directed against which of the following?","o":["GM1","GD1a","GD1b","GalNac-GD1a"],"a":0,"e":"Autoantibodies in GBS due to Campylobacter jejuni infection are directed most commonly against GM1 gangliosidase.","t":"Guillain Barre Syndrome And Other Immune Mediated Neuropathies","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which type of Guillain-Barre syndrome most commonly affects patients in the pediatric age group?","o":["Acute motor axonal neuropathy","Acute inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy","Acute motor sensory axonal neuropathy","Miller Fisher syndrome"],"a":0,"e":"The acute motor axonal neuropathy (AMAN) subtype of Guillain-Barre syndrome (GBS) is more common in pediatric age groups.\n\nType\nAge group affected\n\nAcute inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy (AIDP)\nAdults\n\nAcute motor axonal neuropathy (AMAN)\n\nChildren\n\nYoung adults\n\nAcute motor-sensory axonal neuropathy (AMSAN)\nMostly adults\n\nMiller–Fisher syndrome\n\nAdults\n\nChildren","t":"Guillain Barre Syndrome And Other Immune Mediated Neuropathies","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Acute inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy differs from acute motor axonal neuropathy, in that, in the latter, the complement deposition along with IgG antibodies occurs at:","o":["Nodes of Ranvier","Neuromuscular junction","Oligodendrocytes","Schwann cells"],"a":0,"e":"In AMAN, the complement along with IgG is deposited at the nodes of Ranvier of large motor axons.\n\nIn AIDP, complement deposits along the outer surface of the Schwann cell.","t":"Guillain Barre Syndrome And Other Immune Mediated Neuropathies","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following features would you not expect to see in a patient with Guillian-Barre syndrome?","o":["Absent reflexes in lower limb","Ascending paralysis","Early bladder dysfunction","Bulbar paralysis"],"a":2,"e":"Bladder dysfunction is not usually severe and does not appear early in the course of Guillain-Barre Syndrome(GBS). If bladder dysfunction is a prominent feature and comes early in the course, diagnostic possibilities other than GBS should be considered.\n\nGBS is an acute demyelinating polyradiculoneuropathy. It is an autoimmune disorder triggered by infections like:\n\n• Campylobacter jejuni\n\n• Cytomegalovirus (CMV)\n\n• Epstein Barr virus\n\n• Mycoplasma\n\n• Varicella zoster virus\n\n• Human immunodeficiency virus (HIV), dengue, or influenza\n\nIt manifests as a rapidly evolving areflexic motor paralysis, with or without sensory disturbance. The usual pattern is ascending paralysis. Proximal and distal muscles are equally involved. The lower cranial nerves are frequently affected, causing bulbar weakness.","t":"Guillain Barre Syndrome And Other Immune Mediated Neuropathies","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following sensory deficits is severely affected in a patient with Guillain-Barre syndrome?","o":["Pain","Temperature","Proprioception","Touch"],"a":2,"e":"Loss of proprioception is severe in Guillain-Barre syndrome (GBS).\n\nGBS affects the large sensory fibers, leading to attenuation or loss of deep tendon reflexes and loss of proprioception.\n\nCutaneous sensory deficits (e.g., loss of pain and temperature sensation) are usually relatively mild.","t":"Guillain Barre Syndrome And Other Immune Mediated Neuropathies","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A patient presents with complaints of lower extremity weakness. He initially noticed difficulty climbing the stairs and now he is unable to stand. He gives a history of bloody diarrhea a few weeks ago. Which of the following parameters are expected to be elevated in the CSF of this patient?","o":["Lymphocytes","Neutrophils","Opening pressure","Proteins"],"a":3,"e":"The given scenario is suggestive of Guillain-Barre syndrome (GBS). Protein levels are elevated (100-1000mg/dl) in the CSF of a patient with this condition.\n\nThe CSF cell counts are usually normal. Albuminocytologic dissociation, which is an elevation in CSF protein without an elevation in white blood cells, is characteristic in GBS.\n\nCauses of albuminocytologic dissociation are:\n\n• Guillain-Barre Syndrome\n\n• Spinal cord tumours","t":"Guillain Barre Syndrome And Other Immune Mediated Neuropathies","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following conditions does not present with autonomic dysfunction?","o":["Amyloidosis","Guillain-Barré syndrome","Botulism","Subclavian steal syndrome"],"a":3,"e":"Subclavian steal syndrome does not present with autonomic dysfunction.\n\nOption A: Polyneuropathy is seen in sporadic and familial amyloidosis. In some cases, autonomic insufficiency presents before distal painful polyneuropathy accompanied by sensory loss\n\nOption B: Guillain-Barré syndrome is associated with autonomic instability. This can present with BP fluctuations, arrhythmias, and GI disturbances.\n\nOption C: Botulinum toxin binds presynaptically to cholinergic nerve terminals. After uptake into the cytoplasm, it blocks ACh release. Thus, patients with botulism present with motor paralysis and autonomic disturbances.","t":"Guillain Barre Syndrome And Other Immune Mediated Neuropathies","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A 23-year-old patient presents with rapidly progressing symmetric ascending paralysis since 2 days. Nerve conduction studies are consistent with demyelination. He is found to have an elevated protein level in his CSF. Which of the following autonomic disturbance is likely to be seen in this patient?","o":["Postural hypotension","Urinary incontinence","Diarrhea","Increased sweating"],"a":0,"e":"The given scenario is suggestive of Guillian-Barre syndrome (GBS). Postural hypotension is a common autonomic disturbance seen in this condition.\n\nAutonomic involvement is common and may occur even in patients whose GBS is otherwise mild.\n\nThe usual autonomic manifestations are:\n\n• Loss of vasomotor control with wide fluctuation in blood pressure, postural hypotension\n\n• Cardiac dysrhythmias","t":"Guillain Barre Syndrome And Other Immune Mediated Neuropathies","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"The triad of Miller-Fisher syndrome includes all of the following, except:","o":["Ataxia","Weakness","Areflexia","Ophthalmoplegia"],"a":1,"e":"Weakness is not seen in the triad of Miller-Fisher syndrome (MFS).\n\nMiller Fisher syndrome is a variant of Guillain-Barré syndrome(GBS). Like Guillain-Barré syndrome, symptoms may be preceded by a viral illness. It classically presents as a triad of:\n\n• Ataxia\n\n• Areflexia\n\n• Ophthalmoplegia\n\nThe condition is usually not associated with weakness, and even if present, is very mild. It is strongly associated with antibodies to the ganglioside GQ1b.\n\nThe prognosis in Miller Fisher syndrome is usually good with recovery beginning within 2 to 4 weeks of the symptom onset, and completing by 6 months. Some individuals are left with residual deficits. Relapses are rare.\n\nTreatment for Miller Fisher syndrome is identical to treatment for Guillain-Barré syndrome: intravenous immunoglobulin (IVIg) or plasmapheresis and supportive care.","t":"Guillain Barre Syndrome And Other Immune Mediated Neuropathies","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A patient presented to the ER with tingling, numbness, and weakness in all four limbs. She says that the weakness started in the lower limbs and progressed to the upper limbs within a day. History is significant for a recent COVID-19 infection and surgery for cervical spondylosis and disc herniation 3 years ago. CT brain was normal and an MRI of the lumbar spine showed foraminal narrowing at L2-L3. CSF analysis showed increased protein levels. Which of the following is recommended for the initial therapy of this patient?","o":["Intrathecal steroids","Remdesivir","IV Immunoglobulin","Plasmapheresis"],"a":2,"e":"The given scenario is suggestive of Guillain-Barre syndrome(GBS) probably as a result of COVID-19. IV Immunoglobulin is the recommended initial therapy for patients with GBS.\n\nHigh-dose intravenous immune globulin (IVIg) and plasmapheresis are both equally effective in GBS and can be used as the initial therapy. However, IVIg is often the initial therapy chosen because of its ease of administration and good safety record. It is administered as five daily infusions for a total dose of 2 g/kg body weight. Glucocorticoids have not been found to be effective in GBS.","t":"Guillain Barre Syndrome And Other Immune Mediated Neuropathies","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"In a patient with Guillain-Barre syndrome, chronic inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy is suspected when _______","o":["GBS deteriorates &gt;5 weeks after onset or relapses at least three times","GBS deteriorates &gt;5 weeks after onset or relapses at least two times","GBS deteriorates &gt;9 weeks after onset or relapses at least three times","GBS deteriorates &gt;8 weeks after onset or relapses at least two times"],"a":2,"e":"Chronic inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy (CIDP) should be suspected when GBS deteriorates >9 weeks after onset or relapses at least three times.\n\nOnset is usually gradual over a few months or longer, but in a few cases, the initial attack is indistinguishable from that of GBS.\n\nCIDP is distinguished from GBS by its chronic course. In other respects, this neuropathy shares many features with the common demyelinating form of GBS, including elevated CSF protein levels and the electrodiagnostic studies findings of acquired demyelination.","t":"Guillain Barre Syndrome And Other Immune Mediated Neuropathies","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following conditions are not associated with chronic inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy?","o":["Diabetic mellitus","Systemic lupus erythematosus","Lymphoma","Hepatitis A infection"],"a":3,"e":"Hepatitis A infection is not associated with CIDP.\n\nDiseases associated with CIDP are:\n\n• Hepatitis B\n\n• Hepatitis C\n\n• Lymphoma\n\n• HIV\n\n• Diabetic mellitus\n\n• Systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE)\n\n• Pregnancy\n\n• Paraneoplastic syndrome\n\n• Inflammatory bowel disease\n\n• Amyloidosis","t":"Guillain Barre Syndrome And Other Immune Mediated Neuropathies","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"All are true about chronic inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy, except:","o":["Symptoms present for &gt; 8 weeks duration","Relapse at least 3 times in a patient with Guillain-Barré syndrome","Symmetrical weakness","Small nerve fibers are affected more than large fibers"],"a":3,"e":"Large nerve fibers are affected more than small fibers in CIDP.\n\nDifference between CIDP and Guillain-barré syndrome(GBS):\n\nGBS\nCIDP\n\nDuration\n<4 weeks\n>8 weeks\n\nMotor symptoms\nAffected\nAffected\n\nSensory symptoms\nMild\n\nMore significant\n\nLargefiber > small fiber\n\nAutonomic nervous system\nAffected\nRare\n\nAntecedent event\nPresent\nRare\n\nDeep tendon reflex\nAbsent\nAbsent\n\nSteroid\nNo role\n2nd line of treatment","t":"Guillain Barre Syndrome And Other Immune Mediated Neuropathies","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A patient with chronic inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy is undergoing nerve conduction study. Which of the following findings is least likely to be seen in this patient?","o":["Low amplitude","Decreased conduction velocity","Increased distal latency","Conduction block"],"a":0,"e":"In CIDP, the amplitude is normal since the axons are intact.\n\nNerve conduction study (NCS) in axonal injury and demyelinating diseases:\n\nAxonal injury\nDemyelination\n\nAmplitude\nDecreased\nNormal\n\nDistal latency\nNormal\nProlonged\n\nConduction velocity\nNormal\nSlow\n\nConduction block\nAbsent\nPresent\n\nTemporal dispersion\nAbsent\nPresent\n\nH wave\nNormal or absent\nProlonged or absent\n\nF wave\nNormal or absent\nProlonged or absent","t":"Guillain Barre Syndrome And Other Immune Mediated Neuropathies","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"In which of the following situations is nerve biopsy not indicated in patients with chronic inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy?","o":["When the diagnosis not clear","When other causes of polyneuropathy cannot be ruled out","When electromyography shows axonal involvement","When there is no clinical or electrophysiological evidence of nerve functionality"],"a":3,"e":"Nerve biopsy should not be done if there is no clinical or electrophysiological evidence of nerve functionality of that nerve in CIDP patients. This is because completely degenerated nerves usually convey limited information.\n\nIn CIDP, nerve biopsy is indicated when:\n\n• Diagnosis is not clear\n\n• Other causes of polyneuropathy cannot be ruled out\n\n• Electromyography (EMG) shows axonal involvement","t":"Guillain Barre Syndrome And Other Immune Mediated Neuropathies","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"All of the following can be used for the treatment of a patient with chronic inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy, except:","o":["IV Immunoglobulin","Plasmapheresis","NSAIDs","Glucocorticoids"],"a":2,"e":"NSAIDs are not used in the treatment of CIDP.\n\nThe treatment of chronic inflammatory demyelinating polyneuropathy (CIDP) includes high-dose IV immunoglobulin, plasmapheresis, and glucocorticoids.\n\nInitial therapy is usually with IV immunoglobulin. If all the first-line agents fail, immunosuppressants are attempted either alone or as an adjunctive treatment.","t":"Guillain Barre Syndrome And Other Immune Mediated Neuropathies","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A 63-year-old man presented with complaints of fatiguability and chronic back pain. On evaluation, he was found to have anemia and hypercalcemia. The X-ray of his skull is given below. Which of the following statements is true with regard to neuropathy seen in this condition?","o":["Polyneuropathy is seen in 50% of patients presenting with osteoporotic bone lesions","Polyneuropathy is seen in 5% of patients presenting with osteosclerotic features","The pattern is sensorimotor","Treatment may reverse severe neuropathy"],"a":2,"e":"The given scenario is suggestive of multiple myeloma and the pattern of polyneuropathy seen in this condition is sensorimotor type.\n\nMultiple myeloma-associated polyneuropathies occur in ~5% of patients with the commonly encountered type of multiple myeloma.\n\nMyeloma with osteosclerotic features, although representing only 3% of all myelomas, is associated with polyneuropathy in one-half of cases.\n\nPolyneuropathy generally does not reverse even with successful suppression of the myeloma.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c636aac01984449e93116cc7a831604b.PNG"],"t":"Guillain Barre Syndrome And Other Immune Mediated Neuropathies","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"What is the most common pattern of peripheral neuropathy in patients with polyarteritis nodosa?","o":["Pure motor neuropathy","Pure sensory neuropathy","Symmetric motor-sensory neuropathy","Mononeuropathy multiplex"],"a":3,"e":"Most common pattern of peripheral neuropathy in polyarteritis nodosa (PAN) is mononeuritis multiplex (multifocal motor-sensory neuropathy). It is asymmetric, due to ischemic lesions of nerve trunks and roots.\n\nSome cases present as a distal, symmetric sensorimotor polyneuropathy. Small- to medium-sized arteries of the vasa nervorum, particularly the epineural vessels, are affected in PAN, resulting in a widespread ischemic neuropathy.\n\nCombination therapy with glucocorticoids and rituximab is increasingly recommended as the standard initial treatment, particularly for ANCA-associated vasculitis.","t":"Guillain Barre Syndrome And Other Immune Mediated Neuropathies","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A patient has sensory loss just below the circumferential level of the umbilicus. This corresponds to a lesion at which spinal cord segment?","o":["T10","T11","T8","T12"],"a":0,"e":"The sensory loss just below the circumferential level of the umbilicus corresponds to the lesion at the T10 spinal cord segment. It indicates the involvement of the cord adjacent to T7-T8 vertebral bodies.\n\nThe spinal cord has 31 segments, but they do not always correspond to the same vertebral body.\n\nThe first seven pairs of cervical spinal nerves exit above the same-numbered vertebral bodies. All the subsequent nerves exit below the same-numbered vertebral bodies. This is because of the presence of eight cervical spinal cord segments but only seven cervical vertebrae.\n\nThe lower spinal nerves take an increasingly downward course to exit via intervertebral foramina.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2c70b675ae5a4c96b95d0028c379e834x628x600.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1d14b570712a4e70b99f59420373df9ax1280x1495.JPEG"],"t":"Diseases Of The Spinal Cord","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A patient presents with quadriplegia and difficulty in breathing. This presentation can be seen in spinal cord lesions at what level?","o":["C1 – C4","C5 – C6","C7","C8"],"a":0,"e":"Quadriplegia and weakness of the diaphragm (manifesting as difficulty in breathing) are associated with spinal cord lesion at the level of C1-C4 spinal segments.\n\nDiaphragm is supplied by the phrenic nerve arising from C3 - C5.\n\nThe uppermost level of weakness and reflex loss with lesions:\n\n• At C5-C6 : Biceps\n\n• At C7: Finger, wrist extensors, and triceps\n\n• At C8 : Finger and wrist flexion\n\nNote: Horner’s syndrome (characterized by miosis, ptosis, and facial hypohidrosis) may accompany a cervical cord lesion at any level.","t":"Diseases Of The Spinal Cord","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Beevor’s sign is noted in a patient who has spinal cord injury. At what level is the injury likely to be?","o":["T7 – T8","T8 – T9","T10 – T12","T6 – T7"],"a":2,"e":"The Beevor’s sign localizes the spinal cord injury at T10-T12.\n\nBeevor’s sign is the abnormal upward movement of the umbilicus on abdominal wall contraction in the supine position. This is because a lesion in the T10-T12 level of the spinal cord paralyzes the lower abdominal muscles but the upper abdominal muscles are normally resulting in the upward movement of the umbilicus.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/7e13e6404920426d8e054fc7bb346b62x510x498.GIF"],"t":"Diseases Of The Spinal Cord","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A patient has a spinal cord lesion at the level of L2 – L4 spinal nerve roots. All of the following may be seen in this patient, except:","o":["Paralysis of hip flexion and adduction","Paralysis of knee flexion","Weakened knee extension","Absence of the patellar reflex"],"a":1,"e":"Lesions at L2 – L4 spinal nerve roots do not cause paralysis of the knee flexors.\n\nThe paralysis of the flexion at the knee occurs due to the lesion at L5 – S1.","t":"Diseases Of The Spinal Cord","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following features would you expect to see in a patient with conus medullaris syndrome?\n\n1. Bilateral saddle anesthesia\n\n2. Bladder and bowel dysfunction\n\n3. Normal sexual function\n\n4. Absence of anal reflex\n\n5. Absence of patellar reflex","o":["1, 2, and 4 only","1, 4, and 5 only","1, 3, and 5 only","2,3, and 4 only"],"a":0,"e":"The clinical features of the conus medullaris syndrome include:\n\n• Bilateral saddle anesthesia\n\n• Bladder and bowel dysfunction\n\n• Impotence\n\n• Absence of bulbocavernosus (S2-S4) and anal reflexes (S4-S5)\n\nThe conus medullaris is the tapered caudal termination of the spinal cord, comprising all the sacral and single coccygeal segments. Damage at this level of the cord, often occurs due to injuries to the lumbar vertebra, result in conus medullaris syndrome. Muscle strength is largely preserved in this condition.","t":"Diseases Of The Spinal Cord","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following conditions will you suspect in a patient who has bladder dysfunction early in the progression of the disease?","o":["Cauda equina syndrome","Conus medullaris syndrome","Guillian–Barre syndrome","Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis"],"a":1,"e":"Early loss of bladder control is a feature of the conus medullaris syndrome.\n\nIt is characterized by urinary retention and atonic anal sphincter, which cause overflow urinary incontinence and fecal incontinence, respectively.\n\nOption A: In the cauda equina syndrome, there is relative sparing of bladder control.\n\nOptions C and D: Bladder dysfunction is uncommon in Guillian–Barre syndrome and ALS.","t":"Diseases Of The Spinal Cord","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following features will help you differentiate conus medullaris syndrome from cauda equina syndrome?","o":["Radicular pain","Lower motor neuron injury","Symmetric weakness of lower limbs","Urinary retention"],"a":2,"e":"Symmetric weakness of lower limbs helps in a diagnosis of conus medullaris syndrome.\n\nThere is an asymmetric motor loss in cauda equina syndrome.\n\nMass lesions in the lower spinal canal often produce a mixed clinical picture with elements of both cauda equina and conus medullaris syndromes.","t":"Diseases Of The Spinal Cord","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A patient is diagnosed with Brown–Sequard syndrome with a lesion on the right side. Which of the following sensations on the right side will be affected?","o":["Pain","Crude touch","Proprioception","Temperature"],"a":2,"e":"Proprioception is the type of sensation lost on the same side (ipsilateral) in Brown–Sequard syndrome. This is due to a lesion of the posterior column.\n\nThe sensations of crude touch, pain and temperature are lost on the opposite side, due to the lesions of anterior column or spinothalamic tract.","t":"Diseases Of The Spinal Cord","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A patient was brought to the casualty after he was hit by a plastic pipe on the back by a bully. On examination, the patient was found to have marked upper extremity weakness compared to the lower extremities. He also had a loss of pain and temperature sensations over the shoulders, lower neck, and upper trunk. Other sensations were intact in this region. What is the likely diagnosis?","o":["Brown-Sequard syndrome","Central cord syndrome","Anterior spinal artery syndrome","Foramen magnum syndrome"],"a":1,"e":"The given scenario is suggestive of cape distribution of sensory deficit is seen in central cord syndrome.\n\nThis condition affects the cervical cord producing arm weakness > leg weakness. A dissociated sensory loss i.e. loss of pain and temperature with preservation of light touch, joint position, and vibration sensations is seen over the shoulders, lower neck, and upper trunk (cape distribution).\n\nIt results from selective damage to the gray matter nerve cells and the decussating spinothalamic tracts surrounding the central canal. These decussating fibers carry crude touch, pain, and temperature sensations.\n\nSpinal trauma, syringomyelia, and intrinsic cord tumors are the main causes.\n\nOption A: In Brown–Sequard Syndrome, ipsilateral weakness and loss of joint position and vibratory sense, with contralateral loss of pain and temperature sense, one or two levels below the lesion is seen.\n\nOption C: Anterior spinal artery syndrome is caused due to an ASA infarct. It typically presents as loss of motor function and pain/temperature sensation, with relative sparing of proprioception and vibratory sense below the level of the lesion.\n\nOption D: In foramen magnum syndrome, compressive lesions near the foramen magnum may produce weakness of the ipsilateral shoulder and arm followed by weakness of the ipsilateral leg, then the contralateral leg, and finally the contralateral arm. This is referred to as 'around the clock' pattern.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/630f2145c3d8493cb318ddb82937b538x536x938.PNG"],"t":"Diseases Of The Spinal Cord","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"The MRI spine of a patient is shown below. What is the characteristic deficit seen in this condition?","o":["Pyramidal pattern of weakness","Around the clock pattern of weakness","Suspended distribution of sensory deficit","Segmental pattern of weakness"],"a":2,"e":"The MRI shows a syrinx located at C6–C7 in the cervical spine which is suggestive of syringomyelia. Suspended distribution of sensory deficit is a feature of this condition.\n\nSyringomyelia is a developmental cavity of the cervical cord that may enlarge and produce progressive myelopathy or may remain asymptomatic.\n\nIt presents as a central cord syndrome with unilateral sensory loss in the hands initially. There is a loss of pain and temperature sensation with the sparing of touch and vibration. The sensory deficit has a distribution that is 'suspended' over the nape of the neck, shoulders, and upper arms (cape distribution) or in the hands. Areflexic weakness is seen in the upper limbs.\n\nExtension of the syrinx into the grey matter of the cord causes muscle wasting in the lower neck, shoulders, arms, and hands with asymmetric or absent reflexes in the arms. Extension of the syrinx into the medulla (syringobulbia) causes palatal or vocal cord paralysis, dysarthria, horizontal or vertical nystagmus, episodic dizziness or vertigo, and tongue weakness with atrophy.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/110b729992794f26afdbfac56bdace28.PNG"],"t":"Diseases Of The Spinal Cord","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A patient presents with quadriparesis. He says that it started in his left arm, then progressed to the left leg, and then later to the right leg and arm. What is the likely diagnosis?","o":["Extramedullary tumor","Central cord syndrome","Anterior cord syndrome","Foramen magnum syndrome"],"a":3,"e":"The given scenario is suggestive of an 'around-the-clock' pattern of weakness, which is seen in foramen magnum syndrome.\n\nAround the clock pattern of weakness is described as a sequential weakness of the ipsilateral shoulder and arm followed by weakness of the ipsilateral leg, then the contralateral leg, and finally the contralateral arm. The weakness may begin in any of the limbs.\n\nThe lesions in the foramen magnum area interrupt decussating pyramidal tract fibers destined for the legs, resulting in weakness of the legs. There is typically suboccipital pain spreading to the neck and shoulders in foramen magnum syndrome.","t":"Diseases Of The Spinal Cord","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Anterior spinal artery thrombosis is characterized by all of the following, except:","o":["Loss of pain and touch","Loss of vibration sense","Loss of power in the lower limbs","Sphincter dysfunction"],"a":1,"e":"Anterior spinal artery thrombosis does not lead to loss of vibration sense. Vibration sense is carried by the posterior column.\n\nAnterior spinal artery syndrome or anterior cord syndrome is the result of occlusion or diminished flow in the anterior spinal artery. There is bilateral tissue destruction at several contiguous levels that spares the posterior columns.\n\nAll spinal cord functions (motor, sensory, and autonomic) are lost below the level of the lesion, except vibration and position sensation.","t":"Diseases Of The Spinal Cord","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A patient with neurofibromatosis 1 is found to have multiple tumors above the level of the lumbar spine. Which of the following is an early feature of dysautonomia in this patient?","o":["Large bladder volumes","Flaccid bladder","Overflow incontinence","Spastic bladder"],"a":3,"e":"The early feature of dysautonomia that can occur in this patient is spastic bladder.\n\nDysautonomia due to brain and spinal cord disease above the level of the lumbar spine results first in increased urinary frequency and small bladder volume. Eventually, incontinence occurs. These features are suggestive of an upper motor neuron lesion, i.e., spastic bladder.\n\nOn the other hand, dysautonomia due to peripheral nervous system (PNS) disease of autonomic nerve fibers results in large bladder volumes, increased urinary frequency, and overflow incontinence. These are features suggestive of a lower motor neuron lesion, i.e., flaccid bladder.\n\nPostvoidal residual volume helps to distinguish between upper and lower motor neuron bladder dysfunction in the early stages of dysautonomia.","t":"Diseases Of The Spinal Cord","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following autonomic disorders do not involve the spinal cord?","o":["Syringomyelia","Syringobulbia","Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis","Multiple sclerosis"],"a":1,"e":"Syringobulbia is a condition that affects the brainstem, not the spinal cord. It is a neurological disorder characterized by a fluid-filled cavity (syrinx) which affects the brainstem (medulla).\n\nThe following autonomic disorders are associated with spinal cord involvement:\n\n• Syringomyelia\n\n• Multiple sclerosis and neuromyelitis optica\n\n• Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS)\n\n• Traumatic quadriplegia\n\n• Subacute combined degeneration\n\n• Tetanus\n\n• Stiff-person syndrome\n\n• Spinal cord tumors","t":"Diseases Of The Spinal Cord","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following features would you not expect to see in a patient with an extramedullary tumor in the spinal cord?","o":["Abnormal CSF","Sacral sparing","Spastic weakness of lower limbs","Radicular pain"],"a":1,"e":"Sacral sparing is not a feature of extramedullary tumors, it is seen in intramedullary tumors. Differences between extramedullary and intramedullary syndromes are:\n\nExtramedullary\nIntramedullary\n\nRadicular pain\nPoorly localized burning pain\n\nEarly sacral sensory loss\nSacral sparing\n\nIncontinence due to the superficial location of the corresponding sensory and motor fibers in the spinothalamic and corticospinal tracts\nCorticospinal tract signs appear later\n\nExtramedullary tumors lie outside the cord and compress the spinal cord or its vascular supply. Intramedullary tumors arise within the substance of the cord.\n\nThe spinothalamic tract has a laminated configuration with the sacral fibers outermost, and hence, there is an early sacral sensory loss in extramedullary tumors.","t":"Diseases Of The Spinal Cord","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not a cause of subacute combined degeneration?","o":["Pernicious anemia","Partial gastrectomy","High consumption of refined rice","Diphyllobothrium latum infestation"],"a":2,"e":"Subacute combined degeneration (SACD) is due to the deficiency of vitamin B12. Pernicious anemia, partial gastrectomy, and Diphyllobothrium latum infestation can lead to this condition. High consumption of refined rice leads to thiamine (vitamin B1) deficiency.\n\nThe most severely affected regions in SACD are the posterior columns at the cervical and upper thoracic levels.\n\nClinical features include:\n\n• Signs of both peripheral nerve and spinal cord involvement\n\n• Paresthesias in the hands or feet > Weakness and unsteadiness of gait\n\n• Loss of vibration or joint position sense in the legs\n\n• Mental slowing, depression, confusion, delusions, and hallucinations\n\nSACD is treated by a replacement regimen of intramuscular daily injections of 1000 μg of vitamin B12 for the first week, followed by weekly 1000 μg injections for 1 month, and monthly injections thereafter.","t":"Diseases Of The Spinal Cord","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Myasthenia gravis is associated with which of the following?","o":["Decreased acetylcholine release at the nerve endings","Increased acetylcholine release at the nerve endings","Increased synaptic transmission at the myoneural junction","Decreased synaptic transmission at the myoneural junction"],"a":3,"e":"Myasthenia gravis is associated with a decrease in synaptic transmission at the myoneural junction.\n\nMyasthenia gravis is a neuromuscular disorder that is characterized by weakness and fatigability of skeletal muscles. It is due to autoantibody-mediated destruction of the acetylcholine receptors (AChRs) at the neuromuscular junction. This consequentially leads to a decreased transmission at the neuromuscular (myoneural) junction.","t":"Myasthenia Gravis And Other Neuromuscular Junction Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"In myasthenia gravis, the anti-AChR antibodies reduce the number of available AChRs at the neuromuscular junction by all of the following mechanisms except:","o":["Accelerated turnover of AChRs","Damage to the postsynaptic muscle membrane","Damage to muscle-specific receptor tyrosine kinase","Blockade of the active site of the AChR"],"a":2,"e":"In myasthenia gravis, damage to muscle-specific receptor tyrosine kinase is not a mechanism by which anti-AChR antibodies reduce the number of available AChRs at the neuromuscular junction.\n\nThe anti-AChR antibodies reduce the number of available AChRs at the neuromuscular junction using three distinct mechanisms:\n\n• Accelerated turnover of AChRs\n\n• Damage to the postsynaptic muscle membrane mediated by antibodies and complement\n\n• Blockade of the active site of the AChR\n\nPatients whose sera test negative for both AChR and MuSK antibodies possess antibodies specific to another protein at the neuromuscular junction called low-density lipoprotein receptor-related protein 4 (LRP4). LRP4 plays a functional role in the clustering of AChRs.\n\nAnti-MuSK antibodies are directed against muscle-specific receptor tyrosine kinase (MuSK), a protein involved in AChR clustering at the neuromuscular junction.","t":"Myasthenia Gravis And Other Neuromuscular Junction Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements about myasthenia gravis is false?","o":["Weakness and fatigability of muscles is present","Complete remissions are seen","Lids and extraocular muscles are involved","Proximal limb weakness is seen"],"a":1,"e":"Remissions may occur spontaneously but are usually not complete or permanent.\n\nThe cardinal clinical features of myasthenia gravis include:\n\n• Women are affected by myasthenia gravis more frequently than men, in a ratio of ~3:2.\n\n• Weakness and fatigability of muscles - Weakness may increase upon repeated use or later in the day. It may improve with rest or sleep.\n\n• Diplopia and ptosis - They are common initial clinical complaints. The cranial muscles, particularly the eyelids and the extraocular muscles, are typically involved early in the course of myasthenia gravis.\n\n• Limb weakness - It is often proximal and may be asymmetric.\n\n• Deep tendon reflexes - They are preserved despite muscle weakness.","t":"Myasthenia Gravis And Other Neuromuscular Junction Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A school teacher presents to the OPD with excessive fatigability and double vision. She says that her symptoms are aggravated towards the end of the day. Her colleagues told her that, lately, she has developed a nasal twang in her voice. Which of the following statements is true regarding this condition?","o":["Thymoma is present in more than 75% of the patients with this condition","In most of the cases the thymus is hypoplastic","Myoid cells in thymus serve as the source of autoantigens","Thymus is abnormal in 20% of patients with AChR antibody–positive status"],"a":2,"e":"The given scenario is suggestive of myasthenia gravis (MG). Myoid cells in the thymus serve as the source of autoantigens in this condition.\n\nThe muscle-like cells within the thymus called myoid cells, express AChRs on their surface. These cells are known to serve as a source of autoantigen and trigger autoimmune reactions within the thymus gland. This stimulates the production of autoantibodies, thus causing myasthenia gravis.\n\nThe thymus is abnormal in ~75% of patients with AChR antibody-positive MG. In these cases, the thymus is hyperplastic. 10% of the patients show symptoms of thymoma.","t":"Myasthenia Gravis And Other Neuromuscular Junction Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A 55-year old woman presented with shortness of breath, cough with expectoration and fever for ten days. Chest x-ray revealed mediastinal widening. CECT chest showed a well-circumscribed heterogeneous solid enhancing mass lesion. FNAC showed features in favor of thymoma. Which of the following is not associated with this condition?","o":["Myasthenia gravis","Addison's disease","Systemic lupus erythematosus","Hypergammaglobulinemia"],"a":3,"e":"Thymoma is associated with hypogammaglobulinemia, not hypergammaglobulinemia. Thymoma with hypogammaglobulinemia is also called Good’s syndrome.\n\nThe following conditions are associated with thymomas:\n\n• Myasthenia gravis (30%)\n\n• Pure red cell aplasia (5-8%)\n\n• Hypogammaglobulinemia (~5%)\n\n• Polymyositis\n\n• SLE\n\n• Thyroiditis\n\n• Sjogren's syndrome\n\n• Ulcerative colitis\n\n• Addison's disease\n\n• Pernicious anemia\n\n• Scleroderma\n\n• Panhypopituitarism\n\n• Aplastic anemia\n\n• Limbic encephalitis","t":"Myasthenia Gravis And Other Neuromuscular Junction Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A 45-year-old patient comes with complaints of progressive weakness as the day progresses. He is found to have antibodies against nicotinic acetylcholine receptors. Which is the most common ocular finding in this disease?","o":["Diplopia","Lagophthalmos","Exophthalmos","Ptosis"],"a":3,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of myasthenia gravis and ptosis is the most common ocular feature of the disease.\n\nMyasthenia gravis (MG) is an autoimmune disorder of the neuromuscular junction most commonly caused by anti-acetylcholine receptor antibodies. The disorder is characterized by weakness and fatigability of skeletal muscles where the weakness increases during repeated use and may improve following rest.\n\nPtosis and diplopia are common initial complaints due to early involvement of the lids and extraocular muscles (EOMs). Of them, Ptosis is a more common manifestation than diplopia. Ptosis in myasthenia gravis is insidious, bilateral, and frequently asymmetrical.\n\nInvestigations of myasthenia gravis include:\n\n1. Ice pack test -This tests for a transient improvement of the ptotic eyelid on placing an ice pack for 2 minutes, as the cold inhibits the breakdown of acetylcholine by acetylcholinesterase.\n\n2. Tensilon (Edrophonium) test - This tests for a transient improvement in the weakness on administration of a short-acting anticholinesterase such as edrophonium.\n\n3. Antibody testing - for Acetylcholine receptor (AChR) antibodies, MuSK protein antibodies helps support a diagnosis of myasthenia gravis.","t":"Myasthenia Gravis And Other Neuromuscular Junction Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following features would you not expect to see in patients with ocular myasthenia?","o":["Ptosis may switch from one eye to the other over time","Manually raising the more ptotic lid causes increased ptosis on the opposite side","Extraocular muscle involvement produces binocular diplopia","Pupils are affected in 50% of the cases"],"a":3,"e":"The pupils are always spared in myasthenia gravis (MG), differentiating it from other disorders marked by similar ocular symptoms (e.g. botulism)\n\nIf weakness remains restricted to the extraocular muscles for 3 years, it is likely that it will not become generalized. These patients are said to have ocular MG.\n\nPtosis and/or diplopia are the initial symptoms of MG in up to 85% of patients. The ptosis may increase with sustained upward gaze or by holding up the opposite eyelid with the examiner's finger (curtain sign).\n\nCogan lid twitch is characteristic of MG. It refers to a brief overshoot twitch of the lid when the patient is made to gaze upwards after a period of downgaze.\n\nRNS studies (repetitive nerve stimulation) and anti-AChR antibodies are often negative. Single-fiber EMG (SFEMG) testing may be required for confirming the diagnosis in seronegative patients.","t":"Myasthenia Gravis And Other Neuromuscular Junction Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following conditions is not associated with congenital myasthenic syndrome?","o":["Slow-channel congenital myasthenic syndrome","Fast-channel congenital myasthenic syndrome","Endplate acetylcholinesterase deficiency","Dysferlin deficiency"],"a":3,"e":"Dysferlin deficiency is not associated with congenital myasthenic syndrome (CMS). CMS is an uncommon cause of neuromuscular junction failure in newborns. In CMS, the immune system is unaffected.\n\nThe common syndromes associated with CMS are as follows:\n\n• Slow-channel congenital myasthenic syndrome (SCCMS)\n\n• Fast-channel congenital myasthenic syndrome (FCCMS)\n\n• Primary acetylcholine receptor deficiency\n\n• Endplate acetylcholinesterase deficiency","t":"Myasthenia Gravis And Other Neuromuscular Junction Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A 65-year-old man who is a laborer presents with progressive weakness of the legs. He says that the weakness is more in the morning and as the day progresses, it improves for a while usually. He is a known smoker. Examination reveals diminished deep tendon reflexes in the lower limbs. Which of the following statements is true regarding this condition?","o":["Depletion of presynaptic acetylcholine vesicles","No immunological involvement","Paraneoplastic syndrome seen in lung adenocarcinoma","Incremental response on repetitive nerve stimulation"],"a":3,"e":"The given scenario is suggestive of Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome (LEMS). Incremental response to repetitive nerve stimulation is a feature of this condition.\n\nLEMS is a neuromuscular junction disorder associated with the reduced acetylcholine (Ach) release from the presynaptic nerve terminals, despite normal ACh vesicle numbers.\n\nAntibodies directed against the P/Q voltage-gated calcium channel (VGCC) interfere with the normal calcium flux required for the release of acetylcholine.\n\nApproximately 50% of LEMS cases are associated with malignancy, mainly small cell lung cancer (SCLC).\n\nMost patients with LEMS present with symptoms as slowly progressive proximal muscle weakness. In LEMS, the weakness improves for a while after exertion. Diminished or absent deep tendon reflexes help differentiate between LEMS and myasthenia gravis.\n\nAn increase in the compound muscle action potential (CAMP) amplitude >100% after exercise or with high-frequency repetitive nerve stimulation (RNS) is considered to be of diagnostic significance.","t":"Myasthenia Gravis And Other Neuromuscular Junction Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"For diagnosing patients with suspected myasthenia gravis, which of the following tests has the highest sensitivity?","o":["Single-fiber electromyography","Repetitive nerve stimulation","AChR antibodies","Edrophonium test"],"a":0,"e":"Single-fiber electromyography has the highest sensitivity for the diagnosis of myasthenia gravis.\n\nThe tests used in the diagnosis of myasthenia gravis are:\n\n• Testing for autoantibodies against the acetylcholine receptor (AChR-Ab) should be performed on all patients (most specific).\n\n• If the AChR-Abs test negative, an assay for antibodies against muscle-specific receptor tyrosine kinase (MuSK) should be performed.\n\n• Single-fiber electromyography (SFEMG) simultaneously records action potentials of two muscle fibers innervated by the same motor axon. The variability in time of the second action potential relative to the first is called \"jitter\" and this is increased in myasthenia gravis.\n\n• Repetitive nerve stimulation (RNS) is considered abnormal if there is a decremental response of >10%.\n\n• The edrophonium (\"Tensilon\") test should be used for diagnosis only in those patients with obvious ptosis or ophthalmoparesis and is not confirmatory. This test is not used routinely.\n\nElectrophysiological (RNS/SFEMG) tests are used to diagnose patients affected with seronegative myasthenia gravis.","t":"Myasthenia Gravis And Other Neuromuscular Junction Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is the first line drug of choice in a patient with myasthenia gravis?","o":["Azathioprine","Prednisone","Intravenous immunoglobulin","Pyridostigmine"],"a":3,"e":"Pyridostigmine is the first-line drug of choice in the treatment of symptomatic myasthenia gravis.\n\nTreatment for myasthenia gravis involves:\n\n• Symptomatic treatment: Anticholinesterase agents – Pyridostigmine is the initial therapy of choice.\n\n• Chronic immunomodulating therapy: Glucocorticoids (prednisone) and immunosuppressive drugs (azathioprine, mycophenolate mofetil, cyclosporine) implemented in patients significantly symptomatic on pyridostigmine.\n\n• Rapid immunomodulating treatment: IVIG and therapeutic plasma exchange—Used to tide over severe illness (myasthenic crisis) and as 'bridge' therapy before surgery, or starting chronic immunomodulating treatment.\n\n• Surgical approach: Thymectomy","t":"Myasthenia Gravis And Other Neuromuscular Junction Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements is true regarding thymectomy in patients with generalized myasthenia gravis?","o":["Indicated in most AChR-Ab positive patients with generalized myasthenia gravis","Contraindicated in medication-contolled patients","Induces rapid remission from disease","Indicated exclusively in patients with thymomatous myasthenia gravis"],"a":0,"e":"Thymectomy is indicated in most AChR-Ab positive patients with generalized myasthenia gravis\n\nThymectomy is recommended for all patients aged <60 years with non-thymomatous, acetylcholine receptor (AChR) antibody-associated generalized myasthenia gravis.\n\nSignificant improvement in symptoms is expected within one year after thymectomy. It is indicated in all patients with thymoma if resection is feasible, regardless of the subtype of myasthenia gravis.","t":"Myasthenia Gravis And Other Neuromuscular Junction Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A female patient with myasthenia gravis was admitted with complaints of sore throat, fever, cough, and weakness. She had a history of repeated hospital admissions due to exacerbation of myasthenia gravis. During some of the episodes, she even required intubation. Blood culture on admission revealed MRSA infection. Which of the following drugs can be safely administered to this patient for treating the infection?","o":["Vancomycin","Clindamycin","Daptomycin","Gentamicin"],"a":2,"e":"Daptomycin can be safely administered to treat the patient's MRSA infection. This is because it is not known to unmask or worsen the symptoms of myasthenia gravis.\n\nDrugs that may unmask or worsen myasthenia gravis include:\n\n• Anesthetic agents: Neuromuscular-blocking agents\n\n• Antibiotics:\n\n• Aminoglycosides (eg., gentamicin, neomycin, tobramycin)\n\n• Clindamycin\n\n• Fluoroquinolones (eg., ciprofloxacin, levofloxacin, norfloxacin)\n\n• Ketolides (eg., telithromycin)\n\n• Vancomycin\n\n• Cardiovascular drugs:\n\n• Beta-blockers (eg., atenolol, labetalol, metoprolol, propranolol)\n\n• Procainamide\n\n• Quinidine\n\n• Other drugs:\n\n• Botulinum toxin\n\n• Chloroquine\n\n• Hydroxychloroquine\n\n• Magnesium\n\n• Penicillamine\n\n• Quinine","t":"Myasthenia Gravis And Other Neuromuscular Junction Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Patients with myasthenia gravis are resistant to anesthesia with which of the following drugs?","o":["Depolarizing agents","Non-depolarizing agents","Local anesthetics","Inhalation anesthetics"],"a":0,"e":"Patients with myasthenia gravis are resistant to neuromuscular blockade with depolarizing NMBAs (e.g., succinylcholine), possibly because they have a decreased number of acetylcholine receptors.\n\nLocal anesthetics and inhalation anesthetics are usually well-tolerated in myasthenia gravis with occasional exacerbations.","t":"Myasthenia Gravis And Other Neuromuscular Junction Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"All of the following are the drugs that are used for the treatment of patients with myasthenia gravis. Which of the following does not alter the disease process?","o":["Pyridostigmine","Prednisone","Mycophenolate mofetil","Azathioprine"],"a":0,"e":"Pyridostigmine is an anticholinesterase agent that does not alter the disease process of myasthenia gravis.\n\nAnticholinesterase agents improve muscle contractions by allowing released acetylcholine to accumulate and act on the receptors over a large area and also have the additional direct depolarizing effect on the motor endplate.\n\nThey do not alter the disease process but provide symptomatic relief. The underlying disease process continues and ultimately it may not be possible to restore muscle strength with anticholinesterase agents alone. It is due to the reduction of the receptors by the antibodies.\n\nDrugs like prednisone, mycophenolate mofetil, and azathioprine are immunosuppressive agents that reduce the antibody titers and can modify the disease process.","t":"Myasthenia Gravis And Other Neuromuscular Junction Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements is false regarding demyelinating disorders?","o":["They are immune-mediated","Commonly presents in adulthood","Multiple sclerosis is the most common type","It is caused by biochemical abnormality of myelin"],"a":3,"e":"Demyelinating disorders do not include biochemical abnormalities of myelin. Disorders occuring due to biochemical abnormalities of myelin are called dysmyelinating disorders.\n\nMultiple sclerosis is the most common immune-mediated, inflammatory demyelinating disorder. It commonly presents in adulthood.","t":"Multiple Sclerosis & Other Demyelinating Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements is true regarding multiple sclerosis?","o":["It is an acute inflammatory condition of neurons","It is an autoimmune disease affecting the central nervous system","It may also involve the peripheral nervous system","The condition is always benign and resolves spontaneously"],"a":1,"e":"Multiple sclerosis is an autoimmune disease of the central nervous system. It is characterized by chronic inflammation, demyelination, gliosis (scarring), and neuronal loss.\n\nThe peripheral nervous system is spared in MS.\n\nThe course of the disease may be relapsing-remitting or progressive. The clinical course of manifestation can be variable ranging from a benign condition to a rapidly evolving and incapacitating disease.","t":"Multiple Sclerosis & Other Demyelinating Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A patient with weakness of the limbs and reduced vision, after evaluation, was diagnosed to be a case of multiple sclerosis. Which of the following is not a risk factor for the development of this condition?","o":["Cigarette smoking","Vitamin D deficiency","Exposure to Human Herpesvirus 4","HLA-B27"],"a":3,"e":"HLA-DRB1 is a risk factor for the development of multiple sclerosis, not HLA-B27.\n\nRisk factors supporting the course of development of multiple sclerosis include:\n\n• Cigarette smoking\n\n• Vitamin D deficiency\n\n• Family history, HLA-DRB1\n\n• Exposure to Epstein-Barr virus (Human Herpesvirus 4) after early childhood","t":"Multiple Sclerosis & Other Demyelinating Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Autoantibodies in multiple sclerosis are directed against which of the following?","o":["Myelin basic protein","Both Myelin basic protein &amp; Myelin oligodendrocyte glycoprotein","Myelin oligodendrocyte glycoprotein","None of the above"],"a":1,"e":"The autoantibodies in multiple sclerosis are directed against both myelin basic protein (MBP) and myelin oligodendrocyte glycoprotein (MOG).","t":"Multiple Sclerosis & Other Demyelinating Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following processes lead to the formation of shadow plaques in patients with multiple sclerosis?","o":["Demyelination of axons in the central nervous system","Demyelination of axons in the peripheral nervous system","Remyelination of axons in the central nervous system","Remyelination of axons in the peripheral nervous system"],"a":2,"e":"Remyelination of axons in the central nervous system leads to the formation of shadow plaques.\n\nShadow plaques develop due to the partial remyelination of the naked axons by surviving oligodendrocytes or those that differentiate from precursor cells.","t":"Multiple Sclerosis & Other Demyelinating Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A 40-year-old woman with a history of multiple sclerosis comes to the hospital. She describes an electric shock–like sensation radiating down the back into the legs. What is the name of this sign?","o":["Uhthoff’s phenomenon","Lhermitte’s sign","Stransky's sign","Cornell's sign"],"a":1,"e":"An electric shock-like sensation radiating down the back into the legs is called Lhermitte's sign. It is associated with multiple sclerosis.\n\nIt is induced typically by flexion or other movements of the neck. Lhermitte’s sign can also occur simultaneously with other disorders of the cervical spinal cord like cervical spondylosis.\n\nOption A: Uhthoff ’s phenomenon refers to the unilateral visual blurring that may occur during a hot shower or with physical exercise. It is an exacerbation of the symptoms of multiple sclerosis due to heat.\n\nOption C: Stransky's sign is a clinical sign in which vigorous abduction followed by the sudden release of the little toe causes an extensor plantar reflex. It is seen in pyramidal tract lesions.\n\nOption D: Cornell's sign is a clinical sign in which scratching along the inner side of the extensor hallucis longus tendon elicits an extensor plantar reflex. It is seen in pyramidal tract lesions.","t":"Multiple Sclerosis & Other Demyelinating Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"An MRI image of a patient with multiple sclerosis is shown below. What is this sign called?","o":["Black hole lesions","Molar tooth sign","Tram-track sign","Dawson's fingers"],"a":3,"e":"The MRI image shows Dawson's fingers.\n\nDawson's fingers are demyelinating plaques through the corpus callosum, arranged at right angles along medullary veins (callososeptal location). They are a characteristic feature of multiple sclerosis and presents as T2 hyperintensities.\n\nMRI is the imaging of choice in a patient with multiple sclerosis due to its high sensitivity.\n\nOption A: Black hole lesions are multiple sclerosis plaques indicative of the chronic stage when they display T1 hypointense signals. They signify axonal destruction and irreversible damage.\n\nOption B: The molar tooth sign refers to the appearance of the midbrain in an axial section in which the elongated superior cerebellar peduncles gives the midbrain the appearance of a molar or wisdom tooth. It is seen in Joubert syndrome.\n\nOption C: Tram-track sign in the brain refers to the parallel calcification of the cortex in patients with Sturge-Weber syndrome.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a9e568c8bd8449868e23241007165c21.PNG"],"t":"Multiple Sclerosis & Other Demyelinating Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A woman presents with recurrent episodes of blurry vision, tingling and numbness in the legs, and falls. She says that during hot weather, she finds it difficult to walk and often falls. Recently she had flu, due to which the symptoms aggravated. MRI brain was done which showed Dawson's fingers. What is the criteria used for the clinical diagnosis of her condition?","o":["Kurtzke expanded disability status scale","Revised McDonald criteria","Functional status score","Hess and Hunt's scale"],"a":1,"e":"The given scenario and the MRI finding of Dawson's fingers are suggestive of multiple sclerosis. Revised McDonald’s criteria is used in the diagnosis of multiple sclerosis. It takes into consideration the clinical attacks and objective lesions. In addition, it also involves the assessment of the lesions based on MRI and other imaging techniques.\n\nThe Kurtzke expanded disability status scale (EDSS) and functional status (FS) score are widely used measures of neurologic impairment, which are performed following the confirmed diagnosis of multiple sclerosis.","t":"Multiple Sclerosis & Other Demyelinating Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"In patients with multiple sclerosis, which of the following is used to determine neurological impairment?","o":["Hess and Hunt's scale","Revised McDonald criteria","Kurtzke expanded disability status scale","Modified Well's score"],"a":2,"e":"The Expanded disability status scale (EDSS) gives a measure of neurological impairment in multiple sclerosis.\n\nEDSS scores <3.5: The patient walks normally, shows no disability\n\nEDSS scores >5.5: Progressive disease, gait impairment, and occupational disability\n\nEDSS scores =10.0: Death due to multiple sclerosis","t":"Multiple Sclerosis & Other Demyelinating Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A 45-year-old woman with multiple sclerosis presents with an exacerbation of her symptoms. Which of the following drugs should be prescribed to her for initial management?","o":["Glatiramer","Methylprednisolone","IFN-β-1a","Prednisolone"],"a":1,"e":"Glucocorticoids are used in the clinical management of either first attacks or acute exacerbations.\n\nIntravenous methylprednisolone administration at a dose of 500–1000 mg/day for 3-5 days, followed by a course of oral prednisolone beginning at a dose of 60–80 mg/day and gradually tapered over 2 weeks is given.\n\nThese medications reduce the severity and shorten the duration of attacks.\n\nDisease-modifying therapies for relapsing forms of multiple sclerosis are:\n\n• IFN-β-1a\n\n• IFN-β-1b\n\n• Glatiramer acetate\n\n• Natalizumab\n\n• Fingolimod\n\n• Dimethyl fumarate\n\n• Teriflunomide\n\n• Mitoxantrone\n\n• Alemtuzumab","t":"Multiple Sclerosis & Other Demyelinating Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following patients have Marburg’s variant of multiple sclerosis?","o":["A patient with chronic form of multiple sclerosis with no remissions","A patient with acute form of multiple sclerosis with remissions","A patient with acute form of multiple sclerosis with no remissions","A patient with chronic form of multiple sclerosis with remissions"],"a":2,"e":"Marburg’s variant is an acute form of multiple sclerosis, typically with no remissions.\n\nIt is a fulminant demyelinating process that in some cases progresses, leading to death within 1–2 years. Marburg’s variant does not seem to be associated with infection or vaccination.\n\nClinical management by high-dose glucocorticoids, plasma exchange, and cyclophosphamide have been tried, with uncertain benefit.","t":"Multiple Sclerosis & Other Demyelinating Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A patient has recurrent optic neuritis of both eyes with extensive transverse myelitis. Visual acuity in the right eye is 6/60 and visual acuity in the left eye 6/18. The patient showed a partial response to steroids. What is the likely diagnosis?","o":["Acute disseminated encephalomyelitis","Devic's disease","Riddoch syndrome","Acute fulminant multiple sclerosis"],"a":1,"e":"The given scenario is suggestive of neuromyelitis optica, which is also known as Devic's disease.\n\nIt is an aggressive inflammatory demyelinating disorder characterized by recurrent attacks of optic neuritis and myelitis. Attacks of Optic neuritis can be bilateral or unilateral and myelitis can be severe and involve three or more contiguous vertebral segments.\n\nCore clinical characteristics are:\n\n• Optic neuritis\n\n• Acute myelitis\n\n• Area postrema syndrome: episodes of otherwise unexplained hiccups or nausea or vomiting\n\n• Acute brainstem syndrome\n\n• Symptomatic narcolepsy or acute diencephalic clinical syndrome with NMOSD*-typical diencephalic MRI lesions\n\n• Symptomatic cerebral syndrome with NMOSD*-typical brain lesions\n\nOption A: Acute Disseminated Encephalomyelitis (ADEM) is a demyelinating condition most frequently associated with an antecedent infection\n\nOption C: Riddoch syndrome is the ability to consciously perceive the stimulus in a blind field. It is seen when there is a lesion in the occipital lobe.\n\nOption D: Acute fulminant multiple sclerosis is an acute episode of multiple sclerosis, known as Marburg’s variant\n\n*NMOSD stands for neuromyelitis optica spectrum disorder.","t":"Multiple Sclerosis & Other Demyelinating Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following drugs will you prescribe for a patient with an acute attack of neuromyelitis optica?","o":["Rituximab","Mycophenolate mofetil","Glucocorticoids","Azathioprine"],"a":2,"e":"Acute attacks of neuromyelitis optica are managed by high-dose glucocorticoids or plasma exchange.\n\nDrugs used for prophylaxis against relapses include:\n\n• Mycophenolate mofetil\n\n• Rituximab\n\n• Glucocorticoids + azathioprine","t":"Multiple Sclerosis & Other Demyelinating Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A child presents to the casualty with high-grade fever and altered sensorium. He is suspected to have acute disseminated encephalomyelitis. This condition is associated with which of the following?\n\n1. Measles infection\n\n2. Measles vaccination\n\n3. Rubella infection\n\n4. Rubella vaccination\n\n5. Polio vaccination","o":["2 and 4 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2, 3 and 4 only","2, 4 and 5 only"],"a":2,"e":"Acute disseminated encephalomyelitis (ADEM) is associated with measles infection, measles vaccination, rubella infection, and rubella vaccination.\n\nADEM is a demyelinating disease of the central nervous system that typically presents as a monophasic disorder associated with multifocal neurological symptoms and encephalopathy. It may be :\n\n• Post-infectious (95%) - associated infections are with varicella, measles, rubella, mumps, influenza, EBV, HHV-6, HIV, other viruses, and Mycoplasma pneumoniae.\n\n• Post-vaccination (5%) - presently it is most often associated with measles (most common), mumps, and rubella vaccination.","t":"Multiple Sclerosis & Other Demyelinating Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements is incorrect regarding acute disseminated encephalomyelitis?","o":["Cerebellar involvement is seen in post-varicella ADEM","Autoantibodies to myelin basic protein can be found","Seizures do not occur commonly","Initial treatment is with high-dose glucocorticoids."],"a":2,"e":"Seizures are common in ADEM.\n\nADEM results from a cross-reactive immune response to the infectious agent (most commonly measles virus) or vaccine which then triggers an inflammatory demyelinating response.\n\nAutoantibodies to MBP and to other myelin antigens have been detected in the CSF of some patients with ADEM.\n\nIn ADEM due to chickenpox, cerebellar involvement is often conspicuous.\n\nInitial treatment is with high-dose glucocorticoids.","t":"Multiple Sclerosis & Other Demyelinating Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"What is the most common type of headache?","o":["Medication overuse headache","Tension-type headache","Migraine without aura","Cluster headache"],"a":1,"e":"The most common type of headache is tension-type headache (TTH).\n\nThe pain of TTH can generally be managed with simple analgesics such as acetaminophen, aspirin, or NSAIDs. For chronic TTH, amitriptyline is the only proven treatment.\n\nCauses of primary headache, in decreasing order:\n\n• Tension-type headache\n\n• Migraine\n\n• Idiopathic stabbing\n\n• Exertional\n\n• Cluster\n\nCauses of secondary headache, in decreasing order:\n\n• Systemic infection\n\n• Head injury\n\n• Vascular disorders\n\n• Subarachnoid hemorrhage\n\n• Brain tumor","t":"Headache Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"What is the most common cause of secondary headache?","o":["Head injury","Systemic infection","Vascular disorders","Brain tumor"],"a":1,"e":"The most common cause of secondary headache is a systemic infection.\n\nCauses of primary headache, in decreasing order:\n\n• Tension-type headache\n\n• Migraine\n\n• Idiopathic stabbing\n\n• Exertional\n\n• Cluster\n\nCauses of secondary headache, in decreasing order:\n\n• Systemic infection\n\n• Head injury\n\n• Vascular disorders\n\n• Subarachnoid hemorrhage\n\n• Brain tumor","t":"Headache Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following case scenarios do not depict tension-type headache?","o":["A 34-year-old woman presenting with a chronic headache for &gt;15 days per month","A 41-year-old man presenting with a bilateral headache lasting for 4-6 hours","A 38-year-old man presenting with a tight band-like discomfort around the head","A 34-year-old woman presenting with a pulsatile headache associated with nausea"],"a":3,"e":"A pulsatile headache associated with nausea is not a feature of tension-type headache (TTH).\n\nTension-type headache is a chronic head-pain syndrome characterized by bilateral, tight, band-like discomfort. The pain typically builds slowly, fluctuates in severity, and may persist more or less continuously for many days. The headache may be episodic or chronic (present >15 days per month).\n\nTension headache\nMigraine\nCluster headache\n\nLocation\nBilateral\nUnilateral\nUnilateral\n\nDuration\n>30 min (typically 4-6 hr)\n4-72 hour\n15 min-3 hour\n\nFeature\n\nSteady pain\n\nPulsating pain\n\nRepetitive brief headache with excruciating periorbital pain\n\nAssociations\n\nNo photophobia or phonophobia\n\nNo aura\n\nNausea, photophobia, phonophobia present\n\nMay have aura\n\nLacrimation and rhinorrhea present\n\nMay present with Horner syndrome\n\nTreatment\n\nAnalgesics, NSAIDs, acetaminophen\n\nNSAIDs, triptans, dihydroergotamine\n\nSumatriptan, 100% oxygen\n\nProphylaxis\nAmitriptyline\n\n• Lifestyle changes(eg, sleep,exercise, diet)\n\n• β-blockers\n\n• Calcium channel blockers\n\n• Amitriptyline, topiramate, valproate\n\n• CGRP antagonists - erenumab, fremanezumab, galcanezumab, eptinezumab\n\nVerapamil","t":"Headache Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A patient comes with complaints of non-pulsatile headache of tightening quality on both sides of his forehead. He describes it as wearing a tight cap. He has no nausea, photophobia, or phonophobia. All of the following drugs can be used in the treatment of this condition except","o":["Aspirin","Sumatriptan","Acetaminophen","Naproxen"],"a":1,"e":"A bilateral, non-pulsatile headache of tightening quality without any nausea or photophobia suggests a diagnosis of tension-type headache (TTH). Triptans are not useful in the treatment of this condition. They are useful when the patient has a migraine headache.\n\nTension-type headache\nMigraine\nCluster headache\n\nTreatment\n\nAnalgesics\n\nNSAIDs\n\nAcetaminophen\n\nNSAIDs\n\nTriptans\n\nDihydroergotamine\n\n100% oxygen\n\nSumatriptan\n\nProphylaxis\nAmitriptyline\n\n• Lifestyle changes(eg, sleep,exercise, diet)\n\n• β-blockers\n\n• Calcium channel blockers\n\n• Amitriptyline, topiramate, valproate\n\n• CGRP antagonists - erenumab, fremanezumab, galcanezumab, eptinezumab\n\nVerapamil","t":"Headache Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A 41-year-old man has a 4-year history of severe left-sided excruciating episodic headaches. Most episodes are accompanied by ipsilateral lacrimation, conjunctival injection, and rhinorrhea. There are no cutaneous triggers. It does not respond to indomethacin and attacks get triggered by alcohol. What is the likely diagnosis?","o":["Trigeminal neuralgia","Cluster headache","SUNCT","Paroxysmal hemicranias"],"a":1,"e":"This patient's clinical features are suggestive of cluster headache. Patients with cluster headaches are more often smokers with a higher than average alcohol consumption.\n\nCluster headache causes severe, unilateral periorbital pain with autonomic features, such as unilateral lacrimation, nasal congestion, and conjunctival injection. Occasionally features of Horner's syndrome may be seen. The pain, though severe, is characteristically brief (30–90 minutes). There are 1-8 attacks/day lasting for 15min-3 hours.","t":"Headache Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A man presents with complaints of a recurrent left-sided stabbing headache. He has been experiencing this every day, along with a runny nose during the episodes, for the last week. These episodes last for about an hour. Examination shows the following. Which of the following is not used for the prophylaxis of this condition?","o":["Verapamil","Indomethacin","Steroids","Lithium"],"a":1,"e":"Repetitive brief, unilateral, stabbing headaches associated with lacrimation and rhinorrhea suggest a diagnosis of cluster headaches. Indomethacin is not used for the prophylaxis of these headaches.\n\nThe drug of choice used in the prophylaxis of cluster headaches is verapamil. Steroids and lithium can also be used.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/74957300bbc24d9bb5aa761937a96230.PNG"],"t":"Headache Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"What is the most common positive visual aura seen by patients during an attack of migraine?","o":["Scotoma","Zig zag lines","Transient loss of vision","Key hole vision"],"a":1,"e":"The most common positive visual aura seen in attacks of migraine is a zig-zag line in front of the eyes.\n\nThe most common negative visual aura is scotoma.\n\nThe classic migraine is associated with an aura. The aura can be:\n\n• Visual (most common):\n\n• Zigzag lines\n\n• Tunnel vision\n\n• Scotoma\n\n• Transient visual loss\n\n• Sensory:\n\n• Tingling sensation of hands, which spreads proximally","t":"Headache Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A 26-year old woman presents with a unilateral throbbing headache associated with nausea and vomiting. She has had similar episodes in the past where the headache usually lasts for 1-2 days. Bright light increases her discomfort and she prefers sitting in a dark room. Which of the following is associated with this condition?","o":["Interleukin 1","Calcitonin gene-related peptide","Leukotriene B4","Interleukin 6"],"a":1,"e":"Multiple unilateral throbbing headache associated with nausea, vomiting and photophobia suggests a diagnosis of migraine. Calcitonin gene-related peptide (CGRP) is a vasoactive neuropeptide associated with this condition. It is present at vascular terminations of the trigeminal nerve and within the trigeminal nucleus.\n\nCGRP antagonists approved for migraine prevention are:\n\n• Erenumab\n\n• Fremanezumab\n\n• Galcanezumab\n\n• Eptinezumab\n\nNote:\n\nThe FDA has recently approved Atogepant, a calcitonin gene-related peptide (CGRP) receptor antagonist for the preventive treatment of episodic migraine in adults. The recommended dosage is 10 mg, 30 mg, or 60 mg orally once daily. In severe renal impairment or end-stage renal disease it is 10 mg once daily.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/370f24afac5145039ff456b9033b06a5x600x665.JPEG"],"t":"Headache Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"In patients with familial hemiplegic migraine, which of the following is least likely to be defective?","o":["Voltage-gated calcium channel gene","Sodium potassium ATPase gene","Voltage gated sodium channel","Voltage gated chloride channel"],"a":3,"e":"Familial hemiplegic migraine (FHM) is due to defective voltage-gated calcium channel gene, sodium-potassium ATPase gene, and voltage-gated sodium channel. The voltage-gated chloride channel is not defective in this condition.\n\n Type\nMutation\n\n FHM 1\nVoltage-gated calcium channel (CACNAIA)gene\n\n FHM 2\nSodium -potassium ATPase (ATPIA2) gene\n\n FHM 3\nVoltage-gated sodium channel (SCNIA)","t":"Headache Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A 23-year-old woman experiences severe throbbing headaches on the right side of her forehead. Soon after the headache begins, she becomes very nauseous and occasionally gets dizzy. She has also vomited multiple times during these episodes and is subsequently diagnosed with severe migraine. Which among the following is the most efficacious triptan used to treat this condition?","o":["Sumatriptan","Rizatriptan","Frovotriptan","Naratriptan"],"a":1,"e":"The most efficacious triptans for moderate to severe acute migraines are rizatriptan and eletriptan.\n\nOral dosage forms of triptans are the most convenient to use. Still, they may not be practical in patients experiencing migraine-associated nausea and vomiting, for whom injectable and nasal spray formulations are helpful.\n\nStandard treatment comprises a 6-mg subcutaneous dose of sumatriptan. All triptans have similar pharmacologic properties but vary slightly in terms of clinical efficacy. Rizatriptan and eletriptan are the most efficacious of the triptans currently available.","t":"Headache Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A young woman has been suffering from 1-2 episodes of migraine every week. These episodes are affecting her quality of life. All of the following drugs can be used in the prophylaxis of migraine in this patient except","o":["Propranolol","Amitriptyline","Sumatriptan","Valproate"],"a":2,"e":"Sumatriptan is used in the treatment of an acute attack of migraine not in prophylaxis. It can be given by oral, subcutaneous, intravenous or intranasal routes.\n\nDrugs used in the prophylaxis of migraine:\n\n• Beta-blockers- propranolol\n\n• Calcium channel blocker- flunarizine (verapamil, nimodipine has no role)\n\n• Antidepressants- amitriptyline, venlafaxine (SSRIs like fluoxetine are not used)\n\n• Anticonvulsants- valproate, topiramate\n\n• 5-HT2 antagonist- pizotifen\n\n• CGRP antagonists - erenumab, framanezumab, galcanezumab, eptinezumab\n\nOther modalities of preventative treatment:\n\n• Greater occipital nerve block\n\n• Onabotulinum toxin A\n\n• Supraorbital transcutaneous stimulation","t":"Headache Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Sumatriptan can be given in which of the following patients diagnosed with migraine?","o":["A 42-year-old woman who had taken ergot alkaloids in the past one week","A 34-year-old woman presenting with a positive urine pregnancy test","A 48-year-old man with a history of cerebrovascular disease","A 51-year-old man with a history of coronary artery disease"],"a":0,"e":"Taking ergot alkaloids in the past one week is not a contraindication for sumatriptan. However, they shouldn't be given together as doing so could result in excessive blood vessel narrowing.\n\nErgots constrict arteries and triptans narrow normal heart arteries by up to 20%. For this reason, ergots should not be taken with triptans on the same day.\n\nSumatriptan is a 5HT1B/1D receptor agonist used in an acute migraine attack. It is contraindicated in the following conditions:\n\n• Cerebrovascular disease\n\n• Coronary artery disease\n\nOption B- Sumatriptan is the safest triptan in pregnancy, but its use is recommended only if the benefits outweigh the risks, and if all first line options do not provide relief.","t":"Headache Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"What is the use of MIDAS questionnaire?","o":["To assess severity of migraine","To assess disability of migraine","To help decide treatment of migraine","To assess risk of migraine"],"a":1,"e":"The Migraine Disability Assessment Score (MIDAS) questionnaire was put together to help measure the impact of migraine on patients' lives.","t":"Headache Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is used in the preventive treatment of a patient with chronic migraine?","o":["Onabotulinum toxin A","Sumatriptan","Metoclopramide","Ergotamine"],"a":0,"e":"Onabotulinum toxin A is used in the preventive treatment of patients with chronic migraine.\n\nSumatriptan, ergotamine, and metoclopramide are used in the treatment of an acute attack of migraine.\n\nDrugs used in the prophylaxis of migraine:\n\n• Beta-blockers- propranolol\n\n• Calcium channel blocker- flunarizine (verapamil, nimodipine has no role)\n\n• Antidepressants- amitriptyline, venlafaxine (SSRIs like fluoxetine are not used)\n\n• Anticonvulsants- valproate, topiramate\n\n• 5-HT2 antagonist- pizotifen\n\n• CGRP antagonists - erenumab, framanezumab, galcanezumab, eptinezumab\n\nOther modalities of preventative treatment:\n\n• Greater occipital nerve block\n\n• Onabotulinum toxin A\n\n• Supraorbital transcutaneous stimulation","t":"Headache Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not a trigeminal autonomic cephalalgia?","o":["Cluster headache","SUNA","Hemicrania continua","Nummular headache"],"a":3,"e":"Nummular headache is not a trigeminal autonomic cephalalgia. It is characterized by small circumscribed areas of continuous pain on the head.\n\nTrigeminal autonomic cephalalgia is characterized by pain along the distribution of trigeminal nerve (unilateral) and cranial autonomic symptoms. This is due to increased parasympathetic flow and decreased sympathetic flow.","t":"Headache Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"All are true about patients with trigeminal autonomic cephalgia except:","o":["Cluster headache is more common in males","SUNCT has highest attack frequency rate","Lidocaine is effective in SUNCT","Cutaneous trigger is seen in cluster headache"],"a":3,"e":"The cutaneous trigger is not seen in cluster headache. It is seen in short-lasting unilateral neuralgiform headache with conjunctival injection and tearing (SUNCT).","t":"Headache Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A patient was prescribed indomethacin for his complaint of headache and he felt relief after taking the drug. Which of the following could be a likely diagnosis?","o":["Paroxysmal hemicrania","Cluster headache","SUNCT","Trigeminal neuralgia"],"a":0,"e":"Paroxysmal hemicrania could be the likely diagnosis since it responds to indomethacin.\n\nIt is characterized by frequent unilateral, severe, short-lasting, and frequent episodes of headache. They have marked autonomic features ipsilateral to the pain.\n\nIndomethacin (25–75 mg t.i.d) can completely suppress attacks of paroxysmal hemicrania. It is also effective in:\n\n• Hemicrania continuum\n\n• Primary sexual / exercise headache\n\n• Tension-type headache","t":"Headache Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A 40-year-old male is diagnosed to have SUNCT. It can be distinguished from trigeminal neuralgia by the presence of all except:","o":["Excessive lacrimation","Nasal congestion","Conjuctival injection","Short attacks of stabbing pain"],"a":3,"e":"Short attacks of stabbing pain are common to both SUNCT and trigeminal neuralgia.\n\nSUNCT has autonomic symptoms such as lacrimation, nasal congestion, conjunctival injection, and salivation. Trigeminal neuralgia does not have autonomic symptoms.","t":"Headache Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A 42-year-old man presented with an extremely painful headache that came on suddenly without warning. It reached maximum intensity within 1 minute and lasted for 5 minutes. Which of the following would you not include as a differential?","o":["Subdural hemorrhage","Subarachnoid hemorrhage","Acute hydrocephalus","Pituitary apoplexy"],"a":0,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of a thunderclap headache. Subdural hemorrhage will not produce this type of headache.\n\nCauses of thunderclap headache:\n\n• Subarachnoid hemorrhage\n\n• Acute hydrocephalus\n\n• Pituitary apoplexy\n\n• Cerebral venous thrombosis\n\n• Hypertensive crisis","t":"Headache Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"What is the most common gene mutation seen in patients with familial Parkinson's disease?","o":["Leucine-rich repeat kinase 2","Parkin","Alpha-synuclein gene missense mutation","PTEN-induced putative kinase 1"],"a":0,"e":"The most common form of monogenic familial PD is PARK8, caused by mutations in the leucine-rich repeat kinase-2 (LRRK2) gene.\n\nFamilial forms of parkinsonism (designated PARK1 through PARK13) have been associated with causative mutations in a number of nuclear genes. A few of these are:\n\n• The LRRK2 gene mutation causes autosomal dominant PD (PARK8). In this condition, Middle to late-onset PD is seen, which resembles typical PD and is responsive to levodopa.\n\n• Mutations in alpha-synuclein (PARK 1 and PARK4) cause rare forms of familial PD and constitute the major component of Lewy bodies in sporadic PD.\n\n• Parkin-associated PD (PARK 2) & PINK1-associated PD (PARK6) are autosomal recessive seen in young-onset PD.\n\n• Mutation in the glucocerebrosidase gene (GBA) gene of Gauchers represents the most important risk factor for the development of Parkinson's disease. Patients with PD who are GBA mutation carriers have a younger age at onset.","t":"Movement Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following features would you not expect to see in patients with Parkinson’s disease?","o":["Genitourinary disturbances","Orthostatic hypotension","Exaggerated deep tendon reflexes","Micrographia"],"a":2,"e":"Exaggerated deep tendon reflexes are not a feature of Parkinson's disease. Cardinal motor features in PD include:\n\n• Resting tremors\n\n• Bradykinesia\n\n• Rigidity\n\n• Gait disturbance / postural instability\n\nMnemonic: TRAP - Tremors + Rigidity + Akinesia + Postural instability\n\n• Other motor features include:\n\n• Micrographia\n\n• Masked facies (hypomimia)\n\n• Reduced eye blinking\n\n• Soft voice (hypophonia)\n\n• Dysphagia\n\n• Non-motor features are:\n\n• Anosmia\n\n• Sensory disturbance (pain)\n\n• Mood Disorders (depression)\n\n• Sleep disturbances (rapid eye movement behavior disorder)\n\n• Autonomic:\n\n• GI disturbances\n\n• Genitourinary disturbances\n\n• Sexual dysfunction\n\n• Orthostatic hypotension\n\n• Cognitive impairment\n\nParkinson’s disease(PD) is the second most common neurodegenerative disorder after Alzheimer's disease. UK Brain Bank criteria is used for the diagnosis of PD.","t":"Movement Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A 66-year-old man presents with complaints of resting tremor in his right hand, which improves with movement. He also says that he has difficulty initiating movements and his wife says his movements have been slow. On examination, rigidity is observed to passive arm movement and shuffling gait with a stooped posture is noticed. Which of the following statements is false regarding the pathology of this condition?","o":["Substantia nigra pars reticulata is unaffected by the disease process","Decreased striatal dopamine is seen in this condition","Degeneration of dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra pars compacta","Lewy bodies which containing degenerated tau protein are seen"],"a":3,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of Parkinson's disease and Lewy bodies seen in Parkinson's disease do not contain tau protein. They contain the protein α-synuclein.\n\nPathologic hallmarks of Parkinson's disease include:\n\n• Degeneration of dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra pars compacta (SNc)\n\n• Reduced striatal dopamine\n\n• Intracytoplasmic proteinaceous inclusions called Lewy bodies, which contain the protein α-synuclein","t":"Movement Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A patient with Parkinson's disease says that he cannot even hold a coffee mug properly due to tremors. What is the frequency of the tremors likely to be?","o":["4–6","8–10","10–12","6-10"],"a":0,"e":"The frequency of resting tremors in Parkinson’s disease is 4–6 Hz.\n\nMore than 70 percent of patients with Parkinson's disease have tremors as the presenting feature. The classic parkinsonian tremor begins as a low-frequency, pill-rolling motion of the fingers, progressing to forearm pronation/supination and elbow flexion/extension. Other features of the tremors are:\n\n• Typically asymmetric\n\n• Occurs at rest\n\n• Becomes less prominent with voluntary movement\n\nFrequency of various types of tremors: Physiological tremor (12 Hz ) > Essential tremor (8–10 Hz) > Resting tremor in PD (4–6 Hz ).","t":"Movement Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"What is the most common psychiatric disturbance seen in patients with Parkinson's disease?","o":["Anxiety","Depression","Abulia","Visual hallucination"],"a":1,"e":"The most common psychiatric disturbance seen in Parkinson's disease is depression.\n\nThe non-motor symptoms seen in Parkinson's disease include:\n\n• Cognitive dysfunction and subcortical dementia\n\n• Psychosis and hallucinations - Visual hallucinations are the most common psychotic symptom\n\n• Mood disorders:\n\n• Depression - most common psychiatric disturbance\n\n• Anxiety - second most common psychiatric disturbance\n\n• Abulia/apathy\n\n• Sleep disorders - insomnia, daytime sleepiness with sleep attacks, restless leg syndrome\n\n• Autonomic dysfunction","t":"Movement Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not an example of secondary parkinsonism?","o":["Corticobasal syndrome","Dementia pugilistica","Post-encephalitic PD","Drug-induced Parkinsonism"],"a":0,"e":"Corticobasal syndrome is not an example of secondary parkinsonism. It s a type of Parkinson's plus syndrome.\n\nCauses of secondary Parkinsonism include:\n\n• Repeated head trauma—dementia pugilistica\n\n• Drug-induced\n\n• Tumor\n\n• Post-encephalitic PD\n\n• Toxins: manganese, MPTP, carbon monoxide, cyanide\n\n• Vascular\n\n• Liver failure","t":"Movement Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"What is the most common cause of secondary parkinsonism?","o":["Infection","Vascular","Neuroleptics","Trauma"],"a":2,"e":"Dopamine-blocking agents such as neuroleptics are the most common cause of secondary parkinsonism.\n\nCauses of secondary Parkinsonism include:\n\n• Repeated head trauma—dementia pugilistica\n\n• Drug-induced\n\n• Tumor\n\n• Post-encephalitic PD\n\n• Toxins: manganese, MPTP, carbon monoxide, cyanide\n\n• Vascular\n\n• Liver failure","t":"Movement Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A patient presented to the OPD with a shuffling gait, slowed movements and speech, and cogwheeling. He was started on a new drug recently. Which of the following drugs cannot cause this condition?","o":["Metoclopramide","Bezatropine","α-Methyldopa","Tetrabenazine"],"a":1,"e":"The given scenario is suggestive of secondary parkinsonism. Bezatropine, also known as benztropine, is used in the treatment of parkinsonism. It does not cause secondary parkinsonism.\n\nBenztropine is a centrally acting anticholinergic. It has been used for controlling tremors in drug-induced Parkinson's. Side effects include urinary dysfunction, glaucoma, and cognitive impairment.\n\nDrugs causing secondary parkinsonism:\n\n• Neuroleptics (typical antipsychotics)\n\n• Metoclopramide, chlorpromazine\n\n• Dopamine depleting agents (reserpine, tetrabenazine)\n\n• Calcium channel blocker: Flunnarizine, Cinnarizine\n\n• Lithium carbonate, amiodarone, α-Methyldopa","t":"Movement Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is a peripheral decarboxylase inhibitor?","o":["Benserazide","Benztropine","Tetrabenazine","Trihexyphenidyl"],"a":0,"e":"Benserazide is a peripheral decarboxylase inhibitor.\n\nOptions B & D: Benztropine and trihexyphenidyl are centrally acting anticholinergic agents.\n\nOption C: Tetrabenazine is a dopamine depleting agent. It causes drug-induced Parkinsonism.","t":"Movement Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A 68-year-old woman presents to the casualty after she tripped over a stone. She has been having trouble sleeping and abdominal discomfort. Her daughter said that she has become forgetful and her movements are very slow. She is diagnosed with Parkinson's disease. Which of her symptoms will be relieved by treatment with levodopa?","o":["Forgetfulness","Trouble sleeping","Abdominal discomfort","Slow movements"],"a":3,"e":"Levodopa is useful in alleviating the bradykinesia (slow movement) seen in Parkinson's disease.\n\nLevodopa in Parkinson's disease\n\nLevodopa benefits\n\nClassic motor symptoms of Parkinson's disease:\n\n• Bradykinesia\n\n• Rigidity\n\n• Tremor\n\nLevodopa does not benefit\n\nNon-dopaminergic features:\n\n• Freezing\n\n• Falling\n\n• Sleep disorders\n\n• Dementia\n\n• Autonomic dysfunction (GI disturbances, orthostatic hypotension etc.)","t":"Movement Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is a non-ergot dopamine agonist?","o":["Bromocriptine","Apomorphine","Cabergoline","Pergoilde"],"a":1,"e":"Apomorphine is a non-ergot dopamine agonist.","t":"Movement Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A 75-year-old patient presents with an inability to move and speak. He has been experiencing increasing stiffness and slowness from the past 1 hour. He has an 8-year history of Parkinson's disease and takes his levodopa-carbidopa every day. The last dose was 4 hours back. What drug should be prescribed for this patient?","o":["Rasagiline","Benztropine","Apomorphine","Selegillene"],"a":2,"e":"The clinical features of the patient are suggestive of a severe 'off' episode. The rescue agent for the treatment of severe 'off' episodes is apomorphine.\n\nLevodopa-induced motor complications consist of fluctuations in motor response ('on' episodes when the drug is working and 'off' episodes when parkinsonian features return) and involuntary movements are known as dyskinesias which typically complicate 'on' periods.\n\nApomorphine is a non-ergot dopamine agonist. It has efficacy comparable to levodopa, but it must be administered parenterally (intravenous or subcutaneous) and has a very short half-life and duration of activity (45 min).","t":"Movement Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following drugs has disease-modifying action in Parkinson's disease?","o":["Levodopa","Carbidopa","Apomorphine","Selegiline"],"a":3,"e":"Selegiline is a monoamine oxidase B (MAO-B) inhibitor known to have some disease-modifying action in Parkinson's disease. Studies have shown that selegiline significantly delayed the time until the emergence of disability necessitating the introduction of levodopa in untreated PD patients.\n\nMAO-B inhibitors include:\n\n• Selegiline\n\n• Rasagiline (more potent)\n\nCheese effect: inhibition of MAO-A isoform prevents the metabolism of tyramine in the gut, leading to a potentially fatal hypertensive reaction called the 'cheese effect'. It can be precipitated by tyramine-rich foods such as cheese, aged meat, and red wine.\n\nSelegiline and rasagiline do not functionally inhibit MAO-A isoform and are not associated with cheese effect in doses typically used in clinical practice.","t":"Movement Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements regarding the management of non-motor symptoms in patients with Parkinson's disease is false?","o":["Depression should not be treated with antidepressants","Restless leg syndrome is treated by dopamine agonists","Sialorrhea can be managed by anticholinergics","Low doses of fludrocortisone or midodrine provide control of autonomic symptoms"],"a":0,"e":"Depression is treated with selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRIs). In cases of severe depression, other antidepressants are added.\n\nManifestations in Parkinson's\nTreatment\n\nDepression\n\n• SSRIs\n\nInsomnia\n\n• Zolpidem\n\nRestless leg syndrome\n\n• Gabapentenoids\n\n• Dopamine agonist\n\nErectile dysfunction\n\n• Sildenafil\n\nPostural hypotension\n\n• Rehabilitation with postural exercise\n\n• Adding salt to the diet and elevating the head of the bed to prevent overnight sodium\nnatriuresis\n\n• Low doses of fludrocortisone or midodrine\n\nSialorrhea\n\n• Anticholinergic","t":"Movement Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"All are true about levodopa-induced motor complications except:","o":["More likely to occur in males","Seen in younger individuals with severe disease","Seen with higher doses of levodopa","Rhythmic movements that predominantly involve the lower extremities"],"a":0,"e":"Levodopa-induced motor complications are more likely to occur in females.\n\nThe complications include transient, stereotypic, rhythmic movements that predominantly involve the lower extremities. It is seen in younger individuals with severe disease and also with higher doses of levodopa.","t":"Movement Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A 65-year-old woman on treatment for Parkinson’s disease is brought to the OPD. Her husband is concerned because she has been shopping a lot and reached the maximum spending limit on 2 credit cards in the past week. She has also impulsively been buying several rabbits and raising them in their home. She admits feeling guilty after her purchases. Which of the following drugs is associated with this condition?","o":["Dopamine precursors","Dopamine agonists","Dopamine depletors","Dopamine antagonists"],"a":1,"e":"The given scenario is suggestive of compulsive shopping. Pathological gambling, hypersexuality, and compulsive eating and shopping are the adverse effects of dopamine agonists. Levodopa can also cause these effects, but to a lesser extent than dopamine agonists.","t":"Movement Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A patient with Parkinson's disease has been scheduled to undergo deep brain stimulation. What is the target site for this procedure?","o":["Subthalamic nucleus and globus pallidus internus","Subthalamic nucleus and globus pallidus externus","Substantia nigra pars compacta and globus pallidus externus","Substantia nigra pars reticulata and globus pallidus internus"],"a":0,"e":"The target site for deep brain stimulation in Parkinson’s disease is the subthalamic nucleus (STN) and globus pallidus internus (GPi).\n\nIt helps improve tremors and reduce both 'off' time and dyskinesia. It does not improve features that fail to respond to levodopa. It does not prevent the development or progression of non-dopaminergic features such as freezing, falling, and dementia.","t":"Movement Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is an example of Parkinson's plus syndrome?","o":["Progressive supranuclear palsy","Dementia pugilistica","Post-encephalitic PD","Drug-induced Parkinsonism"],"a":0,"e":"Progressive supranuclear palsy is an example of Parkinson's plus syndrome.\n\nParkinson's plus syndrome or atypical parkinsonism refers to a group of conditions that present with parkinsonism (rigidity and bradykinesia) but are different from PD due to underlying differences in clinical manifestations and pathology. They may have degeneration of the striatum, globus pallidus, cerebellum, and brainstem as well as the SNc. These include multiple system atrophy (MSA), progressive supranuclear palsy (PSP), and corticobasal syndrome (CBS).\n\nPSP is often misdiagnosed as Parkinson’s disease (PD). The substantia nigra is damaged in both diseases and this accounts for the overlapping symptoms which are observed. In PSP, impairment of cognitive functions occurs before the motor or ocular signs and this serves as an important distinguishing feature.\n\nImmunohistochemistry broadly classifies them into two subtypes: synucleopathies and tauopathies.","t":"Movement Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which among the following features is not expected in a patient with atypical parkinsonism?","o":["Late speech and gait impairment","Absence of rest tremor","Poor or no response to levodopa","No motor asymmetry"],"a":0,"e":"Early speech and gait impairment are seen in atypical parkinsonism.\n\nFeatures of atypical parkinsonism include:\n\n• Early speech and gait impairment\n\n• Absence of rest tremor\n\n• No motor asymmetry\n\n• Poor or no response to levodopa\n\n• Aggressive clinical course\n\n• Symptoms of severe early autonomic involvement\n\n• Early severe dementia also points towards atypical parkinsonism","t":"Movement Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A 60-year-old man presented with Parkinsonism and cerebellar features. MRI showed the finding as shown in the image. What is the likely diagnosis?","o":["Drug induced parkinsonism","Multiple systems atrophy","Dementia pugilistica","Progressive supranuclear palsy"],"a":1,"e":"Parkinsonism, cerebellar features, and the 'hot cross bun' sign on MRI suggest multiple system atrophy (MSA).\n\nMSA manifests as a combination of parkinsonian, cerebellar, and autonomic features. Based on predominant clinical features it is further divided into :\n\n• MSA-P:\n\n• Predominantly parkinsonian features are seen\n\n• T2 weighted MRI demonstrates hyperintense putaminal rim\n\n• MSA-C:\n\n• Predominantly cerebellar features are seen\n\n• MRI shows cerebellar and brainstem atrophy causing pontine hot cross bun sign","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a24937c7b9ed4bf1a324b505ba92107a.JPEG"],"t":"Movement Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A 60-year-old man presented with Parkinsonian features which is unresponsive to levodopa. MRI image is given below. What is the probable diagnosis?","o":["Progressive supranuclear palsy","Multiple system atrophy type C","Hallevorden spatz disease","Parkinson's disease"],"a":0,"e":"An elderly male with a history of features of Parkinsonism nonresponsive to levodopa should raise suspicion for Parkinson plus syndromes which include progressive supranuclear palsy (PSP).\n\nProgressive supranuclear palsy (PSP), also known as the Steele-Richardson-Olszewski syndrome, is a tauopathy and is considered a neurodegenerative disease with no current efficacious treatment. It is characterized by decreased cognition, abnormal eye movements (supranuclear vertical gaze palsy), postural instability and falls, as well as parkinsonian features and speech disturbances.\n\nThe atrophy of the midbrain results in hummingbird sign of the brainstem (in the sagittal plane). The preserved pons form the body of the bird, and the atrophic midbrain, the head, with beak extending anteriorly towards the optic chiasm. Midbrain atrophy is best confirmed with the reduction in the midbrain to pons ratio.\n\nMickey mouse appearance, which is the reduction of anteroposterior midline midbrain diameter, at the level of the superior colliculi is seen on axial imaging.\n\nThe following image shows the hummingbird sign:\n\nAccording to a recent imaging study data, the insula and frontal cortex seem to be involved in the development of anxiety in adults with Parkinson's disease.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3380e875f2444465a4ef96ae52e8c73c.JPEG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/0547b0e825084b15ae58a7245aa70786x510x506.GIF"],"t":"Movement Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"An 8-year-old girl exhibits rapid, semi-purposeful, graceful, dance-like, involuntary movements. What is this finding?","o":["Hemiballismus","Myoclonus","Chorea","Athetosis"],"a":2,"e":"Rapid, semi-purposeful, graceful, dance-like, involuntary movements are seen in chorea.\n\nHyperkinetic movement disorders:\n\nTremor\n\nRhythmic oscillation of a body part due to intermittent muscle contractions\n\nDystonia\n\nInvoluntary, sustained or repeated muscle contractions often associated with twisting movements and abnormal posture\n\nAthetosis\n\nSlow, distal, writhing, involuntary movements with a propensity to affect arms and hands\n\nChorea\n\nRapid, semi-purposeful, graceful, dance-like, non patterned, involuntary movements involving distal or proximal muscle groups\n\nMyoclonus\n\nSudden, brief (<100ms), jerk-like arrhythmic muscle twitches\n\nTics\n\nBrief, repeated, stereotyped muscle contractions that can often be suppressed for a short time\n\nSydenham's chorea (also called St. Vitus's dance) is a type of chorea that is typically seen in childhood (5-15 years) with prior exposure to group A streptococcal infection and is thought to be autoimmune in nature. It is characterized by the acute onset of choreiform movements and behavioral disturbances and is self-limiting.","t":"Movement Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"During rounds, you see that a patient diagnosed with stroke exhibits wild, flinging movements on his right side. Where is the lesion likely to be?","o":["Caudate nucleus","Putamen","Subthalamic nucleus","Red nucleus"],"a":2,"e":"Unilateral wild, flinging movements are called hemiballismus and is seen due to a lesion in the subthalamic nucleus. The most common cause of hemiballismus is stroke.","t":"Movement Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"What is the most common acute drug-induced movement disorder?","o":["Akathisia","Dystonia","Tardive Dyskinesia","Myoclonus"],"a":1,"e":"The most common acute drug-induced movement disorder is dystonia.\n\nDrug-induced movement disorders are primarily associated with drugs that block dopamine receptors(neuroleptics) or central dopaminergic transmission. Neuroleptics are the commonest cause of this condition. Drug-induced movement disorders are classified as:\n\n• Acute: Dystonia is the most common acute hyperkinetic drug reaction; generalized in children, focal in adults.\n\n• Subacute: Akathisia is the commonest reaction in this category\n\n• Tardive syndromes: Develop after months to years of initiation of the drug.\n\n• Tardive dyskinesia is the most common. Choreiform movements affecting the mouth, lips, and tongue are seen. In severe cases, trunks, limbs, and respiratory muscles may also be affected.\n\n• In one third, TD remits within 3 months of stopping the drug and improvement occur over several years.","t":"Movement Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is the most common movement disorder?","o":["Dystonia","Chorea","Athetosis","Essential tremor"],"a":3,"e":"The most common movement disorder is essential tremor.\n\nIt can present in childhood but dramatically increases in prevalence over the age of 70 years. Essential tremor is characterized by a high-frequency tremor 6-10 Hz that predominantly affects the upper extremities.\n\nThe tremor is characteristically improved by alcohol and worsened by stress.\n\nEssential tremor\nParkinson's disease\n\nBradykinesia，\nrigidity， micrographia\nAbsent\nPresent\n\nTremor\nUsually postural\nUsually resting\n\nTone\nNormal\nCogwheel rigidity","t":"Movement Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A 65-year-old man presents with a tremor in both hands that worsens when he is trying to eat or write something. He says that it gets worse when he is stressed and reduces when he drinks wine. His father also had similar symptoms. On examination, there are no resting tremors. Which of the following drugs can be used to control this condition?","o":["Valproate","Levetiracetam","Primidone","Carbamazepine"],"a":2,"e":"The given scenario is suggestive of essential tremor. It can be controlled by primidone.\n\nThe drugs used to treat essential tremors are:\n\n• Beta-blockers: propranolol, atenolol\n\n• Anticonvulsants:\n\n• Primidone\n\n• Gabapentine\n\n• Topiramate\n\nIn severe and drug-resistant cases, surgical treatment involves targeting the ventral intermediate (VIM) nucleus of the thalamus.","t":"Movement Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A 70-year-old male patient is scheduled to undergo deep brain stimulation for essential tremor. What is the target site for the procedure?","o":["Subthalamic nucleus and globus pallidus internus","Anterior limb of internal capsule","Substantia nigra pars compacta","Ventral intermediate nucleus of thalamus"],"a":3,"e":"The target for deep brain stimulation in essential tremor is ventral intermediate (VIM) nucleus of the thalamus.\n\nDisease\n\nTarget sites for deep brain stimulation\n\nParkinson’s disease\n\nSubthalamic nucleus (STN)\n\nGlobus pallidus internus (GPi)\n\nDystonia\n\nGlobus pallidus internus (GPi)\n\nDepression\n\nAnterior limb of internal capsule and cingulum\n\nObsessive-Compulsive Disorder (OCD)\n\nAnterior limb of the internal capsule\n\nEssential tremor\n\nVentral intermediate (VIM) nucleus","t":"Movement Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A patient with a family history of Huntington's disease has come for genetic counselling. All of the following are true about this condition, except:","o":["Autosomal dominant inheritance","Trinucleotide repeat of CAG sequence","Westphal akinetic variant has good prognosis","Early onset cognitive impairment seen"],"a":2,"e":"Westphal akinetic variant has the worst prognosis.\n\nHuntington's disease shows an autosomal dominant inheritance. It is a trinucleotide repeat expansion disease with multiple CAG repeats (>40 repeats) in the coding region of the huntingtin gene on chromosome 4p. It demonstrates genetic anticipation with earlier onset if inherited from the father.\n\nIt presents in the second to third decade of life with cognitive impairment as an early feature. The dominant cognitive feature of HD is executive dysfunction. Additionally, dementia is seen later as the disease progresses.\n\nDecreased acetylcholine leads to dementia. Decreased GABA and increased dopamine lead to chorea. Treatment consists of haloperidol, tetrabenazine (Dopamine depletor).\n\nThe following image shows MRI showing dilated ventricles (ex vacuo ventriculomegaly)- due to atrophy of caudate and putamen:\n\nMRI showing atrophy of caudate and putamen","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f3cd4fcd53c84a809407548d00b8c999x1280x1575.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/94daa957c157440d9216e91069083745x1280x1576.JPEG"],"t":"Movement Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"St. Vitus' dance is the other name for which of the following conditions?","o":["Huntigton's chorea","Sydenham's chorea","Athetosis","Hemibalismus"],"a":1,"e":"Sydenham's chorea was originally called St. Vitus's dance.\n\nSydenham's chorea is more common in females. It is typically seen in childhood (5-1 5 years) with prior exposure to group A streptococcal infection and is thought to be autoimmune in nature.\n\nIt is characterized by the acute onset of choreiform movements and behavioral disturbances.\n\nIt is self limited.Treatment is generally restricted to those with severe chorea.The chorea generally responds to dopamine-blocking agents, valproic acid, and carbamazepine.","t":"Movement Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"All of the following features can be seen in patients with restless leg syndrome, except:","o":["Symptoms worsen during the evening or night","Accompanied by an unpleasant sensation in the legs","Symptoms that begin or worsen with activity","Partial or complete relief by movement"],"a":2,"e":"In restless leg syndrome (RLS), symptoms begin or worsen with rest. It is also called as Willis-Ekbom disease.\n\nThe four core symptoms required for the diagnosis of RLS are:\n\n• An urge to move the legs\n\n• Usually caused or accompanied by an unpleasant sensation in the legs (creepy-crawly feeling, paresthesia, or burning)\n\n• Symptoms that begin or worsen with rest and partial or complete relief by movement\n\n• Worsening during the evening or night\n\nRestless leg syndrome is a typical sensory-motor disorder that generally affects the legs during rest periods, especially sleep. There is an irresistible urge to shake the legs associated with burning sensation, itching, and cramps of the legs. In idiopathic cases, dopaminergic hypofunction is postulated.\n\nIn patients with restless legs syndrome, iron stores are initially repleted, and the aggravating factors are eliminated. If RLS still persists, initial therapy with a gabapentinoid (pregabalin, gabapentin, gabapentin enacarbil) is preferred, rather than a dopamine agonist (pramipexole, ropinirole, rotigotine). This is due to the high frequency of intermediate and long-term augmentation with dopamine agonists, as well as their risk of impulse control disorders.\n\nFDA-approved drugs for the treatment of restless leg syndrome:\n\n• Gabapentin\n\n• Ropinirole\n\n• Pramipexole\n\n• Rotigotine\n\nA dopamine agonist is used as an alternative in patients with increased risk for side effects from gabapentinoids, such as those with obesity and its complications, a past or present history of moderate to severe depression, a disorder causing gait instability or respiratory failure, and a history of substance use disorder.\n\nNon-pharmacological treatments that can be used in restless leg syndrome are:\n\n• Avoiding alcohol at bedtime\n\n• Massaging affected legs\n\n• Hot baths\n\n• Applying hot or cold to the affected area\n\n• Engaging in moderate exercises","t":"Movement Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following features are lower motor neuron lesions associated with?","o":["Fasciculations","Spasticity","Proximal muscle weakness","Preserved stretch reflexes"],"a":0,"e":"Lower motor neuron lesions are associated with fasciculations.","t":"Als And Other Motor Neuron Diseases","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is referred to as bulbar paralysis?","o":["UMN palsy of cranial nerves 1 to 6","LMN palsy of cranial nerves 1 to 6","UMN palsy of cranial nerves 9 to 12","LMN palsy of cranial nerves 9 to 12"],"a":3,"e":"Bulbar paralysis refers to LMN palsy of cranial nerves 9 to 12. It is seen due to disease processes affecting primarily the motor nuclei of the cranial nerves.\n\nThe manifestations of bulbar palsy include paralysis of tongue, muscles of swallowing, and facial muscles. There is difficulty handling secretions and maintaining an airway.\n\nCauses of bulbar palsy include:\n\n• GBS\n\n• Polio\n\n• Syringobulbia","t":"Als And Other Motor Neuron Diseases","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which type of disorder is primary lateral sclerosis?","o":["Isolated UMN disorder","Isolated LMN disorder","UMN + LMN disorder","Isolated bulbar disorder"],"a":0,"e":"Primary lateral sclerosis is a progressive isolated upper motor neuron disorder.\n\nThe spectrum of motor neuron disease includes:\n\n• Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS):\n\n• It is the most common form of motor neuron disease\n\n• Includes upper and lower motor neuron pathology (UMN +LMN)\n\n• Primary lateral sclerosis:\n\n• It is a progressive isolated upper motor neuron disorder (UMN)\n\n• Some patients develop lower motor neuron signs later in their clinical course, at which point the disease is called upper motor neuron-onset ALS\n\n• Progressive muscular atrophy:\n\n• It is a progressive isolated lower motor neuron disorder (LMN)\n\n• Some patients develop upper motor neuron signs later in their clinical course, at which point the disease is called lower motor neuron-onset ALS\n\n• Progressive bulbar palsy:\n\n• It is a progressive upper and lower motor neuron (UMN +LMN) disorder of cranial muscles\n\n• This condition may occasionally stay isolated to the bulbar segment, but more commonly, upper and lower motor neuron signs and symptoms spread to involve other segments. This is then referred to as bulbar-onset ALS","t":"Als And Other Motor Neuron Diseases","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is involved in amyotrophic lateral sclerosis?","o":["Anterior horn cell + corticospinal tract","Anterior horn cell + spinothalamic tract","Posterior horn cell + corticospinal tract","Ventral root ganglia + spinothalamic tract"],"a":0,"e":"Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS) involves the anterior horn cells and corticospinal tract.\n\nALS is the most common form of progressive motor neuron disease.\n\nThe pathologic hallmark of motor neuron degenerative disorders is the death of lower motor neurons and upper, or corticospinal, motor neurons. LMN consist of anterior horn cells in the spinal cord and their brainstem homologs innervating bulbar muscles. UMN originate in layer five of the motor cortex and descend via the pyramidal tract to synapse with lower motor neurons, either directly or indirectly via interneurons.\n\nAlthough at its onset, ALS may cause selective loss of function of only upper or lower motor neurons, it ultimately causes progressive loss of both categories of motor neurons.","t":"Als And Other Motor Neuron Diseases","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A middle-aged man presents with progressive atrophy and weakness of his hands and forearms. On examination, he is found to have spasticity of the legs and brisk jaw jerk. Neuroimaging revealed increased signal in the corticospinal tracts on T2 weighted MRI. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Multiple sclerosis","Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis","Subacute combined degeneration","Pregressive spinal muscular atrophy"],"a":1,"e":"The most likely diagnosis is amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS).\n\nThe clinical hallmark of ALS is the presence of signs and symptoms of both upper and lower motor neuron (UMN + LMN) dysfunction. The signs of UMN dysfunction include spasticity and brisk jaw jerk, while the signs of LMN dysfunction include atrophy and weakness of limbs.\n\nConventional MRI is usually normal in ALS and is used to exclude other possible causes although increased signal in the corticospinal tracts on T2-weighted MRI is supportive of the diagnosis of ALS.","t":"Als And Other Motor Neuron Diseases","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A young college student presents with multiple episodes of falls while climbing stairs. He stated he had been feeling clumsy and weak for the past few months. On examination, he was found to have bilateral positive Babinski's sign and atrophy of muscles of lower limbs. Deep tendon reflexes were absent. What is the likely diagnosis?","o":["Kennedy disease","Lou Gehrig's disease","Pelizaeus–Merzbacher disease","Charcot-Marie-tooth disease"],"a":1,"e":"The given scenario with both UMN (positive Babinski sign) and LMN (absent reflexes and wasting of muscles) signs are suggestive of amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS). It is also known as Lou Gehrig's disease, after the famous New York Yankee baseball player who was affected with the disorder.\n\nALS was first described by Charcot in the 19th century and is also referred to as Charcot's disease.\n\nOption A: Kennedy disease is a gradually progressive neuromuscular disorder characterised by wasting of proximal and bulbar muscles. It is caused by a mutation in the androgen receptor gene.\n\nOption C: Pelizaeus–Merzbacher disease is an inherited leukodystrophy characterized by problems with coordination, motor skills, and learning.\n\nOption D: Charcot-Marie-tooth disease encompasses a group of hereditary sensory and motor neuropathies.","t":"Als And Other Motor Neuron Diseases","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A 42-year-old man who presented with progressive weakness was found to have a mix of upper and lower motor neuron findings on examination. His father and his cousin had passed away in their 40s due to similar symptoms. A mutation of the gene encoding which of the following enzymes is likely implicated here?","o":["Superoxide dismutase","NADPH oxidase","Myeloperoxidase","Glutathione peroxidase"],"a":0,"e":"The given scenario is suggestive of familial amyotrophic lateral sclerosis. This condition is linked to mutations of the gene encoding copper-zinc (Cu, Zn) superoxide dismutase type 1 (SOD1). Familial ALS accounts for about 10% of all ALS cases.\n\nOption B: Mutations in the NADPH oxidase genes cause chronic granulomatous disease (CGD).\n\nOption C: Hereditary deficiency of myeloperoxidase (MPO) predisposes to immune deficiency.\n\nOption D: Glutathione peroxidase deficiency leads to hemolytic anemia.","t":"Als And Other Motor Neuron Diseases","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"All of the following features can be seen in patients with amyotrophic lateral sclerosis, except:","o":["Split-hand syndrome","Orthostatic hypotension","Frontotemporal dementia","Dropped head syndrome"],"a":1,"e":"Orthostatic hypotension is not a feature seen in classical ALS. Autonomic symptoms are not classically seen in ALS, although a few cases with autonomic insufficiency have been reported and these are classified separately as ALS-plus syndromes.\n\nOption A: The split-hand syndrome describes a frequent pattern of weakness and atrophy in ALS that predominantly involves the median and ulnar-innervated lateral (thenar) hand intrinsic muscles with relative sparing of the medial (hypothenar) muscles.\n\nOption C: There is a well-established link between ALS and frontotemporal executive dysfunction (dementia) that may precede or follow the onset of upper and/or lower motor neuron dysfunction\n\nOption D: Dropped head syndrome is an important clinical sign in ALS caused by severe neck extensor weakness (LMN), which leads to an inability to maintain level head position.","t":"Als And Other Motor Neuron Diseases","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following patients is unlikely to have amyotrophic lateral sclerosis?","o":["A patient with difficulty in swallowing","A patient who has spastic dysarthria","A patient who starts laughing or crying inappropriately","A patient with bowel and bladder dysfunction"],"a":3,"e":"The patient with bowel and bladder dysfunction is unlikely to have ALS as these findings are not seen in ALS. Even in the late stages of the ALS, sensory & bowel & bladder functions are preserved.\n\nFasciculations, muscle cramps, weakness, atrophy, and bulbar palsy are LMN features seen in ALS.\n\nSpasticity, increased reflexes, flexor spasms, and pseudobulbar palsy are UMN features in ALS.\n\nA frequent bulbar UMN symptom seen in ALS is the pseudobulbar affect. This is manifested as inappropriate laughing, crying, or yawning. The observed affect is often mood incongruent or may be triggered by stimuli that would not have elicited such a response prior to the development of pseudobulbar affect.","t":"Als And Other Motor Neuron Diseases","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"What is the diagnosis of amyotrophic lateral sclerosis predominantly based on?","o":["MRI findings","CSF analysis findings","Muscle biopsy findings","Clinical criteria"],"a":3,"e":"The diagnosis of ALS is based on clinical criteria that include the presence of upper motor neuron and lower motor neuron signs, the progression of the disease, and the absence of an alternative explanation.\n\nThere is no single diagnostic test that can confirm or entirely exclude the diagnosis of motor neuron disease in general and amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS) in particular.\n\nThe revised El Escorial criteria and, more recently, the newer Awaji criteria are being used for the diagnosis of ALS.\n\nPractically, the diagnosis is made when it is suggested by the history and physical examination, supported by electrodiagnostic studies (EMG & NCS), and not excluded by neuroimaging (MRI) and laboratory studies (CSF, blood, and urine analysis). Increased signal in the corticospinal tracts on T2-weighted MRI, if seen, is supportive of the diagnosis of ALS\n\nMuscle biopsy is not a routine part of the diagnostic evaluation of ALS but should be performed if there is clinical suspicion of inflammatory myopathy.\n\nGenetic testing is not a routine part of the diagnostic evaluation in ALS except in cases where familial ALS (10%) is suspected.","t":"Als And Other Motor Neuron Diseases","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is the only drug known to improve survival in patients with amyotrophic lateral sclerosis?","o":["Fingolimod","Riluzole","Tocilizumab","Rotigotine"],"a":1,"e":"Among the given options, riluzole is the only known drug to have any impact on survival in ALS. It can slow ALS progression to a modest degree. It is a neuroprotective drug that blocks glutamatergic neurotransmission in the CNS.\n\nEdaravone is a newer drug that is a free radical scavenger. It is thought to reduce oxidative stress, which has been implicated in the pathogenesis of ALS. Edaravone was found to slow functional deterioration in some patients with ALS. It is recommended as an adjunct to riluzole for all patients with ALS.","t":"Als And Other Motor Neuron Diseases","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following features would you not expect to find in a patient with cerebellar ataxia?","o":["Resting tremor","Dysdiadochokinesis","Scanning speech","Hypotonia"],"a":0,"e":"Intention tremor is a feature of cerebellar ataxia, not resting tremor.\n\nCerebellar ataxia is characterized by the following symptoms:\n\n• Gait impairment\n\n• Unclear speech or scanning speech\n\n• Blurred vision due to nystagmus\n\n• Dysdiadochokinesis\n\n• Intention tremors - tremor on goal-oriented movements\n\nBilateral and symmetric involvement suggests the following etiologies:\n\n• Genetic - SCA gene mutations\n\n• Metabolic - Vitamin B1 or B12 deficiency\n\n• Immune - Antigliadin antibody syndrome\n\n• Toxic - Phenytoin, amiodarone, alcohol\n\nFocal, unilateral symptoms with headache, impaired level of consciousness, ipsilateral cranial nerve palsies and contralateral weakness suggest a space-occupying cerebellar lesion.","t":"Ataxic Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A 70-year-old man presented with gait instability, slurring of speech, and intention tremors, for a few months. He was a former smoker who quit 10 years back. After extensive workup, a paraneoplastic cause was suspected, and chest CT was ordered. It showed a 3cm wide mass near the right hilum. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Adenocarcinoma lung","Non small cell lung cancer","Small cell lung cancer","Squamous cell carcinoma lung"],"a":2,"e":"The given case scenario is suggestive of paraneoplastic cerebellar ataxia, which is most likely to be caused by small cell lung cancer here.\n\nParaneoplastic syndromes causing cerebellar ataxia are associated with various antibodies:\n\nCancer\nAntibody seen\n\nBreast and ovarian cancers\nAnti-Yo antibody\n\nSmall-cell lung cancer\nAnti-PQ-type voltage-gated calcium channel autoantibody\n\nHodgkin's disease\nAnti-Tr antibodies\n\nSometimes, paraneoplastic ataxias may be the only presenting symptom of the underlying cancer. In paraneoplastic cerebellar ataxias, serum anti-glutamic acid decarboxylase (GAD) antibodies may be elevated, affecting speech and gait.","t":"Ataxic Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"What is the most common inherited ataxia?","o":["Machado-Joseph disease","Friedreich's ataxia","Ataxia - telangiectasia","Spinocerebellar ataxia"],"a":1,"e":"Friedreich's ataxia is the most common form of inherited ataxia (autosomal recessive), comprising one-half of all hereditary ataxias.\n\nFriedrich's ataxia has autosomal recessive inheritance and presents before 25 years of age. The median age of death is 35 years. Women have a significantly better prognosis than men.\n\nFriedrich's ataxia presents with clinical features such as dysarthria, progressive staggering gait, frequent falls and titubation. Other neurological symptoms include nystagmus, loss of fast saccadic movements, loss of vibratory and proprioceptive sensation, and ataxia or trunk and limb movements. The lower extremities are more severely involved than the upper ones.\n\nCardiac involvement occurs in 90% of patients. It presents with cardiomegaly, symmetric hypertrophy, and conduction defects. Musculoskeletal deformities are common and include progressive scoliosis, pes cavus, and pes equinovarus.","t":"Ataxic Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A 24-year-old man presents with complaints of swaying while walking, frequent falls and slurring of speech. He also gives a history of involuntary nodding movements of the head. On examination, you note the following findings. Which of the following regions of the spinal cord is involved first in this condition?\n\n• General examination: Kyphoscoliosis noted\n\n• CVS examination:\n\n• Pes cavus present\n\n• Systolic murmur present\n\n• CNS examination:\n\n• Romberg's sign positive\n\n• Nystagmus present\n\n• Lower limb areflexia\n\n• Extensor plantar reflex noted","o":["Dorsal root ganglion","Spinocerebellar tract","Lateral corticospinal tract","Cerebellum"],"a":0,"e":"The given case scenario is suggestive of Friedrich's ataxia, in which the dorsal root ganglion cells are involved first.\n\nThe primary sites of pathology are the spinal cord, dorsal root ganglion cells, and the peripheral nerves.\n\nSpinal cord tracts are involved, and degeneration occurs predominantly in the spinocerebellar tracts, lateral corticospinal tracts, and posterior columns. Degeneration of cranial nerves (glossopharyngeal, vagus, hypoglossal) and deep cerebellar nuclei can occur. Peripheral nerves are also involved, and there's a loss of large myelinated fibers. Cerebellar atrophy is also seen.\n\nCardiac changes occur due to myocyte hypertrophy, fibrosis, and degeneration of cardiac ganglia.\n\nNote: Friedrich's ataxia may also be associated with vitamin E deficiency.","t":"Ataxic Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A middle-aged vegan woman presented with tingling and numbness over the limbs and progressive fatigue. Her peripheral smear is given below. Which of the following neurological manifestation would you not expect to see in this condition?","o":["Sensory ataxia","Optic atrophy","Psychosis","Myopathy"],"a":3,"e":"The given case scenario and smear showing macroovalocytes and hypersegmented neutrophils, is suggestive of megaloblastic anemia caused by vitamin B12 deficiency. Myopathy is not a feature of vitamin B12 deficiency.\n\nVitamin B12 deficiency may produce the following neurological manifestations:\n\n• Loss of vibration sense and proprioception\n\n• Paresthesia - upper limb paresthesias occur before lower limbs are affected.\n\n• Unsteady gait - due to sensory ataxia\n\n• Peripheral neuropathy\n\n• Optic neuropathy\n\n• Psychiatric manifestations such as:\n\n• Irritated behavior\n\n• Severe dementia\n\n• Frank psychosis","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/da5047c145b248da8a2126fa897ff53c.JPEG"],"t":"Ataxic Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A 30-year-old man presented with swaying while walking, tingling in palms and feet, and blurring of vision. On examination, deep tendon reflexes are absent, proprioception and vibration sense are absent over the lower limbs. MRI spine reveals diffuse demyelination. What is the diagnosis?","o":["Multiple sclerosis","Tabes dorsalis","Transverse myelitis","Subacute combined degeneration"],"a":3,"e":"The given case scenario is suggestive of subacute combined degeneration (SACD) due to vitamin B12 deficiency.\n\nSACD presents with the following features:\n\n• Loss of vibration sense and proprioception\n\n• Paresthesia - upper limb paresthesias occur before lower limbs are affected.\n\n• Unsteady gait - due to sensory ataxia\n\n• Loss of deep tendon reflexes\n\n• Peripheral neuropathy\n\n• Optic neuropathy - can present as blurring of vision\n\n• Psychiatric manifestations such as:\n\n• Irritated behavior\n\n• Severe dementia\n\n• Frank psychosis\n\nMRI spine shows myelopathy and areas of demyelination. Myelopathy of SACD tends to be diffuse rather than focal.\n\nTreatment is replacement therapy is with 1000 μg of intramuscular vitamin B12 repeated at regular intervals or by oral vitamin B12.","t":"Ataxic Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"The investigations of a male truck driver are given below. Which of the following is he unlikely to present with?\n\n• VDRL - positive\n\n• FTA-ABs - Antibodies to T. pallidum present\n\n• MRI spine - areas of focal inflammation seen in the dorsal column","o":["Ataxia","Argyll Robertson pupil","Bladder dysfunction","Hyperreflexia"],"a":3,"e":"The given case scenario is suggestive of tabes dorsalis. It presents with loss of reflexes in lower limbs, not hyperreflexia.\n\nTabes dorsalis (or syphilitic myelopathy) is a slow degeneration and demyelination of the neural tracts. It is seen in tertiary syphilis. It primarily involves the dorsal columns of the spinal cord and dorsal roots.\n\nIt presents with the following clinical features:\n\n• Loss of reflexes in lower limbs\n\n• Romberg's sign\n\n• Bilateral Argyll Robertson pupils - accommodation reflex present, pupillary reflex absent\n\n• Loss of position sense and vibration\n\n• Stabbing/pricking pain in the legs - this is fleeting or repetitive\n\n• Ataxia of the legs and gait disturbances\n\n• Paresthesias\n\n• Bladder disturbances\n\n• Acute abdominal pain with vomiting - visceral crisis\n\nNote: Diabetic polyradiculopathy may simulate tabes.","t":"Ataxic Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is false regarding ataxia telangiectasia?","o":["Presents in 4th to 5th decade of life.","Autosomal recessive inheritance is seen","Loss of Purkinje cells occurs","Increased incidence of lymphomas is seen"],"a":0,"e":"Ataxia-telangiectasia is an autosomal recessive condition presenting in the first decade of life.\n\nIt is associated with a high incidence of recurrent pulmonary infections, lymphomas, Hodgkin's disease, breast cancer, and acute T cell leukemias.\n\nHistology shows loss of Purkinje cells, granules, and basket cells in the cerebellar cortex. There is a loss of neurons in the deep cerebellar nuclei, inferior olives of the medulla, and anterior horn and dorsal root of the spinal cord.\n\nClinical features of ataxia-telangiectasia include:\n\n• Progressive telangiectatic lesions\n\n• Oculomotor apraxia\n\n• Truncal and limb ataxia\n\n• Dysarthria\n\n• Extensor plantar reflexes\n\n• Myoclonic jerks\n\n• Areflexia\n\n• Distal sensory deficits\n\n• Nystagmus\n\n• Cellular and humoral immunodeficiency (absent/ill-developed thymus)","t":"Ataxic Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following cranial nerves are affected in cavernous sinus syndrome?","o":["III, IV and V","IV, V and VI","VI and VII","III, IV, V and VI"],"a":3,"e":"The cranial nerves III, IV, V, and VI are affected by cavernous sinus syndrome.\n\nThe cavernous sinus syndrome is a condition caused by any pathology involving the cavernous sinus. The oculomotor (III), trochlear (IV) and trigeminal (V) nerves pass in relation to the lateral wall of the cavernous sinus. The abducent (VI) nerve passes through the cavernous sinus.\n\nPatients with this condition usually present with the following features:\n\n• Orbital or facial pain\n\n• Orbital swelling and chemosis due to occlusion of the ophthalmic veins\n\n• Fever\n\n• Oculomotor neuropathy (III, IV, and VI)\n\n• Trigeminal neuropathy (V)\n\nCauses of cavernous sinus syndrome include:\n\n• Cavernous sinus thrombosis - most common\n\n• Carotid artery aneurysm\n\n• Carotid-cavernous fistula\n\n• Tumors such as meningioma.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1cf0d1fa12ce41508a8fc441e5e37dc9x1280x1403.JPEG"],"t":"Cranial Nerve Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements is true regarding trigeminal neuralgia?","o":["The region of ophthalmic division of trigeminal nerve is always spared","The pain usually last for a few hours","The condition is also known as tic douloureux","It is more common in men"],"a":2,"e":"Tic douloureux is another term for trigeminal neuralgia.\n\nTrigeminal neuralgia is characterized by frequent paroxysms of severe pain in the lips, gums, cheek, or chin. It is sometimes seen in the distribution of the ophthalmic division of the trigeminal nerve.\n\nMiddle-aged and elderly females are primarily affected. The pain usually lasts for a short time (seconds to minutes) but be may intense enough that the patient winces, hence the term 'tic'.\n\nA characteristic feature is the presence of trigger zones, typically on the face, lips, or tongue. Patients usually report excruciating pain during activities such as washing the face or brushing the teeth. However, no signs of sensory loss can be demonstrated.","t":"Cranial Nerve Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"The high frequency of trigeminal neuralgia in patients with multiple sclerosis is mainly attributed to the involvement of ______.","o":["Oligodendrocyte-derived myelin covering the beginning of the nerve","Oligodendrocyte-derived myelin covering the terminal part of the nerve","Schwann cell–derived myelin sheath covering the beginning of the nerve","Schwann cell–derived myelin sheath covering the terminal part of the nerve"],"a":0,"e":"The high frequency of trigeminal neuralgia in patients with multiple sclerosis occurs due to the involvement of oligodendrocyte-derived myelin covering the beginning of the nerve.\n\nMultiple sclerosis is a disorder of oligodendrocyte myelin. It should be ruled out when trigeminal neuralgia is bilateral or develops in a young adult. In such cases, the cause is a demyelinating plaque near the root entry zone of the fifth cranial nerve in the pons. In these patients, a facial sensory loss is also often found in association.","t":"Cranial Nerve Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"In patients with trigeminal neuralgia, the nerve is most commonly compressed by ______.","o":["Superior cerebral artery","Inferior cerebral artery","Superior cerebellar artery","Inferior cerebellar artery"],"a":2,"e":"The superior cerebellar artery most often compresses the trigeminal nerve as it emerges from the brainstem. This causes lancinating pain in the distribution of the nerve which is known as trigeminal neuralgia.\n\nThough trigeminal neuralgia can be diagnosed by the clinical features, MRI is recommended to define the underlying cause. In cases refractory to medical management, it also helps to plan for surgical decompression.","t":"Cranial Nerve Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A food vlogger presents with complaints of episodes of piercing electric-shock like pain on the left side of her face. The episodes are triggered by chewing food and last for a few minutes. She is further distressed as it interferes with her work. She has tried over the counter analgesics to no avail. What is the drug of choice for her?","o":["Gabapentin","Lamotrigine","Carbamazepine","Amitriptyline"],"a":2,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of trigeminal neuralgia and the drug of choice is carbamazepine.\n\nOxcarbazepine is an alternative to carbamazepine that has less bone marrow toxicity and is equally efficacious.\n\nOther drugs used are lamotrigine, gabapentin and baclofen.","t":"Cranial Nerve Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is false about Bell's palsy?","o":["Hyperacusis may be present","Pregnancy is a risk factor","MRI shows enhancement of geniculate ganglion","Maximal weakness within 72 hours"],"a":3,"e":"Maximal weakness occurs within 48 hours as a rule in Bells' palsy.\n\nBell's palsy is an idiopathic, peripheral facial paralysis or paresis of acute onset due to a lower motor neuron (LMN) lesion of the facial nerve.\n\nClinical presentation of Bell’s palsy:\n\n• Pain behind the ear (may precede paralysis and last for 1-2 days)\n\n• Bell's phenomenon\n\n• Asymmetrical face\n\n• Hyperacusis (due to stapedial paralysis)\n\n• Loss of taste (involvement of chorda tympani).\n\nMRI may reveal swelling and uniform enhancement of the geniculate ganglion and facial nerve.\n\nApproximately 80% of patients recover within a few weeks or months. The presence of incomplete paralysis in the first week is the most favorable prognostic sign. Recurrence may be seen in some cases.","t":"Cranial Nerve Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following viruses is most commonly implicated in Bell’s palsy?","o":["Cytomegalovirus","Herpes simplex virus","Epstein-Barr virus","Varicella zoster virus"],"a":1,"e":"Herpes simplex virus is most commonly implicated in causing Bell’s palsy.\n\nVaricella-zoster virus is the second most common cause.","t":"Cranial Nerve Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is true about Bell’s phenomenon?","o":["Eyeballs roll upwards on attempting to open the eyes","Eyeballs roll downwards on attempting to close the eyes","It is a normal phenomenon evident in facial paralysis","It is an abnormal phenomenon evident in facial paralysis"],"a":2,"e":"Bell’s phenomenon refers to a normal phenomenon that becomes evident in facial paralysis. The eyeballs roll up and outwards on attempting to close the eyes.\n\nIt is also known as the palpebral oculogyric reflex. It is a normal protective reflex resulting in the elevation of globes while blinking to protect the eyes from injury. It also serves to provide a wiper blade-like cleansing function of the cornea by the eyelid.\n\nHowever, it occurs behind the closed eyelids in healthy individuals. It becomes noticeable in Bell's palsy as the eyelids fail to close completely.\n\nThe image below shows a patient with evident Bell’s phenomenon.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fa49a30cb746443a93f4cb1ab98e1dc9x1000x750.JPEG"],"t":"Cranial Nerve Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"On returning from work, a man noticed that his mother had developed mild right-sided facial drooping. Believing it to be due to a stroke, he immediately took her to the hospital. After extensive evaluation, the doctor ruled out stroke and diagnosed her with Bell's palsy. Which of the following would not be a part of the management of her condition?","o":["Paper tape to depress the upper eyelid during sleep","Prednisone 60-80 mg daily during the first 5 days","Massage of the weakened muscles","Acyclovir 1g twice daily for 10 days"],"a":3,"e":"Acyclovir may be considered as add-on therapy for patients with severe Bell's palsy as 400mg five times daily for 10 days. Monotherapy is not recommended.\n\nTreatment of Bell's palsy involves prednisone 60-80 mg daily during the first 5 days and then tapered over the next 5 days. Randomized trials found no added benefit of the antiviral agents valacyclovir and acyclovir when compared to glucocorticoids alone.\n\nThe use of paper tape to depress the upper eyelid during sleep prevents corneal drying.\n\nFor patients with permanent paralysis, cosmetic surgical procedures have been used to restore a relatively symmetric appearance to the face.","t":"Cranial Nerve Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A 35-year-old patient presents with facial weakness and pain for the past 2 days. The following signs are seen on examination. What is the cause?","o":["UMN facial nerve palsy","LMN facial nerve palsy","Hemifacial spasm","Facial synkinesis"],"a":1,"e":"The features are suggestive of lower motor neuron (LMN) type of facial palsy.\n\nIn the image, the absence of wrinkling over the right side of the forehead and incomplete closure of the right eyelid is characteristic of LMN type of facial palsy.\n\nIn upper motor neuron (UMN) lesion, the upper part of the face is spared due to bilateral UMN innervation.\n\nFeatures of facial nerve palsy:\n\nFeature\nUMN\nLMN\n\nSide affected\nContralateral\nIpsilateral\n\nFacial muscles involved\nLower\nBoth upper and lower\n\nForehead wrinkling\nPresent\nAbsent\n\nEmotional incontinence\nPresent\nAbsent\n\nCorneal reflex\nNormal\nAbsent\n\nTaste\nIntact\nAffected\n\nAtrophy of facial muscles\nAbsent\nPresent\n\nNote: Deviation of the angle of mouth should not be confused with drooping of the angle of mouth. Deviation of the angle of the mouth occurs on the normal side due to the action of unaffected facial muscles, while drooping occurs on the affected side.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b6c6b3a9c2f14d7ebafe360d156dd9a0.JPEG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/75801651208d4393a6e41506f056d225x1280x2045.JPEG"],"t":"Cranial Nerve Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Identify the correct type of facial nerve palsy in the following patients based on their clinical features:\n\nPatient 1 - Deviation of angle of mouth to left, eyelids completely closed and wrinkling of the forehead.\n\nPatient 2 - Deviation of angle of mouth to left, the right eyelid remains partially open, and no wrinkling of the forehead.","o":["1 - Right LMN, 2 - Left UMN","1 - Right UMN, 2 - Right LMN","1 - Left UMN, 2 - Right LMN","1 - Left LMN, 2 - Right UMN"],"a":2,"e":"Patient 1 has left UMN facial nerve palsy and patient 2 has right LMN facial nerve palsy.\n\nIn an upper motor neuron (UMN) lesion, wrinkling on the forehead and eyelid closure remains intact. In this lesion, the contralateral side of the face is affected. The first-order neurons from the left cerebral hemisphere cross over (left to right) at the cortical level and relay in the right facial nerve nucleus in the pons. From here the second-order neurons (right facial nerve) descend and supply the right side of the face.\n\nHence, a left UMN lesion causes paralysis of the lower half of the face on the right side. In facial palsy, the angle of the mouth deviates towards the normal side. In this patient, since the mouth deviates towards the left, the right facial nerve is affected.\n\nIn lower motor neuron (LMN) lesions, there is an absence of wrinkling on the forehead and incomplete closure of the eyelid on the ipsilateral side of the face.\n\nFeature\nUMN\nLMN\n\nSide affected\nContralateral\nIpsilateral\n\nFacial muscles involved\nLower\nBoth upper and lower\n\nForehead wrinkling\nPresent\nAbsent\n\nEmotional incontinence\nPresent\nAbsent\n\nCorneal reflex\nNormal\nAbsent\n\nTaste\nIntact\nAffected\n\nAtrophy\nAbsent\nPresent","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/cab0c677d4e74b98abac9b0611d89701x1280x2045.JPEG"],"t":"Cranial Nerve Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"During the neurological examination of a patient, you note that he has bilateral lower motor neuron palsy of the facial nerve. Which of the following is least likely to cause this?","o":["Guillain-Barre syndrome","Sarcoidosis","Lymphoma","Diabetes"],"a":3,"e":"Diabetes is associated with unilateral LMN palsy.\n\nCommon causes of facial nerve palsy:\n\n UMN\n LMN\n\nUnilateral\n\nUsually vascular\n\nCerebral tumour\n\nMultiple sclerosis\n\nBell's palsy\n\nParotid tumour\n\nHead injuries\n\nSkull base tumours\n\nDiabetes\n\nHypertension\n\nBilateral\n\nVascular(infarct)\n\nMotor neuron disease\n\nGuillain-Barré syndrome\n\nSarcoidosis\n\nLeukemia\n\nLymphoma\n\nLeprosy","t":"Cranial Nerve Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"You are evaluating a woman with unilateral facial weakness. However, you notice forehead wrinkling is intact. On examination, which of the following findings would you not expect?","o":["Absent Bell's phenomenon","Normal emotional response","Normal corneal reflex","Normal taste sensation"],"a":1,"e":"The given features are suggestive of an upper motor neuron (UMN) lesion of the facial nerve in which the emotional response is affected. Patients with UMN lesions of the facial nerve have emotional incontinence i.e. involuntary, uncontrollable laughter, or crying with a paradoxical deficit of facial expression.\n\nNormally cerebellum modulates motor outputs in response to sensory inputs. UMN facial palsy (type of pseudobulbar palsy) is associated with disruption of cortico-ponto-cerebellar pathways leading to loss of cerebellar modulation resulting in:\n\n• Emotional dysmetria (difficulty in fine-tuning emotional responses)\n\n• Emotional disinhibition (excessive emotional output)\n\nDifference between UMN and LMN facial palsy:\n\nFeature\nUMN\nLMN\n\nSide affected\nContralateral\nIpsilateral\n\nFacial muscles involved\nLower\nBoth upper and lower\n\nForehead wrinkling\nPresent\nAbsent\n\nEmotional incontinence\nPresent\nAbsent\n\nCorneal reflex\nNormal\nAbsent\n\nTaste\nIntact\nAffected\n\nAtrophy\nAbsent\nPresent","t":"Cranial Nerve Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements is true about facial synkinesis?","o":["Refers to simultaneous bilateral facial nerve palsy","Refers to facial nerve palsy associated with trigeminal neuralgia","Occurs due to anomalous regeneration of the facial nerve fibers","Loss of emotional response is seen in this condition"],"a":2,"e":"Facial synkinesis occurs due to anomalous regeneration of facial nerve fibers.\n\nFollowing facial nerve injury , the recovering nerve fibers may aberrantly regenerate and supply other muscles. This results in involuntary muscular movements in the face during voluntary facial movements.\n\nSome examples include:\n\n• Retraction of the mouth on closure of the eyelids:\n\n• Seen when fibers originally connected with the orbicularis oculi aberrantly innervate the orbicularis oris\n\n• 'Crocodile tears' also known as Bogorad syndrome:\n\n• It refers to anomalous tearing occurring with activity of the facial muscles, such as eating.\n\n• Due to aberrant innervation of the lacrimal gland.\n\nNote: Frey's syndrome is an aberrant connection between the auriculotemporal nerve and the great auricular nerve, resulting in gustatory sweating.","t":"Cranial Nerve Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is false regarding hemifacial spasm?","o":["The contractions are painful and irregular","It is a complication of Bell's palsy","Carbamazepine is used for treatment","Local injection of botulinum toxin is effective"],"a":0,"e":"Hemifacial spasms are painless irregular contractions occurring on one side of the face.\n\nThey commonly occur as sequelae to Bell's palsy. It can also be seen in irritative lesions of the facial nerve such as acoustic neuroma and basilar aneurysm.\n\nTreatment is with carbamazepine or gabapentin. In resistant cases, baclofen may be tried. Local injection of botulinum toxin and surgical decompression are alternative options.","t":"Cranial Nerve Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following are features of Melkerrson-Rosenthal syndrome?\n\n1. Recurrent facial nerve palsy\n\n2. Congenital facial weakness\n\n3. Facial edema\n\n4. Plication of tongue\n\n5. Vesicular rash in auditory canal","o":["1, 5","2, 4, 5","2, 3, 4","1, 3, 4"],"a":3,"e":"Melkersson-Rosenthal syndrome is a rare disease characterised by the following triad:\n\n• Recurrent facial nerve palsy\n\n• Facial edema (particularly labial)\n\n• Plication of tongue or fissured tongue\n\nFacial paralysis along with vesicular rash in the external auditory canal and pinna is seen in Ramsay-Hunt syndrome. It occurs due to herpes zoster infection and is also called herpes zoster oticus.\n\nCongenital facial weakness combined with abnormal ocular abduction is seen in Moebius syndrome. Patients may present with bilateral concurrent facial nerve paralysis.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/71797e9b455c4799809847660220b561x507x278.PNG"],"t":"Cranial Nerve Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A 70-year-old man presented with complaints of sudden stabs of pain in the throat on yawning. Each episode only lasted for a few seconds and was exacerbated on trying to clear the throat. Which of the following findings can you see on examination?","o":["Tongue deviated to one side","Loss of taste sensation","Bradycardia","Decreased movement at TMJ"],"a":2,"e":"The clinical features are suggestive of glossopharyngeal neuralgia which is associated with cardiac symptoms such as bradycardia, asystole, hypotension, and fainting.\n\nIt involves the glossopharyngeal (IX) and sometimes portions of the vagus (X) nerve. The condition resembles trigeminal neuralgia but is less common. There is no demonstrable sensory or motor deficit.\n\nIt can result from vascular compression, multiple sclerosis or tumors.\n\nThe drug of choice is carbamazepine. If unsuccessful, surgical procedures are indicated:\n\n• Microvascular decompression (if vascular compression is evident)\n\n• Rhizotomy of glossopharyngeal and vagal fibers in the jugular bulb","t":"Cranial Nerve Disorders","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A 25-day-old baby is brought to the hospital with complaints of poor feeding and lethargy. On examination, he is found to have bulging fontanelles and hypothermia. Which of the following is the most common cause of his condition?","o":["Group B streptococci","L. monocytogenes","E. coli","N. meningitidis"],"a":2,"e":"The clinical features are suggestive of neonatal meningitis and Escherichia coli is the most common cause of this condition.\n\nThe causes of neonatal meningitis in decreasing order of frequency are:\n\n• Escherichia coli\n\n• Group B Streptococci\n\n• Listeria monocytogenes\n\nEmpirical treatment of neonatal meningitis is with ampicillin + 3rd generation cephalosporins.","t":"Infections Of The Nervous System","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is the most likely cause of meningitis in a 30-year-old patient?","o":["Streptococcus pneumoniae","Staphylococcus aureus","Neisseria meningitidis","Haemophilus influenzae"],"a":0,"e":"The most common cause of meningitis in patients aged 2-50 years is Streptococcus pneumoniae.\n\nThe common causes of meningitis in this age group include:\n\n• Streptococcus pneumoniae\n\n• Neisseria meningitidis\n\n• Group B streptococci\n\n• Hemophilus influenzae\n\n• Listeria monocytogenes","t":"Infections Of The Nervous System","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A 66-year-old patient presents with fever, neck rigidity, and headache for the past 3 days. He has a history of metastatic non-small cell lung cancer for which he had received intrathecal chemotherapy via an Ommaya reservoir 10 days ago. On examination, Kernig and Brudzinski's signs are positive. Which organism is most likely implicated here?","o":["Streptococcus pneumonia","Coagulase negative staphylococci","Staphylococcus aureus","Gram negative bacilli"],"a":2,"e":"The clinical features are suggestive of acute bacterial meningitis. This condition is most likely caused by Staphylococcus aureus in a patient with an Ommaya reservoir.\n\nAn Ommaya reservoir is an intraventricular catheter system used for the delivery of drugs into the cerebrospinal fluid. S. aureus and coagulase-negative staphylococci (CoNS) are important causes of meningitis that occurs following invasive neuro-surgical procedures, or as a complication of the use of Ommaya reservoirs. S aureus is a more common cause than CoNS.","t":"Infections Of The Nervous System","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A 45-year-old man presents with fever, headache, photophobia and neck rigidity since the last 2 days. He had undergone a heart transplant one year back and is on immunomodulatory drugs. During examination, the following sign is elicited. What is the therapy of choice for this patient?","o":["Cefepime + Vancomycin + Ampicillin","Ceftriaxone + Vancomycin","Isoniazid + Rifampicin + Prazinamide+ Ethambutol","Liposomal amphotericin B + Fluorocytosine"],"a":0,"e":"The sign being elicited here is Kernig's sign and this along with the given clinical features are suggestive of acute bacterial meningitis. Empirical treatment with cefepime, vancomycin and ampicillin is recommended for patients on immunomodulatory drugs as they are at risk for Listeria infection.\n\nRisk factors for Listeria meningitis include:\n\n• Age>50\n\n• Diabetes\n\n• Chronic illness\n\n• Malignancy\n\n• Immunosuppressive therapy","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c6631b4d7a3243e5b57e4666d9c9d371.GIF"],"t":"Infections Of The Nervous System","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A 21-year-old female complains of fever, headache and vomiting from the past 3 days. She has been living in the army recruitment camp for the last three weeks. On examination, she has neck stiffness and a maculopapular rash was noted over the trunk and extremities. Which of the following is false about the disease she's likely suffering from?","o":["Carriers of this disease can be treated with Rifampicin","It can lead to adrenal hemorrhage","Terminal complement deficiency increases the risk of this disease","Metronidazole is the drug of choice"],"a":3,"e":"The clinical features are suggestive of meningococcal meningitis and the drug of choice is a third-generation cephalosporin such as ceftriaxone. Chloramphenicol is used in patients with penicillin allergy.\n\nIt is caused by Neisseria meningitidis, a gram-negative, oxidase-positive, catalase-positive diplococci. Outbreaks usually occur in mass gatherings such as in army camps or hostel dorms.\n\nThe risk of infection is increased in patients with terminal complement deficiency, hypogammaglobulinemia and hyposplenism. This is because opsonization by complement is a major mechanism by which the bacteria is cleared from the blood.\n\nCharacteristic clinical findings include:\n\n• Maculopapular or purpuric rash\n\n• Shock due to capillary leak\n\n• Bilateral adrenal hemorrhage (Waterhouse–Friderichsen syndrome)\n\nRifampicin, ciprofloxacin and cephalosporins can be used to treat carriers.","t":"Infections Of The Nervous System","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A 45-year-old patient with a history of fever for 1 month presents with headache and ataxia. Imaging reveals dilated ventricles and significant basal exudates. Which of the following would most likely be seen in the CSF?","o":["Lymphocytosis, low glucose, high protein","Lymphocytosis, normal glucose, high protein","Lymphocytosis, low glucose, normal protein","Lymphocytosis, high glucose, high protein"],"a":0,"e":"The features and imaging findings are suggestive of tuberculous meningitis. CSF analysis would likely show lymphocytosis, low glucose and high protein.\n\nIf the CSF is allowed to stand, a 'spider’s web clot' or 'cobweb' coagulam may form due to the high level of protein.","t":"Infections Of The Nervous System","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A college student presents with fever, headache, and confusion. Lumbar puncture was positive for HSV DNA, detected by PCR. An MRI brain is done to localize the lesions. Which area on the brain is most likely to show lesions?","o":["Parietal lobe","Frontal lobe","Temporal lobe","Cerebellar cortex"],"a":2,"e":"The clinical hallmark of HSV encephalitis has been the acute onset of fever and focal neurologic symptoms and signs, especially in the temporal lobe.\n\nInvestigation of choice includes HSV-DNA demonstration in CSF by PCR.\n\nThe image below is a coronal T2-weighted MR image showing a high signal intensity in the temporal lobes.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/e8c33e7dde164fd8adfa7d0badbcc4ddx480x512.JPEG"],"t":"Infections Of The Nervous System","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A 35-year-old man presents with fever, headache, seizures and aphasia. MRI shows hyperintensity in the left frontotemporal region. Which of the following would likely be seen in the CSF analysis?","o":["Glucose &lt; 20 mg/dl","0-5 WBC/microliter","100 RBC/ microliter","Protein &lt; 40mg/dl"],"a":2,"e":"The clinical features are suggestive of herpes simplex encephalitis and the CSF analysis would likely show 100 RBC/microliter. HSV encephalitis is associated with hemorrhagic necrosis of inferomedial temporal and frontal lobe resulting in significant number of RBCs in the CSF.\n\nIt is caused by both HSV 1 and HSV 2. Patients present with fever, headache, seizures, personality, and behavioral changes.\n\nCSF analysis shows lymphocytic pleocytosis, moderately elevated protein levels and normal glucose concentration. EEG shows periodic lateralized spike-wave discharges.\n\nThe drug of choice for the condition is acyclovir.","t":"Infections Of The Nervous System","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"A 60-year-old man with progressive dementia of recent onset presents with intermittent irregular jerky movements. The EEG is given below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["HSV encephalitis","Hepatic encephalopathy","Cerebral toxoplasmosis","Cruetzfeldt-Jakob disease"],"a":3,"e":"The clinical features and EEG point towards a diagnosis of Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease (CJD). The EEG shows periodic biphasic or triphasic sharp-wave complexes seen as spikes.\n\nCJD is a spongiform encephalopathy caused by a prion. It presents as rapidly progressive dementia and is fatal within 1 year of onset of symptoms.\n\nPatients present with myoclonus, akinetic mutism, cerebellar or visual disturbances and pyramidal/extrapyramidal dysfunction (hypokinesia). Insomnia, apathy and other behavioral abnormalities may be seen.\n\nFour types of Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease have been described:\n\n1. Sporadic (sCJD): Accounts for 85-90% of cases\n\n• MRI shows cortical ribbon sign\n\n2. Variant (vCJD): mad cow disease, Kuru\n\n• MRI shows hockey stick sign\n\n3. Familial (fCJD): 10% of cases (these individuals carry a PRPc mutation)\n\n4. Iatrogenic\n\nCSF analysis shows a positive assay for 14-3-3 protein. Management is mainly supportive.\n\nNote: Hepatic encephalopathy will show a triphasic wave pattern (negative-positive-negative wave). They are also seen in Hashimoto encephalopathy and CO2 narcosis.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6c0cc1a9eae540749eaa7b0820921828.JPEG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/13f465558a4645f180780e4fd08879f0x1280x1139.JPEG"],"t":"Infections Of The Nervous System","c":"Neurology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not a hypoproliferative anemia?","o":["Sideroblastic anemia","Anemia of chronic disease","Hemolytic anemia","Megaloblastic anemia"],"a":2,"e":"Hemolytic anemia is not a hypoproliferative anemia.\n\nAnemia can be defined as hemoglobin of < 13 g/dl in men and < 12 g/dl in women. Based on the mechanism of reduction of hemoglobin, anemia can be classified as:\n\n• Hypoproliferative anemias - due to impaired production of RBCs\n\n• Hemolytic anemias and hemoglobinopathies - due to increased destruction of RBCs\n\n• Anemia due to blood loss\n\nHypoproliferative anemias are characterized by an abnormally low reticulocyte response (reticulocyte index <2–2.5) to reduced RBC production. They include:\n\n• Iron-deficiency anemia\n\n• Anemia of chronic disease (inflammation, renal disease, or malignancies)\n\n• Thalassemia\n\n• Sideroblastic anemia\n\n• Aplastic anemia\n\n• Megaloblastic macrocytic anemias (folate and vitamin B12 deficiency)\n\n• Non-megaloblastic macrocytic anemias (alcohol use)","t":"Hypoproliferative Anemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following patients is unlikely to have the following finding on a peripheral smear study?","o":["A 55-year-old alcoholic man with ascites and jaundice","A 19-year-old girl who underwent emergency splenectomy after a road accident","A 25-year-old man with hemoglobin C disease","A 70-year-old woman undergoing dialysis for the past 3 years"],"a":3,"e":"The black arrows in the given image denote target cells or codocytes. They are RBCs with a dense center (hyperchromic bull's eye) surrounded by an area of pallor. They are not a feature of renal disease (Patient D).\n\nTarget cells are seen in the following conditions:\n\n• Thalassemia\n\n• Iron deficiency anemia\n\n• Liver disease\n\n• Post-splenectomy\n\n• Hemoglobin C disease\n\n• As artifacts in improperly prepared slides","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/86b86d9a3b3b4c4a982216e933147966.PNG"],"t":"Hypoproliferative Anemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"A first-year pathology resident prepares the following smear from a patient's blood sample. Which of the following is it suggestive of?","o":["Thalassemia","Autoimmune hemolysis","Iron deficiency anaemia","Lead poisoning"],"a":1,"e":"The small, dense RBCs with absent central pallor seen in the given peripheral smear are called spherocytes. They appear due to decreased membrane surface area and are seen in the following conditions:\n\n• Autoimmune hemolytic anemia\n\n• Hereditary spherocytosis\n\n• Clostridial sepsis\n\n• Snake bites","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/04b01563c72e4d0c9b908173126ca460.PNG"],"t":"Hypoproliferative Anemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"The following finding is not typically seen in which of the following conditions?","o":["Myelodysplasia","Myelofibrosis","Thalassemia","Sideroblastic anemia"],"a":3,"e":"The given peripheral smear shows teardrop cells or dacrocytes. They are not seen in sideroblastic anemia.\n\nTeardrop cells are seen in the following conditions:\n\n• Myelofibrosis\n\n• Myelodysplastic syndromes\n\n• Hemolytic anemia\n\n• Thalassemia\n\n• Severe iron deficiency","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/82e5f60b36044a2eb45bd823b27fe0ee.PNG"],"t":"Hypoproliferative Anemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"The following blood picture is seen in patients with which of the following conditions?\n\n1. Sickle cell disease\n\n2. Thalassemia\n\n3. Megaloblastic anemia\n\n4. Lead poisoning\n\n5. Dyshematopoiesis","o":["1, 2, 5","2, 4, 5","1, 3, 5","1, 3, 4"],"a":2,"e":"The given image shows Howell-Jolly bodies. They are remnants of RBC nuclei that occur in conditions with either defective production (dysplasia) or destruction of RBCs. They are observed in the following conditions:\n\n• Sickle cell disease (postsplenectomy)\n\n• Hyposplenism\n\n• Megaloblastic anemia\n\n• Dyshematopoesis\n\n• Hemolytic anemia\n\nThalassemia presents with microcytic, hypochromic RBCs, and target cells.\n\nLead poisoning presents with basophilic stippling or punctate basophilia on peripheral smears. These are RNA fragments seen as numerous basophilic granules dispersed in the cytoplasm of RBCs, as shown below.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1e5bf92af86f4e4780f8ba396e075ad5.PNG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/30755342e8584676a55db3049f6ba1c7x700x468.JPEG"],"t":"Hypoproliferative Anemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"A 30-year-old man with a BMI of 41 kg/m2 is being evaluated for bariatric surgery. Which of the following procedures does not reduce the surface area for iron absorption?","o":["Duodenal switch","Sleeve gastrectomy","Roux-en-Y gastric bypass","Biliopancreatic diversion"],"a":1,"e":"Iron absorption takes place in the duodenum. In sleeve gastrectomy, a portion of the stomach is resected but the duodenum remains intact. Hence, the surface area of iron absorption is not affected.\n\nDietary iron is efficiently absorbed in its heme form. Non-heme iron is less efficiently absorbed mainly because of interference by phosphates, tannins, oxalates, and other food constituents. Acidic pH enhances iron absorption by promoting the conversion of ferric to ferrous iron by the enzyme ferrireductase.\n\nThe following are malabsorptive procedures that reduce the duodenal surface area and hence, reduce iron absorption:\n\n• Biliopancreatic diversion\n\n• Roux-en-Y gastric bypass\n\n• Duodenal switch","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/74c32a6959fd4c878adb545688406d21x1279x1661.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b763810aa0124fb7b0069e07a9517bbfx1280x1641.JPEG"],"t":"Hypoproliferative Anemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"You have prescribed oral iron supplements for an anemic young woman. Which of the following foods should she avoid taking along with these supplements?","o":["Grapefruit juice","Fresh kiwi fruit","Broccoli","Black tea"],"a":3,"e":"Tea contains tannins, which inhibit iron absorption. Hence, it should not be taken with iron supplements. Iron absorption is also reduced by:\n\n• Calcium supplements and antacids\n\n• Milk and dairy products\n\n• Proton pump inhibitors\n\nGrapefruit and kiwi are acid-containing citrus fruits that create an acidic pH and hence, increase iron absorption.\n\nBroccoli contains ascorbic acid that also enhances iron absorption.","t":"Hypoproliferative Anemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"The levels of which of the following will be detected first in a patient with iron deficiency?","o":["Marrow iron stores","Serum iron","Serum ferritin","Transferrin saturation"],"a":2,"e":"The earliest sign of iron deficiency is a decrease in serum ferritin levels. This is called the state of negative iron balance.\n\nIron deficiency anemia develops in 3 stages:\n\n• Stage of negative iron balance - decrease in iron stores with normal serum iron and RBC morphology\n\n• Stage of iron-deficient erythropoiesis - decrease in serum iron levels with normal RBC morphology\n\n• Stage of iron deficiency anemia - fall in hemoglobin with microcytic hypochromic RBCs","t":"Hypoproliferative Anemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not seen in the stage of negative iron balance in iron-deficiency anemia?","o":["Decrease in marrow iron stores","Decrease in TIBC","Normal RBC morphology","Normal serum iron"],"a":1,"e":"TIBC (total iron-binding capacity) does not decrease during the stage of a negative iron balance of iron deficiency anemia.\n\nThe progression to iron deficiency develops in 3 stages:\n\n• Stage of negative iron balance - Decrease in iron stores with normal serum iron and RBC morphology\n\n• Stage of iron-deficient erythropoiesis - Decrease in serum iron levels with normal RBC morphology\n\n• Stage of iron deficiency anemia - Microcytic hypochromic RBCs\n\nNegative iron balance\nIron-deficient erythropoiesis\nIron deficiency anemia\n\nMarrow iron stores\nDecreased\nDecreased\nDecreased\n\nS. ferritin\nDecreased\nDecreased\nDecreased\n\nTIBC\nIncreased\nIncreased\nIncreased\n\nS. Iron\nNormal\nDecreased\nDecreased\n\nTransferrin saturation\nNormal\nDecreased\nDecreased\n\nRed cell protoporphyrin\nNormal\nIncreased\nIncreased\n\nRed cell morphology\nNormal\nNormal\nMicrocytic hypochromic","t":"Hypoproliferative Anemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Patients with which of the following do not need to be evaluated for iron deficiency?","o":["Unusual craving to eat clay and paper","Insomnia due to a compulsive urge to keep moving the legs","Incidental finding of esophageal webs on upper GI endoscopy","Tingling and numbness of lower extremities"],"a":3,"e":"Tingling and numbness of the lower extremities are suggestive of peripheral neuropathy. It is a feature of vitamin B12 deficiency, not iron deficiency.\n\nIron-deficiency anemia is associated with the following findings:\n\n• Smooth tongue - due to loss of papillae\n\n• Cheilosis - fissuring at corners of mouth\n\n• Brittle nails\n\n• Koilonychia - spooning of nails\n\n• Pica - craving for non-edible foods like ice chips, hair, paper, clay\n\n• Plummer–Vinson syndrome - dysphagia due to the formation of esophageal webs\n\n• Restless legs syndrome - compulsive urge to keep moving the legs which results in insomnia","t":"Hypoproliferative Anemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following PBF findings is least likely to be seen in patients with iron deficiency anemia?","o":["1","2","3","4"],"a":3,"e":"Image 4 shows dense RBCs with loss of central pallor or spherocytes. They are seen in autoimmune hemolytic anemia (AIHA) and hereditary spherocytosis, not iron deficiency anemia.\n\nThe peripheral blood smear in iron deficiency anemia shows:\n\n• Microcytic hypochromic cells\n\n• Target cells\n\n• Pencil-shaped or cigar-shaped cells\n\n• Poikilocytosis- variations in the shape of RBCs\n\n• Anisocytosis- variations in the size of RBCs.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/fe96316e4893406abeab921f3a229fcc.JPEG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/79e8a00a2c01499faaed2a29caaf5eeex1280x1905.JPEG"],"t":"Hypoproliferative Anemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"A 28-year-old woman is brought to the ER after she faints at home. Her husband says that she is very body-conscious and does intermittent fasting. She has been on a vegan diet for the past 2 months. Lab values are given below. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nHb - 6 g/dL\n\nMCV - 67 fL\n\nMCH - 24 pg\n\nRDW - elevated\n\nTIBC - high\n\nPeripheral smear - target cells noted","o":["Anemia of chronic disease","B12 deficiency anemia","Iron deficiency anemia","Folate deficiency anemia"],"a":2,"e":"This clinical scenario is suggestive of iron deficiency anemia due to the following findings:\n\n• Low MCV (N: 87-96 fL)\n\n• Low MCH (N: 27-33 pg)\n\n• Elevated RDW\n\n• High TIBC\n\nOption A: Anemia of chronic disease is associated with low TIBC.\n\nOptions B and D: Anemia due to B12 and folate deficiency presents with a high MCV (macrocytosis).","t":"Hypoproliferative Anemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"A 62-year-old woman is found to have a Hb of 7.2 g/dl and high ferritin levels. She has been undergoing chemotherapy for breast cancer since last year. Which of the following is responsible for this presentation?","o":["Ferritin","DMT1","Hemojuvelin","Hepcidin"],"a":3,"e":"This clinical scenario is suggestive of anemia of chronic disease. The chief mediator of this condition is hepcidin.\n\nAnemia of chronic disease is associated with chronic inflammatory states such as inflammatory bowel disease, rheumatoid arthritis, chronic infections, and malignancies.\n\nHepcidin is a positive acute-phase reactant (ARP) that increases in chronic inflammatory states. It binds with ferroportin which results in both reduced iron intake in the gut and reduced iron transfer from macrophages to erythroid progenitor cells in the bone marrow. Hence, hepcidin is a negative regulator of iron balance.","t":"Hypoproliferative Anemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Normocytic normochromic anemia is seen in patients with which of the following?","o":["Liver disease","Kidney disease","Lead toxicity","Thalassemia"],"a":1,"e":"Normocytic normochromic anemia is seen in chronic kidney disease (CKD).\n\nIt is also seen in the following conditions:\n\n• RBC membrane disorders - hereditary spherocytosis, elliptocytosis\n\n• RBC enzyme disorders - G6PD deficiency, Pyruvate kinase deficiency\n\n• Parvovirus B19 infection\n\n• Anemia of chronic disease - can also present as microcytic anemia","t":"Hypoproliferative Anemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"A 38-year-old woman presented to the OPD with progressive tiredness over the last 2 months. She also reported having heavy menstrual bleeding during her previous 2 cycles. She is a known case of rheumatoid arthritis. Lab investigations are given below. What is the likely diagnosis?\n\nHb - 7 gm/dL\n\nMCV - 55 fL\n\nTIBC - 290 μg/dL\n\nS. Ferritin - 250 μg/L\n\nTransferrin saturation - 20%","o":["Iron deficiency anemia","Anemia of chronic disease","Sideroblastic anemia","Anemia due to blood loss"],"a":1,"e":"This clinical scenario is suggestive of anemia of chronic disease due to the following findings:\n\n• Low MCV (N: 87-96 fl)\n\n• Low TIBC (N: 300-360μg/dL)\n\n• Elevated ferritin level (N: 50-200 μg/L)\n\n• Normal transferrin saturation (N: 25-50%)","t":"Hypoproliferative Anemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"A 60-year-old man with stage 4 chronic kidney disease on hemodialysis is found to have a hemoglobin of 6.3 mg/dl. Which of the following will be additionally seen in this patient?","o":["Increased serum ferritin","Increased TIBC","Increased serum iron","Decreased reticulocytes"],"a":3,"e":"Anemia of CKD is a form of hypoproliferative anemia presenting with decreased reticulocytes.\n\nIt mainly occurs due to a failure of erythropoietin production by the diseased kidney and a reduction in the survival of RBCs. Also, patients with CKD maintained on chronic hemodialysis may develop iron deficiency from blood loss through the dialysis procedure.\n\nThe following features are seen:\n\n• Normocytic and normochromic RBCs\n\n• Decreased reticulocytes\n\n• Serum iron, transferrin saturation, TIBC are usually normal or low\n\n• Decreased serum ferritin\n\nThe degree of anemia usually correlates with the severity of the renal failure.\n\nNote: Anaemia in CKD is not the same as anaemia of chronic disease.","t":"Hypoproliferative Anemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"What is the predominant inheritance pattern of sideroblastic anemia?","o":["Autosomal dominant","Polygenic","X linked","Autosomal recessive"],"a":2,"e":"Inherited forms of sideroblastic anemia are usually X-linked. Rarely, autosomal recessive forms may also occur.\n\nX-linked sideroblastic anemia results from the deficient activity of the erythroid form of ALA-synthase (ALA-synthase 2) and is associated with ineffective erythropoiesis","t":"Hypoproliferative Anemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following would not be seen in patients with sideroblastic anemia?","o":["Erythroid hyperplasia","Low serum iron","High transferrin saturation","Dimorphic blood picture"],"a":1,"e":"High serum iron levels are seen in patients with sideroblastic anemia.\n\nSideroblastic anemia presents with the following features:\n\n• Serum iron level - high/normal\n\n• Transferrin saturation - high\n\n• Serum ferritin levels - high\n\n• TIBC - low/normal\n\n• Peripheral smear - dimorphic population (both microcytes and macrocytes).\n\n• Bone marrow study - marked erythroid hyperplasia.\n\nThe iron profile of sideroblastic anemia is opposite to that of iron deficiency anemia.","t":"Hypoproliferative Anemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"The bone marrow biopsy sample of a patient with chronic anemia is stained with Prussian blue, as shown below. The stained deposits are present in which of the following?","o":["Lysosome","Ribosome","Mitochondria","Endoplasmic reticulum"],"a":2,"e":"The given image shows ringed sideroblasts, which are erythroid precursors in the marrow. In these cells, iron deposits in the mitochondria form a ring around the nucleus and are stained by Prussian blue.\n\nThe mature red blood cell counterparts of ringed sideroblasts in the circulation are called siderocytes. These cells have iron-containing basophilic granules called Pappenheimer bodies, as shown below.\n\nRinged sideroblasts and Pappenheimer bodies are pathognomonic of sideroblastic anemia.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3506aa553aff4185a2225906159f3ecf.JPEG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8e32f789df744f87820cc3cf873c6424x961x720.JPEG"],"t":"Hypoproliferative Anemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following drugs is known to cause sideroblastic anemia?","o":["Cyclophosphamide","Chloramphenicol","Ethionamide","Rifampicin"],"a":1,"e":"Chloramphenicol is known to cause sideroblastic anemia.\n\nThe agents known to cause sideroblastic anemia are:\n\n• Isoniazid\n\n• Chloramphenicol\n\n• Linezolid\n\n• Excessive alcohol","t":"Hypoproliferative Anemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"The peripheral blood smear of a patient is given below. All of the following can cause this except:","o":["Vitamin B12 deficiency","Chronic alcoholism","Folate deficiency","Orotic aciduria"],"a":1,"e":"The given image shows hypersegmented neutrophils (> 5 nuclear lobes), which are seen in megaloblastic anemia. Chronic alcoholism causes non-megaloblastic macrocytic anemia.\n\nMegaloblastic anemia is a form of macrocytic anemia with impaired nucleic acid metabolism and defective erythropoiesis. The causes include vitamin B12 deficiency, folate deficiency, and orotic aciduria. Nuclear maturation lags behind cytoplasmic maturation giving the appearance of hypersegmented neutrophils. Peripheral smear findings include:\n\n• Oval macrocytes\n\n• Anisocytosis\n\n• Hypersegmented neutrophils\n\nOther features of ineffective erythropoiesis seen in megaloblastic anemias include:\n\n• Howell-Jolly bodies are nuclear remnants in red cells. They are usually single, round, dark purple-red in color and peripheral in location, as shown below.\n\n• Basophilic stippling refers to blue granules of various sizes dispersed throughout the cytoplasm of the red cell. They represent ribosomal precipitates.\n\n• Cabot rings are red cell inclusions appearing as fine, purple filamentous loops or figure-of-eight forms, as shown below. They are presumed to be remnants of mitotic spindles.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/efa17d60483d4d6a8921ec0607d93f61.PNG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/255842ca896a466cb7ee3efb50ba23cbx610x600.PNG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/06cdef378c9e47a691a35cdd44420e7ax900x613.JPEG"],"t":"Macrocytic Anemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Patients with which of the following conditions are unlikely to develop cobalamin deficiency?","o":["Tropical sprue","Crohn's disease","Diphyllobothrium latum infection","Ancylostoma duodenale infection"],"a":3,"e":"Ancylostoma duodenale (hookworm) infection does not affect the absorption of cobalamin or vitamin B12. Small amounts of blood loss during the attachment of hookworms to the intestinal mucosa can cause iron-deficiency anemia.\n\nVitamin B12-intrinsic factor (IF) complex is absorbed in the ileum. Tropical sprue, Diphyllobothrium latum (fish tapeworm) infection and Crohn's disease affect the ileum leading to defective absorption of vitamin B12.","t":"Macrocytic Anemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following would not be seen in a patient with cyanocobalamin deficiency?","o":["Glossitis","Conjugated hyperbilirubinemia","Skin hyperpigmentation","Mild fever"],"a":1,"e":"Unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia is seen in cyanocobalamin or vitamin B12 deficiency, not conjugated hyperbilirubinemia.\n\nVitamin B12 deficiency causes megaloblastic anemia due to ineffective erythropoiesis. This leads to the formation of nucleated RBCs in the marrow, which undergo early lysis. This leads to increased unconjugated bilirubin in the plasma.\n\nThe clinical features of cyanocobalamin and folate deficiency include:\n\nSystem\nClinical features\n\nOral\n\nGlossitis\n\nAngular cheilosis\n\nGastrointestinal\n\nAnorexia\n\nWeight loss\n\nDiarrhea\n\nConstipation\n\nSkin\nHyperpigmentation (reversible)\n\nHematological\n\nThrombocytopenia\n\nLeucopenia\n\nIneffective erythropoiesis and unconjugated jaundice\n\nCardiovascular\n\nAtherosclerosis leading to PVD, CAD (due to elevated homocysteine)\n\nNeurological (only B12)\n\nPeripheral neuropathy\n\nSubacute combined degeneration (degeneration of the posterior and pyramidal tracts of the spinal cord)\n\nPregnancy (only folate)\n\nRecurrent fetal loss\n\nNeural tube defects\n\nOthers\n\nMild fever in severe cases\n\nPsychiatric disturbances","t":"Macrocytic Anemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"A 56-year-old male patient is being evaluated for peripheral neuropathy and excessive tiredness. He has uncontrolled diabetes and consumes alcohol nearly every day. Lab investigations reveal the following findings. What is the likely diagnosis?\n\nHemoglobin - 9g/dl\n\nMean corpuscular volume (MCV) - 112 fL\n\nMean corpuscular hemoglobin concentration (MCHC) = 34 g/dl\n\nSerum homocysteine - Elevated\n\nSerum methyl malonic acid - Normal","o":["Folate deficiency","Alcohol-induced macrocytosis","Vitamin B12 deficiency","Thiamine deficiency"],"a":0,"e":"This clinical scenario is suggestive of folate deficiency.\n\nIt is associated with the following clinical and laboratory findings:\n\n• Raised MCV (normal: 80-100 fL) - macrocytosis\n\n• Normal MCHC (normal: 33-36 g/dl)\n\n• Increased MCH (mean corpuscular hemoglobin)\n\n• Elevated RDW (red cell distribution width)\n\n• Decreased reticulocyte count - due to destruction of abnormal erythroid precursors\n\n• Elevated homocysteine\n\n• Normal MMA (methyl malonic acid)\n\nIn vitamin B12 deficiency, elevated homocysteine and elevated MMA levels are seen.\n\nPeripheral neuropathy is a feature of B12 deficiency, not folate deficiency. In this patient, it may be due to diabetic neuropathy.","t":"Macrocytic Anemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following drugs is not associated with megaloblastic anemia?","o":["Nitrofurantoin","Phenytoin","Tetracycline","Temafloxacin"],"a":3,"e":"Temafloxacin is not associated with megaloblastic anemia.\n\nMegaloblastic anemia is associated with the following drugs:\n\n• Anticonvulsant drugs - phenytoin, primidone, barbiturates\n\n• Sulfasalazine\n\n• Nitrofurantoin\n\n• Tetracycline\n\n• Folate antagonists - trimethoprim, methotrexate, pyrimethamine, pentamidine, cotrimoxazole\n\nTemafloxacin is a fluoroquinolone with severe adverse effects like hemolytic anemia and allergic reactions. It was withdrawn from the market and is no longer used.","t":"Macrocytic Anemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"A 45-year-old woman is rushed to the ER following a seizure. She is a known case of epilepsy but is non-compliant with her medications. Her lab values are given below. All of the following drugs can cause this presentation except:\n\nHemoglobin - 10g/dl\n\nMCV - 110 fL\n\nMCH - 40 pg\n\nMCHC - 34 g/dl","o":["Lamotrigine","Phenytoin","Primidone","Amobarbital"],"a":0,"e":"This patient likely has folate deficiency secondary to the use of an anti-folate anti-convulsant drug. Lamotrigine has no effect on folate metabolism and does not cause its deficiency.\n\nThe following anticonvulsants have anti-folate properties:\n\n• Phenytoin\n\n• Barbiturates (amobarbital)\n\n• Primidone\n\nFolate deficiency causes megaloblastic macrocytic anemia which results in the following findings in RBCs:\n\n• Anemia (low Hb = 10g/dl)\n\n• Macrocytic (high MCV = 110 fl)\n\n• Hyperchromic (high MCH = 40 pg)","t":"Macrocytic Anemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"A dose of oral 58-Co-labeled cobalamin was administered to a 65-year-old woman. The 24-hour urinary excretion of radiolabeled cobalamin was found to be &lt; 10%. The test was repeated after a 5-day course of antibiotics and showed urinary excretion &gt; 10%. What is the likely diagnosis?","o":["Pernicious anemia","Lack of pancreatic enzymes","Bacterial overgrowth","Malnutrition"],"a":2,"e":"This clinical scenario refers to Schilling's test done for the determination of the cause of B12 deficiency. Improvement of urinary B12 excretion > 10% after antibiotic administration is suggestive of small intestinal bacterial overgrowth (SIBO).","t":"Macrocytic Anemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"A child diagnosed with megaloblastic anemia failed to respond to treatment with vitamin C, vitamin B12, and folic acid. He was started on uridine monophosphate which improved his symptoms. What is the likely diagnosis?","o":["Thiamine deficiency","Liver disease","Pernicious anemia","Orotic aciduria"],"a":3,"e":"Megaloblastic anemia that is responsive to treatment with uridine is suggestive of orotic aciduria.\n\nOrotic aciduria is a rare inborn error of metabolism in children. It occurs due to a defect in the pyrimidine synthesis pathway causing a failure of conversion of orotic acid to uridine. It manifests in early childhood with anemia and failure to thrive.","t":"Macrocytic Anemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"A 28-year-old woman presents with fatigue and a red, painful tongue. On examination, there is reduced vibration sense and proprioception in the feet. She is under treatment for Hashimoto's thyroiditis. Peripheral smear reveals macrocytes and hypersegmented neutrophils. Which of the following is unlikely to be seen in this patient?","o":["Intrinsic factor blocking antibodies","Antibodies against gastric parietal cells","Intrinsic factor binding antibodies","Antibodies against gastrin"],"a":3,"e":"This clinical scenario in a patient with a history of autoimmune thyroiditis is suspicious for pernicious anemia. It is an autoimmune cause of B12 deficiency. Antibodies against gastrin are not seen in these patients. The following autoantibodies are seen:\n\n• Type I or blocking antibodies - prevent the combination of IF and cobalamin\n\n• Type II or binding antibodies - prevent attachment of IF to the ileal mucosa\n\n• Anti-parietal cell antibodies - target the gastric proton pump located in these cells\n\nThis leads to the deficiency of the intrinsic factor of Castle (IF) and hydrochloric acid produced by the parietal cells. The resulting atrophic gastritis causes high gastrin and low pepsinogen levels. Lack of IF further causes vitamin B12 deficiency and megaloblastic anemia.","t":"Macrocytic Anemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"A pathologist receives a gastric biopsy sample of a patient with pernicious anemia. Which of the following findings is he unlikely to find?","o":["Intestinal metaplasia","Mixed inflammatory cell infiltrate","Hypertrophy of glandular elements","Increased mucus cells"],"a":2,"e":"Gastric biopsy in pernicious anemia shows loss of glandular elements, not hypertrophy.\n\nThis is an autoimmune condition that occurs in genetically susceptible individuals. One of the common triggers of the disease process is Helicobacter pylori infection. Gastric biopsy findings in pernicious anemia include the following:\n\n• Atrophy of all layers of the body and fundus\n\n• Loss of glandular elements\n\n• Absence of parietal and chief cells and replacement by mucus cells\n\n• Mixed inflammatory cell infiltrate (excess of CD4 cells against gastric H+/K+-ATPase)\n\n• Intestinal metaplasia\n\n• Antral mucosa is usually well preserved.","t":"Macrocytic Anemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Patients with pernicious anemia are at a higher risk of which of the following?","o":["Diffuse gastric adenocarcinoma","Gastric carcinoid tumors","Primary gastric lymphoma","Gastrointestinal stromal tumor"],"a":1,"e":"Patients with pernicious anemia have an increased risk of intestinal-type of gastric adenocarcinoma and gastric carcinoid tumors. These patients must undergo regular endoscopic screening for these conditions.\n\nThe presumed mechanism is prolonged achlorhydria due to parietal cell loss. This leads to hypergastrinemia and cellular hyperplasia.","t":"Macrocytic Anemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is the most common cause of folate deficiency?","o":["Malabsorption","Nutritional","Use of anti-folate drugs","Deficiency of enzyme DHF reductase"],"a":1,"e":"Reduced nutritional intake is the most common cause of folate deficiency.","t":"Macrocytic Anemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Dietary folates are absorbed from which of the following sites?","o":["Duodenum","Proximal ileum","Terminal ileum","Ascending colon"],"a":0,"e":"Dietary folates are absorbed from the duodenum and jejunum in the form of monoglutamates.\n\nVitamin B12 absorption takes place in the terminal ileum.","t":"Macrocytic Anemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"A 49-year-old woman is brought with increased forgetfulness and clumsiness for the past 1 month. Since the death of her husband 3 months ago, she has been tired and eating poorly. She began taking over-the-counter iron and folic acid tablets 2 months ago. Examination reveals positive Romberg's sign with eyes closed and loss of vibration in lower extremities. What is the likely diagnosis?","o":["B12 deficiency","Severe folate deficiency","Thiamine deficiency","Pyridoxine deficiency"],"a":0,"e":"This clinical scenario is suggestive of dorsal column involvement of the spinal cord due to underlying vitamin B12 deficiency.\n\nVitamin B12 deficiency causes subacute combined degeneration of the spinal cord (SACD). It affects the following structures:\n\n• Dorsal column - sensory loss, paresthesias, incontinence, sensory ataxia (positive Romberg sign with eyes closed)\n\n• Lateral column - spasticity and paraplegias in later stages\n\nFolate deficiency is typically not associated with neurological manifestations. In patients with combined deficiency of B12 and folate, B12 deficiency must always be corrected first. This is because the administration of folate supplements may improve hematologic abnormalities but worsens neurological manifestations.\n\nTreatment of cobalamin deficiency is as follows:\n\n• Replenishment of body stores: Six 1000-μg IM injections of hydroxocobalamin given at 3- to 7-day intervals.\n\n• Maintenance therapy: 1000 μg hydroxocobalamin IM once every 3 months\n\nTreatment of folate deficiency is as follows:\n\n• 5–15 mg oral folic acid daily for a minimum of 4 months.\n\nOption C: Thiamine deficiency causes Wernicke's syndrome with ophthalmoplegia, ataxia, and nystagmus. As this is due to cerebellar damage, Romberg's sign would be positive even with eyes open.\n\nOption D: Pyridoxine deficiency causes pellagra like syndrome with features of dermatitis, diarrhea, dementia.","t":"Macrocytic Anemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"A 19-year-old girl with a seizure disorder is started on phenytoin therapy. Which of the following must be supplemented in this patient?","o":["Pyridoxine","Thiamine","Cobalamin","Folic acid"],"a":3,"e":"Usage of phenytoin leads to folate deficiency and megaloblastic anemia. Hence, folic acid must be supplemented.","t":"Macrocytic Anemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"A 23-year-old woman comes to you for pre-conceptional counseling. Her previous pregnancy resulted in fetal death due to a neural tube defect. What dose of folic acid would you recommend?","o":["400 μg pre-conceptionally and in first trimester","400 μg pre-conceptionally and throughout pregnancy","4-5 mg pre-conceptionally and in first trimester","4-5 mg pre-conceptionally and throughout pregnancy"],"a":2,"e":"The recommended prophylactic dose of folic acid in women who have had a previous fetus with a neural tube defect is 4-5 mg daily, pre-conceptionally, and during the first trimester of pregnancy. Following the first trimester, these patients are then switched to 400 μg of folic acid for the remainder of the pregnancy.\n\nThis high dose of folic acid (4-5 mg) should be initiated at least one to three months before pregnancy and should be continued for 12 weeks after pregnancy. The patient can be prescribed 1 mg tablet of folic acid and advised to take them 4- 5 times a day or they can be recommended 5mg tablets/capsules once daily.\n\nProphylactic folic acid with 400 μg daily, pre-conceptionally, and throughout pregnancy is indicated in all pregnant women to prevent megaloblastic anemia and reduce the incidence of neural tube defects.","t":"Macrocytic Anemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following would not be seen in a patient with Imerslund-Grasbeck syndrome?","o":["Congenital cobalamin malabsorption","AMN gene mutation","Autosomal dominant inheritance","Non-specific proteinuria"],"a":2,"e":"Imerslund-Grasbeck syndrome (IGS) is an autosomal recessive disorder.\n\nIGS occurs due to mutations in either the cubulin (CUBN) or amnionless (AMN) genes. They form the cubam receptor responsible for the absorption of the Cbl-IF complex. Hence, this condition leads to congenital cobalamin malabsorption. It is the most common cause of megaloblastic anemia due to B12 deficiency in infancy in Western countries.\n\nAffected children present with lethargy, failure to thrive, and neurological symptoms like hypotonia, developmental delay, and movement disorders. Non-specific proteinuria with normal renal function is seen in 90% of patients.","t":"Macrocytic Anemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following will be seen in a patient with intravascular hemolytic anemia?\n\nMCV\nHaptoglobin\nLDH\nUrinary urobilinogen\n\n1.\nHigh\nLow\nLow\nLow\n\n2.\nHigh\nLow\nHigh\nHigh\n\n3.\nLow\nHigh\nLow\nLow\n\n4.\nLow\nHigh\nHigh\nHigh","o":["1","2","3","4"],"a":1,"e":"In intravascular hemolysis, there is elevated MCV, LDH, and urinary urobilinogen, with low haptoglobin.\n\nLaboratory abnormalities seen in intravascular hemolysis:\n\nLab parameters\nDerangement\nMechanism\n\nMCV\nHigh\nCompensatory reticulocytosis\n\nS. LDH\nHigh\nReleased from RBCs\n\nS. Unconjugated bilirubin\nHigh\nHemoglobin from RBCs is converted to bilirubin\n\nUrinary urobilinogen\nHigh\nUnconjugated bilirubin is excreted as urobilinogen in urine\n\nS. Haptoglobin\nLow\nFree hemoglobin in circulation binds to haptoglobin causing reduced haptoglobin levels\n\nAdditionally, hemoglobinuria and hemosiderinemia may be seen on urine examination.","t":"Hemolytic Anemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not due to an extracorpuscular defect in RBCs?","o":["Autoimmune hemolytic anemia","Paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria","Familial hemolytic uremic syndrome","Microangiopathic hemolytic anemia"],"a":1,"e":"Paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria (PNH) is a hemolytic anemia due to an intracorpuscular defect.\n\nIt is caused due to an acquired somatic mutation leading to deficiency of membrane protective factors CD59 and CD55. This makes the RBC extremely sensitive to complement activation and causes intravascular hemolysis.\n\nThe other conditions occur due to extracorpuscular defects.\n\nOption A: In autoimmune hemolytic anemia, RBCs are coated with autoantibodies. This triggers phagocytosis in the reticuloendothelial system leading to extravascular hemolysis.\n\nOption C: Familial hemolytic uremic syndrome (atypical HUS) is an inherited abnormality with excessive complement activation leading to episodic and excessive production of membrane attack complex destroying normal RBCs.\n\nOption D: Microangiopathic hemolytic anemia is non-immune hemolysis resulting from intravascular red blood cell fragmentation due to abnormalities in the microvasculature.","t":"Hemolytic Anemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"A patient with hemolytic anemia develops features of pancytopenia and venous thrombosis. What is the likely diagnosis?","o":["Shwachman-Diamond syndrome","Pyruvate kinase deficiency","G6PD anemia","Paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria"],"a":3,"e":"Paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria (PNH) is a type of hemolytic anemia associated with pancytopenia and venous thrombosis.\n\nIn PNH, there is complement-mediated intravascular hemolysis. Bone marrow dysfunction also occurs, resulting in pancytopenia. Transformation to aplastic anemia or acute myeloid leukemia (AML) may rarely occur.\n\nPNH is also associated with venous thrombosis (cerebral, renal, hepatic). This is the most common cause of death in PNH.\n\nOption A: Shwachman-Diamond syndrome is inherited aplastic anemia with neutropenia with pancreatic insufficiency and malabsorption.\n\nOptions B and C: Pyruvate kinase and G6PD deficiency are inherited RBC enzyme disorders causing extravascular hemolytic anemias. They do not affect other cell lines and are not associated with venous thrombosis.","t":"Hemolytic Anemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"A 31-year-old woman presents with a fever and cough. She also reports excessive tiredness and occasional dark-colored urine for the past 6 months. Chest X-ray reveals lower lobe pneumonia. Labs reveal the following. What is the investigation of choice for this condition?\n\nHb - 7.6 g/dL\n\nRBC - 2 million/mm3\n\nWBC - 5,000/mm3\n\nPlatelets - 100,000/mm3\n\nReticulocyte count - 3.2%\n\nS. LDH - High\n\nS. Haptoglobin - Low\n\nCoombs test - negative","o":["Acidified serum / HAM test","Eosin-5′-maleimide-binding test","Flow cytometry","Acidified glycerol lysis test"],"a":2,"e":"This clinical scenario is suggestive of intravascular hemolysis due to paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria (PNH). The gold standard for diagnosis is flow cytometry.\n\nFlow cytometry is performed with fluorescently labeled monoclonal antibodies that bind to GPI-anchored proteins (CD59, CD55). Most tests also use FLuorescent AERolysin (FLAER), a reagent that directly binds the GPI anchor. Absence of CD59 and CD55 are diagnostic of PNH.\n\nOther investigations in PNH include:\n\n• Ham test (acidified serum lysis) - lysis of RBCs in a mildly acidic pH due to complement fixation.\n\n• Bone marrow biopsy:\n\n• Hypercellularity with erythroid hyperplasia is seen in classical presentations.\n\n• Patients who develop pancytopenia will have hypocellular bone marrow.\n\n• Imaging - to evaluate venous thrombosis.\n\nOption B and D: Eosin-5′-maleimide-binding and acidified glycerol lysis tests are done in hereditary spherocytosis.","t":"Hemolytic Anemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"A 46-year-old woman presents with abdominal pain and yellowish discoloration of her skin. She gives a history of dark-colored urine which is more pronounced in the early mornings. Imaging reveals hepatic venous thrombosis. Flow cytometry reveals the absence of GPI-anchored proteins. What is the drug of choice?","o":["Rituximab","Certolizumab","Eculizumab","Alemtezumab"],"a":2,"e":"This clinical scenario is suggestive of paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria (PNH). C5 complement inhibitors like eculizumab and ravalizumab are the drugs of choice in PNH.\n\nManagement of PNH involves the following:\n\n• C5 complement inhibitors prevent the formation of membrane attack complex and complement-mediated lysis.\n\n• Bone marrow transplant is useful for patients with severe pancytopenia\n\n• Supportive measures:\n\n• Transfusion of filtered RBCs\n\n• Folic acid, and iron supplementation\n\n• Anticoagulants in case of venous thrombosis","t":"Hemolytic Anemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"A 10-year-old boy is brought for evaluation of fatigue. On examination, he is jaundiced and mild splenomegaly is noted. He was adopted and his family history is unknown. Peripheral smear findings are shown below. What is the most specific investigation for this condition?","o":["Eosin-5-maleimide test","Acidified glycerol lysis test","Osmotic fragility test","NESTROFT test"],"a":0,"e":"The peripheral smear shows hyperchromic RBCs with loss of central pallor or spherocytes. These findings are suggestive of hereditary spherocytosis. The eosin-5-maleimide test is the most specific investigation for this condition.\n\nIt is a flow cytometric test based on the interaction between the dye eosin-5-maleimide and band 3 protein.\n\nOther investigations that can be performed in this condition are:\n\nAcidified glycerol lysis test: Spherocytes from patients with hereditary spherocytosis lyse more quickly than normal red blood cells when incubated in a phosphate buffered hypotonic solution with added glycerol. It has greater sensitivity and specificity than the osmotic fragility test.\n\nOsmotic fragility test: Detects hemolysis by measuring the fraction of total hemoglobin released from red cells when suspended in progressively more dilute salt concentrations.\n\nNote: NESTROFT (Naked eye single tube red-cell osmotic fragility test) is a highly sensitive test used as a screening test for thalassemia.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/03fa09fb02f940b9aa4e42257af3ee04.JPEG"],"t":"Hemolytic Anemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is true regarding the treatment of hereditary spherocytosis?","o":["Cholecystectomy is only indicated for patients with clinical cholelithiasis at the time of splenectomy","Cholecystectomy cannot be performed via laparoscopic route for patients with HS and pigment gall stones","Splenectomy is indicated in patients with mild and asymptomatic disease only after puberty","Lifelong penicillin prophylaxis after splenectomy is mandatory for all adult patients"],"a":0,"e":"Cholecystectomy at the time of splenectomy is indicated in patients with hereditary spherocytosis (HS) only if they have symptomatic cholelithiasis at the time of surgery. It is usually carried out via a laparoscopic approach.\n\nPenicillin prophylaxis after splenectomy is controversial. Routine penicillin prophylaxis is not mandatory for adults. It is recommended up to age of five and for at least one year following splenectomy.\n\nGuidelines for the timing of splenectomy in HS:\n\n• Mild cases - avoid splenectomy.\n\n• Moderate cases - delay until puberty\n\n• Severe cases - at 4–6 years of age","t":"Hemolytic Anemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"The peripheral smear of a patient is given below. What is the most common mutation associated with the hereditary form of this condition?","o":["Spectrin","Band 4.2","Band 4.1","Ankyrin"],"a":0,"e":"The given peripheral smear shows oval, elongated, rod, or cigar-shaped RBCs suggestive of elliptocytosis. The hereditary form is most commonly associated with the spectrin genes.\n\nThe following proteins are implicated in hereditary elliptocytosis (HE):\n\n• α-Spectrin (SPTA1 gene) - 65%\n\n• β-Spectrin (SPTB gene) - 30%\n\n• Band 4.1\n\nThe treatment of choice is splenectomy, preferably after 5 years of age.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/f5f168637ef3475cabdb47b10b90886b.JPEG"],"t":"Hemolytic Anemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"A young couple comes to you for genetic counseling. The husband is a known case of G6PD deficiency and is concerned about passing it on to his future children. What is the chance that his children will inherit the disease?","o":["Only male children will be affected","1 in 2 chance","Females can inherit the mutation","1 in 4 chance"],"a":2,"e":"G6PD deficiency is an X-linked recessive disorder. Male children of an affected man are not affected as they receive only the paternal Y chromosome. However, the mutation can be passed on to female children, who are typically carriers of the disease.\n\nG6PD deficiency is the most common enzymatic disorder of RBCs. G6PD enzyme is involved in the generation of NADPH and protects RBCs from oxidative injury. Hence, patients with G6PD deficiency have episodes of acute hemolysis in the setting of oxidant injury from medications, acute infections, and certain foods (fava beans/broad beans).","t":"Hemolytic Anemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"A 35-year-old male with chronic bacterial prostatitis was treated with cotrimoxazole. Within a few days, he developed malaise, jaundice, abdominal pain, and dark urine. The blood picture is given below. What is the likely diagnosis?","o":["Hemolytic uremic syndrome","Autoimmune hemolytic anemia","Paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria","Glucose 6 phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency"],"a":3,"e":"The given clinical features are suggestive of an acute hemolytic reaction following the administration of cotrimoxazole. This is suggestive of glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PD) deficiency.\n\nIn G6PD deficiency uncontrolled oxidative stress causes hemoglobin to denature and form Heinz bodies. Removal of Heinz bodies by macrophages in the spleen results in the formation of characteristic bite cells or degmacytes or blister cells (as shown in the image).\n\nA hemolytic attack starts with abrupt onset of malaise, weakness, and abdominal or lumbar pain. After an interval of several hours to 2–3 days, the patient develops jaundice and often dark urine, due to hemoglobinuria.\n\nDrugs that carry the risk of clinical hemolysis in persons with G6PD deficiency:\n\nDrug groups\n\nExamples\n\nAntimalarials\n\nPrimaquine\n\nChloroquine\n\nDapsone/chlorproguanil\n\nSulphonamides/\n\nsulphones\n\nSulfamethoxazole\n\nSulfasalazine\n\nSulfisoxazole\n\nAntibacterial/\n\nantibiotics\n\nCotrimoxazole\n\nCiprofloxacin\n\nChloramphenicol\n\nNalidixic acid\n\nNorfloxacin\n\nNitrofurantoin\n\nOthers\n\nNaphthalene\n\nMethylene blue\n\nRasburicase\n\nNote: Cotrimoxazole contains sulfamethoxazole.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3834becd3c32423ea85469a923ac8a91.JPEG"],"t":"Hemolytic Anemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"The peripheral smear of a patient is given below. It is characteristic of which of the following conditions?","o":["Glucose 6 Phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency","Pyruvate kinase deficiency","5' nucleotidase deficiency","Cytochrome b5 reductase deficiency"],"a":2,"e":"The above peripheral smear shows basophilic stippling. It is characteristic of 5' nucleotidase deficiency.\n\nPyrimidine 5'-nucleotidase (P5'N-1) deficiency is the third most common enzyme abnormality causing hereditary hemolytic anemia. It is an autosomal recessive trait.\n\nThe hallmark of this enzyme deficiency is the presence of pronounced basophilic stippling and accumulation of pyrimidine nucleotides (RNA remnants) within erythrocytes.\n\nCabot rings may also be seen. They are thin, threadlike, red to violet rings or figure-of-8 shaped inclusions in red blood cells. They are remnants of the mitotic spindle and are seen in conditions causing dyserythropoiesis.\n\nBasophilic stippling is also seen in the following conditions:\n\n• Lead toxicity\n\n• Sideroblastic anemia\n\n• Severe thalassemia\n\n• Conditions with impaired cell division","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d03687414d4f4a9a9b77cff171f60c1f.JPEG"],"t":"Hemolytic Anemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"A patient with SLE is found to have warm autoimmune hemolytic anemia. Name the antibody and the site of hemolysis in this condition?","o":["IgM and Extavascular hemolysis","IgG and extravascular hemolysis","IgM and intravascular hemolysis","IgG and intravascular hemolysis"],"a":1,"e":"The antibody involved in warm autoimmune hemolytic anemia (AIHA) is IgG, which causes extravascular hemolysis.\n\nIgG is called a warm antibody, as it binds to target proteins at 37 oC. On the other hand, cold antibodies or IgM are reactive at 4 to 30oC.\n\nIn AIHA, autoantibodies against RBC antigens are produced which coat the RBC membrane. The Fc portion of the antibody is recognized by the Fc receptor on macrophages causing phagocytosis. RBC destruction takes place in extravascular sites where macrophages are abundant.","t":"Hemolytic Anemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Patients with which of the following are least likely to develop autoimmune hemolytic anemia?","o":["Infectious mononucleosis","Non-Hodgkin's lymphoma","Ulcerative colitis","Chronic myeloid leukemia"],"a":3,"e":"Autoimmune hemolytic anemia (AIHA) is commonly associated with CLL (chronic lymphocytic leukemia). It is not a typical feature of CML (chronic myeloid leukemia).\n\nAIHA can develop secondary to several autoimmune and infectious causes, as well as drugs. Drug-induced hemolytic anemia can occur by the following mechanisms:\n\nMechanism\nDrugs\n\nAdsorption:\n\nDrugs coat the RBC membrane. Antibodies are directed against the membrane–drug complex\n\nCefotetan\n\nCeftriaxone\n\nPiperacillin\n\nInnocent bystander:\n\nDrugs bind to antibodies in the plasma. This immune complex activates the complement system and destroys nearby RBCs (innocent bystanders)\n\nSulfonamides\n\nQuinine\n\nQuinidine\n\nPhenothiazines\n\nSome drugs trigger the production of autoantibodies\nMethyldopa","t":"Hemolytic Anemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following are associated with a positive Coombs test?\n\n1. Paroxysmal cold hemoglobinuria\n\n2. Paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria\n\n3. Cold agglutinin disease\n\n4. Drug-induced hemolytic anemia\n\n5. Hereditary spherocytosis","o":["1, 2, 4","2, 3, 5","2, 4, 5","1, 3, 4"],"a":3,"e":"Paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria (PNH) and hereditary spherocytosis are not Coomb's positive conditions. The following are Coombs-positive hemolytic anemias:\n\n• Autoimmune hemolytic anemia (AIHA)\n\n• Warm AIHA\n\n• Cold agglutinin disease\n\n• Paroxysmal cold hemoglobinuria (PCH)\n\n• Drug-induced hemolytic anemia\n\nIn PCH, the direct Coomb's test will be positive for complement only. This condition is characterized by the presence of the Donath-Landsteiner antibody. It is a unique antibody with anti-P specificity at 4°C. Hence, it binds to the RBC at 4°C. However, it causes complement-mediated lysis at 37°C.\n\nInterpretation of direct Coomb's test results:\n\nCondition\nAgglutination with\n\nanti- IgG\nanti- C3\n\nWarm AIHA\n+\n+\n\nDrug-induced\n+\n+\n\nPCH\n-\n+\n\nCold agglutinin disease (IgM)\n-\n+\n\nPNH\n-\n-","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c77092a1cb4d4020ac7fbc6022a783b5x1280x1197.PNG"],"t":"Hemolytic Anemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"A 20-year-old woman presented with fatigue and intermittent dark-colored urine. Further evaluation revealed positive Donath Landsteiner antibodies. What is the likely diagnosis?","o":["Warm autoimmune hemolytic anemia","Cold autoimmune hemolytic anemia","Paroxysmal cold hemoglobinuria","Paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria"],"a":2,"e":"Donath Landsteiner antibody is specific for paroxysmal cold hemoglobinuria (PCH). PCH is a rare form of autoimmune hemolytic anemia.\n\nThe mechanism of this antibody includes:\n\n• Binding to RBCs at 4°C\n\n• Complement-mediated lysis at 37°C\n\nThis condition presents with intravascular hemolysis and hemoglobinuria. Blood transfusion is required in severe anemia. It is a self-limiting condition.\n\nThe differences between PCH and PNH are given below:\n\nCondition\nParoxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria (PNH)\nParoxysmal cold hemoglobinuria (PCH)\n\nOnset\nChronic with acute exacerbations\nAcute\n\nPathogenesis\nComplement-mediated destruction of CD59/55(–) RBCs\nImmune lysis of normal red cells\n\nDiagnosis\nFlow cytometry to display a CD55/CD59(–) red cell population\nTest for Donath-Landsteiner antibody\n\nTriggers\nInfections, alcohol, exercise, stress, or medications\nTriggered by viral infection","t":"Hemolytic Anemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Microangiopathic hemolytic anemia is seen in all of the following except:","o":["Antiphospholipid antibody syndrome","Vasculitis","Prosthetic heart valves","Eclampsia"],"a":2,"e":"Microangiopathic hemolytic anemia (MAHA) is not seen in prosthetic heart valves. It is associated with macroangiopathic hemolytic anemia.\n\nMAHA refers to cell fragmentation that occurs in microcirculation. Microvascular lesions lead to luminal narrowing due to the deposition of fibrin and platelets. These produce shear stress that mechanically injures passing red cells. Hence, it is a type of non-immune hemolysis (Coombs-negative). On peripheral blood smear, schistocytes are seen.\n\nCommon causes of MAHA include:\n\n• Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura\n\n• Hemolytic uremic syndrome\n\n• Disseminated intravascular coagulation\n\n• Vasculitis\n\n• Malignant hypertension\n\n• APLA syndrome\n\n• Drug-induced hemolysis\n\n• Eclampsia","t":"Hemolytic Anemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"In patients with atypical hemolytic uremic syndrome, which of the following mutations is associated with the best prognosis?","o":["Complement factor H","Complement factor I","Complement factor B","Membrane cofactor protein"],"a":3,"e":"The best prognosis in atypical HUS is seen in membrane cofactor protein (MCP) gene mutation.\n\nFamilial HUS or atypical HUS (aHUS) is the only inherited cause of extracorpuscular hemolytic anemia. It can occur due to mutations in any one of several genes encoding complement regulatory proteins. Mutations in the following genes are seen:\n\n• Membrane cofactor protein - best prognosis\n\n• Complement factor B - worst prognosis\n\n• Complement factor H - most common\n\n• Complement factor I - least common\n\nIn these patients, infection or other triggers cause complement activation through the alternative pathway. This causes hemolysis and damage to endothelial cells in the kidney. It is usually self-limited.\n\nThe standard treatment is plasma exchange. Eculizumab has recently been shown to be beneficial in atypical HUS.","t":"Hemolytic Anemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"You have decided to prescribe an oral iron chelator for a 10-year-old girl with thalassemia. Which of the following will you not prefer?","o":["Deferiprone","Deferasirox","Deferoxamine","Both A and B"],"a":2,"e":"Deferoxamine has poor oral bioavailability and is administered parenterally (SC/IV).\n\nOral formulations of both deferiprone and deferasirox are available.\n\nThe adverse effects of iron chelators include:\n\n• Deferiprone - neutropenia, and agranulocytosis.\n\n• Deferasirox - transaminitis and mild, asymptomatic rise in serum creatinine\n\n• Deferoxamine - occasional cataracts, deafness, and local skin reactions, but is usually well-tolerated","t":"Hemolytic Anemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is the best test to screen patients for thalassemia?","o":["Cellulose acetate hemoglobin electrophoresis","Naked eye single tube red-cell osmotic fragility test","Eosin 5' maleimide flow cytometry","Automated high performance liquid chromatography"],"a":1,"e":"Naked eye single tube red-cell osmotic fragility test (NESTROFT) is the most sensitive test to detect thalassemia in the population. Hence, it is an effective screening test.\n\nThe principle is based on the decreased osmotic fragility of red cells in thalassemia. It is performed as follows:\n\n1. Mix EDTA blood and 0.36% buffered saline solution.\n\n2. Shake the tube and leave it at room temperature for 30 minutes.\n\n3. Shake the tube again and immediately hold the tube in front of a piece of paper with text.\n\nNESTROFT\nInference\nReason\n\nLines visible (negative)\nNormal\nHemolysis has occurred\n\nLines not seen (positive)\nThalassemia\nStable RBC, unable to lyse\n\nThe image below shows a positive NESTROF test (test tube marked as T1) as the line is not visible through the test tube. The test tube marked as C shows the control (Please note that a negative NESTROF test would show visible lines similar to the appearance of the control).\n\nThe following tests are specific and used to confirm the diagnosis of thalassemia:\n\n• Hemoglobin electrophoresis\n\n• High-performance liquid chromatography (HPLC)\n\n• Genetic testing\n\nNote: Eosin 5' maleimide (EMA) flow cytometry is the most specific test for the diagnosis of hereditary spherocytosis.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a609c616f696497193983869d15c7671x360x604.JPEG"],"t":"Hemolytic Anemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"During a master health check-up, a 25-year-old woman was found to have the following lab values. Which of the following will help in establishing a diagnosis in this patient?\n\nHemoglobin - 10g/dl\n\nMCV - 75 fL\n\nMCH - 25 pg\n\nPeripheral smear - Microcytes with target cells\n\nIron profile - Normal\n\nVitamin B12 and folate levels - Normal","o":["Free erythorcyte protoporphyrin levels","Hemoglobin electrophoresis","Schilling test","Bone marrow biopsy"],"a":1,"e":"This clinical scenario is consistent with β-thalassemia minor. The diagnosis can be established by hemoglobin electrophoresis.\n\nThis condition presents with the following features:\n\n• Microcytes (N: 80-96 fL)\n\n• Hypochromic (N: 27-33 pg)\n\n• Mild anemia\n\n• Target cells\n\n• Normal iron profile\n\nHemoglobin electrophoresis findings are as follows:\n\n• ≥4% of HbA2\n\n• ≤5% of HbF","t":"Hemolytic Anemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following clinical manifestations is not associated with sickle cell trait?","o":["Hyposthenuria","Painless hematuria","Papillary necrosis","Acute pain crisis"],"a":3,"e":"Acute pain crisis is not associated with sickle cell trait. Acute pain crisis is classicaly seen in sickle cell disease.\n\nManifestations of sickle cell trait include:\n\n• Often asymptomatic.\n\n• Hyposthenuria\n\n• Painless hematuria - due to papillary necrosis\n\n• Recurrent UTIs\n\n• Risk of renal medullary carcinoma - rare\n\n• Increased risk of CKD\n\n• Sloughing of papillae with urethral obstruction is very rare\n\n• Anemia and painful crises are rare.","t":"Hemolytic Anemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Patients with sickle cell anemia most commonly present with which of the following?","o":["Acute pain crisis","Splenic sequestration crisis","Acute chest syndrome","Isosthenuria"],"a":0,"e":"Acute pain crisis is the most common clinical presentation of sickle cell anemia.\n\nThese are intermittent episodes of vaso-occlusion in connective and musculoskeletal structures. This results in ischemia resulting in acute pain and tenderness, fever, tachycardia, and anxiety. The management includes vigorous hydration, evaluation for underlying causes, and aggressive analgesia.\n\nOther manifestations of sickle cell anemia include:\n\n• Hand-foot syndrome - painful infarcts of the digits and dactylitis\n\n• Priapism - infarction of the penile venous outflow tracts\n\n• Chronic lower leg ulcers - ischemia and superinfection in the distal circulation\n\n• Splenic sequestration crisis - life-threatening complication due to acute venous obstruction that requires emergency transfusion and/or splenectomy\n\n• Aplastic crisis - commonly secondary to parvovirus B19 infection\n\n• Acute chest syndrome - sickling within the lung, producing pain and temporary pulmonary dysfunction. Treatment may include lifelong transfusion support, using partial exchange transfusion\n\n• Stroke - common in children","t":"Hemolytic Anemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"According to WHO 2022 classification, which of the following is not a chronic myeloproliferative neoplasm?","o":["Chronic myeloid leukemia","Chronic eosinophilic leukemia","Essential thrombocythemia","Chronic lymphocytic leukemia"],"a":3,"e":"Chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL) is a lymphoid neoplasm. It is not a myeloproliferative neoplasm.\n\nMyeloproliferative neoplasms (MPN) are a group of disorders that cause an increased number of red blood cells, white blood cells, or platelets. According to 5th edition WHO classification (2022), myeloproliferative neoplasms consist of the following:\n\n• Chronic myeloid leukemia (CML)\n\n• Polycythemia vera (PV)\n\n• Primary myelofibrosis (PMF)\n\n• Essential thrombocythemia\n\n• Juvenile myelomonocytic leukemia (JMML)\n\n• Chronic neutrophilic leukemia (CNL)\n\n• Chronic eosinophilic leukemia\n\n• Myeloproliferative neoplasm, not otherwise specified","t":"Myeloproliferative Disorders And Aplastic Anemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"What is the most common myeloproliferative neoplasm?","o":["Chronic myeloid leukemia","Primary myelofibrosis","Essential thrombocytosis","Polycythemia vera"],"a":3,"e":"The most common myeloproliferative neoplasm is polycythemia vera.\n\nIt is a hematopoietic stem cell disorder that is characterized by uncontrolled proliferation of the red blood cells in the body. It is also frequently associated with an increase in white blood cells and platelets.","t":"Myeloproliferative Disorders And Aplastic Anemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"During a routine health checkup, the following lab reports were obtained for a young male patient. What will be your next step in the evaluation?\n\nHb: 17 g/dl\n\nTLC: 5500 /μL\n\nPlatelets: 1,69,000 /μL","o":["Perform bone marrow aspiration","Measure RBC mass","Measure arterial oxygen saturation","Look for JAK2 mutation"],"a":1,"e":"This patient has increased Hb and normal WBCs and platelets. The next step is to measure RBC mass to evaluate for polycythemia.\n\nA person has to be evaluated for polycythemia if there is:\n\n• Increased hemoglobin: >16.5 g/dL in men or >16.0 g/dL in women, (or)\n\n• Increased hematocrit: >49 percent in men or >48 percent in women\n\nThis condition is due to either a true increase in the number of RBCs (absolute polycythemia) or an apparent increase due to a decrease in the plasma volume (relative polycythemia). Both of these conditions can be differentiated by measuring RBC mass (amount of total blood volume that is occupied by RBCs).\n\nIn patients with absolute polycythemia, erythropoietin (EPO) levels must be measured.\n\n• Primary polycythemia - low or normal EPO levels\n\n• Secondary polycythemia - elevated EPO levels","t":"Myeloproliferative Disorders And Aplastic Anemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"A 45-year-old man is found to have a hemoglobin level of 18 g/dl, a hematocrit of 54%, and an elevated erythropoietin level. Which of the following is the least likely diagnosis?","o":["Carbon monoxide poisoning","Cerebellar hemangioblastoma","Renal cell carcinoma","Polycythemia vera"],"a":3,"e":"Polycythemia vera is associated with low or normal erythropoietin (EPO) levels.\n\nPolycythemia refers to an increase in the number of red blood cells (RBC) in the body. It is classified as:\n\nPrimary polycythemia (polycythemia vera)\nSecondary polycythemia\n\nPathophysiology\nCaused by mutations in the hematopoietic stem cells\n\nHypoxia (COPD, carbon monoxide poisoning)\n\nAutonomous EPO production\n\nErythropoietin (EPO)\nNormal or low\nHigh\n\nWBCs, and platelet count\nHigh (due to increased production of all cell lines)\nNormal\n\nAutonomous production of erythropoietin occurs in renal cell carcinoma (RCC), hepatocellular carcinoma, cerebellar hemangioblastoma.\n\nRelative (spurious) polycythemia refers to an apparent increase in RBC or hemoglobin count due to a decrease in the plasma volume. It is commonly seen in dehydration.","t":"Myeloproliferative Disorders And Aplastic Anemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"What is the most common cytogenetic abnormality in patients with polycythemia vera?","o":["JAK6 mutation","JAK2 mutation","JAK8 mutation","JAK4 mutation"],"a":1,"e":"The most common cytogenetic abnormality in polycythemia vera is the JAK2 mutation.\n\nJAK2 mutations can be found in the following neoplasms:\n\n• Polycythemia vera\n\n• Essential thrombocythemia\n\n• Primary myelofibrosis","t":"Myeloproliferative Disorders And Aplastic Anemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following would not be seen in patients with polycythemia vera?","o":["Elevated platelet count","Elevated serum lactate dehydrogenase","Low to normal erythropoietin levels","Low WBC count"],"a":3,"e":"Low WBC count is not a feature of polycythemia vera. It is associated with an elevated WBC count.\n\nCommon laboratory findings of polycythemia vera are:\n\n• Elevated hemoglobin concentration, hematocrit levels, and RBC count\n\n• Elevated WBCs and platelet counts\n\n• Low to normal erythropoietin levels\n\n• Elevated serum lactate dehydrogenase","t":"Myeloproliferative Disorders And Aplastic Anemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"A 40-year-old man presents with a 1-month history of severe itching every time he takes a hot shower. He also reports a burning sensation in his fingers and frequent headaches. His hemoglobin is 18 g/dL and his EPO levels are low. Which of the following can provide symptomatic relief in this patient?","o":["Dabrafenib","Nilotinib","Eculizumab","Ruxolitinib"],"a":3,"e":"This clinical scenario is suggestive of polycythemia vera. In these patients, ruxolitinib, a Janus-kinase inhibitor is used to provide symptomatic relief.\n\nThe treatment of polycythemia vera is as follows:\n\n• Therapeutic phlebotomy - definitive treatment\n\n• Anticoagulants - in patients with thrombosis\n\n• Symptomatic relief:\n\n• Ruxolitinib\n\n• Hydroxyurea\n\n• Interferon-alpha\n\n• PUVA therapy","t":"Myeloproliferative Disorders And Aplastic Anemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"You are a first-year medicine resident. While examining a patient, you note that his spleen is enlarged 8.5 cm below his costal margin. You would include all of the following in your differential diagnosis except:","o":["Essential thrombocythemia","Chronic myeloid leukemia","Primary myelofibrosis","Polycythemia vera"],"a":0,"e":"Enlargement of the spleen > 8 cm below the costal margin or weight > 1000 g is called massive splenomegaly. It is not a feature of essential thrombocythemia (ET), as it causes only mild splenomegaly.\n\nThe causes of massive splenomegaly include:\n\n• Chronic myeloid leukemia\n\n• Primary myelofibrosis\n\n• Polycythemia vera\n\n• Chronic lymphocytic leukemia\n\n• Lymphoma\n\n• Hairy cell leukemia\n\n• Gaucher's disease\n\n• Sarcoidosis\n\n• Autoimmune hemolytic anemia\n\nET is a neoplasm in which the body produces too many platelets, resulting in a platelet count > 4.5 lakhs/μL. It occurs due to the mutations in the JAK2, CALR, or MPL genes.\n\nThe majority of the patients are asymptomatic and are diagnosed incidentally. Some patients may present with thrombotic events or rarely, hemorrhages. The following findings are seen:\n\n• Peripheral smear - abnormally large platelets\n\n• Bone marrow biopsy - increased number of megakaryocytes, with hyperlobulated nuclei","t":"Myeloproliferative Disorders And Aplastic Anemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"A 24-year-old woman, who was diagnosed with essential thrombocythemia, is being planned for cytoreductive therapy. What first-line drug should she be started on?","o":["Anagrelide","Busulfan","Interferon alpha","Hydroxyurea"],"a":3,"e":"The first-line drug for cytoreductive therapy in patients with essential thrombocythemia is hydroxyurea.\n\nTreatment of essential thrombocythemia is as follows:\n\n• No symptoms or risk of thrombosis:\n\n• Observation alone\n\n• Low dose aspirin may be considered\n\n• High risk of thrombosis (cardiovascular risk factors, prior history of thrombosis)\n\n• Low dose aspirin\n\n• Cytoreduction with hydroxyurea, busulfan, or interferon-alpha","t":"Myeloproliferative Disorders And Aplastic Anemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"A 60-year-old man was referred with severe anemia and hepatosplenomegaly. Peripheral smear showed teardrop cells, nucleated RBCs, and giant abnormal platelets. Bone marrow aspirate could not be obtained despite multiple attempts, hence a bone marrow biopsy was done. The findings are given below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Polycythemia vera","Essential thrombocythemia","Primary myelofibrosis","Chronic myeloid leukemia"],"a":2,"e":"The image shows the deposition of numerous collagen fibers in the bone marrow, which indicates marrow fibrosis. These clinical findings and history of a dry tap on bone marrow aspiration are diagnostic of primary myelofibrosis.\n\nIn this condition, the neoplastic proliferation of hematopoietic stem cells results in increased and abnormal proliferation of the megakaryocytes. These megakaryocytes release excessive amounts of platelet-derived growth factor (PDGF) and TGF-β. Increased growth factors lead to the deposition of collagen in the bone marrow which eventually leads to fibrosis.\n\nThe clinical features include:\n\n• Early phase:\n\n• Thrombocytosis (large, abnormal platelets) and leukocytosis\n\n• Hypercellularity of bone marrow\n\n• Late phase:\n\n• Pancytopenia\n\n• Hypocellularity and fibrosis of bone marrow (dry tap)\n\n• Teardrop-shaped RBCs and leukoerythroblastosis (premature release of nucleated erythroid and early granulocytes into blood)\n\n• Hepatosplenomegaly - due to extramedullary hematopoiesis.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/edbcad7a529649c5a22b29a233ab1c75.JPEG"],"t":"Myeloproliferative Disorders And Aplastic Anemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Bone marrow aspirate of a patient with pancytopenia revealed a hypercellular marrow. Which of the following conditions is unlikely?","o":["Paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria","Fanconi anemia","Megaloblastic anemia","Myelodysplastic syndrome"],"a":1,"e":"Fanconi anemia results in pancytopenia with hypocellular bone marrow. Hence, it is not likely in this patient.\n\nCauses of pancytopenia with hypocellular bone marrow include:\n\n• Inherited aplastic anemia\n\n• Fanconi anemia\n\n• Dyskeratosis congenita\n\n• Shwachman-Diamond syndrome\n\n• Acquired aplastic anemia\n\nCauses of pancytopenia with hypercellular bone marrow include:\n\n• Primary bone marrow disorders\n\n• Paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria (PNH)\n\n• Myelodysplastic syndrome\n\n• Myelofibrosis\n\n• Systemic diseases\n\n• Megaloblastic anemia\n\n• Alcoholism\n\n• Sarcoidosis\n\n• Tuberculosis\n\n• Brucellosis","t":"Myeloproliferative Disorders And Aplastic Anemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"A 20-year-old male patient with the following exam findings is found to have pancytopenia. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Fanconi anemia","Shwachman-Diamond syndrome","Diamond-Blackfan syndrome","Dyskeratosis congenita"],"a":3,"e":"The most likely diagnosis is dyskeratosis congenita. This condition is characterized by pancytopenia along with the clinical triad of:\n\n1. Reticular skin pigmentation\n\n2. Nail dystrophy\n\n3. Oral leukoplakia\n\nOption A: Fanconi anemia is an autosomal recessive disorder characterized by pancytopenia, congenital anomalies, and an increased risk of malignancies.\n\nOption B: Shwachman-Diamond syndrome presents with bone marrow failure and exocrine pancreatic insufficiency.\n\nOption C: Diamond-Blackfan syndrome presents with low RBC counts due to congenital erythroid aplasia.\n\nNote: Fanconi anemia is different from Fanconi syndrome. Fanconi syndrome is a disorder of renal tubular function (type 2 RTA) that results in increased excretion of glucose, amino acids, bicarbonates, and phosphates.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/68b4a3195abe4b2f8fb640974cee72a7.PNG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/170d5aeff3914cea903edffc82a81e60x1280x2771.JPEG"],"t":"Myeloproliferative Disorders And Aplastic Anemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"All of the following can cause acquired aplastic anemia except:","o":["Cyclophosphamide therapy","Radiation exposure","Paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria","Herpes simplex infection"],"a":3,"e":"Herpes simplex infection is not a cause of acquired aplastic anemia.\n\nSecondary causes of acquired aplastic anemia include:\n\n• Radiation\n\n• Toxins - Benzene\n\n• Drugs\n\n• Chemotherapy drugs (including cyclophosphamide)\n\n• Sulfonamides\n\n• Chloramphenicol\n\n• Infections:\n\n• 1-2 months after an attack of seronegative viral hepatitis\n\n• HIV, EBV, CMV\n\n• Parvovirus B19\n\n• Eosinophilic fasciitis\n\n• Paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria\n\n• Graft vs host disease","t":"Myeloproliferative Disorders And Aplastic Anemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"A 25-year-old lady is being evaluated for recurrent epistaxis. On examination, the spleen is not palpable. Investigations reveal Hb of 6 g/dL, platelet count of 18,000/μL, and WBC count of 3000/μL. The results of a bone marrow biopsy are given below. What is the best method of management in this patient?","o":["Administer hematopoietic growth factors","Umbilical cord blood transplantation","Hematopoietic stem cell transplantation","Antithymocyte globulin with cyclosporine"],"a":2,"e":"The clinical and laboratory findings, as well as bone marrow hypocellularity are diagnostic of aplastic anemia. In this young patient, the best method of management is hematopoietic stem cell transplantation.\n\nAplastic anemia presents with pancytopenia and resulting bleeding manifestations, anemia, and frequent infections. Bone marrow biopsy reveals hypocellularity with fatty infiltration of the bone marrow. The management is as follows:\n\n• Hematopoietic stem cell transplantation - best method of management in young patients\n\n• Antithymocyte globulin with cyclosporine - elderly patients and patients without HLA-matched donors\n\n• Eltrombopag - a thrombopoietin analog used as add-on therapy","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a0ad396188a846ff98c7b376a62c296a.PNG"],"t":"Myeloproliferative Disorders And Aplastic Anemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"What is the most common cytogenetic abnormality seen in patients with myelodysplastic neoplasm?","o":["7q deletion","5q deletion","Monosomy 5","Monosomy 7"],"a":1,"e":"The most common cytogenetic abnormality seen in myelodysplastic neoplasm is the 5q deletion.\n\nMyelodysplastic syndromes/neoplasm (MDS) are a group of disorders in which abnormal stem cells produce increased numbers of immature blood cells. These immature cells are defective and die prematurely resulting in lower numbers of mature blood cells. It is characterized by pancytopenia and a high risk of acute myeloid leukemia (AML).\n\nThe cytogenetic abnormalities identified in this condition include:\n\n• 5q deletion\n\n• 3q deletion\n\n• 7q deletion\n\n• Monosomy 7\n\n• Monosomy 5\n\n5th edition WHO Classification of MDS (2022):\n\n1. MDS with defining genetic abnormalities:\n\n• MDS with a low blast and isolated 5q deletion (MDS-5q): <5% blasts in the bone marrow or <2% blasts in the peripheral blood.\n\n• MDS with a low blast and SF3B1 mutation (MDS-SF3B1): Detection of ≥15% ring sideroblasts may substitute for SF3B1 mutation.\n\n• MDS with biallelic TP53 inactivation (MDS-biTP53): <20% blasts in the bone marrow or peripheral blood.\n\n2. MDS, morphologically defined:\n\n• MDS with low blasts: <5% blasts in the bone marrow or <2% blasts in the peripheral blood.\n\n• MDS-hypoplastic: ≤25% bone marrow cellularity\n\n• MDS with increased blasts (IB):\n\n• MDS-IB1: 5–9% of blasts in bone marrow or 2–4% in peripheral blood\n\n• MDS-IB2:10-19% of blasts in bone marrow or 5-19% in peripheral blood or Auer rods\n\n• MDS-fibrosis: 5-19% of blasts in bone marrow or 2–19% in peripheral blood\n\n3. Childhood myelodysplastic neoplasm:\n\n• Childhood MDS with low blasts:<5% blasts in the bone marrow or <2% blasts in the peripheral blood\n\n• Childhood MDS with increased blasts: 5-19% blasts in the bone marrow or 2-19% blasts in the peripheral blood.","t":"Myeloproliferative Disorders And Aplastic Anemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"A patient with myelodysplastic syndrome was found to have 5q deletion. What is the drug of choice for this patient?","o":["Cyclosporine","Cyclophosphamide","Thalidomide","Lenalidomide"],"a":3,"e":"Lenalidomide is the drug of choice in patients with myelodysplastic syndrome (MDS) associated with 5q deletion.\n\nIt is a thalidomide derivative with the following benefits in these patients:\n\n• Patients achieve transfusion independence\n\n• Normal or near-normal hemoglobin levels\n\n• Normalization of cytogenetics\n\nAdverse effects include:\n\n• Myelosuppression (worsening thrombocytopenia and neutropenia, necessitating blood count monitoring)\n\n• Deep vein thrombosis and pulmonary embolism\n\nHowever, hematopoietic stem cell transplant remains the definitive treatment.","t":"Myeloproliferative Disorders And Aplastic Anemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"What is the lifespan of a platelet?","o":["24 hours","5 days","7 days","2 weeks"],"a":2,"e":"The average lifespan of platelets in circulation is 7–10 days.\n\nThrombopoietin (TPO) is the regulator of platelet production. A reduction in platelet and megakaryocyte mass increases the level of TPO, which then stimulates platelet production.\n\nThe shelf life of donor platelets is 5 days. It is stored on a special agitator at 20-24 °C. The half-life of transfused platelets is 3-5 days.","t":"Platelet Disorders","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following would you not expect to see in patients with a platelet disorder?","o":["Petechiae","Ecchymosis","Hemarthrosis","Gastrointestinal bleeds"],"a":2,"e":"Hemarthrosis is highly suggestive of a coagulation disorder, not a platelet disorder.\n\nBleeding diathesis can occur due to:\n\n• Platelet disorders - mucocutaneous bleeding like petechiae, purpura, ecchymoses, epistaxis, menorrhagia, gastrointestinal (GI) bleeds\n\n• Coagulation disorders - hemarthrosis, muscle hematomas, and bleeding into soft tissues\n\n• Disorders of the vessel wall (Henoch-Schönlein purpura, scurvy) - purpura and ecchymosis","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/98bceb172651412e82d9eab512b76b04x1280x2210.JPEG"],"t":"Platelet Disorders","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is a disorder of platelet adhesion?","o":["Glanzmann’s thrombasthenia","Bernard Soulier syndrome","Wiskott Aldrich syndrome","Hermansky Pudlak syndrome"],"a":1,"e":"Bernard Soulier syndrome is a disorder of platelet adhesion. It occurs due to an inherited defect in the GPIb-IX complex.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/56f11346987e42ae8a45ab9a221cab9dx1280x2203.JPEG"],"t":"Platelet Disorders","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Patients with which of the following conditions would have a low platelet count?","o":["Platelet storage pool disorder","Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome","Henoch-Schonlein purpura","Hemophilia"],"a":1,"e":"Thrombocytopenia is a feature of Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome.\n\nIt is an X-linked recessive condition caused by mutations in the WASP gene. It affects T-cells and platelets and presents with the following features:\n\n• Eczema\n\n• Thrombocytopenia - along with decreased platelet size, impaired platelet aggregation response\n\n• Immunodeficiency - low IgM, high IgE, defective regulatory T cells causing recurrent bacterial infections.\n\nOption A: Platelet storage pool disorder is a disorder of defective platelet granule formation.\n\nOption C: Henoch-Schonlein purpura is a type of small vessel vasculitis in children and young adults.\n\nOption D: Hemophilia is a coagulation disorder, hence the platelet count is not affected.","t":"Platelet Disorders","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Immune-mediated destruction of platelets is seen in patients with which of the following conditions?","o":["Bernard-Soulier syndrome","Hermansky-Pudlak syndrome","Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura","Drug-induced thrombocytopenia"],"a":3,"e":"Immune-mediated destruction of platelets is seen in drug-induced thrombocytopenia. Common drugs that cause this are quinidine, heparin, sulfa compounds.\n\nDrug-dependent antibody binding can only be demonstrated in the presence of the drug in the laboratory assays. The thrombocytopenia normally occurs within 21 days of initial exposure and usually gets resolved after 7-10 days once the drug is stopped.\n\nImmune-mediated destruction of platelets is seen in:\n\n• Drug-induced thrombocytopenia\n\n• Infection-induced thrombocytopenia\n\n• Idiopathic/ immune thrombocytopenic purpura (ITP)\n\nOption A: Bernard-Soulier syndrome is a disorder of platelet adhesion. There are no autoantibodies against platelets.\n\nOption B: Hermansky-Pudlak syndrome is an inherited disorder with defective granule formation in platelets. It affects platelet activation and aggregation.\n\nOption C: Thrombotic microangiopathies like thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (TTP) and hemolytic uremic syndrome (HUS) lead to thrombocytopenia due to increased platelet consumption.","t":"Platelet Disorders","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"A young man comes to you with complaints of bleeding gums and the following skin rash. He reports that he had diarrhea last week after eating at a roadside eatery. History is significant for deep vein thrombosis 6 months ago that was treated with heparin. His CBC report revealed a platelet count of 15,000/µL, normal RBC and WBC count. His peripheral smear was normal. What is the likely diagnosis?","o":["Hemolytic uremic syndrome","Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura","Immune thrombocytopenic purpura","Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia"],"a":2,"e":"The given clinical scenario and image showing petechiae are suggestive of immune thrombocytopenic purpura.\n\nImmune thrombocytopenic purpura (ITP) is an acquired disorder with autoantibodies against platelet membrane glycoproteins IIb-IIIa or Ib-IX. It can either be primary (idiopathic) or secondary to autoimmune conditions like SLE or infections like HIV and hepatitis C.\n\nIt presents with features of mucocutaneous bleeding. CBC shows isolated thrombocytopenia and peripheral smear does not reveal any hemolysis.\n\nAcute ITP\nChronic ITP\n\nCommon in children\nCommon in adults (females > males)\n\nAssociated with an antecedent viral infection\nNot associated with antecedent infection\n\nSpontaneous remission in 80% cases\nSpontaneous remission is not usually seen\n\nOptions A and B: HUS and TTP are microangiopathies. They are associated with hemolysis and schistocytes on peripheral smear.\n\nOption D: Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia typically presents within 5-10 days of treatment. Hence, it is not the likely diagnosis in this patient.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b9f86e5242b74faaaab53f43357ffe24.JPEG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1839d0a0b6d64631b0d4aca0475d0791x1280x2203.JPEG"],"t":"Platelet Disorders","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Two weeks after an upper respiratory tract infection, a 5-year-old presents with ecchymosis and petechiae all over the body. Abdominal examination revealed no hepatosplenomegaly. The platelet count is 15,000/µL and the coagulation profile is normal. Which of the following is false regarding this condition?","o":["Peripheral blood smear is normal","Bone marrow biopsy shows increased megakaryocytes","Bleeding into the joints is a common manifestation","The condition resolves spontaneously in 80% patients"],"a":2,"e":"This scenario is suggestive of acute immune thrombocytopenic purpura. Bleeding into the joints is seen in coagulation disorders and is not a manifestation of this condition.\n\nImmune thrombocytopenic purpura or idiopathic thrombocytopenic purpura (ITP) is an acquired disorder in which there is an immune-mediated destruction of platelets. It can be acute or chronic.\n\nAcute ITP is common in children and is usually preceded by viral infection. It presents with mucocutaneous bleeding (epistaxis, petechiae, ecchymosis). Hepatomegaly, splenomegaly, lymphadenopathy, fever and jaundice are absent. The following laboratory features are seen:\n\n• Thrombocytopenia (commonly <20,000 / μL)\n\n• Bleeding time - prolonged\n\n• PT/aPTT/INR - normal\n\n• Bone marrow - normal/increased megakaryocytes\n\n• Peripheral smear - normal\n\nThis condition resolves spontaneously in 80% of the cases. Treatment is as follows:\n\n• Platelet count < 30,000 with risk factors for bleeding (antiplatelet drugs/anticoagulants or concomitant bleeding disorders): Oral glucocorticoids or IVIG\n\n• Severe bleeding symptoms: IV methylprednisolone ± IVIG\n\n• Life-threatening bleeding (like intracranial hemorrhage): platelet transfusion + IV methylprednisolone + IVIG","t":"Platelet Disorders","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"A 50-year-old woman comes to the ER after developing a spontaneous nosebleed. Over the past 1 week, she has noticed blood on her toothbrush while brushing her teeth. She was diagnosed with hepatitis C three months ago. Investigations reveal a platelet count of 32,000/ µL and a bleeding time of 9 minutes . Which of the following is false regarding the treatment of this patient?","o":["She requires regular platelet transfusions until platelet count is normalized","Prednisone or anti-D immunoglobulins can be used as the first-line treatment","Splenectomy may be done if she relapses after medical management","Romiplostim and eltrombopag can be used if there is no response to splenectomy"],"a":0,"e":"This clinical scenario in an adult patient is suggestive of chronic ITP (idiopathic thrombocytopenic purpura). She does not require platelet transfusion. It is recommended only for patients with severe bleeding manifestations (intracranial/ gastrointestinal) or platelet count < 5000 μL.\n\nChronic ITP can either be primary (idiopathic) or secondary to autoimmune conditions like SLE or infections like HIV and hepatitis C. Depending on the clinical features, the management is as follows:\n\nClinical features\nManagement\n\n1.\n\nMinor bleeding manifestations (and/or)\n\nPlatelet count > 5000/ μL\n\nPrednisone (OR)\n\nAnti-D immunoglobulin therapy - effective only in Rh-positive patients (OR)\n\nIntravenous immunoglobulin (IVIG)\n\n2.\n\nSevere ITP (any of the following):\n\n• Significant GI / intracranial bleeding\n\n• Platelet count < 5000/μL\n\n• Signs of impending bleeding (retinal or large oral mucosal bleeds)\n\nPlatelet transfusion (AND)\n\nHigh-dose glucocorticoids (AND)\n\nIVIG / Anti-D immunoglobulins + Rituximab\n\n3.\nRelapse after first-line therapy\nSplenectomy\n\n4.\nUnresponsive to all other therapy or relapse after splenectomy\nThrombopoietin receptor agonists - romiplostim (subcutaneous), eltrombopag (oral)","t":"Platelet Disorders","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"It is your first day as an intern in the medicine ward. The senior resident tells you to check the platelet count of a patient who is being monitored for thrombocytopenia. He was started on a new medication the previous night. What drug was most likely prescribed?","o":["Phenytoin","Ampicillin","Abciximab","Amiodarone"],"a":2,"e":"Abciximab can cause a drop in platelet count within 24 hours of initial exposure. Hence, these patients must be monitored for thrombocytopenia. This is due to naturally-occurring antibodies that cross-react with the drug bound to the platelets.\n\nThrombocytopenia due to other drugs typically occurs several days after exposure (average 21 days) and resolves in 7-10 days after drug withdrawal. Drug-induced thrombocytopenia can occur due to the following mechanisms:\n\n• Bone marrow suppression - chemotherapeutic drugs\n\n• Drug dependent antibodies that react with platelet surface antigens - quinine and sulfonamides\n\n• Naturally occurring antibodies that cross-react with drugs bound to the platelets - platelet Gp IIb/IIIa inhibitors\n\nSome of the drugs that cause thrombocytopenia are:\n\n• Antibiotics - ampicillin, cephalosporins, piperacillin, vancomycine\n\n• Analgesics - ibuprofen, acetaminophen\n\n• Anti-epileptics - phenytoin, carbamazepine\n\n• Heparin\n\n• Amiodarone","t":"Platelet Disorders","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Patients with all of the following usually present with bleeding except:","o":["Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia","Drug-induced thrombocytopenia","Inherited thrombocytopenia","Infection-induced thrombocytopenia"],"a":0,"e":"Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia (HIT) is not associated with bleeding. On the contrary, it is associated with an increased risk of thrombosis.\n\nThis condition occurs due to the production of antibodies against the heparin-PF4 complex (platelet factor 4, a platelet-specific protein). These antibodies can activate platelets and result in thrombosis. It is seen 5-14 days after exposure to heparin.\n\nThese antibodies can be detected using ELISA or serotonin release assay. This condition is managed by discontinuing heparin and using alternative anticoagulants.","t":"Platelet Disorders","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following cannot be used in the treatment of patients with heparin-induced thrombocytopenia?","o":["Danaparoid","Lepirudin","Argatroban","Enoxaparin"],"a":3,"e":"Low molecular weight heparin (LMWH) such as enoxaparin cannot be used in the treatment of heparin-induced thrombocytopenia (HIT). This is because HIT antibodies (against anti-PF4-heparin complex) may cross-react with LMWH.\n\nHIT can occur following exposure to unfractionated heparin and less commonly with LMWH.\n\nThe treatment of HIT is as follows:\n\n• Discontinue heparin\n\n• Anticoagulation with non-heparin anticoagulants even in the absence of thrombosis, since there is a high risk of thrombosis.\n\n• Direct thrombin inhibitors: Argatroban, lepirudin, bivalirudin\n\n• Danaparoid (heparinoid)","t":"Platelet Disorders","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"The CBC of a 33-year-old woman revealed a low platelet count. However, further evaluation revealed this to be an in vitro sampling error. Which of the following is the likely cause of this finding?","o":["EDTA","Sodium citrate","Heparin","Finger stick sample"],"a":0,"e":"This scenario is suggestive of pseudothrombocytopenia. It is seen in blood samples collected with EDTA as the anticoagulant.\n\nEDTA causes a reduction in the calcium content of the collected blood sample. This triggers in vitro platelet clumping and agglutination by antibodies (usually IgG), as shown below:\n\nWhen a low platelet count is obtained with an EDTA sample, the peripheral blood smear should be evaluated. The platelet count must also be determined in blood collected in sodium citrate or heparin collection tubes or fresh fingerstick blood sample.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4f203d48f03b432f94a70bab3cd4348fx750x600.JPEG"],"t":"Platelet Disorders","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is false regarding patients with Bernard-Soulier syndrome?","o":["Platelet aggregation tests with epinephrine and ADP are normal","Ristocetin aggregation test is normal","Peripheral smear shows large platelets","Mild thrombocytopenia is seen"],"a":1,"e":"The ristocetin aggregation test is abnormal (no aggregation) in patients with Bernard-Soulier (BS) syndrome.\n\nIt is an inherited disorder of platelet function or qualitative platelet disorder due to deficiency of the Gp Ib-IX receptor. This is the platelet membrane receptor for the von Willebrand factor (vWF). The binding of the vWF to the GpIb-IX-V complex on the platelet membrane is essential for platelet adhesion to the vessel wall.\n\nThis condition presents with mild thrombocytopenia and peripheral smear shows large platelets.\n\nPlatelet aggregation tests are performed to measure platelet activation and aggregation in vitro. Various platelet agonists like ADP, epinephrine, collagen, and ristocetin can be used to perform these tests. In Bernard-Soulier syndrome, the following responses are seen:\n\n• With ristocetin - No response/no aggregation, as ristocetin specifically binds to the platelet membrane via the Gp Ib-IXa receptor\n\n• With ADP or epinephrine - Normal response, as Gp IIb-IIIa receptor is not involved in this process","t":"Platelet Disorders","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is the most common inherited bleeding disorder?","o":["Hemophilia A","Hemophilia B","Immune thrombocytopenia purpura","Von Willebrand disease"],"a":3,"e":"Von Willebrand disease (vWD) is the most common inherited bleeding disorder.\n\nvWD occurs due to deficient or abnormal von Willebrand factor (vWF). vWF is synthesized by endothelial cells and megakaryocytes (gene located on chromosome 12p). It is then stored in endothelial storage vesicles, Weibel-Palade bodies. Metalloprotease ADAMTS13 cleaves ultra-large multimers of VWF which are responsible for platelet adhesion.\n\nThe functions of vWF and the corresponding symptoms include:\n\n• Adhesion of the platelets to the exposed subendothelium - symptoms of both platelet and bleeding disorders\n\n• Prolongs the half-life of factor VIII by binding to it in circulation - hemophilia A like symptoms (rare)","t":"Platelet Disorders","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is called autosomal hemophilia?","o":["Type 2M von Willebrand disease","Type 2N von Willebrand disease","Type 2A von Willebrand disease","Type 2B von Willebrand disease"],"a":1,"e":"Autosomal hemophilia is also called type 2N von Willebrand disease (vWD), as the clinical presentation is similar to hemophilia.\n\nThe types of vWD are:\n\nType\nInheritance\nMechanism\nImportant characteristics\n\n1\nAD\nPartial quantitative deficiency of VWF\nMost common type\n\n3\nAR\nComplete quantitative deficiency\nMost severe VWD\n\n2A\nAD\nLoss of VWF multimers (increased cleavage by ADAMS13)\nPlatelet adhesion defect\n\n2B\nAD\nIncreased binding of VWF to platelets causing VWF-platelet complex destruction\nThrombocytopenia\n\n2M\nAD\nDecreased affinity of VWF to platelets\nPlatelet adhesion defect, very rare\n\n2N\nAR\nDecreased affinity of VWF to Factor VIII\nAutosomal hemophilia\n\nIn pseudo vWD, the vWF is normal. However, platelets have an increased affinity for vWF. It presents similar to vWD 2B with thrombocytopenia.","t":"Platelet Disorders","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following patients has Heyde's syndrome?","o":["A man with aortic stenosis with gastrointestinal bleeding","A woman with aortic stenosis with intracranial bleed","A man with aortic regurgitation with gastrointestinal bleeding","A woman with aortic regurgitation with intracranial bleed"],"a":0,"e":"Heyde’s syndrome is aortic stenosis with gastrointestinal bleeding, due to angiodysplasia of the gastrointestinal tract.\n\nThe shear stress on blood passing through the stenotic aortic valve produces a change in the von Willebrand factor making it susceptible to serum proteases. Hence, large multimer forms are lost, leading to an acquired type 2 von Willebrand disease (vWD). The condition is reversed with the replacement of the aortic valve.\n\nOther causes of acquired vWD include:\n\n• Multiple myeloma\n\n• Non-Hodgkin's lymphoma\n\n• Polycythemia vera\n\n• Systemic lupus erythematosus","t":"Platelet Disorders","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following laboratory findings is seen in a patient with von Willebrand's disease?","o":["Normal ristocetin aggregation test","Increased bleeding time","Increased prothrombin time","Decreased activated partial thromboplastin time"],"a":1,"e":"Bleeding time is increased in von Willebrand disease (vWD). It is a combination of a platelet adhesion defect with factor VIII deficiency.\n\nThe laboratory findings in vWD include:\n\n• Platelet count is normal (except type 2B)\n\n• Bleeding time is prolonged\n\n• Ristocetin aggregation is abnormal\n\n• Other platelet aggregation tests are normal\n\n• Prothrombin time (PT) is normal\n\n• aPTT is prolonged\n\n• VWF activity is decreased\n\n• Factor VIII activity is decreased","t":"Platelet Disorders","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"A 28-year-old woman is being evaluated for prolonged bleeding following a tooth extraction. She also reports heavy bleeding during menses and recurrent gum bleeds. Labs show increased aPTT, normal PT, and increased bleeding time. Which of the following is the first-line treatment for this patient?","o":["Tranaxemic acid","Factor VIII concentrate","Von Willebrand factor concentrate","Desmopressin"],"a":3,"e":"This clinical scenario is suggestive of von Willebrand's disease. The preferred first-line treatment is desmopressin (DDAVP).\n\nThis drug acts on endothelial V2 receptors causing the release of VWF and factor VIII from endothelial stores. DDAVP can be given intravenously or by an intranasal spray. The side effects include tachyphylaxis and hyponatremia.\n\nVWF concentrate and factor VIII concentrate are reserved for patients with severe disease or prior to major procedures. Antifibrinolytic therapy (tranexamic acid or ε-aminocaproic acid) may be used for the prevention and treatment of mucosal bleeding.","t":"Platelet Disorders","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following features is not seen in patients with Glanzmann’s thrombasthenia?","o":["Abnormal platelet aggregation on adding ADP","Decreased platelet count","Normal von Willebrand factor levels","Normal platelet aggregation to ristocetin"],"a":1,"e":"Thrombocytopenia is not seen in Glanzmann’s thrombasthenia.\n\nGlanzmann’s thrombasthenia is a primary defect of platelet function (aggregation). It occurs due to congenital absence of the GpIIb/IIIa receptor. It presents as superficial mucocutaneous bleeding with normal platelet count and single isolated platelets without clumping on peripheral smear. The levels of vWF are normal.\n\nNormally, release of ADP causes platelets to change shape and bind to fibrinogen through the GpIIb/IIIa receptor. In Glanzmann’s thrombasthenia, absence of the receptor causes abnormal aggregation (absent or reduced) on adding ADP. However, aggregation in response to ristocetin is normal, as it is dependent on GpIb and vWF.\n\nSecondary platelet dysfunction can be seen in the following conditions:\n\n• Uremia\n\n• Paraproteinemia\n\n• Myeloproliferative disorders","t":"Platelet Disorders","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"A physician decides to start a patient with stroke on antiplatelet medication. All of the following drugs would be effective except:","o":["Clopidogrel","Ticlopidine","Ticagrelor","Pagoclone"],"a":3,"e":"Pagoclone is an anxiolytic medication. It is not an antiplatelet drug.\n\nThe thienopyridine drugs are ADP receptor or P2Y12 antagonists that inhibit ADP mediated platelet aggregation. They include the following drugs:\n\n• Clopidogrel - commonly used\n\n• Ticagrelor - reversible drug with a shorter t1/2, does not require activation, may cause mild, self-limited dyspnea\n\n• Prasugrel - 10 times more potent than clopidogrel, rapid onset of action, better absorption, more predictable\n\n• Ticlopidine\n\nClopidogrel and prasugrel are prodrugs that require activation by cytochrome P450 enzymes. CYP2C19 polymorphisms can cause reduced platelet inhibition with clopidogrel and increased risk of cardiovascular events.","t":"Platelet Disorders","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"A 24-year-old woman underwent evaluation for bleeding manifestations and severe anemia. Investigations revealed combined autoimmune hemolytic anemia and idiopathic thrombocytopenic purpura. What is the likely diagnosis?","o":["Evans syndrome","May-Hegglin anomaly","Fechtner syndrome","Rosenthal syndrome"],"a":0,"e":"Combined autoimmune hemolytic anemia (AIHA) and idiopathic thrombocytopenic purpura (ITP) is called Evans syndrome.\n\nOptions B and C: May-Hegglin anomaly and Fechtner syndrome are autosomal dominant hereditary conditions presenting with bleeding, sensorineural hearing loss, glomerulonephritis, and/or eye abnormalities. The severity of the bleeding symptoms is out of proportion to the moderately decreased platelet count.\n\nOption D: Rosenthal syndrome or hemophilia C refers to factor XI deficiency.","t":"Platelet Disorders","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"What is the most common side effect experienced by patients taking enteric-coated aspirin?","o":["Gastrointestinal symptoms","Aspirin sensitivity","Aspirin tolerance","Medication overuse headache"],"a":0,"e":"The most common side effect of aspirin is gastrointestinal symptoms. The use of enteric-coated aspirin does not reduce these side effects.\n\nAspirin irreversibly acetylates and inhibits platelet cyclooxygenase (COX)-1, which causes reduced synthesis of thromboxane A2. At high doses, it also inhibits COX-2, which results in the anti-inflammatory effects of aspirin.\n\nThe major GI side effects include dyspepsia, erosive gastritis, peptic ulcers with bleeding and perforation. The overall risk of major bleeding with aspirin is 1–3% per year, which increases 2-3 fold if there is concurrent use of other antiplatelet drugs or anticoagulants. The risk of bleeding can be reduced by eradication of Helicobacter pylori infection and giving proton pump inhibitors (PPIs).\n\nAspirin sensitivity refers to bronchospasm or urticaria/angioedema seen immediately after administration of aspirin. It is also called Samter's triad (asthma, recurrent nasal polyps, aspirin sensitivity). In patients with aspirin resistance, there is failure of aspirin to produce its normal platelet inhibitory functions.","t":"Platelet Disorders","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is incorrect about the mechanism of action of dipyridamole?","o":["Increases levels of cyclic AMP in platelets","Blocks the reuptake of adenosine","Inhibits phosphodiesterase-mediated cyclic AMP degradation","Increases intracellular adenosine levels"],"a":3,"e":"Dipyridamole increases extracellular adenosine levels.\n\nIt is an antiplatelet drug that increases levels of cyclic AMP (cAMP) in platelets by:\n\n• Blocking the reuptake of adenosine and increasing extracellular adenosine levels\n\n• Inhibiting phosphodiesterase-mediated cAMP degradation\n\nThis increase in cyclic AMP reduces intracellular levels of calcium. This inhibits platelet activation and aggregation.\n\nDipyridamole is a weak antiplatelet used in combination with aspirin for the prevention of stroke in patients with TIA.\n\nSide effects include gastrointestinal and vasodilatory complaints (headache, flushing, dizziness, hypotension). Due to it's vasodilatory effects, it is contraindicated in patients with symptomatic coronary artery disease.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/83f105195b784a118f2d762379820034x1280x1308.JPEG"],"t":"Platelet Disorders","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"A 62-year-old man diagnosed with myocardial infarction is planned for percutaneous intervention. He was diagnosed with diabetic nephropathy 2 months ago. The cardiologist decides to start him on aspirin and an additional antiplatelet drug. Which of the following can be used for this patient?","o":["Abciximab only","Abciximab or eptifibatide","Tirofiban or eptifibatide","Tirofiban only"],"a":0,"e":"Abciximab is safe in patients with renal impairment. It belongs to the group of GpIIb-IIIa receptor antagonists. These are parenteral drugs that are used in the management of patients with acute coronary syndrome.\n\nThere are three important drugs in this group:\n\nFeature\nAbciximab\nEptifibatide\nTirofiban\n\nGp IIb/IIIa antagonist\n Non-specific\nSpecific\nSpecific\n\nPlasma half-life\nShort (min)\nLong (2.5 h)\nLong (2.0 h)\n\nPlatelet-bound half-life\nLong (days)\nShort (s)\nShort (s)\n\nRenal clearance\nNo\nYes\nYes\n\nImmune-mediated thrombocytopenia and bleeding are the most common side effects. Thrombocytopenia can occur within 24 hours of initiation.","t":"Platelet Disorders","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Plasmapheresis is the mainstay of treatment in patients with which of the following?","o":["Autoimmune hemolytic anemia","Disseminated intravascular coagulation","Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura","Hemolytic uremic syndrome"],"a":2,"e":"Plasmapheresis is the mainstay of the treatment of patients with thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (TTP).\n\nThe best response to this therapy is seen in patients with ADAMTS13 autoantibodies. Plasmapheresis is continued until the resolution of platelet count and signs of hemolysis for at least 2 days.\n\nOption A: The first line of treatment of autoimmune hemolytic anemia is glucocorticoids. The second line includes rituximab and splenectomy.\n\nOption B: Treatment of disseminated intravascular coagulation is primarily supportive with measures to treat the underlying cause.\n\nOption D: Treatment of HUS is primarily supportive. Dialysis is usually required. In some varients of HUS plasma infusion/exchange can be effective by replasing the complement-regulatory proteins.","t":"Platelet Disorders","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not seen in patients with thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura?","o":["Hemolytic anemia","Focal neurological deficits","Venous thromboembolism","Elevated serum creatinine"],"a":2,"e":"Large vessel arterial or venous thromboemboli are not seen in thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (TTP). This condition is associated with microvascular thrombosis.\n\nTTP occurs due to deficiency or autoantibodies against the metalloprotease ADAMTS13, which normally cleaves multimers of vWF. In this condition, the vWF molecules persist causing pathogenic platelet adhesion and aggregation. This causes localized platelet thrombi and fibrin deposits at various sites.\n\nIt presents with the following pentad of clinical features:\n\n1. Microangiopathic hemolytic anemia - mechanical shearing of RBCs as they pass through platelet rich-microthrombi\n\n2. Thrombocytopenia - deposition of platelets in microthrombi\n\n3. Renal failure - microthrombi in the renal vasculature\n\n4. Neurologic findings - headache, confusion, transient focal neurologic deficits\n\n5. Fever","t":"Platelet Disorders","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is incorrect regarding patients with thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura?","o":["Clinical features overlap with hemolytic uremic syndrome","PT and aPTT are prolonged","It is common in patients with HIV infection","It can be associated with use of quinine"],"a":1,"e":"In patients with thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (TTP), the PT and aPTT are normal, as consumptive coagulopathy is not seen.\n\nTTP and HUS are thrombotic microangiopathies with overlapping clinical features. TTP can be primary (ADAMTS13 deficiency) or secondary (associated with infections like HIV and drugs like quinine).\n\nNote: In disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC), thrombocytopenia and microangiopathy are seen. It is associated with consumption of clotting factors and fibrinogen resulting in prolonged PT and aPTT.","t":"Platelet Disorders","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"What is the most common genetic abnormality seen in patients with recurrent history of venous thrombosis?","o":["Factor V Leiden mutation","Prothrombin mutation","Protein C deficiency","Antithrombin III deficiency"],"a":0,"e":"Factor V Leiden mutation is the most common inherited cause of venous thrombosis. Venous thrombosis typically manifests as deep vein thrombosis and pulmonary thromboembolism.\n\nHypercoagulable states or thrombophilias can be inherited or acquired:\n\n• Inherited causes in decreasing order of prevalence are as follows:\n\n• Factor V Leiden mutation\n\n• Prothrombin mutation\n\n• Protein C deficiency\n\n• Protein S deficiency\n\n• Antithrombin III deficiency\n\n• Acquired causes include:\n\n• Surgery\n\n• Malignancy\n\n• Antiphospholipid syndrome\n\n• Trauma\n\n• Pregnancy\n\n• Long-haul travel\n\n• Obesity\n\n• Oral contraceptives/hormone replacement\n\n• Myeloproliferative disorders\n\n• Polycythemia vera","t":"Platelet Disorders","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following qualitative platelet disorders are inherited as autosomal dominant?","o":["Glanzman thrombasthenia","Bernard Soulier syndrome","Chediak-Higashi syndrome","Platelet storage pool syndrome"],"a":3,"e":"Platelet storage pool disease (SPD) is an example of an autosomal dominant qualitative platelet disorder. It refers to a group of disorders characterized by a deficiency of platelet granules causing secretion defects.\n\nBernard Soulier syndrome, Glanzman thrombasthenia, and Chediak-Higashi syndrome are inherited as autosomal recessive traits.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/9d84bfdb468e467b9f06e8f391a6bf7ex1280x2203.JPEG"],"t":"Platelet Disorders","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"What is the most common type of leukemia in adults?","o":["Acute myeloid leukemia","Adult acute lymphoblastic leukemia","Chronic myeloid leukemia","Chronic lymphoid leukemia"],"a":3,"e":"Chronic lymphoid leukemia (CLL) is the most common type of leukemia in adults.\n\nThe common types of leukemia in various age groups is as follows:\n\n• Acute myeloid leukemia (AML) - most common acute leukemia in adults\n\n• Acute lymphoid leukemia (ALL) - most common cancer in childhood. The most common subtype of ALL is B-cell ALL\n\n• Non-Hodgkin's lymphoma (NHL) - most common lymphoid malignancy in adults","t":"Acute Leukemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not a risk factor for acute myeloid leukemia?","o":["Down's syndrome","Bloom syndrome","Fanconi anemia","Evan's syndrome"],"a":3,"e":"Evan's syndrome does not predispose to acute myeloid leukemia (AML).\n\nThe following conditions predispose to AML:\n\nGenetic conditions:\n\n• Down's syndrome:\n\n• Young children <4 years\n\n• Typically of the acute megakaryocytic subtype and associated with GATA1 mutations\n\n• Excellent clinical outcomes\n\n• Defective DNA repair in conditions like ataxia telangiectasia, Fanconi anemia, and Bloom syndrome\n\nEnvironmental factors:\n\n• Drugs:\n\n• Alkylating agents - cyclophosphamide, melphalan, dacarbazine, platinum compounds, after 4-6 years of exposure\n\n• Topoisomerase II inhibitors - etoposide, after 1-3 years of exposure.\n\n• Radiation - high dose radiation, therapeutic radiation in patients exposed to alkylating agents\n\n• Chemicals - benzene, petroleum products, pesticide\n\nHematological disorders with increased risk of AML:\n\n• Myeloproliferative disorders\n\n• Paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria\n\n• Aplastic anemia\n\n• Myelodysplastic syndrome\n\n• Multiple myeloma","t":"Acute Leukemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"A 2-year-old girl was referred with recurrent bacterial infections. Parents report that their first child also had similar features and died at the age of 18 months from myeloid leukemia. Investigations revealed markedly reduced absolute neutrophil count. What is the likely diagnosis?","o":["Bloom syndrome","Kostmann disease","Diamond-Blackfan syndrome","Chediak-Higashi syndrome"],"a":1,"e":"This clinical scenario of congenital neutropenia is suggestive of Kostmann syndrome. It is associated with an increased risk of acute myeloid leukemia (AML).\n\nKostmann syndrome occurs due to mutations in the G-CSF receptor and neutrophil elastase. This causes bone marrow failure specifically in the myeloid lineage. It presents in young children as recurrent bacterial infections (such as otitis media, cellulitis) and oral ulcers.\n\nOption A: Bloom syndrome occurs due to defects in DNA repair. It is not associated with neutropenia but has a higher risk of AML.\n\nOption C: Diamond-Blackfan syndrome refers to congenital erythroid aplasia. It is also associated with a higher risk of AML.\n\nOption D: Chediak-Higashi syndrome is due to defective phagolysosome formation. It is not associated with neutropenia.","t":"Acute Leukemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"A 60-year-old man is being evaluated for suspected acute myeloid leukemia. He reports a fast-growing swelling in his mouth for the past 1 week. On examination, there is a greenish gingival mass with a necrotic surface. This presentation is likely associated with which of the following?","o":["AML M2","AML M3","AML M7","AML M5a"],"a":0,"e":"This clinical scenario is suggestive of a chloroma. It is commonly associated with the M2 subtype of acute myeloid leukemia, also called AML with maturation.\n\nChloroma is also called myeloid sarcoma, extramedullary myeloid tumor, or granulocytic sarcoma. It is an extramedullary tumor mass consisting of myeloid blasts, with or without maturation. It is called chloroma due to the peculiar green color, caused by myeloperoxidase. The sites affected include orbit (most common), skull, oral cavity, paranasal sinuses, sternum, ribs, vertebrae, and pelvis.","t":"Acute Leukemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Patients suffering from which type of AML are more prone to develop disseminated intravascular coagulation?","o":["Monocytic","Myelomonocytic","Monoblastic","Acute promyelocytic"],"a":3,"e":"Disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC) is commonly seen in patients with acute promyelocytic leukemia (APL).","t":"Acute Leukemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Oral cavity examination of a 56-year-old man with suspected leukemia revealed the following. What subtype of AML is he likely to be suffering from?","o":["M7","M5","M3","M2"],"a":1,"e":"The given image shows swollen and spongy gums, indicating gum infiltration. This is a characteristic feature of acute monocytic leukemia (AML-M5).\n\nInfiltration of the gingivae, skin, soft tissues or meninges with leukemic blasts is characteristic of the monocytic subtypes and those with 11q23 chromosomal abnormalities.\n\nLess commonly, infiltration is also seen in acute myelomonocytic leukemia (AML-M4).","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b9a5857597db45929ff7e63d72113b3d.JPEG"],"t":"Acute Leukemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following would be seen on flow cytometry of a patient with acute megakaryoblastic leukemia?","o":["CD113","CD 117","CD 61","CD 13"],"a":2,"e":"On flow cytometry, the immunophenotype of acute megakaryoblastic leukemia is CD41 and/or CD61 positive (platelet-specific antigens).\n\nFlow cytometry is useful in establishing the different subtypes of AML:\n\nPhenotype\nCD markers\n\nMyeloblastic\nCD11b,13,33,117\n\nPromyelocytic(M3)\nCD13,33\n\nMyelomonocytic(M4)\nCD11b,13,14,33\n\nMonocytic(M5)\nCD11b,11c,13,14,33\n\nErythroid(M6)\nGlycophorin,spectrin\n\nMegakaryoblastic(M7)\nCD41,61\n\nBasophilic\nCD11,13,33,123\n\nMast cell\nCD13,33,117","t":"Acute Leukemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is true about the WHO classification of acute myeloid leukemia?","o":["The clinical features are not considered to define the subtype","The cutoff value of blast cells for diagnosis is 20%","It is only based on morphological features of myeloid precursors","Chromosomal translocations along with &gt; 20% blasts are needed for diagnosis"],"a":1,"e":"The cutoff value of blast cells for the diagnosis of AML is 20% according to the WHO classification. This classification is based on the clinical features, cytogenetic and molecular abnormalities in addition to morphology. This is in contrast to the previously used French-American-British (FAB) classification which was based on only morphology of myeloid precursors.\n\nThe major changes in the WHO classification in contrast to FAB classification are:\n\n• Blast cutoff for diagnosis of AML is 20% (30% in FAB classification)\n\n• Specific chromosomal rearrangements such as t (8;21), inv(16), t(16;16) and t (15;17) can define AML even with < 20% blasts\n\n• Immunophenotyping (flow cytometry) is used to differentiate AML from ALL and to identify some subtypes of AML\n\n• Myeloid dysplasia with blasts between 20-30% can benefit from therapy for myelodysplastic syndrome (decitabine, 5-azacytidine, cedazuridine)","t":"Acute Leukemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"A 50-year-old man is referred for evaluation of severe anemia, persistent fever, and recurrent epistaxis. Lab investigations are given below. What is the diagnosis?\n\nHb = 6 g/dL\n\nTotal WBC count - 40,000/ mm3\n\nPlatelet count - 12,000/cu.mm\n\nBone marrow - 10% blasts, positive for t(8;21) translocation","o":["Acute lymphoblastic leukemia","Acute myeloblastic leukemia","Preleukemia","Aleukemic leukemia"],"a":1,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of acute myeloblastic leukemia (AML).\n\nAccording to the WHO criteria, the diagnosis of AML requires any one of the following:\n\n• Blast cell count ≥20% either in the peripheral blood or bone marrow\n\n• Presence of any one of the following cytogenetic abnormalities:\n\n• t(15;17)\n\n• t(8;21)\n\n• Inversion 16 or t(16;16)\n\n• t(9;22)\n\n• NPM1 mutation (Nucleophosmin mutation)\n\n• CEBPA mutation\n\n• FLT3-ITD","t":"Acute Leukemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Patients with all of the following are classified as AML with recurrent cytogenetic abnormalities except:","o":["t(16,16)","NPM1 mutation","CEBPA mutation","t(9,22)"],"a":3,"e":"The chromosomal translocation t(9,22) is called the Philadelphia chromosome. It is seen in patients with CML.\n\nAML with recurrent genetic abnormalities includes the following chromosomal aberrations:\n\n• t(15;17)\n\n• t(8;21)\n\n• Inversion 16 or t(16;16)\n\nTwo gene mutations have been added to the criteria of AML with recurrent genetic abnormalities in addition to chromosomal aberrations. Both gene mutations are associated with a good prognosis.\n\n• NPM1 mutation: Nucleophosmin mutation\n\n• CEBPA mutation","t":"Acute Leukemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following cytogenetic abnormalities is seen in patients with acute promyelocytic leukemia?","o":["t(15;17)","t(16;16)","RUNX1-RUNX1T1","CBF-β mutation"],"a":0,"e":"t(15,17) is associated with acute promyelocytic leukemia (APL).","t":"Acute Leukemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"A 65-year-old woman presented with fatigue and fever for the past 2 months. Investigations revealed pancytopenia and the following peripheral smear findings. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["AML","ALL","CML","CLL"],"a":0,"e":"The given clinical scenario and peripheral smear showing blast cells with peroxidase granules are suggestive of acute myeloid leukemia (AML). Peroxidase granules are dark blue-stained granules seen in the cytoplasm on peroxidase staining.\n\nMyeloperoxidase cytochemical activity is negative in chronic myeloid leukemia (CML) and blast crisis.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1de3afe66ebb411baf86ba59f5c91a55.PNG"],"t":"Acute Leukemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following patients with AML has a better prognosis than the others?","o":["A 48-year-old man newly diagnosed with AML with a family history of lymphoma","A 60-year-old woman who developed AML following chemotherapy for ovarian cancer","A 71-year-old man with a longstanding history of myelodysplastic syndrome","A 67-year-old woman evaluated with a 4-month history of pancytopenia prior to diagnosis"],"a":0,"e":"Young age of onset is associated with a good prognosis in patients with AML. Family history does not affect the prognosis.\n\nPoor prognosis is seen in the following cases:\n\n• Advancing age (usually defined as > 60 years)\n\n• Previous hematologic disorders - myeloproliferative neoplasms and myelodysplastic syndrome\n\n• Longstanding cytopenias >3 months before diagnosis\n\n• Therapy-related AML (chemotherapy for other cancers)\n\n• Chromosomal aberrations with poor outcomes","t":"Acute Leukemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"A pathologist is examining the peripheral smear of a patient with suspected leukemia. All of the following features are suggestive of a myeloblast except:","o":["Auer rods","Prominent nucleoli","Phi bodies","Pelger-Huet anomaly"],"a":3,"e":"Pelger-Huet anomaly is not a feature of myeloblasts. Auer rods and Phi bodies are considered pathognomonic of AML.\n\nMyeloblasts have the following characteristics as shown in the following image:\n\n• Large, uniform population of cells\n\n• Prominent nucleoli (yellow arrow in the image)\n\n• Primary cytoplasmic granules\n\n• Auer rods - needle-shaped azurophilic intracytoplasmic inclusion bodies formed by fusion of lysosomes (black arrow)\n\n• Phi-bodies - fusiform or spindle-shaped rods similar to Auer rods that require special stains for hydroperoxidases\n\n• Pseudo-Chediak-Higashi syndrome - Giant lysosomes forming intracytoplasmic inclusion bodies\n\nPelger-Huet anomaly refers to neutrophils with bilobed nuclei that occur as a benign, inherited condition. Pseudo-Pelger-Huet anomaly refers to an acquired defect seen in CML, myelodysplastic syndrome.\n\nNote: Chediak-Higashi syndrome is a rare autosomal recessive disorder with abnormal lysosomal trafficking (LYST gene). Presents with oculocutaneous albinism and immunodeficiency.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/eb2d4b735e274611a79fe8ed2d02baa9x1064x1634.JPEG"],"t":"Acute Leukemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"A patient presents to the ER with a sudden-onset fever and the following painful rash over her face and upper limbs. CBC reveals neutrophilia. This presentation is associated with all of the following conditions except:","o":["Acute myeloid leukemia","Crohn's disease","Systemic lupus erythematosus","Aplastic anemia"],"a":3,"e":"This clinical presentation with painful edematous plaques is suggestive of febrile neutrophilic dermatosis or Sweet syndrome. It is not associated with aplastic anemia. It is associated with the following conditions:\n\n• Acute myeloid leukemia (AML) - most common\n\n• Inflammatory bowel disease (IBD)\n\n• Solid malignancies of the genitourinary tract\n\n• Systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE)\n\n• Drugs - all-trans-retinoic acid, granulocyte colony-stimulating factor (G-CSF)\n\n• Idiopathic Sweet syndrome - common in women following respiratory tract infection\n\nThis condition presents with the following triad:\n\n• Fever\n\n• Neutrophilia\n\n• Dense dermal infiltrate of neutrophils:\n\n• Pinkish-red to reddish-brown edematous painful plaques\n\n• Occur over the head, neck, extremities","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/90fc36735ada43a1ac4e06c99141b0a7.PNG"],"t":"Acute Leukemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"What is the most commonly used complete remission induction regimen in patients with AML?","o":["All-trans-retinoic acid and arsenic trioxide","L- asparginase","Cytarabine with anthracyclines","Cytarabine with alkylating agents"],"a":2,"e":"The most commonly used complete remission induction regimen in patients with AML is cytarabine and an anthracycline (e.g., daunorubicin, idarubicin).\n\nThe treatment of AML is as follows:\n\n• Induction chemotherapy with a 7+3 regimen:\n\n• Cytarabine - continuous intravenous infusion for 7 days\n\n• IV daunorubicin on days 1,2,3\n\n• Side effects include neutropenia, sepsis\n\n• Post-remission therapy:\n\n• High dose cytarabine\n\n• Allogenic hematopoeitic stem cell transplant\n\nNovel agents include gemtuzumab ozogamicin, a CD33 monoclonal antibody.","t":"Acute Leukemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"A 60-year-old man came with generalized bone pain, and easy bruisability for the past 6 months. His WBC count was 60,000 cu. mm and bone marrow aspiration revealed the following. Immunohistochemistry was positive for MPO and cytogenetic studies showed presence of t(15:17). What is the preferred treatment regimen?","o":["Cytarabine","Arsenic trioxide","All transretinoic acid","Arsenic trioxide and all transretinoic acid"],"a":3,"e":"The presence of blast cells with numerous Auer rods, MPO (myeloperoxidase) positive cells, and t(15:17) is diagnostic of acute promyelocytic leukemia (APL). The preferred treatment regimen is arsenic trioxide and all-trans-retinoic acid (ATRA).\n\nAPL is characterized by t(15; 17) translocation resulting in the PML-RAR-alpha fusion gene (promyelocyte-retinoic acid receptor alpha). It is a highly curable subtype of leukemia. The treatment is as follows:\n\n• Induction therapy:\n\n• ATRA + anthracycline (daunorubicin or idarubicin) or cytarabine (OR)\n\n• ATRA + arsenic trioxide\n\n• Consolidation therapy:\n\n• Post remission anthracycline-based chemotherapy","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/673359cd5a6240de8e1730bbd82e271c.JPEG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3a7241b9ca1c4a11b931b0fb6cc49d4fx250x364.JPEG"],"t":"Acute Leukemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Individuals in which age group have the highest incidence of T-cell acute lymphoblastic lymphoma?","o":["20-25 years","1-10 years","10-19 years","26-30 years"],"a":2,"e":"The highest incidence of T-ALL (T-cell acute lymphoblastic lymphoma) is at 10-19 years (adolescence) when the thymus reaches the maximum size.\n\nOn the other hand, B-ALL which is more common has a peak incidence at 3 years (1-10 years). At this age, the number of normal bone marrow pre-B cells (the cell of origin) is at its greatest.","t":"Acute Leukemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Children with Down's syndrome are at an increased risk of developing all of the following except:","o":["Acute lymphoid leukemia","Acute myeloid leukemia","Transient myeloproliferative disorder","Myelodysplastic disorder"],"a":3,"e":"Down's syndrome is not associated with myelodysplastic disorder. These patients are at increased risk of developing the following conditions:\n\n• Acute lymphoid leukemia (ALL) - most commonly B-cell ALL\n\n• Acute myeloid leukemia (AML) - most commonly M7/megakaryoblastic subtype\n\n• Transient myeloproliferative disorder:\n\n• Associated with GATA1 mutations.\n\n• Seen in 10% of neonates, resolves in first 3 months of life\n\n• Require close follow-up as 20-30% may develop AML by 3 years of life","t":"Acute Leukemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"An 8-year-old boy was brought with fever, weight loss, and easy bruisability. Initial examination revealed severe pallor and significant lymphadenopathy. Peripheral smear revealed the following cells, which were also seen on bone marrow studies. Further investigations revealed CD10+ cells. What is the likely diagnosis?","o":["T-cell ALL","Pro B-cell ALL","Pre B-cell ALL","Mature B-cell ALL"],"a":2,"e":"This clinical scenario and image showing blast cells in a young child are suggestive of acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL). The presence of CD10 is diagnostic of pre-B-cell ALL.\n\nCD10 is also called the common ALL antigen (CALLA). It is the specific immunohistochemical marker for pre-B cell ALL. This is the most common type of ALL and is associated with a good prognosis. However, CD10 is negative in infantile leukemia, which is associated with translocation (4;11).\n\nThe immunohistochemistry of ALL is as follows:\n\nType\nCD marker\n\npro-B cell\nCD34, CD19, CD79a\n\npre- B cell\nCD19, CD79a, CD10, Tdt, Cytoplasmic Ig\n\nMature B cell\nCD19, CD79a,CD20, CD21,CD22, Surface IgM and D\n\nT cell\nCD 2,3,4,5,6,7\n\nOther abnormalities in ALL include:\n\nGood prognosis\nBad prognosis\n\nCytogenic\nt(12,21)\n\nt(9,22), t(4,11)\n\nBurkitt lymphoma-associated ALL / ALL-L3 - t(2,8) , t(8,14), t(8,22)\n\nMolecular\n\nHyperdiploidy\n\nHOX\n\nNOTCH 1 mutations\n\nHypodiploidy","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/ec64327e878d43ca8fea3da8142db3eb.JPEG"],"t":"Acute Leukemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following indicate a good prognosis in children diagnosed with acute lymphoblastic leukemia?\n\n1. Age < 1 year\n\n2. Mild hepatosplenomegaly\n\n3. Hyperdiploidy\n\n4. Female sex\n\n5. WBC count < 50,000 per mm3","o":["3, 4, 5","1, 2, 3","2, 4, 5","1, 2, 4"],"a":0,"e":"In children with ALL, a good prognosis is indicated by low WBC count, female sex, and hyperdiploidy.\n\nOther abnormalities in ALL include:\n\nGood prognosis\nBad prognosis\n\nCytogenic\nt(12,21)\n\nt(9,22), t(4,11)\n\nBurkitt lymphoma-associated ALL / ALL-L3 - t(2,8) , t(8,14), t(8,22)\n\nMolecular\n\nHyperdiploidy\n\nNOTCH 1\n\nHOX\n\nHypodiploidy","t":"Acute Leukemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"A 3-year-old boy with Down's syndrome was brought with recurrent infections and bruising over his legs. Further workup was diagnostic of acute lymphoblastic leukemia and he was started on chemotherapy. Which of the following must be added on to prevent metastases to the central nervous system?","o":["L-Asparginase","Prednisolone","Cytarabine","Methotrextate"],"a":3,"e":"Intrathecal methotrexate is used for CNS prophylaxis in acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL). It is essential in this condition as nearly 1/3 of patients develop CNS involvement at relapse without prophylactic therapy.\n\nThe treatment of ALL is as follows:\n\nInduction (4 weeks):\n\n• L-Asparaginase:\n\n• Single-dose\n\n• Side effects - pancreatitis, thrombosis\n\n• Vincristine:\n\n• Weekly\n\n• Side effects - neuropathy, SIADH\n\n• Prednisolone\n\nConsolidation: Intrathecal methotrexate\n\nMaintenance (24-36 months):\n\n• 6-Mercaptopurine\n\n• Methotrexate","t":"Acute Leukemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"What is the most common symptom seen in patients with multiple myeloma?","o":["Easy fatigability","Bone pain","Oliguria","Fever"],"a":1,"e":"Bone pain is the most common symptom in multiple myeloma.\n\nMultiple myeloma is a plasma cell malignancy in which there is an increased proliferation of monoclonal plasma cells. There is an increased production of monoclonal paraprotein (M protein).\n\nClinical features of multiple myeloma:\n\n1. Bone pain (most common):\n\n• Due to proliferation of tumor cells, activation of osteoclasts that destroy bone, and suppression of osteoblasts that form new bone.\n\n• Persistent localized pain in a patient with myeloma usually signifies a pathological fracture.\n\n2. Fever due to recurrent bacterial infections (second most common)\n\n3. Acute kidney injury due to:\n\n• Hypercalcemia (most common)\n\n• Amyloid deposits\n\n• Hyperuricemia\n\n• Recurrent infections\n\n• Infiltration of the kidney by myeloma cells\n\n4. Normocytic-normochromic anemia due to:\n\n• Replacement of normal marrow by expanding tumor cells\n\n• Inhibition of hematopoiesis by factors made by the tumor\n\n• Reduced production of erythropoietin by the kidney\n\nMyeloma defining events (MDE) include the presence of one or more features such as hypercalcemia, renal failure, anemia and lytic bone lesions.","t":"Plasma Cell Disorders","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is true about multiple myeloma?","o":["Increased monoclonal IgG1 causes hyperviscosity syndrome","Monoclonal gammopathy of undetermined significance mostly progresses to multiple myeloma","There is decreased anion gap","Radioisotope bone scanning can be used for diagnosis"],"a":2,"e":"There is decreased anion gap in multiple myeloma due to the M protein.\n\nAnion gap in the serum is calculated as Na⁺ – (Cl– +HCO3–).\n\nThe M protein is cationic, resulting in the retention of chloride causing decreased anion gap.\n\nOnly 1% of patients with monoclonal gammopathy of undetermined significance (MGUS) go on to develop multiple myeloma. Hyperviscosity symptoms in multiple myeloma occurs due to excessive production of M proteins, usually of IgG3 or IgA subtype. Waldenstrom macroglobulinemia (IgM myeloma) is the most common cause for hyperviscosity syndrome due to excess IgM paraprotein in these cases.\n\nRadioisotopic bone scanning is less useful in diagnosis because the bone lesions are lytic in nature and are rarely associated with osteoblastic new bone formation.","t":"Plasma Cell Disorders","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"A 72-year-old man presented with complaints of fatiguability and chronic lower back pain. Laboratory findings are as follows. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nHemoglobin - 9 g/dl\n\nSerum calcium - 11.5 mg/dl\n\nSerum M protein - 50 g/l\n\nBone marrow biopsy showed 15 % clonal plasma cells","o":["Multiple myeloma","Monoclonal gammopathy of unknown significance","Smoldering multiple myeloma","Non-secretory myeloma"],"a":0,"e":"In this scenario, the patient has increased M protein (>30g/l), bone marrow clonal plasma cells >10%, and evidence of end-organ damage (fatiguability and back pain). The most likely diagnosis is multiple myeloma.\n\nDiagnostic criteria for plasma cell disorders:\n\nCriteria\nMonoclonal gammopathy of undetermined significance (MGUS)\nSmoldering multiple myeloma (asymptomatic myeloma)\nMultiple myeloma\nNon-secretory myeloma\n\nM protein (>30g/l)\nNo\nYes\nYes\nNo\n\nBone marrow plasma cells (>10%)\nNo\nYes\nYes\nYes\n\nEnd organ damage (CRAB)\n\nS. Calcium >11 mg/dl\n\nRenal, S. Creatinine >2 mg/dl\n\nAnemia, Hemoglobin <10g/dl\n\nLytic bone lesions\n\nNo\nNo\nYes\nYes\n\nNote: Solitary plasmacytoma of bone has only a single area of bone destruction due to clonal plasma cells, no M protein, bone marrow appears normal, and no end-organ damage.\n\nPlasma cell leukemia is an aggressive plasma cell proliferative disorder with >20% abnormal plasma cells in the peripheral blood.","t":"Plasma Cell Disorders","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"What is the most common cause of renal failure in patients with multiple myeloma?","o":["Light chain deposition","Amyloidosis","Recurrent infections","Hypercalcemia"],"a":3,"e":"Hypercalcemia is the most common cause of renal failure in multiple myeloma.\n\nThe second most common cause of renal failure in multiple myeloma is light chain cast nephropathy. The most common type of myeloma causing renal failure is light chain myeloma.\n\nMechanisms involved in acute kidney injury are:\n\n• Hypercalcemia\n\n• Amyloid deposits\n\n• Hyperuricemia\n\n• Recurrent infections\n\n• Infiltration of the kidney by myeloma cells\n\n• Tubular damage by excretion of light chains (Fanconi's syndrome)","t":"Plasma Cell Disorders","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"A patient was brought to the hospital with dehydration and generalised weakness. He was diagnosed with multiple myeloma 2 years ago. Investigations revealed metabolic acidosis and hypokalemia. Urine analysis showed a pH of 5.1, glycosuria, and proteinuria. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Renal tubular acidosis type 1","Adult Fanconi's syndrome","Renal tubular acidosis type 4","Alport syndrome"],"a":1,"e":"In this patient with hypokalemia and urine pH of 5.1, type 2 renal tubular acidosis (adult Fanconi’s syndrome) is the likely diagnosis. It is commonly associated with multiple myeloma.\n\nNormally, light chains are filtered, reabsorbed in the proximal tubules, and catabolized. In multiple myeloma, there is an increased amount of light chains and the tubular cells are overloaded.\n\nThis leads to proximal tubular damage either directly from light chain toxic effects or indirectly from the release of intracellular lysosomal enzymes.\n\nClinical features of renal tubular acidosis type 2:\n\n• Metabolic acidosis\n\n• Urine pH <5.3\n\n• Proteinuria (only light chains)\n\n• Glycosuria\n\n• Aminoaciduria\n\nAlport's syndrome is characterised by hematuria, hearing loss and eye abnormalities.","t":"Plasma Cell Disorders","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"What is the immunophenotype of tumor cells in multiple myeloma?","o":["CD19","CD38","CD 20","CD 5"],"a":1,"e":"The immunophenotype of tumor cells in multiple myeloma is CD38 and CD138.\n\nMultiple myeloma is derived from plasma cells in which B cell markers (CD19, CD20) are lost.\n\nImmunophenotype of tumor cells in lymphoplasmacytic lymphoma / Waldenstrom's macroglobulinemia is CD19, CD20, surface IgM+, CD38. A lymphoplasmacytic cell retains B cell markers and carries plasma cell markers.","t":"Plasma Cell Disorders","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"A 45-year-old patient presented with multiple fractures and bone pain. Serum electrophoresis revealed elevated serum M protein. What does it most likely to constitute?","o":["Ig M","Ig G","Albumin","Lamda light chains"],"a":1,"e":"In the above clinical scenario, the patient most likely has multiple myeloma as there are pathological fractures and elevated M protein. Serum M protein is likely to constitute IgG.\n\nThe serum M component is IgG in 53% of patients, IgA in 25%, and IgD in 1%.\n\nSerum IgM is elevated in Waldenstrom's macroglobulinemia (lymphoplasmacytic lymphoma).","t":"Plasma Cell Disorders","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"A 60-year-old man presented with fatigue and back pain since 3 months. Investigations were done and are as follows. What is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nHemoglobin=7 g/dl\n\nTotal leukocyte count =9000/cu.mm\n\nSerum protein = 6 gm%\n\nSerum albumin = 3.5 g/dl\n\nSerum protein electrophoresis (SPEP) shows the given pattern\n\nBone marrow clonal plasmacytosis = 15%","o":["Multiple myeloma","Lymphoplasmacytic lymphoma","Monoclonal gammopathy of undetermined significance","Non-secretory myeloma"],"a":3,"e":"In the given clinical scenario, the patient has normal SPEP and clonal plasmacytosis. The most likely diagnosis is non-secretory myeloma.\n\nNon-secretory myeloma is characterised by the absence of M protein in the serum or urine. Bone marrow clonal plasmacytosis > 10% and myeloma defining symptoms.\n\nOption A: In multiple myeloma, SPEP demonstrates a sharp peak (church spire or M band) in the gamma globulin region, as shown below.\n\nOption B: Lymphoplasmacytic lymphoma (Waldenstrom's macroglobulinemia) also demonstrates a localized peak (M band) in the gamma globulin region. Immunofixation is required to identify the type of immunoglobulin.\n\nOption C: Monoclonal gammopathy of undetermined significance (MGUS) does not present with myeloma-defining symptoms. Clonal bone marrow cells will be less than 10%","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1d360e5a02a5420da1642063594ef076.JPEG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/3c5da67cfc2c404fbc297a8d109b53dax1280x1233.JPEG"],"t":"Plasma Cell Disorders","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"A 69-year-old business tycoon is diagnosed with multiple myeloma. He is worried about his business empire and asks you how long he will survive. Which of the following is the best predictor for this?","o":["Durie-Salmon staging system","Percentage of circulating plasma cells","Beta-2 microglobulin levels","Plasma cell labeling index"],"a":2,"e":"Serum β2-microglobulin levels are the best modality to predict the median survival period.\n\nAlbumin levels alone cannot be used to predict the median survival period. It is used in combination with β2-microglobulin for risk stratification.\n\nPatients with β2-microglobulin levels <3.5 mg/L and albumin levels > 3.5 g/dl have a median survival period of 5 years. Those with levels >3.5 mg/L and albumin < 3.5 g/dl have a survival period of fewer than 2 years.\n\nDurie-Salmon staging is unable to predict outcomes with newer agents. Hence it is no longer used.\n\nFactors associated with poor prognosis:\n\n• Plasma cell labeling index\n\n• Chromosomal aberrations\n\n• Circulating plasma cells\n\n• Performance status\n\n• Highly elevated LDH\n\n• Extramedullary disease\n\n• De novo plasma cell leukemia","t":"Plasma Cell Disorders","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"In patients with multiple myeloma, the presence of which of the following is associated with a poor prognosis?","o":["t (11,14)","t (6,14)","17p deletion","13q deletion"],"a":2,"e":"17p deletion is associated with a poor prognosis in multiple myeloma.\n\nChromosomal abnormalities in multiple myeloma are classified as follows:\n\nStandard risk (6-7 years survival):\n\n• t(11;14) (most common)\n\n• t(6;14)\n\n• 13q deletion\n\nHigh risk (2-3 years survival):\n\n• 17p deletion\n\n• t(4;14)\n\n• t(14;16)\n\n• t(14;20)","t":"Plasma Cell Disorders","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"An X-ray skull of a patient with marked hypercalcemia is shown below. Which of the following laboratory parameters is consistent with his diagnosis?","o":["High alkaline phosphatase and normal ESR","High alkaline phosphatase and elevated ESR","Normal alkaline phosphatase and normal ESR","Normal alkaline phosphatase and elevated ESR"],"a":3,"e":"In the above clinical scenario, hypercalcemia along with the skull demonstrating typical 'punched out' lesions is characteristic of multiple myeloma. Normal alkaline phosphatase and elevated ESR are consistent with multiple myeloma.\n\nMultiple myeloma is a malignant plasma cell dyscrasia with the proliferation of plasma cells derived from a single clone. It is associated with an increased osteoclast activity with suppression of osteoblast activity, causing punched-out lytic lesions in the bone and hypercalcemia.\n\nSigns and symptoms:\n\n• Bone pain is the most common symptom\n\n• Pathologic fractures\n\n• Recurrent bacterial infections\n\n• Renal failure\n\n• Normocytic normochromic anemia, elevated ESR, hypercalcemia, normal ALP (ALP is a marker of bone growth and hence is not increased in multiple myeloma)\n\n• Radiography: Punched-out lytic lesions on X-ray. The lesions are purely osteolytic with little or no osteoblastic activity. Hence alkaline phosphatase (ALP) is normal. The most common site of lytic lesion in multiple myeloma is the vertebral column.\n\n• Electrophoresis: M spikes\n\n• Urinalysis: Bence Jones proteinuria\n\n• Bone marrow biopsy: Plasma cells","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/c6a1424ea99b4106878df195ff5d32db.PNG"],"t":"Plasma Cell Disorders","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"A 68-year-old male patient presented with multiple fractures. He has been suffering from recurrent infections for the past 6 months. Bone marrow biopsy showed the following. What is the treatment of choice in this condition?","o":["Lenalidomide + bortezomib + dexamethasone","Thalidomide + cyclophosphamide","Rituximab + cyclophosphamide","Bortezomib + cyclophosphamide + dexamethasone"],"a":0,"e":"In the above clinical scenario, the symptoms and bone marrow biopsy are characteristic of multiple myeloma. Lenalidomide + bortezomib + dexamethasone is the treatment of choice in this condition.\n\nThis combination achieves close to a 100% response. Hence it is one of the preferred induction regimens in transplant-eligible multiple myeloma patients.\n\nOther pharmacological agents are used as second-line and have a 90% response rate. Chemotherapy followed by autologous stem cell transplantation prolongs overall survival.\n\nThe image below shows a bone marrow biopsy with an increased number of plasma cells.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/5b0a6da00a7f427fb24f52f9546620a6.JPEG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c9a0234ddd5945bca2e2420d358c7e43x640x482.JPEG"],"t":"Plasma Cell Disorders","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is a proteasome inhibitor?","o":["Lenalidomide","Rituximab","Cyclophosphamide","Bortezomib"],"a":3,"e":"Bortezomib is a proteasome inhibitor.\n\nIt has a rapid response and is effective in poor-prognosis myeloma. Side effects include neuropathy (both peripheral and autonomic). Subcutaneous and weekly administration can reduce neuropathic side effects.\n\nOther proteasome inhibitors include carfilzomib (second-generation) and ixazomib, an oral proteasome inhibitor, used in relapsed disease.\n\nOption A: Lenalidomide is an immunomodulatory analogue of thalidomide.\n\nOption B: Rituximab is an anti-CD-20 monoclonal antibody.\n\nOption C: Cyclophosphamide is an alkylating chemotherapeutic agent.","t":"Plasma Cell Disorders","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"A 64-year-old male patient is admitted with complaints of weakness of the extremities, blurry vision, and nose-bleeds. On examination, there is lymphadenopathy and dilatation of the retinal veins. Peripheral smear is given below. Which of the following is false regarding this condition?","o":["Bone marrow &gt;10% lymphoplasmacytoid cells","Lytic bone lesions","M component in serum &gt;30g/L","Increased viscosity of blood"],"a":1,"e":"In the above clinical scenario, the hyperviscosity symptoms and rouleaux formation on peripherals smear point to a diagnosis of Waldenstrom's macroglobulinemia. It does not cause lytic bone lesions.\n\nWaldenstrom's macroglöbulinemia is a B-cell malignancy affecting lymphoplasmacytoid and plasma cells. It is characterized by high levels of circulating IgM antibodies.\n\nClinical features include:\n\n• Hyperviscosity symptoms\n\n• Recurrent infections\n\n• Epistaxis\n\n• Visual disturbances\n\n• Peripheral neuropathy - due to myelin-associated glycoprotein causing demyelination.\n\n• Lymphadenopathy\n\n• Hepatosplenomegaly\n\n• Ophthalmoscopic examination may reveal vascular segmentation and dilation of the retinal veins.\n\nInvestigations:\n\n• Bone marrow shows >10% infiltration with lymphoplasmacytic cells\n\n• Serum M component is >30 g/L (3 g/dL). The size of the IgM paraprotein results in little renal excretion, and only 20% of patients excrete light chains.\n\nThe image below shows rouleaux formation.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2b305361d6694819b457aacda727e288.JPEG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/182885ae4286489199a573141b993691x800x600.JPEG"],"t":"Plasma Cell Disorders","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"A 19-year-old boy presented with progressive shortness of breath, and easy fatiguability for 3 years. His older brother died of cardiac arrest during a football match a few years ago. Further evaluation revealed hypertrophic cardiomyopathy. Biopsy and staining of subcutaneous fat showed the following. What mutation is likely in this patient?","o":["Leukocyte chemotactic factor","Transthyretin","Light chain","Beta 2 microglobulin"],"a":1,"e":"The biopsy showing amyloid deposits stained by congo red, along with family history points to a diagnosis of familial amyloidosis (AF). It is most commonly due to the mutation of the transthyretin gene (TTR).\n\nAmyloidosis is a group of protein misfolding disorders characterized by the accumulation of abnormal proteins called amyloid fibrils in various organs.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/d64e4ec50565419d8343de7cce3db658.JPEG"],"t":"Plasma Cell Disorders","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"A 60-year-old man presented with progressive weight loss, generalized edema. On evaluation, he had nephrotic range proteinuria, macroglossia, and raccoon eyes. What is the most probable diagnosis?","o":["Solitary plasmacytoma","Waldenstrom's macroglobulinemia","Amyloidosis","IgA nephropathy"],"a":2,"e":"The patient has non-specific symptoms, which along with macroglossia and raccoon eyes are suggestive of amyloidosis.\n\nClinical features include:\n\n• Fatigue\n\n• Weight loss\n\n• Nephrotic range proteinuria due to renal amyloidosis and is associated with hypoalbuminemia\n\n• Cutaneous ecchymoses around the eyes are called the 'raccoon-eye' sign. Due to easy bruising as a result of amyloid deposits or deficiency of clotting factor X.","t":"Plasma Cell Disorders","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is the most commonly involved organ in amyloidosis?","o":["Heart","Kidneys","Liver","Spleen"],"a":1,"e":"The kidneys are the most frequently involved organ in amyloidosis and are affected in 70–80% of patients.\n\nThe heart is the second most commonly affected organ (50–60% of patients).\n\nThe spleen is frequently involved, and there may be functional hyposplenism in the absence of significant splenomegaly.\n\nLiver involvement causes cholestasis.\n\nMacroglossia, a pathognomonic sign of AL amyloidosis is seen in only 10% of patients.","t":"Plasma Cell Disorders","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"What is the leading cause of death in patients with primary amyloidosis?","o":["Renal failure","Cardiac disease","Liver failure","Recurrent infections"],"a":1,"e":"Cardiac disease is the leading cause of death from primary amyloidosis.","t":"Plasma Cell Disorders","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following patients are at risk of developing chronic myeloid leukemia?","o":["A middle-aged man whose mother died of chronic myeloid leukemia","An oil-refinery worker with occupational benzene exposure for 20 years","A woman who underwent radiotherapy for carcinoma cervix","An elderly man who received chemotherapy for Hodgkin's lymphoma"],"a":2,"e":"Exposure to a high dose of ionizing radiation is the only known risk factor for developing chronic myeloid leukemia (CML). This may be in the form of radiation therapy for malignancy or other conditions and nuclear accidents. It is dose-dependent and low-dose radiation exposure is not a risk factor.\n\nThere are no familial associations, infections, or toxins associated with the development of CML.","t":"Chronic Myeloid Leukemia And Chronic Lymphoid Leukemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is true about the Philadelphia chromosome?","o":["Unbalanced translocation between short arm of chromosomes 9 and 22","Unbalanced translocation between long arm of chromosomes 9 and 22","Balanced translocation between short arm of chromosomes 9 and 22","Balanced translocation between long arm of chromosomes 9 and 22"],"a":3,"e":"Philadelphia chromosome is a balanced translocation between the long arm of chromosomes 9 and 22. It is present in the majority of patients with chronic myeloid leukemia (CML).\n\nIn a balanced translocation, a part of one chromosome is shifted to another chromosome and vice versa. In this case, the ABL gene present in chromosome 9 is translocated next to the BCR gene (breakpoint cluster region) on chromosome 22. It leads to the formation of shortened chromosome 22, which has been named the Philadelphia chromosome after the place of discovery.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1b2167583b68450cabe080e164d59580x1280x2541.JPEG"],"t":"Chronic Myeloid Leukemia And Chronic Lymphoid Leukemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"What is the most common sign in patients with chronic myeloid leukemia?","o":["Lymphadenopathy","Hepatomegaly","Splenomegaly","Weight loss"],"a":2,"e":"Splenomegaly is the most common physical examination finding in patients with chronic myeloid leukemia (CML).\n\nThe majority of the patients with CML are asymptomatic. They are often incidentally diagnosed during routine blood tests when they have elevated white blood cells count. In later stages, the following clinical features develop:\n\n• Anemia - easy fatigability, exercise intolerance\n\n• Increased number of WBCs and platelets - hyperviscosity, leading to MI and stroke\n\n• Increased cell metabolism - night sweats, weight loss, and hyperuricemia (gout)\n\n• Splenomegaly - abdominal pain, early satiety\n\n• Hepatomegaly, lymphadenopathy, skin or subcutaneous lesions","t":"Chronic Myeloid Leukemia And Chronic Lymphoid Leukemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Leukocyte alkaline phosphatase score is decreased in patients with all of the following except:","o":["Chronic myeloid leukemia","Paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria","Sickle cell anemia","Polycythemia vera"],"a":3,"e":"Leukocyte alkaline phosphatase (LAP) score is increased in polycythemia vera and leukemoid reactions.\n\nThe biochemical abnormalities in CML include:\n\n• Low LAP score\n\n• High levels of:\n\n• Vitamin B12\n\n• Uric acid\n\n• Lactic dehydrogenase\n\n• Lysozyme\n\nNote: Leukemoid reaction refers to extreme elevations in WBC > 50,000/mm3 due to reactive causes outside the marrow. This includes severe infection, trauma, Down's syndrome.","t":"Chronic Myeloid Leukemia And Chronic Lymphoid Leukemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"In patients with chronic myeloid leukemia, which of the following are predominantly elevated in the peripheral smear?","o":["Segmented neutrophils","Metamyelocytes","Myelocytes","Band forms"],"a":2,"e":"Myelocytes will be predominantly elevated in the peripheral smear of the patients with chronic myeloid leukemia (CML).\n\nThe clinical hallmark of CML is the uncontrolled production of mature and maturing granulocytes. All of the stages of development of granulocytes can be noted on the peripheral smear in varying numbers:\n\n• Myeloblast - large cells with a round or oval nucleus, cytoplasm is dark and has no granules\n\n• Myelocyte - nucleus is oval and is eccentrically placed.\n\n• Metamyelocyte - nucleus is like a kidney bean-shaped\n\n• Band forms - nucleus is in the shape of C or U.\n\n• Segmented neutrophils- nucleus has 2-5 lobes.\n\nAll the cells except the myeloblast have a granular cytoplasm. Due to the varied appearance of the cells, it is called garden party appearance or college girl's appearance.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/c80a1c5a9d214659b17142f665e788e1x1280x1117.JPEG"],"t":"Chronic Myeloid Leukemia And Chronic Lymphoid Leukemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"A 55-year-old man is found to have the following findings on a routine CBC. A subsequent peripheral smear is performed as shown below. A bone marrow aspiration study is planned for this patient. All of the following are likely to be seen on bone marrow except:\n\nHemoglobin - 8.4 g/dL\n\nWBC count - 1,65,000/cu.mm\n\nPlatelet count - 6,10,000/cu.mm","o":["Hypercellular bone marrow","Myeloid: Erythroid ratio of 15:1","Marrow blasts &gt;20 %","Sea-blue histiocytes"],"a":2,"e":"The image shows a left-shift with a predominance of segmented neutrophils, myelocytes, metamyelocytes that is suggestive of chronic myeloid leukemia (CML). Marrow blasts are usually < 5% in the majority of CML patients.\n\nIn CML, the following bone marrow findings are noted:\n\n• Markedly hypercellularity - due to increased granulocytic precursors\n\n• Myeloid: erythroid ratio (M/E ratio) is significantly increased\n\n• Megakaryocytes - normal or increased\n\n• Erythroid precursors - usually decreased\n\n• Sea blue histiocytes - macrophages with abundant wrinkled, green-blue cytoplasm.\n\n• Bone marrow fibrosis may occur due to the deposition of reticulin.\n\n• Pseudo-Gaucher cells - macrophages with wrinkled tissue paper appearance, as shown below. Due to the increased turnover of the cells, there is an increased accumulation of glucocerebroside within the macrophages.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/953efa8ae72047358cfdecce702a4cc6.JPEG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/af8aa583bc3c45d494bb2e06e5d68374x1280x1117.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/724372ce4d5d41cab064bd8a0bf9cd75x436x356.JPEG"],"t":"Chronic Myeloid Leukemia And Chronic Lymphoid Leukemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"A 48-year-old man presents to the clinic with easy fatiguability and weight loss. After a detailed workup, he is diagnosed with chronic myeloid leukemia. What is the initial treatment of choice?","o":["Allogeneic stem cell transplantation","Monoclonal antibody against C5","Tyrosine kinase inhibitor","CD20 inhibitor"],"a":2,"e":"The initial treatment of choice in patients with chronic myeloid leukemia (CML) is tyrosine kinase inhibitors. These include imatinib, nilotinib, dasatinib, bosutinib, and ponatinib.\n\nAllogeneic stem cell transplant is considered in patients who are negative for Philadelphia chromosome, or who are non-responsive to the tyrosine kinase inhibitors.","t":"Chronic Myeloid Leukemia And Chronic Lymphoid Leukemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"A 61-year-old with CML failed to respond to imatinib therapy. Further evaluation revealed a T315I mutation. What drug should he be started on?","o":["Bosutinib","Nilotinib","Omacetaxine","Ponatinib"],"a":3,"e":"Ponatinib is effective in patients with CML with T315I mutations. It is a third-generation tyrosine kinase inhibitor.\n\nThe T315I mutation in BCR-ABL clones is specifically resistant to therapy with imatinib, dasatinib, nilotinib, and bosutinib but is sensitive to the third-generation agent ponatinib. It is also used in the treatment of accelerated and blast phases.","t":"Chronic Myeloid Leukemia And Chronic Lymphoid Leukemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following can be used to assess the prognosis in patients with chronic myeloid leukemia?","o":["Rai and Binet staging system","Ann Arbor staging system","Hasford scoring system","Matutes scoring system"],"a":2,"e":"Hasford scoring system can be used to assess prognosis in patients with chronic myeloid leukemia (CML).\n\nThe prognosis of CML can be assessed by:\n\n• Sokal score which is based on:\n\n• Age\n\n• Spleen size\n\n• Platelet count\n\n• Percentage of blast cells in the blood\n\n• Hasford score - Similar to Sokal score, but additionally uses the percentage of eosinophils and basophils in the blood\n\nOption A: Rai and Binet staging system is used for staging chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL).\n\nOption B: Ann Arbor staging system is used in Hodgkin and non-Hodgkin lymphomas.\n\nOption D: Matutes scoring system is used for the diagnosis of CLL by flow cytometry.","t":"Chronic Myeloid Leukemia And Chronic Lymphoid Leukemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"All of the following patients with chronic myeloid leukemia are in the accelerated phase except:\n\nPeripheral blast cell count\nPeripheral basophil count\n\nPatient 1\n 20%\n 15%\n\nPatient 2\n 10%\n 25%\n\nPatient 3\n 13%\n 21%\n\nPatient 4\n 15%\n 14%","o":["Patient 1","Patient 2","Patient 3","Patient 4"],"a":0,"e":"A blast count ≥ 20% in peripheral blood is indicative of a blast crisis, not the accelerated phase of chronic myeloid leukemia (CML).\n\nCML can be divided into three phases:\n\n• Chronic stable phase - Blasts < 10% in peripheral blood and bone marrow\n\n• Accelerated phase - any of the following:\n\n• Blasts comprising 10-19% of white blood cells (WBCs) in peripheral blood and/or bone marrow\n\n• Peripheral blood basophils ≥20%\n\n• Thrombocytopenia or thrombocytosis not responsive to therapy\n\n• Additional cytogenetic abnormalities in Philadelphia chromosome cells\n\n• Blast crisis:\n\n• Blasts ≥ 20% of peripheral blood WBCs or bone marrow\n\n• Extramedullary blast proliferation\n\nMajority of the patients present in the chronic stable phase. Without effective treatment, CML in the chronic phase will eventually move into an accelerated phase at first and then into the blast phase.","t":"Chronic Myeloid Leukemia And Chronic Lymphoid Leukemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"What is the classical immunophenotypic picture seen in patients with chronic lymphocytic leukemia?","o":["CD19+, CD20+, CD21+, CD22+, CD 23+, CD5 +","CD19+, CD20+, CD21+, CD22+, CD23-, CD5+","CD79b+, CD19+, CD10+, CD20+, CD22+","CD79b+, CD19+, CD20+, CD22+, CD5-, CD10-"],"a":0,"e":"The classical immunophenotypic feature seen in patients with chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL) is CD19+, CD20+, CD21+,CD22+, CD23+, CD5+. It is significant for coexpression of the B cell marker CD19 with the T cell marker CD5.\n\nMantle cell lymphoma has the same immunophenotype but with absent CD23.\n\nB cell precursors\nDerived lymphomas/leukemia\nImmunophenotype\n\nPro-B cell\nPro-B cell ALL\nCD34, CD19, CD79a\n\nPre-B cell\nPre-B cell ALL\nCD19, CD79a, CD10, Tdt, Cytoplasmic Ig\n\nMature B cell\nCLL/SLL\n\nCD19, CD79a,CD 20, CD21, CD22, surface IgM, CD 23, CD5\n\nMantle zone\nMantle zone lymphoma\nCD19, CD79a,CD 20, CD21, CD22, surface IgM, CD5\n\nGerminal center/Follicular center\n\nFollicular lymphoma\n\nDiffuse large B cell lymphoma\n\nBurkitt lymphoma\n\nCD 79b, CD 19, CD 20, CD22, CD10, CD 23, Bcl-2\n\nCD10, Cd23, Bcl-6\n\nCD10, CD23,Bcl-6\n\nMemory B cells\nExtranodal marginal zone lymphoma\nCD19, CD79a,CD 20, surface Ig","t":"Chronic Myeloid Leukemia And Chronic Lymphoid Leukemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"A 68-year-old man with fever is found to have a WBC count of 1,50,000/cu.mm. His peripheral smear study is given below. Which of the following is true about this condition?","o":["It arises from the myeloid lineage","Radiation exposure is a risk factor","It arises from the T lymphocytes","Patient may have autoimmune hemolytic anemia"],"a":3,"e":"The smear shows small round lymphocytes with scant cytoplasm and the presence of a smudge cell. The most probable diagnosis is chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL). Patients with CLL may have autoimmune hemolytic anemia (AIHA).\n\nChronic lymphocytic leukemia/ small lymphocytic lymphoma refers to the monoclonal proliferation of mature B lymphocytes. It is more common in men than women. Increasing age and family history are important risk factors. CLL is one of the few leukemias not linked to radiation exposure.\n\nThe majority of the patients are asymptomatic and are diagnosed during routine blood tests. Some patients may have painless lymphadenopathy and splenomegaly. Increased B cells may produce autoantibodies leading to autoimmune complications like autoimmune hemolytic anemia and immune thrombocytopenia (ITP).","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b0f88bbb9ba04b488380e527f8a0507e.PNG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/a609f4d7444143fc8177643b337e2d36x1280x1966.JPEG"],"t":"Chronic Myeloid Leukemia And Chronic Lymphoid Leukemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"What is the treatment of choice for a 71-year-old asymptomatic woman diagnosed with chronic lymphocytic leukemia?","o":["Fludarabine only","Fludarabine + Chlorambucil + Rituximab","Chlorambucil + Bendamustine","Expectant management"],"a":3,"e":"Expectant management is the treatment of choice for asymptomatic patients with chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL). This is because there is no clear survival benefit with chemotherapy in asymptomatic patients.\n\nChemotherapy and monoclonal antibodies are used in the presence of the following features:\n\n• Progressive marrow failure - worsening anemia, thrombocytopenia\n\n• Progressive splenomegaly\n\n• Progressive lymphadenopathy\n\n• Presence of constitutional symptoms like weight loss, fever, night sweats\n\nImportant drugs used in the management of CLL are fludarabine, cyclophosphamide, bendamustine, and rituximab.","t":"Chronic Myeloid Leukemia And Chronic Lymphoid Leukemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is true about Richter syndrome?","o":["Lymphadenopathy is absent","Transformation to an aggressive lymphoma","Decreased serum lactate dehydrogenase","Has a good prognosis"],"a":1,"e":"Richter's syndrome is the transformation of chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL) to aggressive lymphoma, most commonly diffuse large B cell lymphoma (DLBCL). It has a poor prognosis.\n\nPatients develop the following features:\n\n• Fever, and weight loss\n\n• Rapidly enlarging lymph nodes\n\n• Elevated serum lactate dehydrogenase","t":"Chronic Myeloid Leukemia And Chronic Lymphoid Leukemia","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"What is the most common non-Hodgkin lymphoma in India?","o":["Burkitt's lymphoma","Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma","Mantle cell lymphoma","Follicular cell lymphoma"],"a":1,"e":"Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (DLBCL) is the most common form of non-Hodgkin lymphoma (NHL) in adults and children over the age of 15 years, worldwide, and in India.\n\nBurkitt's lymphoma is the most common NHL in children under the age of 15 years.","t":"Lymphomas","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not caused by the Epstein-Barr virus?","o":["Hodgkin’s lymphoma","Primary diffuse large B cell lymphoma of the CNS","Post–organ transplant lymphoma","Primary effusion lymphoma"],"a":3,"e":"Human herpesvirus 8 (HHV-8) is the etiological factor for primary effusion lymphoma, not Epstein-Barr virus.\n\nLymphoid malignancies associated with Epstein-Barr virus include:\n\n• Burkitt's lymphoma\n\n• Post-organ transplant lymphoma\n\n• Primary diffuse large B cell lymphoma of the CNS\n\n• Hodgkin's disease\n\n• Extranodal NK/T cell lymphoma, nasal type\n\nPrimary effusion lymphoma is a variant of diffuse large B-cell lymphoma that presents with pleural effusions, usually without apparent tumor mass lesions. It is most common in the setting of immune deficiency disease, especially AIDS.","t":"Lymphomas","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following types of non-Hodgkin's lymphoma and its corresponding grade are correctly matched?","o":["Small lymphocytic lymphoma - High grade","Diffuse large B cell lymphoma - Low grade","Follicular lymphoma - Intermediate grade","Burkitt's lymphoma - High grade"],"a":3,"e":"Burkitt lymphoma is a fast-growing, high-grade type of non-Hodgkin lymphoma (NHL).\n\nNHL was earlier classified on the basis of its morphology and clinical behaviour. However, this classification is not currently used. Based on the clinical behavior, NHL is classified as:\n\n• Low grade - CLL/SLL (small lymphocytic lymphoma), follicular lymphoma\n\n• Intermediate grade - mantle cell and marginal cell lymphoma\n\n• High grade - diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (DLBCL), Burkitt's lymphoma\n\nThe most aggressive NHL is DLBCL, whereas the most rapidly progressive tumor is Burkitt's lymphoma. Most pediatric NHLs are high-grade and aggressive.","t":"Lymphomas","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"What is the most common extranodal site of involvement in patients with non-Hodgkin's lymphoma?","o":["Central nervous system","Stomach","Small intestine","Skin"],"a":1,"e":"The gastrointestinal tract is the most common extranodal site in patients with non-Hodgkin's lymphoma (NHL). The stomach is most commonly involved, followed by the small intestine.\n\nThe skin is the second-most common site. Mycosis fungoides is a mature T-cell (CD4+) NHL with skin involvement with eventual visceral and nodal spread. When malignant T-cells in the skin enter circulation, it is called Sezary syndrome. It presents as erythroderma (diffuse erythema and scaling). Histologically, there are Sézary-Lutzner cells (with cerebriform nuclei) and Pautrier microabscesses in epidermis.\n\nCNS is the most common extranodal site in patients with HIV infection. Primary CNS lymphoma (histologically, DLBCL) is one of the AIDS-defining malignancies.","t":"Lymphomas","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is used to assess the prognosis in patients with non-Hodgkin's lymphoma?","o":["Ann Arbor staging","Rai and Binet score","International Prognostic Index","Hasford scoring"],"a":2,"e":"The prognosis of patients with non-Hodgkin’s lymphoma is best assessed using the international Prognostic Index (IPI). It consists of 5 clinical risk factors for poor prognosis:\n\n• Age >60 years\n\n• Elevated levels of serum lactate dehydrogenase (LDH)\n\n• Performance status ≥ 2 (ECOG) or ≤70 (Karnofsky)\n\n• Ann Arbor stage III or IV\n\n• >1 site of extranodal involvement\n\nOption A: Ann Arbor staging is used for anatomic staging to develop a treatment plan for Hodgkin's and non-Hodgkin's lymphoma.\n\nOption B: Rai and Binet score is used for staging of CLL.\n\nOption D: Hasford scoring is used to assess the prognosis of CML.","t":"Lymphomas","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"A 63-year-old man presented with a 2-month history of fatigue, fever, and night sweats. On examination, generalized painless lymphadenopathy and splenomegaly were noted. Investigations revealed the following. What is the likely diagnosis?\n\nHaemoglobin - 10g/dl\n\nWBC count - 1,50,000/cu.mm\n\nPlatelet count - 2,50,000/cu.mm\n\nImmunohistochemical studies - CD19+, CD20+, CD21+, C22+, CD23(-), CD 5+.","o":["Small lymphocytic lymphoma","Follicular cell lymphoma","Burkitt's lymphoma","Mantle cell lymphoma"],"a":3,"e":"The clinical scenario is suggestive of mantle cell lymphoma. It is diagnosed by its characteristic immunophenotype - CD19+, CD20+, CD21+, CD22+, CD23(-), CD 5+.\n\nComparison between mantle cell lymphoma and chronic lymphocytic leukemia/small lymphocytic lymphoma (CLL/SLL):\n\nMantle cell lymphoma\nCLL/ SLL\n\nCD5\nPositive\nPositive\n\nCD23\nNegative\nPositive\n\nCyclin D1\nPositive\nNegative\n\nFMC7\nPositive\nNegative\n\nCD10, BCL6\nNegative\nNegative","t":"Lymphomas","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is incorrectly matched?\n\nLymphomas\n\nCytogenetics\n\n1.\nMantle cell lymphoma\n t (11;14)\n\n2.\nFollicular lymphoma\n t (14;18)\n\n3.\nDiffuse large B cell lymphoma\n t (14;18)\n\n4.\nBurkitt's lymphoma\n t (18;14)","o":["Option 1","Option 2","Option 3","Option 4"],"a":3,"e":"Translocations seen in Burkitt's lymphoma are t (8;14), t (2;8) and t (8;22).","t":"Lymphomas","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"A 59-year-old came to the OPD after noticing a swelling on her neck. On examination, painless lymphadenopathy was noted in the cervical, axillary and inguinal regions with no hepatosplenomegaly. CBC was normal. Lymph node biopsy showed effacement of nodal architecture with centrocytes and centroblasts. Which of the following immunophenotypic panels will confirm this diagnosis?","o":["CD19, CD 23, CD5","CD19, CD22, CD5","CD19, CD79a, CD10, TdT","CD 19, CD79a, CD 20, CD10, CD 23, Bcl-2"],"a":3,"e":"This clinical scenario and the presence of centrocytes and centroblasts on biopsy are suggestive of follicular lymphoma. Immunophenotyping will be positive for CD19, CD79a, CD20, CD10, CD23, Bcl-2.\n\nFollicular lymphoma presents at a median age of 59 years with no sex predilection. The patients most commonly develop painless generalized lymphadenopathy. Systemic symptoms are absent. A lymph node biopsy is usually adequate for the diagnosis of follicular lymphoma. Characteristic features following lymph node biopsy include:\n\n• The normal nodal architecture is effaced by nodular expansions of tumour cells\n\n• Centrocytes - small lymphocytes with cleaved nuclei\n\n• Centroblasts - larger cells with vesicular chromatin and prominent nucleoli","t":"Lymphomas","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements is false about diffuse large B-cell lymphoma?","o":["It is a high-grade aggressive lymphoma","It is commonly associated with BCL-6 chromosomal rearrangements","It is associated with CNS lymphoma and intravascular lymphoma","It does not present with extranodal involvement"],"a":3,"e":"Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (DLBCL) commonly (>50%) presents with extranodal involvement at diagnosis, with the most common sites being the GI tract and bone marrow.\n\nDLBCL is the most common non-Hodgkin's lymphoma. It is a high-grade aggressive tumour. It is associated with BCL-6 chromosomal rearrangements and t(14;18). It is commonly associated with CNS lymphoma, pleural effusion lymphoma, and intravascular lymphoma. It is considered an AIDS-defining malignancy.\n\nAIDS-defining malignancies include:\n\n• Kaposi sarcoma\n\n• Primary central nervous system lymphoma (DLBCL)\n\n• Non-Hodgkin's lymphoma\n\n• Invasive cervical cancer","t":"Lymphomas","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"A physician is in Africa, working with the Doctors Without Borders group. He encounters a 7-year-old boy with the following finding. His parents say that he had a jaw swelling for 2 months that failed to resolve with antibiotics. A biopsy of the swelling reveals a starry sky appearance. Which of the following statements is true about this patient's condition?","o":["It is associated with n-myc dysregulation","Tumor cells show CD21 expression","Expectant management is the treatment of choice","It is associated with HIV infection"],"a":1,"e":"This clinical scenario is suggestive of the endemic type of Burkitt's lymphoma. In this condition, the tumor cells are CD21+.\n\nBurkitt's lymphoma is an aggressive condition and is the most common NHL in children. This condition is endemic in African countries and is associated with Epstein-Barr virus (EBV) infection. It is associated with c-myc dysregulation, especially in cases in bone marrow involvement.\n\nPatients require aggressive management with high-dose chemotherapy.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6f1af4ec25574db1a3d7ec2b1c0c8609.JPEG"],"t":"Lymphomas","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"The lymph node biopsy of a patient with lymphoma is given below. It shows two different cell populations labeled as A and B. Which of the following statements is false?","o":["Cells labelled A will test positive for CD14, CD40, CD11b &amp; CD64","The fraction of cells with positive Ki67 staining is 100%","Cells labelled B will test positive for CD19, CD10, BCL-6 &amp; CD43","Cells labelled B represent the stars in starry sky appearance"],"a":3,"e":"The given image shows the characteristic starry sky appearance of Burkitt's lymphoma. The reactive macrophages (labelled as A) represent the stars in starry sky appearance. On the other hand, the homogenous tumour cells (labelled as B) represent the sky.\n\nThe neoplastic cells are homogeneous, medium-sized B cells with a high growth fraction. These cells are positive for CD19, CD10, BCL-6 & CD43.\n\nReactive macrophages are scattered throughout the tumour. Their pale cytoplasm in a background of blue-staining tumour cells gives the tumor a so-called starry sky appearance. Reactive leukocytes and macrophages are not stained by special stains. They are positive for CD14, CD40, CD11b & CD64.\n\nThe growth fraction of these lymphomas is very high, with frequent mitotic figures. The fraction of Ki-67+ (MIB-1+) stained cells is nearly 100%.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/3f454d919629410a81c95eb4a556444f.PNG"],"t":"Lymphomas","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Patients with which of the following conditions are at risk of developing salivary gland MALToma?","o":["Helicobacter pylori gastritis","Hashimoto’s thyroiditis","Sjogren's syndrome","Celiac disease"],"a":2,"e":"Sjogren's syndrome is implicated in salivary gland MALToma.\n\nMALT lymphomas are commonly associated with an underlying autoimmune or inflammatory process such as:\n\n• Sjogren’s syndrome - salivary gland\n\n• Hashimoto’s thyroiditis - thyroid\n\n• Helicobacter pylori gastritis - gastric (most common)\n\n• Celiac disease - small intestine\n\nThe most common translocation associated with MALToma is (11,18). It has a good prognosis.","t":"Lymphomas","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not a T-cell lymphoma?","o":["Hairy cell leukemia","Mycosis fungoides","Anaplastic large cell lymphoma","Null cell lymphoma"],"a":0,"e":"Hairy cell leukemia arises from a late, activated memory B cell exhibiting post-germinal center features.\n\nMycosis fungoides and anaplastic large cell lymphoma (also called null cell lymphoma) are both T-cell lymphomas.","t":"Lymphomas","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"The peripheral smear of a patient who presented with generalized lymphadenopathy and hepatosplenomegaly is given below. What is the likely diagnosis?","o":["Mantle cell lymphoma","Peripheral T cell lymphoma","Anaplastic large cell lymphoma","Adult T cell leukemia/lymphoma"],"a":3,"e":"The given peripheral smear shows tumor cells with multilobulated nuclei called cloverleaf or flower cells. This is characteristic of adult T-Cell leukemia/lymphoma.\n\nThis condition occurs in adults infected by human T-cell leukemia retrovirus type 1 (HTLV-1). It is a rapidly progressive disease that is fatal within months to one year following diagnosis. It is characterized by the following clinical features:\n\n• Skin lesions\n\n• Generalized lymphadenopathy\n\n• Hepatosplenomegaly\n\n• Peripheral blood lymphocytosis,\n\n• Hypercalcemia","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/2f8ed0363b2c4c9597af09deb0bae4d2.JPEG"],"t":"Lymphomas","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"A 55-year-old man presented with fatigue and dragging sensation in the left upper abdomen. Physical examination revealed massive splenomegaly and no lymphadenopathy. CBC revealed pancytopenia. Bone marrow aspirate yielded a dry tap. Immunohistochemical staining of the tumor cells tested positive for CD19, CD22, CD11c, CD25, CD103 and CD123. What is the likely diagnosis?","o":["Myelofibrosis","Splenic marginal zone lymphoma","Multiple myeloma","Hairy cell leukemia"],"a":3,"e":"This clinical scenario is suggestive of hairy cell leukemia (HCL).\n\nIn this condition, most patients present with symptoms related to cytopenias or splenomegaly. On bone marrow aspiration, a dry tap is obtained due to HCL-induced marrow fibrosis and the interlocking cell borders of hairy cells (distinct from myelofibrosis).\n\nTumor cells share certain characteristics of memory B cells. CD11c, CD25, CD103, CD123 is the characteristic immunophenotype diagnostic of this condition. Pan-B markers such as CD19, CD20, and CD22 may also be positive.\n\nNote: Splenic marginal zone lymphoma involves both white and red pulp of the spleen, whereas hairy cell leukemia is limited to the red pulp. All non-Hodgkin's lymphomas involve white pulp of spleen except:\n\n• Hairy cell leukemia\n\n• Hepatosplenic lymphoma","t":"Lymphomas","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following best distinguishes hairy cell leukemia from other B cell malignancies?","o":["Hairy cells on a Wright-stained peripheral blood smear","Annexin A1 on bone marrow specimens","Positive tartarate-resistant acid phosphatase stain","Intracytoplasmic inclusion bodies called ribosome-lamellar complexes"],"a":1,"e":"Annexin A1 on immunophenotyping of bone marrow specimens is the best method to differentiate hairy cell leukemia from other B-cell malignancies. It has 100% sensitivity and specificity.\n\nHairy cells on Wright-stained peripheral blood smear are an important diagnostic finding. Typical hairy cells are mononuclear cells with abundant pale blue-grey agranular cytoplasm with elongated (hairy) projections. Intracytoplasmic inclusion bodies called ribosome-lamellar complexes can also be seen in 50% of cases. However, these can be seen in other B-cell disorders also.\n\nTartrate-resistant acid phosphatase (TRAP) stain is an important tool and is positive in 95% of cases of hairy cell leukemia. However, it is not pathognomonic.\n\nThe following pattern of bone marrow involvement is near to pathognomonic for hairy cell leukemia (>99%):\n\n• Diffuse infiltration of tumor cells with honeycomb appearance.\n\n• Individual cells have a fried egg appearance such that the nucleus of each hairy cell is surrounded by a halo of cytoplasm, as shown below.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/61cb819485da4260b0f793724266e5a1x800x533.JPEG"],"t":"Lymphomas","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Patients with hairy-cell leukemia are at a higher risk of infection with which of the following?","o":["Parvovirus B19","Mycoplasma pneumoniae","Mycobacterium avium intracellulare","Cryptococcus neoformans"],"a":2,"e":"Hairy cell leukemia is associated with infections of Mycobacterium avium-intracellulare. This is possibly related to frequent unexplained monocytopenia. Among them, infection with Mycobacterium kansasii is common.","t":"Lymphomas","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"A 45-year-old man presented with progressive fatigue and weight loss for 3 months. CBC showed pancytopenia. On peripheral smear, the following cells were seen, which also showed positive staining with TRAP. What is the drug of choice for this patient?","o":["Vemurafenib","Pentostatin","Interferon α","Cladribine"],"a":3,"e":"The given image shows mononuclear cells with abundant pale blue-grey agranular cytoplasm with elongated (hairy) projections, which are TRAP-positive. This clinical scenario is suggestive of hairy cell leukemia. The drug of choice is cladribine.\n\nHairy cell leukemia is responsive to chemotherapy with interferon α, pentostatin, and cladribine. However, cladribine is the preferred drug of choice due to complete remission in the majority of patients, and higher rates of long-term disease-free survival. It is administered daily for 7 days by continuous intravenous infusion.\n\nChemotherapy-refractory patients have responded to vemurafenib, a BRAF inhibitor. Splenectomy is often associated with prolonged remission.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/a428add7718246468f1e6d3e15155bfd.JPEG"],"t":"Lymphomas","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"What subtype of Hodgkin's lymphoma is most commonly seen in India?","o":["Nodular sclerosing","Mixed cellularity classical","Lymphocyte-rich classical","Lymphocyte depleted classical"],"a":1,"e":"The most common subtype of Hodgkin's lymphoma in India is the mixed cellularity classical Hodgkin's lymphoma. In contrast, nodular sclerosis is most commonly seen in western countries.\n\nThe most common subtype of Hodgkin's lymphoma in:\n\n• Developed/ western countries - nodular sclerosing\n\n• Children - mixed cellularity\n\n• Females - nodular sclerosis (but Hodgkin's lymphoma is more common in males overall)\n\n• HIV-associated - mixed cellularity\n\n• EBV-associated - lymphocyte-depleted","t":"Lymphomas","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not a classical form of Hodgkin lymphoma?","o":["Nodular sclerosing","Lymphocyte depleted","Nodular lymphocyte predominant","Lymphocyte-rich"],"a":2,"e":"Nodular lymphocyte-predominant Hodgkin's lymphoma (HL) is a distinct clinicopathological entity from classical Hodgkin lymphoma as per the WHO classification.\n\nClassical HL\nNodular lymphocyte-predominant HL\n\nSubtypes\n\n• Nodular sclerosis\n\n• Mixed cellularity\n\n• Lymphocyte-rich\n\n• Lymphocyte depleted\n\n-\n\n Key difference\nDo not express many of the genes that define normal B cells\nRetain the immunophenotypic features of germinal center B cells\n\n Immunophenotype\n\n CD15+ CD30+\n\nCD20- CD45-\n\nCD20+ CD45+\n\nCD15- CD30-\n\nNeoplastic cell\nReed-Sternberg cell\nPopcorn cell or L&H (lymphocytic & histiocytic) cells\n\n Common Presentation\nMediastinal lymphadenopathy\nPeripheral lymphadenopathy\n\nThe following image shows the Reed-Sternberg cell in classic HL.\n\nThe following image shows the popcorn cell in nodular lymphocyte-predominant HL.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/ac1824ee43024c86bb1b9a93343a906ax731x599.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1ab94888362a4102b1a3718cbda36e3ax1280x930.JPEG"],"t":"Lymphomas","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"What is the most commonly observed clinical presentation in patients with Hodgkin's lymphoma?","o":["Supraclavicular lymphadenopathy","Axillary lymphadenopathy","Hepatosplenomegaly","Mediastinal mass"],"a":0,"e":"Supraclavicular or cervical lymphadenopathy is the most common clinical presentation of Hodgkin's lymphoma.\n\nThe clinical features of Hodgkin's lymphoma are as follows:\n\n• Asymptomatic lymphadenopathy (non-tender with a rubbery consistency) is the most common presentation.\n\n• Most common - cervical or supraclavicular\n\n• Second-most common presentation - mediastinal mass\n\n• Hepatosplenomegaly in some cases\n\n• Pel-Ebstein fever:\n\n• Refers to recurrent febrile and afebrile intervals, each phase lasting for days to weeks\n\n• More common in older patients with mixed-cellularity Hodgkin’s lymphoma in an abdominal site\n\n• Alcohol-induced pain is a rare, unusual presentation","t":"Lymphomas","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"A 29-year-old man with a 3-week history of fever is found to have multiple rubbery, enlarged lymph nodes. A biopsy reveals abnormal cells with bilobed nuclei that are positive for CD15 and CD30. Which of the following is associated with this man's condition?","o":["Syndrome of inappropriate ADH secretion","Lambert-Eaton syndrome","Subacute cerebellar degeneration","Superficial migratory thrombophlebitis"],"a":2,"e":"This clinical scenario is suggestive of Hodgkin's lymphoma. The paraneoplastic syndrome associated with this condition is subacute cerebellar degeneration.\n\nOther paraneoplastic syndromes associated with Hodgkin's lymphoma include:\n\n• Minimal change disease (nephrotic syndrome)\n\n• Vanishing bile duct syndrome\n\n• Cholangitis\n\n• Alcohol-induced pain - pain typically occurs within a few minutes after the ingestion of even a small amount of alcohol and is localized to areas involved by HL\n\nOption A: Syndrome of inappropriate ADH secretion is associated with small cell lung carcinoma of the lungs.\n\nOption B: Lambert-Eaton syndrome is associated with small cell lung carcinoma of the lungs.\n\nOption D: Migratory superficial thrombophlebitis (Trousseau syndrome) is associated with adenocarcinoma of the pancreas and lungs.","t":"Lymphomas","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"The lymph node biopsy of a 49-year-old woman with painless lymphadenopathy revealed the following finding. What is the likely diagnosis?","o":["Lymphocyte-depleted Hodgkin's lymphoma","Lymphocyte-rich Hodgkin's lymphoma","Nodular sclerosis Hodgkin's lymphoma","Mixed cellularity Hodgkin's lymphoma"],"a":2,"e":"The given image shows a lacunar Reed-Sternberg (RS) cell. It is identified by a folded or multilobed nucleus surrounded by an open space, which is an artifact created by disruption of the cytoplasm during tissue sectioning. It is seen in nodular sclerosis classical Hodgkin's disease (CHL).\n\nThe histological subtypes of classical Hodgkin's disease are as follows:\n\nSubtype\nHistopathology\nClinical correlation\n\nNodular sclerosis\n\nLacunar RS cell\n\nNecrosis with inflammatory infiltrate (eosinophils, macrophages, plasma cells)\n\nFibrous bands dividing cellular areas into nodules\n\n• Mediastinal lymphadenopathy and superior vena cava syndrome\n\n• Excellent prognosis\n\nMixed cellularity\nRS cells with rich inflammatory infiltrate\n\n• Biphasic: Children and elderly\n\n• B symptoms\n\n• Associated with EBV (and HIV)\n\n• Very good prognosis\n\nLymphocyte-rich\nBackground infiltrate rich\nin T-cells\n-\n\nLymphocyte- depleted\nReticular variant, paucity of background reactive cells\n\n• Associated with HIV (and EBV)\n\n• Poor prognosis","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/eebf12b263484254b267443f6f49fa2a.PNG"],"t":"Lymphomas","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following statements is true about Hodgkin's lymphoma?","o":["Reed-Sternberg cell is specific for Hodgkin's disease","Classical Hodgkin's lymphoma is associated with CD20 positive cells","Central nervous system is the most common site of extranodal disease","PET-CT is the most reliable technique for staging Hodgkin's lymphoma"],"a":3,"e":"PET-CT is the most reliable technique for staging Hodgkin's lymphoma.\n\nReed-Sternberg cells on histology, point to a clinical diagnosis of Hodgkin's lymphoma. However, they are not specific for Hodgkin's lymphoma. Reed-Sternberg cells can also be seen in the following conditions:\n\n• Infectious mononucleosis\n\n• Solid tissue cancers\n\n• Large cell non-Hodgkin's lymphomas (most common in immunoblastic type)\n\nClassical Hodgkin's lymphoma is associated with positive test results for CD15 and CD30. Nodular lymphocyte-predominant Hodgkin's lymphoma is associated with CD20 positive cells.\n\nThe central nervous system (CNS) is an uncommon site of extranodal disease in Hodgkin's lymphoma. Lung, liver, bone, and bone marrow are the most common extranodal sites for the disease.","t":"Lymphomas","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"A 68-year-old man presented with fever, night sweats, and weight loss for 2 months. On examination, non-tender, rubbery lymph nodes were palpable in the supraclavicular and inguinal regions on both sides. Biopsy showed the presence of Reed-Sternberg cells. How would you stage this patient according to the Ann Arbor Staging?","o":["Stage IIA","Stage IIB","Stage IIIA","Stage IIIB"],"a":3,"e":"In this scenario of Hodgkin's lymphoma, the patient presented with B symptoms (fever, night sweats, and weight loss) and shows lymph node involvement on both sides of the diaphragm are involved (supraclavicular and inguinal). According to Ann Arbor Staging, this patient is categorized as stage IIIB.\n\nAnn Arbor Staging of Hodgkin's lymphoma\n\nStage I\n\nInvolvement of a single lymph node (I) or a single extra-lymphatic organ or site (IE)\n\nStage II\n\nInvolvement of 2 or more lymph node regions on the same side of the diaphragm (II) or localized involvement of an extra-lymphatic organ or site and 1 or more lymph node regions on the same side of the diaphragm (IIE)\n\nStage III\n\nInvolvement of lymph node regions on both sides of the diaphragm (III), which may be accompanied by:\n\n• Involvement of the spleen (IIIS) [or]\n\n• Localized involvement of an extra-lymphatic organ or site (IIIE) [or]\n\n• Both (IIISE)\n\nStage IV\n\nDiffuse or disseminated involvement of 1 or more extra-lymphatic organs or tissues\n\nWith or without associated lymph node involvement\n\nAdditionally classified as:\n\nA - Asymptomatic\n\nB - Any of the following symptoms:\n\n• Absence or incidence of fever >38°C (100.4°F) for 3 consecutive days\n\n• Drenching night sweats\n\n• Unexplained loss of >10% of body weight in the 6 months","t":"Lymphomas","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"A 49-year-old man is diagnosed with stage IIIB Hodgkin's lymphoma. Lab investigations reveal a hemoglobin of 10.8 g/dl, WBC count of 14,000/ µL, and an absolute lymphocyte count of 450/ µL. Which of the following are poor prognostic factors in this patient?\n\n1. Age\n\n2. Gender\n\n3. Hemoglobin\n\n4. WBC count\n\n5. Absolute lymphocyte count","o":["2, 3, 5","1, 2, 5","3, 4, 5","1, 2, 4"],"a":1,"e":"In this patient with advanced-stage Hodgkin's lymphoma, his age, gender, and absolute lymphocyte count all indicate poor prognosis.\n\nUnfavourable or poor prognostic factors for advanced-stage HL (stages III and IV) include:\n\n• Age > 45 years\n\n• Male gender\n\n• Haemoglobin (Hb) < 10.5 g/dL\n\n• Serum albumin <4 g/dL\n\n• White blood cell (WBC) count ≥15,000/ µL\n\n• Absolute lymphocyte count (ALC) < 600/ µL\n\nUnfavourable or poor prognostic factors for early Hodgkin's lymphoma (stages I and II) include:\n\n• Large mediastinal lymphadenopathy\n\n• Involvement of 4 or more sites\n\n• Age >45\n\n• ESR > 30 mm/hr\n\n• B symptoms - unexplained fever, night sweats or unexplained weight loss\n\nThe selection of initial treatment for Hodgkin's lymphoma is usually based on presenting stage and prognostic factors.","t":"Lymphomas","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"A 48-year-old man with fever, night sweats, and weight loss is diagnosed with stage IIB Hodgkin's lymphoma. What is the treatment of choice for this patient?","o":["R- CHOP regimen","ABVD regimen","ABVD regimen + Field radiation","R-CHOP regimen + Field radiation"],"a":2,"e":"The patient's age (> 45 years) and presence of B symptoms indicate an unfavourable prognosis. The treatment of choice in early-stage Hodgkin's lymphoma (stage I and II) with an unfavourable prognosis is ABVD regimen along with field radiation. ABVD is a combination of doxorubicin (adriamycin), bleomycin, vinblastine, and dacarbazine.\n\nThe treatment of Hodgkin's lymphoma is as follows:\n\n• Early Hodgkin's lymphoma (favourable and unfavourable) - ABVD regimen + limited field radiation\n\n• Advanced Hodgkin's lymphoma - extended course of chemotherapy with ABVD regimen.\n\nThe side effects of this treatment include:\n\n• Doxorubicin - dilated cardiomyopathy\n\n• Bleomycin- interstitial lung disease\n\n• Vinblastine - SIADH, Peripheral neuropathy\n\n• Radiation - hypothyroidism, Lhermitte's syndrome, infertility\n\nNote: R-CHOP regimen is used in the treatment of Non-Hodgkin's lymphoma. It is composed of rituximab + cyclophosphamide, doxorubicin, vincristine (oncovin) and prednisone.","t":"Lymphomas","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following lymphoid neoplasms is incorrectly matched with its corresponding etiology?","o":["H. pylori infection - Extra nodal marginal cell lymphoma","Gluten-sensitive enteropathy - Intestinal T-cell lymphomas","Breast implants - Anaplastic large cell lymphoma","Human immunodeficiency virus - Lymphoplasmacytic lymphoma"],"a":3,"e":"Hepatitis C virus (HCV) is the etiological agent for lymphoplasmacytic lymphoma/ Waldenstrom's macroglobulinemia.\n\nHuman immunodeficiency virus (HIV) is associated with diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (DLBCL) and Burkitt's lymphoma.\n\nOther acquired lymphoid neoplasms include:\n\n• Gastric MALToma/extranodal marginal cell lymphoma - induced by Helicobacter pylori infection\n\n• Intestinal T-cell lymphomas/enteropathy-associated T cell lymphoma - induced by gluten-sensitive enteropathy\n\n• Anaplastic large cell lymphoma in scar tissue - can occur following breast implants","t":"Lymphomas","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"An isolated prolongation of prothrombin time was seen in a patient with chronic liver disease. What does this suggest?","o":["Factor VII deficiency","Factor XII deficiency","Factor XI deficiency","Factor VIII deficiency"],"a":0,"e":"Isolated PT prolongation suggests factor VII deficiency. It can be an inherited or acquired condition (chronic liver disease).\n\n Test\nUsed to assess\nActivated on\nexposure to\nNormal\nvalue\n\nPT\n\nExtrinsic\npathway (factor\nVII)\n\nCommon\npathway (factors\nI, II, V, X)\n\nTissue factor\n12-16s\n\naPTT\n\n Intrinsic\npathway (factors\nVIII, IX, XI, XII)\n\nCommon\npathway (factors\nI, II, V, X)\n\nActivators\n(silica,\nkaolin)\n26-34s\n\nTT\nFibrinogen levels\nThrombin\n15-19s","t":"Coagulation Disorders","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Prolonged aPTT and normal PT are seen in patients with all of the following except?","o":["Hemophilia B","Lupus anticoagulant","Von Willebrand disease","Disseminated intravascular coagulation"],"a":3,"e":"Disseminated intravascular coagulation is associated with prolonged PT and prolonged aPTT.\n\nConditions with prolonged aPTT can be classified on the basis of the bleeding symptoms:\n\n• No bleeding symptoms: deficiency of Factor XII, prekallikrein, HMW kininogen, lupus anticoagulant\n\n• Mild bleeding symptoms: Factor XI deficiency\n\n• Severe bleeding symptoms: Hemophilia, von Willebrand disease\n\nLupus anticoagulant is an antiphospholipid antibody that interferes with aPTT test and causes its prolongation. It is corrected by adding excess phospholipid.\n\nThe clinical manifestation of lupus anticoagulant is thrombosis. Confirmatory tests are dilute Russell's viper venom test and kaolin clotting time.","t":"Coagulation Disorders","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Laboratory investigations done in a chronic alcoholic patient are given below. Which of the following clotting factors is most likely to be normal in this patient?\n\nAlbumin - 3 g/dL\n\nPT - 20 s\n\naPTT - 40 s","o":["Factor II","Factor VII","Factor VIII","Factor XI"],"a":2,"e":"All clotting factors are likely to be abnormal except factor VIII.\n\nThe liver is the site of production of the following factors:\n\n• Fibrinogen (factor I)\n\n• Thrombin (factor II)\n\n• Factor V\n\n• Factor VII (shortest half-life, hence PT affected first)\n\n• Factor IX\n\n• Factor X\n\n• Factor XI\n\nFactor VIII is produced in the endothelial cells and factor XIII A-subunit is produced in the bone marrow.","t":"Coagulation Disorders","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is the most common disorder of coagulation?","o":["Hemophilia A","Hemophilia B","von Willebrand disease","Hemorrhagic diathesis of liver disease"],"a":3,"e":"Hemorrhagic diathesis of liver disease is the most common disorder of coagulation among the given options. It is the increased tendency of spontaneous bleeding associated with fulminant or end-stage liver disease.\n\nAcquired deficiencies of plasma coagulation factors are more frequent than congenital disorders. The most common acquired disorders include:\n\n• Hemorrhagic diathesis of liver disease\n\n• Disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC)\n\n• Vitamin K deficiency.\n\nHemophilia A is the most common inherited disorder of coagulation. Hemophilia A is more common than Hemophilia B.\n\nvon Willebrand disease is the most common bleeding disorder.","t":"Coagulation Disorders","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following conditions are matched correctly with their mode of inheritance?","o":["Factor VII deficiency - X linked recessive","Factor VIII deficiency - autosomal recessive","Factor IX deficiency - X linked recessive","Factor V deficiency - autosomal dominant"],"a":2,"e":"Factor IX deficiency (hemophilia B) is inherited as an X-linked recessive trait.\n\nHemophilia is an X-linked recessive hemorrhagic disease due to mutations in:\n\n• Factor VIII gene (hemophilia A or classic hemophilia) -80% more common than hemophilia B\n\n• Factor IX gene (hemophilia B)\n\nClinically, hemophilia A and hemophilia B are indistinguishable.\n\nAll clotting factor deficiencies except hemophilia are inherited as autosomal recessive.\n\nInherited coagulation disorders treatment:\n\nBlood components\nComposition\nIndications\n\nFactor VIII concentrates\nFactor VIII\nHemophilia A\n\nFactor IX concentrates\nFactor IX\nHemophilia B\n\nCryoprecipitate\n\nFibrinogen\n\nFactor XIII\n\nFactor VIII\n\nVWF\n\nFibrinogen deficiency, factor XIII deficiency, von Willebrand disease\n\nFresh Frozen Plasma (FFP)\n\nCoagulation factors\n\nProtein C\n\nProtein S\n\nAntithrombin\n\nDeficiency of prothrombin, factor V, factor VII, factor X, factor XI","t":"Coagulation Disorders","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"A 5-year-old boy was brought with the following finding. His mother says that he had banged his leg against a chair yesterday. He has had similar episodes in the past but they were never this severe. What lab values will you likely find in this boy?","o":["Normal bleeding time, prolonged PT, prolonged aPTT","Normal bleeding time, prolonged PT, normal aPTT","Prolonged bleeding time, normal PT, prolonged aPTT","Normal bleeding time, normal PT, prolonged aPTT"],"a":3,"e":"In the above clinical scenario, recurrent hemarthrosis in a male child is suggestive of hemophilia. Normal bleeding time, normal PT, prolonged aPTT is seen in hemophilia A and B.\n\nHemophilia A has decreased factor VIII activity and hemophilia B has decreased factor IX activity.\n\nNote: Prolonged bleeding time, normal PT, prolonged aPTT is seen in von Willebrand disease.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6359e01906c647fc90ca09a466f82172.PNG"],"t":"Coagulation Disorders","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is false about acquired hemophilia A?","o":["Autoantibodies are formed against factor VIII","Bethesda assay confirms the diagnosis","It presents in childhood or early adulthood","It may be associated with lymphoproliferative malignancies"],"a":2,"e":"Acquired hemophilia A occurs predominantly in older adults (median age- 60 years).\n\nIt occasionally occurs in pregnant or postpartum women.\n\nIt is an acquired immune-mediated disease characterized by autoantibodies against clotting factor VIII. It is associated with an underlying disease in 50% of patients. They include:\n\n• Malignancies - lymphomas, prostate cancer\n\n• Dermatologic diseases - pemphigoid","t":"Coagulation Disorders","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"In a patient with hemophilia A, how much can infusion of one unit of factor VIII/kg increase the plasma levels of factor VIII?","o":["30%","20%","10%","2%"],"a":3,"e":"One unit of factor VIII/kg body weight increases the plasma levels of factor VIII levels by 2%. Therefore, for a rise in 1 % in the plasma, half unit/ kg will be required, that is 0.5 units/kg.\n\nIt is used in the treatment of hemophilia. One unit of FVIII is the amount of FVIII in 1 mL of plasma.\n\nThe dose required for replacement is calculated as :\n\n• Factor VIII dose = (Target factor VIII levels - baseline factor VIII levels) x bodyweight x 0.5 units/ kg\n\n• Factor IX (IU) = (Target FIX levels – FIX baseline levels) x bodyweight (kg) x 1 unit/kg\n\nCryoprecipitate is used when factor concentrates are not available. Each bag of cryoprecipitate contains 80 IU of Factor VIII.\n\nBlood components\nComposition\nIndications\n\nFactor VIII concentrates\nFactor VIII\nHemophilia A\n\nFactor IX concentrates\nFactor IX\nHemophilia B\n\nCryoprecipitate\n\nFibrinogen\n\nFactor XIII\n\nFactor VIII\n\nVWF\n\nFibrinogen deficiency, Factor XIII deficiency, von Willebrand disease\n\nFresh frozen plasma (FFP)\n\nCoagulation factors\n\nProtein C\n\nProtein S\n\nAntithrombin\n\nDeficiency of prothrombin, factor V, factor VII, factor X, factor XI","t":"Coagulation Disorders","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"What effect does desmopressin have when given to patients with coagulation disorders?","o":["Increases factor VIII","Increases factor IX","Increases factor XI","Increases factor VII"],"a":0,"e":"Desmopressin causes a transient rise in factor VIII and von Willebrand factor (VWF) hence it is given in mild and moderate hemophilia A.\n\nIt does not improve factor VIII levels in severe hemophilia A patients because there are no stores to release. Repeated dosing leads to tachyphylaxis.\n\nIt does not increase factor IX levels.","t":"Coagulation Disorders","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"A teenager with hemophilia A presented with multiple spontaneous bleeds. He has been receiving prophylactic factor VIII infusion on a regular basis. Investigations revealed low factor VIII levels and abnormal plasma mixing study. Which of the following can be used to confirm the diagnosis?","o":["1,3 beta D glucan assay","Platelet activation assay","Serotonin release assay","Bethesda assay"],"a":3,"e":"Bethesda assay is the best test for the confirmation of factor VIII inhibitors.\n\nThe formation of inhibitor/ alloantibodies to FVIII or FIX is the major complication of hemophilia treatment. When this happens, patients do not respond to factor replacement at therapeutic doses.\n\naPTT measurement is done on a mixed sample. It is used to determine the presence of factor VIII inhibitors. aPTT is estimated using a 1:1 mix of patient's plasma and normal plasma. It is interpreted as:\n\n• In hemophilia, aPTT is corrected with a 1:1 mix.\n\n• In patients with inhibitors, aPTT with 1:1 mix is abnormally prolonged (inhibitor neutralizes the normal factors).\n\nBethesda assay is based on the same principle but is superior because it helps to quantify the type and specificity of the inhibitor.\n\nOption A: 1,3 beta D glucan assay is designed to diagnose invasive fungal infections (IFIs).\n\nOptions B and C: It is used to diagnose heparin-induced thrombocytopenia.","t":"Coagulation Disorders","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following are risk factors that can cause disseminated intravascular coagulation in patients?\n\n1. Gram-positive sepsis\n\n2. Chronic pancreatitis\n\n3. Adenocarcinoma of prostate\n\n4. Snakebite\n\n5. Abruptio placentae","o":["1,4","1,2,4,5","2,5","1,3,4,5"],"a":3,"e":"Acute pancreatitis can cause disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC) not chronic pancreatitis.\n\nCommon causes of DIC:\n\n• Sepsis- gram-positive and gram-negative bacteria, viral, mycotic, parasitic, rickettsial\n\n• Acute pancreatitis\n\n• Trauma, burns\n\n• Malignancies- acute promyelocytic leukemia, adenocarcinoma of pancreas and prostate\n\n• Obstetric- abruptio placentae, amniotic fluid embolism, and septic abortion\n\n• Snakebite","t":"Coagulation Disorders","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"A 33-year-old woman with a twin pregnancy was admitted for normal delivery. After medical induction of labor, she started to bleed profusely from her rectum, vagina, and IV sites. She became hypotensive, tachycardic, and pulses were feeble. Which of the following laboratory findings will you see in her case?","o":["Option 2","Option 3","Option 1","Option 4"],"a":3,"e":"In the above clinical scenario, the patient seems to have disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC) due to amniotic fluid embolism following medical induction of labor.\n\nDisseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC) is a condition characterized by widespread intravascular fibrin formation. The central mechanism is the uncontrolled generation of thrombin by exposure of blood to pathologic levels of tissue factor.\n\nLab abnormalities in DIC:\n\n• Low platelet count\n\n• Low fibrinogen\n\n• Prolonged PT and aPTT\n\n• Increased D-dimer\n\n• Increased fibrin-degradation products\n\n• Decreased protein C\n\n• Decreased antithrombin III\n\n• Increased thrombin-antithrombin complex","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/78f95b6a8a4540cea635d79c411de126.JPEG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/264aef213a1d4e52a22f049b24fc9f8dx1280x1953.JPEG"],"t":"Coagulation Disorders","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"The most sensitive test for disseminated intravascular coagulation is _____.","o":["Fibrin degradation products level","PTT and thrombin time","D-dimer assay","Platelet count and fibrinogen levels"],"a":0,"e":"The most sensitive test for disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC) is FDP (fibrinogen degradation products) level.\n\nDiagnostic test\nDiagnostic efficiency\nSensitivity\nSpecificity\n\nFDP and D-dimer combination\n95%\n91%\n94%\n\nFDP\n87%\n100%\n67%\n\nPT/PTT and FDP combination\n86%\n91%\n71%\n\nD-dimer\n80%\n91%\n68%\n\nPT, PTT, thrombin time, platelet count, fibrinogen, and the presence of schistocytes\n57%, 57%, 70%, 67%, 65%, and 51%, respectively\n low\n low","t":"Coagulation Disorders","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"A 3-year-old child is brought to the emergency with petechiae, bruising, and abdominal distension. Examination revealed an abdominal mass. Investigations revealed schistocytes and thrombocytopenia. What is the most probable cause of the mass?","o":["Neuroblastoma","Hepatoblastoma","Wilm's tumor","Hepatic hemangioma"],"a":3,"e":"This patient presents with petechiae, and abdominal distension with schistocytes and thrombocytopenia. This in child most likely has Kasabach-Merritt syndrome (secondary DIC). His abdominal mass is likely to be a hepatic hemangioma.\n\nKasabach-Merritt syndrome is a life-threatening complication that occurs in patients with giant hemangiomas. It is most commonly seen in infants.\n\nClinical features:\n\n• Enlarged abdomen\n\n• Hepatomegaly or jaundice\n\n• Petechiae, bruising, and frank bleeding\n\n• Painful lesions\n\n• Anemia\n\nIt is characterized by profound thrombocytopenia and consumptive coagulopathy leading to a DIC-like picture.\n\nOther options:\n\nOption A: Neuroblastomas are tumors of the sympathetic ganglia and adrenal medulla that are derived from primordial neural crest cells. It is the most common extracranial solid tumor of childhood and the most frequently diagnosed tumor in infancy. Brain tumors are the most common solid tumors in childhood. They present as a flank mass that crosses the midline.\n\nOption B: Hepatoblastoma is the most common childhood liver tumor. Histologically it is composed of small polygonal embryonal cells forming trabeculae, acini, or papillary structures in the developing liver.\n\nOption C: Wilms tumor is the most common primary malignant renal tumor in children and is the second most common malignant abdominal tumor in children. The clinical presentation usually involves an asymptomatic unilateral abdominal mass, hypertension, abdominal pain, and gross painless hematuria. Constitutional symptoms of fever, anorexia, and weight loss can also be present at the time of diagnosis.","t":"Coagulation Disorders","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Patients with protein C deficiency are at an increased risk of developing all of the following except:","o":["Deep vein thrombosis","Purpura fulminans","Warfarin-induced skin necrosis","Spontaneous bleeding"],"a":3,"e":"Spontaneous bleeding is not seen in protein C deficiency. It is characterised by an increased tendency for thrombus formation.\n\nProtein C deficiency is a rare genetic disorder. It predisposes to the following thrombotic complications:\n\n• Deep vein thrombosis (DVT):\n\n• Adults with a mild form of the disease at higher risk of developing venous thromboembolism.\n\n• Purpura fulminans:\n\n• Severe form of DIC\n\n• Thrombosis of extensive areas of the skin\n\n• Affects predominantly young children following viral or bacterial infection.\n\n• Warfarin-induced skin necrosis:\n\n• Rare complication seen in patients with congenital or acquired deficiencies of protein C or S.\n\n• Thrombosis of the skin leads to well-demarcated erythematous lesions with central necrosis on the thighs, buttocks, breasts, or toes(shown below).","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6ad8b3fbe45c4161a1887693df8a08aex416x501.PNG"],"t":"Coagulation Disorders","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"A patient is on prophylactic unfractionated heparin for deep vein thrombosis. Which of the following tests can be used to monitor its anticoagulant effect?","o":["Prothrombin time","Anti-Factor Xa level","PT and Anti-Factor Xa level","No monitoring is required"],"a":1,"e":"The anticoagulant effect of heparin can be monitored using anti-factor Xa level and activated partial thromboplastin time (aPTT). aPTT is prolonged as heparin inhibits intrinsic pathway factors.\n\nProthrombin time and INR are used to monitor the anticoagulant effect of warfarin (inhibits vitamin K dependent factor II, VII, IX, X. Factor VII has the shortest half-life).\n\nNo monitoring is required with the use of low molecular weight heparin (LMWH) and fondaparinux.","t":"Coagulation Disorders","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is false about low molecular weight heparin?","o":["Higher risk of of bleeding than heparin","No laboratory monitoring is required","Lower risk of heparin induced thrombocytopenia","Protamine sulphate is only partially effective against low molecular weight heparin"],"a":0,"e":"Low molecular weight heparin (LMWH) has a lower risk of bleeding than heparin.\n\nIn contrast to heparin which requires monitoring by aPTT and anti-factorXa, laboratory monitoring is not required for LMWH. It is also associated with lower risk of heparin induced thrombocytopenia.\n\nExamples of LMWH:\n\n• Dalteparin\n\n• Enoxaparin\n\n• Tinzaparin\n\n• Nadroparin\n\nProtamine sulphate is the only clinically approved antidote for unfractionated heparin. Protamine sulphate is only partially effective against low molecular weight heparin.","t":"Coagulation Disorders","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is a direct thrombin inhibitor?","o":["Apixaban","Abciximab","Argatroban","Edoxaban"],"a":2,"e":"Argatroban is a parenteral direct thrombin inhibitor.\n\nApixaban and edoxaban are a novel oral anticoagulant (NOAC) that inhibits factor Xa. Abciximab is an antiplatelet drug that inhibits Gp IIb-IIIa receptor.","t":"Coagulation Disorders","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"A patient presented with bleeding gums and skin discoloration. She was given heparin for DVT prophylaxis 5 days ago. Investigations showed thrombocytopenia. Which of the following is the most specific test to diagnose this condition?","o":["4T scoring","Platelet activation assay","Heparin-PF-4 complex antibody assay","Serotonin release assay"],"a":3,"e":"Serotonin release assay is the most specific test for the diagnosis of heparin-induced thrombocytopenia (HIT).\n\nHIT is commonly diagnosed clinically by means of 4T scoring (Thrombocytopenia, Timing of platelet count fall, Thrombosis, Other causes for Thrombocytopenia).\n\nSerotonin release assay is performed by quantifying serotonin release.\n\nPlatelets loaded with labeled serotonin are exposed to patient serum in the absence or presence of varying concentrations of heparin. If the patient serum contains the HIT antibody, heparin addition induces platelet activation and serotonin release.\n\nEnzyme-linked assays such as platelet activation assay and heparin-PF-4 complex antibody assay are sensitive but not specific for HIT.","t":"Coagulation Disorders","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"A 69-year-old male patient with chronic kidney disease presented with bleeding gums and skin rash. He had undergone dialysis 1 week ago. Further investigations revealed heparin-induced thrombocytopenia to be the cause. Which of the following drugs can be given to him?","o":["Lepirudin","Argatroban","Bivalirudin","Fondaparinux"],"a":1,"e":"In the above clinical scenario, the patient requires direct thrombin inhibitors for the treatment of heparin-induced thrombocytopenia. Argatroban is safe in renal disease.\n\nThe parenteral direct thrombin inhibitors are:\n\n• Argatroban - hepatic clearance, approved for the treatment of HIT\n\n• Lepirudin - renal clearance, contraindicated in renal disease\n\n• Bivalirudin- renal clearance, contraindicated in renal disease, shortest half-life\n\nFondaparinux is a factor Xa inhibitor. It is contraindicated in renal disease because it has renal clearance.","t":"Coagulation Disorders","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"A patient on warfarin develops severe gastrointestinal bleeding. What is the treatment of choice?","o":["Vitamin K slow IV infusion","Fresh frozen plasma","Vit K and FFP","Discontinuation of warfarin only"],"a":2,"e":"Patients on warfarin who present with severe bleeding are treated with both IV infusion of vitamin K and fresh frozen plasma regardless of the INR.\n\nWarfarin is an oral anticoagulant that blocks the enzyme vitamin K epoxide reductase. This inhibits factors II, VII, IX, and X, proteins C and S. This can cause an initial procoagulant state.\n\nSince the anticoagulant action of warfarin takes 2-3 days, patients require concomitant treatment (bridging therapy) with a rapidly acting parenteral anticoagulant (heparin, LMWH or fondaparinux) for at least 5 days.\n\nWarfarin has a narrow therapeutic window, hence all patients on warfarin should have their INR determined every 3–4 weeks. The most common side effect of warfarin is bleeding.\n\nSevere or life-threatening bleed includes:\n\n• Hematuria\n\n• Gastrointestinal bleeding\n\n• Intracranial hemorrhage\n\n• Intramuscular or intra-abdominal bleed\n\nManagement of supratherapeutic INR:\n\nINR\nBleeding\nManagement\n\n3.5-10\nNo\nHold warfarin until INR is therapeutic\n\n>10\nNo\nOral vitamin K 2.5-5 mg\n\nAny\nSerious or life-threatening\n10 mg vitamin K slow IV infusion + fresh frozen plasma/prothrombin complex concentrate","t":"Coagulation Disorders","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not a direct oral anticoagulant ?","o":["Dabigatran","Danaparoid","Rivoraxaban","Edoxaban"],"a":1,"e":"Danaparoid is not a direct oral anticoagulant (DOAC). It is a heparinoid administered via IV or SC routes.\n\nDOACs are alternatives to warfarin, easier to administer with fixed doses, and no requirement for monitoring.\n\nDirect oral anticoagulants (DOAC)\n\nThrombin inhibitors (dabigatran)\nFactor Xa inhibitors\n\n• No CYP2C9 polymorphisms\n\n• A standard dose of 150 mg BD\n\n• No PT/INR monitoring\n\n• Dialyzable\n\n• Mild GI bleeding\n\n• Rivaroxaban\n\n• Apixaban\n\n• Edoxaban\n\n• Betrixaban\n\nAll DOACs are contraindicated in patients with metallic/prosthetic heart valves.","t":"Coagulation Disorders","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"In patients with which of the following is dabigatran approved for use?","o":["Elective hip arthroplasty requiring thromboprophylaxis","Total knee arthroplasty requiring thromboprophylaxis","Metallic heart valve requiring stroke prophylaxis","Non-valvular atrial fibrillation requiring stroke prophylaxis"],"a":3,"e":"Stroke prophylaxis in non-valvular atrial fibrillation is the only indication for which all DOACs are approved. All DOACs are contraindicated in patients with metallic/prosthetic heart valves.\n\nNovel oral anticoagulants:\n\nThrombin inhibitors\nFactor Xa inhibitors\n\n• Dabigatran (oral)\n\n• Argatroban\n\n• Lepirudin\n\n• Desirudin\n\n• Bivalirudin\n\n• Rivaroxaban\n\n• Apixaban\n\n• Edoxaban\n\n• Betrixaban\n\nDabigatran is a direct oral anticoagulant (DOAC). DOACs are alternatives to warfarin, easier to administer with fixed doses, and no requirement for monitoring.\n\nOnly rivaroxaban and apixaban are licensed for use as thromboprophylaxis after elective hip or knee arthroplasty.","t":"Coagulation Disorders","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is true about hemoglobin?","o":["Hemoglobin tetramer is highly soluble.","Individual globin chains are soluble.","Each heme moiety can bind to two O2 molecules.","Each heme moiety consists of Iron in the ferric state."],"a":0,"e":"The hemoglobin tetramer is highly soluble in the plasma. However, individual globin chains are insoluble. Unpaired globin precipitates can form inclusions that damage the cell and trigger apoptosis.\n\nEach hemoglobin molecule consists of a tetramer of globin polypeptide chains. This includes a pair of α-like chains and a pair of β-like chains. Each globin chain enfolds a single heme moiety. Heme consists of a protoporphyrin IX ring complexed with a single iron atom in the ferrous state (Fe2+). Each heme moiety can bind one O2 molecule.\n\nThe common forms of hemoglobin include:\n\n• HbA (α2β2): Major adult hemoglobin\n\n• HbF (α2γ2): Fetal hemoglobin\n\n• HbA2 (α2δ2): Minor adult hemoglobin","t":"Haemoglobinopathies","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"A patient with alpha thalassemia is susceptible to which of the following infections?","o":["Plasmodium vivax","Plasmodium falciparum","Plasmodium ovale","Plasmodium malaria"],"a":0,"e":"Patients with α-thalassemia are susceptible to infection with Plasmodium vivax.\n\nThalassemia provides natural protection against infection with the more lethal Plasmodium falciparum. There is no conclusive evidence on the relationship between alpha thalassemia and P. malariae or P. ovale infection.\n\nConditions protective against Plasmodium falciparum include:\n\n1. Sickle cell trait\n\n2. Hemoglobin C\n\n3. Hemoglobin SC\n\n4. Hemoglobin E\n\n5. Hereditary persistence of fetal hemoglobin (Hb F)\n\n6. Alpha and beta-thalassemia\n\n7. Band 3 mutation\n\n8. G6PD deficiency","t":"Haemoglobinopathies","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"The peripheral smear of a patient is given below. This abnormality is due to which of the following point mutations?","o":["5th glutamic acid is replaced by valine","5th valine is replaced by glutamic acid","6th glutamic acid is replaced by valine","6th valine is replaced by glutamic acid"],"a":2,"e":"The given image shows sickled RBCs, suggestive of sickle cell anemia. It occurs due to the replacement of the 6th glutamic acid by valine in the beta-globin gene on chromosome 11.\n\nThis mutation is due to a single nucleotide substitution (from GAG to GTG). The resulting amino acid substitution causes hydrophobic sickling of RBCs.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/8070c1a8e6c841ad83d283ba9b176142.JPEG"],"t":"Haemoglobinopathies","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"What is the most common cause of osteomyelitis in patients with sickle cell disease?","o":["Staphylococcus aureus","Streptococcus","Staphylococcus epidermidis","Salmonella"],"a":3,"e":"Salmonella is the most common organism causing osteomyelitis in sickle cell disease.\n\nStaphylococcus aureus is the most common organism causing osteomyelitis in the general population.","t":"Haemoglobinopathies","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"What will be seen on performing hemoglobin electrophoresis in a patient with suspected sickle cell anemia?","o":["HbS and HbA","HbS and HbF","HbF and HbA","HbA2 and HbF"],"a":1,"e":"HbS and HbF are detected on electrophoresis in patients with sickle cell anemia. HbA and HbA2 are typically absent.\n\nCondition\n\nClinical abnormalities\n\nHemoglobin level (g/dl)\n\nHemoglobin\nElectrophoresis\n\nSickle cell trait\n\nRare painless hematuria may be asymptomatic\n\nNormal\n\nHb S/A:40/60\n\nSickle cell anemia\n\nVasoocclusive crises with infarction of spleen, brain, marrow, kidney, lung; aseptic necrosis of bone; gallstones; priapism; ankle ulcers\n\n7–10\n\nHbS/HbA: 100/0\n\nHbF: 2–25%","t":"Haemoglobinopathies","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following does not increase HbF levels?","o":["Hydroxyurea","5-Azacytidine","Decitabine","Gemcitabine"],"a":3,"e":"Gemcitabine is an anti-metabolite and has no effect on HbF levels.\n\nThe following drugs increase HbF levels and hence can be used in the management of sickle cell anemia:\n\n• Hydroxyurea - mainstay of therapy for patients with severe symptoms\n\n• 5-azacytidine - associated with acute toxicity and carcinogenesis\n\n• 5-deoxyazacytidine or decitabine - alternative with less toxicity","t":"Haemoglobinopathies","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following abnormal hemoglobins would be present in patients with congenital methemoglobinemia?","o":["HbE","HbC","Hb Yakima","Hb M Iwata"],"a":3,"e":"Hb M Iwata causes congenital methemoglobinemia.\n\nMethemoglobin is generated by oxidation of the heme iron moieties to the ferric state, causing a characteristic bluish-brown muddy color resembling cyanosis. Methemoglobin has such high oxygen affinity that virtually no oxygen is delivered. Levels >50–60% are often fatal.\n\nCongenital methemoglobinemia arises from globin mutations that stabilize iron in the ferric state (HbM Iwata) or mutations that impair the enzymes that reduce methemoglobin to hemoglobin (methemoglobin reductase, NADP diaphorase).\n\nAcquired methemoglobinemia is caused by toxins that oxidize heme iron such as:\n\n• Chloroquine\n\n• Dapsone\n\n• Sulfonamides\n\n• Nitroglycerin\n\nThe following image shows chocolate-brown discoloration of blood in methemoglobinemia.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/931ecc82e4b14ec885b2a3fb57ccdd9dx358x297.JPEG"],"t":"Haemoglobinopathies","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following would you not expect in a patient with sickle cell anemia?","o":["Reticulocytosis","Howell jolly bodies","Granulocytosis","Thrombocytopenia"],"a":3,"e":"Thrombocytosis is seen in sickle cell anemia, not thrombocytopenia. Laboratory findings of sickle cell anemia include:\n\n• Peripheral blood smear:\n\n• Irreversibly sickled cells (5–50% of red cells)\n\n• Reticulocytosis (10–25%)\n\n• Nucleated red blood cells\n\n• Howell–Jolly bodies and target cells (due to hyposplenism)\n\n• Granulocytosis - WBC count of 12,000–15,000/mcL\n\n• Reactive thrombocytosis\n\n• High indirect bilirubin levels","t":"Haemoglobinopathies","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"What is the 'A blood antigen' composed of?","o":["Galactose attached to H substance","Glucose attached to H substance","N-acetyl galactosamine attached to H substance","N-acetyl glucosamine attached to H substance"],"a":2,"e":"The blood group 'antigen A' is composed of N-acetyl galactosamine attached to the H substance.\n\nH substance is formed by the addition of fucose to the glycolipid or glycoprotein backbone. It is present in all blood groups, even the O group.\n\nA antigen = N-acetyl galactosamine + H substance\n\nB antigen = galactose + H substance\n\nHowever, in case of the Bombay phenotype, RBCs lack H antigen. Hence, these individuals have anti-A, anti-B, and anti-H antibodies. The only compatible blood donor is another person with the Bombay blood group.","t":"Blood Bank & Transfusion Medicine","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Which chromosome contains the genes that determine the A and B blood groups?","o":["9","7","5","1"],"a":0,"e":"The genes that determine the A and B blood groups are located on chromosome 9. The phenotypes are expressed in the Mendelian codominant manner.\n\nRh genes determining Rh blood grouping are present on chromosome 1.","t":"Blood Bank & Transfusion Medicine","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Individuals with a lack of which of the following blood group antigens have immunity against Plasmodium vivax?","o":["Kell","Lewis","Kidd","Duffy"],"a":3,"e":"Duffy antigens serve as receptors for Plasmodium vivax. Hence, the absence of the Duffy antigen is protective against Plasmodium vivax.\n\nHemoglobinopathies that are protective against Plasmodium falciparum include:\n\n• Sickle cell trait\n\n• Hemoglobin C\n\n• Hemoglobin SC\n\n• Hemoglobin E\n\n• Hereditary persistence of fetal hemoglobin (Hb F)\n\n• Alpha and beta-thalassemia","t":"Blood Bank & Transfusion Medicine","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Donated blood is screened for all of the following infections except:","o":["Kala azar","Hepatitis C","Syphilis","Malaria"],"a":0,"e":"Donated blood is not routinely screened for kala-azar. It is screened for the following infections in the blood bank:\n\n• Human immunodeficiency virus\n\n• Hepatitis B\n\n• Hepatitis C\n\n• Malaria\n\n• Syphilis","t":"Blood Bank & Transfusion Medicine","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"As the medical officer at a blood bank, you are supervising the separation of blood components of donated blood. The preservative used for the packed red cells is SAG-M. Which of the following criteria must be fulfilled in order to safely transfuse this to patients?\n\nStorage temperature\nTo be used within:\n\n1.\n-2° to -6 °C\n 21 days\n\n2.\n+2° to +6 °C\n 42 days\n\n3.\n-2° to -6 °C\n 35 days\n\n4.\n+2° to +6 °C.\n 48 days","o":["1","2","3","4"],"a":1,"e":"The shelf life of packed red cells (pRBCs) in the SAG-M solution is 42 days. The optimum temperature for storage of packed RBCs and whole blood collected in preservatives is +2° to +6° C. This temperature maintains the viability of the cells and prevents bacterial contamination.\n\nBlood component\nStorage temperature\nShelf-life\n\nPRBCs\n+2° to +6 °C\n\nCPD-21 days\n\nCPD-A-35 days\n\nSAG M-42 days\n\nPlatelets\n+20 to +24 °C\n5 days\n\nFresh frozen plasma\n-30 °C or lower\n1 year\n\nCryoprecipitate\n-30 °C or lower\n1 years","t":"Blood Bank & Transfusion Medicine","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Packed red blood cell transfusion is started for a patient in the ward. It must be completed within:","o":["5 hours","2 hours","3 hours","4 hours"],"a":3,"e":"Transfusion of pRBCs (packed red blood cells) or whole blood should be completed within 4 hours after the commencement. Transfusion of platelets, fresh frozen plasma, and cryoprecipitate should be completed within 30 minutes.","t":"Blood Bank & Transfusion Medicine","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Cryoprecipitate is useful for the management of patients with all of the following conditions except:","o":["Hemophilia A","Von Willebrand disease","Hypofibrinogenemia","Christmas disease"],"a":3,"e":"Cryoprecipitate does not contain factor IX. Hence, it is not useful for the management of patients with Christmas disease, which occurs due to deficiency of factor IX.\n\nComponents of cryoprecipitate are:\n\n• Factor VIII\n\n• Factor XIII\n\n• Fibrinogen\n\n• Fibronectin\n\n• Von Willebrand factor\n\nTherefore it can be used in patients with hemophilia A, factor XIII deficiency, hypofibrinogenemia, and von Willebrand disease.","t":"Blood Bank & Transfusion Medicine","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"You are monitoring the falling platelet count of a 31-year-old man with dengue fever. You would recommend prophylactic platelet transfusion when the count drops below what level?","o":["5000 platelets /μL","10000 platelets /μL","15000 platelets /μL","50000 platelets /μL"],"a":1,"e":"The threshold for prophylactic platelet transfusion is 10,000/μL in order to prevent spontaneous bleeding. If the patient is planned for any invasive procedures, the target platelet level is 50,000/μL.\n\nThe shelf life of donor platelets is 5 days. It is stored on a special agitator at 20-24 °C. The half-life of transfused platelets is 3-5 days. One unit of pooled platelets should be transfused within 30 minutes because of the risk of bacterial proliferation.","t":"Blood Bank & Transfusion Medicine","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following blood components does not carry the risk of transmitting cytomegalovirus infection?","o":["Packed red blood cells","Whole blood","Fresh frozen plasma","Random donor platelets"],"a":2,"e":"Fresh frozen plasma (FFP) is an acellular component and generally does not transmit viral infections.\n\nCytomegalovirus (CMV) infects >50% of the general population and is transmitted by infected passenger WBCs in transfused PRBCs or platelet components. Cellular components that are leukocyte-reduced have a decreased risk of transmitting CMV.\n\nInfectious complications of blood transfusion include:\n\n• Viral infections: Hepatitis C virus, hepatitis B virus, HIV, cytomegalovirus\n\n• Bacterial contamination: sudden and fulminant onset of fever, chills, septic shock, and disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC)\n\n• Platelet concentrates stored at room temperature: gram-positive and coagulase-negative staphylococci\n\n• PRBCs: Gram-negative bacteria like Yersinia, Pseudomonas, Serratia, Acinetobacter, and Escherichia species\n\n• Parasites: Malaria, Babesiosis, Chaga's disease\n\n• Others: Creutzfeldt–Jakob disease (CJD)","t":"Blood Bank & Transfusion Medicine","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following reactions occurs most frequently in patients undergoing blood transfusions?","o":["Acute hemolytic transfusion reaction","Febrile non-hemolytic transfusion reaction","Transfusion-related acute lung injury","Anaphylactic reaction"],"a":1,"e":"Febrile non-hemolytic transfusion reaction (FNHTR) is the most frequently occurring transfusion reaction.","t":"Blood Bank & Transfusion Medicine","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Thirty minutes after a blood transfusion is started, a patient develops chills and severe flank pain. Her temperature is 102.4 F, SpO2 is 96%, and her blood pressure is 90/60 mm Hg. She then begins bleeding around her IV catheter site. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Anaphylactic reaction","Acute hemolytic reaction","Febrile non-hemolytic reaction","Anamnestic antibody response"],"a":1,"e":"This clinical scenario is suggestive of acute hemolytic transfusion reaction (AHTR).","t":"Blood Bank & Transfusion Medicine","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"A 40-year-old man is being evaluated in the ward for fatigue and pallor. One week ago, he had undergone a blood transfusion following a road accident. Further evaluation reveals Hb of 8.1 g/dl and positive direct Coomb's test and history of previous blood transfusion. What is the likely mechanism of this condition?","o":["ABO incompatibility reaction","Antibodies against donor leukocytes","Antibodies against donor IgA","Anamnestic antibody response"],"a":3,"e":"This clinical scenario is suggestive of delayed hemolytic transfusion reaction (DHTR). It is due to an anamnestic antibody response. This refers to the rapid production of antibodies in the blood after exposure to an antigen to which the recipient had been previously sensitized.","t":"Blood Bank & Transfusion Medicine","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Which among the following options is the most common cause of death in patients undergoing blood transfusions?","o":["Febrile non-hemolytic transfusion reaction","Acute hemolytic transfusion reaction","Delayed hemolytic transfusion reaction","Transfusion-related acute lung injury"],"a":3,"e":"Transfusion-related acute lung injury (TRALI) is the most common cause of transfusion-related fatalities.\n\nTRALI is due to the presence of anti-HLA class II antibodies in the donor plasma. These antibodies bind recipient leukocytes, which aggregate in the pulmonary vasculature and release mediators that increase capillary permeability. Multiparous women are frequently implicated donors. Recipients with a history of liver disease, smoking, alcohol use are at a higher risk.\n\nIt occurs within 6 hours of transfusion and results in hypoxia and signs of non-cardiogenic pulmonary edema with bilateral interstitial infiltrates on chest X-ray. It is managed with supportive care.\n\nNote: According to the latest edition of Harrison's, transfusion associated circulatory overload (TACO) is the leading cause of death followed by TRALI.","t":"Blood Bank & Transfusion Medicine","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Following a normal vaginal delivery, a 30-year-old woman develops severe postpartum hemorrhage. Over the next 24 hours, she undergoes transfusion of 11 units of pRBCs. She is at risk for all of the following complications except:","o":["Metabolic alkalosis","Hypercalcemia","Hyperkalemia","Hypothermia"],"a":1,"e":"Massive transfusion is defined as transfusion of at least 10 units of red cells in 24 hours or 4 units in 1 hour. Hypocalcemia, not hypercalcemia, is a complication of massive blood transfusion.\n\nBlood is anticoagulated with sodium citrate and citric acid. It binds to calcium and magnesium, leading to low levels of both. Citrate is also metabolized to bicarbonate, causing metabolic alkalosis.","t":"Blood Bank & Transfusion Medicine","c":"Haematology"},
 {"q":"Which of the following case scenarios depict a mode of transmission that carries the highest risk of acquiring HIV?","o":["A 23-year-old drug addict sharing syringes during intravenous drug abuse","A 15-year-old thalassemic adolescent undergoing blood transfusion","A 30-year-old man engaging in unprotected sexual intercourse with multiple partners","A 27-year-old nurse accidentally injuring herself while drawing blood"],"a":1,"e":"Blood transfusion carries the highest risk of acquiring HIV.\n\nSalient features of transmission of HIV:\n\n• The highest risk of acquiring HIV from an infected source is via blood transfusion > needle sharing during drug abuse > receptive anal intercourse > needle-stick injury > vaginal & oral sexual intercourse.\n\n• The most common mode of transmission worldwide is sexual transmission (heterosexual in India) > mother to child > least common modes are blood transfusion & needle-stick injury.\n\n• Male to female transmission > female to male transmission.\n\n• Viral load is highest in blood > semen > vaginal fluid > breastmilk & saliva and minimal to absent in tears, sweat & urine.","t":"Hiv   Aids   Epidemiology And Clinical Features","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"Which statement regarding HIV infection is true?","o":["HIV-2 is more pathogenic than HIV -1","HIV-1 subtype C is the most common form worldwide","HIV-2 is prevalent worldwide","Donated blood is not screened for HIV- 2"],"a":1,"e":"HIV-1 subtype C is the most common form worldwide.\n\nHuman Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) has two serotypes HIV-1 and HIV- 2 based on differences in the env (envelope) gene.\n\nHIV-1 is further divided into 4 distinct groups, group M (major)/N/O/P. The M group comprises nine subtypes A, B, C, D, F, G, H, J, and K.\n\n• Subtype C is the most common form worldwide & is more associated with heterosexual transmission.\n\n• Subtype B is more associated with spread through blood and homosexual contact.\n\nOptions A, C: HIV- 2 is less pathogenic than HIV-1 and is predominantly confined to Africa.\n\nOption D: Donated blood is screened for both HIV -1 & HIV-2.","t":"Hiv   Aids   Epidemiology And Clinical Features","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"A 47-year-old phlebotomist suffered a needle-stick injury while drawing blood from a patient with HIV. Which of the following mutations in him can cause a resistance to acquiring HIV infection?","o":["CXCR4 2q21 mutation","HLA B*35","CCR5 delta 32 mutation","DC-SIGN"],"a":2,"e":"CCR 5 delta 32 mutation causes resistance to HIV infection. It is a mutated allele that causes a lack of surface expression of the CCR5 co-receptor resulting in blockade of HIV entry and resistance to HIV infection.\n\nHomozygous alleles cause complete resistance to HIV infection & heterozygous alleles cause partial resistance.\n\nCXCR4 and CCR5 are chemokine receptors (co-receptors) present on the surface of CD4+ T-helper cells & macrophages that aid in the fusion and entry of the HIV virus.\n\nDC-SIGN is a type of cell surface lectin receptor to which the HIV virus has the affinity to bind. The expression of this type of receptor on dendritic cells (antigen-presenting cells) enhances the infectivity of HIV.","t":"Hiv   Aids   Epidemiology And Clinical Features","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"A 19-year-old woman presents to the casualty with a 24-hour history of fever, sore throat, and diffuse erythematous macular rash. Past history is positive for unprotected intercourse with a new partner. What is true about the likely diagnosis?","o":["Incubation period is around 1 week","Weight loss is not seen","Virus is transmissible","Opportunistic infections do not occur"],"a":2,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of acute HIV syndrome. The virus is transmissible during this early infection period.\n\nIt is characterized by acute burst of viremia and wide virus dissemination and seeding of lymphoid organs (e.g., gut-associated lymphoid tissue), culminating in the destruction of CD4+ cells.\n\nThe p24 antigen capture assay is used as a screening test for HIV infection in patients suspected of having the acute HIV syndrome.\n\nOption A: The incubation period for acute HIV syndrome is around 3 to 6 weeks.\n\nOption B: The acute HIV syndrome is typical of an acute viral syndrome and is very similar to acute infectious mononucleosis. The clinical findings include fever, sore throat, and lymphadenopathy. Rash, headache, arthralgia, and anorexia/weight loss can also be seen.\n\nOption D: Opportunistic infections can occur during this stage of infection, reflecting the immunodeficiency that results from reduced numbers of CD4+T cells.","t":"Hiv   Aids   Epidemiology And Clinical Features","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"What is the correct WHO staging for an HIV-infected child with unexplained persistent parotid enlargement?","o":["Stage 1","Stage 2","Stage 3","Stage 4"],"a":1,"e":"Unexplained persistent parotid enlargement in an HIV-infected child is categorized as stage 2 of HIV disease according to WHO staging.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/bee35d0a8b4c44a8b5b129428a4b73cbx720x671.JPEG"],"t":"Hiv   Aids   Epidemiology And Clinical Features","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"An HIV-positive individual presents with the following finding. What is the diagnosis &amp; WHO staging?","o":["Oral candidiasis &amp; WHO stage 2","Oral hairy leukoplakia &amp; WHO stage 3","Oral candidiasis &amp; WHO stage 3","Oral hairy leukoplakia &amp; WHO stage 2"],"a":2,"e":"Oral candidiasis (thrush) is categorized as WHO stage 3.\n\nOral candidiasis and oral hairy leukoplakia are oral lesions categorized as WHO clinical stage 3. They generally occur in patients with CD4+ T cell counts of <300/μL.\n\nOral candidiasis\nOral hairy leukoplakia\n\nCause\n\nCandida albicans\n\nEpstein-Barr virus\n\nAppearance\n\nWhite, plaque-like lesions with cheesy exudate, on an erythematous mucosa\n\nWhite, frond-like lesions with hair-like growths from folds in the patches\n\nLocation\nPosterior oropharynx\nLateral borders of tongue\n\nClinical features\n\nTypically painful, can be easily brushed off\n\nTypically painless, cannot be brushed\n\nInvestigation\n\nKOH preparation demonstrates budding yeasts, hyphae, or pseudohyphae\n\nDespite its name, it is not considered premalignant.\n\nTreatment\n Antifungals\nAntivirals\n\nThe image given below shows a patient with oral hairy leukoplakia.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/9521c953e6664507b3e6ee8d585b3eff.JPEG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8c2e6a01699046e8969f6f264309a917x334x242.PNG"],"t":"Hiv   Aids   Epidemiology And Clinical Features","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"What is the most common opportunistic infection in HIV-infected individuals?","o":["Tuberculosis","Disseminated candidiasis","Herpes zoster","Pneumocystis jiroveci pneumonia"],"a":0,"e":"Tuberculosisis the most common opportunistic infection in HIV-infected individuals globally and in India.\n\nIn patients with relatively high CD4+ T cell counts, the typical pattern of pulmonary reactivation occurs (60–80%). In patients with lower CD4+ T cell counts, disseminated disease is seen.\n\nPrimary prophylaxis: Isoniazid and pyridoxine for 9 months for HIV-infected individuals meeting the following conditions:\n\n• PPD skin-test reaction of >5 mm or,\n\n• Positive IFN-γ release assay or,\n\n• Close household contacts of persons with active tuberculosis","t":"Hiv   Aids   Epidemiology And Clinical Features","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"A previously untreated HIV-positive patient is diagnosed with tuberculosis and treatment is initiated for both the diseases. Two weeks into treatment, he suddenly complains of high fever, severe malaise and worsening cough. What is the likely explanation?","o":["Resistance to anti-tubercular therapy","Concurrent treatment with anti-retroviral therapy","Bacterial endotoxin release due to death of TB bacilli","Hypersensitivity reaction to isoniazid"],"a":1,"e":"Concurrent treatment with anti-retroviral therapy is the most likely explanation in this case resulting in Immune Reconstitution Inflammatory Syndrome (IRIS). Its characteristics are:\n\n• Temporary paradoxical exacerbation of symptoms (lymphadenopathy, fever or worsening of pulmonary infiltrations, pleural effusion) and radiographic manifestations of TB after starting ART. This occurs as a result of immune reconstitution due to simultaneous administration of ART and ATT.\n\n• It is seen weeks to months following the initiation of combination antiretroviral therapy (cART).\n\n• It is most common in previously untreated patients starting therapy with a CD4+ T cell count <50/μL who experience a sudden drop in viral load.\n\n• The earlier ART is started and the lower the baseline CD4+ T-cell count, the greater the risk of IRIS.\n\n• In severe cases, the use of immunosuppressive drugs such as glucocorticoids may be required to blunt the inflammatory component of IRIS while specific antimicrobial therapy takes effect.\n\nNote: Hypersensitivity to isoniazid is extremely rare and the presentation includes maculopapular rash.","t":"Hiv   Aids   Epidemiology And Clinical Features","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"An HIV-positive man presents with a high-grade fever. Examination reveals the following finding. CSF shows reduced glucose, increased protein, and increased leukocytes. What is the most common cause of this condition in such patients?","o":["Streptococcus pneumoniae","Herpes simplex virus","Toxoplasma gondii","Cryptococcus neoformans"],"a":3,"e":"The given clinical scenario, along with a positive Kernig's sign, is suggestive of meningitis. Cryptococcus is the leading infectious cause of meningitis in patients with AIDS. It generally occurs in untreated patients, with CD4+ T cell counts <100/μL.\n\nMost patients present with a picture of subacute meningoencephalitis. The CSF profile may show modest elevations in WBC or protein levels and decrease in glucose. The CSF opening pressure is usually elevated (yeast colonies obstruct the free flow of CSF causing increased CSF pressure).\n\nThe diagnosis of cryptococcal meningitis is made by identification of Cryptococcus neoformans yeast cells in CSF with india ink stain or by the detection of cryptococcal antigen using latex agglutination (CrAg).\n\nProphylaxis: Oral fluconazole when CD4+ T cell count is <100/μL.\n\nTreatment:\n\n• Induction - IV or liposomal amphotericin B with flucytosine for at least 2 weeks, continuing with amphotericin alone ideally until the CSF culture turns negative.\n\n• Maintenance - Oral fluconazole until the CD4+ T cell count has increased to >200 cells/μL for 6 months in response to cART.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/505060e2c15c41f5a879f53aa4a8efa6.GIF"],"t":"Hiv   Aids   Epidemiology And Clinical Features","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"A 45-year-old HIV-positive woman presented with fever and malaise. She gives a history of bleeding from the following lesions. Warthin-Starry staining of a biopsy sample taken from the lesion showed which organism?","o":["Bartonella henselae","Human Herpes virus - 8","Bartonella bacilliformis","Ebstein Barr Virus"],"a":0,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of bacillary angiomatosis in an immunocompromised patient. Warthin-Starry stain would show Bartonella henselae as the causative organism in these skin lesions.\n\nBacillary angiomatosis is an infectious vascular proliferative lesion presenting as reddish-purple nodules similar to KS lesions (caused by HHV-8). The diagnosis is confirmed with a biopsy. The drug of choice for treatment is erythromycin.\n\nThe image given below shows the Warthin-Starry stained lesion showing tangled pleomorphic bacilli.\n\nOption B: HHV 8 is strongly associated with Kaposi sarcoma.\n\nOption C: Bartonella bacilliformis causes Oroya fever.\n\nOption D: EBV causes infectious mononucleosis.\n\nNote: Bartonella henselae causes cat-scratch disease, a necrotizing granulomatous disorder of lymph nodes, in immunocompetent hosts.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4b7b01fce7a348aba1f4a21a73369ef0.JPEG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/1c3fd558935c49d09f89841c1eaecb67x224x180.JPEG"],"t":"Hiv   Aids   Epidemiology And Clinical Features","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"An HIV-positive woman was rushed to the ICU following an episode of generalized tonic-clonic seizures. There was no history of fever, night sweats or weight loss. MRI scan is shown below. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Cryptococcal meningitis","Primary central nervous system lymphoma","Cerebral toxoplasmosis","Progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy"],"a":2,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of cerebral toxoplasmosis. MRI scan shows multiple ring-enhancing lesions.\n\nCerebral toxoplasmosis is one of the most common AIDS-defining opportunistic CNS infections and usually occurs in patients with CD4+ T cell counts <200/μL. It is usually a reactivation of latent tissue cysts.\n\nIt usually has multiple lesions in multiple locations which are associated with inflammation and central necrosis, and hence show ring enhancement on contrast MRI. Sometimes an eccentric nodular area of enhancement is seen within the ring; this so-called eccentric target sign is typical of cerebral toxoplasmosis. Surrounding edema is also present.\n\nTreatment includes sulfadiazine/pyrimethamine and leucovorin for a minimum of 4–6 weeks.\n\nPrimary prophylaxis: It is given to patients with IgG antibody to toxoplasma and CD4+ T cell count <100/μL. A single double strength tablet of trimethoprim/sulfamethoxazole (TMP/SMX) is given.\n\nSecondary prophyaxis/maintanace therapy: It is given when CD4+ T cell counts <200 cells/μL. Sulfadiazine/pyrimethamine and leucovorin is given. It is discontinued if the CD4+ T cell counts are maintained >200/μL for 6 months.\n\nOption A: Cryptococcal meningitis presents with fever, nausea, vomiting, altered mental status, headache, and meningeal signs. Seizures and focal neurologic deficits are rarely seen. MRI findings include dilated perivascular spaces and basal ganglia pseudocysts.\n\nOption B: Primary CNS lymphoma also presents with seizures and focal neurological deficit. Symptoms of fever, night sweats and weight loss are present in 80% of the patients. On imaging, it is identified as a CT hyperdense enhancing supratentorial mass, with MRI T1 hypointense, T2 iso- to hypointense, vivid homogeneous enhancement as well as restricted diffusion.\n\nOption C: Progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy, caused by JC virus, is characterized by demyelination in subcortical white matter reflected on MRI as multiple, non-enhancing white matter lesions.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/92e01cc7c83d41c2bb2579d461cf39d7.JPEG"],"t":"Hiv   Aids   Epidemiology And Clinical Features","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"An HIV-positive woman was referred to the ophthalmology OPD for decreased visual acuity. She described her vision as blurry with apparent flies floating in her field of vision. Fundoscopy revealed the following finding. What is the CD4+T cell count at which majority of such cases occur?","o":["&lt; 200/μL","&lt; 50/μL","&lt; 150/μL","&lt; 250/μL"],"a":1,"e":"The given clinical scenario, along with the pizza pie appearance on fundoscopy, is suggestive of cytomegalovirus (CMV) retinitis. The majority of such cases occur in HIV-infected patients with a CD4+T cell count <50/μL.\n\nCMV retinitis is a necrotic retinal infection that results in a painless irreversible vision loss. Patients may complain of blurred vision, floaters (described by patient as flies or spiders in the field of vision), and scintillations. It is usually bilateral and is characterized by perivascular hemorrhage and exudates in the retina (pizza pie appearance or scrambled eggs and ketchup appearance).\n\nTreatment:\n\n• Induction - IV ganciclovir (3 weeks)\n\n• Maintenance - Oral valganciclovir (till CD4 count > 100/μl for 6months)","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/b26695e10a7f4c52b271d3f99fcf3e8b.JPEG"],"t":"Hiv   Aids   Epidemiology And Clinical Features","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"A unemployed HIV-positive patient, resident of a missionary home, presents with 2 weeks of fever, cough and night sweats. He was devastated after getting fired from his job because of his diagnosis and has since been non-compliant to cART. His CD4 count is &lt;10/ μL. CT scan shows the following finding. What is the likely diagnosis?","o":["Cytomegalovirus infection","Candidiasis","Mycobacterium avium complex infection","Pneumocystis jiroveci pneumonia"],"a":2,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of mycobacterium avium complex (MAC) infection. It is an AIDS-defining opportunistic infection, occurring predominantly at CD4+ T cell counts of <50/μL.\n\nThe most common presentation is disseminated disease with fever, weight loss, and night sweats. The diagnosis is made by culture of blood or involved tissue.\n\nCT scan shows peribronchial inflammation of the infected lung giving rise to a tree in a bud appearance (small round structures attached to linear structures) indicated by the arrowhead. In severe cases, there might be bronchiectasis and cavity formation.\n\nTreatment consists of clarithromycin / azithromycin with ethambutol/ rifabutin. Primary prophylaxis with azithromycin is indicated in patients with CD4+ T cell counts <50/μL.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4e1d63cd405c4a1985c845e7a600d4f9.PNG"],"t":"Hiv   Aids   Epidemiology And Clinical Features","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"A 50-year-old man with HIV presents with lab reports showing: Low albumin, massive proteinuria. Renal ultrasound shows large, echogenic kidneys. Blood pressure is normal. Which of the following is the most likely cause?","o":["Minimal change disease","Focal segmental glomerulosclerosis","Membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis","IgA nephropathy"],"a":1,"e":"This patient with HIV presents with massive proteinuria and hypoalbuminemia. The most likely cause is HIV-1-associated nephropathy (HIVAN) - a form of focal segmental glomerulosclerosis (FSGS).\n\nProteinuria is the hallmark of this disorder. Edema and hypertension are rare. A definitive diagnosis is obtained through renal biopsy.","t":"Hiv   Aids   Epidemiology And Clinical Features","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is the most common form of heart disease associated with HIV?","o":["Dilated cardiomyopathy","Thrombotic endocarditis","Coronary heart disease","Pericardial effusion"],"a":2,"e":"The most common form of heart disease associated with HIV infection is coronary heart disease.\n\nIn patients with HIV infection, cardiovascular disease may be a direct consequence of HIV infection, a complication of anti-retroviral therapy or due to classical risk factors like smoking.\n\nExposure to HIV protease inhibitors and reverse transcriptase inhibitors have been associated with an increase in total cholesterol and/or risk of MI (Myocardial infarction).\n\nDilated cardiomyopathy associated with congestive heart failure (CHF) is referred to as HIV-associated cardiomyopathy. This generally occurs as a late complication of HIV infection and, histologically, displays elements of myocarditis.","t":"Hiv   Aids   Epidemiology And Clinical Features","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"Anti-retroviral therapy in HIV-infected individuals should be initiated at _______","o":["CD4 count &lt;200 cells/ul and/or WHO clinical stage 2/3","CD4 count &lt;350 cells/ul and/or WHO clinical stage 3/4","CD4 count &lt;250 cells/ul and/or WHO clinical stage 4","Any CD4 count regardless of WHO clinical stage"],"a":3,"e":"Antiretroviral therapy (ART) is recommended for all HIV patients, regardless of CD4 T lymphocyte cell count or clinical stage.\n\nThis is done to reduce the morbidity and mortality associated with HIV infection. A secondary goal of ART is to reduce the risk of HIV transmission.","t":"Hiv   Aids   Management And Prophylaxis","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is true about fourth-generation serological testing for HIV?","o":["It is used to confirm the diagnosis of HIV","It detects HIV antibodies and antigen","It has a lower sensitivity compared to first-generation HIV testing","It cannot be used to detect HIV-2 antibodies"],"a":1,"e":"The 4th generation HIV test combines the detection of antibodies against HIV-1 & HIV-2 with detection of the core p24 antigen of HIV-1. Inclusion of p24 antigen capture allows detection of HIV-1 infection even before seroconversion i.e., presence of antibodies in the blood.\n\nThe approximate time to positivity following infection is 15 to 20 days. Consequently, the window period between infection and detection of the infection is shortened.\n\nFourth-generation HIV testing is highly sensitive (99.9%) and is the CDC-recommended initial screening test for HIV. No further testing is required for specimens that are non-reactive on ELISA screening.\n\nGeneration\nSerological test\n\nFirst generation\nIgG antibody\n\nSecond generation\nIgG antibody\n\nThird generation\nIgM and IgG antibody\n\nFourth generation\nIgM and IgG antibody and p24 antigen","t":"Hiv   Aids   Management And Prophylaxis","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"A 46-year-old man, who is a chronic alcoholic, presented with acute dull and boring abdominal pain. His history is significant for ongoing treatment with ART. Serum lipase levels were noted to be grossly elevated. What is the most likely causative drug?","o":["Nevirapine","Didanosine","Emtricitabine","Stavudine"],"a":1,"e":"Didanosine is the most likely causative drug. The clinical scenario, suggestive of pancreatitis, is a dose-limiting side effect of didanosine and occurs more frequently in alcoholic patients and those with hypertriglyceridemia.\n\nOption A: Nevirapine side-effects include severe liver problems, skin rash, and skin reactions.\n\nOption C: Emtricitabine is contraindicated in pregnancy and young children.\n\nOption D: Stavudine can cause peripheral neuropathy.","t":"Hiv   Aids   Management And Prophylaxis","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"An HIV-positive patient is upset because he has a high viral load despite good compliance with ART. The physician decides to start him on a parenteral drug. Which of the following drugs will be helpful for this patient?","o":["Emtricitabine","Elvitegravir","Enfuvirtide","Efavirenz"],"a":2,"e":"Enfuvirtide is the antiretroviral drug administered parenterally, used only in the treatment of those who have evidence of HIV replication i.e., high viral load, despite ongoing antiretroviral therapy.\n\nEnfuvirtide prevents viral fusion and entry by binding to the gp41 subunit of the HIV-1 envelope. It is not active against HIV-2. It is administered as a twice-a-day subcutaneous injection.\n\nNote: Zidovudine is also available as a parenteral formulation.","t":"Hiv   Aids   Management And Prophylaxis","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"An HIV-positive patient has the following serology results. What is the management of this patient?\n\nMarkers\n\nResult\n\nHBsAg\nPositive\n\nanti-HBc\nPositive\n\nIgM anti-HBc\nPositive\n\nanti-HBs\nNegative","o":["Tenofovir + emtricitabine + abacavir","Tenofovir + lamivudine + dolutegravir","Lamivudine + emtricitabine + nevirapine","Tenofovir + lamivudine + efavirenz"],"a":1,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of an acute infection with hepatitis B virus. The preferred regimen in the management of HIV-1 and hepatitis B co-infection is tenofovir + lamivudine + dolutegravir.\n\nThis regimen is also the first-line management in adults, adolescents, pregnant women, and HIV-TB co-infection.\n\nAntivirals that are effective against hepatitis C infection are sofosbuvir, grazoprevir and glecaprevir.\n\n*Note: Pearl attached below has been updated according to the NACO ART guidelines 2021. Efavirenz has been replaced by Dolutegravir as per the new guidelines.","t":"Hiv   Aids   Management And Prophylaxis","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"Screening of patients for HLA- B5701 is recommended before initiation of which of the following antiretroviral drugs?","o":["Tenofovir","Abacavir","Zidovudine","Emtricitabine"],"a":1,"e":"Screening of patients for HLA-B5701 is recommended before initiation of abacavir since it is associated with hypersensitivity.\n\nThe hypersensitivity syndrome includes fever, maculopapular rash, abdominal pain, gastrointestinal problems, malaise and fatigue. Hence, abacavir should only be used as a last resort and with close monitoring in patients who are HLA-B5701-positive.","t":"Hiv   Aids   Management And Prophylaxis","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"Evaluation of a patient with uncontrolled type 2 diabetes mellitus and hypertension has revealed moderately increased albuminuria. He has recently been diagnosed with HIV infection. Combination ART preparations including which of the following drugs should be avoided?","o":["Tenofovir","Lamivudine","Emtricitabine","Dolutegravir"],"a":0,"e":"The given clinical scenerio is suggestive of early stages of diabetic and hypertensive kidney disease. So combinaton antiretroviral therapy (cART) preparations including tenfovir should not be prescribed to this patient.\n\nTenofovir is primarily eliminated by the kidneys and is contraindicated in patients with renal impairment.","t":"Hiv   Aids   Management And Prophylaxis","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"A 36-year-old HIV-positive man on cART is presented with the following finding. What is the most likely cause?","o":["Abacavir","Entecavir","Nelfinavir","Adefovir"],"a":2,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of fat redistribution resulting in the classic buffalo hump appearance. Nelfinavir is the most likely cause.\n\nIt is a protease inhibitor and its chronic administration can result in fat redistribution and metabolic syndrome.\n\nNote: Atazanavir has the least propensity to cause lipodystrophy and metabolic syndrome.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/6b659a4efa4d47d493572689970cb6ed.JPEG"],"t":"Hiv   Aids   Management And Prophylaxis","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"A 54-year-old HIV-positive woman has a Hb of 8gm/dl. Combination ART preparations including which of the following drugs should be avoided?","o":["Zidovudine","Tenofovir","Raltegravir","Emtricitabine"],"a":0,"e":"Zidovudine should be avoided in this patient because the primary toxicity of zidovudine is bone marrow suppression. It can lead to anemia and neutropenia, which may require transfusions.","t":"Hiv   Aids   Management And Prophylaxis","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"During his routine counselling, a war veteran on cART for the past 2 weeks tells his therapist that he wishes to discontinue his HIV treatment. He explains that since starting them, he has been having extreme dizziness and intense nightmares leading to repeated wakening in the night. What is the likely cause?","o":["Tenofovir","Dolutegravir","Efavirenz","Lamivudine"],"a":2,"e":"The patient is most likely prescribed a cART regimen including efavirenz.\n\nMany patients treated with efavirenz note a feeling of light-headedness and dizziness following the initiation of therapy. They also complain of insomnia, vivid dreams and nightmares.\n\nThese symptoms are self-limiting and tend to disappear gradually after several weeks of therapy. If the sleep problems are persistent, stopping the drug is the only solution.\n\nIt is recommended to take efavirenz before bedtime to minimize side effects.\n\nThe CNS/ psychiatric adverse effects seen with the use of efavirenz are:\n\n• Frank psychosis (depression, hallucinations, and/or mania)\n\n• Impaired concentration\n\n• Dysphoria\n\n• Dizziness\n\n• Vivid dreams\n\n• Insomnia\n\n*Note: Pearl attached below has been updated according to the NACO ART guidelines 2021. Efavirenz has been replaced by Dolutegravir as per the new guidelines.","t":"Hiv   Aids   Management And Prophylaxis","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"A 38-year-old man is tested for HIV and the following band pattern is seen on a standard western blot. Protein gp36 is present on the immunoblotting assay. To which class of antiretroviral drug would he be intrinsically resistant?\n\nEnv (gp160, gp120,gp41)\n Negative\n\nGag (p55, p24, p17)\n Positive\n\nPol (p66, p51, p32)\n Positive","o":["Nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors","Nucleotide reverse transcriptase inhibitors","Non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors","Integrase strand transfer inhibitors"],"a":2,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of HIV-2 infection. It is intrinsically resistant to non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NNRTI) and also to fusion inhibitor enfuvirtide.\n\nWestern blot has a high specificity. It detects specific antibodies against multiple proteins of all 3 major HIV genes - gag, pol, and env.\n\nPersons with HIV-2 will often have an indeterminate western blot pattern showing the presence of Gag bands and Pol bands, but an absence of Env bands. This pattern occurs because HIV-1 and HIV-2 share little similarity in the env gene. Moreover, the presence of gp36 points towards a diagnosis of HIV-2.\n\nIn India, NACO provides western blot confirmatory testing only to resolve indeterminate results on previous serological testing and not for routine confirmation after a positive result on screening tests.","t":"Hiv   Aids   Management And Prophylaxis","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"An individual positive for HIV-1 infection was started on a combination ART regimen of tenofovir–emtricitabine–efavirenz. After 6 months, failure of treatment was established and attributed to efavirenz resistance. Which drug of the same class can be used instead?","o":["Delavirdine","Nevirapine","Nelfinavir","Etravirine"],"a":3,"e":"Etravirine can be used instead of efavirenz.\n\nEtravirine is a non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor (NNRTI) currently approved for the treatment of HIV infection in combination with other agents in treatment-experienced patients.\n\nOptions A, B: Etravirine is active against strains of HIV that are resistant to other NNRTIs such as delavirdine, nevirapine, and efavirenz which all exhibit cross-resistance. Rilpivirine, an NNRTI, is also effective across a broad range of NNRTI-resistant viruses but shares cross-resistance with etravirine.\n\nOption C: Nelfinavir is a protease inhibitor and hence, does not belong to the same class.","t":"Hiv   Aids   Management And Prophylaxis","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"A woman is diagnosed to be positive for HIV in her first trimester. What is the appropriate management?","o":["Start ART immediately and continue till 6 weeks after delivery","Start ART after 1st trimester and continue life-long","Start ART immediately and continue life-long","Start ART after 1st trimester and continue till 6 weeks after delivery"],"a":2,"e":"ART should be initiated immediately and continued lifelong in all pregnant and breastfeeding women with HIV. This is regardless of their WHO clinical stage and CD4 cell count.\n\n*Note: Pearl attached below has been updated according to the NACO ART guidelines 2021. Efavirenz has been replaced by Dolutegravir as per the new guidelines.","t":"Hiv   Aids   Management And Prophylaxis","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"A 29-year-old HIV-positive woman presents to the OPD with fever, cough, and hemoptysis. Chest X-ray is given below. What is the diagnostic test for this condition in this patient?","o":["Cartridge based nucleic acid amiplification test","Sputum microscopy &amp; AFB staining","Interferon gamma release assay","Bronchoalveolar lavage fluid cytology"],"a":0,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of tuberculosis. Cartridge-based nucleic acid amiplification test (CBNAAT) is the diagnostic test for TB in HIV positive patients.\n\nCBNAAT is a rapid molecular test that detects the DNA of TB bacteria in HIV-positive individuals. It uses sputum or any other biological specimen (except blood and blood-contaminated specimens). It can give a result in less than 2 hours. It can also detect the genetic mutations associated with resistance to the drug rifampicin.\n\nSputum microscopy has poor sensitivity for detecting TB in HIV-infected individuals due to fewer organisms in sputum.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/99cd01362b8443a688d701311b3d05c4.JPEG"],"t":"Hiv   Aids   Management And Prophylaxis","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"A 52-year-old man was admitted for multiple episodes of seizures following 7 months of neurocognitive decline. He also had weight loss, paranoia, and delusions. Clinical workup revealed HIV infection along with the following findings on MRI. What is the ideal timing of steroid therapy in this condition?","o":["1 day before performing a biopsy","Just before obtaining the sample for a biopsy","Only after obtaining a biopsy","There is no specific ideal timing"],"a":2,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of primary CNS lymphoma (PCNSL). In this condition, glucocorticoids should be administered only after a biopsy has been obtained. This is because steroids have a cytolytic effect on lymphoma cells and hence may lead to nondiagnostic tissue.\n\nOn CT, PCNSL is identified as a hyperdense enhancing supratentorial mass. On MRI, vivid homogeneous enhancement, as well as restricted diffusion, is seen.\n\nPCNSL is a type of non-Hodgkin's lymphoma. The workup usually includes:\n\n• HIV testing\n\n• CSF analysis\n\n• MRI spine\n\n• PET/CT to assess the extent of the disease\n\n• Bone marrow biopsy","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/35488a35aa56417d9fdf10ff17c6a1e0.JPEG"],"t":"Hiv   Aids   Management And Prophylaxis","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"What is the WHO recommendation regarding the initiation of cotrimoxazole prophylaxis?","o":["Any CD4 count or WHO clinical stage","CD4 count &lt;500 and/or WHO stage 3/4","CD4 count &lt;350 and/or WHO stage 3/4","CD4 count &lt;150 and/or WHO stage 3/4"],"a":2,"e":"WHO recommends cotrimoxazole prophylaxis at CD4 count <350 and/or WHO stage 3/4.\n\nOnly in settings where malaria or severe bacterial infections are highly prevalent, co-trimoxazole prophylaxis should be initiated regardless of WHO clinical stage or CD4 counts.\n\nProphylaxis in HIV patients:\n\nOrganism\nCD4 count below which prophylaxis is started\nDrug given\n\nPneumocystis jirovecii\n\n<200/mm3\n\n (Acc to CDC)\n\n<350 /mm3\n\n(Acc to WHO and NACO)\n\nTMP-SMX (Cotrimoxazole)\n\nMycobacterium-avium complex\n\n<50/mm3\nAzithromycin\n\nToxoplasma gondii\n\n<100/mm3\nTMP-SMX (Cotrimoxazole)","t":"Hiv   Aids   Management And Prophylaxis","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"A cachexic man presented with a non-productive cough and shortness of breath. He was diagnosed with HIV 6 months ago but is non-adherent to ART. Serum LDH is 350 U/L. Giemsa-stained smears are seen below. At what CD4 count should the prophylaxis be started according to the CDC recommendation to prevent this disease?","o":["&lt;100/mm3","&lt;150/mm3","&lt;400/mm3","&lt;200/mm3"],"a":3,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of pneumocystis pneumonia (PCP). The prophylaxis for PCP is started at <200/mm3 CD4 count according to the CDC recommendation. In PCP LDH is elevated and smear stained with Giemsa stain shows trophic forms with dot-like nuclei and pale blue cytoplasm as well as intracystic bodies of Pneumocystis jiroveci.\n\nPrimary prophylaxis of PCP:\n\nIndications\n\n• CD4+ T cell count <200/uL ( CD4 cell %<15)\n\n• Recent oropharyngeal candidiasis and prior PCP\n\n• Any patient with unexplained fever for > 2 weeks.\n\nDrug of choice\n\nTrimethoprim/sulfamethoxazole (TMP/SMX), one double-strength tablet daily.\n\nAlternatives- Dapsone/Pyrimethamine + Leucovorin or Atovaqone or Aerosolised Pentamidine\n\nNote : TMP-SMX also provides protection against toxoplasmosis and some bacterial respiratory pathogens. The prophylaxis is discontinued in those patients treated with ART who maintain good suppression of HIV (<50 copies/mL) and CD4+ T-cell counts >200/ μL for at least 3 months.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/e4519552dd7c43a9b4fdd6cbe7bc3642.PNG"],"t":"Hiv   Aids   Management And Prophylaxis","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"You suffer a bleeding puncture wound on your finger while assisting with surgery on a patient infected with HIV. What regimen should you take in this situation?","o":["Zidovudine + Lamivudine for 4 weeks","Lamivudine + Tenofovir + Efavirenz for 4 weeks","Lamivudine + Tenofovir + Dolutegravir for 4 weeks","Tenofovir + Lamivudine + Nevirapine for 4 weeks"],"a":2,"e":"Recommended post-exposure prophylaxis (PEP) is a three-drug regimen that includes lamivudine, tenofovir, and dolutegravir.\n\nA regimen for HIV post-exposure prophylaxis with two ARV drugs is effective, but three drugs are preferred. The PEP must be initiated ideally within 2 hours and certainly within 72 hours.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/8115059da00d45c195c82188db4e14ccx1280x1488.JPEG"],"t":"Hiv   Aids   Management And Prophylaxis","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"Coronaviruses are _____","o":["Naked helically symmetrical viruses","Enveloped icosahedrally symmetrical viruses","Enveloped helically symmetrical viruses","Variably enveloped viruses with a complex symmetry"],"a":2,"e":"Coronaviruses are enveloped helically symmetrical viruses.\n\nBeing enveloped contributes to their lipid solvent susceptibility. This is the reason alcohol-based hand sanitizers and soaps are used. The lipid membrane dissolution and protein denaturation by ethanol lead to membrane disruption and inhibition of metabolism.\n\nThe coronaviridiae have a single unsegmented ss (+) RNA, which is the largest genome among RNA viruses. They are about 120- 160 nm in size and contain a helical nucleocapsid. A total of seven human coronaviruses have been identified:\n\nName\nIllness\n\nSARS-CoV-2\nCOVID-19\n\nSARS-CoV\nSevere acute respiratory syndrome (SARS)\n\nMERS-CoV\nMiddle East respiratory syndrome (MERS)\n\nHCoV-NL63\nUsually mild respiratory illness\n\nHCoV-229E\n\nHCoV-OC43\n\nHKU1\n\nThe image given below shows the coronavirus.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/105418143db243799a8c028a61bf7753x1280x1089.JPEG"],"t":"Covid 19   Epidemiology And Clinical Features","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"An elderly diabetic man presents with difficulty breathing, cough, and fever. He came back after visiting his son in New York, 10 days ago. His SpO2 is 89%. HRCT shows bilateral, multilobar ground-glass opacification. What is the receptor used by the likely causative organism to gain entry into the cells?","o":["Angiotensin-converting enzyme 1","Angiotensin-converting enzyme 2","Dipeptidylpeptidase 2","Dipeptidylpeptidase 4"],"a":1,"e":"The given clinical vignette showing breathlessness, fever, and cough with a low oxygen saturation and ground-glass opacities on HRCT is indicative of COVID-19 caused by the SARS-CoV-2 virus. Angiotensin-converting enzyme 2 (ACE-2) is the specific functional receptor for SARS-CoV-2 in human cells.\n\nACE2 is expressed in nearly all human organs in varying degrees. In the respiratory system, ACE2 is mainly expressed by the type 2 alveolar epithelial cells in the highest quantity. Hence, the primary target of SARS-CoV-2 is type 2 alveolar cells.\n\nThe functional receptor for SARS-CoV (SARS 2002) is also ACE 2.\n\nNote: Dipeptidylpeptidase 4 is the functional receptor for MERS-CoV (MERS 2012).","t":"Covid 19   Epidemiology And Clinical Features","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"Which viral structure determines the infectious nature and host specificity of SARS-CoV-2?","o":["2","1","4","3"],"a":1,"e":"Spike protein (marked as 1 in the image) determines the infectious nature and host specificity of SARS-CoV-2.\n\nThe spike protein also referred to as the CoV S protein is one of the key components determining virus virulence, tissue tropism, and host range. The coronavirus spike protein mediates coronavirus entry into host cells. Each spike protein has two distinct subunits S1 and S2.\n\n• S1 subunit is responsible for binding to the specific receptor (ACE 2) on the host cell surface facilitating viral attachment to the surface of target cells.\n\n• S2 subunit drives the fusion of the viral and host cell membranes.\n\nThe host protease enzymes, mainly transmembrane protease serine-type 2 (TMPRSS2) cleave the spike protein. This activates the spike protein to induce virus-cell membrane fusion at the cell surface and allows the entry of coronaviruses into the host cell.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/cc731a934b9947bc88771644a36290da.JPEG"],"ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/4289ff5ac08d455882d546141f547f7fx1280x1155.JPEG"],"t":"Covid 19   Epidemiology And Clinical Features","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"Which of the following scenarios is unlikely for transmission of SARS-CoV-2?","o":["A gym trainer transmitted the virus to his client following a sudden, involuntary cough.","A librarian got infected from a reader through the share and exchange of books.","A resident transmitted the virus to his neighbour who entered the same apartment lift after a few hours.","A thalassemic girl got infected following routine blood transfusion."],"a":3,"e":"Blood transfusion is not a mode of transmission of SARS-CoV-2.\n\nOption A: Droplet transmission\n\n• A type of direct transmission\n\n• Occurs when someone coughs, sneezes, speaks, or spits\n\n• Virus-containing droplets from the infected person travel shortly in the air directly contact another person’s conjunctiva, oral or nasal mucosa\n\n• Limited to about 6 feet distance between the source and the host\n\n• Particle size is 5-10 µm in diameter\n\nOption B: Fomite transmission\n\n• A type of indirect transmission\n\n• Objects other than water or food contaminated by infectious agents - clothes, towels, linen, cups, spoons, pencils, books, toys, door handles, taps, etc.\n\n• Transmission occurs when one touches the contaminated objects and touches his mouth, nose, or eyes\n\nOption C: Airborne - droplet nuclei transmission\n\n• A type of indirect transmission\n\n• Droplet nuclei - dried residue of droplets of size <5µm that are generated by the evaporation of droplets coughed or sneezed into the air and by a variety of atomizing devices\n\n• Remain infectious when suspended in the air for some time (typically hours)\n\n• Transmission occurs when one inhales droplet nuclei/aerosols containing the virus in sufficient quantity","t":"Covid 19   Epidemiology And Clinical Features","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"A relative of a COVID-19 positive man, who came in his contact yesterday, reported to the flu clinic. Within how many days is he most likely to develop the disease?","o":["7-14 days","2-3 weeks","1-3 weeks","1-14 days"],"a":3,"e":"The incubation period of SARS-CoV-2 infection ranges from 1-14 days so he is most likely to develop the disease after this period. The median incubation period is around 4-6 days.\n\nThe incubation period is defined as the time interval between invasion by an infectious agent and appearance of the first sign or symptom of the disease.","t":"Covid 19   Epidemiology And Clinical Features","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"Covid 19 outbreak is a type of which of the following kinds of the epidemic?","o":["Propagated epidemic","Point source, single exposure","Point source, multiple exposures","Slow epidemic"],"a":0,"e":"The outbreak of COVID-19 is an example of a propagated epidemic.\n\nThe propagated epidemic results from person-to-person transmission of the infectious agent over a prolonged duration of time. Other examples of this type include hepatitis A and polio.","t":"Covid 19   Epidemiology And Clinical Features","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"What is not included in the definition of a suspect case of SARS-CoV-2?","o":["Myalgia","Coryza","Altered mental status","Convulsions"],"a":3,"e":"All of the given symptoms are included in the definition of a suspect case of SARS-CoV-2/COVID-19 except convulsions.\n\nAs per WHO and MOHFW, a suspect case is defined as:\n\n1. A person who meets the clinical and epidemiological criteria-\n\n1. Clinical criteria:\n\n• Acute onset of fever AND cough; OR\n\n• Acute onset of any 3 or more of the following: fever, cough, general weakness/ fatigue, headache, myalgia, sore throat, coryza, dyspnoea, anorexia/nausea/vomiting, diarrhea, altered mental status.\n\n2. Epidemiological criteria: A person present in any of the following settings for 14 days prior to symptom onset-\n\n• Residing or working in an area with a high risk of transmission of virus OR\n\n• Residing or travel to an area with community transmission OR\n\n• Working in any healthcare setting\n\n2. A patient with severe acute respiratory illness with-\n\n• history of fever or measured fever of ≥38 C° along with cough\n\n• onset within the last 10 days\n\n• requiring hospitalization\n\n3. An asymptomatic person not meeting epidemiologic criteria with a positive SARS-CoV-2 antigen-RDT.","t":"Covid 19   Epidemiology And Clinical Features","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is not considered a probable case of SARS-CoV-2?","o":["An asymptomatic contact of a probable case with a positive COVID-19 rapid antigen test.","A person with acute onset fever and cough who is a contact of a confirmed case.","A suspect case with positive chest radiological findings suggestive of COVID-19 disease.","A person with recent onset of anosmia or ageusia in the absence of any other identified cause."],"a":0,"e":"An asymptomatic contact of a probable case with a positive COVID-19 rapid antigen test is considered as a confirmed case, and not a probable case.\n\nProbable case is defined as:\n\n1. A patient who meets clinical criteria and is a contact of a probable or confirmed case, or linked to a COVID-19 cluster.\n\n2. A suspect case with chest imaging showing findings suggestive of COVID-19 disease.\n\n3. A person with recent onset of anosmia or ageusia in the absence of any other identified cause.\n\n4. Death, not otherwise explained, in an adult with respiratory distress preceding death and was a contact of a probable or confirmed case or linked to a COVID-19 cluster.\n\nConfirmed case is defined as:\n\n1. A person with a positive Nucleic Acid Amplification Test (NAAT) including RT-PCR or any other similar test approved by ICMR.\n\n2. A person with a positive COVID-19 rapid antigen test and meeting either the probable case definition or suspect criteria.\n\n3. An asymptomatic person with a positive COVID-19 rapid antigen test who is a contact of a probable or confirmed case.","t":"Covid 19   Epidemiology And Clinical Features","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"Which of the following people would you not classify as a high risk contact of SARS-CoV2?","o":["A family member who lives in the same household as the patient","A doctor who examined the patient with a surgical mask and gloves","A passenger in the same bus as a symptomatic patient for 6 hours and the patient later tested positive","A sanitary worker who cleaned the linens of the patient"],"a":2,"e":"A person traveling in the same environment and not having significant exposure to the confirmed case is not classified as a high-risk contact.\n\nA contact of COVID-19 is a person living in the same household or having direct physical contact with the patient/infectious secretions without appropriate PPE or a person who was in a closed environment or at a distance of within 1 meter of a COVID-19 case.\n\nHigh-risk contacts:\n\n• Touched body fluids of the patient\n\n• Had direct contact with the body of the patient during the physical examination without proper PPE\n\n• Touched or cleaned the linens, clothes, or dishes of the patient\n\n• Lives in the same household as the patient\n\n• Comes in close contact (within 1 meter) with the patient without necessary precautions\n\n• A passenger in close proximity with the symptomatic patient for 6 hours and the patient later tested positive\n\nLow-risk contacts:\n\n• Shared the same space but does not have a high-risk exposure to the confirmed case.\n\n• Travelled in the same environment but does not have a high-risk exposure to the confirmed case.","t":"Covid 19   Epidemiology And Clinical Features","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"You would classify all of the following patients as having major risk factors for severe COVID-19 except:","o":["A 19 year old female undergoing dialysis for CKD","A 49 year old male who has recently undergone liver transplantation","A 42 year old man who had malaria 2 months ago","A 65 year old male with no comorbidities"],"a":2,"e":"A middle-aged man who had malaria 2 months ago is not at major risk of developing severe COVID-19 disease.\n\nThe major risk factors for severe COVID-19 are:\n\n• Increasing age (>60 years)\n\n• Underlying non-communicable diseases\n\n• Cardiac disease\n\n• Chronic lung disease\n\n• Cerebrovascular disease\n\n• Hypertension\n\n• Diabetes\n\n• Obesity\n\n• Chronic kidney disease\n\n• Immune suppression\n\n• Cancer","t":"Covid 19   Epidemiology And Clinical Features","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"A woman presents with breathlessness and hemoptysis since yesterday. Examination findings are given below. CT scan shows localised ground-glass opacity in the left posterior lobe of the lung. What is the likely diagnosis?\n\nPulse- 96/min,\n\nBP- 118/68 mmHg,\n\nTemperature- 100.3 F\n\nRR- 15 cpm\n\nSpO2- 82% on room air","o":["Tuberculosis","Necrotising pneumonia","Viral pneumonitis","Invasive aspergillosis"],"a":2,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of COVID-19. Hemoptysis is one of the atypical manifestations of COVID-19 and it may be present with more common features such as breathlessness, fever, and cough. CT-finding of localized ground-glass opacity is also supportive of the diagnosis.\n\nOther atypical manifestations of COVID-19 include:\n\n• Respiratory-\n\n• Pulmonary embolism\n\n• Hemoptysis\n\n• Cardiac-\n\n• Myocardial infarction\n\n• Myocarditis\n\n• Pericarditis\n\n• Pericardial effusion\n\n• Neurological-\n\n• Stroke\n\n• Guillain‐Barre Syndrome\n\n• Viral encephalitis\n\n• Toxic encephalopathy\n\n• Acute hemorrhagic necrotizing encephalopathy\n\n• Olfactory and taste disorders\n\n• Gastrointestinal-\n\n• Diarrhea\n\n• Nausea\n\n• Vomiting\n\n• Abdominal pain\n\n• Ocular-\n\n• Conjunctivitis\n\n• Epiphora\n\n• Cutaneous-\n\n• Erythematous rash\n\n• Widespread urticaria\n\n• Chickenpox‐like vesicles\n\n• Localized pruritic lesions\n\n• Petechial skin rash\n\nOption A: Tuberculosis would have a more chronic history and radiologically appears as dense, homogeneous parenchymal consolidation in subpleural sites of lower and middle lobes.\n\nOption B: In necrotising pneumonia, CT imaging is likely to show distinct areas of low attenuation with decreased parenchymal enhancement representing liquefaction.\n\nOption D: Invasive aspergillosis is likely to occur in the setting of immunosuppression. The associated ground glass opacity, occurring due to hemorrhage, surrounds a pulmonary nodule and is known as the halo sign.","t":"Covid 19   Epidemiology And Clinical Features","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"What is the most common lung pathology seen during the autopsy of COVID-19 patients?","o":["Pulmonary infarction","Endothelial injury","Diffuse alveolar damage","Fibrin clots"],"a":2,"e":"Diffuse alveolar damage is the most common lung pathology noted during the autopsy of COVID-19 patients.\n\nCOVID-19 related histopathological changes in the lungs include:\n\n1. Diffuse alveolar damage\n\n2. Hyaline membrane formation\n\n3. Desquamation of pneumocytes\n\n4. Fibrin deposits in lungs\n\n5. Inflammation with mononuclear cells\n\nNote: Diffuse alveolar damage is not specific to COVID-19. It may be seen in all cases of acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS) including pneumonia, burns, near drowning, etc","t":"Covid 19   Epidemiology And Clinical Features","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"During the swab collection for COVID-19 testing, the healthcare professional asks you to tilt your head approximately to what degree?","o":["30 degrees","45 degrees","70 degrees","15 degrees"],"a":2,"e":"For collecting nasal/nasopharyngeal/oropharyngeal specimens for COVID-19 testing, a person’s head should be tilted back 70 degrees.\n\nGuidelines for specimen collection:\n\n• Use appropriate PPE and maintain proper infection control measures\n\n• Restricted entry to visitors or attendants during sample collection\n\n• Complete the requisition form for each specimen submitted\n\n• Proper disposal of all waste generated\n\nRecommended personal protective equipment (PPE) for the healthcare professional who is collecting the specimen or coming within 6 feet of the patient:\n\n• N95 or higher-level respirator (or facemask if a respirator is not available)\n\n• eye protection\n\n• gloves\n\n• gown","t":"Covid 19   Investigations","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is a false statement regarding sample collection for COVID-19 testing?","o":["The nasal swab is placed in a different viral transport media tube as the throat swab.","Oropharyngeal sample is not collected using a calcium alginate swab.","Swab is rubbed over the tonsillar pillars and posterior oropharynx for oropharyngeal swab.","Swab is inserted parallel to the palate for nasopharyngeal swab."],"a":0,"e":"The nasal swab is placed in the same viral transport media tube as the throat swab.\n\nSamples that can be used for COVID-19 testing are:\n\n• Throat and nasal swab\n\n• Oropharyngeal swab\n\n• Nasopharyngeal swab\n\n• Broncho-alveolar lavage\n\n• Endotracheal aspirate\n\nCombined throat and nasal swabs:\n\n• Tilt patient's head back 70 degrees.\n\n• Gently rotate the swab and insert less than one inch into the nostril. Rotate the swab several times against the nasal wall and repeat the same in the other nostril with the same swab.\n\n• Place the tip of the swab into the transport media tube and cut off the applicator.\n\n• For throat swab, take a second swab, insert into the mouth and swab the posterior pharynx and tonsillar areas.\n\n• Place the tip of the swab into the same tube and cut off the applicator tip.\n\nOropharyngeal swab:\n\n• Tilt patient's head back 70 degrees.\n\n• Rub swab over both tonsillar pillars and posterior oropharynx and avoid touching the tongue, teeth, and gums.\n\n• Use only synthetic fiber swabs with plastic shafts. Avoid calcium alginate swabs and swabs with wooden shafts.\n\n• Place the swab immediately into sterile tubes with viral transport medium.\n\nNasopharyngeal swab:\n\n• Tilt the patient's head back 70 degrees\n\n• Insert flexible swab through the nares parallel to the palate (not upwards) until resistance is encountered\n\n• Rub and roll the swab and leave it in place for several seconds to absorb secretions before removing\n\n• Place the swab immediately into sterile tubes with viral transport medium.\n\nViral transport mediums:\n\n• Hanks balanced salt solution with Ca and Mg and heat inactivated fetal bovine serum, gentamicin and amphotericin B (most commonly used)\n\n• Sterile saline\n\n• Phosphate buffered saline-glycerol medium\n\n• Amies medium","t":"Covid 19   Investigations","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"The specimens collected for COVID-19 testing should be transported to the lab at what temperatures?","o":["-30 degree C","-4 to -8 degree C","2 to 8 degree C","12 to 16 degree C"],"a":2,"e":"Specimens collected for COVID-19 testing should be transported to the lab at a temperature of 2 to 8 degrees C as per the WHO guidelines. The ICMR/NIV guidelines recommend 4 degrees C for transport to the laboratory.\n\nBoth guidelines recommend storing specimens at -70°C or below if a delay in testing or shipping is expected.\n\nSpecimen\nSensitivity\n\nBronchoalveolar lavage fluid\n93%\n\nSputum\n72%\n\nNasopharyngeal swab\n63%\n\nOropharyngeal swab\n32%\n\nFaeces\n29%\n\nBlood\n1%","t":"Covid 19   Investigations","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"Which level of BSL (Biosafety level) facility is required during specimen handling for molecular testing of SARS CoV 2?","o":["BSL 1","BSL 2","BSL 3","BSL 4"],"a":1,"e":"Specimen handling for molecular testing of SARS CoV 2 would require BSL-2 or equivalent facilities.\n\nMicrobiology and pathology laboratories performing diagnostic tests on a stool or respiratory specimen should handle potential SARS-CoV specimens using Biosafety Level 2 (BSL-2). Virus culture and initial characterization of viral agents recovered in cultures requires minimum BSL-3 facilities.\n\nNote: Biosafety levels include a specific combination of work practices, safety equipment, and facilities that are designed to minimize the exposure of workers and the environment to infectious agents. There are four biosafety levels.","t":"Covid 19   Investigations","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"An elderly patient was rushed to the casualty following bouts of severe breathlessness. He reported a history of fever and cough for the past 3 days. On examination, his SpO2 was 85% and RR was 32/min. CT scan is shown below. What is the gold standard investigation for his diagnosis?","o":["CLIA","ELISA","RAT","RT-PCR"],"a":3,"e":"The given clinical scenario, along with CT showing bilateral multiple patchy consolidation and grid-like thickness of interlobular septa, is suggestive of COVID-19. The gold standard for its diagnosis is the identification of viral genome targets in the respiratory tract materials by reverse transcriptase polymerase chain reaction (RT-PCR) during the first week of symptoms.\n\nRT-PCR involves the reverse transcription of the genetic material of the virus (RNA) to complementary DNA (cDNA), followed by amplification of some regions of the cDNA. Four regions of the SARS CoV2 genome are used as targets:\n\n• RdRp gene (RNA-dependent RNA polymerase)\n\n• Genes from structural proteins\n\n• E (virus envelope)\n\n• N (virus nucleocapsid),\n\n• ORF1ab gene (open reading frame 1a and 1b)\n\nCBNAAT (cartridge-based nucleic acid amplification test) uses small, chip-based, portable, battery-operated devices that detect target genetic material of SARS CoV2. Examples include TrueNat and Cepheid Xpert Xpress SARS CoV-2\n\nOptions A and B: Antibody testing- Antibodies of IgA, IgM, and IgG isotypes specific to different virus proteins are detected by enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay (ELISA) or chemiluminescence immunoassays (CLIA). CLIA is more sensitive compared to ELISA.\n\nOption C: Rapid antigen test (RAT)- Antigen-based assays are used as point-of-care tests for early detection of SARS-CoV-2. They are used at the field level for early detection of infection and quick containment. Most of such tests have relatively moderate sensitivity but high specificity. They are used for diagnosis along with RT-PCR.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/1f445db4ed8a460a948006ca63a04b9a.JPEG"],"t":"Covid 19   Investigations","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"Choose the correct statement regarding testing of COVID-19:","o":["All newborns must be tested.","All pregnant women must be tested for COVID-19 in the first trimester.","No individual can voluntarily subject themselves to COVID-19 test.","A single positive RT-PCR test is considered confirmatory without repeat testing."],"a":3,"e":"A single RT-PCR/TrueNat/CBNAAT/RAT positive is considered confirmatory without repeat testing. If symptoms develop following a negative RAT, a repeat RAT or RT-PCR should be done.\n\nRoutine surveillance in containment zones and screening at points of entry for all symptomatic cases including:\n\n• Healthcare and frontline workers\n\n• Direct and high-risk contacts and individuals\n\nRoutine surveillance in non-containment areas for all symptomatic cases including:\n\n• History of international travel in the last 14 days\n\n• Contacts of a laboratory-confirmed case\n\n• Healthcare and frontline workers involved in containment and mitigation activities\n\n• Returnees and migrants within 7 days of illness\n\n• All asymptomatic high-risk contacts\n\nIn hospital settings for all symptomatic cases including:\n\n• All patients of Severe Acute Respiratory Infection (SARI)\n\n• Patients presenting in a healthcare setting\n\n• Neonates presenting with acute respiratory/sepsis-like illness\n\n• Patients with atypical manifestations (stroke, encephalitis, hemoptysis, Guillain Barre Syndrome etc.) based on the doctor's discretion\n\n• All pregnant women in/near labor hospitalized for delivery\n\n• Asymptomatic high-risk patients who are hospitalized or seeking immediate hospitalization (eg. immune compromise, malignancy, transplant, chronic comorbidities, elderly > 65 years)\n\nTesting on-demand for all individuals:\n\n• Traveling to countries/states mandating negative COVID-19 test at the point of entry\n\n• Who wish to get themselves tested","t":"Covid 19   Investigations","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"A woman, whose husband has tested positive for COVID-19, comes to the clinic with a negative report on the rapid antigen test. She is currently asymptomatic. What will be the next step?","o":["Retest with rapid antigen test after 5 days","Retest with RT-PCR after 5 days","Retest with RT-PCR immediately","Retest with RT-PCR if symptoms develop"],"a":3,"e":"Asymptomatic individuals with a negative rapid antigen test (RAT) need to be retested only if they develop symptoms of COVID-19 such as cough, fever, and difficulty in breathing.\n\nRapid antigen test, when positive, is reported as positive in both symptomatic and asymptomatic patients. When the test is negative, the patient is retested with another RAT or RT-PCR only if the patient is symptomatic.","t":"Covid 19   Investigations","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"A man presents to the flu clinic with complaints of fever and sore throat for the past 4 days. COVID-19 is suspected and a paper-based test kit as shown below is used for testing. Identify the test.","o":["TRUNAT","FELUDA","COVID Kavach ELISA","Q COVID-19 Ag Test"],"a":1,"e":"The paper-based Covid-19 test kit developed by CSIR and IGIB and approved by the Drugs Controller General of India (DCGI) is named FELUDA.\n\nFELUDA is an acronym for FNCAS9 Editor Linked Uniform Detection Assay. The name is inspired by a fictitious detective character in novels written by Satyajit Ray.\n\nFELUDA uses CRISPR-Cas9 technology (CRISPR = Clustered Regularly Interspaced Short Palindromic Repeats). The CRISPR technology uses a highly specific CAS9 protein to find and bind to the target Covid signature. This is then coupled with paper-strip chemistry to elicit a visual readout on a paper strip. It is a very affordable, sensitive (96%), and specific (98%) strip test which can detect viral genetic material within 45 minutes.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/7342e17f911f48b3a9ccfe6a58ce4bba.JPEG"],"t":"Covid 19   Investigations","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"What is the name of the COVID-19 IgG antibody detection ELISA test kit developed by ICMR and NIV?","o":["COVID Nischay ELISA","COVID Aarogya ELISA","COVID Kavach ELISA","COVID Sankalp ELISA"],"a":2,"e":"COVID Kavach ELISA is an indigenous ELISA test kit for COVID-19 antibody detection developed by ICMR Delhi and NIV Pune.\n\nIt is reported to have a 98.7% sensitivity and 100% specificity and testing is possible even at the district level. As the ELISA kit has an inactivated virus, only minimal biosafety and biosecurity requirements as compared to the real-time RT-PCR test.\n\nIgG antibodies usually start appearing after two weeks of the onset of infection. Therefore, the IgG test is not useful for detecting acute infection. IgG based antibody tests are used in sero-surveillance of the population.","t":"Covid 19   Investigations","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"What is least likely to be seen in a patient suspected of COVID-19?","o":["Elevated transaminases","Elevated fibrinogen","Lymphocytosis","Prolonged prothrombin time"],"a":2,"e":"Lymphocytosis is least likely to be seen in a patient with COVID-19. On the contrary, lymphopenia is likely to be seen.\n\nCommon laboratory abnormalities seen in COVID-19 are:\n\n• Lymphopenia\n\n• Mild thrombocytopenia\n\n• Elevated aminotransaminase levels\n\n• Elevated lactate dehydrogenase levels\n\n• Elevated inflammatory markers - eg, ferritin, C-reactive protein, and erythrocyte sedimentation rate\n\n• Markers of a hypercoagulable state - high fibrinogen, high D-dimer levels, increased fibrin degradation products, prolonged prothrombin time (PT) and activated partial thromboplastin time (aPTT)\n\nThe degree of these abnormalities tends to correlate with disease severity.","t":"Covid 19   Investigations","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"What is the most commonly seen pattern in chest CT of patients affected with COVID-19?","o":["Interlobular septal thickenings","Air bronchograms","Ground glass opacity","Crazy paving pattern"],"a":2,"e":"Ground glass opacity (GGO) is the most commonly seen pattern in chest CT of patients with COVID-19.\n\nSome of the typical features seen in the CT scans of COVID-19 patients include:\n\n• Ground glass opacities\n\n• Consolidation\n\n• Air bronchograms\n\n• Interlobular septal thickenings\n\n• Visible intralobular lines (crazy-paving pattern)\n\n• Reversed halo sign\n\nThe image below shows an axial CT scan with multiple patchy areas of pure ground-glass opacities. GGO are hazy areas with slightly increased density in the lungs without obscuration of underlying bronchial and vascular margins.\n\nThe image given below shows a CT scan with bilateral GGO in the lower lobe and an air bronchogram (arrow) in the left subpleural area. Air bronchograms appear like air-filled bronchi within a highly attenuated parenchymal background. These are dilated bronchi filled with mucus plugs and do not truly contain air.\n\nThe image given below shows a CT scan with multiple consolidation areas and interlobular septal thickening (arrows) in both lungs. The pulmonary interstitial structures such as interlobular septa and intralobular lines can thicken and manifest as small linear opacities.\n\nThe image given below shows a CT scan with crazy-paving pattern (circled area). It is defined as the presence of GGOs with superimposed interlobular septal thickening giving the appearance of irregular paving stones.\n\nThe image given below shows a CT scan with multiple lesions with reversed halo sign (arrows). The reversed halo sign or atoll sign is the presence of a ring-like area of consolidation with a superimposed rounded GGO.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/830631c7af074a2fba6e47e93865feacx986x608.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/d86285ee7a5b44ddae4aa941f7284814x1280x1010.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/2170fe46cbe6435a99b97521af63dca5x1280x1010.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/6bf628c88803466fba7b3f0c0f943153x1280x940.JPEG","https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/b7998c2151bd4abd94bf1c0f4d629fb8x1280x397.PNG"],"t":"Covid 19   Investigations","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"Increased concentrations of all of the following are poor prognostic markers in a COVID-19 patient except:","o":["Glycosylated ferritin","C-reactive protein","N-terminal pro-BNP","Procalcitonin"],"a":0,"e":"Glycosylated ferritin is decreased in severe COVID-19 illness and hence, it is not a poor prognostic marker.\n\nInterestingly, in contrast to ferritin which is actually elevated in individuals with COVID 19, the glycosylated ferritin is decreased. It is a potentially sensitive biomarker that can better discriminate severe COVID than ferritin alone.\n\nIncreased levels of the following laboratory markers is associated with poorer outcomes:\n\n• D-dimer\n\n• C-reactive protein\n\n• Lactate dehydrogenase\n\n• Cardiac troponin\n\n• Ferritin\n\n• Creatinine phosphokinase","t":"Covid 19   Investigations","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"Which inflammatory marker is of prognostic significance in COVID-19 patients?","o":["IL-6","ESR","NLR","PCT"],"a":0,"e":"Elevated pro-inflammatory cytokines like interleukin-6 (IL-6) are associated with critical and fatal COVID-19. It is specifically useful in predicting prognosis of such patients.\n\nIncreased levels of the following laboratory markers are associated with poorer outcomes:\n\n• D-dimer\n\n• C-reactive protein\n\n• Lactate dehydrogenase\n\n• Cardiac troponin\n\n• Ferritin\n\n• Creatinine phosphokinase\n\nOptions B, C and D: Inflammatory markers associated with increased severity of COVID-19 are:\n\n• PCT (procalcitonin test)\n\n• ESR\n\n• WBC\n\n• LMR (lymphocyte/mean platelet volume ratio)\n\n• HsCRP (high-sensitivity C-reactive protein)\n\n• NLR (neutrophil to lymphocyte ratio)","t":"Covid 19   Investigations","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"The airport authority sent a COVID-19 suspect patient to the designated district hospital for a consultation. He came positive on RT-PCR testing. As the duty doctor, you observe that he has mild symptoms that can be treated on an outpatient basis. What will be your recommendation?","o":["Home isolation for 7 days from testing positive","Home quarantine for 10 days from testing positive","Home isolation for 10 days from testing positive","Home quarantine for 7 days from testing positive"],"a":0,"e":"The given scenario is a mild case of COVID-19 who can be recommended home isolation for a duration of 7 days from testing positive.\n\nIsolation is the separation and restriction of movements or activities of persons who are sick, to prevent the transmission of disease.\n\nEligibility for home isolation:\n\n• Clinically assigned as a mild or asymptomatic case\n\n• Facility at their residence for self-isolation and for quarantining the family contacts\n\n• Availability of a caregiver\n\n• Must monitor his health regularly and inform his health status to the district surveillance officer\n\nThe patient can end home isolation after 7 days from testing positive and no fever for 3 successive days. There is no need for testing after the home isolation period is over.\n\nThe patient should seek medical help if he/she develops:\n\n• Unresolved High-grade fever (more than 100° F for more than 3 days)\n\n• Difficulty in breathing,\n\n• Dip in oxygen saturation (SpO2 ≤ 93% on room air at least 3 readings within 1 hour) or respiratory rate >24/ min\n\n• Persistent pain/pressure in the chest\n\n• Mental confusion or inability to arouse\n\n• Severe fatigue and myalgia\n\nQuarantine separates and restricts the movement of people who were exposed to a contagious disease to see if they become sick.","t":"Covid 19   Treatment","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"A COVID-19 positive man is rushed to the casualty due to severe breathlessness. Examination findings are suggestive of pneumonia and his vitals are- PR: 90/min, BP: 134/88 mmHg, RR: 22/min and SpO2: 86%. What will you advise this patient?\n\nPR: 90/min\n\nBP: 134/88 mmHg\n\nRR: 22/min\n\nTemperature: 101 F\n\nSpO2: 86%","o":["Home isolation","Admit to COVID care center","Admit to ward","Admit to ICU"],"a":3,"e":"The patient is suffering from severe COVID-19 and hence has to be admitted to ICU. The disease is said to be severe when there is RR>30/min, breathlessness OR SpO2<90% on room air.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/fe5cb670ee104a838a63332770a2d0b0x1280x4371.JPEG"],"t":"Covid 19   Treatment","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"A man presents with fever and cough for 2 days. His wife recently tested positive for COVID-19 and has been under home isolation. Examination findings are given below. What is not recommended in his management?\n\nPulse- 110/min,\n\nBP- 78/58 mmHg,\n\nTemperature- 101.3 F\n\nRR- 28 cpm\n\nSpO2- 90% on room air","o":["Awake proning","Administration of paracetamol","Oxygen support","Prophylactic LMW heparin"],"a":0,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of moderate COVID-19 (SpO2 <94%, RR≥24). Awake proning should be avoided in patients with hemodynamic instability.\n\nAwake proning in moderate COVID disease is recommended for any patient with severe respiratory distress. It improves oxygenation by keeping the alveolar units open. Timely proning of patients with SpO2 < 94% can help reduce mortality.\n\nSteps for self-proning:\n\n• 1 pillow below the neck\n\n• 1 or 2 pillows below the chest through upper thighs\n\n• 2 pillows below the shins\n\n• Keep alternating between proning, laying on your right, sitting up, and laying on your left, in a lying position, ideally for not more than 30 minutes in each position with an upper limit of 2 hours.\n\n• One may prone up to 16 hours a day, in multiple cycles, if tolerable.\n\nAvoid proning:\n\n• Hemodynamic instability\n\n• For 1 hour after meals\n\n• DVT, cardiac conditions\n\n• Fractures of spine, femur, or pelvis","t":"Covid 19   Treatment","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"A 42-year-old COVID-19 positive man under home isolation develops breathing difficulty. SpO2 is 86%. He is administered oxygen via a face mask with a reservoir bag with a flow rate of 10 L/min. After a trial of one hour, his SpO2 is 88%. What is the next step?","o":["Increase flow rate to 15 L/min","High-flow nasal cannula oxygen therapy","Endotracheal intubation","No specific intervention necessary"],"a":1,"e":"This patient has persistent hypoxia after an adequate trial of standard oxygen therapy and should be started on high-flow nasal cannula oxygen therapy.\n\nWhen respiratory distress and/or hypoxemia of the patient cannot be alleviated after receiving standard oxygen therapy, high–flow nasal cannula oxygen therapy (HFNO), or non–invasive ventilation can be considered. Compared to standard oxygen therapy, HFNO reduces the need for intubation.\n\nPatients on HFNO should be closely monitored, and endotracheal intubation should be done if the patient acutely deteriorates or does not improve after a short trial (about 1 hr).\n\nContraindications to HFNO includes patients with:\n\n• Hypercapnia (exacerbation of COPD)\n\n• Hemodynamic instability\n\n• Multi-organ failure\n\n• Abnormal mental status\n\nNote: If an high flow nasal cannula (HFNC) or simple nasal cannula is used to administer O2, an N95 mask should be applied over it.","t":"Covid 19   Treatment","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"An 84-year-old man is being treated in the ICU for sepsis induced respiratory failure secondary to COVID-19. What is the maximum tidal volume setting permissible for his mechanical ventilation? (PBW-predicted body weight)","o":["4 ml/kg PBW","6 ml/kg PBW","8 ml/kg PBW","10 ml/kg PBW"],"a":2,"e":"A maximum tidal volume of up to 8 ml/kg PBW is allowed in a patient with ARDS who is being mechanically ventilated.\n\nIn patients with ARDS, mechanical ventilation should be implemented using lower tidal volumes (4–8 ml/kg PBW) and lower inspiratory pressures (plateau pressure <30 cmH2O).\n\nIn patients with moderate or severe ARDS, a higher PEEP is suggested. PEEP titration requires consideration of benefits (improving alveolar recruitment) vs. risks (end-inspiratory over distension leading to lung injury).\n\nLung protective ventilation strategy by ARDS net protocol recommendations:\n\nParameters\nValues\n\nTidal volume\n6ml/kg\n\nRR\n15-35/min\n\nPEEP\n5-15cm H2O\n\nTarget plateau pressure\n< 30cm H2O\n\nTarget SpO2\n88-95%\n\nTarget PaO2\n55-80mmHg","t":"Covid 19   Treatment","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"An elderly diabetic man with COVID-19 is desaturating on non-invasive ventilation. You intubate him and place him on a mechanical ventilator. He improves briefly but the SpO2 suddenly drops. You suspect a tubal block and perform closed suctioning. Despite this, he goes into cardiac arrest and you start CPR. You would not expect aerosol generation in:","o":["Non-invasive ventilation","Manual ventilation","Closed suctioning","CPR"],"a":2,"e":"Open suctioning of the respiratory tract is associated with significant amounts of aerosol generation and not closed suctioning.\n\nCommonly performed aerosol-generating procedures are:\n\n• Open suctioning of the respiratory tract\n\n• Bronchoscopy\n\n• Cardiopulmonary resuscitation (CPR)\n\n• Non-invasive ventilation (e.g., BiPAP, CPAP)\n\n• Endotracheal intubation and extubation\n\n• Manual ventilation\n\nIt is recommended that healthcare providers should be protected with PPE while performing these procedures. It must be performed in an adequately ventilated single room with a minimum of 12 air changes/hour.","t":"Covid 19   Treatment","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"A 67-year-old patient with end-stage renal disease is tested positive for COVID-19. Which anticoagulation agent would you recommended for him?","o":["Unfractionated heparin","LMWH","Warfarin","Dabigatran"],"a":0,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of COVID-19 in a patient with end-stage renal disease. Unfractionated heparin is recommended for use in such patients.\n\nProphylactic anticoagulation is used in the management of moderate and severe COVID cases. A prophylactic dose of low molecular weight heparin or unfractionated heparin is used to prevent thromboembolism. In a COVID patient with end-stage renal disease, unfractionated heparin is the preferred agent.","t":"Covid 19   Treatment","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"Which case scenarios along with the drugs prescribed are inappropriate?\n\n1. A man on anti-retroviral therapy with severe COVID pneumonia- Tocilizumab\n\n2. A non-hospitalized middle-aged diabetic man with mild COVID pneumonia- Bamlanivimab\n\n3. A mother on post-partum day 3 with moderate COVID pneumonia maintaining with NIPPV- Remedisivir\n\n4. A woman with COVID-19 disease- Convalescent plasma therapy\n\n5. A woman with moderate COVID pneumonia on supplemental oxygen- Dexamethasone","o":["1, 2, 3, 4, 5","1, 2, 3, and 4 only","3, 4 and 5","1 and 2 only"],"a":1,"e":"The correct answer is 1, 2, 3, and 4.\n\n1. Tocilizumab is contraindicated in HIV-positive patients and those with active bacterial or fungal infection.\n\n2. Bamlanivimab is not authorized for patients with mild COVID-19 pneumonia who are not at a high risk for hospitalization.\n\n3. Remedisivir is contraindicated in postnatal lactating women.\n\n4. Convalescent plasma therapy is no longer recommended in the management of COVID patients.\n\nTocilizumab acts by IL-6 inhibition. It may be considered in moderate-severe COVID patients with progressively increasing oxygen requirements and mechanically ventilated patients not improving despite the use of steroids. Patients should be carefully monitored for secondary infections and neutropenia after treatment with tocilizumab. It is contraindicated in:\n\n• HIV\n\n• Active infections (systemic bacterial/fungal)\n\n• Tuberculosis\n\n• Active hepatitis\n\n• Absolute neutrophil count < 2000/mm3\n\n• Platelet count < 1,00,000/mm3\n\nBamlanivimab is a monoclonal antibody directed against the spike protein of SARS-CoV-2. The FDA has issued an emergency use authorization for bamlanivimab for the treatment of adult and pediatric mild to moderate COVID patients (12 years of age and older weighing at least 40 kilograms) who are at high risk for progressing to severe COVID-19 and/or hospitalization. Possible side effects include anaphylaxis and infusion-related reactions, nausea, diarrhea, dizziness, headache, itching, and vomiting. There is no specific contraindication for this drug.\n\nRemdesivir is an antiviral drug that works by inhibiting RNA-dependent RNA polymerase. It may be considered in moderate and severe hospitalized COVID patients requiring supplemental oxygen/oxygen delivery through a high flow device/ noninvasive ventilation/ invasive mechanical ventilation/ ECMO. It is contraindicated in:\n\n• AST/ALT > 5 times upper limit of normal\n\n• Severe renal impairment (i.e., eGFR < 30ml/min/m2 or need for hemodialysis)\n\n• Pregnancy or lactating females\n\n• Children <12 years of age\n\nConvalescent plasma therapy is no longer recommended in the management of COVID patients, as per the revised guidelines.\n\nDexamethasone is generally given to all patients of moderate and severe COVID-19. Patients with severe COVID-19 can develop a systemic inflammatory response that can lead to lung injury and multisystem organ dysfunction.","t":"Covid 19   Treatment","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"A G2P1 woman presents to the clinic at 36 weeks of gestation. Her husband was COVID positive and now she has also been tested positive. What is true regarding her management?","o":["C-section is the preferred mode of delivery","Awake proning is not recommended","CT scan is absolutely contraindicated","Aim to keep oxygen saturation more than 94% during labour"],"a":3,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of COVID-19 disease in a pregnant patient. The management should include an aim to keep oxygen saturation above 94% during labour in this patient. In such cases, maternal observations and assessment during labour should be continued as per standard practice, with the addition of hourly oxygen saturation.\n\nOption A: There is currently no evidence to favour one mode of birth over another. Mode of birth should not be influenced by the presence of COVID-19, unless the woman’s respiratory condition demands urgent intervention for birth.\n\nOption B: Awake proning is recommended. Although evidence is limited, there are reports that this is feasible with appropriate padding up to at least 28 weeks of gestation.\n\nOption C: CT scan is relatively contraindicated. Pregnant women with abdominal shielding should not be excluded from chest CT if clinically recommended.","t":"Covid 19   Treatment","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"Which of the following is a criterion for discharge in a patient with mild COVID-19 infection?","o":["Discharge after 10 days from testing positive &amp; no fever for 3 days","Discharge after 7 days from testing positive &amp; no fever for 3 days","Discharge if SpO2&gt;98% on room air for 3 consecutive days","Discharge if repeat RT-PCR testing is negative"],"a":1,"e":"COVID-19 cases that are mild/very mild/presymptomatic can be discharged from home isolation after 7 days of testing positive and no fever for 3 successive days. There is no need for testing after the end of home isolation.\n\nCriteria to diagnose asymptomatic COVID-19: Laboratory confirmed case with no symptoms with a saturation of more than 93% in room air.\n\nCriteria to diagnose mild COVID-19: Laboratory confirmed case with upper respiratory tract symptoms with/without fever, without shortness of breath, and saturation of more than 93% in room air.\n\nPatients are advised to isolate from other family members and monitor themselves at home, under proper medical guidance. Patients >60 years with co-morbidities, and immunocompromised patients are allowed to isolate only after receiving clearance from the designated medical officer.\n\nImmediate medical attention is required if the patient experiences the following:\n\n• A 100 ºF fever persists for >3 days and does not resolve on medication.\n\n• Difficulty in breathing\n\n• Oxygen saturation ≤93% (at least 3 readings within 1 hour)\n\n• Respiratory rate >24/min\n\n• Persistent chest pain or pressure\n\n• Mental confusion\n\n• Severe fatigue or myalgia","t":"Covid 19   Treatment","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"A 33-year-old delivery boy tested COVID-19 positive. He started experiencing worsening breathlessness and cough and rushed to the hospital when his pulse oximeter reading was 87%. Following treatment, he showed signs of improvement. What is correct before discharging him?","o":["Discharge after 7 days from symptom onset &amp; no fever for 3 days","Discharge if repeat RT-PCR testing is negative after recovery","Discharge if SpO2&gt;98% on room air for 3 consecutive days","Discharge at the discretion of the treating medical officer"],"a":3,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of severe COVID-19 disease. Severe cases of COVID-19 (and immunocompromised patients) are discharged based on their clinical recovery at the discretion of the treating medical officer.\n\nNote: Mild and moderate cases do not need an RT-PCR test before discharge. But, at the time of discharge, they are advised to self-monitor and continue wearing masks for a duration of 7 days.","t":"Covid 19   Treatment","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"A 10-year-old boy with COVID-19 presents with sore throat, rhinorrhea and cough. He does not have fast breathing. What is recommended for this child?\n\n1. Home isolation\n\n2. Hospital admission\n\n3. Awake proning\n\n4. Antibiotics\n\n5. Low dose steroids","o":["2 and 5","3 only","2 and 4","1 only"],"a":3,"e":"The presence of sore throat, rhinorrhea, cough with no fast breathing are suggestive of mild COVID-19 disease. Hence, home isolation along with supportive care is recommended in this child.","ei":["https://dhmbxeygs57ff.cloudfront.net/uploads/f392507b3b8641adb039521bd36fda16x1280x4635.JPEG"],"t":"Covid 19   Treatment","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"FDA has recently sanctioned emergency use authorization to the first oral antiviral drug combination for the treatment of COVID-19. This drug combination contains:","o":["Nirmatrelvir and Ritonavir","Molnupiravir and Ritonavir","Nirmatrelvir and Indinavir","Molnupiravir and Indinavir"],"a":0,"e":"FDA recently sanctioned emergency use authorization to a combination of nirmatrelvir and ritonavir tablets (Paxlovid) for the treatment of mild-to-moderate COVID-19. It is the first oral antiviral for the treatment of COVID-19.\n\nNirmatrelvir is a peptidomimetic inhibitor of the SARS-CoV-2 main protease (Mpro). Inhibition of this enzyme makes the virus incapable of processing polyprotein precursors and thus prevents viral replication.\n\nRitonavir is an HIV-1 protease inhibitor. It inhibits the CYP3A-mediated metabolism of nirmatrelvir which results in increased plasma concentrations of nirmatrelvir. It is not active against SARS-CoV-2 Mpro.\n\nPatients who tested positive with direct SARS-CoV-2 test can be administered this drug combination, provided they are:\n\n• 12 years of age and older\n\n• Weighing at least 40 kgs\n\n• At high risk for progression to severe COVID-19.\n\nContraindications include hypersensitivity reactions to the active ingredients or any other components, co-administration with drugs highly dependent on CYP3A for clearance, co-administration with potent CYP3A inducers. Adverse reactions include dysgeusia, diarrhea, hypertension, and myalgia.","t":"Covid 19   Treatment","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"Your worried friend calls you for advice after attending a family function. He informs you that his aunt, who also travelled to the function in the same car, tested positive for COVID-19. What is your advice for your friend?","o":["Home isolation for 14 days","Home quarantine for 14 days","Home quarantine for 10 days","Home isolation for 10 days"],"a":1,"e":"Home quarantine for 14 days is advised when a person comes in contact with a COVID-19 positive patient.\n\nThe home quarantined person:\n\n• Should stay in a well ventilated single room and stay away from high-risk individuals\n\n• Should not attend any religious or social gatherings\n\n• Must wash hands often\n\n• Must change mask every 6-8 hrs\n\n• Their surroundings must be disinfected with 1% sodium hypochlorite solution\n\nIn case the person being quarantined becomes symptomatic, all his contacts should be home quarantined for 14 days and followed up for another 14 days or till the report turns out negative on lab testing.\n\nIsolation is the separation and restriction of movements or activities of persons who are sick, to prevent the transmission of disease. Quarantine is the separation and restriction of movements or activities of persons who are not ill but are believed to have been exposed to infection, for the purpose of preventing transmission of diseases.","t":"Covid 19   Prophylaxis And Complications","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"A 50-year-old man recovering from COVID-19 presents as follows. His medical and personal histories are insignificant with no exposure to cold. What would you suspect?","o":["Chilblains","COVID toes","Varicella","Eczema"],"a":1,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of COVID toes. The image shows pernio-like lesions caused due to COVID-19.\n\nPernio / chilblains are skin lesions on the acral surfaces of the body characterised by the development of erythematous-violaceous or purpuric macules. They may be accompanied by edema and pruritus.\n\nIt is usually due to cold exposure or underlying hematological disorders but has also been reported in patients with COVID-19 disease. The underlying mechanism is thought to be an inflammatory process. The lesions appear late (postviral phase) with the patients exhibiting positive IgM/ IgG antibodies for SARS-CoV-2 and negative RT-PCR. However, pernio like lesions can also be seen in early stages of the disease when the patients are still infective.\n\nThere are no treatment guidelines but high-potency topical corticosteroids may be helpful if the lesions are causing discomfort. Other cutaneous manifestations include exanthematous rash, livedo reticularis, urticaria, MIS-C etc.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/659da52e46934adbb21f851fade520a5.JPEG"],"t":"Covid 19   Prophylaxis And Complications","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"While donning a set of PPE, what is the order that you will follow?\n\n1. Coverall\n\n2. First pair of gloves\n\n3. Shoe cover\n\n4. N-95 mask","o":["2→1→3→4","1→2→3→4","1→2→4→3","2→1→4→3"],"a":0,"e":"The correct sequence of donning a PPE is first pair of gloves → coverall → Shoe cover → N-95 mask.\n\nPreparation for donning the PPE:\n\n• To be performed only in the designated area\n\n• The health care worker must be prepared for the shift duty by\n\n• Taking a meal\n\n• Using the washroom\n\n• Drinking water prior to entering the donning area\n\n• Remove all accessories, jewelry\n\n• Change into clean hospital scrubs and closed washable footwear\n\n• Perform hand hygiene with soap and water\n\nSteps of donning/putting on the PPE:\n\n• Hand hygiene\n\n• Wear the first pair of gloves\n\n• Sterile or unsterile\n\n• Make sure the cuff of the gloves goes as far as possible\n\n• Examine and wear coverall\n\n• Examine the coverall for any damage\n\n• Start by wearing the leg sleeves and then the arm sleeves\n\n• Zip up and cover the zip with the flap attached\n\n• Wear shoe cover\n\n• Sit on the clean chair and wear shoe cover\n\n• Pull them up till your calves\n\n• Wear N-95 mask and wear hood\n\n• Hold the mask like a cup in the hand with both the straps hanging out in the front\n\n• Place the mask on the face, pull and wear the lower strap first placing it below the ears\n\n• Then pull and wear the upper strap placing above the ears\n\n• The air leakage should be minimum around the mask when forcibly exhaling\n\n• Wear the hood covering the forehead and the cheeks\n\n• Wear face shield/eye goggles over the hood and ensure the fit\n\n• Wear the second pair of gloves - pull the gloves to cover the sleeve cuff and forearm as much as possible","t":"Covid 19   Prophylaxis And Complications","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"While doffing, what is the first component of the PPE that you would remove?","o":["Coverall","Shoe covers","Face shield","Gloves"],"a":1,"e":"The component of the PPE kit to be removed first is the shoe covers.\n\nPreparation for doffing the PPE:\n\n• To be performed only in the designated area\n\n• Check for any leak or soiling\n\n• Doffing room should have two chairs, dirty and clean\n\n• All the PPE must be discarded in the red bin\n\n• Hand hygiene must be performed after every step\n\nSteps of doffing the PPE:\n\n• Engage the observer and ask him to check the PPE - observer should check for any leak, tear, or contamination of PPE. If contaminated, then it should be disinfected with alcohol-based wipes.\n\n• Disinfect the gloves\n\n• Remove and discard shoe covers\n\n• Sit on the dirty chair with legs apart\n\n• Remove the shoe covers by slowly pulling the outer surface starting from the top and then pull from the toes end. Discard them\n\n• Disinfect and remove outer pair of gloves\n\n• While removing the outer gloves, inner gloves should not be torn or contaminated\n\n• Pinch the first glove on the outer surface at the wrist and pull it inside out\n\n• Hold this glove in the other hand like a ball\n\n• Slide the thumb inside the other glove and remove inside out balling around the previous glove, making it into a single bag. Then discard it.\n\n• Disinfect inner gloves\n\n• Remove face shield/eye goggle - loosen the screw and remove the face shield by holding it from the back handle. Discard it.\n\n• Disinfect inner gloves again\n\n• Remove hood and coverall\n\n• Slide back the hood by holding it from the top\n\n• Separate the zip flap and unzip the coverall suit\n\n• Holding the suit at the arms, slide it off the shoulder slightly, then pull the suit from the side of the waist\n\n• Roll the arms out one by one\n\n• By touching the inner surface of the suit carefully roll out each leg. Discard it\n\n• Disinfect and remove inner gloves as previously described\n\n• Perform hand hygiene and wear a new pair of gloves\n\n• Remove and discard N-95 mask\n\n• Do not touch the exposed surface of the mask\n\n• Stoop forward and first remove the lower strap\n\n• Then pull out the upper strap and remove the mask\n\n• Disinfect gloves\n\n• Sit on the clean chair and disinfect shoes using alcohol wipes\n\n• Disinfect and remove gloves\n\n• Perform hand hygiene\n\n• Wipe the shoes on sodium hypochlorite soaked doormat at exit\n\n• Exit the doffing area and take a shower","t":"Covid 19   Prophylaxis And Complications","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"You are a medical officer at the designated COVID hospital in your city and are required to don PPE every day at work. Which area of the PPE should you not touch while doffing?","o":["Earpiece / ties of the mask","Inside the mask","Front of face shield","Back of isolation gown"],"a":2,"e":"The front of the face shield is considered to be a contaminated area of the personal protective equipment (PPE). Hence, it should not be touched while doffing.\n\nThe areas that are considered clean are the parts that will be touched while removing a PPE:\n\n• Inside the gloves\n\n• Inside and back of the gown including the ties\n\n• Ties or earpieces of the mask, goggles, and face shields\n\nThe areas that are considered contaminated are:\n\n• Outside front and sleeves of the isolation gown\n\n• Front of goggles, mask, respirator, and face shield\n\n• Outside of the gloves","t":"Covid 19   Prophylaxis And Complications","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"After your COVID ICU shift, where would you dispose your face shield?","o":["Red bin","Black bag","Yellow bin","White bag"],"a":0,"e":"The face shield used by a healthcare worker during COVID-19 pandemic is disposed in the red bin.","t":"Covid 19   Prophylaxis And Complications","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"What is recommended for cleaning and disinfecting a healthcare facility against COVID-19?","o":["1% formaldehyde","1% glutaraldehyde","35% isopropyl alcohol","1% sodium hypochlorite"],"a":3,"e":"1% sodium hypochlorite is recommended for cleaning and disinfection against COVID-19.\n\nThe sodium hypochlorite solution should be prepared fresh. Leaving the solution for a contact time of at least 10 minutes is recommended.\n\nNote: Alcohol (e.g. isopropyl 70% or ethyl alcohol 70%) can be used to wipe down surfaces where the use of bleach is not suitable, e.g. metals.","t":"Covid 19   Prophylaxis And Complications","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"Identify the wrong match regarding COVID-19 vaccines:","o":["Pfizer - mRNA based vaccine","Covaxin - Inactivated vaccine","Moderna - Recombinant DNA vaccine","AstraZeneca - Non-replicating viral vector vaccine"],"a":2,"e":"The Moderna COVID-19 vaccine is also an mRNA-based vaccine.\n\nVaccines\nMechanism\n\nPfizer/BioNtech (Comirnaty)\nmRNA based vaccine\n\nModerna\nmRNA based vaccine\n\nOxford University/ Astra Zeneca/ Serum Institute of India (Covishield)\nNon-replicating viral vector\n\nGamaleya (Sputnik V)/ Dr.Reddys\nNon-replicating viral vector\n\nJohnson and Johnson (Janssen Ad26.CoV2.S)\nNon-replicating viral vector\n\nBharat biotech/NIV (Covaxin)\nInactivated vaccine\n\nZydus Cadila (ZyCoV-D)\nPlasmid DNA vaccine\n\nNovavax\nProtein subunit\n\nThe Drugs Controller General of India (DCGI) has granted permission for restricted emergency use of the following vaccines against COVID-19:\n\n• Covaxin (Bharat biotech/NIV)\n\n• Covishield (Oxford University/ AstraZeneca/ Serum Institute of India)\n\n• Sputnik V (Gamaleya Research Institute/ Dr. Reddy's Laboratories)\n\n• ZyCoV-D (Zydus Cadila)\n\nThe FDA has issued an emergency use authorization for the following vaccines against COVID-19:\n\n• Pfizer/BioNtech\n\n• Moderna\n\n• Johnson and Johnson\n\nAccording to the new vaccine guidelines by NEGVAC (National Expert Group on Vaccine Administration for COVID-19):\n\n1. Vaccination is to be deferred by 3 months in the following persons:\n\n• Individuals (proven positive) recovered from the disease.\n\n• Recipients of monoclonal antibodies or convalescent plasma.\n\n• 2nd dose is to be deferred by 3 months if infected with COVID after 1st dose.\n\n1. 4-8 weeks gap, after recovery of patients with other serious illnesses requiring hospitalization/ICU care.\n\n2. Blood donation: Wait for 14 days after receiving the vaccine/testing RT-PCR negative.\n\n3. No screening is required by rapid antigen test (RAT) prior to vaccination.\n\n4. Recommended for all lactating women.\n\nCOVID-19 vaccination during pregnancy:\n\n• MoHFW has recently approved the vaccination of pregnant women against COVID-19.\n\n• COVID-19 vaccine can be taken anytime during pregnancy and should be taken at the earliest.\n\n• If any woman has been infected with COVID-19 during pregnancy, she should be vaccinated soon after delivery.\n\n• COVID-19 vaccine is safe in pregnancy.","t":"Covid 19   Prophylaxis And Complications","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"Four days after being extubated, an elderly diabetic man with COVID-19 developed rhinosinusitis. The tissue obtained from the sinuses showed the following. The physician wanted to start the patient on amphotericin B. However, it was not available. Which other drug can be given to this patient?","o":["Voriconazole","Isavuconazole","Itraconazole","Flucytosine"],"a":1,"e":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of mucormycosis. Intravenous administration of amphotericin B is the first line treatment. Other drugs which are approved for its management include isavuconazole and posaconazole. Surgical debridement is the last line management.\n\nImmunocompromised states are important risk factors including diabetes mellitus and glucocorticoid treatment as well as patients on ventilation.\n\nDiagnosis can be made by culturing the organisms from sterile sites like needle aspirate, pleural fluid, or a tissue biopsy specimen. The biopsy is most sensitive. It reveals characteristic wide, thick-walled, ribbon-like, aseptate hyphal elements that branch at right angles.\n\nIt can present as:\n\n• Rhino-orbital-cerebral mucormycosis - (most common) the initial symptoms are nonspecific and include eye or facial pain and facial numbness followed by the onset of conjunctival suffusion and blurry vision. If untreated it can spread from the sinuses to the orbit, and then to the frontal lobe.\n\n• Pulmonary mucormycosis - (second most common)\n\n• Cutaneous mucormycosis - usually results after trauma\n\n• Gastrointestinal mucormycosis - primarily occurs in premature neonates who have disseminated disease or necrotizing enterocolitis\n\n• Disseminated mucormycosis","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/4bab9392379c46eb85f2565ca6255c5c.JPEG"],"t":"Covid 19   Prophylaxis And Complications","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"A young girl, who recently recovered from COVID-19, presents with fever, abdominal pain and rashes for 4 days. On examination, strawberry tongue is observed and cervical nodes are found to be enlarged bilaterally. What is the likely diagnosis?\n\nHR: 110/min\n\nBP: 80/60 mm Hg\n\nRR: 20/min\n\nCRP: 10 mg/L\n\nESR: 76mm/hr.","o":["Kawasaki disease","Multisystem inflammatory syndrome in children","Bacterial sepsis","Toxic shock syndrome"],"a":1,"e":"This is a case of multisystem inflammatory syndrome in children (MIS-C), associated with COVID-19 infection.\n\nDiagnostic criteria for MIS-C:\n\nChildren and adolescents 0–18 years of age with fever ≥3 days\n\nAND two of these:\n\n• Rash or bilateral non-purulent conjunctivitis or mucocutaneous inflammation signs (palmoplantar erythema, lip hyperemia, periorbital erythema and edema, strawberry tongue, and malar erythema)\n\n• Hypotension or shock.\n\n• Features of myocardial dysfunction, pericarditis, valvulitis, or coronary abnormalities (including ECHO findings or elevated Troponin/NT-proBNP)\n\n• Evidence of coagulopathy (PT, PTT, elevated D-Dimers)\n\n• Acute gastrointestinal problems (diarrhoea, vomiting, or abdominal pain)\n\nAND\n\nElevated ESR(>40 mm), C-reactive protein (>5 mg/L), or procalcitonin.\n\nAND\n\nNo other obvious microbial cause of inflammation including bacterial sepsis, staphylococcal or streptococcal shock syndromes.\n\nAND\n\nEvidence of COVID-19 (RT-PCR, antigen test, or serology positive), or likely contact with patients with COVID-19.\n\nTypes of MIS-C:\n\n• MIS-C with shock/multi-organ dysfunction\n\n• Kawasaki phenotype\n\n• MIS-C without shock\n\nMIS-C is treated with IV methylprednisolone. The dose can be increased in patients with shock. If the condition does not improve for 48-72 hours, specialists can be consulted for the addition of biologicals to the treatment regimen.","t":"Covid 19   Prophylaxis And Complications","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"Which gene increases the risk of collapsing glomerulopathy in COVID-19 patients?","o":["NPHS1","ACTN4","APOL1","TRPC6"],"a":2,"e":"APOL1 gene increases the risk of collapsing nephropathy in patients infected with COVID-19.\n\nGlomerular lesions are reported in a minority of COVID-19 patients. They are associated with collapsing focal segmental glomerulosclerosis (FSGS) and are called COVID-associated nephropathy (COVAN). Such patients present with nephrotic-range proteinuria and acute kidney injury (AKI). It specifically affects individuals of African ancestry who are carriers of this gene variant.\n\nThrombotic microangiopathy is another uncommon finding among these patients. Histopathology is similar to those seen in other forms of collapsing glomerulopathy. But, tubulointerstitial lesions including microcystic tubular dilatation and tubular injury are typically associated with APOL1- related disease.","t":"Covid 19   Prophylaxis And Complications","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"As a healthcare worker working in a COVID hospital, you receive the following vaccine. Which of the following is not an adverse event related to this vaccine?","o":["Cough","Tremors","Demyelinating disorder","Dizziness-giddiness"],"a":2,"e":"Demyelinating disorders are rare adverse events following Covishield, not Covaxin.\n\nComposition of Covaxin:\n\n• Inactivated coronavirus\n\n• Aluminum hydroxide gel\n\n• TLR 7/8 agonist\n\n• 2-Phenoxyethanol and\n\n• Phosphate Buffered Saline/ NKA1\n\n Adverse effects:\n\n Covaxin\nCovishield\n\nFever, cough, sweating\n\nFever and chills\n\nInjection site pain and swelling\n\nInjection site pain and swelling\n\nHeadache, bodyache, fatigue\n\nHeadache, malaise, fatigue\n\nAbdominal pain, nausea, vomiting\n\nNausea\n\nDizziness, giddiness, tremor\n\nMyalgia, arthralgia\n\nRarely, complications like demyelinating disorders, Guillain-Barre syndrome, and vaccine-induced immune thrombotic thrombocytopenia (VITT) are also seen following Covishield vaccine.","i":["https://cdn1.dailyrounds.org/uploads/70fd540698f24905a3154dcbc7c9fed0.JPEG"],"t":"Covid 19   Prophylaxis And Complications","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"In which of the following patients, would you administer Covidshield vaccination?\n\n1. A 51-year-old man who underwent hematopoietic stem cell transplantation 5 weeks ago for aplastic anemia.\n\n2. A 60-year-old man with a history of type-2 diabetes mellitus.\n\n3. A 32-year-old woman who received TT immunization 20 days back following a trivial fall from a bike.\n\n4. A 45-year-old man on dialysis three times a week for chronic kidney disease.\n\n5. A 40-year-old woman on insulin therapy for type-1 diabetes mellitus.","o":["1, 2, 3","1, 2, 4, 5","2, 3, 4, 5","3, 4, 5"],"a":2,"e":"The 51-year-old man should receive COVID-19 vaccination at least 3 months after hematopoietic cell transplantation (and not 5 weeks) to maximize vaccine efficacy.\n\nGraft-versus-host disease and immunosuppressive regimens like systemic corticosteroids can blunt the immune responses to vaccination. Delay of vaccination until immunosuppressive therapy is reduced can be considered.\n\nConsider additional risk factors linked to adverse COVID-19 complications including, but not limited to:\n\n• Patients with advanced age (eg, ≥65 years of age)\n\n• Patients with comorbidities (eg, chronic pulmonary, cardiovascular, or renal disease)\n\n• Social and demographic factors\n\nCOVID-19 vaccines should be prioritized over other vaccines. A gap of 14 days is recommended between COVID-19 vaccines and any other vaccine.\n\nIf COVID vaccine has been given first, then TT vaccine can be given on the same day. If TT vaccine have been given first, then wait for 14 days to give COVID vaccine.","t":"Covid 19   Prophylaxis And Complications","c":"HIV and COVID-19"},
 {"q":"A 24-year-old boy who had SARS CoV-2 infection 3 months back came with complaints of fatigue and shortness of breath for 3 days. The minimum duration of these symptoms to define it as post-Covid-19 condition according to WHO is ____","o":["2 months","2 weeks","4 months","4 weeks"],"a":0,"e":"The minimum duration of symptoms such as fatigue, shortness of breath to define it as post Covid-19 condition according to WHO is 2 months.\n\nWHO clinical case definition of post Covid-19 - Post Covid-19 condition occurs in individuals with a history of probable or confirmed SARS CoV-2 infection, usually 3 months from the onset of Covid-19, with symptoms that last for at least 2 months and cannot be explained by an alternative diagnosis.\n\nCommon symptoms include\n\n• Fatigue\n\n• Shortness of breath\n\n• Cognitive dysfunction.","t":"Covid 19   Prophylaxis And Complications","c":"HIV and COVID-19"}
];
